window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 1001, "domain": "histology", "question": "A breast cancer laboratory reports ER and PR results to guide therapy on an invasive ductal carcinoma fixed in formalin for 24 hours. On the slide, where is the staining located, and what is recorded by the technologist and pathologist?", "options": {"A": "Basement membrane staining, with fibre thickness and length measured", "B": "Nuclear staining, with percentage of positive tumour nuclei and intensity recorded", "C": "Cytoplasmic staining, with granule colour, size and distribution recorded", "D": "Membrane staining, with complete ring pattern scored from 0 to 3+"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Nuclear staining, with percentage of positive tumour nuclei and intensity recorded): ER and PR are nuclear stains, and both the percentage of positive nuclei and staining intensity are recorded. Membrane scoring from 0 to 3+ describes HER2."}, {"id": 1002, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "In targeted NGS, one enrichment method uses synthetic probes that bind to the regions of interest and are then used to pull those fragments out of the library before sequencing. What is this enrichment approach called? The laboratory wants the name that matches this specific approach.", "options": {"A": "Fragmentation enrichment, which selects target fragments by their size on a gel", "B": "Barcode enrichment, which selects patient libraries by their index sequences", "C": "Hybrid capture, which retrieves target fragments by base pairing with complementary probes", "D": "Amplicon enrichment, which amplifies targets with many primer pairs in multiplex PCR"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hybrid capture, which retrieves target fragments by base pairing with complementary probes): Probes that hybridize to targets and pull them out define hybrid capture. Amplicon methods use PCR primers instead. Size selection and barcodes do not select genes, since indexes only label the source library."}, {"id": 1003, "domain": "hematology", "question": "Von Willebrand disease testing includes several assays that together help classify the disorder. A technologist explains that one common population has naturally lower baseline levels, which complicates borderline results. Which blood group is associated with naturally lower von Willebrand factor levels?", "options": {"A": "Blood group AB, so borderline results are interpreted cautiously", "B": "Blood group B, so borderline results are interpreted cautiously", "C": "Blood group O, so borderline results are interpreted cautiously", "D": "Blood group A, so borderline results are interpreted cautiously"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Blood group O, so borderline results are interpreted cautiously): Blood group O individuals naturally have lower VWF levels, which is a reason to interpret borderline results cautiously. The other blood groups tend to have higher baseline levels."}, {"id": 1004, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A cytogenetics manager wants an independent measure of how well the analysts perform across the whole laboratory, beyond internal slide reviews and equipment logs. Which quality assurance activity provides this outside check on the analysts' ability to identify chromosome abnormalities?", "options": {"A": "Counting the number of slides made per week, which measures speed but not accuracy", "B": "Proficiency testing with external unknown cases, compared with the expected result", "C": "Reviewing the incubator log each morning, which measures the medium temperature only", "D": "Comparing two of the laboratory's own slides, which measures only the banding quality"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Proficiency testing with external unknown cases, compared with the expected result): Proficiency testing with external unknown cases independently checks analysts' performance. Incubator logs and slide counts monitor equipment and productivity, and comparing internal slides does not assess interpretation."}, {"id": 1005, "domain": "hematology", "question": "Direct oral anticoagulants are used widely and generally do not require routine monitoring. One of them is a direct thrombin inhibitor, while the others target factor Xa. Which drug is the direct thrombin inhibitor among the DOACs? No routine monitoring is required for these drugs in most patients.", "options": {"A": "Edoxaban, which can be measured by a dilute thrombin time", "B": "Apixaban, which can be measured by a dilute thrombin time", "C": "Dabigatran, which can be measured by a dilute thrombin time", "D": "Rivaroxaban, which can be measured by a dilute thrombin time"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dabigatran, which can be measured by a dilute thrombin time): Dabigatran is the direct thrombin inhibitor and can be measured with a dilute thrombin time. Apixaban, edoxaban and rivaroxaban are anti-Xa drugs and are measured with an anti-Xa test."}, {"id": 1006, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist performs a quadrant streak on a blood agar plate to obtain isolated colonies from a mixed wound specimen. After incubation, the technologist wants single colonies to pick. In which area of the plate will these usually be found?", "options": {"A": "The centre of the plate, where the loop was first placed", "B": "The last quadrant, which carries the most dilute inoculum", "C": "The first quadrant, where the specimen was first rubbed on", "D": "The edge of the lid, where condensation collects"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The last quadrant, which carries the most dilute inoculum): Each quadrant is streaked from the previous one, so the inoculum thins out and the last quadrant yields single colonies. The first quadrant is heavily inoculated with confluent growth, and the lid is not an inoculated surface."}, {"id": 1007, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A report classifies a variant located at the boundary between an exon and an intron and predicts that it will disrupt how the RNA transcript is cut and joined, possibly skipping an exon. How is this variant type named? The classification will be written into the report for the ordering physician.", "options": {"A": "Missense variant, which replaces one amino acid with another", "B": "Fusion, which joins two separate genes into a single product", "C": "Splice-site variant, which alters how the RNA is processed", "D": "Copy number variant, which changes how many exons are present"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Splice-site variant, which alters how the RNA is processed): A change at an exon-intron boundary that alters RNA processing is a splice-site variant. Missense variants swap amino acids, CNVs change dosage, and fusions involve two genes."}, {"id": 1008, "domain": "cytology", "question": "With time for only one set of smears before the radiologist withdraws the needle from a deep thigh mass, a technologist must choose the preparation. The pathologist wants to judge a suspected mucoid background and stroma. Which preparation shows that background best, and which other set is added later?", "options": {"A": "Air-dried Romanowsky smears, with alcohol-fixed Papanicolaou smears added for nuclear detail.", "B": "Air-dried smears stained with hematoxylin and eosin, with no second set needed.", "C": "Alcohol-fixed Papanicolaou smears, with air-dried smears added later to show stroma.", "D": "Cell block sections only, with smears added later for nuclear detail."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Air-dried Romanowsky smears, with alcohol-fixed Papanicolaou smears added for nuclear detail.): Myxoid and mucoid material shows best on air-dried Romanowsky smears, so those are the priority when only one set can be made. Alcohol-fixed smears show fine nuclear detail and are added afterwards. Hematoxylin and eosin is not the stain for dried smears, and a cell block cannot be made before the material is rinsed and processed."}, {"id": 1009, "domain": "histology", "question": "A supervisor describes two designs of tissue processor: a carousel type in which the basket moves between open reagent stations, and an enclosed type that uses a single retort. She asks a trainee how an enclosed processor delivers each solution to the tissue?", "options": {"A": "Fluids stay in the chamber while the tissue is heated to move through each stage.", "B": "Fluids are pumped through one chamber, with vacuum and pressure assisting infiltration.", "C": "The tissue basket is lifted and lowered manually into separate containers by the operator.", "D": "The tissue basket is moved by a robotic arm from one open container to the next."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fluids are pumped through one chamber, with vacuum and pressure assisting infiltration.): An enclosed processor keeps the cassettes still and pumps each reagent and the wax through one retort, using vacuum and pressure to speed infiltration and limiting vapour release. The carousel design is the one that moves the basket between stations, and manual dipping describes hand processing. Reagents must be exchanged for dehydration to progress, so heating alone would not work."}, {"id": 1010, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A technologist prepares a Levey-Jennings chart for a new control lot. Each day's control value will be plotted against horizontal lines that help show how far a value lies from expected. Which set of lines correctly describes the standard layout of this chart?", "options": {"A": "A mean centre line with lines at 1, 2 and 3 SD on each side", "B": "A centre line at the target and lines at plus and minus 5 and 10 percent", "C": "Lines at the reference interval limits and at the critical value cutoffs", "D": "A centre line at zero and lines at each decile of the manufacturer's range"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A mean centre line with lines at 1, 2 and 3 SD on each side): The chart is built on the laboratory's own mean with lines at 1, 2 and 3 standard deviations on each side, which lets the Westgard rules be read at a glance. Deciles and fixed percentages are not standard. Reference intervals and critical values describe patients, not control performance."}, {"id": 1011, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A microarray report identifies a deletion of 300 kilobases in a fetus. The parents have not been tested, no dosage-sensitive genes are involved, and the region appears in databases of healthy individuals at a low frequency. The couple asks the counsellor whether the finding is harmful. What is the most reasoned answer?", "options": {"A": "Definitely pathogenic, because any deletion found in a fetus is harmful", "B": "Meaningless, because arrays cannot detect deletions as small as 300 kilobases", "C": "Likely benign or uncertain, but parental testing can refine the classification", "D": "Definitely benign, because databases contain healthy carriers of similar deletions"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Likely benign or uncertain, but parental testing can refine the classification): Absence of dosage-sensitive genes and presence in healthy individuals lean benign, but with no parental data the call stays uncertain, and reviewing more evidence can refine it. Not every deletion is harmful, database presence alone is not proof, and arrays detect changes much smaller than this."}, {"id": 1012, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "After a batch of arrays runs, a technologist reviews a run-level quality metric that reflects how noisy the probe data are across the genome, such as the derivative log ratio spread. One sample shows a very high value. What does this most likely mean for its interpretation?", "options": {"A": "The genome build is wrong, so all coordinates must be shifted by a fixed amount", "B": "The data are unusually clean, so small changes can be called with more confidence", "C": "The sample is confirmed male, so the sex chromosome check can be skipped", "D": "The data are noisy, so calls may be unreliable and the sample may need repeating"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The data are noisy, so calls may be unreliable and the sample may need repeating): A high noise metric means probe-to-probe variation is large, which can create false calls or hide real ones. It does not signal clean data, sex, or the genome build. The usual response is to review the DNA quality and repeat or re-extract."}, {"id": 1013, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A pathologist asks the technologist to prepare the differential portion of a marrow report. The report must reflect a standard number of nucleated cells so that percentages are reliable. How many nucleated cells are counted in the marrow differential according to the usual reporting standard?", "options": {"A": "At least 50 nucleated cells, since marrow cells are larger than blood cells", "B": "At least 1000 nucleated cells, because the marrow report requires that number for reliable percentages", "C": "At least 100 nucleated cells, the same number counted on blood smears", "D": "At least 500 nucleated cells, counted on well-made marrow smears"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (At least 500 nucleated cells, counted on well-made marrow smears): The marrow differential is counted on at least 500 nucleated cells, more than the 100 counted on blood. Fifty is too few for reliable percentages, and 1000 is not the usual standard."}, {"id": 1014, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Choosing an antiglobulin reagent for routine antibody screening, the supervisor explains that polyspecific reagent contains two components, while monospecific reagent contains one, and the current vial is labelled polyspecific. Which pair describes polyspecific AHG, and why is monospecific anti-IgG often preferred for screening?", "options": {"A": "Anti-C3b plus anti-C4, and anti-IgG alone detects more warm antibodies than complement reagents", "B": "Anti-IgG plus anti-C3d, and anti-IgG alone is cheaper because it requires no enhancement medium", "C": "Anti-IgM plus anti-IgA, and anti-IgG alone avoids reactions caused by cold agglutinins", "D": "Anti-IgG plus anti-C3d, and anti-IgG alone avoids reactions caused by complement only"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anti-IgG plus anti-C3d, and anti-IgG alone avoids reactions caused by complement only): Polyspecific reagent contains anti-IgG and anti-C3d, so it will also react with complement bound to cells. Monospecific anti-IgG avoids reactions caused by complement alone, which matter little for detecting clinically significant IgG antibodies. The price and enhancement arguments are unrelated to the reagent composition."}, {"id": 1015, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A trough level of a therapeutic drug is ordered for a hospitalized patient. The nurse drew the sample at 09:00, and the drug was given at 08:00, but the requisition carries no times. The laboratory receives the tube and is asked to interpret the concentration against the target range. What is the problem?", "options": {"A": "The tube type is wrong, since therapeutic drug levels are drawn into lavender top tubes", "B": "The sample can be corrected by subtracting one hour of estimated drug clearance", "C": "Without the time of the last dose and the sample, the level cannot be interpreted", "D": "The drug level is valid, since trough samples are compared with the same reference range"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Without the time of the last dose and the sample, the level cannot be interpreted): Drug concentrations change with time after a dose, so the exact time of the last dose and of the sample must be written on the requisition. Here a sample drawn an hour after dosing is not a trough, but the laboratory cannot tell without those times. Subtracting an estimate is not valid practice, and tube choice depends on the drug, not on a single colour."}, {"id": 1016, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A student asks which of the lipoproteins carries most of the plasma cholesterol and is most closely linked to atherosclerosis, and which carries triglyceride made by the liver. The instructor gives one answer covering both. What is the accurate pair?", "options": {"A": "HDL carries most cholesterol and drives atherosclerosis, while chylomicrons carry liver-made triglyceride", "B": "LDL carries most cholesterol and drives atherosclerosis, while VLDL carries liver-made triglyceride", "C": "Chylomicrons carry most cholesterol and drive atherosclerosis, while HDL carries liver-made triglyceride", "D": "VLDL carries most cholesterol and drives atherosclerosis, while LDL carries liver-made triglyceride"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (LDL carries most cholesterol and drives atherosclerosis, while VLDL carries liver-made triglyceride): LDL carries most of the cholesterol and drives atherosclerosis, and VLDL carries triglyceride made by the liver. HDL returns cholesterol to the liver. Chylomicrons carry dietary triglyceride, not liver-made. The other pairings swap the roles."}, {"id": 1017, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A woman previously treated with pelvic radiation has a urine specimen containing very large cells with bizarre nuclei and vacuolated cytoplasm. The chromatin is smudged rather than coarse. The screener is worried about malignancy, and the requisition mentions the treatment. How should these cells be handled?", "options": {"A": "Report herpes infection, since bizarre nuclei with nuclear moulding indicate viral cytopathic change", "B": "Report decoy cells, since vacuolated cytoplasm marks polyomavirus reactivation", "C": "Recognize a radiation effect, and review the clinical history before calling malignancy", "D": "Report high-grade carcinoma, since bizarre nuclei with vacuolated cytoplasm are diagnostic of it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recognize a radiation effect, and review the clinical history before calling malignancy): Radiation can cause large cells with bizarre nuclei and vacuolated cytoplasm, so the history should be reviewed before calling malignancy. The smudged chromatin also argues against high-grade carcinoma. Decoy cells have a glassy homogeneous inclusion. Herpes needs multinucleated cells with moulded nuclei and ground-glass chromatin, which are not described."}, {"id": 1018, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A gardener develops a chain of red nodules climbing up the forearm along lymphatic channels a few weeks after a rose thorn puncture. A biopsy of one nodule is cultured and stained with silver. Which description fits the organism most likely responsible for this lymphocutaneous infection?", "options": {"A": "Histoplasma capsulatum, seen as tiny oval yeasts inside macrophages in tissue", "B": "Coccidioides immitis, seen as large spherules filled with endospores in tissue", "C": "Sporothrix schenckii, seen as cigar-shaped yeasts in tissue", "D": "Rhizopus species, seen as broad ribbon-like hyphae with few septa in tissue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sporothrix schenckii, seen as cigar-shaped yeasts in tissue): Sporothrix follows plant or thorn injury and appears as cigar-shaped yeasts. Histoplasma is small and intracellular, Coccidioides produces spherules, and Rhizopus is a mold that infects sinuses and lungs in compromised patients."}, {"id": 1019, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A group A patient's cells give only a weak, mixed-field reaction with anti-A even after extended incubation. The reverse group is consistent with group A. The technologist suspects a weak subgroup such as A3 or Ax. Which further testing can demonstrate that the A antigen is really present on these cells?", "options": {"A": "Acidification of anti-A reagent to prove that the antigen is acquired", "B": "Saline replacement of the plasma followed by repeat forward grouping", "C": "Prewarming the reagent and cells to 37 degrees before repeating the test", "D": "Adsorption of anti-A onto the cells followed by elution of the antibody"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Adsorption of anti-A onto the cells followed by elution of the antibody): Weak subgroups can hold too little antigen to agglutinate directly, but they still adsorb anti-A, and the antibody released by elution proves the antigen was there. Saline replacement and prewarming solve rouleaux and cold autoantibody problems. Acidified reagent is used for acquired B, not for subgroups of A."}, {"id": 1020, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "An oncologist wants a way to follow tumour variants in a lung cancer patient without repeating an invasive procedure, and the laboratory proposes analysing a single tube of blood. Which analyte in plasma allows this noninvasive assessment of tumour variants for treatment selection and monitoring?", "options": {"A": "Circulating tumour DNA released by dying tumour cells into the blood", "B": "Messenger RNA from red blood cells, which reflects tumour gene expression", "C": "Mitochondrial DNA from platelets, which reflects the tumour variant load", "D": "Genomic DNA from the buffy coat, which carries the tumour variants"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Circulating tumour DNA released by dying tumour cells into the blood): Circulating tumour DNA in plasma carries fragments from tumour cells, so it allows non invasive assessment and monitoring. Buffy coat DNA is mostly germline DNA from white cells. Mature red blood cells lack nuclei and do not carry tumour messenger RNA. Platelet mitochondrial DNA does not reflect tumour variant load."}, {"id": 1021, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory is moving from conventional PCR with agarose gel analysis to real-time PCR on a closed platform, and the quality manager expects fewer false positives from contamination over the coming year. Which feature of real-time PCR mainly explains the lower risk?", "options": {"A": "Real-time reactions use fewer cycles, so less amplicon is generated overall.", "B": "Fluorescent probes destroy any amplicon present before the specimen is added.", "C": "Tubes stay sealed after amplification because detection happens during the run.", "D": "Real-time machines filter the air, which removes aerosolized product from the room."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tubes stay sealed after amplification because detection happens during the run.): In real-time PCR fluorescence is read each cycle, so the tube is never opened afterward and amplicon is not released into the laboratory. Cycle number and amplicon amounts are similar, probes do not destroy earlier product, and the instrument does not clean room air."}, {"id": 1022, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Stone disease in one patient has produced a urine specimen containing urothelial cells with enlarged nuclei and visible nucleoli. The chromatin is fine, the nuclear membranes are smooth, and the nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio is only mildly raised. Which interpretation is most appropriate for these cells?", "options": {"A": "Reactive urothelial change, because the chromatin stays fine and the membranes remain smooth", "B": "BCG effect, because enlarged nuclei with nucleoli occur only after intravesical therapy", "C": "High-grade carcinoma, because visible nucleoli are the defining feature of malignant cells", "D": "Polyomavirus infection, because enlarged nuclei with nucleoli are diagnostic of viral effect"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reactive urothelial change, because the chromatin stays fine and the membranes remain smooth): Stones, infection and instrumentation cause reactive cells with enlarged nuclei and nucleoli, but fine chromatin and smooth membranes keep them benign. Nucleoli do not define malignancy. Polyomavirus produces a homogeneous ground-glass inclusion instead. BCG changes need the treatment history, and nucleoli also occur in many other reactive settings."}, {"id": 1023, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient with advanced chronic kidney disease has a normocytic anemia and a reticulocyte count that is inappropriately low for the degree of anemia. The hematologist explains that the damaged kidneys are failing to sense low tissue oxygen and increase output of a specific hormone. Which hormone is deficient and where is it normally made?", "options": {"A": "Thrombopoietin, made mostly by the liver to stimulate marrow megakaryocytes", "B": "Transferrin, made mostly by the liver to carry iron through the plasma", "C": "Erythropoietin, made mostly by kidney cells that sense low tissue oxygen", "D": "Hepcidin, made mostly by the liver in response to inflammation and iron stores"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Erythropoietin, made mostly by kidney cells that sense low tissue oxygen): Erythropoietin is produced mainly by the kidney and drives erythropoiesis, so kidney failure leaves the marrow understimulated. Hepcidin regulates iron availability rather than red cell production. Thrombopoietin acts on platelets, and transferrin is an iron carrier, not a stimulating hormone."}, {"id": 1024, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist opens the incubator and notices the medium in an amniotic fluid flask has turned a deep purple compared with the orange-red seen the previous day. The medium contains phenol red as a pH indicator, and the neighbouring flasks look normal. What does this change most likely suggest, and what should be checked?", "options": {"A": "The medium has become too basic, so the incubator carbon dioxide level should be checked", "B": "The medium has become sterile, so the culture can be left for another week", "C": "The medium has become contaminated with yeast, so the harvest should be doubled", "D": "The medium has become too acidic, so the flask should be harvested immediately"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The medium has become too basic, so the incubator carbon dioxide level should be checked): Purple phenol red indicates a basic medium, which can follow a gas or buffering fault, so the carbon dioxide level and medium lot are checked. Yellow, not purple, indicates acidity or contamination. Colour change does not mean sterility."}, {"id": 1025, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A supervisor explains that the laboratory uses two stains on effusion slides. One is alcohol-fixed and shows nuclear detail, while the other is air-dried and shows cytoplasmic detail and background material well. A trainee is asked to name the air-dried stain commonly used for this purpose. Which is it?", "options": {"A": "A Romanowsky stain such as Diff-Quik, applied to air-dried slides", "B": "A Grocott methenamine silver stain, applied to fungal cysts in fixed sections", "C": "An acid-fast stain, applied to mycobacteria in smears of necrotic granulomas", "D": "A Papanicolaou stain, applied to alcohol-fixed slides for polychromatic cytoplasm"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A Romanowsky stain such as Diff-Quik, applied to air-dried slides): Romanowsky stains such as Diff-Quik use air-dried slides and show cytoplasm and background. The Papanicolaou stain uses alcohol-fixed slides. Grocott silver and acid-fast stains are special stains for organisms, not general stains of effusion cells."}, {"id": 1026, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist places antibiotic disks on a freshly swabbed Mueller-Hinton plate for a routine susceptibility test. The plate has been inoculated and allowed to dry briefly. Within what time frame should the disks be applied after inoculation, so that antibiotic diffusion and organism growth remain properly matched?", "options": {"A": "Within about 15 minutes of inoculating the plate", "B": "Within about 2 hours of inoculating the plate", "C": "Within about 30 minutes after the plate has finished incubating", "D": "Within about 24 hours of inoculating the plate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Within about 15 minutes of inoculating the plate): Disks are applied within about 15 minutes of inoculation so that drug diffusion and organism growth start together. Waiting hours lets the lawn begin growing before the drug diffuses, which shrinks zones. Disks cannot be added after incubation."}, {"id": 1027, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient has grossly lipemic plasma, and the laboratory suspects a falsely low sodium using the routine analyzer. Which method is preferred to obtain a reliable sodium, and why? The technologist must choose the method that gives a trustworthy value for this specimen.", "options": {"A": "Refractometry of the serum, because it measures sodium ions directly from the specimen", "B": "Flame photometry with a larger dilution, because it corrects for the reduced water fraction", "C": "Indirect ion-selective electrode, because dilution removes the lipid and improves the accuracy", "D": "Direct ion-selective electrode, since it measures undiluted plasma directly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Direct ion-selective electrode, since it measures undiluted plasma directly): In extreme lipid or protein elevation the plasma water fraction is reduced, and indirect ISE dilutes the sample and reads falsely low. Direct ISE avoids the volume displacement error. Additional dilution does not solve this problem, and refractometry does not measure sodium."}, {"id": 1028, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Urine arrives from a patient whose urine is diverted through a segment of bowel. The slides show many degenerated cells and a mucous background, and the technologist finds them hard to read. The supervisor asks which specimen type this is and what explains the appearance.", "options": {"A": "An ileal conduit specimen, which comes from a bowel segment and contains degenerated intestinal cells", "B": "A recently irradiated bladder, because radiation causes mucus and fragmented cells in urine", "C": "A BK infection specimen, which sheds smudged nuclei and mucus into every urine collected", "D": "A catheterized specimen from a healthy adult, which contains fresh transitional cells and a clear background"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An ileal conduit specimen, which comes from a bowel segment and contains degenerated intestinal cells): Urine from an ileal conduit passes through a bowel segment, so it contains degenerated intestinal cells and mucus and is harder to read. BK infection produces decoy cells, not mucus. A catheterized specimen from a healthy adult would show fresh urothelial cells on a clear background. Radiation causes large bizarre cells with vacuolated cytoplasm."}, {"id": 1029, "domain": "histology", "question": "A student technologist is learning the freezing steps in the cryostat room. During frozen section preparation, a technologist places trimmed tissue on a metal chuck with a water-soluble embedding compound before freezing. What is the role of freezing in making the tissue ready for cutting?", "options": {"A": "Freezing hardens the water in the tissue, making a solid block to cut", "B": "Freezing removes all water from the tissue so that it becomes a dry powder", "C": "Freezing fixes the proteins and lipids in the tissue, so no stain is needed", "D": "Freezing dissolves the embedding compound so that tissue can spread on the chuck"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Freezing hardens the water in the tissue, making a solid block to cut): Freezing hardens the water in the tissue and turns the soft specimen into a solid block that can be sectioned. It does not dissolve the compound or dry the tissue, and it does not permanently fix proteins in the way a fixative does."}, {"id": 1030, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A Salmonella isolate from a stool culture tests susceptible to a third-generation cephalosporin on the automated panel. The report is ready for release with the full drug list displayed. The technologist reviews the laboratory's reporting rules for this specimen type. What should be done with the cephalosporin result?", "options": {"A": "Convert it to intermediate, since all in vitro cephalosporin results for Salmonella are downgraded", "B": "Suppress the cephalosporin result for this isolate, since it is not reported for stool Salmonella", "C": "Report it only if the patient is over 65 years of age, and suppress it otherwise", "D": "Report it in bold type, because a susceptible result is most useful to physicians"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suppress the cephalosporin result for this isolate, since it is not reported for stool Salmonella): Cephalosporins must not be reported for Salmonella and Shigella from stool cultures because they are ineffective in vivo despite in vitro susceptibility. The rule is not age-based, and results are not simply downgraded."}, {"id": 1031, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A trainee describes non-keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma to a colleague. The colleague asks which set of features distinguishes it from the keratinizing form on a Papanicolaou-stained slide, where the keratinizing form shows orange cytoplasm and tadpole cells. The specimen came from a woman with abnormal bleeding.", "options": {"A": "Multinucleated cells with ground-glass nuclei, thick nuclear membranes and chromatin pushed to the edge", "B": "Feathered columnar groups with rosettes and frequent apoptotic bodies", "C": "Round to oval cells in sheets and syncytia with coarse chromatin and prominent nucleoli", "D": "Spindle cells with dense orange cytoplasm and pyknotic featureless nuclei"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Round to oval cells in sheets and syncytia with coarse chromatin and prominent nucleoli): Non-keratinizing carcinoma has round to oval cells in sheets and syncytia with less cytoplasm, coarse irregular chromatin and often prominent nucleoli. Feathered columnar groups suggest adenocarcinoma in situ. Ground-glass multinucleated cells are herpes, and orange spindle cells are keratinizing."}, {"id": 1032, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A physician asks about platelet counts and bleeding risk in a patient with thrombocytopenia. According to the chapter, bleeding risk generally increases below one count, and spontaneous bleeding becomes a concern below a lower count. What is the lower count threshold for spontaneous bleeding?", "options": {"A": "Spontaneous bleeding becomes a concern below 100 to 150 x10^9/L", "B": "Spontaneous bleeding becomes a concern below 10 to 20 x10^9/L", "C": "Spontaneous bleeding becomes a concern below 30 to 40 x10^9/L", "D": "Spontaneous bleeding becomes a concern below 60 to 80 x10^9/L"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Spontaneous bleeding becomes a concern below 10 to 20 x10^9/L): Bleeding risk generally rises below 50 x10^9/L, and spontaneous bleeding becomes a concern below 10 to 20 x10^9/L. The other ranges are too high to represent the spontaneous bleeding threshold."}, {"id": 1033, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A neonatal nurse asks the laboratory which form of hemolytic disease of the newborn is seen most often. She has heard that Rh disease is the main cause. The technologist explains the frequency and severity of the most common form. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Rh disease is the most common form and is usually mild.", "B": "Kell disease is the most common form and is usually severe.", "C": "ABO disease is the most common form, usually mild.", "D": "Duffy disease is the most common form and is usually fatal."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (ABO disease is the most common form, usually mild.): ABO HDFN is the most common form and is usually mild, occurring mainly in group A or B babies of group O mothers. Rh disease is classic and severe but less frequent since prophylaxis, and Kell and Duffy disease are rare."}, {"id": 1034, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "An overnight FISH hybridization is set up under a coverslip and placed in the oven. When the technologist returns, the edge of the coverslip has dried, and several nuclei near it show no signal and a bright smear. The procedure specifies incubation in a humidified chamber. What was the likely error?", "options": {"A": "The slide was left too long in the wash, which dried the coverslip edge", "B": "The chamber was not humidified, so the probe solution dried and hybridization failed near the edge", "C": "The DAPI counterstain diffused outward, which erased the signal near the edge", "D": "The oven was too humid, which makes the probe precipitate and dry near the coverslip"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The chamber was not humidified, so the probe solution dried and hybridization failed near the edge): Drying of the probe under the coverslip prevents proper hybridization and creates artifact at the edges, which is why a humidified chamber is used. Humidity does not dry a solution. Washes and counterstain come after hybridization and cannot cause a dried coverslip during the incubation."}, {"id": 1035, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A Diff-Quik slide of a thyroid aspirate shows abundant blue-purple colloid in the background, but the nuclear chromatin is less crisp than expected. The technologist wants a slide that better shows nuclear detail. The rapid stain gave a fast answer at the bedside, but the pathologist wants a second slide for the final report. Which additional preparation is most helpful?", "options": {"A": "A slide left unfixed and viewed without any stain", "B": "A wet-fixed slide stained with the Papanicolaou method", "C": "A slide fixed in formalin and stained with orange G only", "D": "A second air-dried slide stained again with Diff-Quik"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A wet-fixed slide stained with the Papanicolaou method): Diff-Quik highlights colloid and cytoplasm but nuclear detail is less crisp, so laboratories often add a wet-fixed Papanicolaou slide. Repeating the same stain adds nothing, and unstained or partial stains do not show chromatin."}, {"id": 1036, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "Each week a laboratory runs a control slide of known normal cells beside its patient FISH slides. On one run, the control shows three signals in most nuclei for a probe that normally gives two, although the patient slides look normal on first viewing. What should the technologist do?", "options": {"A": "Score the control again with a lower cutoff until it looks normal", "B": "Relabel the control as a positive control and continue with the run", "C": "Release the patient results, since the patient slides showed the expected signal counts", "D": "Investigate before releasing results, since the control failed and the run cannot be trusted"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Investigate before releasing results, since the control failed and the run cannot be trusted): A failed control means the run does not meet quality requirements, so results are held while the cause is investigated and corrective action is documented. Normal-looking patient slides do not offset a failed control, and relabelling or adjusting the cutoff to fit the control is not acceptable."}, {"id": 1037, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A patient with a painful, tender thyroid gland after a viral illness has an aspirate showing multinucleated giant cells, granulomas and an inflammatory background of mixed cells. The follicular cells are scant and unremarkable. The clinician asks for the most likely cytological diagnosis in this picture.", "options": {"A": "Anaplastic carcinoma, with bizarre giant cells and spindled tumour cells in necrosis", "B": "Papillary carcinoma, with giant cells, psammoma bodies and powdery nuclear chromatin", "C": "Subacute de Quervain thyroiditis, with granulomas and giant cells against an inflammatory background", "D": "Hashimoto thyroiditis, with small lymphocytes, plasma cells and Hurthle cells throughout the smear"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Subacute de Quervain thyroiditis, with granulomas and giant cells against an inflammatory background): Granulomas and multinucleated giant cells with inflammatory cells after a viral illness describe subacute de Quervain thyroiditis. Hashimoto thyroiditis shows many lymphocytes, plasma cells and Hurthle cells rather than granulomas. Papillary carcinoma requires nuclear features, and anaplastic carcinoma involves bizarre tumour cells with necrosis in a rapidly growing mass."}, {"id": 1038, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A clinic in a small centre uses a lower-dose Rh immune globulin product for RhD-negative women with certain early events. The nurse asks the laboratory what dose is used and in what setting. What dose and use does the laboratory give? The clinic is 300 km from the nearest hospital blood bank.", "options": {"A": "A 120 microgram dose, used in some places after every transfusion.", "B": "A 120 microgram dose, used in some places for early first trimester events.", "C": "A 50 microgram dose, used in some places for delivery at term.", "D": "A 600 microgram dose, used in some places for early miscarriage."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A 120 microgram dose, used in some places for early first trimester events.): A smaller 120 microgram dose is used in some places for early first trimester events, since the fetal red cell volume is very small at that stage. Delivery at term needs the 300 microgram dose or more."}, {"id": 1039, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Cerebrospinal fluid from a patient with headache shows mostly lymphocytes with no atypical cells. The physician asks whether this alone identifies the cause. Cytology comments should point to the right combined workup. Which statement best describes the role of cytology when infection is suspected in cerebrospinal fluid?", "options": {"A": "Lymphocytes prove a normal specimen, and the sample is discarded after cytology review", "B": "Lymphocytes suggest viral, tuberculous or fungal disease, and microbiology completes the workup", "C": "Lymphocytes suggest carcinomatous meningitis, and repeat sampling is unnecessary afterwards", "D": "Lymphocytes confirm bacterial infection, and no further testing is needed after cytology review"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lymphocytes suggest viral, tuberculous or fungal disease, and microbiology completes the workup): Neutrophils suggest bacterial infection, whereas lymphocytes suggest viral, tuberculous or fungal causes, and microbiology and chemistry finish the evaluation. Lymphocytes do not confirm bacterial infection, and they do not by themselves indicate carcinoma or a normal specimen."}, {"id": 1040, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory compares droplet digital PCR with real-time PCR for specimens that carry residual inhibitors after extraction. Real-time results show delayed Ct values, while the digital method still reports usable counts. Why does the digital format cope better with mild inhibition?", "options": {"A": "It uses a polymerase that resists heparin and heme at any concentration tested in practice.", "B": "It measures fluorescence at every cycle, so inhibition is corrected mathematically.", "C": "Partitioning and end-point counting lessen the impact of slowed reactions on results.", "D": "It removes inhibitors through the magnetic beads that are suspended inside each droplet."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Partitioning and end-point counting lessen the impact of slowed reactions on results.): Each partition is read at end point as positive or negative, so a somewhat slower reaction still ends up positive and is counted, and inhibitor is diluted across partitions. Real-time methods depend on the timing of Ct, which inhibition shifts. Digital PCR does not use bead cleanup or cycle-by-cycle correction, and no polymerase tolerates unlimited inhibitor."}, {"id": 1041, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A 19-year-old university student with sore throat, fatigue and cervical lymphadenopathy for ten days has a blood film showing the large cells in the image. The analyzer reports a mildly raised white count. What is the most appropriate next laboratory step?", "options": {"A": "Request a bone marrow biopsy to exclude acute lymphoblastic leukemia in the patient", "B": "Order flow cytometry to confirm a clonal B-cell lymphoproliferative disorder first", "C": "Request an ESR and CRP to exclude a bacterial infection as the cause", "D": "Perform a heterophile antibody test and add EBV-specific serology if it is negative"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Perform a heterophile antibody test and add EBV-specific serology if it is negative): Large reactive lymphocytes in a young patient with pharyngitis and lymphadenopathy suggest infectious mononucleosis, so a heterophile test is done first, with EBV serology if it is negative. Marrow biopsy, flow cytometry for clonality, and inflammatory markers do not address a reactive lymphocytosis.", "image_type": "micrograph", "image_params": {"prompt": "Realistic Wright-Giemsa stained peripheral blood smear photomicrograph, 1000x oil immersion, large reactive lymphocytes (atypical lymphocytes) with abundant pale blue basophilic cytoplasm that flows around and indents neighbouring red cells, a large oval nucleus with moderately open chromatin, among normal red cells.", "caption": "Peripheral blood film", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1041.png"}, {"id": 1042, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A liquid-based slide from a 35-year-old woman shows the four squamous cells in the image at equal magnification. One cell has an enlarged nucleus, but its chromatin and outline fall short of the criteria for a squamous intraepithelial lesion. Which cell best illustrates this borderline pattern suited to an ASC-US interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Cell 1, because its nucleus is enlarged yet the chromatin and outline remain bland and smooth", "B": "Cell 2, because its nucleus is enlarged yet the chromatin and outline remain bland and smooth", "C": "Cell 3, because its nucleus is enlarged yet the chromatin and outline remain bland and smooth", "D": "Cell 4, because its nucleus is enlarged yet the chromatin and outline remain bland and smooth"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cell 3, because its nucleus is enlarged yet the chromatin and outline remain bland and smooth): Cell 3 has a nucleus larger than normal but fine chromatin and a smooth outline, so it is abnormal enough to be questioned yet not enough for LSIL, which fits ASC-US. Cell 2 has dark chromatin and a very high N:C ratio with an irregular outline, so it goes beyond borderline. Cells 1 and 4 have small, bland nuclei of normal size.", "image_type": "cell_micrograph", "image_params": {"cells": [{"label": "1", "nc_ratio": 0.05, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "squamous"}, {"label": "2", "nc_ratio": 0.6, "chromatin": "hyperchromatic", "outline": "irregular", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "squamous"}, {"label": "3", "nc_ratio": 0.22, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "squamous"}, {"label": "4", "nc_ratio": 0.08, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "squamous"}], "caption": "Cells at equal magnification", "context": "cervical", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1042.png"}, {"id": 1043, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A trainee reviewing a sagittal diagram of the female reproductive tract asks what lines the canal running from the external os toward the uterine cavity. The supervisor says this lining secretes mucus and appears on slides as tall narrow cells with basal nuclei. What is this lining?", "options": {"A": "Several layers of flat keratinizing squamous cells that shed as anucleate scales", "B": "A single layer of tall, mucus-secreting columnar glandular cells", "C": "Stratified squamous epithelium with a basal layer and a glycogen-rich surface layer", "D": "A thin layer of cuboidal ciliated cells that sweep debris toward the vagina"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A single layer of tall, mucus-secreting columnar glandular cells): The endocervical canal is lined by a single layer of tall columnar glandular cells that secrete mucus. Squamous epithelium, keratinized or not, covers the ectocervix instead. Ciliated cells are found only in tubal metaplasia, not as the normal lining."}, {"id": 1044, "domain": "histology", "question": "Brown granular deposits appear on a slide in areas with no tissue, and they look like positive staining. The DAB solution was prepared earlier in the day and looks cloudy, and the chromogen step ran for ten minutes. What is the best corrective action?", "options": {"A": "Add more DAB to strengthen the colour on real positive cells", "B": "Prepare fresh DAB and filter it before use, then repeat the run", "C": "Use the same cloudy DAB but shorten the chromogen step time", "D": "Reduce the counterstain time so brown deposits become less visible"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Prepare fresh DAB and filter it before use, then repeat the run): Precipitated DAB leaves brown deposits that mimic positive staining, so it is freshly prepared and filtered. Adding more DAB or shortening time does not remove the precipitate."}, {"id": 1045, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A blood specimen for chromosome analysis arrives in a light-blue-top tube containing sodium citrate, drawn at an outreach clinic. The laboratory has no heparin tube from the same patient on file, and the clinic phones to ask whether the sample can still be used for culture. How should the laboratory answer the clinic?", "options": {"A": "Citrate is unsuitable for cytogenetic culture, so the specimen may need to be rejected", "B": "Citrate is preferred to heparin, so the laboratory can proceed and skip the mitogen", "C": "Citrate is acceptable for a prenatal sample, so it can go directly to the harvest step", "D": "Citrate is acceptable once frozen, because freezing reverses its effect on cell growth"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Citrate is unsuitable for cytogenetic culture, so the specimen may need to be rejected): Citrate, like EDTA, is not suitable for chromosome culture, so recollection in sodium heparin is generally needed. It is not preferred, freezing does not reverse anything, and it has no special role in prenatal samples."}, {"id": 1046, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A laboratory runs hemoglobin electrophoresis on cellulose acetate at alkaline pH every day. A supervisor reviews the quality plan and wants to confirm that every gel contains a material that lets the technologist verify band positions for the most common hemoglobin variants. Which control is included on each run?", "options": {"A": "A control containing hemoglobins A2, D, G and O", "B": "A control containing hemoglobins A, H, Bart's and E", "C": "A control containing only hemoglobin A from a normal donor", "D": "A control containing hemoglobins A, F, S and C"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A control containing hemoglobins A, F, S and C): Each gel includes a control with A, F, S and C so that band positions can be verified for the common variants. A normal control alone cannot confirm the positions of variants. Controls made of H, Bart's, E, D, G or O would not cover the commonly encountered variants."}, {"id": 1047, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist who was trained on conventional smears begins screening liquid-based slides and notes that cells look smaller and the background appears cleaner. A colleague wonders whether the slides were made incorrectly. The colleague has only seen conventional smears until now and is concerned about a possible processing fault. What is the most accurate explanation?", "options": {"A": "These are expected differences of liquid-based slides, and screeners must learn to recognize them", "B": "The slides are faulty, because a clean background means the cells were washed off", "C": "The slides are air dried, because smaller cells prove drying occurred before alcohol fixation", "D": "The slides are contaminated, because a thin layer shows that stain has carried over between racks"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (These are expected differences of liquid-based slides, and screeners must learn to recognize them): Liquid-based slides show a thin layer, smaller cells and a cleaner background, and experienced screeners must adapt to these differences. Cleanliness does not indicate washing away of cells, drying or contamination."}, {"id": 1048, "domain": "histology", "question": "Two slides from different patients are stained in the same run, with handwritten labels in the same rack, and one section is later found to be labelled with the wrong block number. Which quality step most directly prevents this type of error?", "options": {"A": "Tracking the pH of working buffers and reagents at the start and end of each shift", "B": "Recording the temperature of heating steps in the log", "C": "Cleaning the automated stainer on its regular schedule", "D": "Checking patient and block identifiers against the requisition at every handoff"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Checking patient and block identifiers against the requisition at every handoff): Identifier checks at every handoff make sure the stain is applied to the correct specimen. Buffer pH, temperature and stainer cleaning support stain quality but do not prevent mislabelling."}, {"id": 1049, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A patient undergoing therapeutic plasma exchange with citrate anticoagulant reports tingling around the mouth and muscle cramps midway through the procedure. The technologist is asked to link the symptoms to a laboratory value. What should be checked and corrected? The exchange is replacing one plasma volume with albumin.", "options": {"A": "Ionized calcium, since citrate binds calcium and causes hypocalcemia.", "B": "Platelet count, since citrate destroys platelets during the exchange.", "C": "Serum potassium, since citrate releases potassium from the red cells.", "D": "Serum sodium, since citrate solution contains a very high sodium load."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ionized calcium, since citrate binds calcium and causes hypocalcemia.): Citrate anticoagulant binds calcium, and low ionized calcium causes perioral tingling and cramps, so calcium is monitored and replaced. Citrate does not release potassium and does not destroy platelets."}, {"id": 1050, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "The electrolyte module on a large analyzer reports sodium, potassium and chloride. A trainee asks which electrode responds to sodium and what its sensing membrane is made from, since each ion needs a different membrane. Which membrane type does the sodium electrode use?", "options": {"A": "A valinomycin membrane that traps sodium ions in a lipid layer", "B": "A Clark cell with a platinum cathode and a silver anode", "C": "A solid silver chloride crystal that responds to sodium activity", "D": "A specialized glass membrane selective for sodium ions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A specialized glass membrane selective for sodium ions): Sodium is measured with a glass membrane that responds selectively to sodium ions. Valinomycin is the carrier used for potassium. Solid or polymer membranes are used for chloride, and the platinum and silver arrangement is the oxygen sensor on a blood gas analyzer."}, {"id": 1051, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A new reporter is drafting the header for a cervical cytology result. She must choose among the general interpretation categories in the Bethesda System, one of which covers endometrial cells in a woman aged 45 years or older. What are the three general categories?", "options": {"A": "Benign, low-grade, and high-grade, with organisms listed under a separate heading", "B": "Negative for intraepithelial lesion or malignancy, epithelial cell abnormality, and other", "C": "Satisfactory, unsatisfactory, and rejected, based on the number of squamous cells present", "D": "Normal, borderline, and malignant, with an added line for adequacy of the sample"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Negative for intraepithelial lesion or malignancy, epithelial cell abnormality, and other): The Bethesda System groups results as NILM, epithelial cell abnormality, and Other. Satisfactory and unsatisfactory describe adequacy, not interpretation. Normal, borderline, and malignant is not the Bethesda wording."}, {"id": 1052, "domain": "histology", "question": "A skin biopsy from a patient with suspected leprosy is submitted for a stain that preserves a fragile lipid-rich wall. The routine acid-fast method is too harsh for this organism. Which method is designed for weakly acid-fast bacteria such as Mycobacterium leprae?", "options": {"A": "The Congo red method, which uses polarized light after thicker sectioning of the tissue", "B": "The Fite method, which uses a lighter decolourizer and oil during deparaffinization", "C": "The Von Kossa method, which uses silver nitrate to detect calcium", "D": "The Verhoeff method, which uses iron hematoxylin to detect elastic tissue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The Fite method, which uses a lighter decolourizer and oil during deparaffinization): Fite is a modified acid-fast stain with a milder decolourizer and oil deparaffinization to protect the fragile wall. The other methods detect amyloid, calcium and elastic fibres."}, {"id": 1053, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "At the issue bench, a technologist notices a small leak at the port of a red cell unit and pink discolouration in the supernatant. The unit number, blood group and expiry are correct. The patient is waiting. What should the technologist do?", "options": {"A": "Issue the unit only to an anemic patient who needs it urgently", "B": "Do not issue the unit, and report the defect to the supplier", "C": "Mix the unit and issue it because pink supernatant is a normal finding", "D": "Issue the unit after taping the port, since the labels are all correct"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Do not issue the unit, and report the defect to the supplier): A leak and hemolysis in the supernatant fail the visual inspection, so the unit is not issued and the problem is reported. Taping cannot restore sterility. Urgency does not override a failed inspection, and pink supernatant signals hemolysis that is not normal."}, {"id": 1054, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A patient with sepsis, fever and an enlarged spleen has a poor one-hour platelet increment after a single transfusion. The team considers HLA-matched platelets at once. The laboratory advises on the best approach before ordering costly matched products. What should the laboratory recommend first?", "options": {"A": "Switch to group O platelets, since ABO mismatch causes every poor response.", "B": "Order HLA-matched platelets immediately, since one poor increment proves an immune cause.", "C": "Stop platelet transfusions until the fever resolves, then repeat the increment count next week", "D": "Treat the likely non-immune causes and reassess before pursuing HLA testing."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat the likely non-immune causes and reassess before pursuing HLA testing.): Fever, infection and splenomegaly are non-immune causes, and an immune cause is suspected after poor increments on two separate occasions. Treating the cause and repeating the CCI avoids unnecessary matched products."}, {"id": 1055, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A laboratory is writing a critical value policy for glucose in patients treated with glucose-lowering drugs. The quality officer needs the plasma glucose level below which hypoglycemia is usually defined in such patients, and the level below which concern rises sharply. Which pair of values belongs in the policy?", "options": {"A": "Below 5.6 mmol/L defines hypoglycemia, and concern rises sharply below 4.5 mmol/L", "B": "Below 3.0 mmol/L defines hypoglycemia, and concern rises sharply below 1.0 mmol/L", "C": "Below 7.0 mmol/L defines hypoglycemia, and concern rises sharply below 6.1 mmol/L", "D": "Below 4.0 mmol/L defines hypoglycemia, and concern rises sharply below 3.0 mmol/L"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Below 4.0 mmol/L defines hypoglycemia, and concern rises sharply below 3.0 mmol/L): Diabetes Canada defines hypoglycemia in a treated person with diabetes as a plasma glucose below 4.0 mmol/L, with concern rising sharply below 3.0 mmol/L. The 5.6 and 7.0 mmol/L values sit in normal or fasting diagnostic ranges. Setting the definition at 3.0 would miss early hypoglycemia."}, {"id": 1056, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A trainee notes that a Sanger sequencing reaction resembles PCR in several ways, yet it produces a mixture of fragments of different lengths rather than a single product. Which statement best describes how the reaction is built? The trainee wants the answer tied to the reaction components.", "options": {"A": "It uses only dNTPs, and fragment sizes differ because of variable extension times", "B": "It uses probes that hybridize to the target and pull it out of solution", "C": "It uses polymerase and primer as in PCR but adds a small proportion of labelled ddNTPs", "D": "It uses restriction enzymes to cut fragments and label each cut end with dye"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It uses polymerase and primer as in PCR but adds a small proportion of labelled ddNTPs): The reaction resembles PCR but includes a small proportion of dye-labelled dideoxynucleotides that stop strands at different positions, giving fragments ending at every base. Probes belong to hybrid capture, and without ddNTPs the strands would not terminate."}, {"id": 1057, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "An isolate from a ventilated patient is a gram-negative rod that is oxidase negative or weakly positive and grows as lavender-tinged colonies. Susceptibility testing shows resistance to carbapenems. The physician expects a carbapenemase producer. Which organism explains the resistance without a carbapenemase?", "options": {"A": "Escherichia coli, which is intrinsically resistant to carbapenems and produces a green metallic sheen", "B": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which is intrinsically resistant to carbapenems and produces blue-green pyocyanin pigment", "C": "Acinetobacter baumannii, which is intrinsically resistant to carbapenems and is oxidase positive on testing", "D": "Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, which is intrinsically resistant to carbapenems and typically found in hospitalized patients"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, which is intrinsically resistant to carbapenems and typically found in hospitalized patients): Stenotrophomonas maltophilia is oxidase negative or weakly positive, lavender-tinged, and intrinsically resistant to carbapenems, so the resistance need not signal a carbapenemase producer. Pseudomonas produces pyocyanin, Escherichia coli is not intrinsically resistant, and Acinetobacter is oxidase negative."}, {"id": 1058, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A 78-year-old patient with chronic lymphocytic leukemia has forward and reverse typing in gel as shown in the image. The sample is properly identified and the reagents performed correctly with their controls. What is the best next step in resolving the result?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the reverse typing by tube using more serum and longer room temperature incubation", "B": "Report the group as O because no reverse reactions were seen with either reagent cell", "C": "Report the group as A and issue group A cells, since the forward reaction is strong", "D": "Test the patient red cells with anti-A1 lectin to identify an A subgroup before reporting"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeat the reverse typing by tube using more serum and longer room temperature incubation): The forward typing shows group A, but the missing anti-B in the reverse typing is a discrepancy often caused by weak antibody production in older patients with hypogammaglobulinemia. More serum and longer incubation can bring out the weak antibody. Group O does not fit the forward typing, reporting without resolving the discrepancy is unsafe, and subgrouping addresses the forward typing, which is strong.", "image_type": "gel_agglutination", "image_params": {"wells": [{"label": "Anti-A", "reaction": "4+"}, {"label": "Anti-B", "reaction": "negative"}, {"label": "A1 cells", "reaction": "negative"}, {"label": "B cells", "reaction": "negative"}], "caption": "Gel forward and reverse typing"}, "image": "question_images/q_1058.png"}, {"id": 1059, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A trainee reviewing quality indicators is asked what is expected when a sample has enough well-preserved squamous cells but contains no endocervical or transformation zone cells. She must decide how the specimen is classified and whether the report needs a comment.", "options": {"A": "It becomes abnormal and is reported as atypical glandular cells", "B": "It becomes unsatisfactory and must be recollected within seven days of the original visit", "C": "It is rejected at accessioning and no report is issued", "D": "It stays satisfactory, and the report notes the absence as a quality indicator"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It stays satisfactory, and the report notes the absence as a quality indicator): Absence of transformation zone components does not make a sample unsatisfactory, but the report notes it so the clinician can consider repeat sampling for certain patients. Recollection and glandular atypia labels do not apply. The sample is reported normally."}, {"id": 1060, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A newly diagnosed patient with chronic myeloid leukemia asks the laboratory what the Philadelphia chromosome does inside the leukemic cells and why the finding matters for treatment decisions. The technologist explains the molecular consequence of t(9;22). What does t(9;22) do inside the leukemic cells?", "options": {"A": "It creates a duplicated chromosome 22, so treatment focuses on removing the extra copy.", "B": "It creates a loss of the ABL1 gene, so treatment focuses on replacing the missing kinase.", "C": "It creates a BCR::ABL1 fusion producing an active tyrosine kinase that targeted drugs block.", "D": "It creates a PML::RARA fusion producing a retinoic acid receptor that targeted drugs block."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It creates a BCR::ABL1 fusion producing an active tyrosine kinase that targeted drugs block.): The t(9;22) joins ABL1 to BCR, producing a constitutively active tyrosine kinase that tyrosine kinase inhibitors target. PML and RARA belong to the t(15;17) of acute promyelocytic leukemia. The gene is fused, not lost, and no chromosome 22 is duplicated."}, {"id": 1061, "domain": "histology", "question": "The laboratory director asks whether estrogen receptor results from this staining run can go to the pathologist for eight breast core biopsies. Considering the fixation records, the reagent lots, the retrieval buffer and the negative control, what do the control results tell the technologist about the validity of this staining run?", "options": {"A": "Only patient tissue is under-fixed, so the controls stay valid and the run can be reported", "B": "The clean negative control proves the detection kit worked, so patient results are valid", "C": "A step affecting every slide failed, so no patient result from this run is valid", "D": "The weak staining reflects true low receptor expression, so patient results can be reported"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A step affecting every slide failed, so no patient result from this run is valid): A positive control that fails along with every patient slide shows the problem lies in the run itself, not in the individual tissues. Fixation was within range, so under-fixation does not explain the controls, and a clean negative control cannot rescue a failed positive control. Weak staining on a known positive control is not biology.", "cluster_id": "1", "cluster_text": "A laboratory ran an estrogen receptor immunostain on eight breast core biopsies. Nuclear staining is weak or absent on every patient slide and on the external breast positive control. The validated antibody lot, detection kit and DAB were unchanged, and the reagent-omitted negative control is clean. Heat-induced retrieval ran at the usual temperature and time, but the technologist used a newly prepared bottle of retrieval buffer that carries no pH record. Fixation times on the blocks were documented and fall within the validated range.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1062, "domain": "histology", "question": "The laboratory supervisor reviews the failed estrogen receptor run described in the case and wants to find the cause efficiently before any slides are repeated or reagents are discarded. Which single check would most directly identify the cause of the staining failure in this run, given the one reagent change that is described in the case?", "options": {"A": "Repeating the stain on the same slides using a higher concentration of the primary antibody", "B": "Recounting the fixation hours for each block against the cold ischemia log kept in the laboratory", "C": "Replacing the DAB chromogen with a fresh vial and restaining one patient slide for comparison", "D": "Measuring the pH of the new retrieval buffer and comparing it with the validated working value"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Measuring the pH of the new retrieval buffer and comparing it with the validated working value): The only changed element in the case is a retrieval buffer with no pH record, and retrieval depends on the buffer pH, so measuring it tests the suspected cause directly. Raising antibody concentration would alter a validated method, fixation was already documented as acceptable, and DAB was unchanged and is not suspected.", "cluster_id": "1", "cluster_text": "A laboratory ran an estrogen receptor immunostain on eight breast core biopsies. Nuclear staining is weak or absent on every patient slide and on the external breast positive control. The validated antibody lot, detection kit and DAB were unchanged, and the reagent-omitted negative control is clean. Heat-induced retrieval ran at the usual temperature and time, but the technologist used a newly prepared bottle of retrieval buffer that carries no pH record. Fixation times on the blocks were documented and fall within the validated range.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1063, "domain": "histology", "question": "What is the most appropriate way to manage this run once the technologist recognizes that the controls have failed and the cause has been found? Eight patient slides are stained, the physician is waiting for the estrogen receptor results, and the laboratory follows its quality management procedure.", "options": {"A": "Withhold results, correct the buffer, repeat the run with fresh controls, and record it", "B": "Report the slides with a comment about weak staining pending later pathologist review", "C": "Repeat only the positive control and release the patient results if that slide passes", "D": "Extend retrieval time on the existing slides and report them after a quick second reading"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Withhold results, correct the buffer, repeat the run with fresh controls, and record it): Results from a failed run cannot be released, so the failure is documented, the buffer is corrected and all slides are restained with fresh controls. Reporting with a comment still releases unreliable results, rerunning only the control leaves patient slides untested, and extending retrieval on stained slides alters the validated method.", "cluster_id": "1", "cluster_text": "A laboratory ran an estrogen receptor immunostain on eight breast core biopsies. Nuclear staining is weak or absent on every patient slide and on the external breast positive control. The validated antibody lot, detection kit and DAB were unchanged, and the reagent-omitted negative control is clean. Heat-induced retrieval ran at the usual temperature and time, but the technologist used a newly prepared bottle of retrieval buffer that carries no pH record. Fixation times on the blocks were documented and fall within the validated range.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1064, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "The chart shown displays five centrifuged tubes. A patient's serum from the ward, after centrifugation, looks like the third tube from the left. Which specimen condition is present, and what is the best handling step before the results are reported?", "options": {"A": "Hemolyzed serum, so report the potassium with a comment and avoid any repeat collection", "B": "Icteric serum, so dilute the sample tenfold with saline and report the results as measured", "C": "Lipemic serum, so clear the lipid by ultracentrifugation and repeat the affected tests", "D": "Clotted blood, so recentrifuge the tube after warming it to separate the serum layer"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lipemic serum, so clear the lipid by ultracentrifugation and repeat the affected tests): The third tube is cloudy and milky-white, which is lipemia from excess lipid, and ultracentrifugation or a lipid clearing step removes the interference before repeating affected assays. Hemolysis is pink-red, icterus is deep amber, and a clotted tube has no serum layer that recentrifugation could create.", "image_type": "specimen_tube", "image_params": {}, "image": "question_images/specimen_appearance_chart.png"}, {"id": 1065, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A clinic sends nasopharyngeal swabs on calcium alginate tips with wooden shafts for a viral RT-PCR. Several specimens show delayed internal control Ct values compared with swabs from another clinic that uses synthetic tips. Which explanation best fits the pattern?", "options": {"A": "Wooden shafts absorb viral transport medium, which lowers the volume available for testing.", "B": "Calcium alginate tips and wooden shafts can inhibit amplification, so synthetic tips are preferred.", "C": "Calcium alginate binds RNA irreversibly, which removes the target but leaves the internal control.", "D": "Late control Ct values mean the specimens are contaminated with amplicon from the other clinic."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Calcium alginate tips and wooden shafts can inhibit amplification, so synthetic tips are preferred.): Calcium alginate and wood can release substances that inhibit some amplification reactions, which delays the internal control and can cause false negatives. Synthetic tips avoid this. Absorption does not explain a late control, and a target-selective binding effect would not spare the control."}, {"id": 1066, "domain": "histology", "question": "Eosin binds to positively charged proteins in cytoplasm and collagen, and its staining is affected by the acidity of the solution. Which pH condition allows eosin to work best in routine staining? The technologist is preparing a fresh batch of the counterstain solution for use later today on the staining line, and wants it to work well.", "options": {"A": "A strongly alkaline pH above ten for the eosin solution", "B": "A pH identical to that of bluing reagent", "C": "A slightly acidic pH for the eosin solution", "D": "A strongly alkaline pH for the eosin solution"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A slightly acidic pH for the eosin solution): Eosin works best at a slightly acidic pH, where its negative charge can bind positively charged proteins. Strongly alkaline conditions weaken binding, which is why alkaline bluing reagent must be rinsed away before eosin is applied."}, {"id": 1067, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient has total protein of 92 g/L and albumin of 38 g/L. The technologist calculates the globulin gap as a screening tool and considers what the result may suggest to the physician before electrophoresis is requested. What is the calculation and interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Gap of 54 g/L, which is small and rules out a paraprotein without electrophoresis", "B": "Gap of 54 g/L, which is large and raises the possibility of a paraprotein", "C": "Gap of 130 g/L, which is small and rules out any paraprotein", "D": "Gap of 38 g/L, which is large and confirms nephrotic syndrome"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gap of 54 g/L, which is large and raises the possibility of a paraprotein): Total protein minus albumin gives 92 minus 38, which equals 54 g/L, a large gap suggesting excess immunoglobulin and possible paraprotein. Adding rather than subtracting gives 130. A large gap does not rule out a paraprotein, and nephrotic syndrome would lower albumin, not raise the gap."}, {"id": 1068, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "Two patient samples show the same retention time for a catecholamine peak on an HPLC run, but the second sample's peak has double the area of the first. The detector settings and injection volumes were identical for both samples. What does this comparison show?", "options": {"A": "Both contain the same compound, and the second contains about twice the amount", "B": "The second sample stayed twice as long on the column, so it carries twice the charge", "C": "The compounds differ, because a peak area difference means different retention behaviour", "D": "The second sample has a higher mass-to-charge ratio, so the detector counted more of it"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Both contain the same compound, and the second contains about twice the amount): A matching retention time identifies the same compound, and the area under a peak is proportional to the amount present. Retention behaviour is the same here, so the compounds do not differ. Charge and mass-to-charge ratio are not read from an ultraviolet-detected HPLC peak."}, {"id": 1069, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Smears from an adrenal mass in a toddler show small cells forming rosettes within a fibrillar pink background. The differential includes other small round blue cell tumours. Which tumour best fits, and which planning step at ROSE is stressed for these cases?", "options": {"A": "Ewing sarcoma, with extra material collected only for alcohol-fixed smears.", "B": "Neuroblastoma, with extra material collected for cell block, flow and molecular tests.", "C": "Wilms tumour, with material sent in formalin only for permanent sections.", "D": "Osteosarcoma, with extra material spread thickly to preserve the osteoid matrix."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Neuroblastoma, with extra material collected for cell block, flow and molecular tests.): Small cells forming rosettes in a fibrillar background are the pattern of neuroblastoma, and small round blue cell tumours need cell block material, flow cytometry and molecular testing. Ewing sarcoma has a different picture and needs the same broad collection, not smears alone. Osteosarcoma requires malignant cells with osteoid."}, {"id": 1070, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist finds a blood specimen for chromosome analysis that was placed in a refrigerator overnight at the collection site, though the laboratory's instructions did not call for it. The tube is sodium heparin, correctly labelled, and free of clots. How should the technologist handle this tube?", "options": {"A": "Send the sample to hematology for a cell count, since cytogenetics cannot use chilled blood", "B": "Reject the specimen and request a new collection, since cooling kills the dividing cells", "C": "Document the storage condition on receipt, then set up the culture and add a comment", "D": "Freeze the sample at minus twenty degrees before setting up the culture"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Document the storage condition on receipt, then set up the culture and add a comment): Overnight refrigeration is not recommended unless instructed, but the specimen may still grow, so the condition is documented and set-up proceeds with a comment. Freezing kills cells, and hematology testing does not answer the request."}, {"id": 1071, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A renal FNA shows granular cells with round nuclei and small nucleoli, and the pathologist cannot separate oncocytoma from a granular variant of renal cell carcinoma on smears alone. The technologist wants to help resolve the question. What is the most useful step?", "options": {"A": "Report oncocytoma as final, since granular cytoplasm proves benign behaviour.", "B": "Discard the smears and ask for a renal biopsy without any further review.", "C": "Prepare a cell block for immunostains and correlate with the imaging findings.", "D": "Repeat the smears in air-dried form only, because Papanicolaou stain hides granules."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prepare a cell block for immunostains and correlate with the imaging findings.): Oncocytoma can be hard to separate from granular carcinoma, and a cell block with immunostains plus imaging correlation often assists. Granular cytoplasm alone does not prove a tumour benign. Air-dried smears will not settle the question, and discarding the sample wastes diagnostic material."}, {"id": 1072, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A long-term smoker with severe emphysema has hemoglobin 190 g/L, a raised serum erythropoietin, normal white cells and platelets, and a negative JAK2 V617F result. The trainee wants to label the case polycythemia vera because of the high hemoglobin. What is the better interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Secondary polycythemia from chronic hypoxia, since erythropoietin is raised and the mutation is absent", "B": "Relative polycythemia, since smoking lowers plasma volume while the red cell mass stays normal", "C": "Polycythemia vera, since raised erythropoietin is typical of an autonomous marrow", "D": "Primary myelofibrosis, since a raised hemoglobin with negative JAK2 marks that disease"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Secondary polycythemia from chronic hypoxia, since erythropoietin is raised and the mutation is absent): Chronic hypoxia from lung disease or smoking drives erythropoietin up and produces true erythrocytosis without the JAK2 mutation. Polycythemia vera has a low EPO and more than 95 percent carry the mutation. Relative polycythemia has a normal red cell mass, and myelofibrosis is not defined by a raised hemoglobin."}, {"id": 1073, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A new PCR test is evaluated on 200 patients. Fifty have the disease by the reference standard, and the test is positive in 45 of them. Among the 150 patients without disease, the test is negative in 144. What are the clinical sensitivity and specificity?", "options": {"A": "Sensitivity is 96 percent and specificity is 90 percent.", "B": "Sensitivity is 90 percent and specificity is 96 percent.", "C": "Sensitivity is 75 percent and specificity is 98 percent.", "D": "Sensitivity is 22.5 percent and specificity is 72 percent."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sensitivity is 90 percent and specificity is 96 percent.): Sensitivity is true positives divided by all diseased patients, 45 over 50, or 90 percent. Specificity is true negatives divided by all non-diseased patients, 144 over 150, or 96 percent. Swapping the two reverses the definitions. Dividing by 200 for both ignores the correct denominators, and the last pair has no arithmetic basis."}, {"id": 1074, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cytology technologist is asked which preservative is used to fix the pellet of a body fluid so it can be processed like a small tissue biopsy for a cell block. The pellet was prepared in the laboratory after the fluid was spun down, and nothing else was added to it. What is the usual fixative for this step?", "options": {"A": "Ninety-five percent ethanol, followed by air drying and Diff-Quik staining", "B": "Buffered formalin, followed by routine tissue processing and embedding", "C": "Spray fixative, followed by coating removal and Papanicolaou staining", "D": "RPMI culture medium, followed by incubation and flow cytometric analysis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Buffered formalin, followed by routine tissue processing and embedding): Buffered formalin fixes a cell block pellet before routine processing. Ethanol is for wet-fixed smears, RPMI keeps cells alive, and spray fixative is for direct smears rather than pellets."}, {"id": 1075, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist reviews the susceptibility panel shown for a Staphylococcus aureus blood culture isolate and prepares the report for the infection prevention team, who need a clear interpretation. Which statement correctly describes how the beta-lactam results should be reported for this isolate?", "options": {"A": "Report penicillins and cephalosporins as susceptible, since vancomycin and TMP-SMX remain susceptible", "B": "Report as methicillin susceptible, since the oxacillin MIC is below the resistance breakpoint", "C": "Report oxacillin as resistant and leave the cephalosporins as tested for the physician", "D": "Report as methicillin resistant, with penicillins and most cephalosporins resistant"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report as methicillin resistant, with penicillins and most cephalosporins resistant): The table shows a cefoxitin zone of 17 mm, an oxacillin MIC of 4 ug/mL and a positive PBP2a test, all indicating methicillin resistance. Penicillins, most cephalosporins and other beta-lactams are then reported resistant even if they test susceptible in vitro, with ceftaroline the exception. An oxacillin MIC of 4 ug/mL or more is resistant, so a susceptible report is wrong.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "S. aureus susceptibility, blood isolate", "columns": ["Test", "Result", "Interpretation"], "rows": [["Cefoxitin disk", "Zone 17 mm", "R"], ["Oxacillin MIC", "4 ug/mL", "R"], ["PBP2a latex", "POS", "Detected"], ["Vancomycin MIC", "1 ug/mL", "S"], ["TMP-SMX", "Zone 27 mm", "S"]], "flag_col": 1, "caption": "Susceptibility panel"}, "image": "question_images/q_1075.png"}, {"id": 1076, "domain": "histology", "question": "In a frozen section, fatty tissue and dense tissue such as muscle may need different chamber conditions to cut well. Which statement correctly describes how temperature is adjusted for these different tissues in the cryostat? The technologist checks the temperature setting carefully.", "options": {"A": "Fatty tissue needs colder settings, while muscle may cut warmer", "B": "Temperature has no effect since the freezing compound decides", "C": "Fatty tissue needs warmer temperatures, while muscle needs colder", "D": "All tissues cut best at exactly the same fixed temperature"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fatty tissue needs colder settings, while muscle may cut warmer): Fatty tissue stays soft and needs colder chamber temperatures, and dense tissue such as muscle may cut better slightly warmer. The right setting depends on the tissue, so a single fixed temperature would not suit all specimens."}, {"id": 1077, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Fresh from an EBUS procedure, a needle rinse from an unknown patient reaches the cytology bench, and the technologist prepares to open the container without gloves because the result is not urgent. A colleague objects. Which principle from standard precautions should guide the handling of every fresh specimen?", "options": {"A": "Wear eye protection for formalin steps, since infectious specimens do not splash.", "B": "Treat every fresh specimen as potentially infectious, using gloves, a lab coat and eye protection.", "C": "Wear gloves only when the specimen is bloody, since clear fluids carry no infection.", "D": "Treat only specimens from known infected patients as hazardous, and wear gloves for those."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Treat every fresh specimen as potentially infectious, using gloves, a lab coat and eye protection.): Standard precautions treat every fresh specimen as potentially infectious, so gloves, a lab coat and eye protection are used each time. Infection status is often unknown, so limiting protection to known cases leaves gaps. Clear fluids can carry organisms, and infectious material can splash."}, {"id": 1078, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A laboratory identifies an Escherichia coli from a urine culture that is an extended-spectrum beta-lactamase producer. The isolate is confirmed phenotypically. The technologist is asked what steps go with the confirmed result. Which action fits current practice for this finding?", "options": {"A": "Notify the physician and infection prevention promptly, since a confirmed ESBL producer needs isolation precautions", "B": "Include it in the routine batch report because susceptibilities are still pending today", "C": "Treat it as normal flora and delay notification until the culture is repeated tomorrow", "D": "Treat it as a probable contaminant and ask the ward to send a second specimen"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Notify the physician and infection prevention promptly, since a confirmed ESBL producer needs isolation precautions): Extended-spectrum beta-lactamase and carbapenemase producers are confirmed by phenotypic or molecular tests and trigger prompt notification, isolation precautions and infection prevention involvement. Routine batch reporting, treating the isolate as normal flora or calling it a contaminant would delay action and risk spread."}, {"id": 1079, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Thyroid aspirate smears are air-dried and stained with a Romanowsky stain, and the slide contains thick, cracked-looking material and a few flat honeycomb sheets of follicular cells, and the ultrasound favours a benign nodule. The trainee asks what colour colloid takes on this stain. How does colloid appear on this Romanowsky preparation?", "options": {"A": "Magenta, whereas on the Pap stain it looks glassy and cracked", "B": "Pale blue, similar to the background mucin found in mucinous carcinoma", "C": "Bright orange, because keratin lines the wall of a benign cyst", "D": "Apple-green, seen under polarized light after the slide is treated with Congo red"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Magenta, whereas on the Pap stain it looks glassy and cracked): Colloid appears magenta on air-dried Romanowsky stains and glassy and cracked on the Pap stain, with honeycomb sheets pointing to a benign colloid nodule. Apple-green birefringence describes Congo red-stained amyloid. Pale blue mucin belongs to a breast tumour. Orange staining suggests keratin, not colloid."}, {"id": 1080, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A pathologist wants immunohistochemistry to type a metastatic tumour found in a pleural effusion, and the smears have been used up. The cytology laboratory still has the pelleted cell block. The original smears were fully used up for the first stains, and no unstained slide was kept. Why can the block provide the extra material needed?", "options": {"A": "The block contains living cells that can be grown in culture for testing", "B": "The block can be smeared onto new slides and stained with Diff-Quik", "C": "The block can be thawed and resuspended to make a fresh liquid-based vial", "D": "Additional sections can be cut for immunohistochemistry and molecular tests"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Additional sections can be cut for immunohistochemistry and molecular tests): A cell block is processed like tissue, so extra sections are cut for immunohistochemistry and molecular tests. It is fixed and non-viable, so it cannot be resuspended, cultured or smeared."}, {"id": 1081, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "An RhD-negative pregnant woman without anti-D suffers abdominal trauma at 20 weeks, and the physician asks whether a dose is needed now, well before the routine 28-week injection is due. What does the laboratory recommend? Her antibody screen is negative and ultrasound shows no placental abruption.", "options": {"A": "No dose until the routine 28-week injection, since abdominal trauma does not cause fetomaternal bleeding.", "B": "An additional dose with bleed screening, since trauma can cause fetomaternal bleeding.", "C": "An additional dose only after delivery, once the baby is typed.", "D": "A repeat titre in four weeks, then a dose if the titre rises."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An additional dose with bleed screening, since trauma can cause fetomaternal bleeding.): Events that can cause fetomaternal bleeding, such as trauma, miscarriage, amniocentesis and antepartum bleeding, call for additional doses and bleed screening. Waiting for 28 weeks or delivery leaves her exposed, and a titre cannot guide prophylaxis."}, {"id": 1082, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A cord blood DAT is positive on a baby born to an RhD-negative mother. The physician asks the laboratory to identify what is coating the baby's red cells so that the risk can be judged. What test is performed next?", "options": {"A": "A reverse group, to identify the baby's own ABO antibodies.", "B": "A titre on the baby's serum, to identify the maternal titre.", "C": "An eluate, to identify the antibody on the baby's red cells.", "D": "A Kleihauer-Betke test, to identify fetal cells in the baby's blood."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An eluate, to identify the antibody on the baby's red cells.): A positive DAT on a baby is followed by an eluate to identify the antibody attached to the cells. Reverse grouping is not done on cord blood, and a Kleihauer-Betke test looks for fetal cells in the maternal sample."}, {"id": 1083, "domain": "histology", "question": "Before a technologist changes a block or a blade on the rotary microtome, a specific safety action should be taken to prevent the block holder from moving and the sharp edge from causing injury. What should be done first? The block is a paraffin block from a routine specimen.", "options": {"A": "Loosen the block holder clamps while the wheel is still turning", "B": "Lock the handwheel and keep the blade guard in place", "C": "Wipe the blade edge with a gloved finger to remove debris", "D": "Set the thickness dial to its maximum value before touching"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lock the handwheel and keep the blade guard in place): Locking the handwheel stops the block from advancing, and the blade guard covers the very sharp edge. Wiping an edge with a finger or working near a moving holder invites cuts. Changing the dial does not make the instrument safe."}, {"id": 1084, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A laboratory needs a manual platelet count because the analyzer result is unreliable on a patient with fragments. The dilution is prepared and charged into the counting chamber before viewing. Which diluent, dilution and microscopy method are correct for counting platelets in the hemocytometer?", "options": {"A": "Saline at 1 in 100, viewed with polarized light", "B": "1 percent ammonium oxalate, 1 in 100, viewed by phase contrast", "C": "2 percent acetic acid at 1 in 20, viewed by brightfield", "D": "1 percent ammonium oxalate at 1 in 20, viewed by dark field"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (1 percent ammonium oxalate, 1 in 100, viewed by phase contrast): Ammonium oxalate lyses the red cells, and with a 1 in 100 dilution and phase contrast the platelets appear clear and countable. Acetic acid at 1 in 20 is the white cell diluent. Saline does not lyse red cells, so they would crowd the count. The other combination changes the dilution and method."}, {"id": 1085, "domain": "hematology", "question": "On a Wright-Giemsa differential, the technologist must separate a band neutrophil from a metamyelocyte. Both cells have dense chromatin and pink-tan cytoplasm with fine granules, and the count is being finished at 100 cells. What nuclear feature best identifies the band form rather than the metamyelocyte?", "options": {"A": "A round nucleus with fine open chromatin and one or more prominent nucleoli", "B": "A kidney-shaped nucleus with a clear indentation on one side and dense chromatin", "C": "A curved nucleus of even thickness, with no thin filament between any lobes", "D": "Two to five distinct lobes connected together by thin strands of nuclear material"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A curved nucleus of even thickness, with no thin filament between any lobes): A band has a curved nucleus of even thickness without a filament. The kidney-shaped indented nucleus belongs to the metamyelocyte, the lobes with thin strands to the segmented neutrophil, and the round open nucleus with nucleoli to the blast."}, {"id": 1086, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A QF-PCR marker for chromosome 18 shows a single peak in a prenatal sample, while two other chromosome 18 markers show two equal peaks. The interpreting scientist must decide how to report chromosome 18. Which statement best reflects correct reasoning?", "options": {"A": "The single peak proves trisomy 18, since all three copies share one allele", "B": "The other two markers are unreliable, since paired peaks cannot represent a normal diploid patient sample", "C": "The single peak proves monosomy 18, so a deletion should be reported", "D": "The single-peak marker is uninformative because it is homozygous, and the others suggest normal"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The single-peak marker is uninformative because it is homozygous, and the others suggest normal): A person homozygous for a repeat shows one peak, which carries no information about copy number. The other markers, with balanced peaks, indicate two copies. A single peak does not prove monosomy or trisomy, and two equal peaks are the normal heterozygous pattern."}, {"id": 1087, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A physician requests a test that can examine DNA copy number without needing the patient's cells to be dividing or a metaphase spread to be prepared. The laboratory offers the method as a complement to karyotyping for developmental delay. Which method fits this description?", "options": {"A": "G-banded karyotyping, which reads DNA directly from cells in interphase", "B": "Colcemid harvest, which reads DNA copy number after hypotonic swelling", "C": "Phase-contrast microscopy, which counts DNA copies inside each cell nucleus", "D": "Chromosomal microarray, which studies DNA directly and does not need a dividing cell"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chromosomal microarray, which studies DNA directly and does not need a dividing cell): Chromosomal microarray analyzes DNA directly and does not need dividing cells, though the specimen still needs correct collection. G-banding, colcemid harvest and phase-contrast microscopy all depend on metaphase spreads or do not measure copy number."}, {"id": 1088, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A calcium control at level 1 measures 3.4 SD above its mean, while the level 2 control on the same run is within 1 SD of its mean. No calibration change has occurred today. The technologist applies the multirule scheme. What is the correct decision?", "options": {"A": "Reject the run under the 2-2s rule and investigate for systematic error", "B": "Treat the value as a 1-2s warning and continue testing patients", "C": "Reject the run under the 1-3s rule and investigate, likely for random error", "D": "Accept the run because only one of the two controls is outside its limits"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reject the run under the 1-3s rule and investigate, likely for random error): One control beyond 3 SD violates the 1-3s rule, which rejects the run and usually points to random error. It does not matter that the other level is in control. The value is beyond the warning threshold, and 2-2s needs two consecutive values beyond 2 SD."}, {"id": 1089, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A physician orders a hereditary cancer gene panel and sends an EDTA tube with a requisition that lacks any documentation of patient consent. The specimen is labelled correctly and is within its stability limit. What is the best action for the technologist receiving the specimen?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with extraction, since the physician's order implies consent for every genetic test.", "B": "Reject the specimen and require a new collection with fresh paperwork before testing", "C": "Hold testing and contact the provider to obtain consent documentation before proceeding.", "D": "Run the panel but withhold the report until consent arrives with a later batch."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold testing and contact the provider to obtain consent documentation before proceeding.): Consent is required for genetic testing, particularly for hereditary conditions, so testing waits until the provider supplies documentation. An order alone does not imply consent. Running the panel first commits resources without authority, and permanent rejection is unnecessary when the paperwork can simply be supplied."}, {"id": 1090, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "Quality control zones for Escherichia coli ATCC 25922 are slightly smaller than usual for many different drugs on the same plate. The technologist prepared the bacterial suspension by eye, without comparing it to a turbidity standard. Other conditions were normal. What is the most likely cause of the shrunken zones across several drug classes?", "options": {"A": "An inoculum heavier than 0.5 McFarland, which shrinks zones across many drug classes", "B": "Agar poured too thin, which shrinks zones across many drug classes at once", "C": "Disks opened while still cold, which shrinks only the aminoglycoside zones", "D": "An inoculum lighter than 0.5 McFarland, which shrinks zones across many drug classes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An inoculum heavier than 0.5 McFarland, which shrinks zones across many drug classes): A suspension heavier than the standard produces smaller zones and false resistance for many drugs at once. A lighter inoculum enlarges zones, and thin agar also enlarges them. Cold-opened disks would affect individual disks through moisture, not every class together. Adjusting to the turbidity standard fixes the cause."}, {"id": 1091, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A smear from a patient with a low platelet count shows several platelets ringed around individual neutrophils. The patient has no bleeding and the count was taken from an EDTA tube. What is this finding called, and what effect can it have on the platelet count?", "options": {"A": "Platelet activation from lipemia, which raises the platelet count", "B": "Platelet satellitism, which can give a falsely low platelet count in EDTA", "C": "Platelet phagocytosis by neutrophils in the body, confirming true thrombocytopenia", "D": "Rouleaux formation, which gives a falsely high platelet count in EDTA"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Platelet satellitism, which can give a falsely low platelet count in EDTA): Platelets sticking around neutrophils is called satellitism, an in vitro effect of EDTA that removes platelets from the count and causes pseudothrombocytopenia. Rouleaux refers to stacked red cells. The finding does not show true phagocytosis. Lipemia has no relation to platelets ringing around neutrophils."}, {"id": 1092, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist prepares to open a culture of Mycobacterium tuberculosis for a diagnostic workup. Which containment level and precaution are appropriate for handling these cultures? The request came from a physician who suspects tuberculosis in an outpatient, and the work has to be planned before any culture is opened.", "options": {"A": "Containment level 1 on an open bench with gloves only", "B": "Containment level 3 with controlled access and directional airflow", "C": "Containment level 2 on an open bench without a cabinet", "D": "Containment level 2 with an open bench and a standard laboratory coat"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Containment level 3 with controlled access and directional airflow): Cultures of M. tuberculosis need level 3 because inhalation causes serious disease, and this adds controlled access, directional airflow, and respiratory protection as needed. Levels 1 and 2 are insufficient, and level 4 is reserved for agents without treatment."}, {"id": 1093, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory director compares two ways of enriching target genes from fragmented DNA before sequencing. One uses probes that bind and pull down regions of interest, and gives more even coverage over larger targets. What is the name of this enrichment approach?", "options": {"A": "Dideoxy termination, which halts strands with labelled nucleotides in every reaction", "B": "Amplicon enrichment, which uses many primer pairs to amplify each target", "C": "Hybrid capture, which uses probes to select the regions of interest", "D": "Whole genome amplification, which copies the entire genome without selection"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hybrid capture, which uses probes to select the regions of interest): Hybrid capture uses probes to bind and pull out the target regions and usually gives more even coverage and handles larger regions. Amplicon methods use many PCR primers and need less input, but a variant under a primer can be missed. Dideoxy termination is the chemistry of Sanger sequencing, not an enrichment step."}, {"id": 1094, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The student is new to the bench. A screening swab from an intensive care patient grows a gram-positive coccus that is coagulase positive. The infection control team asks how methicillin resistance is detected in the laboratory. Which test is used to identify MRSA in routine practice?", "options": {"A": "A novobiocin disk test or a PYR test performed on the Staphylococcus aureus isolate", "B": "A cefoxitin screen or a PBP2a test performed on the Staphylococcus aureus isolate", "C": "A bacitracin disk test or a CAMP test performed on the Staphylococcus aureus isolate", "D": "An optochin disk test or a bile solubility test performed on the Staphylococcus aureus isolate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A cefoxitin screen or a PBP2a test performed on the Staphylococcus aureus isolate): MRSA is detected with a cefoxitin screen or a PBP2a test. Novobiocin distinguishes coagulase-negative species, optochin and bile solubility are for Streptococcus pneumoniae, and bacitracin and CAMP belong to streptococcal identification."}, {"id": 1095, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A collector with excellent technique redraws a patient whose first sample was hemolyzed. The second tube, drawn with a 21 gauge needle, dry skin and gentle inversion, is also visibly pink. The patient received a red cell transfusion two hours ago and now has back pain and dark urine. What does this suggest?", "options": {"A": "The pink colour is icterus from bilirubin, so a different colorimetric method should be used", "B": "Hemolysis is still caused by collection, so the collector should switch to a smaller needle", "C": "The pink colour is lipemia from the recent transfusion, so ultracentrifugation should clear it", "D": "Hemolysis is occurring inside the patient, so a transfusion reaction should be reported urgently"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hemolysis is occurring inside the patient, so a transfusion reaction should be reported urgently): When correct technique twice produces a hemolyzed sample, the cause may be in vivo hemolysis, which arises from disease such as hemolytic anemia or from a transfusion reaction. Back pain and dark urine after transfusion make a reaction likely, so the clinical team must be alerted urgently. A smaller needle would increase in vitro hemolysis. Lipemia is milky and icterus is yellow-brown, not pink."}, {"id": 1096, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Before reverse transcription, a technologist needs the concentration of an RNA extract. The extract is measured without any dilution and gives an absorbance of 0.50 at 260 nm on a 1 cm path. Using the standard conversion for RNA, what concentration should be recorded?", "options": {"A": "80 µg/mL, from 0.50 multiplied by 40 and then by two for both strands.", "B": "25 µg/mL, from 0.50 multiplied by 50 µg/mL per absorbance unit.", "C": "20 µg/mL, from 0.50 multiplied by 40 µg/mL per absorbance unit.", "D": "16.5 µg/mL, from 0.50 multiplied by 33 µg/mL per absorbance unit."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (20 µg/mL, from 0.50 multiplied by 40 µg/mL per absorbance unit.): An absorbance of 1.0 at 260 nm corresponds to about 40 µg/mL of RNA, so 0.50 x 40 = 20 µg/mL. The factor of 50 applies to double-stranded DNA and 33 to single-stranded oligonucleotides. RNA is not doubled for strands."}, {"id": 1097, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A lung biopsy from a man who hunts near a river in Wisconsin shows large thick-walled yeasts, 8 to 15 micrometres across, each with a single broad-based bud and a double-contoured wall. Silver stain highlights them in the granulomatous inflammation. Which dimorphic fungus should the technologist suspect from this tissue appearance?", "options": {"A": "Histoplasma capsulatum, since its yeasts are tiny with narrow-based buds inside macrophages", "B": "Blastomyces dermatitidis, since its yeasts are large with a single broad-based bud", "C": "Sporothrix schenckii, since its yeasts are cigar-shaped and found after a puncture injury", "D": "Coccidioides posadasii, since it forms large spherules packed with endospores in tissue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Blastomyces dermatitidis, since its yeasts are large with a single broad-based bud): Large thick-walled yeasts with one broad-based bud are typical of Blastomyces. Histoplasma is very small and intracellular, Coccidioides forms endospore-filled spherules, and Sporothrix is cigar-shaped."}, {"id": 1098, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A urine specimen from a man with a known bladder-neck mass contains malignant glandular cells with vacuolated cytoplasm and prominent nucleoli, quite unlike typical high-grade urothelial cells. Imaging suggests a tumour extending from the prostate. Which Paris System category best fits this specimen?", "options": {"A": "Atypical urothelial cells, since prominent nucleoli are the main criterion for this category", "B": "Other malignancies, primary or metastatic, including tumours invading from nearby organs", "C": "Low-grade urothelial neoplasia, since glandular cells with nucleoli are typical of this category", "D": "High-grade urothelial carcinoma, since every malignant cell in urine is assigned to this category"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Other malignancies, primary or metastatic, including tumours invading from nearby organs): Adenocarcinoma and prostate carcinoma can reach urine by direct spread, and the Paris System reports non-urothelial tumours as other malignancies, primary or metastatic. Not every malignant cell in urine is urothelial. Low-grade neoplasia has monotonous papillary cells, and atypia is defined by nuclear ratio and chromatin criteria rather than nucleoli."}, {"id": 1099, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A liquid-based gynecologic slide is thin, well stained and clean, but it has only about 3,000 squamous cells. The vial was properly labelled and processed on the correct instrument. A colleague wants to report it as satisfactory because the slide looks so clean. How should this slide be reported?", "options": {"A": "Report it as satisfactory, since a clean background compensates for the low count", "B": "Report it as unsatisfactory, since the cell count is below the liquid-based minimum", "C": "Report it as satisfactory, since the vial was labelled and processed correctly", "D": "Report it as negative, since no abnormal cells were seen in the cells present"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report it as unsatisfactory, since the cell count is below the liquid-based minimum): A liquid-based slide needs at least 5,000 well-preserved squamous cells, so 3,000 is inadequate however clean the slide looks. A clean background, correct processing and a lack of abnormal cells do not replace adequate cellularity."}, {"id": 1100, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A frozen serum aliquot is thawed for a second assay after being tested once and then refrozen twice over three weeks. The technologist notices the thawed serum was pipetted from the top without mixing and the results are inconsistent. What is the appropriate handling of frozen aliquots?", "options": {"A": "Thaw each aliquot once and mix it well, since thawed serum layers by density", "B": "Refreeze aliquots several times, since layering disappears with repeated cycles", "C": "Warm aliquots to 37 degrees Celsius for a day and avoid mixing before sampling", "D": "Sample from the top layer of the thawed serum, since it is most representative"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Thaw each aliquot once and mix it well, since thawed serum layers by density): Repeated freezing and thawing damages some analytes, and thawed serum forms layers of different density, so an aliquot is thawed once and mixed well before sampling. Sampling from the top gives a non-representative portion, and extended warming can degrade unstable analytes. Refreezing does not remove layering."}, {"id": 1101, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A pancreatic cystic lesion is aspirated by EUS, and the smears contain thick mucin with few cells. The gastroenterologist asks the laboratory which chemistry tests can be added to the fluid to help classify the cyst. Which tests are appropriate to request on cyst fluid?", "options": {"A": "Arginase-1 and Hep Par 1, which are stains for hepatocellular differentiation.", "B": "CEA and amylase, measured on the cyst fluid to help classify the type of cyst.", "C": "Troponin and creatine kinase, which help classify cardiac injury rather than cyst type.", "D": "Acid-fast stain and mycobacterial culture, which detect infection within the cyst."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (CEA and amylase, measured on the cyst fluid to help classify the type of cyst.): Cystic pancreatic lesions yield mucin, and CEA and amylase measured on the fluid help classify them. Cardiac enzymes have no role, and arginase-1 and Hep Par 1 are immunostains for liver cells. Acid-fast testing looks for mycobacteria, which are not the usual question in a cyst."}, {"id": 1102, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A fetus has lost all four alpha globin genes. Ultrasound shows generalized edema and heart failure at 28 weeks of gestation, and cord blood contains mostly hemoglobin composed of four gamma chains. Which name describes this hemoglobin and this clinical condition?", "options": {"A": "Hemoglobin A2, a delta chain pair that causes mild microcytosis with normal indices", "B": "Hemoglobin H, a beta chain tetramer that causes a chronic hemolytic anemia", "C": "Hemoglobin Bart's, a gamma chain tetramer that causes hydrops fetalis", "D": "Hemoglobin E, a beta chain variant that causes a mild microcytic anemia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hemoglobin Bart's, a gamma chain tetramer that causes hydrops fetalis): Loss of all four alpha genes leaves gamma chain tetramers called hemoglobin Bart's, which carry oxygen very poorly, and the fetus develops hydrops. Hemoglobin H forms from beta chains when three genes are lost. Hemoglobin A2 is a normal minor fraction, and the last does not fit the description."}, {"id": 1103, "domain": "hematology", "question": "The flow cytometry report on a peripheral blood sample from an older patient lists the typical immunophenotype of a common leukemia of older adults. The lymphocytes are small and mature looking. Which marker combination is typical of chronic lymphocytic leukemia?", "options": {"A": "CD138 positive only, with cytoplasmic light chain restriction on plasma cells", "B": "CD3 positive only, with strong CD14 and CD64 monocytic marker expression", "C": "CD34 and TdT positive, with strong myeloperoxidase and CD13 expression", "D": "CD5, CD19 and CD23 positive, with weak surface immunoglobulin and weak CD20"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (CD5, CD19 and CD23 positive, with weak surface immunoglobulin and weak CD20): CLL is typically CD5, CD19 and CD23 positive with weak surface immunoglobulin and weak CD20. CD34 and TdT indicate immaturity, CD14 and CD64 indicate monocytes, and CD138 indicates plasma cells, so those patterns belong to other diseases."}, {"id": 1104, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In the laboratory, A laboratory receives a liquid-based cervical sample in a collection medium that the HPV assay manufacturer has not validated for that assay. The clinician urgently wants both the slide and the HPV result. What is the appropriate laboratory action?", "options": {"A": "Add the validated medium to the vial and run the test without any documentation", "B": "Run the assay anyway because the same cells are in the vial and the slide", "C": "Report the HPV result as negative to avoid a delay in patient care today", "D": "Use only an assay validated for that medium, since others can give false results"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use only an assay validated for that medium, since others can give false results): Assays must be used only with collection media they are validated for, because incompatible fixatives can cause false results. Running unvalidated combinations, improvising additions or reporting a made-up negative places the patient at risk and violates quality requirements. Validation of each collection medium is part of the quality system, not an optional extra."}, {"id": 1105, "domain": "histology", "question": "A pathologist finds a section that has small round holes scattered through the tissue, and the technologist suspects a wax problem rather than a blade problem. Which of the following is a recognized cause of holes in a section? The pathologist has not requested any change.", "options": {"A": "Correct differentiation in dilute acid alcohol after hematoxylin", "B": "A slightly clean flotation bath with fresh warm water", "C": "A refractive index that is close to that of glass", "D": "Air bubbles in the wax or incompletely infiltrated tissue"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Air bubbles in the wax or incompletely infiltrated tissue): Holes come from air bubbles in the wax, incomplete processing or tissue that was dropped, and a poorly infiltrated block needs reprocessing. A clean bath, proper differentiation and a good mounting medium do not create holes."}, {"id": 1106, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist examines a slide showing squamous cells with perinuclear halos and red-staining cytoplasm, along with many neutrophils and degenerating cells. Small grey-blue oval organisms are present in the background. What is the best interpretation of the squamous cell changes?", "options": {"A": "True koilocytosis, confirming productive HPV infection in those squamous cells with sharp halos present", "B": "Reactive changes from Trichomonas infection, which can mimic dysplasia if judged carelessly", "C": "True koilocytosis, confirming productive HPV infection in those cells", "D": "Atrophy, confirmed by a predominance of parabasal cells across the smear"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reactive changes from Trichomonas infection, which can mimic dysplasia if judged carelessly): Trichomonas can cause reactive changes such as perinuclear halos and red-staining pseudoeosinophilia that mimic dysplasia, so the cells must be judged with care. True koilocytes have sharp halos with irregular enlarged nuclei. Herpes and atrophy have their own distinct features."}, {"id": 1107, "domain": "histology", "question": "A lymph node biopsy from an older adult shows a diffuse infiltrate of small atypical lymphoid cells. Immunohistochemistry results on the neoplastic cells are shown in the image, with controls acceptable. Which diagnosis is best supported by the panel of results?", "options": {"A": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia, supported by CD5 with CD23 expression on the neoplastic cells", "B": "Follicular lymphoma, supported by CD10 expression with germinal centre marker positivity", "C": "Marginal zone lymphoma, supported by CD20 positivity with negative CD5 and cyclin D1 results", "D": "Mantle cell lymphoma, supported by CD5 with cyclin D1 and SOX11 expression"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mantle cell lymphoma, supported by CD5 with cyclin D1 and SOX11 expression): CD5-positive B cells that express cyclin D1 and SOX11 and lack CD23 and CD10 fit mantle cell lymphoma. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia would be CD23 positive and cyclin D1 negative, follicular lymphoma would express CD10, and marginal zone lymphoma would lack CD5 and cyclin D1.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "IHC panel, lymphoid infiltrate", "columns": ["Marker", "Result", "Staining"], "rows": [["CD20", "POS", "Membranous"], ["CD5", "POS", "Membranous"], ["CD23", "Negative", "None"], ["CD10", "Negative", "None"], ["Cyclin D1", "POS", "Nuclear"], ["SOX11", "POS", "Nuclear"]], "caption": "Immunohistochemistry results on neoplastic cells", "flag_col": 1}, "image": "question_images/q_1107.png"}, {"id": 1108, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A metaphase FISH using a locus-specific probe for chromosome 22 shows the expected signal on both chromosome 22 homologues, but also a bright signal on the short arm of a different acrocentric chromosome. The same extra signal appears in normal control cells too. What is the most likely explanation?", "options": {"A": "Autofluorescence of the slide glass, so a different mounting medium is needed", "B": "A gain of the target gene, so the extra signal should be scored as a duplication", "C": "Cross-hybridization to a similar sequence, so the probe lot and stringency should be reviewed", "D": "A true trisomy of the acrocentric chromosome, so the karyotype should be reported as abnormal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cross-hybridization to a similar sequence, so the probe lot and stringency should be reviewed): An extra signal seen consistently in normal controls as well points to the probe binding a related sequence elsewhere, which is cross-hybridization. It should be handled through probe review and wash stringency. A true trisomy or duplication would not appear in normal controls, and glass autofluorescence would not give a chromosome-specific signal."}, {"id": 1109, "domain": "histology", "question": "Hematoxylin is red under acid conditions and only turns blue when the environment becomes mildly alkaline. During the staining run, a technologist has several choices of solution for the bluing step. Which of these is commonly used for bluing? The eosin looks acceptable on the same slides.", "options": {"A": "A solution of eosin adjusted to a strongly acid pH", "B": "Scott tap water substitute or dilute ammonia water", "C": "Absolute alcohol followed by xylene in a clearing dish", "D": "Dilute acetic acid solution mixed with acid alcohol"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Scott tap water substitute or dilute ammonia water): Bluing reagents are mildly alkaline, such as dilute ammonia water or Scott tap water substitute, and tap water may also work. Acid solutions would keep hematoxylin red. Alcohol and xylene dehydrate and clear, and eosin is a different dye."}, {"id": 1110, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A general practitioner asks whether a routine cholesterol screening test for a healthy 45-year-old must be collected after an overnight fast. The laboratory follows current Canadian guidance. What is the appropriate answer, and when would a repeat fasting sample be requested?", "options": {"A": "Non-fasting is acceptable, and a repeat is requested if total cholesterol is normal", "B": "Non-fasting is acceptable, and a fasting repeat may be requested if triglycerides are very high", "C": "Fasting is mandatory, and a non-fasting repeat is requested if triglycerides are high", "D": "Fasting is mandatory, and a repeat is requested only if HDL is very high"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Non-fasting is acceptable, and a fasting repeat may be requested if triglycerides are very high): Current Canadian guidance allows non-fasting lipid panels for routine screening, since a normal meal changes cholesterol little, but triglycerides rise after meals, so a very high non-fasting value may lead to a 10 to 12 hour fasting repeat. Fasting is not mandatory."}, {"id": 1111, "domain": "cytology", "question": "The first ROSE pass on a liver mass shows only blood and necrotic debris on the first pass, and a second pass from the centre gave the same result. Imaging shows a viable rim around a necrotic core. The radiologist asks whether to continue. What should the technologist advise?", "options": {"A": "Repeat passes from the necrotic centre until abundant cells are obtained.", "B": "Switch to a thicker needle and keep sampling the same central spot.", "C": "Sample a different part of the lesion, such as the viable rim.", "D": "Stop the procedure and report the aspirate as adequate and benign."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sample a different part of the lesion, such as the viable rim.): ROSE guides the operator to a different part of the lesion when the first passes show only blood or necrosis, and the viable rim is where tumour cells are likely to be found. Necrosis is not an adequate or benign sample. Repeating passes in the same dead centre will not yield diagnostic cells."}, {"id": 1112, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory must screen 48 exons of one large gene in 30 patients each week. The supervisor compares running Sanger sequencing on every exon with using a targeted NGS panel. Which limitation of Sanger sequencing most strongly argues for choosing the NGS panel?", "options": {"A": "Sanger requires tumour tissue, whereas panels can use blood or saliva", "B": "Each region needs its own separate reaction, so throughput is low for many regions", "C": "Sanger cannot detect single nucleotide variants, so variants in these exons would be missed", "D": "Sanger reads are too short to cover any single exon of the gene"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Each region needs its own separate reaction, so throughput is low for many regions): Sanger runs one region per reaction, so 48 exons for 30 patients means well over a thousand reactions, while NGS reads many targets together. Sanger detects SNVs well, and its 500 to 1,000 base reads cover an exon. It works on any suitable specimen, not only tumour tissue."}, {"id": 1113, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A tube arrives for crossmatching with a correct surname, first name, and collection date. The health card number on the tube reads 4528 916 307, while the requisition lists 4528 916 370, the last two digits reversed. The ward phones and says the patient in bed 12 is certainly the right person. How should the transfusion service handle this tube?", "options": {"A": "Reject the tube and request a new sample labelled correctly at the bedside", "B": "Test the sample but flag the record until the ward sends a revised requisition", "C": "Correct the health card number on the tube after recording the phone conversation", "D": "Accept the tube because the name and date agree with the requisition"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reject the tube and request a new sample labelled correctly at the bedside): Identifiers must match the requisition exactly, and a transposed digit is enough to reject. The laboratory does not correct labels or rely on telephone reassurance, since a wrong-patient draw would be hidden by that shortcut. Matching name and date do not cancel a mismatched identifier, and testing an unverified tube wastes reagent and risks a wrong result."}, {"id": 1114, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "The DAT on one patient is 1+ positive with anti-IgG. Hemoglobin is 132 g/L, bilirubin and reticulocyte count are normal, and there is no transfusion or drug history. The physician asks the laboratory what the result means for the patient. Which interpretation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "The result proves warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, and treatment should start immediately", "B": "The result indicates a delayed hemolytic reaction, and a transfusion investigation should begin", "C": "The result is a false positive caused by antiglobulin reagent, so the test is invalid", "D": "A positive DAT can occur without hemolysis, so interpret it alongside the clinical picture"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A positive DAT can occur without hemolysis, so interpret it alongside the clinical picture): The DAT can be positive in some patients who have no hemolysis, so the result is read together with hemoglobin, bilirubin and history. Here the normal values do not support hemolysis, so the finding alone proves neither autoimmune hemolytic anemia nor a delayed reaction. A positive control result would be needed to call the test invalid."}, {"id": 1115, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A urine culture yields an isolate that the automated system identifies as Bacteroides fragilis, which does not fit a urine specimen from an outpatient. The technologist has confirmed the plate looks pure. What should be done before the result is released?", "options": {"A": "Release the result with a comment that the organism commonly causes urinary infection", "B": "Discard the plate and report no growth, since anaerobes cannot be present in urine", "C": "Repeat the tests, recheck for a mixed culture, and confirm the result makes clinical sense", "D": "Release the result as it stands, because an automated identification cannot be wrong"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat the tests, recheck for a mixed culture, and confirm the result makes clinical sense): When an organism does not fit the specimen, the technologist repeats the tests, rechecks for contamination or mixed culture, and asks whether the result makes clinical sense. Releasing it unchecked is unsafe, and Bacteroides is not a common cause of urinary infection."}, {"id": 1116, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A gastroenterologist preparing a teaching session asks which sites can be reached by an endoscopic ultrasound needle passed through the esophagus or stomach wall. The list will be posted for trainees preparing for ROSE work. Which group of sites is correctly listed for EUS-guided FNA?", "options": {"A": "Liver dome, spleen, both kidneys, bladder wall and pelvic lymph nodes.", "B": "Lung periphery, pleura, breast, axillary nodes and subcutaneous soft tissue lumps.", "C": "Pancreas, celiac and mediastinal nodes, left adrenal gland and submucosal masses.", "D": "Pancreas, right adrenal gland, thyroid, salivary glands and peripheral neck nodes."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pancreas, celiac and mediastinal nodes, left adrenal gland and submucosal masses.): From the esophagus or stomach, EUS reaches the pancreas, celiac and mediastinal nodes, the left adrenal gland and submucosal masses. The thyroid, salivary glands and neck nodes are sampled by direct or ultrasound-guided skin approaches. The bladder, pelvis, breast and axilla are outside the reach of the endoscope."}, {"id": 1117, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A cytochemical stain is used to identify monocytic cells on a marrow smear. The technologist adds sodium fluoride to a duplicate slide to confirm the identification. Which stain is positive in monocytic cells and is inhibited by sodium fluoride in the laboratory?", "options": {"A": "Nonspecific esterase using alpha-naphthyl acetate as the substrate", "B": "Specific esterase using chloroacetate as the substrate for granulocytes", "C": "Tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase used to identify hairy cell leukemia cells", "D": "Periodic acid-Schiff used to detect glycogen inside the cytoplasm of lymphoblasts"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Nonspecific esterase using alpha-naphthyl acetate as the substrate): Nonspecific esterase stains monocytic cells and is inhibited by sodium fluoride. Specific esterase stains granulocytes, TRAP identifies hairy cells, and PAS detects glycogen, so those do not describe a fluoride-inhibited monocyte stain."}, {"id": 1118, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A laboratory receives a red cell sample with a positive DAT and needs to identify the antibody attached to the cells in the body. A student asks which technique frees antibody from red cells and recovers it in a small volume of fluid so that it can be tested against a panel.", "options": {"A": "Prewarming, which detaches IgG from red cells at 37 degrees Celsius before the DAT", "B": "Elution, which frees coating antibody from the cells so it can be tested on a panel", "C": "Enzyme treatment, which strips IgG from red cells so that the DAT becomes negative", "D": "Adsorption, which uses the patient's own cells to remove antibody from serum for testing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Elution, which frees coating antibody from the cells so it can be tested on a panel): Elution uses acid, heat or commercial methods to release antibody from red cells, and the eluate is then tested against a panel like serum. Adsorption removes antibody from serum rather than from cells. Enzymes and prewarming do not recover coating antibody for identification."}, {"id": 1119, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist counts 200 interphase nuclei from a marrow smear using a BCR::ABL1 dual-fusion probe and finds 30 nuclei with one red, one green, and two fusion signals. The laboratory reports percentages of abnormal cells. What should the report state for this finding?", "options": {"A": "3 percent of nuclei abnormal, based on 30 of 1000 scored", "B": "85 percent of nuclei abnormal, based on the normal nuclei", "C": "15 percent of nuclei abnormal, based on 30 of 200 scored", "D": "30 percent of nuclei abnormal, based on 30 counted"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (15 percent of nuclei abnormal, based on 30 of 200 scored): The percentage is abnormal nuclei divided by nuclei scored, so 30 divided by 200 equals 0.15, or 15 percent. Reporting the raw count as a percentage or using the normal fraction is wrong, and the denominator must reflect the 200 nuclei actually scored."}, {"id": 1120, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Pan-reactivity appears in a serum tested with 22 percent albumin but was absent when the same specimen was tested with LISS. The autocontrol is positive at the albumin phase, and the patient has a known warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia. Which explanation is most reasonable for the change in reactivity?", "options": {"A": "Albumin neutralized the antihuman globulin, which produces false positives with all cells", "B": "Albumin can raise warm autoantibody reactivity, causing the extra reactions", "C": "Albumin concentrated water in the tube, which strengthens IgM antibodies present", "D": "Albumin destroyed the Duffy antigens, leaving only Rh antigens on the panel cells"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Albumin can raise warm autoantibody reactivity, causing the extra reactions): Albumin can enhance warm autoantibody reactivity, which produces reactions with every cell and the autocontrol. Neutralized antiglobulin reagent would give false negative results, not positive ones. Destroying antigens is an enzyme effect, and concentrating antibody by removing water is a property of PEG."}, {"id": 1121, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist reviews the hazards of chemicals used in the histology laboratory before a safety refresher and lists the effects of xylene on staff who work at the processor and coverslipper. Which description best matches xylene exposure as taught in laboratory safety training?", "options": {"A": "It is a corrosive acid that dissolves bone and must be neutralized before disposal.", "B": "It is flammable, affects the nervous system, and repeated contact cracks skin.", "C": "It is a non-flammable liquid that mainly causes cancer after a single brief exposure.", "D": "It is an explosive solid when dry and must be stored under water"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is flammable, affects the nervous system, and repeated contact cracks skin.): Xylene is a flammable organic solvent whose vapour causes headache and dizziness, and repeated skin contact strips oils, leaving skin dry and cracked. The explosive dry solid stored under water describes picric acid. A corrosive acid that dissolves bone describes a decalcifier, which xylene is not."}, {"id": 1122, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist stores newly received susceptibility disks and later prepares to use a working cartridge. To protect the disks from moisture damage that could alter zone sizes, the cartridge is handled in a specific way before opening. What is that step?", "options": {"A": "Warm the cartridge in an incubator at 35 degrees C for several hours before opening", "B": "Soak the disks in sterile saline for ten minutes before placing them on the agar", "C": "Open the cartridge immediately while cold, so that condensation forms on the disks", "D": "Allow the cartridge to reach room temperature before opening it"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Allow the cartridge to reach room temperature before opening it): Working cartridges are allowed to reach room temperature before opening to prevent moisture condensing on the disks, which can cause deterioration. Heating in an incubator or soaking the disks would damage them."}, {"id": 1123, "domain": "histology", "question": "Certain stains, such as some fat stains, would be dissolved by the alcohols and xylene used in routine dehydration. For these sections, the technologist selects a different type of mounting medium. Which type is used in these cases? The technologist must therefore avoid the usual dehydration steps.", "options": {"A": "A resin mounting medium dissolved in xylene solution", "B": "A wax mounting medium melted on a hotplate", "C": "An aqueous mounting medium, used directly from water", "D": "A silicone oil applied under a plain coverslip"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An aqueous mounting medium, used directly from water): Aqueous mounting media are used for stains that would be lost in alcohol, so the section is mounted directly from water. Resin media need clearing in xylene first, which would dissolve fat stains, and wax or oil are not standard routine choices."}, {"id": 1124, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A protocol lists 72 °C for the extension step of every cycle, while primer annealing is set to 55 °C and denaturation to 95 °C. A trainee asks why the extension temperature is higher than the annealing temperature. Which explanation is correct?", "options": {"A": "It is the working temperature of Taq polymerase, which adds nucleotides to primer 3' ends.", "B": "It activates the hot-start modification so the enzyme begins cycling efficiently.", "C": "It melts primer-dimers, ensuring that only specific products are extended.", "D": "It is the temperature at which primer-template hydrogen bonds form most strongly."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It is the working temperature of Taq polymerase, which adds nucleotides to primer 3' ends.): Taq polymerase works best near 72 °C, where it extends primers by adding nucleotides at their 3' ends. Primer binding is favoured at the lower annealing temperature, and hot-start activation occurs in the initial denaturation step."}, {"id": 1125, "domain": "cytology", "question": "An immunocytochemistry run on a pleural effusion cell block is recorded in the image. The antibody detects a nuclear marker that the laboratory runs weekly, and all three slides were stained together. What is the most appropriate action for the patient result from this run before it can be reported?", "options": {"A": "Report the patient as positive since the positive control confirms the reagents were working correctly", "B": "Report the patient as negative because the control sections disagree with their own expected results", "C": "Reject the run because the negative control stained, and repeat the stain with fresh reagents", "D": "Accept the patient result and record the control discrepancy in the run log for later review"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reject the run because the negative control stained, and repeat the stain with fresh reagents): Staining on the negative control means the reaction is nonspecific, for example from background binding or a reagent fault, so a positive on the patient slide cannot be trusted. The run is rejected and repeated. A working positive control does not rescue a failed negative control, and a discrepancy cannot just be logged and accepted.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "ICC run record, pleural fluid cell block", "columns": ["Slide", "Expected", "Observed"], "rows": [["Positive control", "POS", "POS"], ["Negative control", "Negative", "POS"], ["Patient cell block", "Unknown", "POS"]], "flag_col": 2, "caption": "Nuclear marker, same run"}, "image": "question_images/q_1125.png"}, {"id": 1126, "domain": "histology", "question": "A new technologist works the early shift in the frozen section area. The operating room calls to say a lymph node specimen is on its way for a frozen section. How should the specimen arrive, and what should the technologist do on receipt?", "options": {"A": "Frozen solid in dry ice, then thawed and only later checked for identifiers", "B": "Wrapped in dry gauze with no label so it does not leak into the container", "C": "Fixed in formalin for one hour first, then logged once the slides are ready", "D": "Fresh in a labelled container, unfixed, then logged with identifiers verified"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fresh in a labelled container, unfixed, then logged with identifiers verified): The specimen arrives fresh in a labelled container without fixative unless the pathologist agrees otherwise, and it is logged with identifiers verified. Formalin fixation, an unlabelled specimen, and delayed identity checks are all incorrect."}, {"id": 1127, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist is running PT and aPTT on a deeply jaundiced patient using a photo-optical analyzer. The clot curves look erratic and flagged, and the tube has no clots and is filled correctly. What is the best next step to obtain a reliable result?", "options": {"A": "Refrigerate the sample overnight to let the bilirubin settle, then rerun it on the optical analyzer", "B": "Repeat the testing on a mechanical clot detection method that is unaffected by icterus", "C": "Dilute the plasma 1 to 4 with saline and multiply the result by the dilution factor", "D": "Report the flagged optical result with a comment about the jaundice"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat the testing on a mechanical clot detection method that is unaffected by icterus): Icteric samples disturb optical readings, and a mechanical steel ball or magnetic sensor method can still detect clot formation. Diluting changes the factor concentration and cannot be corrected by multiplication. Refrigeration activates factor VII and platelets, and reporting a flagged result risks releasing an unreliable value."}, {"id": 1128, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A pregnant patient types as D negative on direct testing at her first prenatal visit. The laboratory policy states that pregnant patients and newborns receive extra Rh testing that ordinary recipients do not. What is the purpose of testing this patient for weak D?", "options": {"A": "To confirm that the patient can receive group O red cells safely", "B": "To decide whether Rh immune globulin is needed for a D-negative woman", "C": "To determine whether the patient has naturally occurring anti-D in her serum", "D": "To predict whether the baby will have a positive direct antiglobulin test"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (To decide whether Rh immune globulin is needed for a D-negative woman): Many services test pregnant patients and newborns for weak D because a D-negative woman of childbearing potential should receive Rh immune globulin when appropriate. The test detects antigen on red cells and does not look for antibodies. It does not predict the baby's antiglobulin result and is not related to group O selection."}, {"id": 1129, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A spectrophotometer displays an absorbance of 0.301 for a blank-corrected solution, and the technologist needs the equivalent percent transmittance to record on a maintenance log. Using the relationship between absorbance and transmittance, what percentage of the incident light is transmitted through the solution?", "options": {"A": "About 30%, because transmittance is the absorbance multiplied by 100", "B": "About 33%, because transmittance equals 1 divided by the absorbance value read on the display", "C": "About 50%, because absorbance of 0.301 equals 2 minus the log of 50", "D": "About 70%, because transmittance equals 1 minus the absorbance as a fraction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 50%, because absorbance of 0.301 equals 2 minus the log of 50): Absorbance equals 2 minus log of percent transmittance, so a value of 0.301 means the log is 1.699 and the transmittance is 50%. Multiplying absorbance by 100 or subtracting it from 1 treats the scale as linear. Taking the reciprocal likewise ignores the logarithmic relationship."}, {"id": 1130, "domain": "hematology", "question": "Blood from a patient with a suspected cutaneous T-cell lymphoma shows circulating lymphocytes with a cerebriform, convoluted nucleus, and the clinician's note mentions a skin biopsy showing epidermal infiltration. The technologist must select the correct cell name for the report comment. Which term describes this cell?", "options": {"A": "Hairy cell, which is a lymphocyte with fine cytoplasmic projections and TRAP positivity", "B": "Flame cell, which is a plasma cell with reddish cytoplasm at the periphery", "C": "Sezary cell, which is a lymphocyte with a cerebriform convoluted nucleus", "D": "Buttock cell, which is a small lymphocyte with a cleaved nucleus in follicular lymphoma"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sezary cell, which is a lymphocyte with a cerebriform convoluted nucleus): Sezary cells have a cerebriform convoluted nucleus and occur in cutaneous T-cell lymphoma. Flame cells are plasma cells, buttock cells are follicular lymphoma cells with notched nuclei, and hairy cells have cytoplasmic projections."}, {"id": 1131, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "Interphase FISH is performed on peripheral blood from a patient with chronic lymphocytic leukemia using a red chromosome 12 centromere probe and a green chromosome 8 centromere probe. The nuclei shown in the image are scored. Which finding does the image show?", "options": {"A": "Normal pattern with two red and two green signals in nuclei", "B": "Trisomy 12 shown by three red and two green signals", "C": "Monosomy 12 shown by one red and two green signals per nucleus", "D": "Trisomy 8 shown by three green and two red signals per nucleus"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Trisomy 12 shown by three red and two green signals): each nucleus shows three red chromosome 12 signals and two green chromosome 8 signals, so chromosome 12 is gained. A normal result would show two red signals, monosomy would show one, and trisomy 8 would require three green signals.", "image_type": "fish_nuclei", "image_params": {"nuclei": [{"red": 3, "green": 2, "fusion": 0}, {"red": 3, "green": 2, "fusion": 0}, {"red": 3, "green": 2, "fusion": 0}], "legend": {"red": "CEP12 probe", "green": "CEP8 probe"}, "caption": "Interphase FISH, CLL panel"}, "image": "question_images/q_1131.png"}, {"id": 1132, "domain": "cytology", "question": "An effusion contains a mixed population of small mature lymphocytes of varying size, with a few macrophages, and the clinician worries about lymphoma. A colleague notes that reactive lymphocytes differ from lymphoma in a specific way. Which feature best favours a reactive process over lymphoma?", "options": {"A": "A clonal population confirmed on flow cytometry with restricted surface markers", "B": "A single monotonous population of discohesive cells with scattered mitoses and apoptotic bodies", "C": "A single population of large cells with prominent nucleoli and basophilic cytoplasm", "D": "A polymorphous mixture of small mature lymphocytes instead of one monotonous cell population"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A polymorphous mixture of small mature lymphocytes instead of one monotonous cell population): Reactive lymphocytes form a mixed, mature population, whereas lymphoma is monotonous. Monotony with mitoses and apoptosis, large cells with nucleoli, and clonality on flow all point toward lymphoma, so those descriptions would argue against a reactive process."}, {"id": 1133, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "During aCGH, the technologist labels patient DNA green and adds normal reference DNA labelled red to the same array. A trainee asks why the reference is needed when the patient's DNA already contains every sequence being tested. What is the role of the reference?", "options": {"A": "It gives a two-copy baseline, so the colour ratio at each probe shows dosage", "B": "It supplies extra probe DNA, so hybridization to the array is completed faster", "C": "It acts as the counterstain, so nuclei on the array can be located", "D": "It neutralizes the formamide, so the array can be washed at lower temperature"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It gives a two-copy baseline, so the colour ratio at each probe shows dosage): The reference has a normal two copies everywhere, so comparing the two colours at each probe reveals whether the patient has relatively more or less DNA. It does not speed hybridization, neutralize formamide, or counterstain, since arrays hold no nuclei to locate."}, {"id": 1134, "domain": "histology", "question": "Slides from a run show weak, patchy staining in both patient and control sections. The slides were dried at 60 degrees for one hour and stained with the same polymer kit as usual. The technologist finds wax residue on the sections after staining. Which cause best explains this pattern?", "options": {"A": "The DAB was too fresh and too well filtered for good colour", "B": "The hematoxylin counterstain ran too long and darkened the nuclei enough to mask the brown chromogen", "C": "Incomplete deparaffinization left wax that prevented reagents from reaching the tissue evenly", "D": "The primary antibody was too concentrated for the tissue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Incomplete deparaffinization left wax that prevented reagents from reaching the tissue evenly): Wax left on the section blocks reagent contact, producing weak, uneven staining. Too-concentrated antibody would overstain, and fresh filtered DAB is good practice."}, {"id": 1135, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist describes what a random rescreen of a portion of slides reported as negative is designed to measure within a gynecologic cytology quality assurance program. The trainee must select the purpose of this regulated practice from the choices given.", "options": {"A": "Measuring how quickly reports reach the physician after screening", "B": "Estimating the laboratory's false-negative rate by rechecking negative slides", "C": "Counting how many slides each screener completes during a shift", "D": "Confirming that every slide carries two correct patient identifiers matching the requisition"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Estimating the laboratory's false-negative rate by rechecking negative slides): Random rescreening of negative slides estimates how often abnormalities are missed, meaning the false-negative rate. Slide counts are workload monitoring, and identifier checks belong to labelling procedures. Turnaround time is a separate indicator."}, {"id": 1136, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "During a massive transfusion the patient's core temperature falls to 34 degrees Celsius, and acidosis and bleeding are worsening. Products are being infused rapidly from the cooler. What is the appropriate action by the team and laboratory? The cooler temperature log shows units leaving storage at 4 degrees Celsius.", "options": {"A": "Slow the infusion to avoid warming, since heat damages stored red cells.", "B": "Add calcium to the bags, since low temperature lowers ionized calcium.", "C": "Stop plasma and give only red cells, since cold plasma causes acidosis.", "D": "Use a blood warmer, since cold, acidosis and coagulopathy worsen each other."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use a blood warmer, since cold, acidosis and coagulopathy worsen each other.): Cold blood lowers body temperature, and hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy reinforce each other, so a blood warmer is used. Calcium should not be added into the bags, and stopping plasma worsens dilutional coagulopathy."}, {"id": 1137, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A physician suspects Cushing syndrome and asks which laboratory tests can be used to investigate excess cortisol. Which set of tests is described as appropriate? The endocrinologist asks the laboratory which testing options are appropriate for the first-line investigation of this condition.", "options": {"A": "Early-morning serum sodium, random urine glucose, or a fasting lipid panel done at the clinic", "B": "Serum TSH, free T4, or an overnight fasting blood glucose challenge test in the ward", "C": "Midday serum potassium, urine ketones, or a random tumour marker profile from the patient", "D": "Late-night salivary cortisol, 24-hour urine cortisol, or dexamethasone suppression"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Late-night salivary cortisol, 24-hour urine cortisol, or dexamethasone suppression): Cushing syndrome is investigated with a late-night salivary cortisol, 24-hour urinary free cortisol, or an overnight dexamethasone suppression test in which cortisol should fall after the drug. Adrenal insufficiency is assessed with a morning cortisol and a stimulation test."}, {"id": 1138, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A supervisor reviews the laboratory's list of results that need special communication. A technologist finds unexpected malignant cells on a routine screening slide late in the day. Which action best meets professional and quality expectations? She is alone in the laboratory after hours.", "options": {"A": "Notify the pathologist immediately, then document the phone call with time, person and content", "B": "Hold the slide until the weekly review meeting and discuss it there with the other technologists", "C": "Wait until the next morning and mail the report with routine results", "D": "Tell the patient directly by phone before informing any pathologist"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Notify the pathologist immediately, then document the phone call with time, person and content): Unexpected malignancy is reported to the pathologist immediately and communicated to the clinician by a documented call recording time, person notified and content. Waiting or mailing delays treatment. Contacting the patient first bypasses the pathologist and the responsible clinician."}, {"id": 1139, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "An RT-PCR uses primers that span an intron, giving a 200 bp product from cDNA and a 650 bp product from genomic DNA. The gel shown includes patient RNA processed with and without reverse transcriptase. What does the pattern of bands indicate?", "options": {"A": "Reverse transcriptase failed, so the 200 bp product arose from contaminating cDNA", "B": "The primers bound a second transcript, producing a larger spliced form of the target gene", "C": "Genomic DNA carried over into the RNA, so the RNA needs DNase treatment", "D": "Product carryover from earlier runs entered only the reaction lacking reverse transcriptase"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Genomic DNA carried over into the RNA, so the RNA needs DNase treatment): A 650 bp band in the reaction without reverse transcriptase can only come from genomic DNA in the RNA preparation, which is removed by DNase treatment. A failed enzyme would remove the 200 bp band, the size difference matches the intron rather than a second transcript, and carryover of earlier products would give 200 bp, not 650 bp.", "image_type": "dna_gel", "image_params": {"lanes": [{"label": "Ladder", "bands": [100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800]}, {"label": "cDNA control", "bands": [200]}, {"label": "RNA with RT", "bands": [200, 650]}, {"label": "RNA no RT", "bands": [650]}], "ladder_labels": [100, 200, 400, 600, 800], "caption": "Intron-spanning RT-PCR"}, "image": "question_images/q_1139.png"}, {"id": 1140, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A 45-year-old patient with marked leukocytosis, basophilia, an enlarged spleen and a left-shifted differential has interphase FISH done on peripheral blood using BCR and ABL1 probes. The laboratory scores two representative nuclei, shown in the image, with the probe legend printed beside them. What do these nuclei demonstrate about the BCR and ABL1 loci?", "options": {"A": "Loss of one ABL1 signal, consistent with a deletion of chromosome 9 material", "B": "A t(9;22) BCR::ABL1 fusion, supporting a diagnosis of chronic myeloid leukemia", "C": "Extra copies of the BCR locus, with three red signals in each nucleus", "D": "A normal two-signal pattern, with no rearrangement of either probe locus"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A t(9;22) BCR::ABL1 fusion, supporting a diagnosis of chronic myeloid leukemia): Each nucleus shows yellow fusion signals where the BCR and ABL1 probes sit together, with fewer separate red and green signals than the normal two of each, which is the pattern of the t(9;22) BCR::ABL1 rearrangement of chronic myeloid leukemia. A normal nucleus has no fusion signals, and the image shows neither a deletion nor extra BCR copies.", "image_type": "fish_nuclei", "image_params": {"nuclei": [{"red": 1, "green": 1, "fusion": 2}, {"red": 1, "green": 1, "fusion": 2}], "legend": {"red": "BCR probe", "green": "ABL1 probe", "fusion": "Fusion signal"}, "caption": "Interphase FISH, peripheral blood"}, "image": "question_images/q_1140.png"}, {"id": 1141, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A calibration curve for a photometric enzyme method bends and flattens at the highest calibrator. The technologist is asked to name a cause of lost linearity at high concentration, since patient results in that region cannot be trusted. Which of the following is a recognized cause of this deviation from linearity?", "options": {"A": "Stronger antibody binding as antigen concentration rises in the reaction", "B": "Excess absorptivity caused by using a shorter cuvette path length", "C": "Substrate depletion or stray light at high analyte concentration", "D": "Contamination of the reference electrode by hydrogen ions from the cleaning solution"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Substrate depletion or stray light at high analyte concentration): Above the linear limit the curve bends because of stray light, chemical effects, or the reagent substrate running out. A shorter path length lowers absorbance and would not cause a plateau. Reference electrode contamination affects ISE methods, and antibody binding belongs to immunoassays."}, {"id": 1142, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A validation run for a quantitative assay uses five ten-fold dilutions of a standard, and the software reports a standard curve slope of -3.60. The supervisor asks what this says about amplification efficiency. Using efficiency equals 10 to the power of (-1 divided by slope) minus 1, which conclusion is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Efficiency is about 90 percent, below ideal, so each cycle falls short of a full doubling.", "B": "Efficiency is about 60 percent, which means the assay fails outright by any accepted standard.", "C": "Efficiency is about 110 percent, which shows enhanced doubling and is desirable.", "D": "Efficiency is about 100 percent, so the curve is ideal and needs no review."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Efficiency is about 90 percent, below ideal, so each cycle falls short of a full doubling.): With a slope of -3.60, 1 divided by 3.60 is 0.278, and 10 to the power 0.278 is about 1.90, so efficiency is about 0.90, or 90 percent. Ideal efficiency needs a slope near -3.32. Values above 100 percent come from slopes shallower than -3.32 and signal a problem rather than a benefit. Sixty percent would need a much steeper slope."}, {"id": 1143, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A patient with weeks of bloating and greasy stools has had one negative stool ova and parasite examination, but symptoms continue. The physician asks how to increase the chance of detecting Giardia cysts, which are shed in unpredictable amounts from day to day. What collection schedule is recommended?", "options": {"A": "Collect a single specimen after antibiotics, since treatment increases parasite shedding", "B": "Collect one large specimen each week for a month and pool them together", "C": "Collect three specimens on separate days, since parasites are shed intermittently", "D": "Collect three specimens within one hour, so all are from the same bowel movement"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Collect three specimens on separate days, since parasites are shed intermittently): Shedding varies from day to day, so three specimens on separate days are recommended. Specimens collected within an hour are effectively the same, and pooling weekly stool or timing after antibiotics is not standard."}, {"id": 1144, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory designs a 500 kb panel for hereditary cardiomyopathy and needs even coverage across all targets, including regions where the sequence varies and primers may fail. Which enrichment approach fits this need, and what is its trade-off compared with amplicon methods?", "options": {"A": "Hybrid capture, giving more even coverage of large regions but needing more input", "B": "Hybrid capture, which gives more even coverage but cannot cover regions larger than a single gene", "C": "Amplicon-based enrichment, which gives more even coverage of large regions and needs less input", "D": "Amplicon-based enrichment, which is unaffected by variants under primers and finishes faster"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hybrid capture, giving more even coverage of large regions but needing more input): Hybrid capture usually gives more even coverage and handles larger regions, at the cost of more input and a longer workflow. Amplicon methods are quicker and use less input but can miss variants under a primer. Hybrid capture has no single-gene size limit."}, {"id": 1145, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient has a low free T4 and a TSH within the reference interval that is inappropriately normal for the low hormone. No thyroid disease is known, but the patient has a pituitary tumour. Which condition is most likely? Choose the likely condition.", "options": {"A": "Primary hypothyroidism, because a failing thyroid gland raises TSH above the reference interval", "B": "Graves disease, because a pituitary tumour raises free T4 and suppresses TSH", "C": "Central hypothyroidism, since the pituitary fails to raise TSH despite a low free T4", "D": "Subclinical hypothyroidism, because free T4 must be normal when TSH is normal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Central hypothyroidism, since the pituitary fails to raise TSH despite a low free T4): Central hypothyroidism arises from pituitary or hypothalamic disease and shows a low free T4 with a low or inappropriately normal TSH. Primary disease would show high TSH. Graves disease has a suppressed TSH with high free T4, and subclinical disease has a normal free T4."}, {"id": 1146, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A physician calls the laboratory asking why a meningitis isolate was tested by an MIC method instead of only disk diffusion. The technologist wants to give the clinical reason. Which statement best explains why an actual MIC value matters in some infections?", "options": {"A": "Drug penetration is poor at some sites, and dosing depends on distance from the breakpoint", "B": "An MIC removes the need to apply any breakpoint or interpretive category to the result", "C": "MIC values are cheaper to produce than zone diameters for the organisms tested", "D": "Disk diffusion cannot be performed on any cerebrospinal fluid isolate because of the agar composition"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Drug penetration is poor at some sites, and dosing depends on distance from the breakpoint): A numerical MIC matters at sites such as meningitis or endocarditis where drug penetration is poor, and where dosing depends on the distance from the breakpoint. MIC still needs a breakpoint for interpretation, and it is not simply cheaper."}, {"id": 1147, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In a laboratory's quality program, a trainee asks what the pathologist's routine quality assurance review of screened slides covers. She knows the pathologist checks every abnormal result, but she wonders whether negative slides are also part of the review, and if so which ones.", "options": {"A": "The abnormal findings alone, with none of the negative slides rescreened", "B": "Slides for which the clinic requests a second opinion in writing", "C": "All abnormal findings plus a selected set of slides the cytotechnologist called negative", "D": "A random sample of abnormal findings, with every negative slide accepted as read"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (All abnormal findings plus a selected set of slides the cytotechnologist called negative): The pathologist reviews all abnormal findings and a set of negative slides, which is how missed abnormalities are found. Reviewing every slide is not the standard. Ignoring the negatives or reviewing only on request would leave errors undetected."}, {"id": 1148, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A bone marrow specimen from a patient with suspected leukemia yields very few metaphases across the direct, 24-hour and 48-hour cultures, though the reagents and incubator records are all in order and other cases processed the same day grew well. What is the most reasonable interpretation?", "options": {"A": "A biologic cause is possible, such as a hypocellular marrow or few dividing cells", "B": "A reagent fault is certain, since every failed marrow culture is caused by the medium", "C": "A colcemid dilution error is the cause, since marrow yields many metaphases when handled correctly", "D": "A labelling error is certain, since low metaphase counts occur only with mixed-up tubes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A biologic cause is possible, such as a hypocellular marrow or few dividing cells): When technical records are in order and other cases grew well, a hypocellular marrow or non-dividing cells is a genuine biologic explanation. Certainty about reagent, colcemid or labelling faults is not supported by the evidence."}, {"id": 1149, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A laboratory reviews how often quality control must be run for susceptibility testing. A new supervisor asks under which condition testing may move from a daily schedule to a weekly one. What is the condition according to the standard described in this chapter?", "options": {"A": "Approval from the ordering physician, who must sign a waiver for each testing run", "B": "Seven consecutive days of acceptable QC results for a single drug and strain", "C": "Thirty consecutive days of acceptable QC results, as defined in the standard", "D": "Any month in which no patient results were reported as resistant to a drug"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Thirty consecutive days of acceptable QC results, as defined in the standard): QC is performed each testing day, or weekly if the laboratory has demonstrated 30 consecutive days of acceptable results per the standard. Seven days is too short, and physician approval or the resistant rate has no bearing on QC frequency."}, {"id": 1150, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A FISH run on a blood smear shows crisp blue DAPI nuclei, but neither the test probe nor the control probe on the same slide gives any signal in any cell. The wash buffer, filters, and lamp were all checked and are fine. Which cause fits best?", "options": {"A": "A counterstain mixed too concentrated, which would wash out the hybridized signal from both probes", "B": "A cutoff value that was set too low, which would hide every hybridized signal", "C": "A failed denaturation or degraded probe, possibly from wrong temperature or light exposure", "D": "A wash that was too cool, which would strip the signal from every nucleus"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A failed denaturation or degraded probe, possibly from wrong temperature or light exposure): Loss of both probes with normal DAPI points to a problem before or during hybridization, such as inadequate denaturation or damaged probe. A cool wash would leave background, not remove all signal. Counterstain concentration and cutoff values do not change whether a signal is physically present."}, {"id": 1151, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A stool specimen from a hiker with foul-smelling, greasy diarrhea shows pear-shaped trophozoites with two nuclei that give a face-like appearance, and a few oval cysts with visible axonemes. Which organism does this describe, and which rapid method can also support the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Trichuris trichiura, and a cellophane tape preparation from the perianal skin can support the diagnosis", "B": "Cyclospora cayetanensis, and a modified acid-fast stain of the oocysts can support the diagnosis", "C": "Giardia lamblia, and a rapid immunoassay for Giardia antigen can support the diagnosis", "D": "Entamoeba coli, and a direct fluorescent antibody stain for Cryptosporidium can support the diagnosis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Giardia lamblia, and a rapid immunoassay for Giardia antigen can support the diagnosis): Pear-shaped, two-nucleus trophozoites are typical of Giardia, and antigen immunoassays are common. Entamoeba coli lacks this shape, Cyclospora is an oocyst, and Trichuris is a helminth with barrel-shaped eggs."}, {"id": 1152, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A laboratory opens a new lot of level 2 chemistry control material. The package insert gives an expected mean and range, and the supervisor asks how the laboratory should establish the mean and standard deviation to be used on its Levey-Jennings chart. What is the correct approach?", "options": {"A": "Adopt the manufacturer's range exactly as printed and plot it without any changes", "B": "Run the control at least 20 times over several days and calculate its own", "C": "Run the control three times on the first day and average those results", "D": "Use the mean and standard deviation from the previous lot of control"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Run the control at least 20 times over several days and calculate its own): Each laboratory sets its own mean and SD for each control lot, using at least 20 runs spread over several days to capture normal variation. The insert range is only a starting guide. Three runs on one day miss day-to-day variation, and a previous lot may have a different mean."}, {"id": 1153, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A batch of nasopharyngeal specimens is run for a respiratory virus. The positive control amplifies well and the no-template control is clean, but one patient specimen shows no target signal. Which control tells the technologist whether that particular specimen was extracted and amplified successfully?", "options": {"A": "The internal control added to each specimen before extraction.", "B": "The standard curve, because it defines the range of detectable concentrations.", "C": "The positive control, because it shows the reagents and cycling were satisfactory.", "D": "The no-template control, because it shares every reagent with the patient reaction."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The internal control added to each specimen before extraction.): Only a sequence carried through extraction and amplification inside each specimen can show that a given specimen behaved properly. The positive control proves reagents and cycling for the run, not the individual extract. The no-template control checks contamination. A standard curve relates Ct to quantity and says nothing about one specimen's extraction."}, {"id": 1154, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A trainee reads that light bands on a G-banded chromosome differ from dark bands in base composition and gene content, and wants to understand what the banding pattern actually reflects about the DNA. Which statement correctly describes the light bands?", "options": {"A": "They contain no DNA and are made only of protein left after trypsin", "B": "They are rich in AT and hold fewer genes that are actively expressed", "C": "They are identical to dark bands and differ only in stain concentration", "D": "They are rich in GC and hold more of the genes that are actively expressed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (They are rich in GC and hold more of the genes that are actively expressed): Light bands are GC-rich and gene-rich, while dark bands are AT-rich and gene-poor. The other statements reverse this or deny that the bands reflect real chromatin differences."}, {"id": 1155, "domain": "hematology", "question": "The thrombin time measures how quickly added thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin in patient plasma, bypassing the earlier intrinsic and extrinsic steps. A technologist reviews which conditions can prolong this test when results come back abnormal and the sample was collected correctly. Which of the following can prolong a thrombin time?", "options": {"A": "Vitamin K deficiency, low factor IX, and elevated protein C in the sample", "B": "Lupus anticoagulant, low factor VIII, and elevated hematocrit in the sample", "C": "Heparin, low or abnormal fibrinogen, and fibrin degradation products", "D": "Warfarin therapy, low factor VII, and elevated platelet counts in the sample"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Heparin, low or abnormal fibrinogen, and fibrin degradation products): The TT is prolonged by heparin, low or abnormal fibrinogen and fibrin degradation products because it tests the final step of clotting directly. The other lists describe factors affecting earlier steps, which do not influence the thrombin time."}, {"id": 1156, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A total calcium is low, but the ionized calcium on the same patient is normal, and the serum albumin is 24 g/L. What is the best explanation of these findings? The technologist must explain the difference between the two results to the requesting nurse.", "options": {"A": "Low albumin has no relationship with calcium, so one result must be mislabelled", "B": "Low albumin reduces bound calcium, so total falls while ionized calcium stays normal", "C": "Low albumin blocks calcium absorption, so ionized calcium should also be reduced", "D": "Low albumin raises ionized calcium, so the total calcium result must be an analytical error"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Low albumin reduces bound calcium, so total falls while ionized calcium stays normal): About half of total calcium is bound, mostly to albumin. When albumin is low, total calcium falls even if free ionized calcium is normal, which is the physiologically active portion. Ionized calcium is the better test when protein status is abnormal."}, {"id": 1157, "domain": "hematology", "question": "Platelet function testing by light transmission aggregometry requires careful handling of the patient sample before and during testing. The technologist is asked to describe the correct conditions for keeping the specimen. How should a sample for platelet function testing be held before testing?", "options": {"A": "Frozen at minus 70 degrees, to preserve platelet activity", "B": "Fresh, kept at room temperature, and never chilled before testing", "C": "Chilled on ice immediately, to preserve platelet activity until testing", "D": "Warmed at 37 degrees overnight, to preserve platelet activity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fresh, kept at room temperature, and never chilled before testing): Platelet function needs a fresh sample kept at room temperature because chilling activates platelets. Freezing destroys platelets and prolonged warming allows deterioration, so neither preserves function."}, {"id": 1158, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A laboratory is asked whether a chromosomal microarray can still be performed when a blood culture has failed and no dividing cells are available. The requisition lists blood, saliva, and a tissue biopsy as possible specimens. What does the test require from the specimen?", "options": {"A": "Extracted DNA from blood, saliva, tissue, or prenatal samples, with no culture needed", "B": "Only metaphase spreads prepared from cultured blood lymphocytes after mitogen stimulation", "C": "Only fixed interphase nuclei on a slide, with the DNA left in place", "D": "Only intact paraffin sections, because extraction destroys the copy number information"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Extracted DNA from blood, saliva, tissue, or prenatal samples, with no culture needed): Arrays work from purified DNA, so any specimen that yields adequate, good-quality DNA can be used, and dividing cells are not required. Metaphase spreads and fixed nuclei on slides are needed for karyotype and FISH, and extraction does not destroy copy number information."}, {"id": 1159, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A laboratory uses several staining methods on the same culture to answer different questions. One method produces a banding pattern that is the reverse of the routine G-banded appearance, so bands that are dark on the usual preparation appear pale. Which method is being used?", "options": {"A": "R-banding, which gives dark and light bands in the reverse of G-banding.", "B": "C-banding, which highlights the centromere region and constitutive heterochromatin instead.", "C": "NOR staining, which highlights the nucleolar organizer regions on acrocentric short arms.", "D": "Q-banding, which requires a light microscope with no fluorescence to view the bands."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (R-banding, which gives dark and light bands in the reverse of G-banding.): R-banding produces the reverse of the G-band pattern. C-banding stains centromeric heterochromatin, and NOR staining shows the nucleolar organizer regions of acrocentric short arms, so neither produces a reversed band pattern. Q-banding depends on fluorescence, so the last statement is also false."}, {"id": 1160, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A hospital plans to send blood components to a ward by pneumatic tube system to save time. The quality manager asks the blood bank what must be done before components are sent this way. What does the chapter say about the use of these systems?", "options": {"A": "They may be used only for platelets because platelets are stored cold", "B": "They must be validated for each component to prevent hemolysis", "C": "They can be used without validation because all components tolerate impact", "D": "They must be operated at a speed that freezes the unit during transit"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (They must be validated for each component to prevent hemolysis): Pneumatic tubes must be validated for each component, because speed and impact can cause hemolysis. Skipping validation is unsafe. Platelets must not be chilled, and freezing units in transit would harm them, so neither of those statements describes proper use."}, {"id": 1161, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A fresh urine sediment from an adult at a routine checkup, who has no symptoms and a normal blood pressure, is viewed under high power, and the field is shown. How should the structures shown in the image be interpreted by the technologist?", "options": {"A": "Granular casts, which indicate tubular necrosis and need urgent nephrology referral", "B": "Hyaline casts, which appear in healthy people with exercise or concentrated urine", "C": "Red cell casts, which indicate glomerular bleeding and call for reflex testing", "D": "Fungal hyphae, which indicate a yeast infection and call for culture of the specimen"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hyaline casts, which appear in healthy people with exercise or concentrated urine): The pale, cell-free cylinders with smooth outlines are hyaline casts, which are made of protein and are often normal in small numbers. Granular casts contain granules, red cell casts contain visible red cells, and hyphae would be thin branching filaments.", "image_type": "micrograph", "image_params": {"prompt": "Realistic brightfield photomicrograph of fresh unstained urine sediment under high power (400x), a few long pale, nearly transparent cylindrical hyaline casts with smooth parallel edges and rounded ends and low refractility, no cells inside them, on a clean pale background.", "caption": "Urine sediment, high power", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1161.png"}, {"id": 1162, "domain": "cytology", "question": "At a shift change, A technologist is about to leave at the end of a shift with several cases still in progress, including one with a pending immunostain. The next technologist arrives late. What professional behaviour best supports patient safety at this point?", "options": {"A": "Discard the unfinished cases so that nothing is left over for the next shift", "B": "Leave brief notes only on the cases that seem urgent to the departing person", "C": "Give an accurate handover of the pending cases, and ask if anything is unclear", "D": "Leave the cases on the bench and let the next technologist find them"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give an accurate handover of the pending cases, and ask if anything is unclear): Professionalism includes teamwork, clear communication and accurate handover between shifts, with willingness to ask when unsure. Partial notes, assumptions or discarding work leave gaps that could cause delayed results, lost specimens or errors affecting patients. A good handover keeps pending stains and results from being lost between shifts."}, {"id": 1163, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A positive blood culture bottle from an older adult with endocarditis is Gram stained, as shown in the image. Subculture colonies are catalase negative and blacken bile esculin agar. Which additional result best separates Enterococcus from other bile esculin positive organisms such as the Streptococcus bovis group?", "options": {"A": "Positive hippurate hydrolysis after four hours at 35 degrees Celsius", "B": "Positive CAMP reaction next to a Staphylococcus aureus streak", "C": "Growth in broth containing 6.5 percent sodium chloride", "D": "Susceptibility to a 0.04 unit bacitracin disk placed on blood agar"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Growth in broth containing 6.5 percent sodium chloride): The image shows gram-positive cocci in chains, and enterococci grow in broth with 6.5 percent sodium chloride whereas the Streptococcus bovis group does not. Hippurate hydrolysis and the CAMP reaction identify group B streptococci, and bacitracin susceptibility is used to screen for group A.", "image_type": "micrograph", "image_params": {"prompt": "Realistic Gram stain photomicrograph, 1000x oil immersion, pink-red background, purple Gram-positive cocci in short chains and pairs, background pale pink, from a blood culture.", "caption": "Gram stain of a positive blood culture bottle", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1163.png"}, {"id": 1164, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A trainee is asked how the E7 protein of high-risk human papillomavirus contributes to cancer development, in contrast with E6. The trainee must name the host protein that E7 binds and describe what happens to the infected cell once that protein is bound.", "options": {"A": "It binds p53 and marks it for breakdown so that damaged cells survive and keep dividing", "B": "It binds the basement membrane and lets cells invade the stroma", "C": "It binds the retinoblastoma protein, releasing the cell to divide without restraint", "D": "It binds the viral capsid and halts assembly of new virus particles"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It binds the retinoblastoma protein, releasing the cell to divide without restraint): E7 binds the retinoblastoma protein (Rb), which frees the cell to divide without its normal restraint. Destruction of p53 is the role of E6. Viral capsid assembly and basement membrane invasion are not the mechanisms by which E7 promotes cancer."}, {"id": 1165, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A supervisor reviews the reading schedule for routine bacterial plates on the day shift after a delayed culture report was traced to plates being read too late. The laboratory wants its routine plates read at the standard times. When are routine plates usually read?", "options": {"A": "Around 18 to 24 hours, and again at 48 hours to catch slower growers", "B": "Every ten minutes during the first hour of incubation", "C": "Only after seven full days of incubation at room temperature", "D": "Only when the ordering physician telephones to request a result"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Around 18 to 24 hours, and again at 48 hours to catch slower growers): Routine plates are read at about 18 to 24 hours and again at 48 hours so slower organisms are not missed. Seven days is excessive for routine cultures, ten-minute checks are impractical, and waiting for a call risks delayed reporting."}, {"id": 1166, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A physician asks how many days of sputum collection the laboratory prefers to improve the yield of cells from the lower airway for cytology. The clinic wants a plan that patients can follow easily at home before delivering the containers to the laboratory. Which collection schedule and specimen type does the laboratory recommend?", "options": {"A": "One day only, using a specimen taken late in the evening", "B": "Two days apart, using a specimen produced after a large meal", "C": "Three consecutive days, using an early morning specimen from a deep cough", "D": "Seven consecutive days, using a specimen pooled into one large container"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Three consecutive days, using an early morning specimen from a deep cough): Early morning deep-cough specimens on three consecutive days raise the chance of capturing shed cells. Pooling seven days in a container would cause degeneration, and a single evening specimen is less representative."}, {"id": 1167, "domain": "histology", "question": "An immunohistochemistry technologist reports that some antigens stain weakly on tissue left in formalin for many days, while a sister block fixed for one day stains well. She explains that this is a known effect of long fixation. What is the underlying cause of the weakened staining?", "options": {"A": "Decalcification from over-fixation removes the calcium that the antibodies need for binding.", "B": "Excess cross-linking from over-fixation masks certain antigens and can make the tissue brittle.", "C": "Insufficient cross-linking from over-fixation leaves the antigens free to wash away in processing.", "D": "Formic acid generated by over-fixation dissolves the antigens out of the tissue during storage."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Excess cross-linking from over-fixation masks certain antigens and can make the tissue brittle.): Prolonged formalin exposure keeps adding methylene bridges between proteins, which hides epitopes from the antibody and stiffens the tissue. Heat or enzyme antigen retrieval can partly reverse this. Over-fixation increases cross-links rather than reducing them, buffered formalin does not make acid, and calcium removal has no role in antibody binding."}, {"id": 1168, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A 63-year-old's slide has sheets of small cells with high N:C ratios, smooth evenly stained nuclei, and a background of debris and inflammation, with no superficial cells present. A trainee sees the crowding and wants to call high-grade dysplasia. What should be considered first, before any category is chosen?", "options": {"A": "Small cell carcinoma, supported by sheets of small cells and background debris", "B": "High-grade squamous lesion, confirmed by the small size of the cells and their crowding", "C": "Reparative change, because the sheets of cells show fine chromatin and clear halos", "D": "A benign atrophic mimic, correlated with the patient's age and hormonal history"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A benign atrophic mimic, correlated with the patient's age and hormonal history): Atrophic parabasal cells look like high-grade cells because of their high N:C ratio, but their nuclei are smooth and evenly stained, with a debris background. In a postmenopausal woman atrophy is the first consideration. Smooth even chromatin argues against a lesion."}, {"id": 1169, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A technologist is loading a centrifuge with four serum tubes for a routine run. Two tubes are full and two contain much less serum. The centrifuge is only partly loaded. What must be done before starting the rotor, and what must never be done while it is still spinning?", "options": {"A": "Use any two tubes across the rotor, and open the lid slowly during deceleration", "B": "Balance opposite tubes with similar mass, and keep the lid closed until the rotor stops", "C": "Load tubes uncapped to let pressure escape, and open the lid to check speed", "D": "Place heavy tubes side by side to save space, and brake hard to save time"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Balance opposite tubes with similar mass, and keep the lid closed until the rotor stops): Opposing tubes need similar mass to keep the rotor balanced, tubes must be capped to prevent aerosols, evaporation and pH change, and the lid stays closed until the rotor has stopped. Uncapped tubes create aerosols and abrupt braking can resuspend cells. Unbalanced loads and lid opening while spinning are hazards to the operator and the instrument."}, {"id": 1170, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A 34-year-old woman had a baby 2 months ago. A pretransfusion sample was collected on Monday for a planned transfusion on Friday of the same week, and hospital policy requires a sample no older than 3 days for such patients, with the collection day counted as day 0. What should the technologist do on Friday?", "options": {"A": "Use the Monday sample after repeating the antibody screen on it once", "B": "Use the Monday sample, since it is only four days old on Friday", "C": "Use the Monday sample, since a recent pregnancy does not change antibody screening validity", "D": "Request a new sample, since this one expired at the end of Thursday"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Request a new sample, since this one expired at the end of Thursday): Monday is day 0, so Thursday is day 3 and the sample expires at the end of that day. A recent pregnancy allows new antibodies to form, which is why the strict window applies. Four days is beyond the limit, and repeating the screen on an expired sample does not extend its validity."}, {"id": 1171, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A blood gas result is being released to the ward. Which additional information should be recorded with the result to allow correct interpretation by the clinician? The nurse asks what else the laboratory needs on the report for the physician to interpret the result.", "options": {"A": "The patient's height and the exact weight of the syringe used for the collection", "B": "The name of the phlebotomist and the brand of heparin used in the syringe today", "C": "The patient temperature and the inspired oxygen fraction when collected", "D": "The room humidity and the date when the analyzer was last cleaned and serviced"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The patient temperature and the inspired oxygen fraction when collected): Patient temperature and the fraction of inspired oxygen affect the interpretation of pO2 and other values and should be documented with the result. The other details are not needed for clinical interpretation of the blood gas."}, {"id": 1172, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A multiplex respiratory PCR panel on a nasopharyngeal swab from a hospitalized child reports influenza A with a cycle threshold of 24, and internal controls passed. Influenza is reportable in the province. Which action best fits good laboratory practice for this result?", "options": {"A": "Hold the result until a cell culture confirms live virus at two weeks", "B": "Report the result to the clinician and forward it to public health as required", "C": "Report only to the patient's family, since panel results are private", "D": "Delete the result after one day, since respiratory panels are not retained"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the result to the clinician and forward it to public health as required): Results for reportable viruses go to the clinician and to public health. Waiting for culture delays action, and reporting to the family or deleting results breaches confidentiality and regulations."}, {"id": 1173, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A parotid aspirate from a painful, swollen gland in a 50-year-old with a fever shows acinar cells, ductal cells and many neutrophils and lymphocytes, with no atypical cells and no tumour matrix. Under the Milan System, which category best describes this smear?", "options": {"A": "Neoplasm of uncertain malignant potential, requiring surgical excision for classification.", "B": "Non-neoplastic, consistent with an inflammatory condition such as sialadenitis.", "C": "Suspicious for malignancy, requiring immediate repeat aspiration of the gland.", "D": "Atypia of undetermined significance, requiring a core biopsy for reassurance."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Non-neoplastic, consistent with an inflammatory condition such as sialadenitis.): Acinar and ductal cells with inflammation and no atypia or tumour matrix belong in the non-neoplastic category, as in sialadenitis. The other categories require atypical or neoplastic cells, which are not described. Repeat aspiration and excision would be excessive here."}, {"id": 1174, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A bronchoalveolar lavage shows branching, septate hyphae with dichotomous branching at about 45 degrees in a patient with a weak immune system. The clinician wants the organism identified so treatment can start quickly. Which organism does this morphology describe, and what feature identifies it on the slide?", "options": {"A": "Candida, which shows yeast forms and pseudohyphae in the mouth", "B": "Aspergillus, which shows septate hyphae branching at acute angles", "C": "Pneumocystis, which shows foamy honeycomb material with silver-stained cysts", "D": "Cryptococcus, which shows budding yeast with a thick capsule halo"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Aspergillus, which shows septate hyphae branching at acute angles): Septate hyphae branching at acute angles, about 45 degrees, are characteristic of Aspergillus. Candida shows yeast with pseudohyphae, Cryptococcus is a capsulated budding yeast, and Pneumocystis appears as foamy alveolar casts with cysts, not hyphae."}, {"id": 1175, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist prepares to run a Ziehl-Neelsen stain on tissue from a suspected tuberculosis case and a chromic acid silver method the same afternoon. A chromic acid waste container sits near the stain bench. Which safety practice applies to unfixed or infectious material and hazardous reagents?", "options": {"A": "Use appropriate biosafety precautions, PPE, and safety data sheets for hazardous reagents", "B": "Handle fixed slides without gloves, because formalin fixation inactivates the organisms in the tissue", "C": "Skip WHMIS labels on working solutions that are used within one hour", "D": "Store chromic acid waste with regular solvent waste for easier disposal"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use appropriate biosafety precautions, PPE, and safety data sheets for hazardous reagents): Infectious cases need biosafety precautions, and hazardous reagents need PPE, safety data sheets and correct WHMIS labelling. Chromic acid needs special waste handling, and formalin fixation does not remove all risk."}, {"id": 1176, "domain": "hematology", "question": "During fibrinolysis, the enzyme plasmin breaks down fibrin into fragments that circulate in the blood. A laboratory test is used to help rule out venous thromboembolism in patients with low clinical probability. Which fragment forms only when cross-linked fibrin is degraded?", "options": {"A": "Antithrombin, which is released when plasmin digests cross-linked plasma", "B": "Fibrinogen, which is released when plasmin digests cross-linked platelets", "C": "Thrombin, which is released when plasmin digests cross-linked prothrombin", "D": "D-dimer, which is released when plasmin digests cross-linked fibrin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (D-dimer, which is released when plasmin digests cross-linked fibrin): D-dimer forms only when cross-linked fibrin is broken down by plasmin, so it signals fibrin formation and breakdown. Fibrinogen, antithrombin and thrombin are not products of plasmin digesting fibrin."}, {"id": 1177, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A red cell unit was issued at 09:00 to a ward that did not start it. A nurse returns it at 09:45 in an uncontrolled plastic bag, and asks the blood bank to reissue it for the same patient. The unit temperature is now 12 °C. What is the best decision?", "options": {"A": "Reissue it after warming it to room temperature to speed up the transfusion", "B": "Reissue it, since 45 minutes is still well within the four-hour transfusion window", "C": "Reissue it after irradiating it to remove any bacteria that grew during the delay", "D": "Do not reissue it, since it exceeded the start window and its history is unsafe"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Do not reissue it, since it exceeded the start window and its history is unsafe): The unit left controlled storage more than 30 minutes earlier, was not kept in a validated cooler and now reads above its range, so its safety cannot be shown. The four-hour window applies only after a timely start. Warming worsens the risk, and irradiation does not kill bacteria."}, {"id": 1178, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A physician requests a urine albumin to creatinine ratio for a patient with diabetes. The laboratory technologist notes a reporting issue that could cause misinterpretation when results from different laboratories are compared. What is the reporting issue that must be handled clearly?", "options": {"A": "The result must be reported only as a percent, because units cause confusion", "B": "The sample must be a 24-hour collection, because random samples give unusable ratios", "C": "The units mg/mmol and mg/g are different, so the units must be reported clearly", "D": "The ratio must be reported without creatinine, because creatinine varies with age"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The units mg/mmol and mg/g are different, so the units must be reported clearly): The ratio can be expressed in mg/mmol or mg/g, which are different, so units must be stated clearly. It is not reported in percent. Creatinine is the denominator used to correct for urine concentration, and a random sample is the preferred early marker sample."}, {"id": 1179, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A tumour specimen is expected to carry a somatic variant at about 3% VAF. The panel was validated with a limit of detection of 5% VAF at 500x mean depth. This run gave only 120x at the position of interest, and no variant was called. How should the negative result be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Negative, because 120x exceeds the 100x minimum commonly used for germline testing", "B": "Negative, because no variant reads were called by the software at that position", "C": "Present at 3%, since the expected fraction can be reported without supporting reads", "D": "Not excluded, since the depth is too low for a variant at that fraction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Not excluded, since the depth is too low for a variant at that fraction): Depth must be high enough to detect a variant in only a few percent of reads, and 120x is well below the validated depth, so the absence of a call is not reliable evidence. A germline benchmark does not apply to a 3% somatic variant. Reporting the variant without reads is unsupported."}, {"id": 1180, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A quality officer prepares for an inspection of the hospital transfusion service. The officer must list the national standard that governs blood and blood components in Canadian transfusion services, used together with the Canadian Society for Transfusion Medicine hospital standards. Which standard is this?", "options": {"A": "ISO 15189, the international standard for quality and competence in medical laboratories", "B": "CSA Z902, the CSA Group standard for blood and blood components", "C": "WHMIS 2015, the hazard communication standard for chemicals in workplaces", "D": "CLSI M100, the performance standard for antimicrobial susceptibility testing in microbiology"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (CSA Z902, the CSA Group standard for blood and blood components): Hospital transfusion services in Canada follow CSA Z902 together with the CSTM Standards for Hospital Transfusion Services and provincial regulations. ISO 15189 is a general laboratory standard, CLSI M100 belongs to microbiology and WHMIS covers chemical hazards, so none of them governs blood components."}, {"id": 1181, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "During lysis of a buccal swab, the procedure adds proteinase K and incubates at 56 °C for 10 minutes before binding the lysate to a column. A trainee asks what this enzyme contributes to the extraction. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "It cleaves DNA into fragments small enough to pass through the silica membrane.", "B": "It digests proteins, including nucleases, so the nucleic acid is freed and protected.", "C": "It removes RNA from the lysate so that only genomic DNA is recovered.", "D": "It binds magnesium in the lysate so that DNases lose their required cofactor."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It digests proteins, including nucleases, so the nucleic acid is freed and protected.): Proteinase K is a protease that breaks down proteins during lysis, releasing nucleic acid from protein complexes and destroying nucleases. It does not cut DNA, digest RNA, or work by chelating magnesium."}, {"id": 1182, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist prepares a 1 in 20 dilution of blood for a manual white cell count on a hemocytometer. The diluting fluid contains 2 percent acetic acid rather than saline, which the trainee finds surprising because saline was used for other dilutions that morning. What is the reason for choosing this diluent?", "options": {"A": "It preserves the red cells so they can be counted with the white cells", "B": "It anticoagulates the blood so a clot cannot form in the chamber", "C": "It stains platelets so they can be seen under phase contrast", "D": "It lyses red cells and leaves white nuclei visible"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It lyses red cells and leaves white nuclei visible): Two percent acetic acid destroys the red cells and leaves white cell nuclei that can be counted. Ammonium oxalate with phase contrast is used for platelets. Preserving red cells would obscure the white cells. Anticoagulation is done by EDTA in the tube before dilution."}, {"id": 1183, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory has a regulator-approved PCR kit for respiratory swabs. The director wants to use it on bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, a specimen type the manufacturer does not list. The kit steps stay unchanged. What must the laboratory do before patient reporting?", "options": {"A": "Perform verification only, because the kit itself carries regulatory approval.", "B": "Ask the manufacturer to add the fluid, with no laboratory studies required.", "C": "Perform no studies, provided proficiency testing later includes this fluid type.", "D": "Perform a full validation, since this use is outside the approved claims."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Perform a full validation, since this use is outside the approved claims.): Approval covers the claims the manufacturer proved, and a new specimen type is a use outside them, so the laboratory must establish performance itself through validation. Verification only confirms stated claims. Proficiency testing after launch does not replace studies before it. A verbal request to the manufacturer does not substitute for evidence."}, {"id": 1184, "domain": "histology", "question": "A batch of slides from one technologist is being reviewed by a supervisor at the end of the week. A section falls off the slide during staining. The technologist has ruled out a fault in the staining line. Which cause is consistent with this problem?", "options": {"A": "An excessively warm cryostat chamber used for a frozen block", "B": "A stain solution that is too fresh and has been well filtered", "C": "A blade that is far too sharp for the type of tissue being cut", "D": "Dirty or uncharged slides, or insufficient drying time"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dirty or uncharged slides, or insufficient drying time): Sections that fall off the slide can come from a cold water bath, insufficient drying, dirty or uncharged slides, or poorly processed or over-fixed tissue. Blade sharpness, fresh stain, and cryostat temperature are unrelated."}, {"id": 1185, "domain": "cytology", "question": "On a urine slide the surface layer of the urothelium is represented by very large cells, some with two nuclei. The nuclei are smooth, round and evenly stained, and the nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio is low. A trainee asks what these benign cells are called. Which name is correct?", "options": {"A": "Plasmacytoid cells, which have eccentric nuclei and granular salt-and-pepper chromatin", "B": "Umbrella cells, which are benign despite frequently carrying two nuclei", "C": "Signet-ring cells, which hold a mucin vacuole that pushes the nucleus to one side", "D": "Decoy cells, which carry a ground-glass nuclear inclusion and marginated chromatin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Umbrella cells, which are benign despite frequently carrying two nuclei): Umbrella cells form the surface layer of the urothelium and are large, often binucleated, with smooth round nuclei and a low ratio. Decoy cells are infected cells with a glassy inclusion. Signet-ring cells have a mucin vacuole. Plasmacytoid cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin belong to medullary thyroid carcinoma."}, {"id": 1186, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A fluid smear shows a dense neutrophil background with degenerating cells and bacteria, and the physician asks what the neutrophils suggest. Cytology comments in an effusion should correlate the background with clinical conditions. Which condition does a neutrophil-rich background most strongly suggest?", "options": {"A": "Infection in the cavity, which is completed with microbiology testing", "B": "Lymphoma in the cavity, which is completed with flow cytometry", "C": "Allergic reaction in the cavity, which is completed with skin testing", "D": "Asbestos exposure in the cavity, which is completed with electron microscopy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Infection in the cavity, which is completed with microbiology testing): Many neutrophils suggest infection, and microbiology confirms the organism. Allergy is linked to eosinophils, and lymphoma is a lymphocyte problem tested with flow cytometry. Asbestos exposure relates to ferruginous bodies and mesothelioma, not a neutrophil background."}, {"id": 1187, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The laboratory receives a new batch of prepared media for routine use. A technologist wants to confirm they are free of contamination before use. Which quality control step addresses this, and what result is expected? The laboratory wants to detect slow growers without wasting time, so written procedures set out when each plate is examined and recorded.", "options": {"A": "Examine the batch under a microscope and expect gram-positive cocci", "B": "Store the batch at room temperature for a week and expect no colour change", "C": "Incubate a sample of plates from the batch and expect no growth", "D": "Inoculate the batch with a reference organism and expect heavy growth"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Incubate a sample of plates from the batch and expect no growth): Sterility testing incubates a sample from each batch, and any growth means contamination. Reference organism testing checks performance rather than sterility, and storage or microscopy does not test sterility."}, {"id": 1188, "domain": "cytology", "question": "During EBUS-TBNA for lung cancer staging, the operator samples several lymph node stations in sequence and passes each needle rinse to the technologist. The technologist is responsible for keeping the specimens distinguishable for the pathologist who will stage the disease. What labelling detail is essential for these specimens?", "options": {"A": "The time of day each pass was rinsed into preservative.", "B": "The numbered lymph node station sampled, recorded on each specimen.", "C": "The needle gauge used for each pass, without any anatomic location.", "D": "The bronchoscope serial number recorded on every slide."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The numbered lymph node station sampled, recorded on each specimen.): Each station is a separate staging question, so the operator records the station number on every specimen. The scope serial number and needle gauge do not tell the pathologist which node was sampled. A time stamp alone cannot separate stations if several were sampled close together."}, {"id": 1189, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A report lists a single base substitution in an exon that changes a codon from GAA to GAG. Both codons specify glutamic acid. How is this single nucleotide variant classified according to its effect on the protein? The report also states that the exon is well covered.", "options": {"A": "Silent, since the amino acid sequence of the protein is unchanged", "B": "Missense, since the changed codon produces a different amino acid in the protein", "C": "Nonsense, since the changed codon terminates translation early", "D": "Frameshift, since a base was altered within the reading frame"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Silent, since the amino acid sequence of the protein is unchanged): A change that alters the codon but not the amino acid is silent. A missense change would encode a different amino acid, a nonsense change would create a stop codon, and a frameshift needs an insertion or deletion, not a substitution."}, {"id": 1190, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A supervisor asks a trainee to describe Trichomonas vaginalis as seen on a Papanicolaou-stained smear. The trainee must recall the organism's colour, shape, size and nucleus, along with the typical background seen in this infection. The requisition notes vaginal discharge in a young woman.", "options": {"A": "Pink jointed rods piercing squamous cells with a clean background", "B": "Cells thickly coated by coccobacilli with almost no neutrophils present", "C": "Tangled woolly colonies with radiating filaments lying beside an intrauterine device in situ", "D": "Pale grey-blue pear-shaped organisms with small eccentric nuclei among many neutrophils"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pale grey-blue pear-shaped organisms with small eccentric nuclei among many neutrophils): Trichomonas is a pale grey-blue to green, pear-shaped or oval protozoan about the size of a neutrophil, with a small eccentric nucleus, and it comes with many neutrophils. Jointed rods describe Candida, woolly colonies describe Actinomyces, and a coccobacillary coating describes bacterial vaginosis."}, {"id": 1191, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A leukemia patient's bone marrow is being processed, and the technologist includes a direct preparation harvested within hours of collection alongside 24-hour and 48-hour cultures. A trainee asks why a direct preparation is useful for this specimen type. What is the correct reason?", "options": {"A": "Marrow cells cannot divide in culture, so a direct preparation is the only possible method", "B": "Direct preparations suit peripheral blood specimens, while marrow needs a 72-hour culture", "C": "Direct preparations avoid every use of colcemid, so chromosomes remain uncondensed at harvest", "D": "Marrow cells are often already dividing in the body, so they can be harvested at once"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Marrow cells are often already dividing in the body, so they can be harvested at once): Marrow cells are often dividing already, so a direct preparation captures them before culture conditions alter the population. Marrow cells can divide in culture too, and colcemid is still used in harvest."}, {"id": 1192, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A newer analyzer uses electrochemiluminescence to measure ferritin. The technologist is asked what triggers light emission in this technique, which differs from acridinium ester chemiluminescence with its chemical trigger. The reagent cartridge carries magnetic particles and a labelled antibody. What triggers the light in electrochemiluminescence?", "options": {"A": "Enzymatic conversion of a substrate by alkaline phosphatase into a coloured product", "B": "Hydrogen peroxide added in alkaline solution after magnetic washing", "C": "An electric current applied at an electrode surface", "D": "A xenon lamp exciting the label at a fixed wavelength"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An electric current applied at an electrode surface): In electrochemiluminescence the light is generated by a current at an electrode surface. Lamp excitation describes fluorescence. The peroxide trigger is the chemical trigger for acridinium labels, and the enzyme reaction describes enzyme immunoassay."}, {"id": 1193, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A pregnant woman with anti-Kell has a low titre of 4, but the obstetric ultrasound shows signs of severe fetal anemia and early hydrops. The clinician wonders why the titre was reassuring. What is the best explanation for the discrepancy?", "options": {"A": "Titres are only valid when compared with the paternal antibody level.", "B": "Anti-Kell suppresses fetal red cell production, so titres mislead.", "C": "A titre of 4 is critical for Kell, so the result was misread.", "D": "Anti-Kell is IgM and so does not correlate with the titre."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Anti-Kell suppresses fetal red cell production, so titres mislead.): Anti-Kell can suppress fetal erythroid precursors as well as destroy mature cells, so the titre is a poor predictor of severity and the fetus is monitored by other means. Kell antibodies relevant here are IgG, not IgM, and paternal levels are not used."}, {"id": 1194, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A droplet digital PCR reaction produces 20,000 accepted droplets, of which 6,000 are positive for the target. The technologist applies Poisson correction, where the mean copies per droplet equals the negative natural log of the fraction of negative droplets. Approximately how many target copies were in the reaction?", "options": {"A": "About 3,000 copies, since half of positives are counted as background.", "B": "6,000 copies, since each positive droplet holds a single molecule.", "C": "About 7,100 copies, since some positive droplets held more than one molecule.", "D": "14,000 copies, the number of negative droplets counted in the run."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 7,100 copies, since some positive droplets held more than one molecule.): The fraction negative is 14,000 divided by 20,000, or 0.70. The negative natural log of 0.70 is 0.357 copies per droplet, and 0.357 multiplied by 20,000 droplets gives about 7,130 copies. Simply counting positives underestimates because droplets can hold two or more molecules. The negative droplet count and a half-value rule have no basis in Poisson statistics."}, {"id": 1195, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A pregnant woman has a total T4 higher than normal, but her free T4 and TSH are both normal. Why is free hormone preferred over total hormone for thyroid testing? The laboratory is asked to explain the discrepancy before results are reviewed with the patient.", "options": {"A": "Total T4 changes with meal timing, because it is absorbed slowly into the circulation", "B": "Total T4 is measured only by mass spectrometry, so it is rarely available in laboratories", "C": "Total T4 degrades in light, while the free fraction is stable at room temperature", "D": "Total T4 changes with binding protein levels, such as in pregnancy or with estrogen"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Total T4 changes with binding protein levels, such as in pregnancy or with estrogen): Thyroid hormone is largely bound to carrier proteins, and pregnancy or estrogen raise binding protein levels and total T4. The free fraction is the active portion and is unaffected by binding changes. This makes free T4 the better test."}, {"id": 1196, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A small laboratory owns an older analyzer that reports lymphocytes, mid-sized cells and granulocytes only. A physician asks for separate eosinophil and basophil percentages on a patient with a suspected allergy. Which limitation of this type of differential applies to the request?", "options": {"A": "It reports all five types, but with lower precision than a manual count", "B": "It uses side scatter and dyes, so eosinophils are named but basophils are not", "C": "It splits cells by size alone, so eosinophils and basophils are not named", "D": "It classifies by nucleic acid dyes, which cannot separate granulocytes from monocytes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It splits cells by size alone, so eosinophils and basophils are not named): A three-part differential groups cells by volume into lymphocytes, mid-sized cells and granulocytes, so a specific granulocyte type cannot be named. Five-part instruments use scatter and dyes to name all five. Nucleic acid dyes are part of five-part systems and are not the basis for a three-part split, so the request needs a smear review."}, {"id": 1197, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist notices that Papanicolaou slides from one run show good nuclei but the cells are covered in a hazy film after coverslipping. The final dehydration and clearing baths were changed at the start of the run. Xylene appears cloudy in the last bath. What most likely explains the hazy film over the cells?", "options": {"A": "Air drying before fixation, so the clinic needs retraining in collection", "B": "Sputum containing tuberculosis organisms, so the cabinet airflow needs checking", "C": "Water carried over into the clearing bath, so the dehydration alcohols need replacing", "D": "Excess hematoxylin left on the slide, so the bluing solution needs replacing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Water carried over into the clearing bath, so the dehydration alcohols need replacing): A cloudy clearing bath and hazy slides mean water has been carried through, so the dehydrating alcohols and xylene must be replaced. Hematoxylin excess does not cloud xylene, and air drying would show as poor nuclear detail from collection instead."}, {"id": 1198, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A blue-green colony with a powdery surface grows on Sabouraud agar from a bronchoalveolar lavage of a neutropenic patient. A lactophenol cotton blue mount shows septate hyphae branching at forty-five degrees, ending in a columnar conidial head with a single row of phialides. Which organism is the best match?", "options": {"A": "Aspergillus fumigatus, a common cause of invasive mold infection in immunocompromised patients", "B": "Rhizopus species, a ribbon-like mold that branches at right angles with few septa", "C": "Aspergillus niger, a mold recognized by its black spores and dark colony", "D": "Microsporum species, a dermatophyte identified by its spindle-shaped macroconidia with thick rough walls"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Aspergillus fumigatus, a common cause of invasive mold infection in immunocompromised patients): Septate hyphae with acute branching and a blue-green colony with a columnar head describe A. fumigatus. Rhizopus has broad aseptate hyphae, A. niger produces black spores, and dermatophytes are identified by macroconidia and infect keratin."}, {"id": 1199, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist finds a cell line in a leukemia marrow that looks similar to a known changed pattern but is not one of the recurrent abnormalities and cannot be identified for certain. The clinician wants a definite label. Which reporting approach reflects professional practice?", "options": {"A": "Ask the patient's family to decide which interpretation should appear on the report.", "B": "Leave the finding out of the report because uncertain changes are not clinically useful.", "C": "Describe the finding as uncertain in the report without overstating what the karyotype shows.", "D": "Assign the closest recurrent abnormality so the clinician has a definite label."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Describe the finding as uncertain in the report without overstating what the karyotype shows.): Professional practice means describing uncertain findings as uncertain and not overstating the result. Naming the nearest recurrent change could lead to wrong treatment, and omitting the finding hides information. Interpretation is the responsibility of the qualified laboratory staff, not the family."}, {"id": 1200, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In smears from a lymph node, there are foreign cells in groups and gland-like clusters with vacuolated cytoplasm and prominent nucleoli. The pathologist asks how mucin appears on the two stains used. Which statement correctly describes mucin on Papanicolaou and Romanowsky stains?", "options": {"A": "Clear vacuoles in Papanicolaou and pale purple material in Romanowsky.", "B": "Pale purple material in Papanicolaou and clear vacuoles in Romanowsky stain.", "C": "Brown pigment granules in Papanicolaou and magenta fibrils in Romanowsky.", "D": "Bright orange globules in Papanicolaou and dark blue crystals in Romanowsky."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Clear vacuoles in Papanicolaou and pale purple material in Romanowsky.): Mucin appears as clear vacuoles on Pap stain and as pale purple material on Romanowsky stains. The reversed pairing is a common slip. Orange cytoplasm is keratin, brown granules are pigment, and magenta fibrils describe pleomorphic adenoma stroma."}, {"id": 1201, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A stem cell transplant patient dies weeks after a transfusion, and the investigation finds that donor lymphocytes attacked the recipient's tissues. The transfusion service reviews what went wrong with the component preparation. Which statement about the missing safeguard is correct?", "options": {"A": "Frozen storage was needed, since lymphocytes only survive in components kept cold in the bank", "B": "Washing was needed, since plasma proteins carry the lymphocytes that attack tissues", "C": "Irradiation was needed, since filtration alone leaves enough lymphocytes to cause disease", "D": "Volume reduction was needed, since lymphocytes multiply in the extra plasma volume given"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Irradiation was needed, since filtration alone leaves enough lymphocytes to cause disease): TA-GVHD is prevented by irradiation, and leukoreduction does not replace it because enough lymphocytes can remain. Washing, volume reduction and frozen storage do not stop donor lymphocytes from dividing, so they do not prevent the disease."}, {"id": 1202, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A broth microdilution tray is read after overnight incubation. The wells across the antibiotic dilution series show the expected pattern, but the positive growth control well is clear with no turbidity. The sterile control well is also clear. What should the technologist conclude about this tray?", "options": {"A": "The tray is invalid, because the growth control failed and the MICs cannot be trusted", "B": "The tray is invalid, but only the sterile control needs repeating before release", "C": "The tray is valid, and every well should be reported as below the first dilution", "D": "The tray is valid, because a clear sterile control proves all MIC values are correct"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The tray is invalid, because the growth control failed and the MICs cannot be trusted): The growth control must show growth to prove that the organism was viable and the medium supported it. If it is clear, no negative well can be interpreted as inhibition, so the whole tray is invalid and the test must be repeated."}, {"id": 1203, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A microbiology laboratory plates a wound specimen on Columbia colistin-nalidixic acid agar alongside other media. The purpose is to recover gram-positive cocci from a specimen with abundant mixed flora. What feature of this medium accomplishes that goal? The technologist is choosing between several plates, and the specimen has abundant gram-negative rods that could overgrow the target.", "options": {"A": "Bile salts and lactose separate lactose fermenters from nonfermenters using a colour indicator", "B": "A reducing agent removes oxygen from the deeper layers of a liquid medium tube", "C": "Heated blood supplies X and V factors to help fastidious organisms recover on the plate", "D": "Colistin and nalidixic acid suppress gram-negative rods but allow gram-positive cocci to grow"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Colistin and nalidixic acid suppress gram-negative rods but allow gram-positive cocci to grow): The two antibiotics inhibit gram-negative rods so gram-positive cocci can grow through. Bile salts with lactose describe MacConkey, heated blood describes chocolate agar, and a reducing agent describes thioglycolate broth."}, {"id": 1204, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A specimen arrives leaking in the wrong container after transport, and the sender is a busy clinic. The technologist is unsure whether the sample is still usable. The clinic is busy and asks for a quick decision, but the laboratory must follow its documented policy. What is the appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Document the problem, notify the sender, and process it only if laboratory policy allows", "B": "Discard it immediately, tell the courier to return the container, and keep no record of this", "C": "Repack it in a fresh container and send it on to another site", "D": "Process it normally and report the result without mentioning the leak"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Document the problem, notify the sender, and process it only if laboratory policy allows): Leaking or wrong containers are documented and the sender is notified, with processing only if policy allows. Ignoring the problem or discarding without a record undermines quality and traceability."}, {"id": 1205, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist cuts sections for a Congo red stain from a rectal biopsy that was fixed in neutral buffered formalin and embedded in paraffin. The birefringence is difficult to see on 4 µm sections used for routine work. What change is recommended for Congo red sections?", "options": {"A": "Cut sections thicker, at about 8 to 10 µm, to make birefringence easier to see", "B": "Cut frozen sections at 4 µm to preserve the amyloid protein structure", "C": "Cut sections at 4 µm but stain them twice as long in hematoxylin", "D": "Cut sections thinner, at about 1 to 2 µm, to reduce optical scatter"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cut sections thicker, at about 8 to 10 µm, to make birefringence easier to see): Congo red sections are commonly cut thicker, around 8 to 10 µm, so apple-green birefringence is more visible. Thinner sections reduce the signal. Frozen sections are not required for amyloid."}, {"id": 1206, "domain": "histology", "question": "The laboratory supervisor calls a short meeting on Friday about safety and incidents. After a formalin spill and a sharps injury in the same week, the supervisor asks staff to write reports. Which approach to reporting and follow-up reflects a well-run quality system?", "options": {"A": "Reports are filed, but causes are not investigated until repeat events occur in the laboratory", "B": "Reports name the person at fault so discipline can be applied quickly by managers", "C": "Reports are written only when the injury needed outside medical treatment and follow-up", "D": "Reports are prompt and factual, causes are investigated, and the fix is confirmed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reports are prompt and factual, causes are investigated, and the fix is confirmed): Reports are factual and prompt without blame, followed by root cause investigation, correction, and follow-up to confirm the correction worked. Punishment, selective reporting, and delayed investigation prevent learning and repeat prevention."}, {"id": 1207, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Fragments prepared for a short-read platform are amplified into clusters on a flow cell before reading begins. A trainee asks why the fragments are copied into clusters rather than read as single molecules on the flow cell. What is the purpose of a cluster?", "options": {"A": "Clusters remove the adapters so that only genomic sequence is read", "B": "Many copies of one fragment together give a signal strong enough to image each cycle", "C": "Clusters cut fragments to a uniform length before the first cycle begins", "D": "Each cluster holds a different patient's fragments, so the pooled library separates itself by location"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Many copies of one fragment together give a signal strong enough to image each cycle): A cluster is a group of identical copies of one fragment, and their combined fluorescence is bright enough to detect at each cycle. Patient separation depends on barcodes, adapters are needed for priming, and fragment length is set during library preparation."}, {"id": 1208, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Two primers for a new assay have calculated melting temperatures of 52 °C and 68 °C. The scientist proposes a single annealing temperature of 60 °C for the run and expects reliable performance from both. Which problem is most likely to result?", "options": {"A": "The polymerase runs out of dNTPs, since mismatched primers consume more nucleotides.", "B": "The higher primer is cleaved by the polymerase, which shortens every amplicon produced.", "C": "Both primers bind perfectly, since 60 °C lies between the two values.", "D": "One primer binds poorly and the other binds non-specifically, so yield or specificity suffers."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (One primer binds poorly and the other binds non-specifically, so yield or specificity suffers.): Primers in a pair need similar melting temperatures because one annealing temperature must suit both. A temperature well above one primer's melting temperature weakens its binding, and a temperature well below the other's allows non-specific binding. Averaging does not fix the mismatch."}, {"id": 1209, "domain": "histology", "question": "A resident is shadowing in the laboratory during a busy operating day. A surgeon asks the laboratory whether frozen sections are useful for checking spread of cancer to nearby lymph nodes during an operation. Which use is a recognized purpose of intraoperative consultation?", "options": {"A": "Checking whether lymph nodes contain metastasis when the result changes the operation", "B": "Setting the final cancer stage in place of the later paraffin section report", "C": "Replacing routine outpatient biopsies with faster reports that need no permanent sections", "D": "Confirming bacterial infection in the tissue by growing culture during the operation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Checking whether lymph nodes contain metastasis when the result changes the operation): Frozen sections are commonly used to check whether lymph nodes contain metastasis, whether margins are clear, and whether tissue is adequate. They do not give final staging, replace routine biopsies, or confirm bacteria by culture."}, {"id": 1210, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A liquid-based slide from a 48-year-old woman with abnormal uterine bleeding contains the glandular and squamous cells in the image at equal magnification. Which cell shows nuclear changes that would raise concern for an atypical or malignant glandular process requiring pathologist review?", "options": {"A": "Cell 1, because its glandular nucleus is large, dark and irregular compared with its neighbours", "B": "Cell 2, because its glandular nucleus is large, dark and irregular compared with its neighbours", "C": "Cell 3, because its glandular nucleus is large, dark and irregular compared with its neighbours", "D": "Cell 4, because its glandular nucleus is large, dark and irregular compared with its neighbours"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cell 2, because its glandular nucleus is large, dark and irregular compared with its neighbours): Cell 2 is columnar with a large, dark nucleus and an irregular outline, a marked change from the other glandular cells. Cells 1 and 4 are columnar with small, fine, smooth nuclei, so they look normal. Cell 3 is a squamous cell with a small bland nucleus, so it is neither glandular nor atypical.", "image_type": "cell_micrograph", "image_params": {"cells": [{"label": "1", "nc_ratio": 0.2, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "columnar"}, {"label": "2", "nc_ratio": 0.5, "chromatin": "hyperchromatic", "outline": "irregular", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "columnar"}, {"label": "3", "nc_ratio": 0.05, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "squamous"}, {"label": "4", "nc_ratio": 0.25, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "columnar"}], "caption": "Cells at equal magnification", "context": "cervical", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1210.png"}, {"id": 1211, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A donor sample is tested for D with the immediate-spin and antiglobulin-phase reactions shown in the image. The laboratory follows its procedure for confirming D status in donors. What does this pattern indicate about the D antigen on the red cells?", "options": {"A": "Weak expression of the D antigen, detected after the antiglobulin phase", "B": "Absence of the D antigen, because the immediate-spin reaction with anti-D was negative", "C": "Presence of an anti-D antibody in the sample, because the antiglobulin phase reaction was positive", "D": "Mixed-field reaction from a recently transfused population, because the antiglobulin reaction was weaker"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Weak expression of the D antigen, detected after the antiglobulin phase): A negative immediate-spin reaction followed by a positive antiglobulin reaction with a negative Rh control shows weakly expressed D antigen. Absence of D would be negative at both phases, the test detects antigen on cells rather than antibody in the sample, and a mixed-field pattern looks like a split population of cells.", "image_type": "gel_agglutination", "image_params": {"wells": [{"label": "Anti-D (IS)", "reaction": "negative"}, {"label": "Anti-D (IAT)", "reaction": "2+"}, {"label": "Rh control (IAT)", "reaction": "negative"}], "caption": "Gel D typing with antiglobulin phase"}, "image": "question_images/q_1211.png"}, {"id": 1212, "domain": "histology", "question": "A student technologist is new to the frozen section area and is learning why extra care is taken. A technologist is preparing to cut a frozen section and asks why the cryostat needs special attention compared with a routine paraffin microtome. Which reason is correct?", "options": {"A": "The cryostat cuts only fixed tissue, so infection is not a real concern", "B": "The cryostat blade stays sharp for months, so blade maintenance is rarely needed", "C": "The cryostat makes aerosols and tissue fragments from fresh, unfixed tissue", "D": "The cryostat runs on formalin vapour that must be vented continuously during cutting"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The cryostat makes aerosols and tissue fragments from fresh, unfixed tissue): Fresh tissue can carry infectious agents, and cutting on a cryostat produces aerosols and fragments that need control. It does not use formalin vapour, its blades still dull, and it cuts fresh, not fixed, tissue."}, {"id": 1213, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A collector finishes a venipuncture, releases the tourniquet, withdraws the needle and activates the safety device. The patient is bleeding slightly at the site, and the collector reaches for the cap of the used needle out of habit. What is the correct handling of the used needle?", "options": {"A": "Break the needle off the holder before placing it in general waste", "B": "Discard it in a sharps container without recapping", "C": "Recap it using a one-handed scoop technique before disposal", "D": "Bend the needle so it cannot be reused and drop it in the biohazard bag"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Discard it in a sharps container without recapping): Needles are placed directly into a sharps container after the safety device is activated, without recapping, because recapping is a common cause of needlestick injury. Breaking or bending a needle also exposes the collector to injury and is not permitted. Biohazard bags are not puncture-resistant containers, so a sharp inside one can injure staff later."}, {"id": 1214, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient is on a drug that is known to stick to serum separator gel, and a therapeutic level is ordered. The drawing room stocks gold gel tubes and plain red-top tubes. The results from previous gold-top samples were consistently lower than the clinical picture suggested. Which tube should be used?", "options": {"A": "A gray top, since fluoride keeps drug concentrations stable in transit", "B": "A gold top again, since the gel makes serum separation more complete", "C": "A plain red top without gel, so the drug is not adsorbed", "D": "A lavender top, since EDTA prevents the drug from binding to plastic"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A plain red top without gel, so the drug is not adsorbed): Gels can adsorb some drugs and lower the measured concentration, so therapeutic drug monitoring often uses a plain red top without gel. That fits the observation of low results from gold tops. EDTA and fluoride add other additives that do not solve adsorption, and a more complete separation does nothing to prevent the drug from sticking to the gel."}, {"id": 1215, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Several IAT tubes in the batch were noticed to be filled almost to the brim with red cell suspension and were spun for much longer than the procedure states. The reactions look stronger than usual across the whole rack. What is the appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Dilute the serum in each tube with saline and reread, since volume affects the antibody titre", "B": "Report the stronger reactions as enhanced antibodies, since extra centrifugation improves sensitivity", "C": "Recognize that overfilling and overspinning cause false results, and repeat the affected tubes correctly", "D": "Accept the results, since check cells added after the wash will correct any excess volume"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recognize that overfilling and overspinning cause false results, and repeat the affected tubes correctly): Overfilling or overspinning a tube can produce false results, so the technologist follows the written procedure and repeats the affected tubes rather than interpreting them. Extra spin time does not make a test more sensitive in any validated way. Check cells verify the antiglobulin reagent and cannot correct a technical error, and diluting the serum would change the test itself."}, {"id": 1216, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist prepares to perform a 100-cell differential on a well-stained smear with many nucleated red blood cells. The analyzer's white count did not exclude them. What should the technologist do about the nucleated red cells during the differential and count reporting?", "options": {"A": "Leave them out of the count and report the white cell count as measured", "B": "Include them in the 100 cells since they are nucleated cells", "C": "Count them separately from the 100 white cells and correct the white cell count", "D": "Count them as lymphocytes because their round dense nuclei resemble those of small lymphocytes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Count them separately from the 100 white cells and correct the white cell count): Nucleated red cells are counted separately, and the white cell count is corrected when they are numerous, since they would falsely raise the count. Including them in the differential or ignoring them distorts results. They are not lymphocytes."}, {"id": 1217, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A break-apart probe is applied to a lymphoma sample. The technologist finds many nuclei with one fused yellow signal plus one separate red and one separate green signal. What does this pattern indicate about the gene flanked by the probe?", "options": {"A": "The gene is rearranged on one homologue, with the other homologue intact", "B": "The gene is deleted on one homologue, with the other homologue intact", "C": "The gene is amplified, with extra intact copies on the same homologue", "D": "The gene is normal on both homologues, showing the usual two fusion signals"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The gene is rearranged on one homologue, with the other homologue intact): One fusion signal represents the intact copy, while separated red and green signals show the copy that has broken apart. A deletion would reduce signals, an amplification would increase fusion signals, and normal nuclei show two fused signals with nothing separated."}, {"id": 1218, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A quality officer audits a cytogenetics section and asks the technologist to show evidence of routine checks that protect culture quality and result reliability. The section has incubators, reagents and slides in storage. Which set of records is appropriate to present?", "options": {"A": "Staff lunch schedules, reagent prices, courier invoices, and parking permits.", "B": "Only the final reports, because reagent and incubator records are not audited.", "C": "Incubator temperatures from the previous year, since older records must be destroyed after twelve months", "D": "Incubator temperature logs, reagent expiry checks, slide and image storage, and proficiency testing."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Incubator temperature logs, reagent expiry checks, slide and image storage, and proficiency testing.): Quality assurance covers incubator temperature checks, reagents used within expiry, stored images and slides, and proficiency testing. Schedules and invoices say nothing about result reliability. Reports alone do not show how they were produced, and discarding older records defeats traceability."}, {"id": 1219, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A woman has had three unexplained first-trimester miscarriages, and her physician orders chromosome analysis on both partners. The laboratory receives a heparinized peripheral blood specimen from her partner, and a trainee asks why blood is the appropriate tissue to study for a couple with this history rather than pregnancy tissue.", "options": {"A": "Blood shows only changes acquired after birth, which are the usual cause of losses", "B": "Blood is the only specimen containing dividing cells suitable for solid tumour study", "C": "A balanced rearrangement in one partner can cause repeated loss without affecting that person's health", "D": "Blood cells carry the fetal chromosomes, so the pregnancy loss can be studied directly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A balanced rearrangement in one partner can cause repeated loss without affecting that person's health): Balanced rearrangements are constitutional and can cause repeated pregnancy loss while the carrier is healthy, and blood is used to find them. Maternal blood does not contain fetal chromosomes, and blood analysis targets constitutional, not acquired, changes."}, {"id": 1220, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A laboratory director reminds staff of the minimum cellularity for liquid-based gynecologic slides. A trainee should state how many well-visualized squamous cells a liquid-based slide needs to be adequate. The laboratory director is checking that the new hire knows the lower minimum used for this preparation method. Which figure is the correct minimum?", "options": {"A": "At least 50,000 well-preserved squamous cells, the figure for endometrial samples", "B": "At least 5,000 well-preserved squamous cells, lower than the conventional smear estimate", "C": "At least 12,000 well-preserved squamous cells, the conventional smear estimate", "D": "At least 500 well-preserved squamous cells, the same as for a cytospin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (At least 5,000 well-preserved squamous cells, lower than the conventional smear estimate): A liquid-based slide needs at least 5,000 squamous cells, lower than the 8,000 to 12,000 estimate for a conventional smear because the cells are evenly spread. The other figures are wrong."}, {"id": 1221, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A formalin-ethyl acetate concentrate from one patient shows a barrel-shaped egg about 50 micrometres long with a clear plug at each end. In another patient, the concentrate shows a round egg about 60 micrometres wide with a thick, bumpy outer shell. Which two helminths are respectively identified?", "options": {"A": "The barrel-shaped egg is a hookworm egg, and the bumpy round egg is Enterobius vermicularis", "B": "The barrel-shaped egg is Ascaris lumbricoides, and the bumpy round egg is Trichuris trichiura", "C": "The barrel-shaped egg is Enterobius vermicularis, and the bumpy round egg is Ascaris lumbricoides", "D": "The barrel-shaped egg is Trichuris trichiura, and the bumpy round egg is Ascaris lumbricoides"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The barrel-shaped egg is Trichuris trichiura, and the bumpy round egg is Ascaris lumbricoides): Trichuris has barrel-shaped eggs with a plug at each end, while Ascaris eggs are round with a bumpy coat. Pinworm eggs are flat on one side, and hookworm eggs are oval and thin-shelled with a segmented embryo."}, {"id": 1222, "domain": "histology", "question": "A supervisor reviews the slides at the start of the morning before they are sent to the pathologist. Hematoxylin staining on a day's slides is consistently pale, even though the stain time is unchanged. Which cause should be checked, and which corrective step is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Exhausted or old stain, so replace it and confirm the result", "B": "Water carried into xylene, so replace only the xylene on the line", "C": "A blunt blade, so replace the blade on the microtome and recut", "D": "Wax that is too cool, so raise the embedding centre temperature"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Exhausted or old stain, so replace it and confirm the result): Pale hematoxylin comes from exhausted or old stain, or too short a time in the stain, so the stain is refreshed. Water in xylene causes haze, a blunt blade causes lines, and wax temperature does not affect staining intensity."}, {"id": 1223, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A critical value list is being reviewed and a technologist must report a result of INR 6.2 in a patient on warfarin. The laboratory's limit is above 5. Which documentation is required with the critical result report? The result has been confirmed on repeat testing.", "options": {"A": "Time of notification, the receiver's name, and the technologist's own name", "B": "Time of the order, the phlebotomist's name, and the courier's delivery route", "C": "Time of the draw, the analyzer serial number, and the reagent lot number", "D": "Time of the run, the physician's signature, and the patient's family contact"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Time of notification, the receiver's name, and the technologist's own name): Critical results are documented with the time, the name of the person who received it, and the reporter's own name, along with read-back. The other items are not the required communication record."}, {"id": 1224, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "An arterial blood gas has a high pH and a high bicarbonate. Which primary acid-base disorder does this pattern indicate? The patient has been vomiting for several days and has lost stomach acid. The technologist checks the calculated bicarbonate and pH before reporting.", "options": {"A": "Metabolic acidosis, where the pH is lowered and the bicarbonate is greatly raised", "B": "Respiratory acidosis, where the pH is lowered and the pCO2 is greatly reduced", "C": "Metabolic alkalosis, where both the pH and the bicarbonate are raised", "D": "Respiratory alkalosis, where the pH is raised and the bicarbonate is greatly reduced"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Metabolic alkalosis, where both the pH and the bicarbonate are raised): In metabolic alkalosis, pH and bicarbonate are both high. In respiratory alkalosis, pH is high with a low pCO2. The lungs control pCO2 and the kidneys control bicarbonate, so identifying which value moves with the pH points to the primary disorder."}, {"id": 1225, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cell block made from a bloody aspirate shows sheets of red cells with very few diagnostic cells on the section. The pathologist needs immunostains for a lung primary. Which factor most likely explains the poor block and what should the technologist consider?", "options": {"A": "A validated automated system, so replace it with manual clot preparation methods", "B": "Blood contamination hiding the target cells, so review collection and pellet preparation steps", "C": "Excessive antibody dilution, so restain the same section with double the concentration", "D": "An overly thin microtome section, so increase the thickness for all future blocks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Blood contamination hiding the target cells, so review collection and pellet preparation steps): Blood contamination can hide target cells and is a recognized cause of poor blocks, so collection and pellet preparation are reviewed. Section thickness and antibody dilution do not change how many diagnostic cells the block contains, and a validated system is not the fault here. Reviewing the collection and pellet steps addresses the cause rather than the symptom."}, {"id": 1226, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A phlebotomist preparing to draw blood for a DNA test and an RNA transcript test asks which tube to use, since a lavender top, a green top, and a red top with clot activator are on the tray. Which tube is the preferred choice for most molecular blood work?", "options": {"A": "The red top with clot activator, because serum contains less cellular DNA than plasma.", "B": "The green top containing heparin, because it prevents clotting without chelating magnesium ions.", "C": "The lavender top containing EDTA, because it does not inhibit the polymerase.", "D": "The gel separator tube, because it isolates plasma from cells during centrifugation."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The lavender top containing EDTA, because it does not inhibit the polymerase.): EDTA is the preferred anticoagulant for most DNA and RNA work on blood because it does not interfere with amplification. Heparin binds the polymerase and inhibits PCR. Serum tubes and gel separators are not chosen for cellular nucleic acid extraction."}, {"id": 1227, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A laboratory receives a bag of tubes from a remote health centre. The technologist checks the transport package for compliance with Transportation of Dangerous Goods rules for diagnostic specimens before opening it. Which packaging description meets the requirement described for specimens sent between sites?", "options": {"A": "A sealed leak-proof biohazard bag with a separate pocket for the requisition", "B": "An open rack of tubes wrapped in paper towels with the requisition folded inside", "C": "A plain paper bag with the requisition stapled to the outside of the tube caps", "D": "A cooler with loose tubes and the paperwork sealed against the specimen surface"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A sealed leak-proof biohazard bag with a separate pocket for the requisition): Specimens travel in sealed, leak-proof biohazard bags with a pocket that keeps the requisition separate, inside a container that meets Transportation of Dangerous Goods rules for diagnostic specimens. Loose tubes, open racks and paper bags do not contain a leak, and paperwork in contact with specimens can be contaminated."}, {"id": 1228, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cytology student is asked which stain is applied to urine slides prepared by cytocentrifugation, membrane filtration or liquid-based thin-layer processing when the laboratory needs nuclear detail for the Paris System. Slides are fixed in alcohol beforehand. Which stain is used?", "options": {"A": "Congo red stain, applied to air-dried preparations to demonstrate amyloid deposits", "B": "Periodic acid-Schiff stain, applied to alcohol-fixed preparations to highlight glycogen and mucin", "C": "Gram stain, applied to heat-fixed preparations to classify bacteria in the urine", "D": "Papanicolaou stain, applied to alcohol-fixed cytocentrifuge, filter or thin-layer preparations"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Papanicolaou stain, applied to alcohol-fixed cytocentrifuge, filter or thin-layer preparations): Urine slides are stained with the Papanicolaou stain, which shows the nuclear detail needed for reporting. Periodic acid-Schiff is a special stain for glycogen or mucin. Congo red is for amyloid, as in medullary carcinoma. Gram stain is a microbiology stain and does not show urothelial nuclear features."}, {"id": 1229, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A tube of peripheral blood set up for chromosome analysis cracks in the centrifuge, and about 3 mL of unfixed blood spills across the bucket and bench. The technologist has not yet added any fixative to this specimen. Which response best follows safe practice for cytogenetic laboratories?", "options": {"A": "Add methanol and acetic acid to the spill, since fixed blood carries no risk.", "B": "Leave the bucket closed until the end of the shift, then clean it with water.", "C": "Wipe the bench with dry paper towel and continue, since the blood is unfixed.", "D": "Treat it as potentially infectious, contain and disinfect the spill wearing gloves, and report the incident."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat it as potentially infectious, contain and disinfect the spill wearing gloves, and report the incident.): All patient samples are handled as potentially infectious, so a spill is contained and disinfected with protective equipment and recorded as an incident. Dry wiping spreads material and does not disinfect. Fixative does not make a spill safe, and delaying cleanup leaves staff exposed."}, {"id": 1230, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The patient is otherwise stable. A coastal stool specimen is plated on TCBS agar and produces green colonies of oxidase-positive curved gram-negative rods. The colonies do not ferment sucrose. Which organism fits, and which exposure is most consistent with the infection?", "options": {"A": "Vibrio cholerae, linked with seafood and salt water exposure in routine practice", "B": "Vibrio parahaemolyticus, linked with seafood and salt water exposure", "C": "Yersinia enterocolitica, linked with seafood and salt water exposure", "D": "Campylobacter jejuni, linked with seafood and salt water exposure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Vibrio parahaemolyticus, linked with seafood and salt water exposure): Vibrio parahaemolyticus is sucrose negative and forms green colonies on TCBS, linked with seafood. Vibrio cholerae ferments sucrose and gives yellow colonies. Campylobacter and Yersinia do not grow on TCBS as characteristic green colonies."}, {"id": 1231, "domain": "histology", "question": "A recut of a routine block is being done at the microtome by a technologist. A block cuts with a crumbly, shattering texture and the sections fragment as they leave the blade. Working backward through processing, which explanation should the technologist investigate first?", "options": {"A": "A stain that was filtered too many times", "B": "Incomplete dehydration or over-hardening in hot wax during processing", "C": "A water bath that is too warm to hold the section", "D": "Under-processing that leaves the tissue soft and mushy when it is cut"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Incomplete dehydration or over-hardening in hot wax during processing): Tissue that is too hard and shatters is often poorly processed, with incomplete dehydration or over-hardening in hot wax. Mushy tissue is the sign of under-processing, and the water bath and stain filtering do not affect cutting behaviour."}, {"id": 1232, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "Before G-banding, a technologist ages freshly made slides by baking them in an oven or leaving them at room temperature for a period. A trainee asks why this waiting step is done and what it achieves for the banding result. How should the trainee reply?", "options": {"A": "It hardens the chromosomes enough to take the trypsin banding treatment properly", "B": "It swells the chromosomes, which makes them longer before the trypsin step", "C": "It converts the chromosomes to protein, which allows the Giemsa stain to enter", "D": "It removes colcemid from the slide, which would otherwise block every band"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It hardens the chromosomes enough to take the trypsin banding treatment properly): Aging hardens the chromosomes so they respond well to trypsin banding. It does not convert them to protein or remove colcemid, and swelling belongs to the hypotonic step."}, {"id": 1233, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "For quality control of the Gram stain, the laboratory stains a control slide with known organisms at regular intervals. Which pair of control organisms is appropriate for verifying both gram-positive and gram-negative staining? The laboratory tests the stain reagents at set intervals, and the slide must cover both major groups of bacteria in one check.", "options": {"A": "Neisseria meningitidis and Haemophilus influenzae, two fragile fastidious species that need enriched media", "B": "Streptococcus pneumoniae and Campylobacter jejuni, two organisms that need special atmospheric incubation", "C": "Staphylococcus aureus and Escherichia coli, one stable gram-positive and one stable gram-negative species", "D": "Proteus mirabilis and Klebsiella pneumoniae, two gram-negative rods with different urease results"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Staphylococcus aureus and Escherichia coli, one stable gram-positive and one stable gram-negative species): S. aureus is a stable gram-positive control and E. coli a stable gram-negative control, so one slide checks the whole procedure. The other pairs include fragile organisms or two gram-negative rods, which cannot test both sides of the stain."}, {"id": 1234, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A neonate with fever and seizures has cerebrospinal fluid sent for herpes simplex virus testing. Viral culture would take days, and the clinician needs an answer within hours to guide antiviral therapy. Why is PCR the preferred method for this small specimen?", "options": {"A": "It distinguishes latent virus from active viral replication in the brain tissue itself.", "B": "It detects viral nucleic acid rapidly with high sensitivity in small fluid volumes.", "C": "It grows the virus in cell lines faster than routine cultures can in the laboratory.", "D": "It measures antibody titres, which rise within hours of infection in neonates."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It detects viral nucleic acid rapidly with high sensitivity in small fluid volumes.): PCR finds tiny amounts of viral genome in a small volume of cerebrospinal fluid within hours, which allows prompt treatment. It detects nucleic acid, so it cannot distinguish latency from replication. It does not measure antibody, and neonates make a limited response anyway. PCR does not involve growing virus."}, {"id": 1235, "domain": "cytology", "question": "During Papanicolaou screening, a technologist sees small round cells with a granular pale background. The trainee is unsure whether these are HSIL or Candida glabrata, which appears as small round budding yeast without pseudohyphae. Which approach is the most reliable for distinguishing them?", "options": {"A": "Classify every small round cell in a granular background as high-grade dysplasia and report it immediately", "B": "Discard the slide because yeast cannot be separated from dysplasia", "C": "Look for budding yeast outlines and absent nuclear detail rather than squamous nuclear irregularity", "D": "Decide by cell colour alone since both stain the same orange shade"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Look for budding yeast outlines and absent nuclear detail rather than squamous nuclear irregularity): Yeast has a budding outline and no true nucleus, whereas HSIL cells have nuclei with coarse chromatin and irregular membranes. Reporting every small round object as HSIL overcalls, and discarding the slide gives up. Colour alone is unreliable."}, {"id": 1236, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "To concentrate a dilute 100 µL DNA sample, a technologist adds salt and cold ethanol, centrifuges the tube to form a visible pellet, and pours off the supernatant. The procedure then calls for adding 70% ethanol and centrifuging again before removing it. What is the purpose of this final 70% ethanol addition?", "options": {"A": "It resuspends the pellet in water so the DNA concentration can be measured immediately.", "B": "It precipitates any RNA remaining in the tube so it can be removed.", "C": "It denatures nucleases that remain bound to the DNA pellet.", "D": "It washes residual salt from the pellet without dissolving the precipitated DNA."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It washes residual salt from the pellet without dissolving the precipitated DNA.): After precipitation, a 70% ethanol wash removes salts while DNA stays insoluble, and the pellet is then dried and resuspended in buffer. The wash does not resuspend DNA, and it does not target nucleases or RNA."}, {"id": 1237, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A phlebotomy supervisor is choosing a tube for glucose collection when the specimen cannot be separated promptly. The supervisor wants a tube additive that inhibits glycolysis by acting on a specific enzyme, paired with an anticoagulant. Which additive combination fits?", "options": {"A": "Sodium fluoride inhibiting enolase, combined with potassium oxalate to prevent clotting", "B": "EDTA inhibiting glucose oxidase, combined with sodium fluoride to prevent clotting", "C": "Potassium oxalate inhibiting enolase, combined with sodium citrate to bind calcium", "D": "Sodium fluoride inhibiting hexokinase, combined with heparin to prevent clotting"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sodium fluoride inhibiting enolase, combined with potassium oxalate to prevent clotting): Fluoride inhibits enolase in the glycolytic pathway and is combined with potassium oxalate, which prevents clotting. Hexokinase is not the enolase target. Oxalate is the anticoagulant, not the glycolysis inhibitor. Glucose oxidase is an assay reagent enzyme, not a target of EDTA."}, {"id": 1238, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "An investigator reviews unexplained positives across several days and notes that each positive shares the same target sequence as an in-house positive control that was amplified in the same laboratory. The laboratory concludes carry-over rather than true infection. What is amplicon carry-over?", "options": {"A": "Transfer of extracted patient DNA between wells during automated pipetting of a run.", "B": "Contamination of new reactions by amplified product from earlier runs, usually escaping when tubes are opened.", "C": "Persistence of inhibitors on the extraction instrument, blocking amplification in later runs.", "D": "Loss of primers into the pipette tip during dispensing, lowering later reaction yield."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contamination of new reactions by amplified product from earlier runs, usually escaping when tubes are opened.): Amplicon carry-over is contamination by the billions of copies made in earlier reactions, which is why post-PCR products are kept away from clean areas. Well-to-well transfer of patient DNA is cross-contamination of another kind, and primer loss or inhibitors cause false negatives rather than false positives."}, {"id": 1239, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A woman feels a breast lump, and aspiration removes clear, non-bloody fluid. The lump disappears completely after the aspiration, and nothing suggests a solid lesion. The referring physician asks whether cytology of the fluid is still needed. What does standard practice say?", "options": {"A": "Cytology is required, since every cyst fluid must be examined for malignant cells", "B": "A core biopsy is required, since resolved cysts are considered premalignant lesions", "C": "Cytology may not be needed, since the fluid is not bloody and the lump resolved", "D": "Mammography must be repeated within a day, since fluid recurs after aspiration"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cytology may not be needed, since the fluid is not bloody and the lump resolved): When a cyst disappears after aspiration and the fluid is not bloody, cytology may not be necessary. Not every cyst fluid needs examination. Resolved cysts are not treated as premalignant, so a core biopsy is not required. There is no rule to repeat mammography within a day because of fluid recurrence."}, {"id": 1240, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A patient has a peripheral lung mass that lies far from the large airways and is not reachable with a routine bronchoscope wash. The multidisciplinary team wants a cytology sample of this mass. Which technique among those used for respiratory cytology is appropriate here?", "options": {"A": "Fine needle aspiration of the mass, which is used for peripheral lung lesions", "B": "Deep-cough sputum collected on three mornings, which reaches peripheral masses easily", "C": "Bronchial washing with saline, which is the best method for masses without an airway", "D": "Bronchoalveolar lavage in a wedged position, which is used for solid nodules"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fine needle aspiration of the mass, which is used for peripheral lung lesions): Fine needle aspiration is used for peripheral masses beyond the reach of a bronchial wash. Sputum favours central tumours, washing needs a visible airway lesion, and lavage samples alveoli for infection or diffuse disease rather than a solid nodule."}, {"id": 1241, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist begins a manual differential at 100x oil immersion on a smear, and at the current position the red cells are stacked and overlapping so that white cell detail is hard to see. Where on the slide should the cells be counted?", "options": {"A": "Count only at low power to finish the differential faster", "B": "Move toward the feathered edge where red cells just touch", "C": "Stay at the thick tail where cells are most concentrated", "D": "Count along the side edges where large cells tend to settle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Move toward the feathered edge where red cells just touch): Cells are examined in the monolayer, the area near the feathered edge where red cells are evenly spread and just touching, and the count follows a meander pattern. The thick area hides morphology. Cells that settle along the edges give a biased differential. Identification of cells needs 100x, not low power."}, {"id": 1242, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A routine smear from an asymptomatic older adult shows many smudge cells that make the differential hard to classify. The laboratory procedure allows a step before smearing to reduce this artifact. What can be added to the blood before smearing to reduce the smudge cells?", "options": {"A": "A drop of tartrate, to inhibit acid phosphatase activity in the neoplastic cells", "B": "A drop of Prussian blue, to stain iron stores in the developing cells", "C": "A drop of albumin, following the laboratory's own written procedure", "D": "A drop of sodium fluoride, to inhibit nonspecific esterase in the lymphocytes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A drop of albumin, following the laboratory's own written procedure): Adding albumin before smearing can reduce smudge cells, but the technologist should follow the laboratory procedure. Sodium fluoride and tartrate are esterase and phosphatase inhibitors, and Prussian blue is an iron stain, none of which protect fragile cells."}, {"id": 1243, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "Which statement correctly describes phosphate in the blood and factors that affect its measurement? The specimen was collected from a patient with kidney failure, and the tube stood unspun for several hours before it reached the laboratory. Choose the statement that is accurate.", "options": {"A": "Refrigeration falsely raises it, and cortisol rather than PTH regulates it", "B": "Hemolysis and delay falsely lower it, and it moves with calcium under insulin control", "C": "It is stable in whole blood for days and unrelated to calcium or PTH", "D": "Hemolysis and delay falsely raise it, and it moves inversely to calcium under PTH"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hemolysis and delay falsely raise it, and it moves inversely to calcium under PTH): Phosphate is released from red cells, so hemolysis and delayed separation give falsely high values. PTH lowers phosphate by increasing renal excretion while raising calcium, giving the inverse relationship. High phosphate is common in kidney failure."}, {"id": 1244, "domain": "histology", "question": "After staining, a technologist sees that the nuclear detail is obscured because the cytoplasm is a very deep red and the nuclei blend into the background. Hematoxylin and bluing appear correct. Which adjustment should be made for future runs? The staining line has run normally today.", "options": {"A": "Lengthen the hematoxylin time so nuclei become almost black", "B": "Skip the bluing step so that nuclei turn red-purple", "C": "Shorten the eosin time or dilute the eosin", "D": "Use thicker sections so that the eosin looks paler"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shorten the eosin time or dilute the eosin): Eosin that is too intense hides nuclear detail, so time or concentration is reduced. Darkening nuclei will not restore contrast, skipping bluing gives reddish nuclei, and thicker sections tend to look darker and blurrier."}, {"id": 1245, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A stool culture is requested from a patient with abdominal pain and diarrhea after eating undercooked pork. The laboratory suspects Yersinia enterocolitica, an Enterobacterales member that is lactose negative. Which handling feature distinguishes recovery of this organism from the routine stool pathogens grown at body temperature?", "options": {"A": "It grows best with X and V factors and needs chocolate agar for recovery", "B": "It grows only under strict anaerobic conditions and needs charcoal medium for recovery", "C": "It grows best at lower temperatures and needs selective media for recovery", "D": "It grows best at 42 degrees Celsius and needs microaerophilic conditions for recovery"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It grows best at lower temperatures and needs selective media for recovery): Yersinia enterocolitica grows best at lower temperatures and needs selective media. Growth at 42 degrees under microaerophilic conditions describes Campylobacter, and X and V factors belong to Haemophilus. It is a facultative anaerobe, so strict anaerobic conditions are not required."}, {"id": 1246, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A technologist compares group A, B, and O red cells for the quantity of H antigen on the surface. Because only some of the H substance is converted into other antigens by the A or B enzymes, red cell groups differ in how much H remains. Which group carries the greatest amount of H antigen?", "options": {"A": "Group A, since the A enzyme adds a sugar that increases H expression", "B": "Group AB, since both enzymes are present to modify the H substance", "C": "Group O, since no active enzyme converts the H substance", "D": "Group B, since the B enzyme converts most of the H substance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Group O, since no active enzyme converts the H substance): The O gene makes no active enzyme, so all H substance is left as H, and group O cells carry the most. In group AB, both enzymes convert much of the H substance and leave the least. The A and B enzymes each consume H to make their own antigen, so they do not increase it."}, {"id": 1247, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A trainee scoring an interphase FISH slide for a gain reports an abnormal result because many nuclei show three signals. A senior technologist notes that most of these nuclei are touching in clumps and that the smear was thick. What should happen next?", "options": {"A": "Score only intact, separate nuclei because overlap mimics gains, and rescore or repeat the slide", "B": "Increase the cutoff to 50 percent and keep counting the clumps", "C": "Report loss instead, since clumped nuclei hide one signal from the fluorescence filter", "D": "Accept the result, because three signals in touching nuclei reflect a true gain"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Score only intact, separate nuclei because overlap mimics gains, and rescore or repeat the slide): Overlapping nuclei can superimpose signals and mimic gains, so only intact, well-separated nuclei should be counted. Overlap does not hide signals in a consistent way, and adjusting the cutoff to fit poor material is not valid."}, {"id": 1248, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A 60-year-old patient with chronic leukemia has a forward group with a weak 1+ reaction with anti-A and no reaction with anti-B. The reverse group shows strong agglutination of B cells and none with A1 cells. The technologist wonders about the true group. Which is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Incubate the reverse cells at 4 degrees to strengthen the anti-A1 reaction", "B": "Prewarm the reverse cells to 37 degrees Celsius to remove a cold autoantibody", "C": "Enhance the forward test with longer incubation or adsorption and elution to prove weak A", "D": "Replace the serum with saline to disperse rouleaux in the forward test"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Enhance the forward test with longer incubation or adsorption and elution to prove weak A): The reverse group points to group A, and leukemia can weaken antigen expression, giving a group II discrepancy with a weak forward reaction. Longer incubation, anti-A,B, or adsorption and elution helps show the weak antigen. Prewarming and saline replacement address problems that do not fit this presentation."}, {"id": 1249, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A marrow study from an adult with unexplained low blood counts shows an extra copy of chromosome 8 in 14 of 20 metaphases and no other change. The resident asks how this finding is usually described in myeloid disorders and how it should be written in ISCN. Which description and ISCN string match this marrow finding?", "options": {"A": "It is a common recurrent gain, written 47,XX,+8[6]/46,XX[14] for a female patient.", "B": "It is a common recurrent gain, written 47,XX,+8[14]/46,XX[6] for a female patient.", "C": "It is a balanced rearrangement, written 46,XX,t(8;8)[14]/46,XX[6] for a female patient.", "D": "It is a rare recurrent loss, written 45,XX,-8[14]/46,XX[6] for a female patient."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is a common recurrent gain, written 47,XX,+8[14]/46,XX[6] for a female patient.): Trisomy 8 is a common gain in myeloid disorders, and the plus sign with the cell counts in brackets records 14 abnormal and 6 normal cells. A loss would carry a minus sign, and reversing the bracketed numbers misstates the counts. A gain of a whole chromosome is not a balanced translocation."}, {"id": 1250, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cytotechnologist is explaining the three collection approaches to a student. A bronchial brushing is obtained by passing a small brush against the airway wall during bronchoscopy. What type of specimen does this represent according to how the cells are obtained?", "options": {"A": "An exfoliative specimen, collected from cells that shed naturally into fluid", "B": "A concentrated specimen, collected by spinning a fluid onto a glass slide", "C": "An aspirated specimen, collected by drawing cells through a thin needle", "D": "An abrasive specimen, collected by scraping or brushing cells from a surface"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An abrasive specimen, collected by scraping or brushing cells from a surface): Brushing or scraping a surface defines an abrasive specimen. Exfoliative specimens shed on their own, and aspirates come through a needle. Concentration is a laboratory technique rather than a collection category."}, {"id": 1251, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The isolate was confirmed today. A stool sample from a patient with bloody diarrhea arrives, and the request lists Salmonella, Shigella, and Campylobacter culture. An isolate of Salmonella is recovered. Which reporting step applies to the confirmed pathogen beyond reporting to the physician?", "options": {"A": "Report the isolate to the public health authority as required by provincial rules", "B": "Report the isolate only if the patient works in food handling or child care", "C": "Report the isolate to the public health authority only after a second stool has grown it", "D": "Report the isolate only to the laboratory director since stool pathogens are not reportable"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the isolate to the public health authority as required by provincial rules): Enteric pathogens such as Salmonella are reported to the public health authority as required by provincial rules. They are not limited to certain occupations, and confirmation with a second stool is not required."}, {"id": 1252, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A cardiologist telephones about a patient with a variant of uncertain significance in a cardiomyopathy gene and asks to begin predictive testing of the patient's two teenage children on the basis of that result alone. How should the laboratory respond?", "options": {"A": "Agree, because relatives should be tested for every variant found in the panel", "B": "Offer testing of the children for a separate gene without genetic counselling", "C": "Reclassify the variant as likely pathogenic to make cascade testing possible", "D": "Explain that the variant is not a diagnosis, and refer the question to the genetics service"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that the variant is not a diagnosis, and refer the question to the genetics service): A variant of uncertain significance should not be used to make clinical decisions, and this includes cascade testing. Reclassification requires evidence, not convenience. Germline testing of minors also implies family consequences, so pre test counselling and referral to genetics are appropriate."}, {"id": 1253, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Anti-c develops in a patient after several units, and a later pregnancy raises a concern for the fetus. The laboratory manager reviews the general characteristics of Rh system antibodies for a new staff orientation. Which description of Rh antibodies is correct?", "options": {"A": "They are IgG but enzyme treatment destroys them, and they cause no newborn hemolytic disease", "B": "They are IgM, react best in the cold, and cause complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis in adults", "C": "They are IgG, react by IAT, and cause extravascular hemolysis and newborn hemolytic disease", "D": "They are IgA, react at immediate spin, and are not clinically significant"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (They are IgG, react by IAT, and cause extravascular hemolysis and newborn hemolytic disease): Antibodies to D, C, E, c and e are IgG, react by IAT and are enhanced, not destroyed, by enzymes. They can cause extravascular hemolysis and hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn. Cold reactive IgM antibodies describe anti-M or anti-P1 instead."}, {"id": 1254, "domain": "histology", "question": "A pathologist sees dark deposits in a section of a calcified arterial plaque from the media of a coronary artery, and orders a stain to demonstrate calcium salts. The section was cut at 4 µm. Which stain colours calcium deposits black?", "options": {"A": "The Von Kossa silver method, which reacts with calcium salts in tissue", "B": "The Perls Prussian blue method, which reacts with ferric iron in tissue", "C": "The Masson trichrome method, which separates collagen from muscle in tissue", "D": "The Congo red method, which reveals amyloid under polarized light in tissue"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The Von Kossa silver method, which reacts with calcium salts in tissue): Von Kossa colours calcium salts black through silver reaction. Prussian blue detects ferric iron, trichrome shows collagen, and Congo red shows amyloid. Each targets a different component."}, {"id": 1255, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A pathology laboratory receives a formalin-fixed, paraffin-embedded block for FISH testing. Before the probe can be applied to the cut section, the wax and the protein that could block probe access must be dealt with. Which preparation steps do such tissue sections need?", "options": {"A": "Staining the section with DAPI counterstain before denaturation so the probe can locate the nuclei", "B": "Culturing the section for three days in medium with a mitogen to obtain dividing cells", "C": "Freezing the block at -80 degrees Celsius so the wax turns into a gel", "D": "Deparaffinization, pretreatment, and protease digestion so the probe can reach the DNA"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Deparaffinization, pretreatment, and protease digestion so the probe can reach the DNA): Sections must be deparaffinized and pretreated, with protease digestion to expose the nuclear DNA. A section cannot be cultured, and freezing does not remove wax. DAPI is applied after hybridization and washing, not before denaturation, and would not help probe access."}, {"id": 1256, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A technologist finds that a patient's forward and reverse groups do not follow Landsteiner's rule. The sample is properly labelled, and the patient is a 55-year-old woman receiving chemotherapy. Before choosing any special technique, which first steps does the standard approach recommend?", "options": {"A": "Prewarm every tube and reagent and report the group after one repeat", "B": "Perform adsorption and elution with reagent anti-A, then report the reverse group and skip clinical history", "C": "Repeat the test, check the sample identity, and review diagnosis, medications, and transfusion history", "D": "Assign the group from the forward result and finish the investigation later"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat the test, check the sample identity, and review diagnosis, medications, and transfusion history): The first steps are always to repeat the test, confirm identity, and review clinical details such as diagnosis, drugs, age, and transfusion history. These help decide which of the four discrepancy groups applies. Assigning a group from one part of the testing risks an error, and special techniques are chosen only after the cause is understood."}, {"id": 1257, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A transfusion service audits its quality program. One auditor asks how often equipment used for ABO and Rh typing, such as centrifuges, incubators, refrigerators, and pipettes, should be checked. The technologist points to the written procedures. Which statement matches the expected practice?", "options": {"A": "Equipment is calibrated or checked on a defined schedule and results are recorded", "B": "Equipment is checked once at installation and then left unchanged", "C": "Equipment is checked by the supplier's sales representative once each year", "D": "Equipment is checked whenever a technologist suspects a fault with a result"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Equipment is calibrated or checked on a defined schedule and results are recorded): Centrifuges, incubators, refrigerators, and pipettes are calibrated or verified on a schedule, with records kept for review. Waiting for a control to fail lets faulty equipment affect earlier results. A single check at installation does not detect drift, and only a qualified staff check with documentation satisfies quality standards."}, {"id": 1258, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A cerebrospinal fluid specimen for herpes simplex PCR arrives with a note that it sat at room temperature for 36 hours, whereas the procedure allows 24 hours before refrigeration. The tube is labelled and the volume is adequate. The lumbar puncture cannot easily be repeated. What should the technologist do?", "options": {"A": "Test the specimen and report a negative as definitive, since cerebrospinal fluid is normally sterile.", "B": "Discard the specimen silently and record it as not received for quality reasons.", "C": "Test the specimen and add a bleach step to the extraction to sterilize contaminants.", "D": "Notify the provider under the rejection policy, and let the provider decide about a comment-qualified result."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Notify the provider under the rejection policy, and let the provider decide about a comment-qualified result.): A specimen outside its stability limit is handled by the laboratory's rejection policy, and the requesting provider is contacted so the decision is shared and documented. A negative could be false if nucleic acid degraded, bleach would destroy the target, and silent disposal is dishonest reporting."}, {"id": 1259, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A screener finds a squamous abnormality in which the cells are few and small, resemble immature metaplastic cells and have high N:C ratios. The trainee wants to know how the follow-up urgency for an ASC-H result compares with that of an ASC-US result.", "options": {"A": "ASC-H carries a lower risk than ASC-US, so follow-up can wait a year", "B": "ASC-H carries a higher risk of an underlying high-grade lesion, so follow-up is more urgent", "C": "ASC-H carries no risk of high-grade disease, so follow-up is optional for the patient and clinic", "D": "ASC-H carries the same risk as ASC-US, so follow-up is identical"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (ASC-H carries a higher risk of an underlying high-grade lesion, so follow-up is more urgent): Because a high-grade lesion cannot be ruled out, ASC-H has a higher risk than ASC-US and leads to colposcopy and more urgent follow-up. Identical follow-up ignores that risk. Optional or delayed follow-up would risk missing a precancer."}, {"id": 1260, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A 6-year-old child of West African ancestry has a hemoglobin of 70 g/L, mild jaundice and painful swelling of both hands. The blood film is shown in the image. Which laboratory test best confirms the hemoglobin variant involved in this child?", "options": {"A": "Osmotic fragility testing to measure red cell membrane stability in saline", "B": "Hemoglobin electrophoresis or HPLC to identify and quantitate the variant", "C": "Kleihauer-Betke acid elution to count cells containing fetal hemoglobin", "D": "Fluorescent spot testing to detect a deficiency of G6PD in red cells"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hemoglobin electrophoresis or HPLC to identify and quantitate the variant): Elongated, crescent-shaped red cells point to a sickling disorder, and electrophoresis or HPLC identifies and quantitates hemoglobin S and distinguishes disease from trait. Osmotic fragility, Kleihauer-Betke and the G6PD spot test evaluate membrane, fetal cell and enzyme problems, not the hemoglobin variant.", "image_type": "micrograph", "image_params": {"prompt": "Realistic Wright-Giemsa stained peripheral blood smear photomicrograph, 1000x oil immersion, numerous sickle-shaped (crescent) red cells with pointed ends among normal and target red cells, a few polychromatic cells, a few platelets.", "caption": "Peripheral blood film", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1260.png"}, {"id": 1261, "domain": "histology", "question": "An IHC control stains correctly, and the negative control tissue is clean with the same antibody lot, but a patient bone biopsy is negative for several markers. The specimen was decalcified with a strong acid. What is the most reasonable explanation the technologist should bring to the pathologist?", "options": {"A": "Hydrogen peroxide blocking destroys epitopes in bone tissue during the staining run", "B": "The control tissue must have been fixed in a different buffer", "C": "Strong acid decalcification may have damaged epitopes, causing false-negative staining", "D": "Polymer detection cannot be used on any tissue containing calcium"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Strong acid decalcification may have damaged epitopes, causing false-negative staining): Strong acid can damage epitopes, so the patient slide may be negative even when the control works. The pathologist decides how to interpret it. Peroxide blocking and polymer detection are not the cause."}, {"id": 1262, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A cerebrospinal fluid specimen from a febrile adult with a stiff neck and a new rash is Gram stained, as shown in the image, and the culture grows colonies on chocolate agar. The technologist must now subculture and identify the suspect colonies. Which practice best protects staff while the suspect colonies are manipulated?", "options": {"A": "Use a Bunsen burner flame at the open bench to sterilize the loop between plates", "B": "Seal plates with tape and handle them at the open bench wearing a surgical mask", "C": "Work inside a biological safety cabinet, since aerosols can cause laboratory-acquired disease", "D": "Refrigerate subcultures overnight to reduce viability before any identification testing begins"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Work inside a biological safety cabinet, since aerosols can cause laboratory-acquired disease): The image shows gram-negative diplococci in cerebrospinal fluid, which suggests Neisseria meningitidis, a recognized cause of laboratory-acquired infection through aerosols, so manipulations belong in a biological safety cabinet. An open-bench flame and a surgical mask do not control aerosols, and refrigeration does not make Neisseria safer and in fact kills the organism.", "image_type": "micrograph", "image_params": {"prompt": "Realistic Gram stain photomicrograph, 1000x oil immersion, pink-red background, several neutrophils with pink-red Gram-negative diplococci in pairs with flattened adjacent sides (coffee-bean shape), some inside neutrophils, in cerebrospinal fluid sediment, pale pink background.", "caption": "Gram stain of cerebrospinal fluid", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1262.png"}, {"id": 1263, "domain": "cytology", "question": "During workup of a metastatic effusion in the cytology laboratory, A pathologist wants a quick pattern to narrow the possible site of origin of a metastatic adenocarcinoma found in an effusion. Which pair of cytokeratin stains is commonly used together for this purpose in cytology?", "options": {"A": "CK7 and CK20 used together as a pattern", "B": "DAB and hematoxylin used together as a pair", "C": "HPV 16 and HPV 18 used together as genotypes", "D": "Kappa and lambda used together as a ratio"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (CK7 and CK20 used together as a pattern): CK7 and CK20 give a quick staining pattern that narrows the likely primary site. DAB and hematoxylin are the chromogen and counterstain, kappa and lambda are light chains studied by flow cytometry, and HPV types 16 and 18 relate to cervical screening rather than site-of-origin staining. The pattern narrows the likely site of origin but is interpreted together with the clinical history."}, {"id": 1264, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In the operating room the surgeon sends a small fragment of a brain tumour, and the technologist gently squashes it between two slides and stains it within minutes. Smears show stringy fibrillary processes and oval nuclei. The surgeon needs a quick answer. What is the most appropriate interpretation for this smear?", "options": {"A": "A metastatic carcinoma, since cohesive cells with sharp borders form clusters.", "B": "A lymphoma, since dispersed monotonous cells lack cohesion between them.", "C": "A glioma, since fibrillary processes with oval nuclei suggest glial origin.", "D": "A meningioma, since these cells form whorls with psammoma bodies."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A glioma, since fibrillary processes with oval nuclei suggest glial origin.): Stringy fibrillary processes and oval nuclei are the smear pattern of glioma. Meningioma shows whorls and psammoma bodies, metastatic carcinoma shows cohesive cells with sharp edges, and lymphoma shows a dispersed monotonous population. None of those features is described here."}, {"id": 1265, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A glucose result of 2.2 mmol/L arrives from an outpatient sample that sat unspun for six hours on a courier route, and the delta check shows a previous value of 5.1 mmol/L. The patient reported no symptoms at collection. What should the technologist do before phoning it as a critical value?", "options": {"A": "Report the result as final and add a comment that the patient must fast again", "B": "Add fluoride to the tube and reanalyze it immediately to confirm the original value", "C": "Phone the value at once as a critical result, before verifying the sample", "D": "Verify sample age and tube type, then request recollection before calling it critical"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Verify sample age and tube type, then request recollection before calling it critical): A critical value is verified before it is called: the technologist checks the tube type, the sample age, the delta check and the clinical context. A glucose falling in an unspun tube reflects consumption by red cells, so the low value may not be real. Adding fluoride after six hours cannot restore glucose already consumed."}, {"id": 1266, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A trainee reads that FNA of the breast has recognized limitations and asks which cancers are most likely to look benign on smears. The mentor explains that some tumours show cells that are too bland or too ordered to alarm. Which cancers can look benign on FNA?", "options": {"A": "Mucinous carcinoma, because mucin dilutes the cells and hides every nuclear feature", "B": "Tubular and other low-grade cancers, which is a recognized limitation of the technique", "C": "Anaplastic carcinoma of the breast, because its giant cells mimic fibroadenoma stroma", "D": "High-grade ductal carcinoma with necrosis, because necrosis makes cells look degenerated and benign"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tubular and other low-grade cancers, which is a recognized limitation of the technique): Tubular and other low-grade cancers can look benign on FNA, which is a recognized limitation. High-grade ductal carcinoma shows obvious pleomorphism. Giant anaplastic cells do not resemble fibroadenoma stroma. Mucinous carcinoma is recognized by clusters within extracellular mucin, not hidden by it."}, {"id": 1267, "domain": "hematology", "question": "Frozen plasma is sometimes kept for later coagulation testing, and the storage and thawing conditions affect the results. A technologist reviews the laboratory rules for longer term storage of platelet-poor plasma. What are the recommended conditions for storing and thawing frozen plasma?", "options": {"A": "Store at 4 degrees Celsius and thaw slowly in a refrigerator", "B": "Store at minus 70 degrees Celsius and thaw quickly at 37 degrees Celsius", "C": "Store at minus 20 degrees Celsius and thaw slowly at room temperature", "D": "Store at minus 70 degrees Celsius and thaw slowly on the bench"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Store at minus 70 degrees Celsius and thaw quickly at 37 degrees Celsius): Plasma for longer storage is kept at minus 70 degrees Celsius and thawed rapidly at 37 degrees Celsius to preserve factor activity. Storing at higher temperatures or thawing slowly allows factor loss and cryoprecipitate formation. Refrigeration is also unsuitable for coagulation plasma."}, {"id": 1268, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A 66-year-old who stopped menstruating fourteen years ago has a smear collected without hormone therapy, and the requisition notes a dry, thin-looking vaginal mucosa on examination. The epithelium is thin because estrogen levels are low. Which squamous cell types should the technologist expect to dominate the slide?", "options": {"A": "Mainly navicular cells because glycogen builds up as ovarian function declines", "B": "Mainly superficial cells because the epithelium keeps full thickness after menopause", "C": "Mainly endometrial cells because the uterine lining sheds steadily after cycles stop", "D": "Mainly parabasal and intermediate cells because the epithelium is thin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mainly parabasal and intermediate cells because the epithelium is thin): With low estrogen the epithelium does not mature fully, so parabasal and intermediate cells predominate, a pattern called atrophy. Superficial cells reflect estrogen effect. Navicular cells belong to pregnancy, and endometrial cells are not a squamous type."}, {"id": 1269, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A woman aged twenty-six has a cytology result of HSIL, and the clinic asks the laboratory about the usual next step and how urgent it is. Which response is consistent with standard follow-up teaching for this category? Her last screen two years earlier was negative.", "options": {"A": "Repeat the sample in three years because HSIL commonly resolves without any treatment", "B": "Repeat the sample in three years because HSIL commonly resolves", "C": "Return to routine screening if the next HPV test is negative", "D": "Colposcopy with biopsy is arranged, as HSIL is among the most urgent categories"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Colposcopy with biopsy is arranged, as HSIL is among the most urgent categories): HSIL is a true precancer and leads to colposcopy with biopsy, and it is one of the most urgent categories. Repeating the sample in three years delays care, and a negative HPV test would not cancel a confirmed HSIL cytology result."}, {"id": 1270, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A smear shows large Dohle-like inclusions in the neutrophils together with giant platelets in an otherwise well patient. The technologist recognizes an inherited disorder and must choose the correct name for it. Which inherited anomaly best fits this combination of findings on the smear, according to the reference material?", "options": {"A": "May-Hegglin anomaly, which shows large Dohle-like inclusions with giant platelets", "B": "Pelger-Huet anomaly, which shows bilobed neutrophil nuclei with coarse dense chromatin", "C": "Chediak-Higashi syndrome, which shows tiny red granules covering every neutrophil", "D": "Toxic granulation, which shows dark coarse granules during severe bacterial infection"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (May-Hegglin anomaly, which shows large Dohle-like inclusions with giant platelets): May-Hegglin anomaly combines large Dohle-like inclusions with giant platelets. Pelger-Huet involves nuclear shape, and toxic granulation is a reactive finding of infection without giant platelets. The Chediak-Higashi option describes a different inherited disorder."}, {"id": 1271, "domain": "hematology", "question": "At the molecular level, sickle cell disease results from a single point mutation in the beta globin gene. When the hemoglobin loses oxygen, the abnormal molecules polymerize and distort the red cell. Which amino acid change at position 6 creates hemoglobin S?", "options": {"A": "Lysine is replaced by glutamine, which alters the delta chain of hemoglobin A2", "B": "Valine is replaced by glutamic acid, which reverses the normal beta chain sequence", "C": "Glutamic acid is replaced by valine, which drives polymerization of deoxygenated hemoglobin", "D": "Glutamic acid is replaced by lysine, which produces the hemoglobin C variant instead"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Glutamic acid is replaced by valine, which drives polymerization of deoxygenated hemoglobin): Hemoglobin S arises from replacement of glutamic acid by valine at position 6 of the beta chain. Glutamic acid replaced by lysine produces hemoglobin C. The reversed substitution and the lysine glutamine change do not describe hemoglobin S."}, {"id": 1272, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "Results from a complete 24 hour urine collection and a matching serum sample are shown for a stable outpatient seen in clinic for kidney function assessment. Which creatinine clearance and calculation are correct for this patient, before any correction for body surface area, using the units given in the table?", "options": {"A": "0.1 mL/min, from using serum creatinine in umol/L without converting it to mmol/L", "B": "144 mL/min, from using the volume in litres per day instead of mL per minute", "C": "173 mL/min, from multiplying by 1.73 without dividing by the patient's body surface area", "D": "100 mL/min, from urine creatinine times flow per minute divided by serum creatinine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (100 mL/min, from urine creatinine times flow per minute divided by serum creatinine): Urine flow is 1800 mL over 1440 minutes, or 1.25 mL/min, and serum creatinine is 0.100 mmol/L, so clearance is 8.0 x 1.25 / 0.100 = 100 mL/min. The other values come from unit errors in the serum concentration or the volume, or from misusing the 1.73 correction.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "24 hour urine and serum", "columns": ["Test", "Result", "Units", "Reference interval"], "rows": [["Urine creatinine", "8.0", "mmol/L", "n/a"], ["Urine volume, 24 h", "1.8", "L", "n/a"], ["Serum creatinine", "100", "umol/L", "60 to 110"]], "caption": "Creatinine clearance data"}, "image": "question_images/q_1272.png"}, {"id": 1273, "domain": "cytology", "question": "An adequacy statement is being drafted for a 30-year-old's liquid-based slide with abundant squamous cells. The screener finds groups of endocervical columnar cells and small metaplastic cells. Why does the report note whether such a transformation zone component is present?", "options": {"A": "Whether endocervical or metaplastic cells are present shows the sampler reached the junction area", "B": "It replaces the squamous cell count in deciding whether a liquid-based sample is satisfactory for interpretation", "C": "It proves the specimen contains no lesion and that further screening can be delayed", "D": "It shows the patient is postmenopausal and that estrogen therapy should be considered"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Whether endocervical or metaplastic cells are present shows the sampler reached the junction area): Endocervical and metaplastic cells are evidence that the sampler reached the transformation zone, where nearly all precancers begin. Their presence does not exclude a lesion or replace the squamous cell count. They say nothing about menopausal status."}, {"id": 1274, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A thin film from a patient returning from Sarawak shows compact trophozoites in band forms resembling Plasmodium malariae, but the patient has fever, a platelet count of 40 x 10^9/L, and a parasitemia rising from 1 to 5 percent over a day. What should the technologist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Report P. malariae with confidence, since it typically causes very high parasitemia", "B": "Report Babesia, since band forms are the classic sign of that parasite", "C": "Consider Plasmodium knowlesi, which can resemble P. malariae and progress rapidly, and refer for confirmation", "D": "Exclude malaria, since band forms are not a feature of true Plasmodium infections"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Consider Plasmodium knowlesi, which can resemble P. malariae and progress rapidly, and refer for confirmation): P. knowlesi can look like P. malariae yet cause high parasitemia and rapid progression, so confirmation by molecular testing is needed. P. malariae rarely causes severe high-count disease, and band forms are seen in malaria, not Babesia."}, {"id": 1275, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist reading a constitutional peripheral blood case follows the laboratory's validated working standard. She has counted the chromosomes in a set number of cells and now needs to know how many of them are analyzed band by band and how many are fully karyotyped in the routine case. What is the working standard?", "options": {"A": "Count 20 cells, analyze the bands of all 20, and karyotype only 1.", "B": "Count 50 cells, analyze the bands of at least 2, and karyotype at least 5.", "C": "Count 5 cells, analyze the bands of at least 20, and karyotype at least 10.", "D": "Count 20 cells, analyze the bands of at least 5, and karyotype at least 2."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Count 20 cells, analyze the bands of at least 5, and karyotype at least 2.): The common working standard is to count 20 cells, analyze at least 5 by band and produce at least 2 karyotypes. More cells are counted when mosaicism is suspected. The other patterns invert or exaggerate the numbers and do not match usual validated policy."}, {"id": 1276, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A supervisor compares two enzymatic glucose methods and notes that one reads a coloured product after a coupled peroxidase reaction and is more prone to interference. Which method is being described, and what type of substance most commonly causes the interference?", "options": {"A": "Glucose oxidase, with interference from strong reducing substances such as ascorbic acid", "B": "Glucose oxidase, with interference from high concentrations of albumin in serum", "C": "Hexokinase, with interference from cephalosporin antibiotics acting on the coupled step", "D": "Hexokinase, with interference from strong reducing substances such as ascorbic acid"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Glucose oxidase, with interference from strong reducing substances such as ascorbic acid): The glucose oxidase method uses a coupled peroxidase colour reaction and is more affected by strong reducing substances. Hexokinase is the reference method and is less prone to this interference. Albumin and cephalosporins are not the typical interferents for glucose."}, {"id": 1277, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A sequencing report describes a nonsense variant creating a premature stop codon in a tumour suppressor gene, where loss of protein function is the known disease mechanism. How does the type of change contribute to the classification evidence for this variant?", "options": {"A": "A stop codon variant is neutral because the remaining exons keep the protein active", "B": "A stop codon variant is evidence for benign because it appears in every gene", "C": "A variant type that destroys the gene is strong evidence toward pathogenicity", "D": "The variant type is used only to decide which laboratory method to repeat"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A variant type that destroys the gene is strong evidence toward pathogenicity): A change that clearly destroys the gene, such as a nonsense or frameshift variant in a gene where loss of function causes disease, is strong evidence for pathogenicity. It does not leave a working protein behind. Variant type informs classification, not only repeat methods, and stop codons are not a benign marker."}, {"id": 1278, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A surgical tissue biopsy arrives in the microbiology laboratory with a container label that lacks the collection time, and the tissue cannot be collected again. The requisition is otherwise complete. What is the best action for the technologist? The surgeon has already left the operating room.", "options": {"A": "Discard the tissue and wait for the ward to send a repeat sample tomorrow morning", "B": "Process it and hide the labelling problem from the final report", "C": "Reject the tissue and cancel the culture without contacting anyone", "D": "Process the tissue, note the labelling problem on the report, and notify the clinician"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Process the tissue, note the labelling problem on the report, and notify the clinician): Irreplaceable specimens are processed, and the limitation is documented and communicated so a paperwork gap does not erase a result. Rejection or discard would lose an unrecoverable sample, and concealing the problem is unprofessional."}, {"id": 1279, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "After PCR, a technologist runs samples on an agarose gel and includes a lane loaded with fragments of 100, 200, 300, 400, and 500 base pairs. A student asks why that lane is needed. What purpose does this lane serve?", "options": {"A": "It stains the sample DNA so the bands fluoresce under light.", "B": "It provides known sizes so the size of sample bands can be estimated.", "C": "It supplies additional primers so faint bands amplify further during the run.", "D": "It acts as a negative control to detect contamination in the reagents."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It provides known sizes so the size of sample bands can be estimated.): A ladder is a set of fragments of known length, and the sample bands are compared against it to estimate their sizes. It contains no primers, and no amplification occurs during electrophoresis. Staining is done by the gel dye, not the ladder. Contamination is checked with a no-template control amplified in the PCR, not by the ladder."}, {"id": 1280, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist has completed testing on an isolate and the result is unusual: a vancomycin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is suggested by the panel. QC has passed and the identification has been confirmed once. The patient chart shows a request for a preliminary report. What should the technologist do before communicating the result?", "options": {"A": "Release the result immediately as a routine report, since QC has passed on that day", "B": "Verify and confirm the rare phenotype, then communicate it promptly to the clinician and IPAC", "C": "Delete the result and report no growth, because the organism cannot exist clinically", "D": "Wait until the annual antibiogram is prepared, then include the result in the summary"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Verify and confirm the rare phenotype, then communicate it promptly to the clinician and IPAC): Unexpected findings such as vancomycin-resistant S. aureus must be confirmed and communicated before release, since they are critical values with major infection control implications. It is not a routine result and cannot be ignored or delayed until the antibiogram."}, {"id": 1281, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In a molecular pathology briefing at a teaching hospital, A laboratory director explains why alcohol fixation is often useful when a cytology preparation is destined for molecular testing. Which advantage of alcohol fixation is most relevant to DNA-based assays in a cytology sample?", "options": {"A": "It cross-links proteins so the DNA is locked inside the nucleus and stays intact for extraction", "B": "It enhances the brown colour of the chromogen used in routine immunostaining", "C": "It preserves DNA well, unlike prolonged formalin fixation that fragments nucleic acids", "D": "It removes the need to estimate the tumour cell percentage on the slide"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It preserves DNA well, unlike prolonged formalin fixation that fragments nucleic acids): Alcohol fixation preserves DNA, whereas long formalin fixation cross-links and fragments nucleic acids. It does not remove the need to estimate tumour cell content, and it does not affect chromogen colour. Fixation time should stay within the laboratory's validated range regardless of fixative."}, {"id": 1282, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A high-volume transfusion service uses a microplate method in which antigen or antibody is fixed to the walls of each well. A technologist reads the plate after the reaction step and notes different appearances in various wells. What does a positive reaction look like in this method?", "options": {"A": "A single solid clump of cells with a clear supernatant", "B": "A tight button of cells at the centre of the well", "C": "A band of red cells trapped near the top of a column", "D": "A mat of red cells covering the bottom of the well"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A mat of red cells covering the bottom of the well): In solid phase testing, positive reactions spread as a layer across the well, while negative reactions form a compact button. Cells trapped in a column describe gel. A single clump in a clear supernatant describes a tube method 4+ reaction."}, {"id": 1283, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A laboratory manual states the specimen adequacy threshold for a conventional glass smear, which differs from the liquid-based figure. A new technologist asks for the accepted range of well-visualized squamous cells needed for a conventional smear to be called satisfactory.", "options": {"A": "At least 8,000 to 12,000 well-visualized squamous cells on the smear", "B": "At least 3,000 to 4,000 well-visualized squamous cells on the smear", "C": "At least 25,000 to 30,000 well-visualized squamous cells on the smear", "D": "At least 1,000 to 2,000 well-visualized squamous cells on the smear"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (At least 8,000 to 12,000 well-visualized squamous cells on the smear): Conventional smears need about 8,000 to 12,000 well-visualized squamous cells, more than the 5,000 needed for liquid-based slides. Ranges of 1,000 to 4,000 are too low for adequate assessment. Figures around 25,000 are not accepted thresholds."}, {"id": 1284, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A breast aspirate shows cells with abundant granular cytoplasm and round nuclei with a central nucleolus, sitting beside foamy macrophages and flat sheets of ductal cells. The nuclei are uniform and the background is clean. The screener worries the visible nucleoli indicate carcinoma. Which diagnosis best explains these uniform granular cells?", "options": {"A": "Apocrine cells of fibrocystic change, whose round uniform nuclei with central nucleoli should not be overcalled", "B": "Fibroadenoma, since granular cytoplasm and foamy macrophages are hallmarks of its stromal component", "C": "Lobular carcinoma, since abundant cytoplasm with central nucleoli forms signet-ring cells", "D": "Invasive ductal carcinoma, since prominent nucleoli and granular cytoplasm signal malignant cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apocrine cells of fibrocystic change, whose round uniform nuclei with central nucleoli should not be overcalled): Apocrine cells with granular cytoplasm, round uniform nuclei and central nucleoli, alongside foamy macrophages and flat sheets, describe fibrocystic change. Uniformity and clean background argue against carcinoma. Fibroadenoma shows antler-shaped sheets and bipolar nuclei. Lobular carcinoma cells are small, discohesive and often in files."}, {"id": 1285, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A caller who says she is a clinician's assistant phones the cytogenetics laboratory asking for a patient's chromosome result. She is not on the list of authorised recipients, the result is ready, and she insists the matter is urgent. What is the appropriate professional response?", "options": {"A": "Read the result over the phone, since urgent callers are allowed to receive results", "B": "Protect privacy by releasing results only to authorised recipients and referring her to the clinician", "C": "Post the result on the shared corridor board, since staff should be able to check it", "D": "Email the result to the caller after noting the address in the log"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Protect privacy by releasing results only to authorised recipients and referring her to the clinician): Patient privacy is protected at every step and results go only to authorised recipients, so the caller is referred to the responsible clinician. Urgency, unverified email and public posting all breach privacy."}, {"id": 1286, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A patient develops hives and itching during a red cell transfusion but has no airway swelling, low blood pressure or wheezing. The nurse asks what causes this milder type of allergic reaction. Which explanation is correct? Which explanation is correct for this reaction?", "options": {"A": "Cytokines released by white cells stored in the unit", "B": "Complement destruction of ABO-incompatible red cells in the circulation", "C": "Donor antibodies against the recipient's neutrophils in the lung capillaries", "D": "Recipient antibodies against proteins in the donor plasma"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recipient antibodies against proteins in the donor plasma): Mild allergic reactions with hives and itching come from recipient antibodies against donor plasma proteins. Donor antibodies against neutrophils describe TRALI, cytokines from white cells describe febrile reactions, and complement destruction of red cells describes an acute hemolytic reaction."}, {"id": 1287, "domain": "histology", "question": "A laboratory runs an immunohistochemistry stain that requires heat retrieval on sections cut onto plain slides. Several slides show tissue lifting, and the drying oven was turned up very high to help adhesion. What problem might excessive heat cause here?", "options": {"A": "Loss of the accession number written on the frosted end", "B": "Damage to the tissue or to the antigens being tested", "C": "Conversion of eosin into hematoxylin within the section", "D": "Improved binding of the tissue to the plain glass surface"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Damage to the tissue or to the antigens being tested): Too much heat may damage tissue morphology or antigens, harming immunohistochemistry. The better fix is a positively charged or adhesive slide. Heat does not convert one stain into another, and labelling issues depend on the marker used."}, {"id": 1288, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "No Fy(a+) or Fy(b+) cells react with a patient's serum on a panel, and the patient's own red cells type as Fy(a-b-). The technologist reviews the Duffy system before writing the interpretation for the file. Which statement about the Fy(a-b-) phenotype is correct?", "options": {"A": "It results from ficin treatment of the patient's cells and disappears once treatment stops", "B": "It is common in people of African ancestry and may lead to anti-Fy3 after transfusion", "C": "It is found in patients with autoimmune hemolytic anemia and marks a warm autoantibody", "D": "It is common in people of European ancestry and increases susceptibility to malaria"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is common in people of African ancestry and may lead to anti-Fy3 after transfusion): The Fy(a-b-) phenotype is common in people of African ancestry, protects against some forms of malaria, and those patients may make anti-Fy3 after transfusion. It is an inherited phenotype and not an enzyme artefact. It has no link to autoimmune hemolytic anemia."}, {"id": 1289, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The patient ate raw shellfish recently. An abscess fluid from the abdomen is Gram stained and shows gram-negative rods, but the aerobic culture is negative. The anaerobic culture recovers a bile-tolerant organism. Which organism is the most common anaerobe in intra-abdominal infection?", "options": {"A": "Clostridium tetani, a bile-tolerant gram-negative anaerobe found in the gut flora in routine practice", "B": "Bacteroides fragilis, a bile-tolerant gram-negative anaerobe found in the gut flora", "C": "Prevotella species, a bile-tolerant gram-negative anaerobe found in the gut flora", "D": "Cutibacterium acnes, a bile-tolerant gram-negative anaerobe found in the gut flora"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bacteroides fragilis, a bile-tolerant gram-negative anaerobe found in the gut flora): Bacteroides fragilis is bile tolerant and the most common anaerobe in intra-abdominal infection. Cutibacterium acnes is a gram-positive skin organism, Clostridium tetani is a gram-positive spore-former, and Prevotella is not the leading intra-abdominal anaerobe."}, {"id": 1290, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A Gram stain of a colony shows gram-positive cocci in clusters. The technologist then performs a catalase test from a nutrient plate colony, and vigorous bubbling occurs. Which conclusion and next step are most appropriate? A colleague suggests skipping the coagulase test, but the technologist prefers to follow the accepted sequence of tests for gram-positive cocci.", "options": {"A": "Presumptive Pseudomonas, so proceed with an oxidase test on the isolate", "B": "Presumptive Escherichia coli, so proceed with an indole test on the isolate", "C": "Presumptive Staphylococcus, so proceed with a coagulase test to separate species", "D": "Presumptive Streptococcus, so proceed with a PYR test to identify Enterococcus"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Presumptive Staphylococcus, so proceed with a coagulase test to separate species): Clusters and catalase positivity point to Staphylococcus, and coagulase then separates Staphylococcus aureus from coagulase-negative species. Streptococci are catalase negative, and Escherichia coli and Pseudomonas are gram-negative rods that would not match the smear."}, {"id": 1291, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A voided urine from a 70-year-old man with painless hematuria contains urothelial cells, and four of them are shown in the image at equal magnification. Which cell most closely meets the cytologic criteria for high-grade urothelial carcinoma and should be sent to the pathologist as suspicious?", "options": {"A": "Cell 1, because its nuclear size, chromatin and membrane contour match high-grade urothelial carcinoma", "B": "Cell 2, because its nuclear size, chromatin and membrane contour match high-grade urothelial carcinoma", "C": "Cell 3, because its nuclear size, chromatin and membrane contour match high-grade urothelial carcinoma", "D": "Cell 4, because its nuclear size, chromatin and membrane contour match high-grade urothelial carcinoma"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cell 3, because its nuclear size, chromatin and membrane contour match high-grade urothelial carcinoma): Cell 3 has a very high N:C ratio, hyperchromatic chromatin and an irregular nuclear membrane, which are the main criteria for high-grade urothelial carcinoma. Cells 1, 2 and 4 have small to modest nuclei with fine chromatin and smooth outlines, so they look like benign urothelial cells.", "image_type": "cell_micrograph", "image_params": {"cells": [{"label": "1", "nc_ratio": 0.2, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "round"}, {"label": "2", "nc_ratio": 0.15, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "round"}, {"label": "3", "nc_ratio": 0.75, "chromatin": "hyperchromatic", "outline": "irregular", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "round"}, {"label": "4", "nc_ratio": 0.25, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "round"}], "caption": "Cells at equal magnification", "context": "urine", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1291.png"}, {"id": 1292, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A pediatric clinic wants to monitor a child's kidney function, and the current Jaffe creatinine method is known to show interference. Which change would most improve reliability, and what should the technologist tell the clinic about the reason for the change?", "options": {"A": "Switch to a serum albumin method, which directly measures glomerular filtration according to common laboratory workflow", "B": "Switch to a urea method, which avoids interference because it measures muscle mass", "C": "Switch to an enzymatic creatinine method, which avoids Jaffe interference from glucose, ketones and cephalosporins", "D": "Continue the Jaffe method, but require every child to fast for 12 hours first"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Switch to an enzymatic creatinine method, which avoids Jaffe interference from glucose, ketones and cephalosporins): Enzymatic creatinine methods avoid the Jaffe interference and are preferred, especially in children. Urea depends on protein intake and liver function, fasting does not fix Jaffe interference, and albumin is not a filtration measure."}, {"id": 1293, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A blood karyotype reads 46,XX[15]/47,XX,+21[5] for a young girl with mild features. The parents ask what the slash and the numbers in brackets mean and whether all of her cells carry the extra chromosome. Which explanation should the technologist give?", "options": {"A": "Two cell lines exist, and all 20 cells analyzed carry the extra chromosome 21.", "B": "Two cell lines exist, and 5 of 20 cells analyzed carry the extra chromosome 21.", "C": "Two patients' samples were mixed, and 5 cells are from the wrong patient.", "D": "One cell line exists, and 15 of 20 cells analyzed carry the extra chromosome 21."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Two cell lines exist, and 5 of 20 cells analyzed carry the extra chromosome 21.): A slash separates cell lines, and the bracketed numbers give the number of cells in each line, so 15 normal and 5 trisomic cells were seen. Not all cells carry the extra chromosome. The finding is mosaicism from a single zygote, and it does not by itself point to a sample mix-up."}, {"id": 1294, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A technologist wants to explain to a student why hemolysis raises a potassium result. Which statement about the distribution of potassium in the body is correct and most relevant to this problem? The student also asks what happens to the result if the specimen is visibly red after centrifugation.", "options": {"A": "Red cells hold about twenty times more potassium than plasma, so lysis adds potassium", "B": "White cells contain most potassium, so hemolysis has no effect on the reported result", "C": "Plasma contains about twenty times more potassium than red cells, so lysis dilutes the sample", "D": "Red cells and plasma hold equal potassium, so lysis only changes the sample color"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Red cells hold about twenty times more potassium than plasma, so lysis adds potassium): Potassium is the main intracellular cation, and red cells hold about 20 times more than plasma. Hemolysis therefore releases it and raises the result. Diluting effects do not occur, and the color change does not explain the error. Hemolyzed samples should be flagged or recollected."}, {"id": 1295, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient with macrocytic anemia, neurologic symptoms and a positive antibody test has an autoimmune disease of the stomach. Antibodies are directed against parietal cells and a protein required for absorption in the terminal ileum. Which laboratory finding supports pernicious anemia?", "options": {"A": "A low serum folate with normal methylmalonic acid, seen in isolated folate deficiency", "B": "A positive direct antiglobulin test with complement only, seen in cold agglutinin disease", "C": "A positive intrinsic factor antibody test, showing autoimmune loss of vitamin B12 absorption in the ileum", "D": "A raised hemoglobin A2 on electrophoresis, seen in beta thalassemia trait"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A positive intrinsic factor antibody test, showing autoimmune loss of vitamin B12 absorption in the ileum): Intrinsic factor antibodies support pernicious anemia, since intrinsic factor is needed for B12 absorption. A complement positive DAT relates to cold agglutinin disease, a raised A2 relates to beta thalassemia trait, and low folate with normal methylmalonic acid points away from B12 deficiency."}, {"id": 1296, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A patient with chronic myeloid leukemia has been monitored for two years with the same quantitative RT-PCR method on peripheral blood. The laboratory switches to a new method with a different reporting scale for the next result, showing 0.8 percent. What is the main problem with interpreting this value?", "options": {"A": "The result cannot be compared with earlier values without a conversion to the same scale", "B": "Peripheral blood becomes an invalid specimen once a new method is used", "C": "A higher value from the new method means the disease is progressing", "D": "MRD results are only meaningful if measured by digital PCR and not RT-PCR"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The result cannot be compared with earlier values without a conversion to the same scale): MRD monitoring relies on consistency: the same method, specimen type, and reporting scale over time, so that serial results are comparable. A change of method and scale means the values may not line up without conversion. A higher number from a new method does not itself show progression, and peripheral blood remains valid."}, {"id": 1297, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A physician is deciding which prenatal procedure suits a patient who is 11 weeks pregnant with a family history of a chromosome problem, and asks the laboratory about the usual gestational timing of each test. Which pairing correctly states when each procedure is generally performed?", "options": {"A": "Chorionic villus sampling from about 20 weeks, and amniocentesis from about 6 weeks.", "B": "Chorionic villus sampling from about 15 weeks, and amniocentesis from about 10 to 13 weeks.", "C": "Chorionic villus sampling at any stage of pregnancy, and amniocentesis only before 10 weeks.", "D": "Chorionic villus sampling from about 10 to 13 weeks, and amniocentesis from about 15 weeks."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chorionic villus sampling from about 10 to 13 weeks, and amniocentesis from about 15 weeks.): Villus sampling is done earlier, from about 10 to 13 weeks, and amniocentesis from about 15 weeks. Reversing the timing is a common slip. Amniocentesis is not done at 6 weeks, and neither procedure can be done at any stage or only before 10 weeks."}, {"id": 1298, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A laboratory receives a cervical sample where more than three quarters of the squamous cells are hidden by blood and thick inflammation, although about six thousand cells are countable on a liquid-based slide. The technologist debates the reporting decision. What is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Report negative and add a comment about the blood present", "B": "Report satisfactory because six thousand countable cells exceed the minimum, adding a comment about the blood", "C": "Report unsatisfactory because most cells are obscured, giving the reason and requesting repeat testing", "D": "Report ASC-US because obscured cells should be treated as atypical"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report unsatisfactory because most cells are obscured, giving the reason and requesting repeat testing): A sample is unsatisfactory when more than 75% of squamous cells are obscured, even if the raw count seems adequate. The reason is given and the test is repeated, usually after inflammation is treated. Satisfactory and negative reports overstate what can be seen, and obscuring is not atypia."}, {"id": 1299, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A technologist in the specimen preparation area realizes that the pipette on the bench belongs to the post-PCR area, since it carries a different coloured label. The reaction plate for the run has not been set up yet. What is the correct response?", "options": {"A": "Use it only for reagents, not specimens, and return it at the end of the shift.", "B": "Use it after wiping the shaft with ethanol, since ethanol destroys amplicon.", "C": "Remove it, use the dedicated pipette of that area, and report the misplaced equipment.", "D": "Use it with filter tips, since tips prevent contamination of the pipette body"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove it, use the dedicated pipette of that area, and report the misplaced equipment.): Each area has dedicated pipettes so that amplicon never enters clean areas, and equipment from post-PCR is not used in pre-PCR. Ethanol and filter tips reduce but do not remove risk, and limiting use to reagents would still contaminate them. The misplacement is reported so the cause can be corrected."}, {"id": 1300, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A molecular laboratory requests a lung adenocarcinoma cell block for EGFR testing. The cytology laboratory notes that the block was made with a different fixative than usual and worries about DNA quality. What rule about fixatives and later molecular tests should the technologist follow?", "options": {"A": "Use any alcohol fixative, since all fixatives preserve nucleic acids equally well.", "B": "Fix in unbuffered formalin for several days, since longer fixation improves results.", "C": "Follow a validated procedure, since different fixatives can affect later molecular testing.", "D": "Skip fixing the pellet altogether, since formalin removes DNA from every cell."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Follow a validated procedure, since different fixatives can affect later molecular testing.): Fixatives influence DNA and RNA quality, so the laboratory uses a validated procedure that has been shown to work with its molecular tests. Fixatives are not all equivalent. An unfixed pellet would autolyze, and prolonged unbuffered fixation degrades nucleic acids."}, {"id": 1301, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A woman aged forty has an atypical glandular cell result on liquid-based cytology and reports no symptoms. The clinic asks whether follow-up can be delayed until her next scheduled screen. Which reply best matches the risk associated with this category?", "options": {"A": "No, but only because AGC represents invasive cervical cancer in every case", "B": "No, AGC needs prompt colposcopy because significant rates of high-grade or glandular disease are found", "C": "Yes, AGC is a reactive finding that resolves in almost every patient", "D": "Yes, AGC has a lower risk than ASC-US and can wait for routine screening"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No, AGC needs prompt colposcopy because significant rates of high-grade or glandular disease are found): A substantial share of women with AGC have high-grade squamous lesions, adenocarcinoma in situ, or cervical or endometrial cancer, so follow-up is prompt. It is not simply reactive, and it does not mean cancer in every case. It is followed up more aggressively than ASC-US."}, {"id": 1302, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A specimen reports a hemoglobin of 150 g/L but a hematocrit of 0.60 L/L. A technologist applies the rule of three as a quick consistency check on the two values before reporting the CBC. What does this comparison suggest and what should be done?", "options": {"A": "The hemoglobin is too high, so dilute the sample with saline and repeat", "B": "The hematocrit is expected, so report after recalculating only the MCV", "C": "The hematocrit conflicts with the hemoglobin, so look for interference first", "D": "The values agree, since hematocrit is normally about four times the hemoglobin"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The hematocrit conflicts with the hemoglobin, so look for interference first): The hematocrit in percent should be roughly three times the hemoglobin in g/dL, so 150 g/L predicts about 0.45 L/L, not 0.60. A large gap points to interference such as lipemia or a cold agglutinin, or to a mislabelled specimen, and must be resolved first. The multiplier of four is wrong. Recalculating the MCV would carry the error forward."}, {"id": 1303, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "The sodium result in the case meets the laboratory critical value policy, so the technologist must decide whether it is real before calling the physician. Using the measured osmolality and the osmolality calculated from sodium, glucose and urea, how should the low sodium be classified before it is accepted?", "options": {"A": "True hypotonic hyponatremia, because measured and calculated osmolality are both low and agree", "B": "Pseudohyponatremia, because the measured osmolality is normal and lipids falsely lower the sodium result", "C": "Hyperglycemic translocational hyponatremia, because high glucose draws water out of cells into plasma", "D": "Specimen dilution from drip contamination, because glucose and urea fall in the same proportion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (True hypotonic hyponatremia, because measured and calculated osmolality are both low and agree): The calculated osmolality is about 2 x 118 + 5.2 + 4.0 = 245 mmol/kg, close to the measured 248, so the low sodium reflects a real hypotonic state. The serum is clear and the glucose is normal, which excludes lipid interference and glucose related water shifts, and a diluted sample would not leave glucose and urea normal.", "cluster_id": "2", "cluster_text": "A 67-year-old woman from a long-term care home is brought in after a fall and new confusion. Her sodium is 118 mmol/L (135 to 145), glucose 5.2 mmol/L, urea 4.0 mmol/L and measured serum osmolality 248 mmol/kg (280 to 300). Urine osmolality is 480 mmol/kg and urine sodium is 62 mmol/L. The serum is clear, the tube was properly collected, no recent intravenous fluids were given, and analyzer quality control was acceptable. The laboratory critical value policy lists sodium below 120 mmol/L.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1304, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "The technologist reviews the case results before releasing a critical sodium value to the physician. Considering the urine osmolality and urine sodium in the case, which cause of the low serum sodium is most consistent with the findings in this patient, who has a clear specimen, a normal glucose and no recent intravenous fluids?", "options": {"A": "Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion, with concentrated urine despite low serum osmolality", "B": "Primary polydipsia, with maximally dilute urine and a very low urine sodium concentration", "C": "Central diabetes insipidus, with large volumes of dilute urine and a raised serum sodium", "D": "Uncontrolled diabetes mellitus, with osmotic diuresis and a markedly raised serum glucose result"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion, with concentrated urine despite low serum osmolality): Urine that stays concentrated at 480 mmol/kg while serum osmolality is low, with urine sodium above 40 mmol/L, fits inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion. Polydipsia would give dilute urine, diabetes insipidus raises serum sodium, and uncontrolled diabetes would show a high glucose.", "cluster_id": "2", "cluster_text": "A 67-year-old woman from a long-term care home is brought in after a fall and new confusion. Her sodium is 118 mmol/L (135 to 145), glucose 5.2 mmol/L, urea 4.0 mmol/L and measured serum osmolality 248 mmol/kg (280 to 300). Urine osmolality is 480 mmol/kg and urine sodium is 62 mmol/L. The serum is clear, the tube was properly collected, no recent intravenous fluids were given, and analyzer quality control was acceptable. The laboratory critical value policy lists sodium below 120 mmol/L.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1305, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "The sodium result is confirmed after checking the sample and quality control. According to the critical value policy in the case, what is the most appropriate laboratory action for this result from a long-term care resident, before the report is finalized and the next specimen is processed?", "options": {"A": "Hold the result until the next scheduled batch review, then add a comment to the report", "B": "Repeat the test on a diluted sample and release the average of the two values obtained", "C": "Release the result with an automatic flag only, because the policy covers hospital inpatients", "D": "Notify the responsible clinician immediately by telephone with read-back, and document the time and recipient"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Notify the responsible clinician immediately by telephone with read-back, and document the time and recipient): A sodium of 118 mmol/L is below the policy limit of 120, so it is a critical value that needs immediate verbal communication with read-back and documentation. Delaying for a batch, averaging diluted repeats, or relying on a flag alone would leave the patient result uncommunicated and break the policy.", "cluster_id": "2", "cluster_text": "A 67-year-old woman from a long-term care home is brought in after a fall and new confusion. Her sodium is 118 mmol/L (135 to 145), glucose 5.2 mmol/L, urea 4.0 mmol/L and measured serum osmolality 248 mmol/kg (280 to 300). Urine osmolality is 480 mmol/kg and urine sodium is 62 mmol/L. The serum is clear, the tube was properly collected, no recent intravenous fluids were given, and analyzer quality control was acceptable. The laboratory critical value policy lists sodium below 120 mmol/L.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1306, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A peripheral lung adenocarcinoma is sampled by fine needle aspiration in a non-smoker, and only a limited amount of material remains after slide preparation. The oncologist will need molecular testing to guide targeted treatment. What is the best way to handle the remaining material?", "options": {"A": "Discard the remaining material because morphology alone directs all treatment decisions", "B": "Keep enough material, often as a cell block, so molecular testing can be performed", "C": "Air-dry the remaining material on slides because molecular tests require dried smears", "D": "Use all the remaining material for extra stains and leave none for molecular tests"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep enough material, often as a cell block, so molecular testing can be performed): Adenocarcinoma samples are tested for molecular changes that direct targeted therapy, so enough material, often a cell block, must be saved. Discarding it or exhausting it on stains prevents testing, and dried smears are not the preferred material."}, {"id": 1307, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A panel pipeline detects single nucleotide variants and small indels but has not been validated for copy number changes. A patient's hereditary cancer panel returns no variants. What should the laboratory state in the report so the negative result is not over-interpreted?", "options": {"A": "That copy number variants were not assessed, so a negative result does not exclude them", "B": "That the panel excludes all variants in the listed genes, including copy number changes", "C": "That copy number variants are absent because none were called by the pipeline for this patient", "D": "That copy number variants are absent because none were called by the pipeline"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (That copy number variants were not assessed, so a negative result does not exclude them): A statement of limitations protects the reader from over-interpreting a negative, and knowing what a test cannot see is part of interpretation. A pipeline that never looked for copy number changes cannot report their absence. Limitations belong in the report itself."}, {"id": 1308, "domain": "histology", "question": "A pathologist reviews a slide stained with Verhoeff iron hematoxylin and ferric chloride differentiation, with no separate control, and notices elastic fibres in a normal vessel wall stained correctly. What term describes normal structures on the patient slide that confirm the stain worked?", "options": {"A": "A reagent blank prepared from buffer and reagents without any tissue", "B": "A validation panel of known tissues assembled before a new method starts", "C": "A proficiency sample sent from an outside laboratory for comparison", "D": "An internal control, using normal structures already present on the same slide"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An internal control, using normal structures already present on the same slide): Normal structures on the same slide, such as elastic fibres in a vessel wall, act as an internal control. A reagent blank, proficiency sample and validation panel are separate quality tools with different purposes."}, {"id": 1309, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "An amplicon based panel finds no variants in a gene at a position where the patient's sibling carries a known pathogenic single base change. Review shows the patient has a common polymorphism under one forward primer binding site at that position. What explains the missed variant?", "options": {"A": "Allele dropout, since a variant under a primer can prevent amplification of that allele", "B": "Hybrid capture failed, because probes cannot bind to a region containing a polymorphism", "C": "A dideoxynucleotide was incorporated, since it terminated all extension at that position", "D": "Poor Phred scores at every base, since the run had low cluster density"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Allele dropout, since a variant under a primer can prevent amplification of that allele): Amplicon methods can miss variants under a primer because that allele may fail to amplify, which is called allele dropout. This is a known limit of the approach. The scenario describes an amplicon panel, so hybrid capture is not involved, and general poor quality would not explain a single missed position."}, {"id": 1310, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist prepares thick and thin films from an EDTA sample for suspected malaria and stains both with Giemsa at pH 7.2. The physician asks why both are needed, and a student wrongly assumes the thin film is more sensitive because its red cells are intact. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "The thick film keeps red cells intact for species identification and parasitemia calculation", "B": "The thick film is the most sensitive screen, and the thin film identifies the species", "C": "Both films are equally sensitive, so the thick film is prepared only for backup", "D": "The thin film is the most sensitive screen, and the thick film identifies the species"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The thick film is the most sensitive screen, and the thin film identifies the species): The thick film concentrates the blood about twenty times and lyses the red cells, which makes it the most sensitive screen. The thin film keeps red cells intact, so it is used to identify the species and calculate parasitemia. Reversing the roles or calling the films equal misstates how they are used."}, {"id": 1311, "domain": "histology", "question": "A histology supervisor wants to catch processor problems before they reach the microtome, and the laboratory currently changes reagents by feel and has had two unexplained runs with brittle tissue. Which practice best supports long-term monitoring of reagent changes and quality drift?", "options": {"A": "Change reagents only when a pathologist complains about the quality of the slides.", "B": "Use a fresh set of reagents for each run, so that no log is required.", "C": "Keep a processor log of reagent changes and alarms, plus a control block each run.", "D": "Record reagent changes in each technologist's notebook, without a shared record of the runs."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep a processor log of reagent changes and alarms, plus a control block each run.): A shared log of reagent changes, run times and alarms, with a control tissue block cut and stained each run, shows trends and lets the team trace a fault to a date and a reagent. Waiting for complaints means bad slides are already reported. Personal notebooks cannot be audited, and replacing every reagent each run is wasteful and still leaves no record."}, {"id": 1312, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A marrow smear from a patient with suspected monocytic leukemia is stained with two esterase methods, one specific and one nonspecific. The blasts show strong nonspecific esterase that disappears when sodium fluoride is added. Which lineage does this stain pattern support?", "options": {"A": "Granulocytic lineage, since nonspecific esterase is fluoride-inhibited in neutrophil precursors", "B": "Erythroid lineage, since nonspecific esterase is strongly positive in normoblasts", "C": "Monocytic lineage, since nonspecific esterase is fluoride-inhibited in monocytic cells", "D": "Lymphoid lineage, since nonspecific esterase is strongly positive in all lymphoblasts"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Monocytic lineage, since nonspecific esterase is fluoride-inhibited in monocytic cells): Fluoride-inhibited nonspecific esterase positivity supports monocytic lineage. Granulocytes are stained by the specific esterase method instead. Lymphoblasts and erythroid precursors are not identified by this stain pattern."}, {"id": 1313, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "An EDTA blood sample for a viral load test is grossly hemolysed and icteric. The extraction went smoothly and yields look normal, but the internal control Ct is four cycles later than in the run's other specimens. Which explanation is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Hemolysis lowers the viral load, so the internal control amplifies later for that reason.", "B": "Hemoglobin breakdown products and bilirubin carried into the eluate are inhibiting amplification.", "C": "Hemolysed cells release RNase, which specifically degrades the internal control but spares viral target.", "D": "Icterus raises the fluorescence baseline, which shifts the threshold to later cycles."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hemoglobin breakdown products and bilirubin carried into the eluate are inhibiting amplification.): Hemolysis and bilirubin can carry inhibitors through extraction, and a raised internal control Ct in one specimen signals inhibition. The internal control is independent of viral load, and a baseline shift or a selective RNase effect is not how these substances act."}, {"id": 1314, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In a cytology quality program, A rescreening program includes a review of negative slides from patients who have a prior abnormal result or a clinical concern raised by the clinician. Which type of rescreen does this describe within a cytology quality program?", "options": {"A": "A targeted rescreen of negatives from patients with a high-risk history", "B": "A proficiency test sent by an external program with unknown slides", "C": "A random rescreen of a fixed percentage of all negatives", "D": "A rapid prescreen that separates normal from suspicious slides quickly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A targeted rescreen of negatives from patients with a high-risk history): Targeted rescreening reviews negative slides from patients with a high-risk history or clinical concern. Random rescreening samples a percentage of negatives, rapid prescreening is a quick sorting look, and proficiency testing uses unknown slides supplied by an external program. Any abnormal finding goes to a pathologist for final decision."}, {"id": 1315, "domain": "cytology", "question": "At the end of the week in a hospital laboratory, A pathologist asks a technologist to audit who has opened a specific patient's cytology record over the past month because a colleague suspects inappropriate access. Which LIS feature makes this review possible in the laboratory system?", "options": {"A": "An audit trail recording who viewed or changed each record", "B": "A shared password used by every member of the department", "C": "An interface that transfers results to the clinician automatically", "D": "A two-identifier check performed at each specimen receipt step"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An audit trail recording who viewed or changed each record): Audit trails record who viewed or changed a record, which allows suspected inappropriate access to be reviewed. Shared passwords defeat accountability, interfaces move results between systems, and two-identifier checks reduce patient misidentification but do not log who accessed the file. Audit trails also discourage inappropriate access, because users know their activity is recorded."}, {"id": 1316, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A technologist reading a case study learns that a patient's abnormal plasma proteins and a plasma expander given during surgery led to false agglutination in a tube test. The student is asked which findings in the patient can cause this problem, which belongs to group III discrepancies. Which patient findings explain this false agglutination?", "options": {"A": "Weak subgroups of A or B and mixed-field reactions after transplant", "B": "Hypogammaglobulinemia and weak antibody production in newborns and elderly patients", "C": "High globulin or fibrinogen levels and the use of plasma expanders", "D": "Cold autoantibodies and unexpected alloantibodies in the reverse cells"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (High globulin or fibrinogen levels and the use of plasma expanders): Group III discrepancies arise from protein or plasma abnormalities such as high globulin or fibrinogen and plasma expanders, which cause rouleaux. Weak subgroups belong to group II. Weak antibodies belong to group I, and cold autoantibodies or alloantibodies belong to group IV."}, {"id": 1317, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A CBC on a stable outpatient shows a WBC of 3.1 x10^9/L, and the analyzer attaches a suspect flag for blasts while the differential lists 4 percent immature cells. No previous result is available for comparison. What should the technologist do?", "options": {"A": "Release the analyzer differential because the flag is only advisory", "B": "Phone the physician to report leukemia before examining any smear", "C": "Rerun the sample in citrate to clear the blast flag", "D": "Make a smear and hold the result until it has been reviewed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Make a smear and hold the result until it has been reviewed): A flag is a request to look, not a diagnosis, so the smear is examined and the result held until the review is done, in line with the laboratory's review criteria. Releasing the differential skips the check the flag demands. Citrate does not clear a scatter flag. Reporting leukemia before seeing any cells is an unverified diagnosis."}, {"id": 1318, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A karyotype from a child with a short stature and learning problems shows a chromosome that has formed a circle after both ends broke and rejoined, with loss of the tips. The trainee asks what this structural abnormality is called and how it would be written in ISCN. Which structure and ISCN symbol fit this finding?", "options": {"A": "An isochromosome, written with the symbol i in ISCN.", "B": "A marker chromosome, written with the symbol mar in ISCN.", "C": "A ring chromosome, written with the symbol r in ISCN.", "D": "A derivative chromosome, written with the symbol der in ISCN."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A ring chromosome, written with the symbol r in ISCN.): A circle formed after both ends break and join is a ring chromosome, with the symbol r. An isochromosome has two identical arms, a derivative chromosome results from a rearrangement between chromosomes, and a marker is an unidentified extra chromosome."}, {"id": 1319, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "Testing a tumour sample for amplification of an oncogene with a locus-specific probe and a centromeric reference probe, the technologist sees nuclei with many more orange gene signals than green centromere signals. Why is a reference probe on the same chromosome included?", "options": {"A": "It shows whether the gene copies rise relative to the chromosome, separating amplification from extra chromosomes", "B": "It confirms the slide was counterstained, because both colours bind DAPI", "C": "It shows how long the hybridization ran, which sets the signal ratio between the two probes", "D": "It labels the nuclei outline, which makes overlapping cells easier to identify and count"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It shows whether the gene copies rise relative to the chromosome, separating amplification from extra chromosomes): A ratio of gene signal to centromere signal shows true amplification, whereas a gain of the whole chromosome raises both equally. The reference probe does not measure time, outline nuclei, or confirm counterstaining, since DAPI is a separate blue dye."}, {"id": 1320, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist compares nitric acid with formic acid for decalcifying routine bone. The supervisor notes that one acid removes mineral quickly but can damage cell detail, while the other is slower and gentler on nuclei. Which pairing correctly describes these two acids?", "options": {"A": "Nitric acid is a chelating agent that binds calcium ions; formic acid works by dissolving mineral.", "B": "Nitric acid is slow and gentle; formic acid is fast and often damages cell detail.", "C": "Nitric acid is fast but can damage detail; formic acid is slower and gentler.", "D": "Both acids work at the same speed, but nitric acid preserves nucleic acids far better."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Nitric acid is fast but can damage detail; formic acid is slower and gentler.): Strong mineral acids such as nitric and hydrochloric work quickly at some cost to nuclear staining, whereas formic acid is slower, gentler and a common choice for routine bone. Neither acid preserves nucleic acids well. Chelation is the mechanism of EDTA, not of nitric acid, and both acids remove calcium by dissolving its salts."}, {"id": 1321, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A pregnant patient is on low molecular weight heparin, and the physician requests monitoring with the anti-Xa assay. The nurse asks when the blood should be drawn relative to the dose. When should the sample be collected? Timing of the sample is important for interpreting the peak effect.", "options": {"A": "About twenty-four hours after the dose, at the expected trough", "B": "About four hours after the dose, at the expected peak", "C": "Immediately after the dose, at the expected onset", "D": "Immediately before the dose, at the expected trough"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About four hours after the dose, at the expected peak): LMWH is monitored with anti-Xa about four hours after the dose, at peak. Trough or immediate sampling would not reflect the peak drug effect the physician needs."}, {"id": 1322, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A peripheral blood flow cytometry panel on a patient with splenomegaly and fine-projection lymphocytes is reviewed. The pathologist expects a marker that is typical of hairy cell leukemia, along with a positive TRAP stain. Which marker is typically positive in this leukemia?", "options": {"A": "CD103, which is typically positive in hairy cell leukemia", "B": "CD14, which is typically positive on the majority of lymphoid cells in blood", "C": "CD34, which is typically positive on mature circulating lymphocytes only", "D": "CD138, which is typically positive on circulating hairy cell leukemia cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (CD103, which is typically positive in hairy cell leukemia): Hairy cell leukemia cells are typically TRAP positive and CD103 positive. CD34 marks immature cells, CD138 marks plasma cells, and CD14 marks monocytic cells, so none of these is characteristic of the disease."}, {"id": 1323, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A patient asks why cervical cancer screening can prevent cancer when the virus is so common among sexually active people. The technologist explains how long a persistent high-risk infection typically takes to progress to invasive cancer. Which statement reflects the accepted timeline?", "options": {"A": "Progression takes about twenty-four hours once the virus enters the basal cells of the cervix", "B": "Most infections progress to invasive cancer within one to two years", "C": "Cancer usually appears within a few weeks of the first infection", "D": "The steps commonly take ten or more years, allowing precancer to be found and treated"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The steps commonly take ten or more years, allowing precancer to be found and treated): Progression from persistent infection to precancer to cancer usually takes ten or more years, which is why regular screening can find and treat precancer first. Most infections clear within one to two years and never progress. Cancer does not appear within weeks."}, {"id": 1324, "domain": "histology", "question": "A laboratory audits four breast core biopsy blocks submitted for estrogen receptor testing, and the fixation log appears in the image. The pathologist wants to know which block gives the least reliable biomarker result. Which block is it, and why is the result in doubt?", "options": {"A": "Block 4413, because cold ischemia time exceeded the limit validated for biomarker testing", "B": "Block 4415, because formalin fixation ran longer than biomarker testing can tolerate", "C": "Block 4412, because the formalin fixation time was too short to preserve hormone receptors", "D": "Block 4414, because formalin fixation was far shorter than the validated minimum time"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Block 4414, because formalin fixation was far shorter than the validated minimum time): Block 4414 had only two hours in formalin, well below the validated minimum of about six hours, so under-fixation explains the weak result and makes it unreliable. Thirty-five minutes of cold ischemia and thirty hours of fixation are both acceptable, and eighteen hours in formalin is adequate to preserve receptor antigens.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Breast biopsy fixation log", "columns": ["Block", "Cold ischemia (min)", "Formalin (h)", "ER result"], "rows": [["4412", "20", "18", "POS"], ["4413", "35", "24", "POS"], ["4414", "25", "2", "Weak POS"], ["4415", "40", "30", "POS"]], "caption": "Fixation log for four breast core biopsies"}, "image": "question_images/q_1324.png"}, {"id": 1325, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A prenatal culture shows a 46,XX karyotype and the mother is also female. A colleague suggests this proves the analysed cells are fetal and that no further check for maternal cell contamination is needed before reporting. How should the technologist respond?", "options": {"A": "Agree that molecular testing is not needed, since one culture is enough", "B": "A sex difference can give a clue, but matching sex cannot exclude maternal cells", "C": "Agree that a female result proves fetal origin, since maternal cells fail to grow", "D": "Disagree because male results are required, since female fetuses cannot be studied"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A sex difference can give a clue, but matching sex cannot exclude maternal cells): A sex difference between mother and fetus is only a clue, and with matching sex it cannot exclude maternal cells, so multiple cultures and molecular methods are used. Maternal cells can grow, and female fetuses can be studied."}, {"id": 1326, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "An emergency department physician telephones the transfusion service about a bleeding patient and asks the technologist to test an unlabelled tube found at the bedside, saying the tube came from that patient a moment ago. No one saw the draw, and the patient is now being resuscitated. How should the technologist answer the physician's request for testing?", "options": {"A": "Test the tube and issue group O red cells based on the physician's word", "B": "Test the tube and hold the results until the physician signs the requisition", "C": "Decline to test the tube and ask for a new, properly labelled sample", "D": "Test the tube and have the physician add a label to it after results"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline to test the tube and ask for a new, properly labelled sample): An unlabelled tube cannot be traced to a patient, so it is rejected regardless of urgency, and a properly collected sample is requested while emergency-release group O red cells are supplied as local policy allows. Labelling after testing or on a physician's verbal word defeats the identification rules. Testing the tube first wastes time and creates results for an unknown person."}, {"id": 1327, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A patient has ASC-US and a positive reflex high-risk HPV result. Her physician asks whether to repeat the cytology in a year or refer her. Considering the standard algorithm and the risk carried by a positive HPV result, what is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Refer for colposcopy, since a positive high-risk HPV result with ASC-US needs further evaluation", "B": "Repeat cytology in one year since ASC-US is a category of uncertainty", "C": "Return to routine screening because ASC-US with HPV is expected in adults", "D": "Begin excision treatment directly, since a positive HPV result confirms a high-grade cervical lesion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer for colposcopy, since a positive high-risk HPV result with ASC-US needs further evaluation): ASC-US with a positive high-risk HPV result leads to colposcopy. A one-year repeat would ignore the added risk. Excision without biopsy is not indicated because HPV positivity does not itself prove a high-grade lesion."}, {"id": 1328, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A transfusion service keeps records of a patient's historical ABO and Rh types and of clinically significant antibodies found years earlier. A new technologist asks how long these long-term records are kept compared with samples and cell segments. What retention period applies to these historical records?", "options": {"A": "They are kept for exactly 3 days, matching the sample validity window", "B": "They are kept far longer under provincial and national standards", "C": "They are discarded after 7 days along with the tested sample", "D": "They are discarded once the patient's current group has been confirmed twice"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (They are kept far longer under provincial and national standards): Historical types and significant antibodies are retained much longer than samples, because a patient may need to be compared with their own history years later. The 7-day and 3-day figures refer to sample retention and sample age. Confirming a group twice does not remove the need for the long-term record."}, {"id": 1329, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The patient is a young adult. A technologist is teaching a student the first steps used to sort gram-negative rods that grow on MacConkey agar. Which two properties define the Enterobacterales as a group before lactose fermentation is used for the first branching step?", "options": {"A": "They ferment glucose and are oxidase negative, and they grow readily on MacConkey agar", "B": "They do not ferment glucose and are oxidase negative, and they grow readily on MacConkey agar", "C": "They ferment glucose and are oxidase positive, and they grow readily on MacConkey agar", "D": "They do not ferment glucose and are oxidase positive, and they grow readily on MacConkey agar"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They ferment glucose and are oxidase negative, and they grow readily on MacConkey agar): The Enterobacterales are facultative anaerobes that ferment glucose and are oxidase negative. Nonfermenters such as Pseudomonas are oxidase positive and do not ferment glucose, which is why oxidase is a useful early test."}, {"id": 1330, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A jaundiced patient with obstructive liver disease and a raised alkaline phosphatase has a blood film showing the red cells in the image. Which mechanism best explains the shape of these red cells in this patient, given the liver disease?", "options": {"A": "Unpaired globin chains precipitate and are pitted from the cells by the spleen", "B": "Excess membrane cholesterol and phospholipid increase surface area relative to cell volume", "C": "Loss of membrane skeleton proteins reduces surface area relative to cell volume", "D": "Oxidized hemoglobin forms inclusions that damage the membrane during splenic passage"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Excess membrane cholesterol and phospholipid increase surface area relative to cell volume): Target cells have a central dark spot ringed by pallor, and in obstructive liver disease the membrane takes up extra cholesterol and phospholipid, enlarging surface area relative to volume. Globin precipitation, skeletal protein loss and oxidized hemoglobin produce other abnormalities.", "image_type": "micrograph", "image_params": {"prompt": "Realistic Wright-Giemsa stained peripheral blood smear photomicrograph, 1000x oil immersion, a field dominated by classic target cells (codocytes): at least fifteen red cells each with a clearly dark purple-pink central spot, a surrounding ring of pale clear halo, and a darker outer rim, like a bullseye; a few normal red cells and occasional platelets.", "caption": "Peripheral blood film", "quality": "high"}, "image": "question_images/q_1330.png"}, {"id": 1331, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A test slide shows metaphases in which the chromosomes lie in crowded overlapping clumps that cannot be separated for analysis. The cell membranes are still intact and none of the cells appear burst, and the hypotonic step was only five minutes on this run rather than the usual twenty. Identify the probable cause and the fix.", "options": {"A": "Under-swelling, so the hypotonic time should be lengthened slightly on the next attempt", "B": "Old fixative, so the potassium chloride should be replaced with fresh methanol", "C": "Excess trypsin exposure, so the banding time should be lengthened on the next attempt", "D": "Over-swelling, so the hypotonic time should be lengthened even further on the next attempt"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Under-swelling, so the hypotonic time should be lengthened slightly on the next attempt): Crowded chromosomes with a short hypotonic step point to under-swelling, corrected by extending the time slightly. Over-swelling scatters chromosomes and would call for a shorter time. Trypsin and fixative age affect banding and spreading differently."}, {"id": 1332, "domain": "histology", "question": "A pathology assistant explains that glutaraldehyde has a special purpose in the laboratory and is not the usual routine fixative for surgical specimens. It is stored in small sealed ampoules in the refrigerator. Which application is glutaraldehyde specifically used for in histology and pathology?", "options": {"A": "Preparing tissue for electron microscopy, which needs very fine preservation of structure.", "B": "Clearing tissue after dehydration, which requires a solvent that mixes with wax.", "C": "Decalcifying dense bone samples, which need a chelating agent to bind calcium.", "D": "Fixing routine surgical specimens before processing, which is where formalin is unsuitable."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Preparing tissue for electron microscopy, which needs very fine preservation of structure.): Glutaraldehyde cross-links proteins rapidly and preserves ultrastructure, so it is the standard primary fixative for electron microscopy. It penetrates slowly and gives poor antigen retention, which is why routine surgical tissue stays in formalin. Decalcifiers are acids or chelators, and clearing agents are solvents such as xylene."}, {"id": 1333, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "An arterial sample was iced for a two-hour delay to preserve gas values, and the requesting unit also asks the laboratory to report potassium and ionized calcium from the same syringe. The laboratory has not validated iced samples for those analytes. What should the technologist do?", "options": {"A": "Report the blood gas values, and decline potassium and ionized calcium from the iced syringe", "B": "Report all analytes, since cooling improves stability of electrolytes in whole blood", "C": "Reject the whole syringe, since ice makes every arterial gas result unusable", "D": "Warm the syringe to body temperature for an hour and then report all analytes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the blood gas values, and decline potassium and ionized calcium from the iced syringe): Iced samples are suitable for gases when the delay is long, but cold impairs the sodium-potassium pump and shifts potassium and ionized calcium, so those analytes are not reported from an iced sample unless the laboratory has validated it. Ice does not make gases unusable, and warming a syringe for an hour would let cells resume metabolism and change the gas values."}, {"id": 1334, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A hospital laboratory uses a continuous-monitoring blood culture instrument that flags positive bottles around the clock. What does this instrument measure to detect microbial growth in a bottle? Staff are relying on this instrument to alert them at any time of day or night, including weekends and holidays.", "options": {"A": "The level of oxygen in the laboratory room, which falls when many bottles are loaded", "B": "Carbon dioxide production by growing organisms, which the instrument senses every few minutes", "C": "The colour of the Gram stain in the broth, which the instrument reads automatically", "D": "The temperature difference between adjacent bottles, which rises when organisms multiply there"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Carbon dioxide production by growing organisms, which the instrument senses every few minutes): Growing microbes produce carbon dioxide, which the instrument senses to flag a bottle as positive. It does not perform Gram stains, compare bottle temperatures, or measure room oxygen."}, {"id": 1335, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A nipple discharge smear shows cohesive papillary clusters with fibrovascular cores and bland cells in a bloody background. There is no marked nuclear atypia, and the discharge came from a single duct. The clinician asks which lesion this pattern suggests. Which breast lesion best fits this discharge smear?", "options": {"A": "Mucinous carcinoma, suggested by cell clusters floating in abundant extracellular mucin", "B": "Duct ectasia, suggested by foamy macrophages and debris with no epithelial clusters", "C": "Fibrocystic change, suggested by apocrine cells and foamy macrophages in a clean background", "D": "Intraductal papilloma, suggested by bland cohesive papillary clusters with fibrovascular cores"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Intraductal papilloma, suggested by bland cohesive papillary clusters with fibrovascular cores): Cohesive papillary clusters with fibrovascular cores and bland cells in blood suggest intraductal papilloma. Fibrocystic change and duct ectasia are recognized by apocrine cells, macrophages and debris rather than papillary clusters. Mucinous carcinoma is defined by clusters floating in extracellular mucin."}, {"id": 1336, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A chronically transfused patient needs extended antigen-matched units. The patient's own red cells were typed today for Kidd and Duffy, but the patient received two units three days ago, and the results show mixed-field reactions. Which statement best describes the problem and the solution?", "options": {"A": "Recent transfusion has no effect on typing, so the mixed-field reactions should be ignored", "B": "Donor cells mask the true phenotype, so use a pretransfusion sample or separated patient cells", "C": "The mixed field is caused by enzyme treatment, so typing must be repeated using untreated antisera", "D": "The mixed field proves the patient is a chimera, so donor typing should replace patient typing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Donor cells mask the true phenotype, so use a pretransfusion sample or separated patient cells): A recently transfused patient's phenotype can be hidden by donor cells, so the typing must be done on a pretransfusion sample or on separated patient cells to be accurate. Mixed-field reactions here reflect two red cell populations, not chimerism or enzymes. Ignoring them would give an unreliable phenotype for matching."}, {"id": 1337, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Thyroid FNA smears are made gently to avoid crush artifact, and slides are prepared for different stains depending on the laboratory's protocol. A student is asked how the smears are handled before staining for the Romanowsky method compared with the Papanicolaou method. Which pairing is standard?", "options": {"A": "Heat-fixed for the Gram stain and air-dried for the Congo red stain", "B": "Air-dried for the Pap stain and alcohol-fixed for Romanowsky stains", "C": "Formalin-fixed for Romanowsky stains and alcohol-fixed for hematoxylin only", "D": "Air-dried for Romanowsky stains and alcohol-fixed for the Pap stain"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Air-dried for Romanowsky stains and alcohol-fixed for the Pap stain): Some thyroid smears are air-dried for Romanowsky stains while others are alcohol-fixed for the Pap stain. Reversing the pairing ruins morphology because air-drying spoils Pap nuclear detail. Gram and Congo red are not the routine thyroid stains here. Formalin fixation of smears for Romanowsky staining is not the standard method."}, {"id": 1338, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Red cells are needed for a patient with cold agglutinin disease. The cold antibody is anti-I, and thermal amplitude testing shows strong reactions at 37 degrees Celsius. Prewarmed IAT testing still reacts with the patient's serum. Compared with a weak cold antibody, what does this finding mean for management?", "options": {"A": "The reaction is a false positive from weak washing, so the prewarmed test should be repeated", "B": "The antibody is clinically significant, and the units should be given through a blood warmer", "C": "The antibody is insignificant, since anti-I is IgM and does not cause hemolysis in the body", "D": "The antibody must be removed by alloadsorption before any crossmatching can be attempted"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The antibody is clinically significant, and the units should be given through a blood warmer): Cold autoantibodies matter clinically when they are strong at 37 degrees Celsius, which the thermal amplitude shows, and patients with significant cold agglutinins may receive blood through a warmer. Calling every IgM antibody harmless ignores the thermal amplitude. Alloadsorption addresses warm autoantibodies and hidden alloantibodies, and the prewarmed result is not explained by washing."}, {"id": 1339, "domain": "cytology", "question": "During a demonstration of the ThinPrep system, the trainer measures the cell spot on a slide. Students are asked to state the approximate diameter of the circular cell area produced by this membrane-based method. The trainee measures the spot with a small transparent ruler, and the answer must be the approximate value. What is the approximate diameter?", "options": {"A": "About 5 mm, giving a dense and heavily overlapping cluster of cells", "B": "About 20 mm, giving a thin and evenly spread layer of cells", "C": "About 13 mm, giving the round spot typical of the density gradient method", "D": "About 40 mm, giving a scattered and widely separated layer of cells"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 20 mm, giving a thin and evenly spread layer of cells): ThinPrep produces a circular spot about 20 mm across, while the SurePath spot is about 13 mm. The other sizes do not match either system."}, {"id": 1340, "domain": "histology", "question": "A section of decalcified femoral head tears the microtome knife and leaves score marks across the ribbon. Gritty fragments can be felt on the block face when the technologist runs a probe over it. What is the most likely cause of this cutting problem?", "options": {"A": "Under-decalcification, where mineral remains in the tissue and nicks the knife.", "B": "Over-decalcification, where too much acid has left the tissue soft and washed out.", "C": "Under-fixation, where the tissue was not fixed and has begun to putrefy in the block.", "D": "Wax contamination, where clearing agent has weakened the paraffin around the tissue."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Under-decalcification, where mineral remains in the tissue and nicks the knife.): Gritty tissue that scores the knife still contains calcium salts, so decalcification was incomplete and the block should be returned to acid and re-tested. Over-decalcification gives pale nuclei and poor staining, not hard grit. Contaminated wax makes crumbling sections, and under-fixation gives mushy tissue, neither of which leaves mineral fragments."}, {"id": 1341, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A toxicology section separates volatile compounds in a blood sample using a chromatography method in which an inert gas carries the sample through the column. The technologist explains a requirement of this method to a student. What must be true of compounds analyzed by gas chromatography?", "options": {"A": "They must be vaporized so the carrier gas can move them through the column", "B": "They must be labelled with an enzyme before the separation begins", "C": "They must carry an electric charge so they migrate through the column", "D": "They must be dissolved in a liquid that is forced through the column at high pressure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They must be vaporized so the carrier gas can move them through the column): In gas chromatography the mobile phase is a gas, so compounds have to be in vapour form to travel with it. Charged migration describes electrophoresis. A liquid forced through at high pressure describes HPLC, and enzyme labelling belongs to immunoassays."}, {"id": 1342, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A research collaborator supplies DNA prepared by phenol-chloroform extraction from 200 µL of blood, reports an excellent yield, and asks why the diagnostic laboratory now prefers column or bead methods. Which statement best describes the trade-off between organic extraction and the newer methods for routine clinical work?", "options": {"A": "Organic extraction gives high yield but uses hazardous chemicals and is now uncommon clinically.", "B": "Organic extraction is faster than columns, but it cannot separate proteins from nucleic acids well.", "C": "Organic extraction gives lower yield and needs a magnet to separate the aqueous and organic layers.", "D": "Organic extraction needs no lysis step, but it damages RNA more than columns do."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Organic extraction gives high yield but uses hazardous chemicals and is now uncommon clinically.): Phenol and chloroform separate proteins from nucleic acid effectively and give high yield, but the chemicals are hazardous and the method is hard to automate, so it is now uncommon in clinical work. It does not require magnets, and it does need cell lysis."}, {"id": 1343, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "An infection prevention team member asks which organisms require airborne precautions in a negative pressure room. The laboratory provides a list to update the isolation policy. Which group of organisms is correctly matched to airborne precautions? The list will be added to the hospital isolation policy.", "options": {"A": "MRSA, VRE, and Clostridioides difficile, managed with gown and gloves", "B": "ESBL producers and carbapenemase-producing organisms, managed with routine practices alone", "C": "Tuberculosis, measles, and varicella, managed in a negative pressure room", "D": "Influenza and Neisseria meningitidis, managed with a mask and droplet precautions"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tuberculosis, measles, and varicella, managed in a negative pressure room): Airborne precautions with a negative pressure room apply to tuberculosis, measles and varicella. MRSA, VRE, C. difficile, ESBL producers and CPO need contact precautions, and influenza and meningococcus need droplet precautions."}, {"id": 1344, "domain": "histology", "question": "A tumour in a breast biopsy has a validated HER2 result that varies between two blocks from the same case, the first block giving 2+ and the second 0 with the same antibody lot. One block sat in formalin for the recommended time and the other was under-fixed. How should fixation be handled?", "options": {"A": "Ignore fixation for predictive markers because controls correct any differences", "B": "Shorten formalin fixation to a few minutes, allowing deeper antibody penetration and much stronger staining", "C": "Control fixation time, since under-fixation and over-fixation both reduce staining of predictive markers", "D": "Extend all fixation to several weeks so staining becomes stronger and reliable"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Control fixation time, since under-fixation and over-fixation both reduce staining of predictive markers): Predictive tests such as HER2 depend on controlled fixation because too little or too much reduces staining. Very long fixation does not improve results, and controls cannot correct fixation error in the patient tissue."}, {"id": 1345, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A radiograph shows a destructive sclerotic lesion of the distal femur in a teenager. Smears show pleomorphic malignant cells embedded in pink, glassy matrix that the technologist identifies as tumour bone. What is this matrix called and what tumour does it indicate?", "options": {"A": "Chondroid matrix, which is produced by cartilage-forming cells of chondrosarcoma.", "B": "Osteoid, which is produced by malignant bone-forming cells of osteosarcoma.", "C": "Colloid, which is produced by thyroid follicular cells of papillary carcinoma.", "D": "Mucin, which is produced by glandular cells of metastatic adenocarcinoma."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Osteoid, which is produced by malignant bone-forming cells of osteosarcoma.): Pink osteoid is tumour bone matrix and suggests osteosarcoma. Chondroid matrix belongs to cartilage tumours, colloid to the thyroid, and mucin to glandular cells. The clinical and radiologic setting must be known for a bone lesion."}, {"id": 1346, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "In an automated chemistry analyzer, a robotic arm delivers small volumes of reagent and sample into reaction vessels, which are held at a controlled temperature before each reading. What temperature is usually maintained in the incubator of a chemistry autoanalyzer during the reaction?", "options": {"A": "25 degrees Celsius, matching the usual room temperature of the laboratory", "B": "4 degrees Celsius, the temperature used to preserve reagents", "C": "56 degrees Celsius, the temperature used to inactivate complement", "D": "37 degrees Celsius, to keep reaction conditions constant"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (37 degrees Celsius, to keep reaction conditions constant): Enzyme and other reaction rates depend on temperature, so analyzers hold reactions at 37 degrees Celsius. Room temperature varies through the day and would make results unstable. The 56 degree value belongs to serology heat inactivation, and 4 degrees is a storage temperature."}, {"id": 1347, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A hematology student is asked to list conditions where a central dark spot surrounded by a pale ring, with a rim of hemoglobin outside it, is seen on the smear. These cells look like a bullseye. Which disorders commonly produce these codocytes?", "options": {"A": "Thalassemia, liver disease, hemoglobin C and the period after splenectomy", "B": "Malaria, babesiosis, polycythemia vera, lead poisoning and cold agglutinin disease", "C": "Megaloblastic anemia, myelofibrosis, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia and marrow infiltration by cancer", "D": "Hereditary spherocytosis, warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, G6PD deficiency and oxidant drug exposure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Thalassemia, liver disease, hemoglobin C and the period after splenectomy): Target cells (codocytes) occur in thalassemia, liver disease, hemoglobin C and after splenectomy. Spherocytes and bite cells belong to the other groups of hemolytic disorders. Macro-ovalocytes, teardrop cells and fragments define the megaloblastic, myelofibrotic and mechanical groups."}, {"id": 1348, "domain": "histology", "question": "It is a busy afternoon with many biopsies received. Two patients had core biopsies received on the same afternoon. A slide from one patient carries tissue that clearly belongs to the other. What classification and response does good quality practice require?", "options": {"A": "Ask the pathologist to reinterpret the slide as a different patient without notification", "B": "Replace the label and continue since no result has been issued", "C": "Treat it as a minor staining issue and recut the slide silently", "D": "Treat it as an incident, report it factually, and investigate the root cause"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat it as an incident, report it factually, and investigate the root cause): A wrong tissue on a slide is a mislabel and serious incident, reported promptly and investigated for root cause. Silent recuts and relabelling hide the error, and reinterpreting the slide without notification risks patient harm."}, {"id": 1349, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A technologist compares two calibration schemes on a chemistry analyzer. One uses a blank and a single calibrator, and the other uses six calibrators of increasing concentration to plot a curve for a method whose response is not perfectly straight. Which description matches a multi-point calibration?", "options": {"A": "A single calibrator with a blank defines a straight line through zero", "B": "Several calibrators of different concentrations are used to draw a curve", "C": "Control materials at three levels replace calibrators for defining the curve", "D": "Patient samples with known results are used to adjust the curve daily"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Several calibrators of different concentrations are used to draw a curve): A multi-point calibration uses several calibrators to define the curve, which suits methods that are not perfectly linear. A blank plus one calibrator is a one-point calibration. Controls check a calibration and do not define it, and patient samples are not used to build the curve."}, {"id": 1350, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Following a long weekend delay, A cell block sits in formalin over a long holiday weekend before processing, well beyond the laboratory's validated fixation time, and is then requested for an EGFR mutation panel. What is the most likely concern with this sample?", "options": {"A": "Loss of the paraffin, so the block cannot be sectioned", "B": "Improved antigen retrieval that will cause background staining on every slide", "C": "An excess of tumour cells that dilutes the normal DNA in the extract", "D": "Cross-linked and fragmented nucleic acids that may cause a failed or unreliable assay"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cross-linked and fragmented nucleic acids that may cause a failed or unreliable assay): Extended formalin exposure cross-links and fragments nucleic acids, so the fixation time must stay within the validated range. Tumour excess does not harm an assay, retrieval is unrelated to nucleic acid quality, and paraffin is added after fixation during processing. Fixation time should stay within the laboratory's validated range for any specimen meant for molecular work."}, {"id": 1351, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A new bottle of xylene arrives without a supplier label, and a technologist wants to decant some into a labelled dish. The safety officer explains which system requires hazard labelling and access to information on each chemical. Which workplace system covers this requirement?", "options": {"A": "WHMIS, with labelled containers and safety data sheets available to staff", "B": "CLSI, with a written procedure filed under each reagent name", "C": "GLP, with a signed log kept only by the laboratory manager", "D": "TDG, with placards and shipping documents attached to every container"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (WHMIS, with labelled containers and safety data sheets available to staff): WHMIS requires hazard labelling and safety data sheets for workplace chemicals. Transportation of Dangerous Goods applies to shipping, and the other terms do not govern workplace chemical labelling."}, {"id": 1352, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A hematologist sending a sample for cytogenetic study of suspected acute leukemia asks which specimen gives the best chance of a useful karyotype. The laboratory notes that circulating blast counts are low, and asks the technologist to recommend a specimen type. What is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "Urine sediment, because it contains circulating cells of the abnormal clone.", "B": "Peripheral blood, because it contains more dividing cells than the marrow does.", "C": "Bone marrow, because it contains many dividing cells of the abnormal clone.", "D": "Buccal swab, because it contains dividing cells from every tissue of the body."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Bone marrow, because it contains many dividing cells of the abnormal clone.): Marrow is the preferred specimen because it holds many dividing cells, and blood is used only when many blasts are circulating, which is not the case here. Urine and buccal cells do not contain the leukemic clone and are not routine karyotyping specimens."}, {"id": 1353, "domain": "histology", "question": "A student technologist is about to receive fresh unfixed specimens from the operating room, none of which carry any infection warning. Which principle should guide how the student handles every one of these specimens, regardless of what is known about the patient?", "options": {"A": "Fresh specimens are sterile until fixed, so protective equipment is needed only after fixation begins", "B": "Protective equipment is required only when cutting on the cryostat, and not during receipt or grossing", "C": "Routine practices: treat every specimen as possibly infectious, using gloves, coat, and eye protection", "D": "Extra precautions apply only to specimens labelled as infectious, with ordinary gloves used for all others"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Routine practices: treat every specimen as possibly infectious, using gloves, coat, and eye protection): Routine practices, also called standard precautions, treat every specimen as though it could be infectious. Relying on labels alone misses unrecognized infections. Fresh tissue is not sterile, and exposure risk exists at receipt and grossing, not only at the cryostat."}, {"id": 1354, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A patient with new anemia has an antibody screen performed in gel as shown in the image, including the autocontrol. No transfusion history is available and the patient is not taking any known medications. Which conclusion is best supported by these reactions?", "options": {"A": "A warm autoantibody is likely, so a DAT and further workup precede any crossmatch", "B": "A single alloantibody to a low-prevalence antigen is likely, so crossmatching proceeds with random units", "C": "A cold agglutinin with anti-I specificity is likely, so the screen is repeated at room temperature", "D": "Reagent contamination is likely, so the screen is repeated using a fresh vial of cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A warm autoantibody is likely, so a DAT and further workup precede any crossmatch): Reactions with every screening cell and the autocontrol suggest an autoantibody, typically a warm one at the antiglobulin phase, so a DAT and further workup come first. A low-prevalence alloantibody would not react with all screening cells, and contamination is unlikely when the pattern is consistent.", "image_type": "gel_agglutination", "image_params": {"wells": [{"label": "Screen cell I", "reaction": "2+"}, {"label": "Screen cell II", "reaction": "2+"}, {"label": "Screen cell III", "reaction": "2+"}, {"label": "Autocontrol", "reaction": "2+"}], "caption": "Gel antibody screen with autocontrol"}, "image": "question_images/q_1354.png"}, {"id": 1355, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "While reviewing the previous shift's records, a technologist finds a panel signed off as anti-Fya although two clinically significant antibodies were never ruled out. No units have been issued yet. Which response best reflects Canadian standards and the technologist's professional duty in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Issue Fy(a-) units now, since the finding of anti-Fya is probably correct anyway", "B": "Wait for the day shift to decide, and leave the result unchanged until then", "C": "Correct the panel sheet quietly, since documenting the error would only delay patient care", "D": "Hold issue, complete the missing rule-outs, document the discrepancy, and inform the supervisor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold issue, complete the missing rule-outs, document the discrepancy, and inform the supervisor): Every result must be traceable and every discrepancy documented, and the workup is not finished until all significant antibodies are identified or excluded. Holding issue while the rule-outs are completed protects the patient. Quiet correction removes the audit trail and leaving the result unchanged risks harm."}, {"id": 1356, "domain": "hematology", "question": "After a heel puncture on an infant, the technologist is holding a used lancet in one hand and a paper towel soaked with blood in the other. She must dispose of both correctly before labelling the microtube. Which action is the safe practice?", "options": {"A": "Leave the lancet on the tray until the collection round is finished", "B": "Wrap the lancet in the blood-stained paper towel and discard it in general waste", "C": "Recap the lancet carefully with one hand, then put it in the regular trash", "D": "Drop the lancet into a sharps container at once, without recapping"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Drop the lancet into a sharps container at once, without recapping): Used lancets go straight into a sharps container, without recapping, and specimens and contaminated materials are treated as biohazards with gloves worn. Recapping and general waste both risk needlestick injuries to staff and waste handlers. Leaving a lancet on the tray creates a hazard for the next person."}, {"id": 1357, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A blood gas analyzer has three sensors for pH, pCO2 and pO2. A trainee asks how the partial pressure of carbon dioxide is measured, expecting a design different from the sensor used for pH. Which description of the pCO2 electrode is correct?", "options": {"A": "A pH electrode modified with a gas-permeable membrane and bicarbonate solution", "B": "A valinomycin membrane that binds carbon dioxide molecules selectively", "C": "An amperometric electrode measuring current from carbon dioxide reduction at a cathode", "D": "A solid membrane of silver chloride responding to dissolved carbon dioxide"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A pH electrode modified with a gas-permeable membrane and bicarbonate solution): Carbon dioxide crosses the gas-permeable membrane, changes the pH of the bicarbonate solution, and the pH electrode reads that change. The amperometric design is the oxygen sensor. Valinomycin binds potassium, and the solid silver chloride design responds to chloride."}, {"id": 1358, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The child is otherwise well. A throat swab from a child with pharyngitis grows beta-hemolytic colonies with a wide zone. A bacitracin disk shows a zone of inhibition around it. The technologist adds a PYR test. Which result confirms Streptococcus pyogenes?", "options": {"A": "A negative PYR reaction, which together with bacitracin susceptibility identifies group A streptococcus", "B": "A positive CAMP reaction, which together with bacitracin susceptibility identifies group A streptococcus", "C": "A positive hippurate reaction, which together with bacitracin susceptibility identifies group A streptococcus", "D": "A positive PYR reaction, which together with bacitracin susceptibility identifies group A streptococcus"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A positive PYR reaction, which together with bacitracin susceptibility identifies group A streptococcus): Group A streptococcus is beta hemolytic, bacitracin susceptible, and PYR positive. Hippurate hydrolysis and CAMP belong to group B. A negative PYR would not support group A, so the positive PYR is the confirmatory match."}, {"id": 1359, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "Trichrome-stained cysts about 12 micrometres across, each with four nuclei and small central karyosomes, look like Entamoeba histolytica on a stool smear from a patient with mild loose stools and no blood. The physician needs to know whether this is the pathogenic species. What is the appropriate laboratory approach?", "options": {"A": "Report the complex and confirm by antigen or PCR, since microscopy cannot separate E. dispar", "B": "Report Giardia lamblia directly, since four nuclei with a face-like pattern are typical on trichrome", "C": "Report a non-pathogenic amoeba directly, since four nuclei confirm Entamoeba dispar by microscopy", "D": "Report pathogenic amoebiasis directly, since four nuclei confirm the pathogenic species by microscopy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the complex and confirm by antigen or PCR, since microscopy cannot separate E. dispar): E. histolytica and E. dispar are morphologically identical, so antigen or PCR testing confirms the pathogen. Giardia trophozoites have two nuclei with a face-like look."}, {"id": 1360, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A hematology clinic reviews why a patient does not respond to platelet transfusions. The team lists both immune and non-immune explanations. Which of the following is a recognized non-immune cause of platelet refractoriness? His count rises very little after each pooled unit.", "options": {"A": "Fever, infection, active bleeding, splenomegaly or DIC in the recipient.", "B": "Anti-HLA class I antibodies directed against donor platelet antigens.", "C": "Antibodies against HPA platelet-specific antigens acquired through prior pregnancy.", "D": "Group A platelets given to a group O recipient."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fever, infection, active bleeding, splenomegaly or DIC in the recipient.): Fever, infection, bleeding, splenomegaly, DIC and certain drugs reduce platelet response without antibodies. Anti-HLA and platelet-specific antibodies are immune causes. ABO-incompatible platelets can reduce increments, but that is a matching issue, not a classic non-immune cause."}, {"id": 1361, "domain": "histology", "question": "A piece of tissue has just been removed from the decalcifier after a clear end point test with ammonium oxalate. The technologist is preparing to load the cassette into the processor for the overnight run. What should be done to the tissue before it enters processing?", "options": {"A": "Place it directly into absolute alcohol so that the residual acid evaporates quickly.", "B": "Return it to fresh decalcifier overnight to confirm that no mineral remains.", "C": "Wash or neutralize it thoroughly to remove residual acid before processing begins.", "D": "Dry it on absorbent paper so that no fluid carries over into the processor."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wash or neutralize it thoroughly to remove residual acid before processing begins.): Residual acid left in the tissue damages staining and carries into processor reagents, so tissue is washed in running water or placed in a neutralizing solution first. Alcohol will not evaporate acid out of tissue. Drying the tissue would distort it, and more decalcifier after a clear test only over-decalcifies."}, {"id": 1362, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A pharmacogenomics report for a patient starting a new antidepressant lists CYP2D6 with two alleles of no function, and the laboratory also lists the resulting metabolizer category. Which pair of elements should the report present so that a prescriber can use the result safely?", "options": {"A": "The drug dose, calculated by the laboratory for the patient", "B": "The phenotype only, since genotypes cannot be reported for enzyme genes", "C": "The genotype and the predicted phenotype, with the guidance source cited", "D": "The genotype alone, with the laboratory leaving phenotype interpretation to the ordering physician"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The genotype and the predicted phenotype, with the guidance source cited): Pharmacogenomic results are usually reported as a genotype and a predicted phenotype, such as poor metabolizer, with clinical guidance from a body such as the Clinical Pharmacogenetics Implementation Consortium cited. Dosing decisions remain with the prescriber. Reporting only one element leaves out information the prescriber needs."}, {"id": 1363, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A laboratory has used the same breakpoint reading chart for three years. All QC results are within range and every test was performed correctly. During an accreditation review, the assessor cites the laboratory for a quality failure related to interpretation. What is the most likely reason?", "options": {"A": "QC results cannot be within range unless the reading chart is replaced every month", "B": "Breakpoints are updated regularly, so an outdated table is a quality failure despite correct testing", "C": "Breakpoints are fixed by the manufacturer, so a three-year-old chart meets the standard if QC passes", "D": "The laboratory has not used both CLSI and EUCAST tables together for every isolate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Breakpoints are updated regularly, so an outdated table is a quality failure despite correct testing): Breakpoints are revised often, sometimes yearly, so charts, software and panel interpretations must be kept current. Even flawless testing can give wrong categories against outdated breakpoints. Using both systems together is not permitted."}, {"id": 1364, "domain": "histology", "question": "Embedding wax that is too cool when it meets the tissue causes a specific problem in the finished block. A technologist has noticed this defect several times after switching to a faster cold plate routine. Which problem is caused by wax that is too cool?", "options": {"A": "Cracks in the block or poor bonding of the wax layers", "B": "Heat damage that shrinks and hardens the tissue cells", "C": "Bleaching of the tissue colour before staining begins", "D": "Excess wax that spills over the edges of the cassette"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cracks in the block or poor bonding of the wax layers): Wax that is too cool sets unevenly, causing cracks and layers that do not bond. Wax that is too hot is the one that damages tissue. Spilling is a volume issue, and colour bleaching is unrelated to wax temperature."}, {"id": 1365, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A boy with intellectual disability and a family history of fragile X syndrome has a normal result on a short read NGS neurodevelopmental panel that has no repeat expansion assay. The physician reads the report as excluding the disorder. What should the laboratory communicate?", "options": {"A": "The result is invalid, since panels can only be run on tumour tissue", "B": "The panel cannot detect repeat expansions, so a negative result does not exclude fragile X", "C": "The panel should be repeated at higher depth, which will reveal the expansion", "D": "The normal panel excludes fragile X, since expansions appear as frameshifts in short reads"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The panel cannot detect repeat expansions, so a negative result does not exclude fragile X): Repeat expansions often need special PCR or long read methods that a standard short read panel will not detect, so knowing what a test cannot see is part of interpreting a negative result. Depth does not overcome that limit. Expansions are not frameshifts, and panels are used on germline samples as well as tumours."}, {"id": 1366, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "In array comparative genomic hybridization, the results appear as a plot of log ratio values arranged along each chromosome. A stretch of adjacent probes shows values consistently shifted toward the reference colour. What does this indicate about the patient's DNA?", "options": {"A": "A duplication of DNA, meaning more than two copies at those probes", "B": "A normal dosage, meaning two copies with a log ratio near zero", "C": "A deletion of DNA, meaning fewer than two copies at those probes", "D": "A balanced rearrangement, meaning two copies but moved to another place"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A deletion of DNA, meaning fewer than two copies at those probes): When the reference colour dominates, the patient has less DNA than normal at those probes, which means a loss. A gain would shift toward the patient colour, and a balanced change or normal dosage would give a flat ratio with no shift at all."}, {"id": 1367, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "Interphase FISH is performed on a neuroblastoma touch preparation using a red MYCN probe and a green chromosome 2 centromere probe. The nuclei shown in the image are scored by the technologist for the pathologist. Which interpretation does the image support?", "options": {"A": "Normal MYCN copy number with two red and two green signals per nucleus", "B": "MYCN amplification shown by red signals far exceeding two green signals", "C": "MYCN deletion shown by fewer red signals than the two green signals", "D": "MYCN gain of one copy shown by three red and two green signals"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (MYCN amplification shown by red signals far exceeding two green signals): the nuclei carry many red MYCN signals against two green control signals, which defines MYCN amplification and a high-risk marker. A normal result would show two red signals, a deletion would show fewer red than green, and a single-copy gain would show only three red signals.", "image_type": "fish_nuclei", "image_params": {"nuclei": [{"red": 5, "green": 2, "fusion": 0}, {"red": 5, "green": 2, "fusion": 0}], "legend": {"red": "MYCN probe", "green": "Chromosome 2 centromere probe"}, "caption": "Interphase FISH, tumour touch preparation"}, "image": "question_images/q_1367.png"}, {"id": 1368, "domain": "histology", "question": "The morning cutting session in the histology laboratory began well but slides look poor now. A technologist finds the water bath surface covered with debris and set to a temperature that is too cool. Sections wrinkle and fail to flatten. What should be corrected?", "options": {"A": "Add a drop of formalin to the bath to fix debris in place", "B": "Clean the surface and set the correct bath temperature", "C": "Raise the bath to a rolling boil to melt away the debris", "D": "Use much colder water in the bath to slow the wrinkles down"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Clean the surface and set the correct bath temperature): Debris and contamination cause floaters, and a bath that is too cool causes wrinkles, so the bath is cleaned and set to the proper temperature. Boiling water ruins sections, formalin does not remove debris, and colder water worsens wrinkles."}, {"id": 1369, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A serum protein electrophoresis tracing shows a flat alpha-1 region in a patient with early lung disease and no other unusual bands. The technologist is asked what condition the tracing should prompt the physician to consider. Which is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Multiple myeloma, which usually presents with a narrow spike in the gamma region", "B": "Nephrotic syndrome, which usually presents with a prominent alpha-2 band", "C": "Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, a genetic cause of early emphysema and liver disease", "D": "Cirrhosis, which usually presents with a bridge between the beta and gamma regions"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, a genetic cause of early emphysema and liver disease): A flat alpha-1 region suggests alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. Myeloma gives a narrow gamma spike, cirrhosis gives beta-gamma bridging from raised IgA, and nephrotic syndrome gives low albumin with a prominent alpha-2 band."}, {"id": 1370, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A pathologist asks why each specimen is logged at receipt and followed from accessioning through staining, screening and reporting, with slides and blocks kept for a legally required period. The audit team asked the technologist to explain the value of the tracking system during a routine inspection. What is the purpose of this documentation trail?", "options": {"A": "It allows the laboratory to skip the labelling of slides on the frosted end", "B": "It allows results to be traced and reviewed if a question arises later", "C": "It allows technologists to screen more slides per day than the usual limit", "D": "It allows the fixative to be reused for the next specimen received"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It allows results to be traced and reviewed if a question arises later): A complete trail makes each specimen traceable and lets results be reviewed later. It does not replace labelling, change screening limits or relate to fixative reuse."}, {"id": 1371, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist looks at an unbanded test slide under a phase-contrast microscope before proceeding to G-banding of the whole set. The slide was made from a blood harvest with a routine cell density. Which findings should be assessed on this slide to decide whether the slides are suitable and whether the suspension needs adjustment?", "options": {"A": "The pH of the fixative, the volume of the pellet, and the colcemid lot number", "B": "The colour of the medium, the incubator temperature, and the age of the specimen", "C": "The trypsin time, the Giemsa batch, and the patient's health number", "D": "The number of metaphases, how well they are spread, and whether cytoplasm remains"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The number of metaphases, how well they are spread, and whether cytoplasm remains): A test slide is checked for metaphase number, spreading quality and freedom from cytoplasm, and the suspension is diluted or concentrated accordingly. Medium colour, incubator settings and reagent lots are separate checks."}, {"id": 1372, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A group A patient's reverse group shows the expected reaction with B cells, but the serum also agglutinates the A1 cells and the patient's own cells are not agglutinated by the serum. Testing the serum with a panel shows a reaction pattern with cells that carry the M antigen. What is the likely cause of the unexpected reverse reaction?", "options": {"A": "Rouleaux caused by raised fibrinogen that disappears after saline replacement", "B": "A subgroup of A that makes the patient's serum contain anti-B and anti-H", "C": "An unexpected alloantibody such as anti-M reacting with antigens on the reverse cells", "D": "A cold autoantibody that reacts with all reverse cells and the patient's own cells"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An unexpected alloantibody such as anti-M reacting with antigens on the reverse cells): Reverse cells that carry other antigens can react with an unexpected alloantibody such as anti-M or anti-P1, and a panel identifies which one. A cold autoantibody would react with the patient's own cells too. Rouleaux disperses in saline, and a subgroup of A would disturb the forward group, not add a panel-specific pattern."}, {"id": 1373, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A 29-year-old's smear collected on day 24 of a 28-day cycle shows mostly intermediate cells that are folded, clustered, and curled at the edges, giving a crowded look. Which hormone is dominant at this point, and what cell pattern does it favour?", "options": {"A": "Progesterone, which favours intermediate cells that fold and cluster together", "B": "Testosterone, which favours keratinized cells with orange-pink cytoplasm and small nuclei", "C": "Estrogen, which favours superficial cells with pyknotic nuclei and a clean background", "D": "Follicle-stimulating hormone, which favours parabasal cells with dense cytoplasm and large nuclei"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Progesterone, which favours intermediate cells that fold and cluster together): After ovulation progesterone takes over, and intermediate cells predominate, folded and clustered with curled edges. Estrogen dominates mid-cycle and gives superficial cells. Parabasal cells indicate low estrogen, not follicle-stimulating hormone effect."}, {"id": 1374, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "The emergency department needs sodium, potassium and creatinine results within minutes for a patient in shock, and waiting for a red-top tube to clot would cause delay. Which tube provides plasma without a clotting wait and is commonly used for stat chemistry?", "options": {"A": "Red top tube with no additive, which needs time to clot", "B": "Green top tube with lithium or sodium heparin, which inhibits thrombin", "C": "Light blue top tube with sodium citrate, used for coagulation tests", "D": "Lavender top tube with EDTA, which chelates calcium and magnesium"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Green top tube with lithium or sodium heparin, which inhibits thrombin): Heparin inhibits thrombin so the sample stays liquid and plasma can be separated immediately, which is why green tops serve stat chemistry. A plain red top must clot first, which takes about 30 minutes to an hour. EDTA would bind calcium and add potassium, and citrate is intended for coagulation testing, not routine chemistry."}, {"id": 1375, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A conventional PCR for a 350 bp target was run with a positive control, a no-template control, an extraction blank, and one patient sample, as shown in the image. What does the pattern indicate about the source of the problem?", "options": {"A": "Master mix contamination, because the extraction blank received the same reagents as other reactions", "B": "Contamination introduced during extraction, because the no-template control shows the amplification reagents are clean", "C": "Primer cross-reactivity with extraction reagents, producing a true positive in the blank lane", "D": "Positive control carryover into the blank alone, since both lanes were loaded one after another"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contamination introduced during extraction, because the no-template control shows the amplification reagents are clean): The clean no-template lane shows the master mix and setup were free of target, so the product in the extraction blank points to contamination during extraction. Master mix contamination would have appeared in the no-template lane as well, and a primer cross-reaction or a one-lane loading carryover is a far less likely explanation.", "image_type": "dna_gel", "image_params": {"lanes": [{"label": "Ladder", "bands": [100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600]}, {"label": "Positive", "bands": [350]}, {"label": "No template", "bands": []}, {"label": "Extract blank", "bands": [350]}, {"label": "Patient", "bands": [350]}], "caption": "PCR with extraction blank, expected product 350 bp"}, "image": "question_images/q_1375.png"}, {"id": 1376, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A pregnant patient has anti-K identified during prenatal antibody screening at the IAT phase, and her partner types as K positive. The obstetric team asks the transfusion service about the risk to the fetus of this antibody. Which statement about anti-K and the fetus is accurate?", "options": {"A": "It can cause severe fetal hemolytic disease and may also suppress fetal red cell production", "B": "It is mostly IgM and cannot cross the placenta, so the fetus is safe", "C": "It causes mild disease at most, because K is a weaker immunogen than the D antigen", "D": "It matters for the mother's later transfusions, but has no known effect on a fetus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It can cause severe fetal hemolytic disease and may also suppress fetal red cell production): Anti-K is IgG, crosses the placenta and can cause severe hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn, and it may also suppress fetal red cell production. K is highly immunogenic, second only to D outside the ABO system. An IgM antibody would not cross the placenta, but anti-K is not IgM."}, {"id": 1377, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A hospital ward starts using a point-of-care INR device operated by nurses with no laboratory training, and the laboratory director asks the point-of-care coordinator what the laboratory must do before nurses may report patient results. Which responsibility belongs to the laboratory in managing this program?", "options": {"A": "Leaving control testing to each ward because operators know their device best", "B": "Repeating every ward result on the central analyzer before it enters the chart", "C": "Selecting devices, certifying operators, and auditing quality control and competency records", "D": "Buying devices for each ward without consulting accrediting bodies or manufacturers"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Selecting devices, certifying operators, and auditing quality control and competency records): The laboratory normally manages the program: device selection, operator training and certification, quality control, and audits. Repeating every result would defeat the purpose of testing at the bedside. Leaving control testing to the ward removes oversight, and ignoring accreditation puts the program at risk."}, {"id": 1378, "domain": "cytology", "question": "During a review of a discrepant case, A pathologist compares a negative cervical cytology result with a biopsy showing high-grade disease and finds abnormal cells were present on the slide but were not detected by the screener. Which remedy best matches this type of error?", "options": {"A": "Reversal of the pathologist's decision, because the biopsy was wrong", "B": "More rescreening and education, because this is a screening error", "C": "Better collection training, because the device missed the lesion", "D": "Report the case as an unavoidable screening limitation and document no change to procedures"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (More rescreening and education, because this is a screening error): Cells present but missed constitute screening error, addressed by rescreening, feedback and education. Better collection training fits sampling error, where cells were not on the slide. The biopsy is not automatically wrong, and while cytology cannot be perfect, discrepancies still need investigation."}, {"id": 1379, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist compares the end-point detection methods used by automated coagulation analyzers. One method uses a color released from a synthetic substrate and is used for anti-Xa, antithrombin and some protein C assays. Which method is described? Laboratories choose among these methods based on the assay being run.", "options": {"A": "Mechanical detection, measured by viscosity change in the reaction", "B": "Optical detection, measured by light scatter change in the reaction", "C": "Immunologic detection, measured by latex agglutination in the reaction", "D": "Chromogenic detection, measured by color change in the reaction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chromogenic detection, measured by color change in the reaction): Chromogenic assays measure color released from a synthetic substrate. Mechanical methods detect viscosity, optical methods detect light changes as fibrin forms, and immunologic methods use latex-enhanced antibody reactions for D-dimer and VWF antigen."}, {"id": 1380, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A pathology resident reviewing adenocarcinoma cases in never smokers asks which EGFR changes are the most frequent activating mutations in non small cell lung cancer and predict response to EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors. The technologist names the two most common alterations. What are they?", "options": {"A": "Exon 19 deletions and the L858R change in exon 21", "B": "The V617F change in exon 14 and deletions of exon 22", "C": "The T790M change in exon 20 and the V600E change in exon 15", "D": "Exon 21 deletions and the G12C change in exon 2"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Exon 19 deletions and the L858R change in exon 21): EGFR activating mutations are most often exon 19 deletions and L858R in exon 21, and they predict a response to EGFR inhibitors. T790M is an acquired resistance change. V600E and V617F belong to other genes."}, {"id": 1381, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cervical smear shows a ragged, dirty background with blood and necrotic debris. The cytotechnologist looks for a nuclear feature that favours invasion over a lesion confined to the epithelium. Which cell in the image carries the feature most helpful for suggesting invasive disease alongside the other malignant nuclear changes?", "options": {"A": "Cell 1, because its enlarged nucleoli accompany coarse chromatin and an irregular nuclear outline", "B": "Cell 3, because its enlarged nucleoli accompany coarse chromatin and an irregular nuclear outline", "C": "Cell 4, because its enlarged nucleoli accompany coarse chromatin and an irregular nuclear outline", "D": "Cell 2, because its enlarged nucleoli accompany coarse chromatin and an irregular nuclear outline"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cell 2, because its enlarged nucleoli accompany coarse chromatin and an irregular nuclear outline): Cell 2 combines prominent nucleoli with coarse chromatin and an irregular outline, and prominent nucleoli in malignant cells support invasive carcinoma, since in situ high-grade lesions usually lack them. Cell 1 is malignant but has no nucleoli. Cell 4 has one nucleolus within a bland nucleus, a reactive pattern, and Cell 3 is normal.", "image_type": "cell_micrograph", "image_params": {"cells": [{"label": "1", "nc_ratio": 0.55, "chromatin": "hyperchromatic", "outline": "irregular", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "squamous"}, {"label": "2", "nc_ratio": 0.5, "chromatin": "coarse", "outline": "irregular", "nucleoli": 3, "cell": "squamous"}, {"label": "3", "nc_ratio": 0.06, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "squamous"}, {"label": "4", "nc_ratio": 0.15, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 1, "cell": "squamous"}], "caption": "Cells at equal magnification", "context": "cervical", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1381.png"}, {"id": 1382, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A urine culture from a catheterized patient shows colonies that swarm across the surface of sheep blood agar. A Gram stain of a colony is shown in the image. Which set of results best supports the identification of this swarming organism?", "options": {"A": "Positive oxidase, positive lactose fermentation, and positive indole production", "B": "Positive urease, positive phenylalanine deaminase, and negative indole production", "C": "Positive citrate, positive Voges-Proskauer, and negative hydrogen sulfide production", "D": "Positive lysine decarboxylase, positive lactose fermentation, and negative urease production"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Positive urease, positive phenylalanine deaminase, and negative indole production): The image shows gram-negative rods, and a swarming organism that is urease positive, phenylalanine deaminase positive and indole negative fits Proteus mirabilis. Oxidase positivity with lactose fermentation fits no common swarmer, citrate with Voges-Proskauer suggests Klebsiella or Enterobacter, and lactose-fermenting, decarboxylase-positive, urease-negative organisms fit Escherichia coli.", "image_type": "micrograph", "image_params": {"prompt": "Realistic Gram stain photomicrograph, 1000x oil immersion, pink-red background, pink-red Gram-negative rods of varying length (some long, pleomorphic) on a pale pink background, from a bacterial colony smear.", "caption": "Gram stain of a colony from a urine culture", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1382.png"}, {"id": 1383, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A thin film from a patient returning from Papua New Guinea shows many delicate rings, some cells containing two or three rings, a few with double chromatin dots, and occasional crescent shaped gametocytes. Parasitemia is 8 percent. What is the most likely species, and how should the result be handled?", "options": {"A": "Plasmodium falciparum, reported immediately as a critical result because high parasitemia is dangerous", "B": "Plasmodium vivax, reported in the routine report since Schuffner dots identify a mild infection", "C": "Babesia microti, reported after treatment since Maltese crosses identify tick-borne infection", "D": "Plasmodium malariae, reported the next morning since band forms indicate a chronic infection"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Plasmodium falciparum, reported immediately as a critical result because high parasitemia is dangerous): Multiple delicate rings per cell, double chromatin dots and crescent gametocytes indicate Plasmodium falciparum, and a parasitemia of 8 percent is dangerous and needs immediate notification. Vivax shows Schuffner dots and ameboid forms, malariae shows band forms, and Babesia shows tetrads without gametocytes."}, {"id": 1384, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A courier delivers a blood specimen with a small clot in the tube, a collection time several hours before pickup, and a note on the requisition suggesting the wrong tube was used. The technologist must decide how to document and handle it according to policy. Which approach reflects good practice?", "options": {"A": "Record each problem, then accept, reject or accept with a comment according to laboratory policy", "B": "Change the collection time on the form so the specimen appears to meet requirements", "C": "Discard the tube without notifying anyone, since problems cannot be trended or explained", "D": "Ignore the clot, since heparin will dissolve it once the culture reaches the incubator"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Record each problem, then accept, reject or accept with a comment according to laboratory policy): Recording clots, delays and wrong-tube problems supports a policy decision and helps explain any culture failure later. Heparin does not dissolve clots in an existing sample, and altering records or silent disposal breaks quality rules."}, {"id": 1385, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A troponin of 4,800 ng/L is obtained on a sample from an emergency patient with chest pain. The technologist has confirmed that quality control was acceptable, and the identification on the tube is checked against the requisition. Which action is appropriate for communicating the result safely?", "options": {"A": "Hold the result for batch review and skip identity checks once controls are acceptable", "B": "Delay reporting until the routine morning laboratory summary is issued, then discard the specimen tube afterward", "C": "Report it by email and repeat the test daily until the value falls", "D": "Verify patient identity, report as a critical value quickly and document the caregiver's read-back"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Verify patient identity, report as a critical value quickly and document the caregiver's read-back): Patient identity must be verified on every specimen, and very high troponin is reported quickly as a critical value with documented read-back. Delaying to a batch or emailing risks patient harm, and good controls do not remove the identity check."}, {"id": 1386, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cell block from a lung tumour is stained to decide the type of non-small cell carcinoma. The technologist is asked which markers support squamous origin rather than adenocarcinoma. Which immunostains are used to support a squamous cell carcinoma in the cell block prepared from this specimen?", "options": {"A": "p40 and p63, which are the markers that support a squamous origin", "B": "TTF-1 and napsin A, which are the markers that support a squamous origin", "C": "Calretinin and D2-40, which are the markers that support a squamous origin", "D": "Synaptophysin and chromogranin, which are the markers that support a squamous origin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (p40 and p63, which are the markers that support a squamous origin): p40 and p63 support squamous origin. TTF-1 and napsin A favour adenocarcinoma of the lung, and synaptophysin and chromogranin indicate neuroendocrine tumour. Calretinin and D2-40 are mesothelial markers, so they do not support squamous carcinoma."}, {"id": 1387, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A negative cervical slide from a 34-year-old with regular cycles is being reviewed by a technologist who has already confirmed adequacy and a normal maturation pattern. She now examines the non-epithelial findings on the slide. Besides epithelial cells, what would she expect to see in a normal background?", "options": {"A": "Necrotic debris, old blood, and clumped inflammatory cells", "B": "A background of mucus, a few neutrophils, and lactobacilli", "C": "Many neutrophils, yeast hyphae, and clue cells throughout the smear", "D": "Granular blue blobs, degenerated parabasal cells, and abundant histiocytes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A background of mucus, a few neutrophils, and lactobacilli): A normal background contains mucus, a few neutrophils, and lactobacilli. Necrotic debris suggests malignancy, and many neutrophils with organisms suggest infection. Blue blobs and degenerated parabasal cells are features of atrophy."}, {"id": 1388, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A phlebotomist is about to draw blood cultures on an adult patient who has a fever. A skin preparation with chlorhexidine in alcohol is used. Which step is needed for the antiseptic to work properly before the venipuncture is performed?", "options": {"A": "Applying a second antiseptic over the first while still wet", "B": "Touching the vein site again with a bare finger to confirm the vein position", "C": "Wiping the skin with dry gauze immediately after application", "D": "Allowing the skin to dry for the full antiseptic contact time"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Allowing the skin to dry for the full antiseptic contact time): The antiseptic kills skin flora only after the full contact time and drying, which lowers contamination of the bottles. Wiping it off or covering it with another product shortens the effective exposure, and palpating the site again reintroduces contamination."}, {"id": 1389, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A patient with congestive heart failure develops bilateral pleural fluid, and the laboratory measures low protein and low lactate dehydrogenase compared with blood values. A trainee asks which mechanism explains this type of effusion and what the fluid should be called. What is the best explanation?", "options": {"A": "An exudate, caused by infection or cancer spreading directly across the pleural surface itself", "B": "A transudate, caused by tumour cells shedding into the cavity and raising the fluid protein", "C": "An exudate, caused by pressure changes that leave the fluid low in protein and cells", "D": "A transudate, caused by pressure or protein imbalance instead of serosal disease"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A transudate, caused by pressure or protein imbalance instead of serosal disease): Heart failure raises hydrostatic pressure and gives a low-protein transudate, without serosal disease. An exudate reflects infection, inflammation or cancer of the serosa. Tumour shedding is a cause of exudates, and an exudate is high in protein and cells."}, {"id": 1390, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A blood supplier tests donor units for Rh type before labelling them for distribution to hospitals. A unit types as negative by direct testing with anti-D reagent. Which additional testing does Canadian practice generally require on donor units so that every D-positive unit is correctly labelled?", "options": {"A": "Adsorption and elution using anti-D on all D-positive units", "B": "Molecular genotyping of the RHD gene on every donor unit", "C": "Weak D testing by the indirect antiglobulin test on units that type as D negative", "D": "Reverse grouping of the donor unit with group O reagent cells"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Weak D testing by the indirect antiglobulin test on units that type as D negative): Donor units that appear D negative are tested for weak D so that weak D units are labelled D positive, and no weak D unit reaches a D-negative recipient unlabelled. Genotyping every unit is not the routine approach. Adsorption and elution address weak A and B, and reverse grouping is an ABO test."}, {"id": 1391, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A coagulation laboratory follows the order of draw when collecting several tubes from the same patient. The light blue-top tube should go in a specific position to avoid additive carry-over. Where does the light blue tube belong in the order of draw?", "options": {"A": "Last, after every other tube, including those containing heparin or EDTA", "B": "After tubes containing EDTA and before blood culture bottles or serum tubes", "C": "Before blood culture bottles and after tubes containing heparin or EDTA", "D": "After blood cultures and before serum tubes and additive tubes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (After blood cultures and before serum tubes and additive tubes): The light blue tube follows blood culture bottles and precedes serum and additive tubes so that carry-over does not contaminate the sample. The other positions would allow additives such as EDTA or heparin to enter the citrate tube."}, {"id": 1392, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A quality auditor reviews the cytology procedure log for FNAs performed with ROSE. She finds adequacy statements written without a time or the name of the person who assessed the slide. Which items must the laboratory record for each ROSE assessment according to good practice?", "options": {"A": "The final diagnosis, the pathologist's name and the date the report was signed.", "B": "The patient's insurance number, the assessor's name and the stain lot number.", "C": "Time, the assessor's name, the number of passes and the adequacy statement.", "D": "Only the adequacy statement, since other details appear on the final report."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Time, the assessor's name, the number of passes and the adequacy statement.): Good documentation of ROSE records the time, who performed it, how many passes were made and the adequacy statement. The final report does not carry those details. Insurance numbers and the final diagnosis are not part of the on-site record."}, {"id": 1393, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A blood culture from an important case has failed with almost no metaphases, and a new sample cannot easily be recollected because the patient has since begun chemotherapy. The supervisor asks what can be done to still try to answer the clinical question. Which option reflects good practice?", "options": {"A": "Report a normal karyotype, since a failed culture means no abnormality exists", "B": "Wait for the culture to recover after harvest, since fixed cells can be regrown", "C": "Use held backup cultures, or try FISH on uncultured cells or a microarray if appropriate", "D": "Repeat the hypotonic step on the fixed pellet, since it can then be re-cultured"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use held backup cultures, or try FISH on uncultured cells or a microarray if appropriate): Backup flasks and alternative tests such as FISH on uncultured cells or microarray may still answer the question. A failed culture does not mean a normal result, and fixed cells cannot be regrown."}, {"id": 1394, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A blood agar and chocolate agar culture from a patient with fever and a rash grows a gram-negative diplococcus. Sugar utilization tests show that the isolate oxidizes both glucose and maltose. Which organism is identified, and what laboratory precaution applies?", "options": {"A": "Neisseria meningitidis, which is handled on the open bench because it is a fragile organism", "B": "Neisseria meningitidis, which is handled in a biosafety cabinet because of laboratory-acquired infection risk", "C": "Neisseria gonorrhoeae, which is handled in a biosafety cabinet because of laboratory-acquired infection risk", "D": "Moraxella catarrhalis, which is handled in a biosafety cabinet because of laboratory-acquired infection risk"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Neisseria meningitidis, which is handled in a biosafety cabinet because of laboratory-acquired infection risk): Neisseria meningitidis oxidizes glucose and maltose and is handled in a biosafety cabinet due to the risk of laboratory-acquired infection. Neisseria gonorrhoeae oxidizes glucose only. Moraxella does not ferment sugars. Fragility does not make open-bench work safe."}, {"id": 1395, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A technologist reviews the level 1 Levey-Jennings chart shown, where the points that break a Westgard rule are marked in red on days 9 and 10. Which rule is violated, and what type of error does it most often indicate?", "options": {"A": "R-4s rule, consecutive controls differing by more than 4 SD, indicating random error", "B": "2-2s rule, two consecutive controls beyond 2 SD on the same side, indicating systematic error", "C": "1-3s rule, a single control beyond 3 SD from the mean, indicating random error", "D": "4-1s rule, four consecutive controls beyond 1 SD on one side, indicating systematic error"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (R-4s rule, consecutive controls differing by more than 4 SD, indicating random error): One point lies above the 2 SD line and the next below the minus 2 SD line, a spread of more than 4 SD, which is the R-4s rule and reflects random error. The two points are on opposite sides, so a 2-2s rule is not met, and no point reaches 3 SD or forms a run beyond 1 SD.", "image_type": "qc_chart", "image_params": {"points": [0.2, -0.4, 0.5, 0.1, -0.3, 0.6, -0.2, 0.4, 0.0, -0.5, 0.3, 0.1, -0.1, 0.2, -0.3], "violation_day": 9, "violation_type": "r_4s", "caption": "Level 1 control"}, "image": "question_images/q_1395.png"}, {"id": 1396, "domain": "histology", "question": "A very large mastectomy specimen has been placed whole in formalin for a full day, but the surgeon later says the core cannot be judged fixed. The laboratory wants to speed fixation without harming the tissue, and the specimen is still intact in its bucket. Which principle explains the best next action?", "options": {"A": "Heating the specimen above 90 degrees Celsius hastens penetration and protects the tissue detail.", "B": "Formalin penetrates the whole tissue within minutes, so more time will not change anything.", "C": "Formalin penetrates about a millimetre in the first hour, so slicing reaches the centre sooner.", "D": "Slicing has little effect on fixation, because formalin reaches the centre at the same rate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Formalin penetrates about a millimetre in the first hour, so slicing reaches the centre sooner.): Formalin diffuses slowly, about 1 mm in the first hour and then more slowly, so a thick specimen left whole has an unfixed centre for many hours. Making thin slices exposes more surface and brings fixative to the core sooner. Extreme heat would cook and distort the tissue, and diffusion depth clearly depends on thickness."}, {"id": 1397, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with known multiple myeloma has a urine dipstick reading negative for protein, yet the physician suspects large amounts of urinary light chains. What is the best interpretation of the dipstick result? The physician asks how to interpret the dipstick result.", "options": {"A": "The protein pad detects mainly albumin, so a negative strip does not exclude a paraprotein", "B": "A negative strip proves no light chains are present, since the pad detects every protein", "C": "The negative strip indicates that the strip has expired, so the whole lot must be discarded", "D": "The negative strip should be repeated on the same sample until the pad changes colour"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The protein pad detects mainly albumin, so a negative strip does not exclude a paraprotein): The reagent strip protein pad is most sensitive to albumin and can miss light chains, so a paraprotein needs specific testing. Strips should be within their expiry date and controlled daily, but nothing here suggests they are expired. Repeating the strip on the same sample adds nothing."}, {"id": 1398, "domain": "histology", "question": "A dermatologic surgeon is removing skin cancer in thin layers, and the tissue arrives in the laboratory with a hand-drawn map. During Mohs surgery, the technologist must locate any tumour-positive area exactly on the patient once the slides are read. Which preparation step makes this possible?", "options": {"A": "Fixing the tissue in formalin so the position cannot move", "B": "Cutting the tissue into random pieces so each is examined separately", "C": "Marking the specimen with dyes or a map so orientation is preserved", "D": "Photographing the tissue after staining to compare with the patient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Marking the specimen with dyes or a map so orientation is preserved): The specimen is oriented and marked with dyes or maps so any positive area can be traced back to a location on the patient. Random pieces lose orientation, and formalin or later photographs do not preserve the map."}, {"id": 1399, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A hospital reviews a newly diagnosed lung carcinoma and needs to decide on treatment planning. The pathologist reminds the group that small cell and non-small cell types are managed very differently. Which statement about the treatment of small cell carcinoma is correct?", "options": {"A": "It is treated with chemotherapy and radiation, so an accurate diagnosis matters", "B": "It is treated with observation only, so the report is given low priority", "C": "It is treated with antibiotics, so the diagnosis is confirmed with culture", "D": "It is treated with surgery alone, so the cytology report rarely changes the plan"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It is treated with chemotherapy and radiation, so an accurate diagnosis matters): Small cell carcinoma grows fast and spreads early, so it is treated with chemotherapy and radiation rather than surgery. Antibiotics and observation are not treatments for it, and the cytology report clearly changes management."}, {"id": 1400, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A 66-year-old man with marked splenomegaly and bone marrow fibrosis has a leukoerythroblastic smear. Many red cells are shaped like a drop of water with a single tapered tail, and a few nucleated red cells are present. Which cell type is this, and what does it typically indicate?", "options": {"A": "Schistocytes, which are helmet shaped fragments from mechanical destruction of red cells", "B": "Elliptocytes, which appear in hereditary elliptocytosis and in iron deficiency", "C": "Teardrop cells (dacrocytes), which appear in myelofibrosis and other marrow infiltration", "D": "Stomatocytes, which show a slit like central pallor and appear in alcohol related disease"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Teardrop cells (dacrocytes), which appear in myelofibrosis and other marrow infiltration): Teardrop cells, or dacrocytes, appear in myelofibrosis and marrow infiltration, where cells are distorted as they leave a scarred or crowded marrow. Elliptocytes are oval or pencil shaped, stomatocytes have a slit like pallor, and schistocytes are fragments caused by mechanical shearing."}, {"id": 1401, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A hematologist requests a bone marrow aspirate and biopsy on a 58-year-old adult with unexplained pancytopenia (hemoglobin 82 g/L, platelets 40 x 10^9/L). The technologist confirms the tray and positions the patient prone for the procedure. Which anatomical site is usually used for the puncture in adults?", "options": {"A": "The sternum, which is the standard first choice for adult marrow sampling", "B": "The vertebral spine, which is routinely used in adults for marrow collection", "C": "The proximal tibia, which is the usual adult site because of easy access", "D": "The posterior iliac crest, which provides a safe site with ample marrow"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The posterior iliac crest, which provides a safe site with ample marrow): The posterior iliac crest is the usual adult site. The sternum is used only in special circumstances, and the tibia is used for infants rather than adults. Routine spinal sampling is not standard practice."}, {"id": 1402, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "An array finds a 1.5 megabase deletion in a child with mild learning difficulty and delayed speech. The deletion overlaps a known syndrome region, and the same deletion is found in the child's clinically unaffected father on targeted testing. What is the most reasonable interpretation?", "options": {"A": "It has no clinical meaning, because size alone decides every classification of a deletion", "B": "It may still be pathogenic, since some deletions show incomplete penetrance and pass through unaffected carriers", "C": "It has no clinical meaning, because size alone decides all classification", "D": "It is benign, because a deletion found in a healthy parent excludes any link to disease"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It may still be pathogenic, since some deletions show incomplete penetrance and pass through unaffected carriers): Some pathogenic CNVs have incomplete penetrance, so an unaffected parent can carry one, and the matching syndrome region keeps it a concern. Inheritance from a healthy parent supports a benign or uncertain call but does not always exclude disease. It cannot be de novo if the father has it."}, {"id": 1403, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory validating a new Neisseria gonorrhoeae PCR tests it against nucleic acid extracts of Neisseria meningitidis, Neisseria lactamica, and normal commensal species from the throat. None of these should amplify in the assay. Which performance characteristic is being assessed?", "options": {"A": "Reportable range, meaning the span of quantities the assay can measure.", "B": "Carry-over, meaning contamination between adjacent wells on the plate.", "C": "Analytical sensitivity, meaning the lowest detectable concentration of target.", "D": "Analytical specificity, including cross-reactivity with closely related organisms."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Analytical specificity, including cross-reactivity with closely related organisms.): Challenging an assay with related organisms and interfering flora shows whether it detects only its intended target, which is analytical specificity. Sensitivity is found with dilutions of the true target. Reportable range applies to quantitative assays. Carry-over is studied by alternating high positive and negative samples."}, {"id": 1404, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "In a melt curve analysis after SYBR Green qPCR run in triplicate, two different amplicons from two assays melt at 79 °C and 86 °C. A trainee asks which properties of a double-stranded product decide its melting temperature. What should the technologist explain?", "options": {"A": "Its 3' primer sequence and the brand of polymerase used.", "B": "Its concentration and the number of cycles used in amplification.", "C": "Its length and its proportion of guanine and cytosine bases.", "D": "Its fluorescence intensity and the position of the threshold line."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Its length and its proportion of guanine and cytosine bases.): Longer products and those rich in guanine and cytosine have more hydrogen bonds holding the strands together and melt at higher temperatures. Concentration changes signal height but not the temperature at which strands separate. Polymerase brand does not alter the duplex's stability. The threshold line is only a software setting for Ct."}, {"id": 1405, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A bronchial wash shows granulomas with necrosis, and the pathologist suspects a mycobacterial infection. The trainee asks which method confirms Mycobacterium tuberculosis, since the Papanicolaou stain does not identify it. Which approach is correct for confirming this organism in the laboratory workflow?", "options": {"A": "Acid-fast staining with culture or molecular tests, not Papanicolaou staining", "B": "Mucicarmine staining alone, since capsule halos identify all mycobacteria in cytology", "C": "Romanowsky staining alone, since lymphoglandular bodies identify mycobacteria", "D": "Papanicolaou staining alone, since granulomas with necrosis are diagnostic of tuberculosis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acid-fast staining with culture or molecular tests, not Papanicolaou staining): Suspected mycobacteria are confirmed by acid-fast stain plus culture or molecular tests. Granulomas with necrosis only raise suspicion. Mucicarmine marks the Cryptococcus capsule, and Romanowsky staining does not confirm mycobacteria."}, {"id": 1406, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A new technologist sets up the daily antibody screen using a commercial screening set taken from the reagent refrigerator. The package insert describes the composition of the set and the phases at which it is read. Which description matches a standard antibody screening set used for patient serum testing?", "options": {"A": "Two group AB cells from donors who lack all the clinically significant antigens", "B": "Two or three group O cells that together carry the most important red cell antigens", "C": "One pooled group A cell suspension that carries every red cell antigen in a single vial", "D": "Eleven or more group O cells arranged in a pattern to identify individual antibody specificities"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Two or three group O cells that together carry the most important red cell antigens): A screening set uses two or three group O cells chosen so that the clinically important antigens are all represented, and a positive result then leads to a panel. Cells arranged to identify specificity describe a panel of 11 or more cells. Group O cells are used so that anti-A and anti-B in the patient's serum do not react."}, {"id": 1407, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist needs a reference strain with a well-characterized ESBL to check that the clavulanate confirmatory test is working. The strain must give a known result each time. Which ATCC strain is the appropriate control for ESBL testing? The control must be a known, characterized organism for this test.", "options": {"A": "Escherichia coli ATCC 25922, a susceptible general-purpose reference strain", "B": "Streptococcus pneumoniae ATCC 49619, a reference strain for fastidious organisms", "C": "Staphylococcus aureus ATCC 25923, a reference strain for staphylococcal disk diffusion", "D": "Klebsiella pneumoniae ATCC 700603, a well-characterized ESBL-producing reference strain"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Klebsiella pneumoniae ATCC 700603, a well-characterized ESBL-producing reference strain): Klebsiella pneumoniae ATCC 700603 is an ESBL-producing control strain used for confirmatory testing. E. coli 25922 is a susceptible general control, S. aureus 25923 is used in disk diffusion of staphylococci, and S. pneumoniae 49619 is used for fastidious organisms."}, {"id": 1408, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory wants one tube to detect influenza A, influenza B, and an internal control in the same real-time reaction, reading each target in a separate optical channel. The technologist must choose a chemistry that can distinguish the three products. Which detection approach allows this?", "options": {"A": "SYBR Green dye, since it fluoresces in a different colour for each amplicon.", "B": "Hydrolysis probes labelled with a different reporter dye for each target.", "C": "Ethidium bromide staining of the three products in a gel after the run.", "D": "One intercalating dye in the tube, since dye colour depends on amplicon length."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hydrolysis probes labelled with a different reporter dye for each target.): Probes bind a specific internal sequence and carry a distinct reporter, so several targets can be read at once in separate channels. An intercalating dye emits one colour for any double-stranded product, so it cannot tell three targets apart in a single channel. Gel staining is an open-tube end-point step that does not provide real-time multiplex detection."}, {"id": 1409, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A laboratory director reviews rates and finds ASC-US reported far more often than SIL by one screener, while the laboratory average for ASC-US stays under about five percent. What is the appropriate interpretation and action? The review covers the last three months of slides.", "options": {"A": "The ratio suggests under-calling, so lower the threshold for negative reports", "B": "The ratio suggests excellent skill, so assign that screener additional abnormal slides for the week", "C": "The ratio is meaningless, so stop tracking ASC-US and SIL rates", "D": "The ratio suggests over-calling, so review that screener's slides with the pathologist and retrain"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The ratio suggests over-calling, so review that screener's slides with the pathologist and retrain): A high ASC-US to SIL ratio suggests a screener is over-calling the uncertain category, so slides are reviewed with the pathologist and the screener is retrained. It does not indicate skill. The ratio is a useful monitoring tool and is not to be discontinued."}, {"id": 1410, "domain": "cytology", "question": "The background of a 64-year-old's smear looks granular, with dark blue-purple clumps lying between parabasal cells and neutrophils. The clumps show no nuclear detail. A screener wants the term for these degenerating remnants that often accompany atrophy. What are they called, and what do they contain?", "options": {"A": "Tumour diathesis, formed by old blood and necrotic material from an invasive cancer", "B": "Psammoma bodies, formed by calcified concentric layers from degenerating tumour papillae", "C": "Lactobacilli clumps, formed by dense rods sitting over glycogen-rich cells", "D": "Blue blobs, formed by degenerated parabasal cells and inflammatory debris"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Blue blobs, formed by degenerated parabasal cells and inflammatory debris): Blue blobs are degenerated parabasal cells and debris that give the atrophic smear its granular background. They add to atrophy being a false-positive trap. Tumour diathesis and psammoma bodies point to malignancy and are not described here."}, {"id": 1411, "domain": "histology", "question": "Older bone marrow and lymph node protocols used fixatives such as B-5 and Zenker solution, which gave crisp nuclear detail. Many laboratories have since abandoned these fluids. A new technologist asks for the specific safety and environmental reason these fixatives are now uncommon?", "options": {"A": "They contain picric acid and become explosive whenever the container is opened.", "B": "They contain mercury, and the hazardous waste is difficult to dispose of safely.", "C": "They contain glutaraldehyde and cross-link tissue so strongly that it cannot be cut.", "D": "They contain xylene and their vapours ignite at the temperatures used in processors."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (They contain mercury, and the hazardous waste is difficult to dispose of safely.): B-5 and Zenker both rely on mercuric chloride, so spent solution is toxic waste that needs costly handling and disposal. Glutaraldehyde is not a component of either, and xylene is a clearing agent rather than a fixative ingredient. Picric acid becomes explosive when it dries out, not simply when a bottle is opened."}, {"id": 1412, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A laboratory needs to report a patient's aPTT relative to heparin dosing. The therapeutic range is set locally by comparing aPTT results with anti-Xa levels. What is a typical anti-Xa therapeutic range for unfractionated heparin? Results are reported in international units per millilitre.", "options": {"A": "A typical range is about 3.0 to 7.0 IU/mL of plasma", "B": "A typical range is about 0.03 to 0.07 IU/mL of plasma", "C": "A typical range is about 1.5 to 2.5 IU/mL of plasma", "D": "A typical range is about 0.3 to 0.7 IU/mL of plasma"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A typical range is about 0.3 to 0.7 IU/mL of plasma): A typical anti-Xa range for UFH is about 0.3 to 0.7 IU/mL. The 1.5 to 2.5 figure is the aPTT ratio expressed relative to the control, and the other ranges are off by an order of magnitude."}, {"id": 1413, "domain": "histology", "question": "In the chemistry of the H&E stain, hematoxylin is combined with a metal salt to make a positively charged complex. Which metal salt is usually used as this mordant for routine nuclear staining? This mordant links the oxidized dye to nuclear chromatin in the section. The technologist is preparing fresh reagent for the staining line today.", "options": {"A": "Sodium, in the form of common table salt", "B": "Aluminum, in the form of alum salt, is the usual mordant", "C": "Calcium, in the form of hard water carbonate", "D": "Silver, in the form of a silver nitrate solution"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Aluminum, in the form of alum salt, is the usual mordant): Routine hematoxylin uses aluminum as alum, forming a positively charged dye-mordant complex that binds nuclear chromatin. Table salt and calcium do not act as mordants, and silver nitrate is used for a different class of stains."}, {"id": 1414, "domain": "cytology", "question": "After coverslipping, a slide shows a bubbly, refractile look over the cells that hides detail. The mounting medium was applied to slides that had not fully cleared, and drying occurred. The hazy look extended across several cells on one slide only, not on the other slides in the same rack. What is the best description of this problem?", "options": {"A": "A true finding of gland cell mucin, which should be reported by the pathologist", "B": "A stain precipitate from an old reagent, which should be filtered next time", "C": "An artefact from drying on the mounting medium, reducing slide quality", "D": "A floater from another slide, which should be reported to the supervisor"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An artefact from drying on the mounting medium, reducing slide quality): Drying on the mounting medium creates a bubbly, refractile look that obscures detail and is a processing artefact. It does not represent mucin, contamination from another slide or reagent precipitate."}, {"id": 1415, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A chromosome study of a young woman finds a small number of metaphases where one chromosome is left behind at the cell equator during division, producing a daughter cell with 45 chromosomes. The trainee asks the name of this mechanism, which differs from the failure of paired chromosomes to separate. Which term names this mechanism of chromosome loss?", "options": {"A": "Endoreduplication, in which the whole set is copied twice with no division.", "B": "Anaphase lag, in which a chromosome is left behind and lost from the daughter cell.", "C": "Nondisjunction, in which paired chromosomes fail to separate and go to one daughter cell.", "D": "Dispermy, in which two sperm fertilize one egg and a whole extra set is added."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Anaphase lag, in which a chromosome is left behind and lost from the daughter cell.): When a chromosome lags behind during anaphase and is not included in the daughter nucleus, the result is a monosomic cell line. Nondisjunction sends both chromosomes to the same cell, producing one trisomic and one monosomic daughter. Dispermy and endoreduplication change the whole set, not one chromosome."}, {"id": 1416, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A woman aged thirty-one has a cytology result of LSIL on a liquid-based sample. She is anxious and asks the technologist what the result means biologically. Which explanation is the most accurate and appropriately reassuring within the technologist's scope? She was told the result by phone the same afternoon.", "options": {"A": "It shows the vaccine failed and the cells will progress within months", "B": "It reflects productive HPV infection with mild change, and many such lesions regress on their own", "C": "It indicates a bacterial infection that antibiotics alone will fully resolve", "D": "It confirms invasive cancer, and surgery is needed within the next week"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It reflects productive HPV infection with mild change, and many such lesions regress on their own): LSIL reflects productive HPV infection and mild dysplasia (CIN 1), and many of these lesions regress without treatment. It does not indicate invasive cancer. It is not a bacterial infection, and it does not imply vaccine failure."}, {"id": 1417, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with black stools and recent vomiting has a blood urea of 24 mmol/L with a normal creatinine of 88 micromol/L. The physician asks why the urea is disproportionately raised. Which explanation does the technologist offer for the pattern?", "options": {"A": "Dehydration destroys creatinine, whereas urea is produced only by skeletal muscle", "B": "Gastrointestinal bleeding and dehydration raise urea, whereas creatinine depends mainly on muscle mass", "C": "Vomiting raises creatinine production, whereas urea is filtered without any reabsorption", "D": "Gastrointestinal bleeding lowers urea output, whereas creatinine depends mainly on dietary protein intake and hydration"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gastrointestinal bleeding and dehydration raise urea, whereas creatinine depends mainly on muscle mass): Urea rises with dehydration, high protein load and gastrointestinal bleeding, while creatinine is produced steadily in proportion to muscle mass. Bleeding does not lower urea, and urea is made in the liver rather than muscle."}, {"id": 1418, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist compares two groups of squamous cells with mildly enlarged nuclei. Both show perinuclear halos and both have nucleoli, but only one group worries her. Which nuclear feature, absent in reactive cells, would push her toward calling that group abnormal?", "options": {"A": "Cytoplasmic vacuoles and a clear perinuclear rim around each nucleus", "B": "Binucleation with smooth nuclear membranes and evenly distributed fine chromatin", "C": "Hyperchromasia with irregular chromatin clumping in the nucleus", "D": "Visible nucleoli in nuclei up to twice the normal intermediate size"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hyperchromasia with irregular chromatin clumping in the nucleus): Reactive cells do not show hyperchromasia or irregular chromatin clumping, so that combination is the warning feature. Binucleation, vacuoles, halos, and mild enlargement with nucleoli all occur in benign reactive change."}, {"id": 1419, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A dual-colour, dual-fusion probe is used on a marrow sample. Most nuclei show one red, one green, and one fusion signal, and not two fusions. The technologist recalls that break points and small deletions near the junction can change the pattern. How should this be handled?", "options": {"A": "Recognize it as a known variant pattern and score it according to the laboratory's validated criteria", "B": "Discard every nucleus because only one red, one green, and two fusion signals count", "C": "Call it normal, since a normal nucleus has exactly one fusion signal and one intact pair", "D": "Report a deletion of the green gene because a fusion signal has been lost"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Recognize it as a known variant pattern and score it according to the laboratory's validated criteria): Variant patterns such as a single fusion occur with certain break points or nearby deletions, and validation criteria explain how to score them. A normal nucleus has no fusion. Discarding every variant cell would miss disease, and the pattern does not by itself indicate a loss of one gene."}, {"id": 1420, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A supplier states that its leukoreduced red cell units meet a defined residual white cell limit. A student asks what upper limit per unit is used to define a leukoreduced component in the chapter. Which limit is correct for these units?", "options": {"A": "Fewer than 5 x 10^3 white cells per unit", "B": "Fewer than 25 x 10^6 white cells per unit", "C": "Fewer than 5 x 10^9 white cells per unit", "D": "Fewer than 5 x 10^6 white cells per unit"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fewer than 5 x 10^6 white cells per unit): A leukoreduced unit contains fewer than 5 x 10^6 white cells. A limit a thousand times higher would leave far too many cells to be useful, and a limit a thousand times lower is not the defined standard. The 25 figure is the irradiation dose in gray and is unrelated to the white cell count."}, {"id": 1421, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A resident sees a karyotype written 47,XY,+13 on a report for a newborn with a cleft lip, extra fingers and severe heart defects. The resident asks which condition this result represents, and what the plus sign in the ISCN string means. Which diagnosis and symbol meaning fit this report?", "options": {"A": "Patau syndrome, with the plus sign showing loss of part of chromosome 13.", "B": "Down syndrome, with the plus sign showing a gained copy of chromosome 13.", "C": "Edwards syndrome, with the plus sign showing a gained copy of chromosome 13.", "D": "Patau syndrome, with the plus sign showing a gained copy of chromosome 13."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Patau syndrome, with the plus sign showing a gained copy of chromosome 13.): Three copies of chromosome 13 is trisomy 13, called Patau syndrome, and the plus sign marks the gained chromosome. Edwards syndrome is trisomy 18 and Down syndrome is trisomy 21. A plus sign before a whole chromosome number means a gain, not a partial loss."}, {"id": 1422, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A study group compares low-risk and high-risk human papillomavirus types. One student states which types are responsible for genital warts and for mild cell changes that rarely progress to cancer. Which statement should the instructor confirm as correct? The comparison involves one virus family but very different disease behaviour.", "options": {"A": "Types 6 and 11 cause genital warts and rarely progress to cancer", "B": "Types 6 and 11 cause most cervical cancers and rarely produce warts", "C": "Types 31 and 45 cause genital warts and rarely progress to cancer", "D": "Types 16 and 18 cause genital warts and rarely progress to cancer"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Types 6 and 11 cause genital warts and rarely progress to cancer): Types 6 and 11 are the low-risk types linked to condylomata and mild changes. Types 16 and 18 are high-risk and cause about 70% of cervical cancers. Types 31 and 45 are also high-risk."}, {"id": 1423, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient is told to come to the collection site for a fasting glucose test. The technologist checks the patient's history and wants to confirm the minimum time without caloric intake that meets the definition of fasting for this test. What is the minimum?", "options": {"A": "At least 16 hours without any liquid or solid intake", "B": "At least 2 hours after the last full meal of the day", "C": "At least 4 hours without any solid food or sweet drinks", "D": "At least 8 hours without food or drink other than water"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (At least 8 hours without food or drink other than water): Fasting glucose requires at least 8 hours without caloric intake, usually 8 to 12 hours, and water is allowed. Four hours is too short, so recent food can still raise glucose. Sixteen hours with no liquid is unnecessary and could dehydrate the patient. Two hours after a meal describes a postprandial state, not a fast."}, {"id": 1424, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A fluid slide from a patient with a suspected drug reaction shows many eosinophils, and the trainee asks which conditions eosinophils in an effusion usually suggest. The senior technologist reviews the possibilities with the trainee. Which group is the most appropriate answer?", "options": {"A": "Tuberculosis, lymphoma or a long-standing chronic effusion from another cause", "B": "Allergy, air or blood in the cavity, or a drug reaction", "C": "Heart failure, cirrhosis or a simple transudate with few cells", "D": "Bacterial infection, abscess formation or an acute pyogenic process"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Allergy, air or blood in the cavity, or a drug reaction): Eosinophils suggest allergy, air or blood in the cavity, or drug reaction. Bacterial infection brings neutrophils, and tuberculosis or lymphoma brings a lymphocyte-rich background. Transudates from heart failure and cirrhosis are usually low in cells."}, {"id": 1425, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A blood draw from a heparin-containing line is requested for an aPTT, and the result is markedly prolonged and inconsistent with the clinical picture. The technologist suspects contamination. Which test can help confirm heparin contamination in the sample? The line was flushed before collection.", "options": {"A": "Platelet count by impedance, since it is lowered by heparin alone", "B": "D-dimer by immunoturbidimetry, since it is raised by heparin alone", "C": "Fibrinogen by the Clauss method, since it is prolonged by heparin alone", "D": "A thrombin time or heparin neutralizer step, which flags heparin's presence"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A thrombin time or heparin neutralizer step, which flags heparin's presence): Heparin contamination can mimic an inhibitor, and a prolonged thrombin time or correction with a heparin neutralizer can support it. Fibrinogen, D-dimer and platelet count do not identify heparin in the sample."}, {"id": 1426, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "On a Giemsa-stained thin film from a traveller back from India, most infected red cells are visibly larger than neighbouring cells, and the parasites inside are ameboid trophozoites with fine pink stippling scattered through the cell. Which species is suggested, and what change to the infected cell is characteristic?", "options": {"A": "Plasmodium vivax, which enlarges infected red cells and shows Schuffner's dots", "B": "Plasmodium falciparum, which leaves infected red cells normal in size and shows crescent gametocytes", "C": "Babesia microti, which leaves infected red cells normal in size and shows a Maltese cross", "D": "Plasmodium malariae, which shrinks infected red cells and shows band forms"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Plasmodium vivax, which enlarges infected red cells and shows Schuffner's dots): P. vivax enlarges infected red cells and shows Schuffner's dots, and P. ovale is similar but oval and fimbriated. P. falciparum cells stay normal in size, and P. malariae is known for band forms and rosettes."}, {"id": 1427, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A tracing shows a continuous bridge between the beta and gamma regions, with an albumin of 24 g/L, in a patient with long-term heavy drinking. The technologist is asked to name the pattern and the protein change that explains it. Which pairing fits?", "options": {"A": "Beta-gamma bridging of myeloma, from a narrow monoclonal IgG spike", "B": "Beta-gamma bridging of plasma, from a rise in fibrinogen alone", "C": "Beta-gamma bridging of nephrotic syndrome, from retained large proteins", "D": "Beta-gamma bridging of cirrhosis, from a rise in IgA"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Beta-gamma bridging of cirrhosis, from a rise in IgA): Beta-gamma bridging is a feature of cirrhosis from a rise in IgA. Nephrotic syndrome shows a prominent alpha-2 band, myeloma gives a narrow spike, and fibrinogen from plasma forms a discrete band between beta and gamma, which is a specimen artifact and not bridging."}, {"id": 1428, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "After a suspected reaction, a nurse returns items to the blood bank for the laboratory workup. The technologist checks the package for the items needed for a full investigation. Which set of items should be returned along with a new post-transfusion blood sample?", "options": {"A": "The empty tube from the original crossmatch sample only", "B": "The patient's chart only, since the unit has already been used", "C": "The nursing schedule and the unit's shipping box only", "D": "The bag, the administration set and all the labels"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The bag, the administration set and all the labels): The bag, administration set and labels go back with a fresh post-transfusion sample so the laboratory can check identity and test the unit. The chart alone, the old tube or packaging would not allow the clerical check, the repeat typing or the culture of the unit."}, {"id": 1429, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A quantitative viral load control gives Ct values within 0.3 cycles when tested five times in one run, but three technologists get results that differ by more than one log between days. Which characteristic is acceptable, and which is a concern?", "options": {"A": "Reproducibility is acceptable, while repeatability within the run is a concern.", "B": "Neither is a concern, because control values naturally vary somewhat between days.", "C": "Repeatability is acceptable, but between-day reproducibility is a concern to investigate.", "D": "Both are acceptable, since Ct values that agree inside one run show good accuracy."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeatability is acceptable, but between-day reproducibility is a concern to investigate.): Tight results within a run show good repeatability, but a difference of a log between days and operators shows poor reproducibility, which points to variables such as reagent handling, extraction or calibration. Within-run agreement does not prove accuracy. A one-log shift far exceeds normal day-to-day variation, so it must be investigated."}, {"id": 1430, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist reviews which tests are affected by anticoagulant therapy when planning thrombophilia workups. Genetic tests are unaffected by drugs, but several functional assays are affected. Which test can be performed reliably at any time despite anticoagulation? The result will be used to plan long-term treatment.", "options": {"A": "Antithrombin functional assay run by chromogenic methods on plasma", "B": "Factor V Leiden gene mutation testing by molecular methods", "C": "Protein C functional assay run by clot-based methods on plasma", "D": "Lupus anticoagulant screen run by clot-based methods on plasma"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Factor V Leiden gene mutation testing by molecular methods): Genetic tests such as factor V Leiden are not affected by anticoagulants or acute clotting. The functional assays for antithrombin and protein C and the lupus anticoagulant screen can be altered by heparin, warfarin or DOACs."}, {"id": 1431, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A clinic from another country faxes a fasting glucose of 90 mg/dL for a transferred patient, and the receiving laboratory chart requires the value in SI units used on Canadian reports. Applying the accepted conversion factor for glucose, which result correctly expresses this value?", "options": {"A": "2.3 mmol/L, obtained by dividing 90 by 38.7", "B": "5.0 mmol/L, obtained by dividing 90 by 18", "C": "1620 mmol/L, obtained by multiplying 90 by 18", "D": "0.20 mmol/L, obtained by dividing 18 by 90"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (5.0 mmol/L, obtained by dividing 90 by 18): Glucose in mg/dL is divided by 18 to give mmol/L, so 90 becomes 5.0. Multiplying by 18 moves the number in the wrong direction and gives an impossible value. Inverting the fraction is a slip, and 38.7 is the factor for cholesterol, not glucose."}, {"id": 1432, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "When a real-time PCR run is analysed, the software draws a horizontal threshold line across the amplification plots on the fluorescence axis. The technologist checking the setup wants to confirm that the line is positioned correctly for reliable Ct values. Where should the threshold sit relative to the amplification curves?", "options": {"A": "Within the exponential phase, well above the background baseline fluorescence.", "B": "At the cycle where the no-template control first shows signal above background", "C": "In the plateau phase, where fluorescence reaches its highest values across cycles.", "D": "Below the baseline, so that every well crosses it at the very first cycle."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Within the exponential phase, well above the background baseline fluorescence.): Ct values are most reproducible when the threshold crosses curves during the exponential phase, where amplification is proportional to starting template, and above baseline noise. In the plateau phase, curves converge and lose their relationship to starting amount. A line below baseline would give meaningless Ct values. The no-template control is not used to place the threshold."}, {"id": 1433, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A woman's CBC results are shown in the image, and the printed report also includes the red cell indices that the analyzer calculates from the primary results. A technologist is verifying one index by hand. Using only the values shown in the image, what is the mean cell hemoglobin, and how is it calculated?", "options": {"A": "25.0 pg, from hemoglobin divided by the red cell count", "B": "31.7 pg, from hemoglobin divided by the hematocrit in litres per litre", "C": "79 pg, from hematocrit divided by the red cell count in the image", "D": "361 pg, from hemoglobin multiplied by the red cell count"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (25.0 pg, from hemoglobin divided by the red cell count): MCH is hemoglobin divided by the red cell count: 95 g/L divided by 3.8 x10^12/L gives 25.0 pg. Hemoglobin divided by hematocrit is the MCHC, hematocrit divided by red cell count is the MCV, and multiplying hemoglobin by red cell count has no meaning.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "CBC, adult woman", "columns": ["Test", "Result", "Units", "Reference interval", "Flag"], "rows": [["WBC", "6.2", "x10^9/L", "4.0 to 11.0", ""], ["RBC", "3.8", "x10^12/L", "4.0 to 5.2", "L"], ["Hemoglobin", "95", "g/L", "120 to 160", "L"], ["Hematocrit", "0.30", "L/L", "0.36 to 0.46", "L"], ["Platelets", "240", "x10^9/L", "150 to 400", ""]], "flag_col": 4, "caption": ""}, "image": "question_images/q_1433.png"}, {"id": 1434, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A trainee is asked which of three sequencing scopes produces the most data and the highest number of variants of uncertain meaning, while also being the best of the three options at finding structural variants in noncoding regions. Which testing scope answers the question?", "options": {"A": "Whole exome sequencing, which covers only the protein coding regions", "B": "Single gene testing, which covers only the coding exons of one gene", "C": "A hereditary cancer gene panel, which targets a curated set of genes", "D": "Whole genome sequencing, covering coding and noncoding regions alike"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Whole genome sequencing, covering coding and noncoding regions alike): Whole genome sequencing covers coding and noncoding regions and suits structural variants, but generates more data and more variants of uncertain meaning. Exome sequencing covers only protein coding regions, roughly one to two percent of the genome. Panels and single genes target far less, so they produce less data."}, {"id": 1435, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A smear from a patient with chronic bleeding shows red cells that are small and pale with widened central pallor. Some cells are thin and elongated, resembling pencils, and the RDW is raised. Which laboratory diagnosis do these morphologic features support?", "options": {"A": "Thalassemia trait, with a normal RDW and a raised RBC count", "B": "Iron deficiency anemia, with hypochromic microcytic cells, pencil cells, a raised RDW and low ferritin", "C": "Hereditary spherocytosis, with dense small cells lacking central pallor and a raised MCHC", "D": "Megaloblastic anemia, with macro-ovalocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils and a low reticulocyte count"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Iron deficiency anemia, with hypochromic microcytic cells, pencil cells, a raised RDW and low ferritin): Hypochromic microcytes with pencil cells and a raised RDW are the typical smear picture of iron deficiency. Thalassemia trait tends to have a normal RDW. Megaloblastic anemia gives large cells, and hereditary spherocytosis gives small dense cells without pallor."}, {"id": 1436, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A technologist is performing tests on a cord blood sample and reviews the list of tests done. She notes that one of the routine grouping steps is deliberately omitted for cord blood. Which step is not performed? The baby is 20 minutes old and the mother is group O.", "options": {"A": "Reverse grouping, since the baby's serum antibodies are maternal.", "B": "Forward grouping, since the baby's cells are not yet fully developed.", "C": "Rh typing, since the mother's status already answers the question.", "D": "The direct antiglobulin test, since cord cells are too immature to react in that test"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reverse grouping, since the baby's serum antibodies are maternal.): Reverse grouping is not done on cord blood because a newborn has not made its own ABO antibodies. Forward grouping, Rh typing and the DAT remain part of the testing that guides Rh immune globulin decisions."}, {"id": 1437, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In a quality assurance course for staff who screen slides, Cytology corrective action follows a defined pattern intended to promote learning rather than blame. Which sequence correctly lists the steps in the order described in laboratory quality practice for handling a problem?", "options": {"A": "Document the problem, blame the screener, retrain everyone, and close the file quickly", "B": "Find the cause, wait for repeated events, and then identify the problem", "C": "Act on the problem, ignore the cause, and document the event only if it recurs", "D": "Identify the problem, find the cause, act, check that the action worked, and document"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Identify the problem, find the cause, act, check that the action worked, and document): Corrective action moves from identifying the problem, finding the cause and acting, to checking whether the action worked and documenting it. The aim is learning, though repeated unsafe practice cannot be ignored. Skipping cause analysis or documentation leaves the same error free to recur."}, {"id": 1438, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A ward nurse prints identification labels for a patient at the nursing station, attaches them to two empty lavender-top tubes, and carries the tubes to the patient's room for a pretransfusion draw. The transfusion service later receives the tubes and learns how they were prepared. Why is this labelling practice unacceptable?", "options": {"A": "The printed label ink can react with the EDTA additive inside the tube", "B": "A label prepared away from the patient can be attached to the wrong tube", "C": "The tube cannot be centrifuged properly if the label was applied before collection", "D": "Nursing staff are not permitted to handle tubes destined for the transfusion service"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A label prepared away from the patient can be attached to the wrong tube): Labels must be applied at the bedside after the patient is identified, so the tube and patient stay linked. A label made in advance can end up on a tube collected from someone else. Label ink does not react with the additive, and there is no rule barring nurses from collecting samples, so those reasons do not apply."}, {"id": 1439, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist sees cells with multinucleation, chromatin pushed to the nuclear edge, and nuclei pressed tightly against one another with a smooth ground-glass appearance. A trainee asks which organism this pattern represents and what shorthand summarizes its features. The smear background shows scattered inflammatory cells.", "options": {"A": "Herpes simplex virus, recalled by multinucleation, margination and moulding", "B": "Candida species, recalled by pseudohyphae, budding and piercing", "C": "Trichomonas vaginalis, recalled by pear shape, granules and eccentric nucleus", "D": "Actinomyces species, recalled by cotton balls, filaments and starbursts"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Herpes simplex virus, recalled by multinucleation, margination and moulding): Multinucleation, margination of chromatin and moulding of nuclei, the three Ms, identify herpes simplex virus. Candida shows pseudohyphae, Trichomonas is a pear-shaped protozoan, and Actinomyces forms tangled filamentous colonies."}, {"id": 1440, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist sustains a needlestick while working at the microbiology bench with a clinical isolate. After washing the site, what is the required next step in the laboratory safety procedure? The technologist is calm and has already washed the area with soap and water, but the next steps must follow procedure.", "options": {"A": "Continue working and mention it at the end of the week", "B": "Recap the needle to prevent further injury before reporting", "C": "Report the exposure at once and document it", "D": "Apply an adhesive bandage and take no further action"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the exposure at once and document it): Exposures are reported immediately so follow-up and treatment can be arranged, and they are documented. Delaying, recapping the needle, or ignoring it puts the technologist and others at risk."}, {"id": 1441, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cerebrospinal fluid cytospin shows a few clustered cells with round nuclei and delicate cytoplasm shortly after a spinal procedure. The clinical history gives no evidence of tumour. The pathologist wonders whether these are benign cells that can mimic tumour. Which cell types are known to appear after a procedure and cause this problem?", "options": {"A": "Ependymal and choroid plexus cells, which can be mistaken for tumour", "B": "Ciliated bronchial cells, which are sometimes mistaken for a tumour on smears", "C": "Signet-ring cells, which can be mistaken for tumour", "D": "Alveolar macrophages, which can be mistaken for tumour"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ependymal and choroid plexus cells, which can be mistaken for tumour): Ependymal and choroid plexus cells may appear after a procedure and mimic tumour. Ciliated bronchial cells and alveolar macrophages belong to the respiratory tract, and signet-ring cells are a malignant feature of stomach or breast cancer."}, {"id": 1442, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A marrow from a patient with a myelodysplastic picture shows monosomy 7 in 12 cells, deletion of 5q in 10 of those same cells, and trisomy 8 in 3 other cells, along with several more changes. The clinician asks how this karyotype affects the outlook. How should the technologist frame the prognostic meaning of this complex karyotype?", "options": {"A": "A complex karyotype with these changes generally carries a favourable prognosis.", "B": "A complex karyotype only reflects poor culture quality and has no clinical meaning.", "C": "A complex karyotype with these changes proves the patient has chronic myeloid leukemia.", "D": "A complex karyotype with monosomy 7 and 5q deletion generally carries a poorer prognosis."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A complex karyotype with monosomy 7 and 5q deletion generally carries a poorer prognosis.): Several abnormalities together, plus monosomy 7 and 5q deletion, generally predict a poorer outcome. A favourable outlook is linked to changes like t(8;21). Poor culture quality does not create reproducible clonal changes, and CML is defined by the Philadelphia chromosome."}, {"id": 1443, "domain": "histology", "question": "A pathologist has inked the surgical margin of a resected skin lesion, and the report depends on how close the tumour comes to that margin. The tissue is being embedded. How should the technologist place the specimen in the mould?", "options": {"A": "Oriented at random since ink is visible in every position", "B": "Oriented so the inked edge faces the cassette backing plate", "C": "Oriented so the inked edge sits deepest in the block", "D": "Oriented so the inked margin is cut first or plainly seen"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Oriented so the inked margin is cut first or plainly seen): The margin must appear on the slide, so the inked edge is placed to be cut first or to be plainly visible. Burying it or facing it toward the backing risks losing it during facing. Ink is visible only if that edge is actually cut."}, {"id": 1444, "domain": "cytology", "question": "An endocervical group on an inflamed slide from a 50-year-old with a recent vaginal infection is being judged by a screener who sees enlarged nuclei and several nucleoli. She wants to know which nuclear and cell border features are compatible with reactive change in endocervical cells rather than an atypical glandular lesion.", "options": {"A": "Nuclear molding with crushed smeared chromatin and scant cytoplasm in tightly packed small cells", "B": "Hyperchromatic nuclei with irregular membrane notches on a background of tumour diathesis", "C": "Marked pleomorphism with coarse clumped chromatin, irregular membranes, and feathered poorly defined borders", "D": "Slightly crowded enlarged nuclei with prominent nucleoli, fine chromatin, and sharp cell borders"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Slightly crowded enlarged nuclei with prominent nucleoli, fine chromatin, and sharp cell borders): Reactive endocervical cells can show enlarged, slightly crowded nuclei and prominent nucleoli, but the chromatin stays fine and the borders stay sharp. Coarse chromatin, feathering, and irregular membranes point away from a benign process. Molding with smeared chromatin belongs to small cell malignancy."}, {"id": 1445, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A follow-up bone marrow karyotype shows three cells with the same missing chromosome 7 and 17 cells that are normal. The trainee is unsure whether this counts as a clone or a random loss. According to the cancer clonality rule taught in this laboratory, how should it be classified?", "options": {"A": "Not a clone, because a loss needs to appear in more than half the cells", "B": "A clone, because a loss needs at least three cells with the same missing chromosome.", "C": "A clone, because any loss seen in even one cell defines a clone by itself.", "D": "Not a clone, because a loss needs at least four cells with the missing chromosome."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A clone, because a loss needs at least three cells with the same missing chromosome.): A loss is clonal when at least three cells share the same missing chromosome, and this case has exactly three. The threshold is not four cells and is not a proportion of the total. A loss in a single cell is likely random and does not define a clone."}, {"id": 1446, "domain": "histology", "question": "A very small endoscopic biopsy fragment, smaller than a grain of rice, is being placed into a cassette, and the technologist worries that it may slip through the perforations during processing. Which practical step best prevents loss of the fragment and helps it to be seen at embedding?", "options": {"A": "Wrap it in lens paper or use a biopsy bag or foam pad, possibly with eosin.", "B": "Leave it in the specimen container and process it later with the larger specimens.", "C": "Place it loose in the cassette and rely on the lid to hold it in place.", "D": "Cut it into several smaller pieces so that each one can fit between the holes."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wrap it in lens paper or use a biopsy bag or foam pad, possibly with eosin.): Wrapping or bagging keeps tiny fragments inside the cassette, and a touch of eosin stains them pink so they can be found at embedding. Loose fragments can escape through the perforations or stick to the lid. Cutting a small biopsy into pieces only multiplies the fragments that can be lost, and delaying processing risks autolysis."}, {"id": 1447, "domain": "hematology", "question": "The technologist counts an average of 12 platelets per oil immersion field over ten fields, and the laboratory factor is 20. The analyzer platelet count on the same specimen is 62 x10^9/L. What is the smear estimate, and what should be done next?", "options": {"A": "24 x10^9/L, which agrees with the instrument after rounding", "B": "240 x10^9/L, which disagrees, so repeat and investigate", "C": "2.4 x10^9/L, which is too low to compare with the instrument", "D": "62 x10^9/L, which matches the instrument, so release the result"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (240 x10^9/L, which disagrees, so repeat and investigate): The estimate equals the average number per field times the factor, so 12 x 20 gives 240 x10^9/L. That is nearly four times the instrument value, so the results disagree and the sample is repeated and investigated for causes such as fragments or clumping. The other numbers misuse the factor, and a disagreement is never released unresolved."}, {"id": 1448, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A patient recovering from septicemia is found to have T-activated polyagglutinable red cells. The patient now needs a transfusion for anemia, and the transfusion service has both standard red cell units and washed red cells with very little residual plasma. Which product is preferred for this patient, and why?", "options": {"A": "Group AB plasma products, because AB plasma lacks anti-A and anti-B and cannot agglutinate them", "B": "Frozen red cells, because polyagglutination is a reagent artifact that freezing removes from the donor units", "C": "Standard red cells, because the patient's own serum contains the antibody", "D": "Washed red cells, because adult plasma contains antibodies that can agglutinate the patient's cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Washed red cells, because adult plasma contains antibodies that can agglutinate the patient's cells): Most adult sera contain antibodies against the exposed antigen, so plasma-rich products can cause a reaction, and washed cells or low-plasma products are preferred. Standard units carry more plasma. Group AB plasma is not a red cell product and still contains these antibodies, and polyagglutination is a property of the patient's cells, not a reagent artifact that freezing would remove."}, {"id": 1449, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A ward manager argues that point-of-care testing should replace central laboratory testing for all electrolytes because results come back faster. The laboratory supervisor disagrees, noting that the devices would be run by nurses and respiratory therapists. Which is a recognized disadvantage of point-of-care testing compared with central laboratory analyzers?", "options": {"A": "Larger sample volumes and longer turnaround times than the automated central analyzers require", "B": "A requirement for radioactive labels that restrict use outside the laboratory", "C": "Lower precision and more chances for operator error than automated central analyzers", "D": "Inability to measure glucose, blood gases or hemoglobin at the bedside"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lower precision and more chances for operator error than automated central analyzers): Point-of-care devices tend to be less precise, and testing by many operators outside the laboratory adds error opportunities. Their advantages are speed and small sample volume, which is the opposite of the first distractor. Glucose, gases and hemoglobin are common bedside tests, and no radioactive labels are needed."}, {"id": 1450, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A trainee is asked to describe the nuclear features of Cell 2 in the image before assigning a category, using the same magnification as Cell 1 beside it. Which description of Cell 2 is accurate when compared with the normal mature cell shown next to it?", "options": {"A": "A small nucleus with fine chromatin and a smooth round outline within abundant cytoplasm", "B": "A nucleus filling most of the cell, with pale chromatin and a smooth round outline", "C": "A nucleus filling most of the cell, with very dark chromatin and irregular outline", "D": "A moderately enlarged nucleus with coarse chromatin, a smooth outline and several prominent nucleoli"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A nucleus filling most of the cell, with very dark chromatin and irregular outline): Cell 2 has a nucleus occupying most of the cell, with very dark chromatin and an irregular outline, and it has no nucleoli. The small bland nucleus described in one wrong choice belongs to the normal Cell 1. The pale, smooth nucleus and the nucleoli described in the other wrong choices are not present in Cell 2.", "image_type": "cell_micrograph", "image_params": {"cells": [{"label": "1", "nc_ratio": 0.05, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "squamous"}, {"label": "2", "nc_ratio": 0.6, "chromatin": "hyperchromatic", "outline": "irregular", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "squamous"}], "caption": "Cells at equal magnification", "context": "cervical", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1450.png"}, {"id": 1451, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A physician suspects gonorrhea and asks for a genital culture. The laboratory inoculates a chocolate-based agar that contains several antibiotics to suppress normal flora. Which medium is this, and what is it selective for? The specimen arrives in a swab tube, and the laboratory wishes to inhibit normal flora while allowing the fastidious target to grow.", "options": {"A": "Sheep blood agar, an enriched medium that is selective for beta-hemolytic streptococci only", "B": "MacConkey agar, a bile salt and crystal violet medium that is selective for lactose-fermenting Enterobacterales", "C": "Eosin methylene blue agar, a dye medium that is selective for gram-positive cocci", "D": "Thayer-Martin agar, a chocolate-based medium with antibiotics, selective for pathogenic Neisseria species"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Thayer-Martin agar, a chocolate-based medium with antibiotics, selective for pathogenic Neisseria species): Thayer-Martin is a chocolate-based medium with antibiotics that permits pathogenic Neisseria while suppressing other flora. The other media are not chocolate-based and serve different purposes; sheep blood agar is a general enriched medium, not a selective one."}, {"id": 1452, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A small amount of fixative containing glacial acetic acid is spilled on the bench beside the fume hood, and the vapour is strong. A technologist also has a splash on a glove and a few drops on the forearm. Which response shows correct safety practice for this situation?", "options": {"A": "Cover the spill with a tissue and leave it, since it will evaporate safely", "B": "Use the spill kit, and flush the skin at the eyewash or shower if needed", "C": "Ignore the vapour and continue working, since acetic acid is not corrosive", "D": "Wipe it up with a paper towel and pour it down the sink with water"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use the spill kit, and flush the skin at the eyewash or shower if needed): Acetic acid is corrosive with a strong vapour, so staff use the spill kit, eyewash or shower where needed and follow the procedure, and they must know where these are. Sink disposal or ignoring the spill is unsafe."}, {"id": 1453, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A teacher draws three squamous cells on a whiteboard from the deepest layer to the surface and asks how the nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio changes with maturation. A student must also explain why a large nucleus in a mature-looking cell is a warning sign. What is the trend?", "options": {"A": "It steadily decreases, so superficial cells have the lowest ratio of the squamous series", "B": "It stays constant across layers, and only nuclear staining changes with maturation", "C": "It rises in the intermediate layer and then returns to baseline in the surface layer", "D": "It steadily increases, so superficial cells carry the highest ratio of the squamous series"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It steadily decreases, so superficial cells have the lowest ratio of the squamous series): As squamous cells mature the cytoplasm grows and the nucleus shrinks, so the ratio falls from parabasal to superficial. In abnormal cells this falling pattern is lost, which is why a big nucleus in a mature-looking cell is a warning sign."}, {"id": 1454, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A patient with dark urine, jaundice, and an ALT of 1,450 U/L who shared a meal with a sick food handler two weeks earlier is anti-HAV IgM positive. A second, asymptomatic patient with a normal ALT is anti-HAV IgG positive only. How should these results be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Neither has hepatitis A, since these antibodies only show hepatitis B exposure", "B": "Both have acute hepatitis A infection, since both antibody classes indicate active disease", "C": "The first has past hepatitis A, while the second has acute infection", "D": "The first has acute hepatitis A, while the second has past infection or vaccination"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The first has acute hepatitis A, while the second has past infection or vaccination): IgM marks acute infection, whereas IgG alone marks past infection or vaccination. Both are hepatitis A markers, not hepatitis B ones."}, {"id": 1455, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A lymph node biopsy shows a dense lymphocytic infiltrate that is difficult to classify as reactive or malignant. The pathologist requests a PCR test on the extracted DNA that looks for a dominant product from immunoglobulin heavy chain genes. What is this test evaluating?", "options": {"A": "Viral load, meaning the copies of lymphotropic virus in the tissue.", "B": "Chimerism, meaning the proportion of donor cells in a transplant recipient.", "C": "Ploidy, meaning the total chromosome number in the malignant lymphocytes.", "D": "Clonality, meaning whether the lymphocytes share one identical gene rearrangement."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clonality, meaning whether the lymphocytes share one identical gene rearrangement.): Reactive lymphocytes carry many different receptor gene rearrangements, giving a polyclonal pattern, while a malignant population shares one rearrangement and gives a dominant product. That is clonality testing. Chimerism applies to transplant engraftment. Viral load measures a pathogen. Ploidy is assessed by cytometry or cytogenetics, not by this PCR."}, {"id": 1456, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist finds brown staining on the negative control slide of an immunocytochemistry run, and the positive control and patient slide also stain. The patient cells look convincingly positive. How should the technologist interpret this run before any result is released?", "options": {"A": "The background is a problem, so the patient result cannot be trusted until repeated", "B": "The negative control staining proves the antigen is present in every single specimen tested", "C": "The result is valid because the patient cells and the positive control both stained well", "D": "The result may be released if the pathologist agrees the staining looks specific enough"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The background is a problem, so the patient result cannot be trusted until repeated): A negative control that stains shows nonspecific background or reagent problems, so the patient result cannot be trusted. Visual convincingness does not override failed controls. Staining in the negative control does not indicate antigen presence, and pathologist preference cannot validate a compromised run."}, {"id": 1457, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A group A newborn born to a group O mother has a positive cord DAT. An eluate is prepared, and the last wash supernatant is nonreactive. The eluate reacts with group A1 cells but not with group O cells on the panel. What does this finding indicate?", "options": {"A": "A cold autoantibody is present, since reactions with A1 cells occur only at room temperature", "B": "The eluate is invalid, since a nonreactive last wash means antibody was not recovered", "C": "Anti-D from the mother is coating the baby's cells, consistent with Rh hemolytic disease", "D": "Anti-A from the mother is coating the baby's cells, consistent with ABO hemolytic disease"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anti-A from the mother is coating the baby's cells, consistent with ABO hemolytic disease): A nonreactive last wash shows the eluate is valid, and reactivity with A1 cells but not O cells identifies anti-A. Together with a group O mother and group A baby, this points to ABO hemolytic disease of the newborn. Rh disease would require D-positive cells to react, and a nonreactive wash is what makes the eluate acceptable."}, {"id": 1458, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A nurse asks why a heel stick is preferred to a fingerstick in a nine-month-old baby, having seen an adult finger used earlier that day. She also wants to know which part of the heel to avoid. Which answer is correct for the site to avoid on the heel?", "options": {"A": "The central heel, because bone lies close to the skin and can be injured", "B": "The back of the heel, because the sample from there clots too quickly", "C": "The inner medial edge, because large veins run close beneath the surface", "D": "The outer lateral edge, because the skin there is too thick for a lancet"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The central heel, because bone lies close to the skin and can be injured): Punctures are made on the lateral or medial plantar surface, and the central part of the heel is avoided because the calcaneus is close under the skin and osteomyelitis or bone damage can result. Both edges are the recommended sites. The finger is avoided in infants because there is not enough tissue depth, not because it is contaminated."}, {"id": 1459, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist sets up fungal cultures from a lung tissue biopsy taken from a patient with a chronic cavitary lesion. Both Sabouraud dextrose agar and brain heart infusion agar with antibiotics are inoculated, and the physician asks when a negative report can be issued. What is the standard practice for fungal culture incubation?", "options": {"A": "Hold cultures at minus 20 degrees C for one week before reading them under the microscope", "B": "Hold cultures at 42 degrees C for three days, since most pathogenic fungi grow that fast", "C": "Hold cultures at 25 to 30 degrees C for up to four weeks", "D": "Hold cultures at 35 degrees C for forty-eight hours, the interval used for routine bacterial plates"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold cultures at 25 to 30 degrees C for up to four weeks): Many fungi grow slowly, so plates are held up to four weeks at 25 to 30 degrees C, with a second set sometimes at 35 degrees C. Short incubation misses slow growers, and freezing or high temperature would prevent growth."}, {"id": 1460, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with severe diarrhea has sodium 136, chloride 108 and bicarbonate 14 mmol/L. What is the anion gap, and what type of acidosis does the pattern support? The physician asks the laboratory to interpret the gap and to name the type of acidosis.", "options": {"A": "The gap is 14 mmol/L, which is high and supports an acidosis from added lactic acid", "B": "The gap is 30 mmol/L, which is high and supports an acidosis from ketone accumulation", "C": "The gap is 22 mmol/L, which is normal and supports an acidosis from kidney failure", "D": "The gap is 14 mmol/L, which is normal and fits bicarbonate loss"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The gap is 14 mmol/L, which is normal and fits bicarbonate loss): The gap is 136 minus (108 plus 14), which equals 14 mmol/L and lies within the 8 to 16 range. Chloride rises to replace lost bicarbonate, giving a normal gap, hyperchloremic acidosis, as seen with diarrhea. A high gap would suggest added acids."}, {"id": 1461, "domain": "histology", "question": "A stain run for a routine special stain fails when the positive control does not react, but the patient slide looks acceptable at a glance. The supervisor asks for the corrective action to be documented. What information belongs in the corrective action log entry?", "options": {"A": "A statement that the patient slide was released to save time", "B": "The pathologist's final diagnosis for the patient case", "C": "Only the initials of the technologist who ran the stain", "D": "The date, the problem found and the fix that was applied"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The date, the problem found and the fix that was applied): The log records the date, the problem found and the fix, so the failure and its resolution can be traced during accreditation. The technologist's initials alone are insufficient, and releasing the patient slide after a failed control is not acceptable."}, {"id": 1462, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist stains a routine batch of slides at the staining bench in the afternoon and reviews them under the microscope. Blue-black particles appear scattered over the sections on a slide after routine staining. Which cause is the most likely explanation for this defect?", "options": {"A": "Unfiltered hematoxylin that carries dye particles onto the section", "B": "Exhausted eosin that leaves particles on the section during counterstaining", "C": "A cold water bath that traps small particles under the section", "D": "An overly dull blade that drags debris across the section surface"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Unfiltered hematoxylin that carries dye particles onto the section): Blue-black particles on a slide can come from unfiltered hematoxylin, so the stain is filtered. A dull blade causes lines or chatter, exhausted eosin gives poor pink staining, and a cold water bath causes wrinkles."}, {"id": 1463, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Measurement of a purified RNA extract gives an A260/A280 of 2.05 and an A260/A230 of 2.1, with a concentration of 85 ng/µL. The procedure acceptance range for RNA purity is about 2.0 for the first ratio. How should these purity values be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "They show protein contamination, because ratios above 1.8 mean excess protein.", "B": "They show DNA contamination, because pure RNA should read exactly 1.8.", "C": "They show salt carry-over, because the second ratio should be above 2.5.", "D": "They meet expectations for clean RNA, so no re-extraction is needed for purity."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (They meet expectations for clean RNA, so no re-extraction is needed for purity.): Pure RNA gives an A260/A280 of about 2.0, and a clean sample gives an A260/A230 between about 2.0 and 2.2, so both values are acceptable. A ratio of 1.8 belongs to pure DNA, and protein contamination lowers the first ratio rather than raising it."}, {"id": 1464, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A sequencing reaction produced a trace with small peaks whose heights fall steadily along the read until the end is barely visible above baseline. The PCR product concentration measured before cycle sequencing was far below the amount the protocol specifies. What is the most likely cause, and what should the technologist do?", "options": {"A": "Residual primer, so trim the read and report the clean portion", "B": "Too little template, so requantify the product and repeat with an adequate amount", "C": "Too much template, so dilute the product and repeat the reaction", "D": "A dye blob, so trim the affected bases and report the remaining sequence"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Too little template, so requantify the product and repeat with an adequate amount): Weak signal with a low measured concentration points to insufficient template, and the reaction is repeated with the proper input. Excess template would be suggested by a high measured concentration. Residual primer gives overlapping sequences, and a dye blob is a localized artefact rather than a global weak signal."}, {"id": 1465, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The laboratory processes many such smears every week, so speed matters. A sputum specimen for tuberculosis culture is inoculated onto Lowenstein-Jensen medium. The technologist asks how long visible colonies of Mycobacterium tuberculosis usually take to appear. What is the expected range on solid medium?", "options": {"A": "Two to three days, and liquid systems do not change the time to detection", "B": "Two to eight weeks, and liquid systems can shorten the time to detection", "C": "Six to twelve months, and liquid systems can shorten the time to detection", "D": "Overnight to two days, and liquid systems make the time to detection longer"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Two to eight weeks, and liquid systems can shorten the time to detection): Mycobacterium tuberculosis is slow growing, and colonies appear in two to eight weeks on Lowenstein-Jensen medium, while automated liquid systems shorten detection. Overnight or two-day growth describes common rapid organisms, and months is much too long."}, {"id": 1466, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A liquid-based cervical slide has about 7,000 well-preserved squamous cells and is otherwise clean, but no endocervical or metaplastic cells are seen. The trainee proposes calling it unsatisfactory. The reviewing cytotechnologist has already confirmed that the preservative and the vial were correct for this system. What is the best judgement?", "options": {"A": "Satisfactory, because absence of transformation zone components alone does not make it unsatisfactory", "B": "Unsatisfactory, because 7,000 cells is below the minimum needed for liquid-based slides", "C": "Satisfactory, because transformation zone cells are not part of the adequacy criteria for liquid-based slides", "D": "Unsatisfactory, because a slide lacking endocervical or metaplastic cells fails the adequacy criteria for liquid-based preparations"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Satisfactory, because absence of transformation zone components alone does not make it unsatisfactory): The squamous count exceeds the 5,000 minimum, and missing transformation zone cells do not make a sample unsatisfactory, though the absence may be noted. The transformation zone is assessed, but is not a rejection criterion."}, {"id": 1467, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Only a few cells in a urine specimen show hyperchromatic nuclei, irregular membranes and high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratios. The changes look developed and worrying, but too few cells are affected to reach a diagnosis of malignancy. The pathologist must select the Paris category. Which one applies?", "options": {"A": "High-grade urothelial carcinoma, since even a few cells with those features suffice for the category", "B": "Other malignancy, since limited cells indicate a metastatic tumour from a nearby organ", "C": "Atypical urothelial cells, since hyperchromasia and high ratios match the atypical criteria exactly", "D": "Suspicious for high-grade urothelial carcinoma, since changes approach malignancy but too few cells are affected"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Suspicious for high-grade urothelial carcinoma, since changes approach malignancy but too few cells are affected): Suspicious for high-grade urothelial carcinoma applies when changes approach malignancy but there are too few cells or the changes are not fully developed. The malignant category needs a meaningful number of cells. Atypical is reserved for milder changes that stop short of suspicion. Other malignancy is for non-urothelial tumours."}, {"id": 1468, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A 55-year-old's requisition says she takes oral estrogen for menopausal symptoms. Her slide shows about 60 percent superficial cells and 40 percent intermediate cells, no atypia, and a clean background. A junior colleague is unsure whether to escalate. How should the slide be handled and reported?", "options": {"A": "Report an epithelial cell abnormality because superficial cells are unexpected at that age", "B": "Correlate the pattern with the listed hormone therapy and report accordingly without alarm", "C": "Ask the clinic to stop the therapy and collect a repeat sample", "D": "Refer the slide to the pathologist as a suspected estrogen-secreting ovarian tumour"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Correlate the pattern with the listed hormone therapy and report accordingly without alarm): Hormone therapy shifts the pattern toward estrogen effect, and the requisition explains it. There is no mismatch to escalate. Calling an abnormality or stopping treatment would be a response to a normal finding."}, {"id": 1469, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A swab from a distant clinic will take several hours to reach the laboratory. The nurse places it in Amies transport medium. What is the function of this type of medium during transit? The clinic is located in a remote area, and the courier picks up specimens only twice a day.", "options": {"A": "It supports rapid multiplication so that colony counts rise before arrival", "B": "It changes colour to show which organisms are present", "C": "It kills contaminants so only the pathogen remains viable in transit", "D": "It keeps organisms alive without allowing them to multiply"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It keeps organisms alive without allowing them to multiply): Transport media maintain viability while preventing overgrowth, so the culture reflects the specimen at collection. Growth media would shift the ratios of organisms, and transport media neither kill selected organisms nor act as differential indicators."}, {"id": 1470, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist finds that a slide was mislabelled during a busy shift, and a patient's result may have been attached to the wrong person. No harm has been reported yet. The technologist worries about looking careless. What is the best course of action?", "options": {"A": "Discard the slide so the discrepancy cannot be traced back to anyone in the lab", "B": "Wait for the pathologist to notice the mistake during routine sign-out", "C": "Correct the label quietly and say nothing unless the error is found later", "D": "Report it promptly through the quality system, since hiding errors may allow harm"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report it promptly through the quality system, since hiding errors may allow harm): Errors such as mislabelled slides are reported promptly through the quality system, because hiding them may let patient harm continue and a safe reporting culture catches problems early. Quiet corrections, waiting, or discarding evidence remove the chance to protect the patient and to learn from the event. A culture where staff feel safe to report near misses helps catch problems early."}, {"id": 1471, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A 66-year-old with a known lung carcinoma has a new 3 cm adrenal mass on CT, and FNA is planned. The technologist is asked what the aspirate is meant to distinguish and which cause is most common. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "It separates adenoma from metastasis, most often from lung carcinoma, or pheochromocytoma.", "B": "It separates adrenal from renal tissue only, since both appear on kidney imaging.", "C": "It separates primary liver tumours from bile duct tumours lying near the gland.", "D": "It separates infection from inflammation, most often from tuberculosis of the gland."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It separates adenoma from metastasis, most often from lung carcinoma, or pheochromocytoma.): Adrenal FNA distinguishes an adenoma from a metastasis, most often lung carcinoma, as well as pheochromocytoma. Distinguishing kidney from adrenal tissue is a question for imaging. Tuberculosis and liver or bile duct tumours are not the usual diagnostic questions at this site."}, {"id": 1472, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A four-year-old with fever and swollen glands has a smear showing many large reactive lymphocytes with abundant blue cytoplasm. A rapid monospot test performed on the first day of illness returns negative. The physician asks whether this rules out infectious mononucleosis. What is the best response from the laboratory?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because a negative rapid test excludes infection at every age and stage", "B": "No, because the heterophile test can be negative early in illness or in young children", "C": "No, because a negative test means the lymphocytes are certainly neoplastic instead", "D": "Yes, because reactive lymphocytes appear only when the heterophile test is positive"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No, because the heterophile test can be negative early in illness or in young children): The monospot can be negative early or in young children even when the infection is present, so a negative result does not rule it out. The smear findings remain compatible with reactive change. Reactive cells do not depend on the test, and a negative test does not imply neoplasia."}, {"id": 1473, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Six weeks ago a patient with a warm autoantibody was last transfused, so autoadsorption cannot be done safely. The laboratory chooses alloadsorption to look for hidden alloantibodies. Which cells should be used to remove the warm autoantibody from the serum in this procedure?", "options": {"A": "Several differently phenotyped cells that lack the antigens for the alloantibodies being sought", "B": "The patient's own red cells after acid treatment to release the coating antibody", "C": "Enzyme-treated donor cells, because ficin makes autoantibodies bind without affecting alloantibodies", "D": "A single group O cell that carries every common antigen, to remove all antibodies at once"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Several differently phenotyped cells that lack the antigens for the alloantibodies being sought): Alloadsorption uses several differently phenotyped cells that lack the antigens of the alloantibodies being sought, so the autoantibody is removed while any alloantibody remains in the serum. The patient's own cells may contain donor cells. A cell carrying every antigen would remove the alloantibodies as well."}, {"id": 1474, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A technologist is asked to name the two genotypes that a person of blood group A can have. The class already knows that each parent gives one ABO gene and that the O gene produces no active enzyme. Which pair of genotypes is possible for a group A individual?", "options": {"A": "Genotype AA or genotype AO, since the O gene is silent", "B": "Genotype AO or genotype BO, since one parent gives an O gene", "C": "Genotype AA or genotype AB, since both genes carry the A antigen", "D": "Genotype AA or genotype OO, since silent genes combine with A genes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Genotype AA or genotype AO, since the O gene is silent): An A gene plus either another A gene or a silent O gene gives the group A phenotype. An AB person is group AB, and BO is group B. Two silent O genes give group O because no antigen is formed."}, {"id": 1475, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A fresh unpreserved stool from a patient with watery diarrhea arrives in the laboratory two hours after collection, sitting in a plain container on the courier shelf. The physician wants motile trophozoites identified. The saline wet mount shows only rounded, non-moving, degraded forms. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Stain it directly with trichrome without fixation and report the trophozoite findings", "B": "Examine it as a saline wet mount, since trophozoites stay motile for many hours", "C": "Refrigerate it overnight and examine it the next morning for best results", "D": "Explain that trophozoites degrade quickly, so request a fresh specimen or one collected in preservative"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that trophozoites degrade quickly, so request a fresh specimen or one collected in preservative): Unpreserved stool should be examined within about 30 minutes to see motile trophozoites, so a delayed sample is inadequate and should be recollected or preserved. Overnight refrigeration does not restore motility, and skipping fixation gives poor trichrome results."}, {"id": 1476, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A cord blood sample from the baby of an RhD-negative mother is tested and gives a weak reaction with anti-D that is confirmed as weak D. The mother has no anti-D. The physician asks whether Rh immune globulin is needed. Should the mother receive a dose?", "options": {"A": "Yes, a weak D baby counts as D positive, so she needs the dose.", "B": "Yes, but only if a Kleihauer-Betke test shows more than 1 percent.", "C": "No, the mother must have anti-D before any dose is considered.", "D": "No, a weak D baby is D-negative for the purposes of this decision."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, a weak D baby counts as D positive, so she needs the dose.): A baby who tests weak D positive counts as D positive for the Rh immune globulin decision, so the RhD-negative mother should receive the dose. Waiting for maternal anti-D defeats the purpose, since prophylaxis prevents its formation."}, {"id": 1477, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Forward and reverse typing performed at room temperature on an adult patient give the reactions shown in the image, with an autocontrol included. The sample is correctly identified. What is the best way to resolve the unexpected reverse reaction and confirm the patient's ABO group?", "options": {"A": "Report the patient as group A and issue group A red cells without further testing", "B": "Repeat the reverse typing with prewarmed serum and cells, or with cold-autoadsorbed serum", "C": "Test the reverse typing again with enzyme-treated reagent red cells to remove the cold reaction", "D": "Treat the patient cells with dithiothreitol and repeat the forward typing with anti-A and anti-B"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat the reverse typing with prewarmed serum and cells, or with cold-autoadsorbed serum): The forward typing indicates group A, but the reverse typing shows an unexpected reaction with A1 cells and a reactive autocontrol, which points to a cold autoantibody interfering at room temperature, and prewarmed testing or cold-autoadsorbed serum can resolve it. Reporting the group without resolving the discrepancy ignores the interference, enzyme treatment enhances cold reactions, and dithiothreitol treatment of patient cells does not address a reverse typing problem.", "image_type": "gel_agglutination", "image_params": {"wells": [{"label": "Anti-A", "reaction": "4+"}, {"label": "Anti-B", "reaction": "negative"}, {"label": "A1 cells", "reaction": "2+"}, {"label": "B cells", "reaction": "3+"}, {"label": "Autocontrol", "reaction": "3+"}], "caption": "Gel typing at room temperature with autocontrol"}, "image": "question_images/q_1477.png"}, {"id": 1478, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with a gamma-glutamyltransferase of 310 U/L and an alkaline phosphatase of 260 U/L reports drinking heavily in the last month. A technologist is asked what the combination of enzymes points to before the physician orders further tests. Which source is most likely?", "options": {"A": "A pancreatic source, with acute pancreatitis as the likely cause of both enzymes", "B": "A cardiac source, with myocardial infarction as the likely cause of both enzymes", "C": "A liver source, with cholestasis or alcohol intake as possible causes", "D": "A bone source, with growth or fracture healing as the likely cause"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A liver source, with cholestasis or alcohol intake as possible causes): GGT rises with cholestasis and alcohol, and a high ALP with a high GGT points to a liver source. A bone source would show a normal GGT. Pancreatitis is investigated with lipase, and myocardial infarction with troponin, not these enzymes."}, {"id": 1479, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A lab technologist explains why the PT is prolonged first in a patient starting warfarin. The new resident asks which factor's short half-life accounts for this early PT effect. Which factor is responsible? Warfarin reduces factors II, VII, IX and X, but not at the same speed in the blood.", "options": {"A": "Factor X, because it has the shortest half-life of the vitamin K dependent factors", "B": "Factor VII, because it has the shortest half-life of the vitamin K dependent factors", "C": "Factor IX, because it has the shortest half-life of the vitamin K dependent factors", "D": "Factor II, because it has the shortest half-life of the vitamin K dependent factors"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Factor VII, because it has the shortest half-life of the vitamin K dependent factors): Factor VII has the shortest half-life among the vitamin K dependent factors, so it falls first and prolongs the PT early. Factors II, IX and X have longer half-lives and fall more slowly."}, {"id": 1480, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The susceptibility panel shown is reported for an Enterococcus faecium isolate from a blood culture. The infection control team asks which acquired glycopeptide resistance genotype best explains the vancomycin and teicoplanin results in the table, taking into account the species and the susceptibility to linezolid.", "options": {"A": "vanC, an intrinsic low-level vancomycin trait found in Enterococcus gallinarum and casseliflavus", "B": "vanB, an acquired gene giving variable vancomycin resistance while teicoplanin stays susceptible", "C": "vanA, an acquired gene giving high-level resistance to vancomycin and teicoplanin", "D": "vanE, a rare gene giving low-level vancomycin resistance with full teicoplanin susceptibility"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (vanA, an acquired gene giving high-level resistance to vancomycin and teicoplanin): The table shows a vancomycin MIC of 256 ug/mL with teicoplanin also resistant, the high-level cross resistance pattern of vanA. The vanB type gives variable vancomycin resistance with teicoplanin susceptible, vanC is an intrinsic low-level trait of other species, and vanE is a rare low-level type that stays teicoplanin susceptible.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "E. faecium susceptibility, blood isolate", "columns": ["Antimicrobial", "MIC (ug/mL)", "Interpretation"], "rows": [["Ampicillin", "64", "R"], ["Vancomycin", "256", "R"], ["Teicoplanin", "32", "R"], ["Linezolid", "2", "S"]], "flag_col": null, "caption": "Susceptibility panel"}, "image": "question_images/q_1480.png"}, {"id": 1481, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist reviewing recurrent cancer changes reads about an acute myeloid leukemia with t(8;21)(q22;q22) found in the marrow of a young adult whose blood count shows anemia and circulating blasts. The resident asks what the finding means for prognosis and how such changes are generally grouped. Which prognostic grouping and companion change fit this finding?", "options": {"A": "It is diagnostic of mantle cell lymphoma, along with t(11;14), in lymph nodes.", "B": "It is diagnostic of chronic myeloid leukemia, along with t(9;22), in peripheral blood.", "C": "It is a favourable-risk change, along with inv(16), in acute myeloid leukemia.", "D": "It is a poor-risk change, along with monosomy 7, in acute myeloid leukemia."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It is a favourable-risk change, along with inv(16), in acute myeloid leukemia.): The t(8;21) and inv(16) are recurrent changes associated with a favourable outlook in acute myeloid leukemia. Monosomy 7 and complex karyotypes carry a poorer outlook. The t(11;14) belongs to mantle cell lymphoma and t(9;22) to chronic myeloid leukemia."}, {"id": 1482, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A tube arrives with the label reading a different surname than the requisition. The collector is unavailable, and the ordering unit is calling for the result. The technologist must decide what to do first. Which sequence follows the laboratory's action order for a specimen problem?", "options": {"A": "Relabel the tube to match the requisition, test it, and note the change later", "B": "Hold the tube for the collector to return and ask the unit to wait without contact", "C": "Stop results being reported, contact the collector or unit, request recollection, and document who was told", "D": "Test the sample, report the result, and then contact the collector for confirmation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop results being reported, contact the collector or unit, request recollection, and document who was told): The order is to stop reporting when the problem could give a wrong result, contact the collector or ordering unit with the patient details and the reason, request recollection, and document the reason, the date and time, the person notified and the response. Testing first releases a possibly wrong result, and the laboratory never relabels a tube on the collector's behalf."}, {"id": 1483, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A laboratory is deciding whether to send low-concentration vitamin D and steroid hormone testing to a reference laboratory that uses LC-MS/MS or to keep it on an in-house immunoassay. Cross-reactivity from similar metabolites has produced discrepant results before. Which statement best gives the advantage and the drawback of LC-MS/MS?", "options": {"A": "It measures light absorbance for speed, but shows more interference from metabolites", "B": "It uses antibody binding for specificity, but needs radioactive labels and licensing", "C": "It separates proteins by charge, but cannot detect molecules below the gamma region", "D": "It measures molecules by mass and fragments, but needs skilled staff and longer turnaround"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It measures molecules by mass and fragments, but needs skilled staff and longer turnaround): LC-MS/MS identifies a molecule by its mass and fragments, so it is less affected by antibody cross-reactivity, though it needs specialized staff, method validation and more time. It does not use antibodies or radioactive labels. Absorbance and charge separation describe other techniques."}, {"id": 1484, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist has been cutting blocks for hours at a bench that is too low and reports increasing shoulder and wrist pain. The blades and guards are used correctly, and no injury from a blade has occurred. What is the most appropriate professional response?", "options": {"A": "Cut faster so that the total time at the bench is shorter", "B": "Stop using the blade guard so that movement is easier", "C": "Adjust chair, bench height and posture to reduce strain", "D": "Ignore the pain since it does not involve a sharp injury"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Adjust chair, bench height and posture to reduce strain): Repetitive motion can cause strain, so chair, bench height and posture are adjusted. Speeding up worsens strain, removing the guard adds a laceration risk, and ignoring symptoms is unsafe and can lead to chronic injury."}, {"id": 1485, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A trainee draws the NGS process on a board and lists four steps out of order: analysis, library preparation, sequencing, and extraction with quality assessment. The instructor asks the trainee to arrange them in the order they happen from specimen to result. Which order is correct?", "options": {"A": "Extraction, sequencing, library preparation, then analysis of the barcoded reads", "B": "Sequencing, extraction, analysis, then library preparation for the final report", "C": "Extraction and quality assessment, library preparation, sequencing, then analysis", "D": "Library preparation, extraction, analysis, then sequencing of the aligned reads"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Extraction and quality assessment, library preparation, sequencing, then analysis): The nucleic acid is extracted and assessed first, made into a library with adapters and barcodes, sequenced, and only then are reads aligned and interpreted. Analysis cannot precede sequencing, and adapters must be attached before a fragment can be sequenced."}, {"id": 1486, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient with Hb 82 g/L, MCV 90 fL and a corrected reticulocyte count of 8 percent is seen after a car accident with a fractured pelvis. Hemoglobin was normal last week. The physician asks what the reticulocyte response suggests about the cause of this normocytic anemia.", "options": {"A": "The marrow is responding, so blood loss or destruction is likelier than marrow failure", "B": "The reticulocyte count is uninformative, so the cause can only be found by MCV classification", "C": "The marrow is failing to respond, so aplastic anemia or infiltration is likely", "D": "The marrow is responding, so a B12 deficiency is the most likely cause of the anemia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The marrow is responding, so blood loss or destruction is likelier than marrow failure): A high corrected reticulocyte count means the marrow is working, and the problem is loss or destruction of cells, as with hemorrhage from the pelvic fracture. Marrow failure gives a low count. The reticulocyte count is exactly the kinetic tool that complements MCV, and B12 deficiency gives a low count."}, {"id": 1487, "domain": "histology", "question": "A stained slide shows a fine black precipitate scattered across the tissue and a brownish scum in places. The hematoxylin staining dish has been used all week and never filtered. Which is the best correction for the laboratory to adopt as routine?", "options": {"A": "Filter the hematoxylin daily and change reagents on schedule", "B": "Extend differentiation in acid alcohol until the black clears", "C": "Dilute the hematoxylin with tap water to reduce the precipitate", "D": "Add extra eosin to cover up the precipitate on the slide"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Filter the hematoxylin daily and change reagents on schedule): Precipitate and scum come from unfiltered hematoxylin, so daily filtering and scheduled reagent changes prevent it. Diluting or over-differentiating harms staining, and adding eosin only hides an artifact that should be corrected."}, {"id": 1488, "domain": "cytology", "question": "While cleaning up after a procedure, a technologist pricks her finger on a needle used for a liver FNA while cleaning up the procedure tray, and the small wound bleeds slightly. She asks a colleague what to do first, since the next patient is already waiting. Which sequence follows the laboratory's exposure plan?", "options": {"A": "Squeeze the wound hard and wait for symptoms before reporting to the supervisor.", "B": "Give immediate first aid, report the injury, and start the follow-up process.", "C": "Apply an alcohol-based fixative to the wound, then resume screening as usual.", "D": "Finish the current slide, then wash the hand when the procedure room is free."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give immediate first aid, report the injury, and start the follow-up process.): A contaminated needle can transmit hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV, so the exposure plan requires immediate first aid, reporting and follow-up. Delaying reporting loses the window for follow-up. Fixatives are not first aid and can irritate the wound."}, {"id": 1489, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A fluid contains single cells with intracytoplasmic vacuoles, and other cells lie in cohesive groups arranged in single-file lines. The clinical history mentions a breast biopsy years earlier. A trainee is asked which breast tumour type these patterns typically represent in effusions. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Papillary serous carcinoma, with layered calcified psammoma bodies", "B": "Small cell carcinoma, with nuclear molding and crushed chromatin", "C": "Keratinizing squamous carcinoma, with dense orange cytoplasm and tadpole-shaped cells", "D": "Lobular carcinoma, with single cells and Indian-file lines"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lobular carcinoma, with single cells and Indian-file lines): Lobular breast carcinoma appears as single vacuolated cells or Indian-file lines. Papillary serous carcinoma shows papillary groups with psammoma bodies. Squamous and small cell carcinomas have different, characteristic features and are not typical breast patterns in fluids."}, {"id": 1490, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "An array performed for developmental delay also shows a duplication that carries a gene linked to a hereditary cancer risk, unrelated to the child's condition. The consent form and laboratory policy address unrelated findings. How should the laboratory handle this incidental finding?", "options": {"A": "Report the duplication to the family, since hereditary cancer genes belong in every array report", "B": "Send it directly to the child's school so support can be arranged", "C": "Report it as a certain cancer diagnosis, since the gene is linked to risk", "D": "Handle it according to laboratory policy and the consent the patient or family gave"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Handle it according to laboratory policy and the consent the patient or family gave): Incidental findings are managed by written policy and the consent obtained, which sets whether and how they are disclosed. A risk-associated gene is not a diagnosis, and privacy rules forbid sending health information to a school."}, {"id": 1491, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist reviews a set of G-banded slides that all show pale, ghost-like chromosomes with thin outlines and no dark bands. The trypsin bath had been mixed from a new batch, and the slides were several days old and had been kept in a dry cabinet. What is the best plan?", "options": {"A": "Discard all slides, since ghost chromosomes cannot be improved by any adjustment", "B": "Shorten trypsin time on a test slide, since slide age, dryness and the new batch matter", "C": "Repeat the harvest with more colcemid, since ghost chromosomes come from short exposure", "D": "Lengthen trypsin time on a test slide, since older slides need more enzyme to band"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Shorten trypsin time on a test slide, since slide age, dryness and the new batch matter): Ghost-like chromosomes signal too much trypsin, and slide age, humidity and batch all shift the ideal time, so a test slide is used to shorten it. Lengthening would worsen the result. The harvest and colcemid are not the cause."}, {"id": 1492, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A specimen from a patient with a suspected anemia gives a hemoglobin of 96 g/L and an RBC count of 3.2 x10^12/L. The technologist calculates the indices before the result is autoverified. What is the MCH, and how should it be interpreted against the adult reference interval of 27 to 33 pg?", "options": {"A": "3.0 pg, which is far below the reference interval for hemoglobin content", "B": "30 pg, which is within the normal range for hemoglobin per red cell", "C": "300 pg, which is far above the reference interval for hemoglobin content", "D": "30 g/L, which is a normal concentration of hemoglobin in the cell"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (30 pg, which is within the normal range for hemoglobin per red cell): MCH equals the hemoglobin in g/L divided by the RBC count in x10^12/L, so 96 / 3.2 gives 30 pg, inside the 27 to 33 pg interval. The other numbers are decimal slips. The unit g/L belongs to MCHC, not MCH, so a concentration answer mixes up the two indices."}, {"id": 1493, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory's external proficiency testing survey returns this month and shows that the laboratory classified one challenge variant as likely benign, while most participating laboratories classified it as pathogenic, and the survey coordinator has sent the peer results. What should the laboratory do with this discordant result?", "options": {"A": "Reclassify every previously reported variant in that gene without any review", "B": "Ignore it, since proficiency testing has no relation to patient reporting", "C": "Investigate the cause, correct the process if needed, and document the review", "D": "Replace the survey answer with the consensus and submit it without comment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Investigate the cause, correct the process if needed, and document the review): Proficiency testing shows whether the laboratory calls and classifies variants the same way as others. Any discordant result is investigated, with corrective action and documentation. Ignoring it or changing the answer removes the value of the program, and mass reclassification without review is not proportionate."}, {"id": 1494, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "During a lecture on sample preparation, students list the reagents used in order: a spindle inhibitor, a swelling solution, and then a mixture that preserves the chromosome structure before slides are dropped. Which mixture is used for the final fixation step in routine cytogenetic preparations?", "options": {"A": "One part hydrochloric acid to three parts colcemid, diluted in saline.", "B": "Two parts buffered formalin to one part Giemsa stain, diluted in phosphate buffer.", "C": "Three parts methanol to one part glacial acetic acid, freshly prepared.", "D": "Equal parts absolute ethanol and trypsin solution, warmed to 37 degrees Celsius."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Three parts methanol to one part glacial acetic acid, freshly prepared.): The standard fixative is methanol with acetic acid, which preserves chromosome morphology for dropping onto slides. Formalin and Giemsa serve different purposes, trypsin is used for banding after slides are made, and colcemid is applied before harvest, not as a fixative."}, {"id": 1495, "domain": "histology", "question": "During embedding, the technologist uses warmed forceps to press several biopsies flat and then moves on to the next patient without wiping the forceps. Later, one block contains an extra tiny fragment that does not belong to that patient. What is the best explanation for this event?", "options": {"A": "Contamination of the fixative before the tissue reached the laboratory", "B": "Poor infiltration of wax during the overnight processing cycle", "C": "Tissue carried over on the forceps between cases, forming a floater", "D": "Normal tissue that shifted while the section was cooling on ice"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tissue carried over on the forceps between cases, forming a floater): Forceps that are not cleaned between cases can carry tissue from one block to another, creating a floater, so only one case is embedded at a time. Fixative contamination or infiltration would not put a foreign fragment inside a block."}, {"id": 1496, "domain": "cytology", "question": "The final report on a thyroid nodule is malignant, for example papillary carcinoma with abundant classic nuclear features. The clinician asks the laboratory to state the approximate malignancy risk and the usual management step attached to the malignant Bethesda category. What risk figure and follow-up should the laboratory quote?", "options": {"A": "Roughly 10 to 30 percent, and repeat FNA or molecular testing is advised", "B": "Roughly 50 to 75 percent, and lobectomy or thyroidectomy is advised after repeat FNA", "C": "Under 5 percent, and clinical and ultrasound follow-up is usually advised", "D": "Above 95 percent, and surgery is the usual management step for the patient"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Above 95 percent, and surgery is the usual management step for the patient): The malignant category carries a risk above 95 percent and the usual step is surgery. A risk of 50 to 75 percent belongs to suspicious for malignancy. The 10 to 30 percent band with repeat testing is atypia of undetermined significance. Under 5 percent with follow-up is the benign category."}, {"id": 1497, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A quality supervisor is asked what a documented external proficiency testing program adds to a cytogenetics laboratory that already uses internal controls on every FISH run, validates each new probe lot, and reviews every abnormal case with a second scientist. Which answer describes its purpose?", "options": {"A": "It checks the whole testing process regularly using samples of independent origin", "B": "It lets the laboratory set its own cutoff without any normal control data", "C": "It permits results to be released without review by a second person", "D": "It replaces the need to validate each new probe lot before patient use"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It checks the whole testing process regularly using samples of independent origin): External samples test the complete process against outside comparison and reveal problems that internal controls might miss. It does not replace lot validation, second review, or the need for normal control data to set a cutoff."}, {"id": 1498, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist screening a specimen from a woman with an intrauterine device finds tangled, woolly cotton-ball colonies with thin radiating filaments lying against neutrophils. The requisition lists the device. Which organism is most consistent with this appearance? She has a history of vaginal discharge.", "options": {"A": "Actinomyces species, filamentous bacteria seen with intrauterine device use", "B": "Gardnerella, tiny coccobacilli forming a coating over squamous cells", "C": "Candida glabrata, small round budding yeast lacking any pseudohyphae", "D": "Trichomonas vaginalis, pear-shaped flagellated protozoa with small eccentric nuclei and granules"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Actinomyces species, filamentous bacteria seen with intrauterine device use): Tangled cotton-ball colonies with radiating filaments are typical of Actinomyces in IUD users. Candida glabrata is small round budding yeast. Trichomonas is a pear-shaped protozoan, and Gardnerella appears as a fine coccobacillary coating rather than woolly colonies."}, {"id": 1499, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In a lung cancer workup, A laboratory receives an aspirate for lung cancer workup and the pathologist requests an immunocytochemical surrogate for a rearrangement that would otherwise need a molecular assay. Which pair below lists common examples of such surrogates mentioned for cytology testing?", "options": {"A": "Kappa and lambda immunostaining, used to judge clonality in lymphoid cell populations", "B": "Calretinin and BerEP4 immunostaining, used to separate mesothelium from adenocarcinoma cells", "C": "Papanicolaou and hematoxylin staining, used for routine morphology on screening slides", "D": "ALK and PD-L1 immunostaining, used alongside molecular methods on cytology material"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (ALK and PD-L1 immunostaining, used alongside molecular methods on cytology material): ALK and PD-L1 immunostaining are described as immunocytochemical surrogates used alongside molecular methods, though PD-L1 scoring needs a validated protocol. Kappa and lambda relate to clonality, calretinin and BerEP4 separate mesothelium from adenocarcinoma, and Papanicolaou is a routine morphologic stain. Molecular assays may still be needed, but surrogate staining can give a faster answer from the same block."}, {"id": 1500, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A laboratory arranges for a cytotechnologist or pathologist to attend some fine needle aspiration clinics with a microscope. The goal is to reduce the number of repeat procedures for patients. The hospital has noticed that repeat aspirations cause discomfort and delay treatment planning for many patients. What is the purpose of rapid on-site evaluation?", "options": {"A": "To finalize the diagnosis and report the result to the patient before leaving", "B": "To confirm at the time of collection that enough diagnostic material was obtained", "C": "To fix the material so that no further processing is needed later", "D": "To replace the Papanicolaou stain with a quick and less expensive alternative"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (To confirm at the time of collection that enough diagnostic material was obtained): On-site evaluation checks that the aspirate has adequate material so the procedure need not be repeated. It does not replace final interpretation or the laboratory's routine processing and staining."}, {"id": 1501, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "The spectrophotometer reports an A260/A280 ratio of 1.85 for this extract, which will be used for a KRAS variant assay. The technologist reviews each purity ratio separately against the laboratory criteria in the case. What does this specific ratio indicate about the extract when it is considered on its own, apart from the other values?", "options": {"A": "Substantial RNA contamination, because the ratio is clearly above the value for pure DNA", "B": "Little protein contamination, since the ratio is near the value for pure DNA", "C": "Heavy protein contamination, because the ratio is above the value expected for pure DNA", "D": "Fragmented DNA, because the ratio reflects the average length of the molecules present"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Little protein contamination, since the ratio is near the value for pure DNA): Pure DNA has an A260/A280 near 1.8, so 1.85 shows little protein or phenol carryover. RNA contamination would push the ratio toward 2.0 and is not suggested here, protein contamination lowers the ratio rather than raising it, and the ratio does not measure fragment length.", "cluster_id": "3", "cluster_text": "A technologist extracts DNA from two formalin-fixed, paraffin-embedded colon tumour sections for a KRAS variant assay. The spectrophotometer reports 12 ng/µL, an A260/A280 ratio of 1.85, and an A260/A230 ratio of 0.9. The laboratory's validated acceptance criteria are at least 5 ng/µL and an A260/A230 of at least 1.8. The extraction used a guanidine-based lysis buffer and an ethanol wash. The technologist notes that the wash step on this specimen was rushed and the column was not fully dried. The internal amplification control failed for this specimen, while another specimen from the same extraction batch amplified normally.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1502, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Which explanation best accounts for the A260/A230 ratio of 0.9 together with the failed internal amplification control in this tumour specimen, given the spectrophotometer readings, the extraction chemistry used, and the fact that another specimen from the same extraction batch amplified normally?", "options": {"A": "Insufficient DNA concentration is limiting the reaction below the validated minimum for the assay", "B": "RNA contamination is competing with the primers for binding sites on the template DNA", "C": "Carryover of guanidine salt or ethanol from the wash step is inhibiting the polymerase", "D": "A defective polymerase lot is responsible, since the reaction mix is shared by the batch"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Carryover of guanidine salt or ethanol from the wash step is inhibiting the polymerase): A low A260/A230 ratio signals salt or organic carryover, and a rushed wash with an undried column is the usual cause, so the polymerase is inhibited. The 12 ng/µL yield is above the 5 ng/µL minimum, RNA would not lower the A260/A230 in this way, and a defective polymerase lot would have affected the other specimen too.", "cluster_id": "3", "cluster_text": "A technologist extracts DNA from two formalin-fixed, paraffin-embedded colon tumour sections for a KRAS variant assay. The spectrophotometer reports 12 ng/µL, an A260/A280 ratio of 1.85, and an A260/A230 ratio of 0.9. The laboratory's validated acceptance criteria are at least 5 ng/µL and an A260/A230 of at least 1.8. The extraction used a guanidine-based lysis buffer and an ethanol wash. The technologist notes that the wash step on this specimen was rushed and the column was not fully dried. The internal amplification control failed for this specimen, while another specimen from the same extraction batch amplified normally.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1503, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "After reviewing the spectrophotometer data and the failed internal amplification control, the technologist must decide what to do with this formalin-fixed specimen, and the oncologist is waiting on the result for treatment planning. The laboratory's validated acceptance criteria apply to every extract. What is the best action before reporting a KRAS result?", "options": {"A": "Report the specimen as wild type, since the concentration is above the minimum requirement", "B": "Increase the number of amplification cycles and report any late signal that appears", "C": "Add extra polymerase to the same reaction and report if the control then passes", "D": "Re-purify or re-extract, confirm ratios meet criteria, and repeat amplification with controls"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Re-purify or re-extract, confirm ratios meet criteria, and repeat amplification with controls): Inhibitor carryover must be removed by cleanup or a fresh extraction, and the quality criteria rechecked, before the assay is repeated with controls. Reporting wild type on a failed internal control is unsafe, and extra cycles or extra enzyme are unvalidated changes that do not remove the inhibitor.", "cluster_id": "3", "cluster_text": "A technologist extracts DNA from two formalin-fixed, paraffin-embedded colon tumour sections for a KRAS variant assay. The spectrophotometer reports 12 ng/µL, an A260/A280 ratio of 1.85, and an A260/A230 ratio of 0.9. The laboratory's validated acceptance criteria are at least 5 ng/µL and an A260/A230 of at least 1.8. The extraction used a guanidine-based lysis buffer and an ethanol wash. The technologist notes that the wash step on this specimen was rushed and the column was not fully dried. The internal amplification control failed for this specimen, while another specimen from the same extraction batch amplified normally.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1504, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Over one month, a laboratory sees an occasional late Ct in the no-template control on different runs, each time repeated once and found clean. The supervisor asks the technologist how to proceed with this pattern of sporadic positives. Which approach follows good practice?", "options": {"A": "Accept blank signals as background instrument noise, since late Ct values are not evidence of contamination.", "B": "Follow documented corrective action, and investigate reagents, workflow, and surfaces for the source.", "C": "Keep repeating each run until the control is clean, then record only the final result.", "D": "Raise the Ct cut-off for positives above the blank Ct and continue routine testing."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Follow documented corrective action, and investigate reagents, workflow, and surfaces for the source.): Sporadic positives in a negative control indicate an unresolved contamination source, so the laboratory follows its corrective action process and looks for the cause. Repeating until clean hides a real problem, and moving cut-offs or dismissing late Cts leaves patient results at risk."}, {"id": 1505, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "During a review of quality control data, a supervisor notes a sudden change in the mean of one control that continued across several runs. Later she finds a gradual drift in another control. What kind of error do a shift and a trend represent?", "options": {"A": "Random error, which changes results unpredictably and affects mainly the precision of the method", "B": "Pre-analytical error, which arises from specimen collection and transport delays", "C": "Reference interval error, which arises from using the wrong healthy population", "D": "Systematic error, which moves results consistently in one direction and affects accuracy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Systematic error, which moves results consistently in one direction and affects accuracy): A shift is a sudden change in the mean and a trend is a gradual drift, and both are forms of systematic error that bias results in one direction. Random error scatters values without direction. Pre-analytical and reference interval problems are not detected through control charts."}, {"id": 1506, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A blood bank keeps a par level for each ABO and Rh group and checks it against actual stock every day. The supervisor reminds staff how stock rotation should be managed so that outdating is minimized. Which practice is the correct approach to rotation?", "options": {"A": "Use the units with the longest shelf life first for all stable patients", "B": "Use the oldest units first and report shortages to the supplier early", "C": "Use the newest units first and report shortages only when stock runs out", "D": "Use units of any age at random and order more only after each outdate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use the oldest units first and report shortages to the supplier early): Rotating stock so the oldest units are used first reduces waste, and reporting shortages early lets the supplier respond. Using the newest units first creates outdating. Random use and delayed ordering create shortages and waste, and using the longest-dated units first leaves older units to expire."}, {"id": 1507, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A pleural fluid contains cells with large atypical nuclei, and the screener suspects malignant mesothelioma because of the patient's asbestos history. The cell block stains support a mesothelial origin. The clinician asks whether the cytology alone can confirm the diagnosis. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Cytology may suggest mesothelioma, but tissue biopsy is generally needed to confirm invasion", "B": "Cytology confirms mesothelioma only when the fluid is grossly bloody and has many red cells", "C": "Cytology can confirm mesothelioma, since atypical nuclei on their own prove tissue invasion", "D": "Cytology cannot suggest mesothelioma, since mesothelial cells lack markers that a cell block can show"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cytology may suggest mesothelioma, but tissue biopsy is generally needed to confirm invasion): Cells in fluid cannot prove invasion, so biopsy is generally needed to confirm mesothelioma even when cytology and immunostains suggest it. Claiming cytology always confirms it is wrong. Immunostains do help suggest mesothelioma, and bloody fluid has no diagnostic role."}, {"id": 1508, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A physician suspects Lyme disease in a hiker who reports a tick bite in a wooded area last summer and now has swollen, painful knees and fatigue. The laboratory receives a serum sample. Which diagnostic approach applies for Borrelia burgdorferi, and which vector transmits it?", "options": {"A": "Dark-field microscopy alone, and the Ixodes tick transmits the organism", "B": "Two-tier serology, and the Ixodes tick transmits the organism", "C": "Two-tier serology, and contact with water contaminated by animal urine transmits the organism", "D": "Culture on charcoal yeast extract agar, and the Ixodes tick transmits the organism"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Two-tier serology, and the Ixodes tick transmits the organism): Lyme disease is diagnosed by two-tier serology, and Ixodes ticks transmit Borrelia burgdorferi. Dark-field microscopy suits a syphilis chancre, and charcoal yeast extract agar suits Legionella. Contamination by animal urine is the route for Leptospira."}, {"id": 1509, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A sample for a routine PT is delivered to the laboratory after being stored in a refrigerator at 4 degrees Celsius overnight. The technologist has to decide about testing. What is the concern with refrigerated coagulation samples? The specimen was otherwise collected and labelled correctly.", "options": {"A": "Cold can destroy fibrinogen and calcium, so results are falsely prolonged", "B": "Cold can activate factor VII and platelets, so results may be unreliable", "C": "Cold can activate citrate and heparin, so results are falsely shortened", "D": "Cold can neutralize warfarin and platelets, so results are falsely normal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cold can activate factor VII and platelets, so results may be unreliable): Refrigeration activates factor VII and platelets, so routine coagulation samples should not be chilled. The other statements describe effects that do not occur."}, {"id": 1510, "domain": "histology", "question": "A whole colon specimen arrives in a large bucket and the loops of tissue float above the surface of the formalin. The lid is loose and a strong odour is noticeable at the grossing bench. Which action best supports proper fixation and protects staff at the same time?", "options": {"A": "Leave the tissue floating because the vapour above the fluid will fix the exposed loops.", "B": "Remove the lid so that vapour can escape and the tissue is exposed to fresh air.", "C": "Cover exposed tissue with formalin-soaked gauze, turn it early, and close the lid tightly.", "D": "Add a little water to the bucket so that the level rises over the floating tissue."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cover exposed tissue with formalin-soaked gauze, turn it early, and close the lid tightly.): Floating tissue is not fixed where it sits above the fluid, so soaked gauze or turning brings formalin to every surface, and a tight lid limits irritant, carcinogenic vapour. Vapour alone does not fix reliably. Adding water dilutes the fixative below an effective concentration."}, {"id": 1511, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A sputum smear shows abundant oral squamous cells, bacteria, and budding yeast with pseudohyphae in a healthy outpatient with no respiratory symptoms. A colleague suggests reporting a significant fungal lung infection. What is the most appropriate approach given the source of the specimen?", "options": {"A": "Recognize Candida as a common mouth contaminant, and interpret it in the clinical setting", "B": "Confirm Candida with an acid-fast stain because mycobacteria produce pseudohyphae", "C": "Reject the slide, because yeast cells cannot be seen in respiratory samples", "D": "Report invasive fungal pneumonia immediately, because Candida in sputum indicates deep tissue infection"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Recognize Candida as a common mouth contaminant, and interpret it in the clinical setting): Candida is a frequent mouth contaminant in sputum, so its meaning depends on the clinical setting. Calling it always pathogenic risks overcalling infection. Yeast is seen in respiratory samples, and acid-fast stains identify mycobacteria, not Candida."}, {"id": 1512, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory evaluates sequencers. One platform is described as detecting a pH change as nucleotides are incorporated, and another as detecting changes in electrical current as DNA passes through a pore. How do these platforms differ from the most common short-read chemistry?", "options": {"A": "They detect other signals instead of imaging fluorescently labelled nucleotides added one cycle at a time", "B": "They need no library preparation, since the specimen is loaded directly", "C": "They read only RNA, since pH and current cannot report DNA bases", "D": "They rely on chain-terminating nucleotides and size separation, like Sanger sequencing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They detect other signals instead of imaging fluorescently labelled nucleotides added one cycle at a time): Other platforms detect a change in pH or an electrical current rather than imaging fluorescent nucleotides. They are not Sanger-style, and they read DNA. Library preparation is still required."}, {"id": 1513, "domain": "histology", "question": "A renal biopsy from a patient with nephrotic-range proteinuria shows amorphous eosinophilic material filling parts of the glomeruli on the routine hematoxylin and eosin section. The special stain findings are shown in the image. The pathologist asks the technologist which material the stain results best identify, using the staining reactions and polarized light finding together.", "options": {"A": "Glycogen, since the PAS stain shows magenta deposits that disappear after diastase treatment", "B": "Immune complexes, since the silver stain shows spikes projecting from the basement membrane", "C": "Amyloid, since the Congo red deposits show apple-green birefringence under polarized light", "D": "Hyalinized collagen, since the trichrome stain shows dense blue fibres replacing the glomerulus"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Amyloid, since the Congo red deposits show apple-green birefringence under polarized light): Congo red positive deposits that give apple-green birefringence under polarized light are diagnostic of amyloid. The silver stain in the image shows no basement membrane spikes, so immune complex disease is not supported, and the PAS and Congo red findings listed do not describe glycogen or hyalinized collagen.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Renal biopsy special stains", "columns": ["Stain", "Deposit staining", "Polarized light"], "rows": [["Congo red", "Orange-red glomerular deposits", "Apple-green birefringence"], ["PAS", "Mesangium not expanded", "Not applicable"], ["Jones silver", "No basement membrane spikes", "Not applicable"]], "caption": "Special stain findings on a renal biopsy"}, "image": "question_images/q_1513.png"}, {"id": 1514, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "The emergency chemistry results shown come from a patient with nausea and rapid deep breathing, collected during an urgent overnight emergency visit. Calculating the anion gap from sodium, chloride and bicarbonate, and reviewing the other results listed, which interpretation is best supported by the report?", "options": {"A": "High anion gap metabolic acidosis from ketoacids, as in diabetic ketoacidosis", "B": "Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis from bicarbonate loss, as in severe diarrhea", "C": "Respiratory acidosis from hypoventilation, with a raised pCO2 and retained bicarbonate", "D": "Metabolic alkalosis from vomiting, with chloride loss and compensatory bicarbonate retention"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (High anion gap metabolic acidosis from ketoacids, as in diabetic ketoacidosis): The anion gap is 134 minus 98 minus 10, or 26 mmol/L, which is high, and the low bicarbonate with raised glucose and beta-hydroxybutyrate points to ketoacids. Diarrhea would keep the gap normal, and respiratory acidosis or metabolic alkalosis would not give a low bicarbonate.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Emergency chemistry", "columns": ["Test", "Result", "Units", "Reference interval", "Flag"], "rows": [["Glucose", "31.0", "mmol/L", "4.0 to 7.8", "H"], ["Sodium", "134", "mmol/L", "135 to 145", "L"], ["Chloride", "98", "mmol/L", "98 to 107", ""], ["Bicarbonate", "10", "mmol/L", "22 to 29", "L"], ["Beta-hydroxybutyrate", "6.2", "mmol/L", "less than 0.6", "H"]], "flag_col": 4, "caption": "Emergency chemistry results"}, "image": "question_images/q_1514.png"}, {"id": 1515, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A student reading a lymphoma report asks where Reed-Sternberg cells are found. These are large binucleate cells with prominent nucleoli, often described as having an owl-eye appearance. Where are these cells of Hodgkin lymphoma typically identified by the laboratory in practice?", "options": {"A": "In the urine sediment, where they are shed from the kidney of affected patients", "B": "In tissue such as a lymph node biopsy, not in the peripheral blood", "C": "In the peripheral blood smear, where they are counted as part of the differential", "D": "In the serum, where they are detected as a monoclonal band on electrophoresis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (In tissue such as a lymph node biopsy, not in the peripheral blood): Reed-Sternberg cells are found in tissue, not in blood, so a lymph node biopsy is the usual source. They are not part of the routine differential, and they are not found in urine or as a serum protein band."}, {"id": 1516, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "When preparing 20 stool concentrates with 10 percent formalin and ethyl acetate, a technologist works at an open bench with the door closed and pours the leftover supernatant into the sink. A colleague notes a strong solvent odour and eye irritation. What is the correct chemical safety practice for these reagents?", "options": {"A": "Handle them in a biological safety cabinet and autoclave the chemical waste directly", "B": "Handle them in a fume hood, label per WHMIS, and dispose as hazardous waste", "C": "Handle them at the sink under running water and discard them as regular waste", "D": "Handle them on the open bench with gloves alone and pour small amounts down the sink"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Handle them in a fume hood, label per WHMIS, and dispose as hazardous waste): Formalin and ethyl acetate are chemical hazards handled in a fume hood, labelled under WHMIS, and disposed of as hazardous waste. A biological safety cabinet does not protect from chemical vapours, and autoclaving chemicals is unsafe."}, {"id": 1517, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "Total carbon dioxide, often reported as bicarbonate, is measured on a serum specimen. Which handling error is most likely to lower this result? The result on a fasting patient is lower than the clinical picture suggests, and the tube was found to be only half full.", "options": {"A": "Recording the collection time on the requisition using the twenty-four hour clock", "B": "Storing the tube upright so that red cells settle to the bottom of the tube", "C": "Leaving the tube uncapped or underfilled so carbon dioxide escapes from it", "D": "Adding a small amount of saline to the sample before loading it on the analyzer"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Leaving the tube uncapped or underfilled so carbon dioxide escapes from it): Bicarbonate is the main buffer, and carbon dioxide escapes from open or underfilled tubes, so the result falls falsely. Tubes should be kept capped and filled. Upright storage and correct time documentation are good practice and do not lower the result, though dilution would lower it."}, {"id": 1518, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A sputum sample for mycobacterial culture from a patient with a chronic cough and night sweats contains many normal respiratory bacteria that would overgrow the slow-growing organism. The technologist prepares the specimen before inoculation. Which processing step is used, and why?", "options": {"A": "Decontamination with N-acetyl-L-cysteine and sodium hydroxide to remove other flora, then centrifugation", "B": "Decontamination with hydrogen peroxide and boiling to kill mycobacteria, then direct inoculation", "C": "Decontamination with N-acetyl-L-cysteine and sodium hydroxide to remove mycobacteria, then discarding the sediment", "D": "Dilution in sterile saline to reduce the flora count, then inoculation without any centrifugation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decontamination with N-acetyl-L-cysteine and sodium hydroxide to remove other flora, then centrifugation): Specimens are decontaminated with N-acetyl-L-cysteine and sodium hydroxide to remove faster growing flora, then concentrated by centrifugation. The step is designed to spare mycobacteria, so removing them would defeat the purpose, and saline alone does not suppress flora."}, {"id": 1519, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A woman received a bone marrow transplant from a male donor. A technologist applies X and Y centromeric probes to her post-transplant marrow interphase nuclei and scores 200 cells, finding 196 with one X and one Y signal and 4 with two X signals. What does this result indicate?", "options": {"A": "A mosaic Klinefelter pattern in the recipient, since XY nuclei are present in a woman", "B": "A failed hybridization, since a female patient's nuclei should not show a Y signal", "C": "Mostly donor cells, about 98 percent, with a small residual recipient population", "D": "Mostly recipient cells, about 98 percent, with a small residual donor population"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mostly donor cells, about 98 percent, with a small residual recipient population): The recipient is female, so XX nuclei are hers, and XY nuclei must come from the male donor. With 196 of 200 nuclei XY, the donor fraction is 196 divided by 200, or 98 percent. The result is a valid chimerism finding, not Klinefelter mosaicism or a failed hybridization."}, {"id": 1520, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A patient's serum contains anti-Jka that reacts 3+ with a Jk(a+b-) screening cell but only 1+ with a Jk(a+b+) cell. A student asks why the two cells differ in strength even though both carry the antigen. What is the correct explanation for the difference?", "options": {"A": "The homozygous cell carries a double dose of the antigen, and the antibody shows dosage", "B": "The heterozygous cell has fewer antigen sites by chance and the difference is unrelated to inheritance", "C": "The homozygous cell has been treated with enzymes, which enhance the reactions of Kidd antibodies", "D": "The heterozygous cell carries a double dose, and complement binding weakens the reaction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The homozygous cell carries a double dose of the antigen, and the antibody shows dosage): A cell from a donor homozygous for the antigen expresses a double dose, and antibodies that show dosage react more strongly with it than with a heterozygous cell. Heterozygous cells carry a single dose because of inheritance, not chance. Enzyme treatment and complement are not what distinguishes the two cells."}, {"id": 1521, "domain": "histology", "question": "A brain biopsy arrives with a container label that spells the patient's given name differently from the requisition. The sample cannot be collected again, and the surgeon is still in the operating room. The requisition number, date of birth and site all agree. How should the technologist proceed with this tissue?", "options": {"A": "Process the specimen under the container name and mention nothing in the record.", "B": "Correct the label quietly to match the requisition and process the tissue at once.", "C": "Discard the specimen because of the mismatch and ask the surgeon to repeat the biopsy.", "D": "Hold the specimen safely, notify the sender, and document the issue as a nonconformance."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the specimen safely, notify the sender, and document the issue as a nonconformance.): An irreplaceable specimen is kept, and the discrepancy is resolved with the sender while the tissue is protected. Discarding is impossible for tissue that cannot be re-collected, and silent relabelling hides an error that a nonconformance record is meant to capture. Agreement of the other identifiers helps the sender confirm identity quickly."}, {"id": 1522, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Urine from a patient who recently received intravesical BCG therapy contains enlarged, strangely shaped urothelial cells. The screener is uncertain whether they represent malignancy or a treatment effect, and the requisition gives no treatment details. What is needed to interpret these cells safely?", "options": {"A": "A Congo red stain, since treatment effect is identified by apple-green birefringence", "B": "Nothing more, since treated cells consistently meet the criteria for high-grade carcinoma", "C": "A repeat collection within one hour, because treatment effect disappears in a fresh specimen", "D": "The clinical history of BCG or chemotherapy, because these treatments create enlarged, bizarre cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The clinical history of BCG or chemotherapy, because these treatments create enlarged, bizarre cells): BCG and chemotherapy can produce enlarged, odd cells, so the treatment history is essential before calling malignancy. Treatment change does not vanish in an hour. Assuming treated cells are malignant would cause overcalling. Congo red detects amyloid, which has nothing to do with therapy effect."}, {"id": 1523, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A laboratory assistant asks why trisomy 21, trisomy 18 and trisomy 13 are the autosomal trisomies most often seen in liveborn babies, while trisomies of most other chromosomes end in early pregnancy loss. The technologist gives the usual reason. Which reason explains why these three trisomies reach birth?", "options": {"A": "These chromosomes are acrocentric, so the extra copy is silenced during development.", "B": "These chromosomes carry relatively little genetic material, so survival to birth is possible.", "C": "These chromosomes carry the most genetic material, so survival to birth is more likely.", "D": "These chromosomes lack banding, so an extra copy is not detected in early pregnancy."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (These chromosomes carry relatively little genetic material, so survival to birth is possible.): Chromosomes 13, 18 and 21 hold relatively little genetic material, so an extra copy is sometimes compatible with birth. Carrying more material would make survival less likely. Chromosome 18 is not acrocentric, and banding does not decide whether a pregnancy continues."}, {"id": 1524, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A karyotyping student compares chromosome shapes on a printed spread. One chromosome has its centromere almost exactly in the middle, so both arms look about the same length, whereas another has the centromere very close to one end with tiny satellites. Which term correctly describes the first chromosome?", "options": {"A": "Metacentric, because the centromere lies near the middle of the chromosome.", "B": "Telocentric, because the centromere sits exactly at the terminal end of the chromosome.", "C": "Submetacentric, because the centromere is placed clearly off centre on the chromosome.", "D": "Acrocentric, because the centromere lies near one end of the chromosome."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Metacentric, because the centromere lies near the middle of the chromosome.): A centromere near the middle gives a metacentric chromosome with arms of similar length. Acrocentric describes the second chromosome, which has the centromere near an end. A submetacentric chromosome has clearly unequal arms, and a true telocentric chromosome is not found in the normal human set."}, {"id": 1525, "domain": "cytology", "question": "EUS-guided FNA of a 2 cm pancreatic tail lesion shows dispersed plasmacytoid cells with eccentric round nuclei and coarse stippled chromatin. There are no crowded sheets with loss of the honeycomb pattern. The cell block is positive on a neuroendocrine immunostain. Which tumour fits best?", "options": {"A": "Gastric contamination from the needle path, which shows mucin and honeycomb sheets.", "B": "Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, which shows crowded sheets with irregular, variably sized nuclei.", "C": "Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour, which the cell block stain for synaptophysin supports.", "D": "Acinar cell carcinoma, which shows loosely cohesive cells with prominent single nucleoli."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour, which the cell block stain for synaptophysin supports.): Dispersed plasmacytoid cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin and neuroendocrine marker positivity, such as synaptophysin, indicate a neuroendocrine tumour. Ductal adenocarcinoma shows crowded, disorganized sheets. Contamination shows regular honeycomb sheets and mucin, and acinar carcinoma is not marked by a neuroendocrine stain."}, {"id": 1526, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A 4-year-old from a Mediterranean family has severe anemia and hepatosplenomegaly. The smear shows nucleated red cells, target cells, basophilic stippling and marked anisopoikilocytosis. HPLC shows little or no hemoglobin A. Which fraction is most likely to be markedly raised, and what is the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Hemoglobin F, which compensates for the absent beta chains in beta thalassemia major", "B": "Hemoglobin S, which forms sickle cells in a patient with sickle cell disease", "C": "Hemoglobin A2 alone, the finding in the mild carrier state of beta thalassemia", "D": "Hemoglobin H, from excess beta chains after loss of three alpha genes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hemoglobin F, which compensates for the absent beta chains in beta thalassemia major): Severe anemia with nucleated red cells, targets, stippling and almost no hemoglobin A indicates beta thalassemia major, where hemoglobin F is markedly raised. Hemoglobin S would show sickle cells. Hemoglobin H disease has a different smear and inclusions, and beta trait is a mild condition."}, {"id": 1527, "domain": "histology", "question": "A container of tissue arrives at the laboratory dry, with no fixative added, several hours after surgery. The requisition does not mention any delay in transport, and the operating room has since closed for the day. What should the technologist do about this condition when accepting the specimen?", "options": {"A": "Reject the specimen, because dry tissue cannot be used for diagnosis and must be recollected", "B": "Place it in fixative and say nothing, since the tissue is now in the correct medium.", "C": "Place it in fixative promptly and record that it arrived dry, so the pathologist is aware.", "D": "Freeze the specimen immediately because drying has already made fixation impossible."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Place it in fixative promptly and record that it arrived dry, so the pathologist is aware.): Tissue received dry or unfixed is put into fixative at once, and the condition is recorded so the pathologist can weigh drying and autolysis artifact during interpretation. Saying nothing hides a relevant preanalytic fault. Dry tissue can still yield a diagnosis, and freezing would add ice crystal damage rather than solve anything."}, {"id": 1528, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A microbiology laboratory adopts a policy of reporting only narrow-spectrum drugs first for some isolates, and releasing broader agents only if the isolate is resistant to first-line drugs. What is the main purpose of this practice? The policy is written in the laboratory's antimicrobial stewardship procedure manual.", "options": {"A": "To reduce the technologist's workload by testing fewer drugs on every isolate submitted", "B": "To satisfy the breakpoint committee, which requires all broad-spectrum results to be suppressed", "C": "To support antimicrobial stewardship by steering physicians toward the narrowest effective drug", "D": "To hide resistance results from clinicians until an infection specialist has reviewed the case"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (To support antimicrobial stewardship by steering physicians toward the narrowest effective drug): Selective or cascade reporting supports stewardship by guiding clinicians to narrow-spectrum drugs and limiting unnecessary broad-spectrum use. It is not about hiding resistance or reducing workload, and no committee requires that broad agents be suppressed."}, {"id": 1529, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A point-of-care blood gas device and a large chemistry analyzer both report potassium using ion-selective electrodes, but one dilutes the sample before measuring and the other does not. The technologist explains the difference to a new colleague. Which statement correctly describes direct ISE?", "options": {"A": "It dilutes the sample first and calculates the result for whole plasma volume", "B": "It measures ion activity in the water portion of an undiluted sample", "C": "It requires a colour reaction and a spectrophotometer to read the ion concentration", "D": "It measures the current produced by ion reduction at a platinum cathode"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It measures ion activity in the water portion of an undiluted sample): Direct ISE reads the undiluted sample, so it reflects ion activity in the plasma water, and this is the design used in blood gas and point-of-care devices. Dilution and whole-plasma calculation describe indirect ISE. Current at a platinum cathode is amperometry, and colour reactions are photometry."}, {"id": 1530, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Taq polymerase is added to a reaction in which primers are annealed to single-stranded template, and the temperature is then raised to 72 °C for the extension step. From which site, and in which manner, does the enzyme build the new strand of DNA?", "options": {"A": "It starts at the template's free end and adds nucleotides without needing a primer.", "B": "It adds nucleotides to the 3' end of each primer, copying the template.", "C": "It adds nucleotides to the 5' end of each primer, moving backward along the template.", "D": "It joins two primers together, then fills the gap with nucleotides from both sides."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It adds nucleotides to the 3' end of each primer, copying the template.): DNA polymerase can only extend an existing 3' end, so the primer provides the starting point and the enzyme copies the template by adding nucleotides. It does not extend from a 5' end, work without a primer, or join primers."}, {"id": 1531, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A technologist reviews how 22 percent albumin improves detection of IgG antibodies in the IAT. The training document describes an effect on the electrical charge environment that keeps red cells apart in suspension. What is the mechanism by which albumin enhances agglutination in this test?", "options": {"A": "It lowers the zeta potential around red cells so they can move closer together", "B": "It increases ionic strength so that antibody attaches to antigen sites at a faster rate", "C": "It removes water from the reaction and concentrates the antibody near the cell surface", "D": "It destroys the Duffy and MN antigens, which lowers the background reactivity of the cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It lowers the zeta potential around red cells so they can move closer together): Albumin reduces the zeta potential, the repelling charge around each red cell, so cells approach closely enough for antibody to link them. Removing water is the mechanism of polyethylene glycol, and speeding uptake by ionic strength describes LISS, which lowers rather than raises ionic strength. Destroying antigens is an enzyme effect."}, {"id": 1532, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A pleural fluid is received in the cytology laboratory with the paired chemistry results in the image, and the clinician asks how the fluid should be classified before the cytology report is issued. The serum LDH upper reference limit is 222 U/L. Which classification do the results support?", "options": {"A": "Transudate, because the fluid protein concentration is lower than the serum protein concentration", "B": "Transudate, because the fluid LDH value is lower than the serum upper reference limit", "C": "Indeterminate, because just one of the two fluid to serum ratios exceeds its usual cutoff", "D": "Exudate, because fluid to serum ratios for protein and LDH exceed the usual cutoffs"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Exudate, because fluid to serum ratios for protein and LDH exceed the usual cutoffs): The protein ratio is 0.62, above 0.5, and the LDH ratio is 1.63, above 0.6, and the fluid LDH of 310 U/L is above two thirds of the serum upper limit, so the fluid meets Light's criteria for an exudate. A fluid protein below serum is expected in all effusions. The fluid LDH is not below the serum limit, and both ratios exceed their cutoffs.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Pleural fluid and serum chemistry", "columns": ["Test", "Fluid", "Serum", "Ratio"], "rows": [["Protein (g/L)", "42", "68", "0.62"], ["LDH (U/L)", "310", "190", "1.63"]], "caption": "Serum LDH upper limit 222 U/L"}, "image": "question_images/q_1532.png"}, {"id": 1533, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist has a failed blood culture with several possible causes and wants to find the source. The suspected steps include harvest timing, hypotonic time and fixation, and all three were run by different staff on different days. What is the best method for troubleshooting so that the real cause becomes clear?", "options": {"A": "Change only the culture medium, since harvest steps have little effect on metaphase count", "B": "Change one variable at a time, and record the sign each step produces on the slide", "C": "Change nothing and repeat the harvest, since chance alone explains poor slides", "D": "Change all variables at once, since a full change is the fastest approach"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Change one variable at a time, and record the sign each step produces on the slide): Altering one variable at a time makes the cause clear, and each sign, such as crowding or over-condensation, points to a specific step. Changing everything at once hides the cause, and harvest steps do affect results."}, {"id": 1534, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A laboratory changes its creatinine method to one from a different manufacturer. The package insert lists a reference interval derived from a similar population. The supervisor asks what should be done to confirm the interval suits the laboratory's own patients before the method goes live.", "options": {"A": "Use the control material's range instead because it covers the same values", "B": "Verify it by testing a small number of healthy people, or establish its own", "C": "Adopt the interval unchanged because reference intervals are identical between methods", "D": "Set the interval from patients admitted this week with kidney disease"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Verify it by testing a small number of healthy people, or establish its own): A laboratory verifies a manufacturer's or published interval with a small healthy group, or establishes its own, when the method or population differs. Intervals are not identical across methods. Control ranges describe the control material, and a diseased group cannot define a healthy interval."}, {"id": 1535, "domain": "histology", "question": "A laboratory joins an external programme that sends unknown samples and compares its results with other laboratories. Results are scored against a reference panel, and any discrepancy triggers an investigation and corrective action. Which quality assurance activity does this describe?", "options": {"A": "Reagent expiry checking performed by the laboratory each morning before runs start", "B": "Scheduled instrument cleaning and maintenance of automated stainers", "C": "Daily positive control staining performed with every patient run", "D": "Participation in an external proficiency testing programme with other laboratories"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Participation in an external proficiency testing programme with other laboratories): External proficiency testing lets a laboratory compare results with other laboratories, and errors are investigated. Expiry checks, daily controls and maintenance are internal activities."}, {"id": 1536, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A collector accidentally draws a chemistry panel that includes sodium and potassium into a gray-top tube because no other tubes were left. The laboratory's serum electrolytes look plausible but the potassium is suspiciously high. Why should gray-top plasma not be used for these two electrolytes?", "options": {"A": "The tube contains sodium fluoride and potassium oxalate, which add both analytes", "B": "The tube contains lithium heparin, which adds lithium and interferes with the electrodes", "C": "The tube contains EDTA, which binds sodium and potassium and lowers both values", "D": "The tube contains a gel barrier that absorbs both electrolytes from the plasma"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The tube contains sodium fluoride and potassium oxalate, which add both analytes): Sodium fluoride and potassium oxalate contribute sodium and potassium to the sample, so both electrolytes are falsely raised. Gray tops are meant for glucose and lactate, where fluoride blocks glycolysis. The tube does not contain heparin or a gel, and EDTA would raise potassium rather than lower it."}, {"id": 1537, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A laboratory director asks which organizations' reference systems HbA1c methods should be traceable to so that results are comparable between laboratories. The quality manager must include this in the method validation plan for an analyzer that reports HbA1c in both percent and mmol/mol. Which pair of systems is correct?", "options": {"A": "National Glycohemoglobin Standardization Program and International Federation of Clinical Chemistry", "B": "Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute and the World Health Organization reference measurement procedure", "C": "College of American Pathologists and the International Diabetes Federation", "D": "Westgard rules and the Friedewald reference system for glycated hemoglobin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (National Glycohemoglobin Standardization Program and International Federation of Clinical Chemistry): HbA1c methods should be traceable to the NGSP and IFCC reference systems, and results can be reported in percent or mmol/mol. The other pairs name real bodies or tools but not the accepted HbA1c reference systems, and Westgard rules are for quality control."}, {"id": 1538, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A provincial screening program has moved to primary HPV testing. A clinic asks the laboratory why cytology is now used only when the HPV result is positive, and why a negative result allows a longer interval between screens, commonly five years.", "options": {"A": "HPV testing is cheaper, so intervals lengthen whatever the result", "B": "HPV testing identifies cell shape, so cytology adds no further information", "C": "HPV testing is more sensitive for high-grade lesions, so a negative result gives longer reassurance", "D": "HPV testing is less sensitive, so positive results are simply ignored"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (HPV testing is more sensitive for high-grade lesions, so a negative result gives longer reassurance): HPV testing finds high-grade lesions more sensitively than cytology, so a negative test offers longer reassurance and cytology triages the positives. Cost is not the reason for long intervals. The test detects viral nucleic acid and not cell shape."}, {"id": 1539, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A validated PCR gives strong product at an annealing temperature of 58 °C. After a protocol edit, the annealing step is accidentally set to 72 °C, and the gel shows no band in specimens that were positive before. Which explanation best fits this result?", "options": {"A": "Excess magnesium precipitated at that temperature, which removed cofactor from the reaction.", "B": "Taq polymerase was denatured at 72 °C, so no extension was possible.", "C": "Template strands failed to separate, because denaturation was no longer performed.", "D": "Primers did not stay annealed at that temperature, so little or no product formed."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Primers did not stay annealed at that temperature, so little or no product formed.): An annealing temperature that is too high prevents primers from binding, so the yield falls or is lost. Taq works at 72 °C, the denaturation step was not changed, and magnesium does not precipitate under these conditions."}, {"id": 1540, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A blood culture harvest yields well-spread metaphases, but the technologist cannot get chromosomes to separate from one another because the cells stayed tightly packed on the slide after fixation. Before fixation, the cell pellet was resuspended in a solution meant to swell the cells. Which reagent was this step designed to use?", "options": {"A": "A hypotonic solution, which swells the cells so the chromosomes spread apart.", "B": "Colcemid, which arrests cells in metaphase so the chromosomes spread apart.", "C": "A hypertonic solution, which shrinks the cells so the chromosomes lie close together.", "D": "Methanol and acetic acid, which swell the cells so the bands appear on staining."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A hypotonic solution, which swells the cells so the chromosomes spread apart.): Hypotonic treatment draws water into the cells and swells them so that chromosomes can spread on the slide. A hypertonic solution would shrink the cells and keep chromosomes crowded. Colcemid arrests cells in metaphase without swelling them, and methanol and acetic acid fix the cells after swelling."}, {"id": 1541, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A 63-year-old patient with pancytopenia and fatigue for several weeks has the bone marrow differential shown in the image, from an aspirate with the cellularity noted in the caption. The pathologist asks the technologist which diagnostic category the blast percentage alone supports before cytochemistry and flow results return. Which statement best matches this marrow result?", "options": {"A": "Myelodysplastic syndrome, since marrow blasts remain below the usual 20 percent threshold", "B": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia, since marrow lymphocytes exceed the usual 30 percent threshold", "C": "Reactive marrow, since blast counts up to 40 percent occur during recovery", "D": "Acute leukemia, since blasts exceed the usual 20 percent diagnostic threshold"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acute leukemia, since blasts exceed the usual 20 percent diagnostic threshold): A marrow blast count of 34 percent is above the usual 20 percent threshold for acute leukemia, and lineage is then assigned by morphology, cytochemistry and flow cytometry. Myelodysplasia is defined by fewer blasts, lymphocytes here are normal, and reactive marrow does not show this many blasts.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Bone marrow differential", "columns": ["Test", "Result", "Units", "Reference interval", "Flag"], "rows": [["Blasts", "34", "%", "less than 5", "H"], ["Promyelocytes", "6", "%", "1 to 5", "H"], ["Myeloid maturing cells", "32", "%", "30 to 60", ""], ["Erythroid precursors", "18", "%", "15 to 30", ""], ["Lymphocytes", "10", "%", "5 to 20", ""]], "flag_col": 4, "caption": "Marrow cellularity 90 percent"}, "image": "question_images/q_1541.png"}, {"id": 1542, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A blood harvest yields plenty of metaphases, but the chromosomes are very short and stubby, and there is no visible band detail after G-banding. The technologist reviews the record and finds the colcemid exposure was 90 minutes rather than the usual 30 minutes. What should be changed for the next harvest?", "options": {"A": "Skip the hypotonic step, and change the fixative ratio at the same time", "B": "Lengthen the colcemid exposure further, since a longer arrest improves banding quality", "C": "Increase the trypsin time, and review the colcemid step only after banding is checked", "D": "Shorten the colcemid exposure to the usual time and change only that variable"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shorten the colcemid exposure to the usual time and change only that variable): Over-long colcemid exposure over-condenses chromosomes, so shortening it is the fix, and changing one variable at a time shows what worked. Lengthening exposure worsens condensation, and altering trypsin or skipping hypotonic does not address the cause."}, {"id": 1543, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A trainee is asked which components colour the cytoplasm in the Papanicolaou stain, after hematoxylin has coloured the nuclei. The trainee should name the counterstains that give cells their different colours, such as orange or pink in keratinized cells. Which two stains supply those cytoplasmic colours?", "options": {"A": "Periodic acid and Schiff reagent from the carbohydrate stain group", "B": "Giemsa and May-Grunwald stains from the Romanowsky group", "C": "Orange G (OG-6) and the polychrome EA stain", "D": "Hematoxylin and Scott's tap water substitute for bluing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Orange G (OG-6) and the polychrome EA stain): OG-6 and EA colour the cytoplasm, giving orange or pink in keratinized cells and blue-green in non-keratinized cells. Hematoxylin colours nuclei, and the other pairs belong to different staining methods."}, {"id": 1544, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A bronchial brushing shows a tight group of enlarged cells with prominent nucleoli, but the cells have a smooth nuclear outline, even chromatin, and cilia with a terminal bar on the surface. The clinical history includes recent radiation therapy. What is the most appropriate interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Small cell carcinoma, since tight groups of cells with nucleoli prove nuclear molding", "B": "Reactive bronchial cells, since cilia and smooth even chromatin favour benign change", "C": "Adenocarcinoma, since enlarged cells with prominent nucleoli indicate malignancy", "D": "Contaminant pollen, since round cells in tight groups are unrelated to airway tissue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reactive bronchial cells, since cilia and smooth even chromatin favour benign change): Radiation and irritation can enlarge bronchial cells and produce nucleoli, but cilia, a terminal bar, a smooth outline and even chromatin favour benign change. Nucleoli alone do not prove adenocarcinoma. Small cell carcinoma has small cells with molding, and pollen has a distinct wall."}, {"id": 1545, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A student is learning venipuncture site selection and asks an instructor which vein is usually the first choice in an adult when the arm is intact and no other limitation applies. The instructor also lists the cephalic and basilic veins as alternatives. Which vein is the usual first choice?", "options": {"A": "The basilic vein along the inner side of the upper arm", "B": "The median cubital vein at the front of the elbow", "C": "The dorsal hand vein on the back of the wrist", "D": "The cephalic vein along the thumb side of the forearm"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The median cubital vein at the front of the elbow): The median cubital vein is large, well anchored and usually easy to find at the front of the elbow, so it is chosen first. The basilic and cephalic veins are used when the median cubital vein is unsuitable. The basilic lies close to the brachial artery and a nerve, which adds risk, and hand veins are reserved for situations where the arm veins cannot be used."}, {"id": 1546, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A Proteus mirabilis isolate from a urine culture is resistant to ceftriaxone and aztreonam. The clavulanate combination disk produces a large increase in zone size, and cefoxitin and meropenem remain susceptible. The clinician asks which agent is normally the treatment of choice. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "A carbapenem, because ESBL enzymes do not destroy it, making it the usual treatment", "B": "A cephamycin alone, because every ESBL is resistant to carbapenem drugs in routine practice", "C": "A third-generation cephalosporin, because the clavulanate result suggests the enzyme is not clinically important", "D": "Vancomycin, because it is the drug of choice for beta-lactamase-producing gram-negative bacilli"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A carbapenem, because ESBL enzymes do not destroy it, making it the usual treatment): The pattern indicates an ESBL, which hydrolyzes penicillins, third- and fourth-generation cephalosporins and aztreonam, but not carbapenems. Infections are usually treated with a carbapenem. Vancomycin is not active against gram-negative bacilli."}, {"id": 1547, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A patient has persistently elevated hemoglobin and a raised platelet count, and the hematologist suspects a myeloproliferative neoplasm. The molecular laboratory is asked to test a whole blood sample for a common point mutation by PCR. Which target is most commonly requested?", "options": {"A": "The JAK2 V617F point mutation in genomic DNA extracted from blood.", "B": "The BCR-ABL1 fusion transcript measured by reverse transcription PCR.", "C": "The EGFR exon 19 deletion detected on a lung tissue block.", "D": "The BRAF V600E mutation, which defines this group of blood disorders."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The JAK2 V617F point mutation in genomic DNA extracted from blood.): JAK2 V617F is a point mutation found in most patients with polycythemia vera and in many with essential thrombocythemia, and PCR detects it in DNA from blood. BCR-ABL1 marks chronic myeloid leukemia. BRAF V600E is associated with melanoma, thyroid and hairy cell leukemia. EGFR deletions are lung tumour markers."}, {"id": 1548, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A medullary thyroid carcinoma is diagnosed on an aspirate, and the report mentions that the finding can lead to genetic counselling because some cases are inherited. A student asks which hereditary syndrome is linked to some medullary carcinomas. Which inherited syndrome should the student name?", "options": {"A": "Li-Fraumeni syndrome, which involves early breast cancer, sarcoma and brain tumours", "B": "Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1, which involves pituitary, parathyroid and pancreatic tumours", "C": "Lynch syndrome, which involves inherited colorectal and endometrial cancer", "D": "Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2, which is why counselling can follow the finding"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2, which is why counselling can follow the finding): Some medullary carcinomas are part of multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2, so the diagnosis can prompt genetic counselling. Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 involves pituitary, parathyroid and pancreatic tumours. Li-Fraumeni and Lynch syndromes carry other cancer patterns and are not the syndromes of medullary thyroid carcinoma."}, {"id": 1549, "domain": "cytology", "question": "On a breast smear, the cytotechnologist notices small clear vacuoles within the cytoplasm of some epithelial cells, and a few appear as signet-ring forms. The trainee wants the correct name for these cytoplasmic vacuoles and the type of cancer where they are often seen. What are these vacuoles called, and where do they occur?", "options": {"A": "Psammoma bodies, which are calcified structures that support papillary thyroid carcinoma", "B": "Intranuclear pseudo-inclusions, which are cytoplasm-filled holes in nuclei of papillary carcinoma", "C": "Chromatin margination, which describes chromatin pushed to the membrane in decoy cells", "D": "Intracytoplasmic lumina, which can be seen in ductal and especially lobular carcinoma"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Intracytoplasmic lumina, which can be seen in ductal and especially lobular carcinoma): Small clear vacuoles within the cytoplasm are intracytoplasmic lumina, seen in ductal carcinoma and especially in lobular carcinoma with signet-ring cells. Psammoma bodies are calcified round structures. Pseudo-inclusions are holes inside nuclei, not cytoplasm. Chromatin margination describes the nucleus of a BK-infected decoy cell."}, {"id": 1550, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A technologist is preparing agarose gels stained with ethidium bromide, a mutagen. A colleague pours the used stain-containing buffer into the sink and wipes down the spilled bench area with a paper towel. How should this waste and area be handled?", "options": {"A": "Collect it as chemical waste and decontaminate surfaces per the approved procedure.", "B": "Mix it with bleach in the sink to neutralize the stain before rinsing it away.", "C": "Autoclave the buffer, which destroys the ethidium and then allows safe drain disposal.", "D": "Flush with plenty of water since dilution makes the stain harmless to plumbing."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Collect it as chemical waste and decontaminate surfaces per the approved procedure.): Ethidium bromide is a mutagen and needs collection as chemical waste, with surface decontamination following the written procedure. Dilution down the drain releases it to the environment. Autoclaving does not destroy ethidium, and bleach mixed in a sink is not a validated way of inactivating it. Many laboratories now use safer dyes."}, {"id": 1551, "domain": "histology", "question": "An auditor asks the laboratory to prove where a block was and who handled it at each stage from grossing through embedding, cutting and filing, after an identification error was suspected on a prostate case. Which laboratory feature allows this reconstruction?", "options": {"A": "The pathologist's final report, which lists the diagnosis but not any handling steps.", "B": "The verbal recollection of the technologist who handled the specimen on the day.", "C": "An audit trail from scans or a manual log of who handled each item.", "D": "A single annual inventory of all blocks that lists only their storage cabinet locations."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An audit trail from scans or a manual log of who handled each item.): Barcode scans or a manual log at each station record who handled the item, at what time and at which step, giving an audit trail an investigation can follow. An annual inventory shows only where blocks are stored. Memory is unreliable and unverifiable, and a final report describes the diagnosis rather than the handling."}, {"id": 1552, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The patient recently had surgery. An anaerobic culture from a tissue biopsy shows no growth at 24 hours in the anaerobic chamber. The technologist wonders whether to discard the plate and report no growth. What is the appropriate course of action for anaerobic cultures?", "options": {"A": "Freeze the plate at minus 20 degrees and read it after several days of storage", "B": "Move the plate to an aerobic incubator at 35 degrees to help the organisms recover", "C": "Discard the plate and report no growth, since anaerobes appear within 24 hours", "D": "Continue incubation, since anaerobic cultures are usually held for 48 hours or more before being read"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue incubation, since anaerobic cultures are usually held for 48 hours or more before being read): Anaerobic cultures are usually held for 48 hours or more before reading because anaerobes grow slowly. Moving to air would kill them, and freezing would stop the growth. Reporting no growth at 24 hours is premature."}, {"id": 1553, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "By IAT, a serum reacts with S-positive cells and does not react with S-negative cells. The reactions occur at 37 degrees Celsius and at the antiglobulin phase but not at immediate spin. The technologist decides how to describe the antibody in the report. Which statement about anti-S is correct?", "options": {"A": "It is destroyed by enzymes, so its reactions vanish when ficin-treated cells are tested", "B": "It is mostly IgM and cold reactive, so it is usually not clinically significant", "C": "It matters in pregnancy but is not a concern for transfusion", "D": "It is IgG and clinically significant, so S-negative units are needed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is IgG and clinically significant, so S-negative units are needed): Anti-S and anti-s are IgG antibodies in the MNS system and are clinically significant, so antigen-negative units are needed. Anti-M and anti-N are the MNS antibodies that are mostly IgM and cold reactive. Enzymes do not explain the reaction pattern here."}, {"id": 1554, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A technologist confirms a suspected TRALI case and needs to decide who must be informed. The team explains why the notification step matters beyond the care of the individual patient. Why must the blood supplier be told about every suspected TRALI case?", "options": {"A": "So that the supplier can irradiate the remaining components from the same donation", "B": "So that the hospital can return the unit to inventory for another patient later", "C": "So the implicated donors can be investigated and other components traced", "D": "So that the patient can be billed for the additional testing performed by the laboratory"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (So the implicated donors can be investigated and other components traced): Notifying the supplier lets it investigate the implicated donors and look back at other components from the donation. Billing has no role in the notification, a suspect unit is never returned to inventory, and irradiation does not remove the HLA or neutrophil antibodies responsible."}, {"id": 1555, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The specimen came from a farm injury. A deep wound culture grows a large gram-positive rod that is a strict anaerobe. On egg yolk blood agar the colonies show a double zone of hemolysis and lecithinase activity. Which organism and clinical condition does this pattern indicate?", "options": {"A": "Clostridium tetani, the organism that causes gas gangrene in contaminated deep wounds", "B": "Clostridium botulinum, the organism that causes gas gangrene in contaminated deep wounds", "C": "Clostridium perfringens, the organism that causes gas gangrene in contaminated deep wounds", "D": "Bacillus cereus, the organism that causes gas gangrene in contaminated deep wounds in routine practice"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clostridium perfringens, the organism that causes gas gangrene in contaminated deep wounds): Clostridium perfringens shows a double zone of hemolysis with lecithinase activity and causes gas gangrene. Clostridium tetani has a terminal round spore and causes tetanus, and Clostridium botulinum produces a paralyzing neurotoxin. Bacillus cereus is not an anaerobe."}, {"id": 1556, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A midwife asks why ABO hemolytic disease is usually mild even though group O mothers carry IgG anti-A and anti-B that cross the placenta. The technologist explains a feature of the fetal red cells that limits the damage. Which feature is responsible?", "options": {"A": "Fetal red cells carry fewer A and B sites than adult cells.", "B": "Fetal red cells are coated with maternal complement that protects them.", "C": "Fetal red cells carry extra Rh sites that block the antibody attachment.", "D": "Fetal red cells are removed by the liver before the antibody attaches."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fetal red cells carry fewer A and B sites than adult cells.): Fetal red cells have fewer A and B antigen sites than adult cells, so less maternal IgG binds and hemolysis is usually mild, and the newborn DAT may be weak or even negative. The other statements describe protective mechanisms that do not exist."}, {"id": 1557, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient has a prolonged PT and aPTT and liver disease is suspected, but DIC is also possible. The physician asks how to distinguish the two using factor VIII. What finding supports liver disease over DIC? Both conditions are serious and can occur together in a septic patient.", "options": {"A": "Low factor VIII levels, since it is consumed in widespread clot formation", "B": "Normal fibrinogen levels, since fibrinogen is made outside the liver and rises in disease", "C": "High platelet counts, since they are made by the liver in large numbers", "D": "Normal or high factor VIII, since it is made outside hepatocytes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Normal or high factor VIII, since it is made outside hepatocytes): Liver disease usually has normal or high factor VIII, while consumption in DIC lowers it. Fibrinogen is made in the liver and platelets are made in bone marrow, so the other statements are incorrect."}, {"id": 1558, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A physician reads a report where the interpretive category for one drug is listed as susceptible-dose dependent. The clinician asks the microbiology laboratory what this category means for treatment. How should the technologist explain the meaning of this category? The clinician also wants to know how it affects dosing.", "options": {"A": "The drug works at the usual dose in every patient, whatever the infection site", "B": "Success depends on using a higher or more frequent dose than the usual regimen", "C": "The drug is unlikely to work at any dose and should be avoided", "D": "The result is invalid because only a susceptible breakpoint exists for that pair"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Success depends on using a higher or more frequent dose than the usual regimen): Susceptible-dose dependent means the drug can work if a higher dose or more frequent dosing than the usual regimen is used. Resistant means unlikely to work, and non-susceptible is the category used when only a susceptible breakpoint exists."}, {"id": 1559, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A woman with an unknown blood type arrives in labour and needs emergency plasma before her type is confirmed. The laboratory must select a plasma product that will be compatible with any red cell group she may turn out to have. Which plasma is chosen?", "options": {"A": "Group AB plasma, which lacks both anti-A and anti-B activity.", "B": "Group O plasma, because it lacks both anti-A and anti-B.", "C": "Group O plasma, because it lacks both A and B antigens.", "D": "Group B plasma, because it is the rarest and least likely to react."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Group AB plasma, which lacks both anti-A and anti-B activity.): Group AB plasma contains no anti-A or anti-B and is universal, and group A plasma is used in many protocols before the type is known. Group O plasma carries both anti-A and anti-B, so it can hemolyze recipient cells."}, {"id": 1560, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A college student develops jaundice and dark urine two days after eating fava beans and taking primaquine for malaria prophylaxis. The enzyme deficiency in this X-linked disorder normally protects red cells from oxidant damage by supplying reduced cofactor. Which cofactor does G6PD normally produce?", "options": {"A": "ATP, which maintains the sodium and potassium pump of the membrane", "B": "2,3-bisphosphoglycerate, which lowers the oxygen affinity of hemoglobin", "C": "NADH, which carries electrons in the glycolytic pathway of mature red cells", "D": "NADPH, which protects hemoglobin and membranes from oxidant stress"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (NADPH, which protects hemoglobin and membranes from oxidant stress): G6PD generates NADPH, which keeps glutathione reduced and protects hemoglobin from oxidants such as fava beans and primaquine. NADH, ATP and 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate are produced by other pathways in the red cell and do not supply the oxidant defence."}, {"id": 1561, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "The sodium electrode on a chemistry analyzer shows a slope that has dropped gradually over three weeks and now fails calibration. Sample lines show no air bubbles, the reference solution was replaced last week, and the cleaning cycle has run on schedule. What is the most likely cause and the appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "A failed photometer lamp, so replace the lamp and repeat the calibration", "B": "Deteriorated antibody reagent, so replace the reagent pack and recalibrate the module", "C": "Excess hemolysis in samples, so reject every specimen sent to the module", "D": "An aged, protein-coated membrane, so replace the electrode and recalibrate before reporting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An aged, protein-coated membrane, so replace the electrode and recalibrate before reporting): A slope that declines slowly with no bubbles or reference problems points to a worn or fouled membrane, and electrodes are replaced when slope falls below a set value. The photometer lamp is not part of the ISE module. Hemolysis affects individual samples, and antibody reagents do not exist in an electrolyte module."}, {"id": 1562, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cytotechnologist asks a trainee to describe the nuclear features that support an LSIL call in mature squamous cells instead of ASC-US. The trainee must give the size of the nucleus relative to a normal intermediate nucleus, plus the outline typical of this lesion.", "options": {"A": "Nuclei twice the normal size, with scant dense cytoplasm and coarse notched membranes throughout", "B": "Nuclei smaller than a normal intermediate nucleus, with smooth outlines", "C": "Nuclei larger than three times a normal intermediate nucleus, with irregular outlines", "D": "Nuclei about the same size as normal, with a thin smooth outline"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Nuclei larger than three times a normal intermediate nucleus, with irregular outlines): LSIL nuclei exceed three times the size of a normal intermediate nucleus and show irregular outlines and hyperchromasia, often with a halo. Nuclei of normal size or smaller are not atypical. Scant dense cytoplasm with notched membranes points to HSIL instead."}, {"id": 1563, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cytology laboratory wants to reduce the chance of false-positive results from cross-contamination during staining. The manager considers several changes to the workflow. The manager wants a practical measure that can be introduced this week without buying new equipment or instruments. Which change is most effective at reducing floaters?", "options": {"A": "Rinse each slide in the same alcohol bath to remove any loose cells", "B": "Filter the stains regularly and use separate staining lines for gynecologic and non-gynecologic slides", "C": "Keep using the same stains for several weeks without replacement, so staining results remain consistent", "D": "Stain all specimens together in one line to save time and reagent"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Filter the stains regularly and use separate staining lines for gynecologic and non-gynecologic slides): Filtering, timely replacement and separating staining lines reduce floaters. Reusing stains and shared baths spread cells between slides."}, {"id": 1564, "domain": "histology", "question": "A special procedure on nervous tissue requires thicker paraffin sections than the usual diagnostic range. The technologist checks the thickness dial before cutting. About what thickness is described as suitable for such special purposes? The specimen comes from a laboratory that handles brain biopsies.", "options": {"A": "About 1 to 2 micrometres, for clearer cellular outlines", "B": "About 20 to 30 micrometres, for maximum stain intensity", "C": "About 0.1 micrometre, for use in electron microscopy work", "D": "About 6 to 10 micrometres, for nervous tissue"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 6 to 10 micrometres, for nervous tissue): Thicker sections of about 6 to 10 micrometres are used for nervous tissue and some special stains. Thinner sections give better nuclear detail for kidney and immunohistochemistry. Sections thicker than this would be dark and blurred."}, {"id": 1565, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient with severe vitamin B12 deficiency has a hypercellular marrow, but many developing red cells die before leaving the marrow, and the CBC also shows leukopenia and thrombocytopenia. The technologist is asked which chemistry results reflect this wasted production. What results reflect this ineffective erythropoiesis?", "options": {"A": "Raised direct bilirubin and raised alkaline phosphatase from obstruction of the bile ducts", "B": "Low indirect bilirubin and low lactate dehydrogenase from suppressed marrow production", "C": "Raised indirect bilirubin and raised lactate dehydrogenase from the breakdown of dying precursors", "D": "Raised haptoglobin and raised urine urobilinogen from increased red cell survival"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raised indirect bilirubin and raised lactate dehydrogenase from the breakdown of dying precursors): When megaloblasts die in the marrow, hemoglobin and cell contents are released, raising indirect bilirubin and LDH. Direct bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase reflect biliary disease. Haptoglobin is typically low, not raised, when red cells are being broken down."}, {"id": 1566, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A cerebrospinal fluid tube arrives for herpes simplex PCR with a label that shows only a first name and no second identifier. The transport container is intact and the volume is adequate. The provider insists it is urgent. What should the technologist do?", "options": {"A": "Hold the tube, contact the provider, and follow the unlabelled specimen policy.", "B": "Discard it and request a new lumbar puncture without notifying anyone else.", "C": "Add the second identifier from memory of the requisition and then proceed with testing.", "D": "Test it now and note the missing second identifier on the final report afterwards."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold the tube, contact the provider, and follow the unlabelled specimen policy.): Two patient identifiers are needed to link a specimen to the right patient, and a mislabelled result could harm someone else. Because cerebrospinal fluid is hard to recollect, the specimen is held while the provider is contacted and the documented policy is followed. Testing first, discarding without communication, or completing the label from memory all bypass proper identification."}, {"id": 1567, "domain": "histology", "question": "A barcode scanner is used at every station in a laboratory that tracks specimens. A technologist notices one cassette label that does not match the container it came from, although the scanner accepted it without any warning. The pathologist has not yet seen the case. What is the technologist's responsibility?", "options": {"A": "Reprint the label from the system and attach it over the existing one silently.", "B": "Confirm personally that the label matches the container, since scanning cannot replace that duty.", "C": "Wait for the pathologist to detect the mismatch during microscopic review, since diagnosis is checked there.", "D": "Trust the scanner result, since the barcode system has already verified the identifiers."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Confirm personally that the label matches the container, since scanning cannot replace that duty.): Barcodes reduce transcription errors, but the technologist remains responsible for confirming that the label matches the contents in hand. Overwriting a label without investigation hides the error, and waiting for the pathologist means the wrong tissue may already be processed and reported. The mismatch should also be recorded so the tracking system shows where it arose."}, {"id": 1568, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A grossly bloody pleural fluid arrives in the laboratory, and clotting is starting in the container, which could trap tumour cells and compromise the sample. The technologist knows one step is used for bloody fluids to prevent this problem. What should be done?", "options": {"A": "Add an anticoagulant so that the specimen does not clot and trap cells", "B": "Refrigerate the container without agitation so the clot can settle undisturbed", "C": "Add a large volume of formalin so that red cells are removed by fixation", "D": "Discard the clotted portion and process only the thin liquid layer"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Add an anticoagulant so that the specimen does not clot and trap cells): Anticoagulant is used in bloody fluids to prevent clotting that traps cells. Formalin does not remove red cells and harms alcohol-based staining. Settling a clot leaves cells trapped inside it, and discarding it loses diagnostic cells."}, {"id": 1569, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A smear shows immature granulocytes and nucleated red cells together, a pattern that is not typical of a simple infection in this 67-year-old patient with bone pain. The technologist recognizes a leukoerythroblastic picture. What is the most appropriate next step in reporting this smear?", "options": {"A": "Report it as infectious mononucleosis, since immature cells appear during that viral infection", "B": "Bring it to the pathologist's attention, since it suggests marrow infiltration or stress", "C": "Report it as normal, since nucleated red cells are common in adults", "D": "Report it as a leukemoid reaction only, without any referral or comment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bring it to the pathologist's attention, since it suggests marrow infiltration or stress): A leukoerythroblastic picture suggests marrow infiltration or stress and needs the pathologist's attention. Nucleated red cells are not normal in adult blood, infectious mononucleosis shows reactive lymphocytes, and a leukemoid reaction label alone omits the required referral."}, {"id": 1570, "domain": "histology", "question": "A pathologist looks at serial 4 µm sections of the same cirrhotic liver, one stained with an elastic method and one with a trichrome method, and asks which stains collagen red. Which stain colours collagen red and elastic fibres black?", "options": {"A": "Prussian blue with nuclear fast red, a stain for ferric iron", "B": "Ziehl-Neelsen with methylene blue counterstain, a stain for acid-fast bacilli such as mycobacteria", "C": "Periodic acid-Schiff with diastase, a stain for glycogen and mucin", "D": "Verhoeff-Van Gieson, an elastic stain that leaves other background tissue pale yellow"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Verhoeff-Van Gieson, an elastic stain that leaves other background tissue pale yellow): In Verhoeff-Van Gieson elastic fibres are black and collagen is red. Prussian blue and nuclear fast red show iron, PAS shows carbohydrate, and Ziehl-Neelsen detects acid-fast organisms."}, {"id": 1571, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A serum protein electrophoresis on a 45-year-old man with chronic osteomyelitis and fever shows a broad-based increase in the gamma region and a mildly reduced albumin band. No sharp, discrete band is seen anywhere on the scan. How should the technologist describe the gamma pattern on the report?", "options": {"A": "Alpha-2 macroglobulin band caused by protein loss from nephrotic syndrome into the urine", "B": "Polyclonal increase in gamma globulins, consistent with chronic inflammation or infection", "C": "Monoclonal gammopathy that needs immunofixation to identify the paraprotein class", "D": "Hypogammaglobulinemia with reduced immunoglobulins in every region of the scan"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Polyclonal increase in gamma globulins, consistent with chronic inflammation or infection): A broad rise in gamma globulins reflects many antibody clones responding at once, which is typical of chronic infection or inflammation. A monoclonal protein would appear as a sharp dense band. The gamma region here is increased rather than reduced, and nephrotic loss affects the alpha-2 region."}, {"id": 1572, "domain": "hematology", "question": "Daily controls have been in range all week, yet the moving average of patient MCV values has crept upward by 4 fL. The reagent lot and the staff have not changed. What does this pattern suggest, and what should the laboratory do?", "options": {"A": "Cold agglutinins in many patients, so warm every sample before running", "B": "A true population change in patients, so no action is needed", "C": "Calibration drift missed by controls, so verify the calibration", "D": "Deteriorated control material, so only the control lot should be replaced"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Calibration drift missed by controls, so verify the calibration): Patient-based QC such as a moving average of MCV, MCH and MCHC can show a shift suggesting calibration drift even when controls look normal, so calibration is checked and verified with controls. A sudden population change within one week is unlikely. Control deterioration would show on the chart. Cold agglutinins affect individual samples, not a whole week."}, {"id": 1573, "domain": "histology", "question": "Sections of a decalcified rib specimen show pale, washed-out nuclei and overly red cytoplasm after staining with hematoxylin and eosin. The technologist reviews the record of the decalcification run, which lists acid type, volume and start and finish times. What is the most probable cause of this staining appearance?", "options": {"A": "Insufficient dehydration in the processor, which left water in the nuclei of the cells.", "B": "Too short a formalin fixation, which prevented cross-links from forming in the cytoplasm.", "C": "Wax at too low a temperature, which prevented the eosin from binding to the proteins.", "D": "Overexposure to acid during decalcification, which impairs nuclear staining of the tissue."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Overexposure to acid during decalcification, which impairs nuclear staining of the tissue.): Prolonged acid exposure hydrolyzes nucleic acids and removes the basophilia that hematoxylin needs, leaving pale nuclei against strongly eosinophilic cytoplasm. That is why the type of decalcifier and its time are recorded. Poor dehydration produces cutting and clearing problems, and cold wax affects sectioning, not dye binding."}, {"id": 1574, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "On a sodium run over four consecutive days, the level 1 control reads +1.2, +1.4, +1.1 and +1.3 SD above its mean. No single value exceeded 2 SD, and level 2 was normal. The technologist plots the values on the fourth day. What is the appropriate interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Normal variation, because all four points remain inside 2 SD of the mean", "B": "R-4s violation pointing to random error, so repeat the control until it is acceptable", "C": "4-1s violation pointing to systematic error, so reject and investigate before reporting", "D": "1-2s warning only, so the run is accepted and no investigation is needed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (4-1s violation pointing to systematic error, so reject and investigate before reporting): Four consecutive values beyond 1 SD on the same side violate the 4-1s rule, which indicates systematic error even though no point is extreme. No value reached the 2 SD warning threshold, so a 1-2s reading is wrong. R-4s needs values on opposite sides, and repeating controls until they pass is not acceptable."}, {"id": 1575, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A student sets up an improved Neubauer chamber for a manual count and needs to know the volume over one large square. Each large square measures 1 mm by 1 mm, and the coverslip sets a fixed depth over the ruled grid. What volume does one large square hold?", "options": {"A": "0.1 microlitre, from 1 mm x 1 mm x 0.1 mm depth", "B": "0.9 microlitre, from nine large squares at 0.1 mm depth", "C": "1.0 microlitre, from 1 mm x 1 mm x 1 mm depth", "D": "0.01 microlitre, from 1 mm x 1 mm x 0.01 mm depth"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (0.1 microlitre, from 1 mm x 1 mm x 0.1 mm depth): The chamber depth is 0.1 mm, so one large square of 1 mm2 holds 0.1 mm3, which is 0.1 microlitre. A depth of 1 mm or 0.01 mm would be a wrong chamber. The value 0.9 confuses the total for the nine large squares with a single square."}, {"id": 1576, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A blood bank thaws and pools cryoprecipitate for a bleeding patient, but the patient is moved to the operating room and the transfusion is delayed. The technologist is asked how quickly the pooled product must be used and why. What is the correct answer?", "options": {"A": "Within a few hours, because thawed pools are prone to red cell antigen loss", "B": "Within a few hours, because labile factor VIII is lost quickly at room temperature", "C": "Within several days, because fibrinogen is stable at room temperature after thawing", "D": "Within one week, because the thawed pool is returned to the freezer safely"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Within a few hours, because labile factor VIII is lost quickly at room temperature): Once thawed and pooled, cryoprecipitate must be transfused within a few hours, since labile factor VIII declines quickly at room temperature. Fibrinogen stability does not extend the outdate that far. Red cell antigens are not in cryoprecipitate, and thawed product is not refrozen and reused."}, {"id": 1577, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A patient from the South Pacific with leg swelling is suspected of lymphatic filariasis. A day-time blood draw at 10:00 shows nothing, and the laboratory is told that the local nocturnally periodic strain of Wuchereria bancrofti peaks around midnight. How should blood films be collected?", "options": {"A": "On a stool specimen, since microfilariae are found only in the intestine", "B": "At any hour of the day, since microfilariae circulate at the same level continuously", "C": "Only during fever spikes, since microfilariae circulate only when the patient has a fever", "D": "At the hour of highest circulating numbers, with Knott concentration to increase yield"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (At the hour of highest circulating numbers, with Knott concentration to increase yield): Microfilariae show periodicity, so collection is timed to the peak, and Knott concentration improves detection. Circulation is not constant or fever-driven, and these worms are found in blood rather than stool."}, {"id": 1578, "domain": "cytology", "question": "During correlation, a biopsy taken after a negative smear shows a high-grade lesion. The laboratory finds the smear was read as negative by a cytotechnologist and the slide was not part of the rescreen set. What is the correct quality response?", "options": {"A": "Retrain the cytotechnologist and treat the biopsy as proof of negligence without review", "B": "Correct the original report quietly and say nothing about the earlier interpretation", "C": "Discard the slide since the biopsy result is the final diagnosis for the patient", "D": "Record the discrepancy and submit it for review so the cause can be identified"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Record the discrepancy and submit it for review so the cause can be identified): Discrepancies between cytology and follow-up biopsy are recorded and reviewed so the cause can be found. Silent correction hides the problem, discarding the slide destroys evidence, and assuming negligence skips the review."}, {"id": 1579, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A student asks why the blood bank never refrigerates platelet components, even though refrigeration would make storage easier and reduce bacterial growth. The technologist knows what happens to chilled platelets after transfusion. Which explanation is best? Which explanation is best supported by the teaching in this chapter?", "options": {"A": "Chilled platelets lose their ABO antigens and become incompatible with every recipient", "B": "Chilled platelets are cleared quickly from the circulation after they are transfused", "C": "Chilled platelets form clots inside the bag and block the administration set", "D": "Chilled platelets release large amounts of potassium into the unit before issue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Chilled platelets are cleared quickly from the circulation after they are transfused): Cold-stored platelets are removed rapidly from the recipient's circulation, so they are stored at room temperature with agitation. Potassium leakage is a red cell storage issue. Platelets do not lose ABO antigens in this way, and clotting in the bag is not the concern."}, {"id": 1580, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "In a patient's germline NGS data, every exon of a gene shows a normalized depth near 1.0 compared with control samples, except exon 4, which shows about 0.5 across all its amplicons in this patient. What does this pattern most likely indicate?", "options": {"A": "A heterozygous deletion of exon 4, a copy number variant seen as halved relative depth", "B": "A homozygous deletion of exon 4, which produces a relative depth ratio near 0.5 as well", "C": "A sequencing failure of exon 4, which is confirmed by removing the sample", "D": "A heterozygous duplication of exon 4, which produces a depth ratio near 0.5"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A heterozygous deletion of exon 4, a copy number variant seen as halved relative depth): CNVs are found through depth analysis, and one of two copies lost gives about half the expected depth. A homozygous deletion would drop the depth near zero, and a duplication would raise it toward 1.5. Failure of every amplicon in one exon is more consistent with a real dosage change than with removal of the sample."}, {"id": 1581, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Blood and inflammation cover much of a conventional smear from a 43-year-old. The technologist estimates what fraction of epithelial cells is hidden in order to decide whether to add a comment on obscuring factors. At what proportion should partial obscuring be noted?", "options": {"A": "More than about 50 to 75 percent of the epithelial cells are obscured", "B": "More than about 25 to 30 percent of the epithelial cells are obscured", "C": "More than about 10 to 20 percent of the epithelial cells are obscured", "D": "Once nearly all epithelial cells are hidden and none can be evaluated"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (More than about 50 to 75 percent of the epithelial cells are obscured): Partial obscuring by blood, inflammation, or other factors is noted when more than 50 to 75 percent of the epithelial cells are obscured. Lower fractions are common and do not need comment. A sample with almost nothing readable would move toward unsatisfactory."}, {"id": 1582, "domain": "cytology", "question": "After making direct smears from a thyroid nodule, the operator rinses the needle in a vial of preservative solution instead of discarding it. A newly hired technologist asks why the rinse is collected and sent to the laboratory. What is the purpose of this step?", "options": {"A": "To build a cell block or supply material for immunostains and molecular tests", "B": "To sterilize the needle before it is reused on the patient's second nodule", "C": "To wash away contaminating skin cells so the slides can be released faster", "D": "To dissolve thick colloid so that the smears become adequate for reporting"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (To build a cell block or supply material for immunostains and molecular tests): The needle rinse gathers leftover cells for a cell block or for immunostains and molecular tests. Needles are single use and are not reused. The rinse does not change the adequacy of the direct smears. Skin cells are not the reason for collecting the rinse."}, {"id": 1583, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A patient consents to whole exome sequencing at a genetics clinic but ticks the option refusing submission of her variants to public research databases. The laboratory later finds a novel variant that would be valuable in a shared database. What is the appropriate handling of this variant?", "options": {"A": "Submit it anyway, since novel variants must be shared for the benefit of science", "B": "Keep it out of the shared database, since the patient declined that use", "C": "Submit it if her physician agrees, since the physician can consent on her behalf", "D": "Submit it after removing her name, since removal of the name satisfies the refusal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep it out of the shared database, since the patient declined that use): Consent covers whether data may be shared in databases, and a patient may decline this use, which the laboratory respects. Removing the name or getting a physician's agreement does not replace her own refusal. A novel finding does not create a duty to share."}, {"id": 1584, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A blood karyotype from a man with infertility shows 46,XY[20], and the physician asks the laboratory whether a Y chromosome deletion could explain the problem. The technologist is asked what the routine karyotype can and cannot show. Which statement is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "It cannot show any structural change, since it only counts the chromosomes in each cell.", "B": "It can show every deletion of any size, so a normal result excludes all genetic causes.", "C": "It can show gross changes, but small deletions may lie below the band resolution studied.", "D": "It can show changes of the sex chromosomes but does not display the autosomes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It can show gross changes, but small deletions may lie below the band resolution studied.): Karyotyping is limited by resolution, so small deletions can be missed and other tests may be needed. Claiming that it excludes every genetic cause overstates the method. It does show structural changes by banding, and it studies all chromosomes, not just the sex chromosomes."}, {"id": 1585, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A bronchoalveolar lavage from a patient with AIDS (CD4 count 50 x 10^6/L), fever, and diffuse bilateral infiltrates is stained with a fluorescent dye that binds chitin, and small round cysts with a central dot fluoresce. Bacterial and fungal cultures stay negative. Which organism is being sought?", "options": {"A": "Pneumocystis jirovecii, whose cysts are detected with calcofluor and fluorescent stains", "B": "Cryptococcus neoformans, whose capsules are usually shown with India ink or antigen testing", "C": "Blastomyces dermatitidis, whose thick-walled yeasts grow readily on routine fungal media", "D": "Mucorales species, whose ribbon-like hyphae are easily grown on Sabouraud agar within days"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pneumocystis jirovecii, whose cysts are detected with calcofluor and fluorescent stains): Calcofluor is used for Pneumocystis cysts and microsporidia in addition to fungi. Cryptococcus is normally shown by India ink or antigen, and the other choices do not match the stated dye or clinical clue."}, {"id": 1586, "domain": "cytology", "question": "During a staff meeting held to update cervical screening workflows, A province explains its cervical program to laboratory staff, describing HPV testing first with cytology done afterward only on samples that test positive. What is this second step of cytology on positive samples called?", "options": {"A": "Reflex cytology, performed afterward on the samples that test HPV positive", "B": "Cytohistologic correlation, performed by comparing cytology with the later tissue biopsy result", "C": "Rapid prescreening, performed as a quick look to sort normal from suspicious slides", "D": "Random rescreening, performed by a second person on a sample of negative slides"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reflex cytology, performed afterward on the samples that test HPV positive): In HPV-only screening, cytology performed afterward on positive samples is called reflex cytology. Random rescreening reviews negatives, correlation compares cytology with tissue, and rapid prescreening sorts clearly normal slides from suspicious ones. Provincial details vary, so local program guidance should be followed."}, {"id": 1587, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A spray-fixed gynecologic slide is inspected on receipt, and the cell edges look dried and there are areas where cells appear blown off the glass. The clinic admits it sprayed the slide from a very short distance and after some delay. What was the error?", "options": {"A": "Applying the spray in a fume hood, which causes the coating to evaporate quickly", "B": "Spraying from too close and too late, which displaces cells and lets edges dry", "C": "Spraying from too far away and too early, which leaves a coating that is too thick", "D": "Using a spray fixative, since spray fixation cannot preserve cell detail on gynecologic slides"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Spraying from too close and too late, which displaces cells and lets edges dry): Fixative sprayed too close can blow cells off, and delay lets edges dry. The correct distance is about 20 to 25 cm and the spray is applied evenly right after smearing. A fume hood does not cause these artefacts."}, {"id": 1588, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A qualitative real-time assay detects a bacterial target in a specimen at Ct 27, and the internal control in that same well amplifies four cycles later than in other wells. All run controls are acceptable. How should the technologist handle the result?", "options": {"A": "Report positive at a reduced quantity based on the four-cycle delay of the control.", "B": "Report negative, because a delayed internal control invalidates every target in that well.", "C": "Report indeterminate and repeat the entire extraction before any result is released.", "D": "Report positive, since target amplification proves detection despite a delayed internal control."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report positive, since target amplification proves detection despite a delayed internal control.): The internal control exists to prevent false negatives, so a target that amplifies is a valid positive even if the control is delayed by competition or mild inhibition. Calling it negative or indeterminate ignores that the target was demonstrated. A qualitative assay does not report a quantity, and no correction factor is derived from the control delay."}, {"id": 1589, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A physician receives a blood result of 45,X[5]/46,XX[15] in a girl with short stature and asks whether the ovaries would show the same ratio. The technologist prepares a comment to include on the report to prevent misunderstanding. Which comment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "The ratio of cell lines in blood may not reflect the ratio in other tissues.", "B": "The ratio of cell lines in blood proves the patient is a chimera.", "C": "The ratio of cell lines in blood shows that the ovaries must be normal.", "D": "The ratio of cell lines in blood is identical in every tissue of the body."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The ratio of cell lines in blood may not reflect the ratio in other tissues.): Mosaic levels can differ between blood and gonads, so the report names the tissue studied and cautions that other tissues may differ. Assuming identical ratios or normal ovaries goes beyond the data. Chimerism needs two zygotes, and this pattern fits mosaicism from one."}, {"id": 1590, "domain": "histology", "question": "A laboratory compares FISH and CISH for probe-based detection of HER2 amplification in breast tumour sections. The laboratory owns both a fluorescence microscope and a standard brightfield microscope. Which statement correctly describes fluorescence in situ hybridization and how it is read?", "options": {"A": "It uses enzyme colour probes and is read with a light microscope", "B": "It uses labelled antibodies and is read with polarized light", "C": "It uses silver deposits and is read with a dissecting microscope", "D": "It uses fluorescent probes and is read with a fluorescence microscope"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It uses fluorescent probes and is read with a fluorescence microscope): FISH uses fluorescent labelled nucleic acid probes and requires a fluorescence microscope. CISH uses enzyme-based colour readable by light microscopy. Antibody and silver methods are not hybridization techniques."}, {"id": 1591, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "After the final fixative change, a technologist has a clean white cell pellet in fresh fixative but cannot make slides until the next morning because the drying chamber is in use. A trainee asks what to do with the pellet and the remaining material overnight. Which practice is correct?", "options": {"A": "Discard the pellet tonight, since fixed cells cannot be stored for slide making", "B": "Add fresh colcemid to the pellet, since fixed cells need more before dropping", "C": "Leave the pellet at room temperature for a week, since fixative dries out safely", "D": "Store the pellet in fixative in a refrigerator or freezer, and retain leftovers until case closure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Store the pellet in fixative in a refrigerator or freezer, and retain leftovers until case closure): Fixed pellets can be stored cold so slides can be made later, and specimens are kept until the case is complete. Discarding early loses irreplaceable material, and colcemid has no role after fixation."}, {"id": 1592, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A molecular technologist is asked what a typical high-risk HPV assay reports on a liquid-based sample, and which types are usually identified separately because they carry the greatest cancer risk. Which description of the assay output is correct? The assay was run on a residual vial from routine screening.", "options": {"A": "Only type 6 and type 11 are reported, each with a full viral load", "B": "Cervical cell abnormality is reported by the assay, and viral types are not identified", "C": "Any high-risk type is reported, with types 16 and 18 identified separately", "D": "Each of the more than one hundred types is named on every result"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Any high-risk type is reported, with types 16 and 18 identified separately): Many assays report the presence of any high-risk type and separately identify types 16 and 18, which carry the greatest risk. Types 6 and 11 are low-risk and not the target. Naming every type is not typical, and the assay detects viral nucleic acid, not cell abnormality."}, {"id": 1593, "domain": "hematology", "question": "An underfilled coagulation tube, at 70% of the stated draw volume, is received for a PT and aPTT on an urgent patient. The collector suggests adding blood from another tube to reach the fill line. What is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Add blood from a lavender tube and mix gently before testing the specimen", "B": "Reject it per policy and request a new, properly filled collection", "C": "Remove some citrate with a pipette and retest the adjusted specimen", "D": "Run the specimen and report the results with a comment about the low fill"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reject it per policy and request a new, properly filled collection): An underfilled tube has excess citrate that dilutes the sample and prolongs times, and topping up from another tube is not permitted. The specimen should be rejected and recollected."}, {"id": 1594, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A bone marrow culture that was set up for a leukemia study shows 92 chromosomes in three cells while every other cell has 46 chromosomes and no abnormalities. The clinician asks whether the patient has a tetraploid leukemia clone. How should the technologist interpret the finding?", "options": {"A": "A balanced translocation, since the total number of chromosomes has doubled.", "B": "Often a culture artifact, so it should not be reported as a true clone without support.", "C": "A sign of triploidy, since 92 chromosomes is a whole extra haploid set.", "D": "A true tetraploid clone, since two cells with the same gain define a clone."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Often a culture artifact, so it should not be reported as a true clone without support.): Tetraploid cells in a culture are often a culture artifact from cells that failed to divide, particularly when all other cells are normal. Triploidy would show 69 chromosomes, and a translocation does not double the count. Reporting a leukemic clone without other evidence overstates the finding."}, {"id": 1595, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A patient has an elevated red cell mass, a low erythropoietin level, and no secondary cause for the raised counts. The physician orders a mutation test that is positive in most patients with polycythemia vera. Which variant would the laboratory be looking for?", "options": {"A": "JAK2 V617F, detected by allele specific PCR or by sequencing", "B": "EGFR L858R, detected by allele specific PCR on tumour tissue", "C": "BCR-ABL1 p210, detected by quantitative RT-PCR on peripheral blood", "D": "BRAF V600E, detected by sequencing of plasma circulating DNA"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (JAK2 V617F, detected by allele specific PCR or by sequencing): JAK2 V617F is present in most patients with polycythemia vera and supports a diagnosis of a myeloproliferative neoplasm. BCR-ABL1 defines chronic myeloid leukemia. EGFR L858R is a lung cancer variant and BRAF V600E occurs in melanoma and other tumours."}, {"id": 1596, "domain": "cytology", "question": "An air-dried smear from a deep 8 cm thigh mass in a 61-year-old shows a myxoid background with spindle cells and a few large cells whose nuclei are indented by clear fat vacuoles. Mature fat cells are also present. Which cell type is this and what does it suggest?", "options": {"A": "Signet ring cells, suggesting metastatic gastric adenocarcinoma in soft tissue.", "B": "Strap cells, suggesting rhabdomyosarcoma with myogenin positivity on staining.", "C": "Lipoblasts, suggesting liposarcoma rather than a benign lipoma of mature fat cells.", "D": "Tingible-body macrophages, suggesting a reactive lymph node in the thigh."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lipoblasts, suggesting liposarcoma rather than a benign lipoma of mature fat cells.): Lipoblasts have fat vacuoles that dent the nucleus and point toward liposarcoma, while a lipoma shows only mature fat cells. Signet ring cells hold mucin that pushes the nucleus aside. Tingible-body macrophages contain nuclear debris, and strap cells are elongated muscle-type cells."}, {"id": 1597, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A pathologist explains that different respiratory specimens are better for different tumours. Sputum is inexpensive but less sensitive than bronchoscopy. A trainee asks which type of lung cancer is most likely to be found in sputum because it exfoliates from central airways. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Small cell carcinoma, which is located in the pleura and best found by lavage", "B": "Mesothelioma, which is located in the alveoli and best found by sputum", "C": "Squamous cell carcinoma, which is centrally located and often found in sputum", "D": "Peripheral adenocarcinoma, which is located in the outer lung and best found by brushing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Squamous cell carcinoma, which is centrally located and often found in sputum): Centrally located squamous cell carcinoma sheds into the airway and is often found in sputum. Peripheral adenocarcinoma is better sampled by brushing or needle aspiration. Small cell carcinoma is not a pleural tumour, and mesothelioma is a serosal tumour, not alveolar."}, {"id": 1598, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with a lipid profile order tells the collector that they had toast and coffee with milk about two hours ago and were not told to fast. The requisition has no fasting field completed. The collector is deciding how to handle the sample and the paperwork. What is the appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Collect the sample and record that the patient was not fasting on the requisition", "B": "Cancel the collection and rebook the patient for another day after a 12 hour fast", "C": "Collect the sample and enter fasting on the requisition so the reference ranges apply", "D": "Collect a gray top instead of a serum tube to offset the effect of the meal"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Collect the sample and record that the patient was not fasting on the requisition): Non-fasting lipid sampling is accepted by Canadian guidelines for many screening purposes, so the sample can be collected, but the requisition must state the patient was not fasting so the laboratory can report it in a comment. Declaring the patient fasting when they were not gives a false record. A gray top does not change the lipid content of the sample."}, {"id": 1599, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist replaces the reagents in the staining line and wants the change to be traceable for quality assurance. Later, a supervisor asks how long each dish of alcohol has been in use. What should have been recorded on each dish?", "options": {"A": "The number of slides cut on the microtome that day", "B": "The batch number of the glass slides in the stainer", "C": "The name of the reagent and the date it was changed", "D": "The name of the pathologist who will view the slides"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The name of the reagent and the date it was changed): Each dish is labelled with the reagent and the date it was changed so deterioration and carry-in can be tracked. The pathologist's name, slide batch and daily counts do not show reagent age, which is the information the supervisor needs."}, {"id": 1600, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cytospin operator is asked why the laboratory's validated procedure specifies the centrifuge speed rather than leaving it to individual choice. A student increases the setting to shorten the run time, hoping the cells will land on the slide faster. Which problem results from using a speed that is too high?", "options": {"A": "Very high speeds dissolve the filter card, so no fluid can be absorbed", "B": "Very high speeds fix the cells to the slide, so stain cannot be applied later", "C": "Very high speeds distort the cells, so settings must follow the validated procedure", "D": "Very high speeds cause the fluid to clot, so the sample must be reheated"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Very high speeds distort the cells, so settings must follow the validated procedure): High speed distorts cells, which is why settings follow the laboratory's validated procedure. It does not cause clotting, dissolve the filter or fix the cells."}, {"id": 1601, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A serum sample has a free hemoglobin of 3 g/L from a hemolysis index. The reported potassium is 5.9 mmol/L. Assuming the typical rise of about 0.3 mmol/L per 1 g/L of free hemoglobin, what is a reasonable estimate of the true potassium, and how should the laboratory act?", "options": {"A": "About 6.8 mmol/L; hemolysis lowers potassium so the true value is higher", "B": "About 4.1 mmol/L; hemolysis triples potassium so divide the result by three", "C": "About 5.0 mmol/L; treat it as unreliable and request recollection under laboratory limits", "D": "About 5.6 mmol/L; correct the number automatically and release it without a comment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 5.0 mmol/L; treat it as unreliable and request recollection under laboratory limits): With a rise of about 0.3 mmol/L for each 1 g/L, 3 g/L raises potassium by about 0.9 mmol/L, so 5.9 minus 0.9 gives roughly 5.0 mmol/L. Laboratories use their own validated thresholds and do not simply subtract an estimate, so the sample is treated as unreliable and recollection is requested. Hemolysis raises potassium, and it does not triple it."}, {"id": 1602, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist is asked to explain to a student why a diet lacking certain nutrients leads to poor red cell production. Heme synthesis requires one nutrient, while the DNA synthesis needed by rapidly dividing marrow cells requires two others. Which nutrients fit those roles?", "options": {"A": "Iron is needed for DNA synthesis, while vitamin B12 and folate are needed to form heme", "B": "Iron is needed to build heme, while vitamin B12 and folate support DNA synthesis", "C": "Folate is needed to form heme, while iron and vitamin B12 are needed for DNA synthesis", "D": "Vitamin B12 is needed to form heme, while iron and folate support globin synthesis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Iron is needed to build heme, while vitamin B12 and folate support DNA synthesis): Iron is built into heme, whereas B12 and folate supply steps in DNA synthesis. Swapping the roles gives a false statement. Globin chain synthesis is a separate gene controlled process and does not depend on B12 or folate in this way."}, {"id": 1603, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Two control materials for a quantitative assay are extracted identically. Material X crosses the threshold at Ct 20.0 and material Y at Ct 33.3, and the reaction efficiency is close to 100 percent in both. About how many times more target does material X contain than material Y?", "options": {"A": "About 10,000 times more target in material X than in material Y.", "B": "About 100 times more target in material X than in material Y.", "C": "About 13 times more target in material X than in material Y.", "D": "About 1,000 times more target in material X than in material Y."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 10,000 times more target in material X than in material Y.): The gap is 33.3 minus 20.0, which is 13.3 cycles, and about 3.32 cycles equal a ten-fold difference at ideal efficiency. Dividing gives about 4.0 ten-fold steps, so 10 to the power 4, or 10,000. Cross-check: 2 to the power 13.3 is roughly 10,000. The other values come from counting too few ten-fold steps or treating cycles as units."}, {"id": 1604, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A pathology resident reviews a patient with pneumonia and a CRP of 180 mg/L whose albumin is 26 g/L, although the ALT, bilirubin and INR are all normal. The resident asks why albumin is low when the liver is working well. What property of albumin explains this?", "options": {"A": "Albumin is a negative acute phase reactant, so its concentration falls during inflammation", "B": "Albumin is bound to bilirubin in inflammation, so it is removed by the kidney", "C": "Albumin is a positive acute phase reactant, so its concentration rises during inflammation", "D": "Albumin is destroyed by the biuret reagent in inflammation, so its result falls"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Albumin is a negative acute phase reactant, so its concentration falls during inflammation): Albumin is a negative acute phase reactant, so it falls during inflammation regardless of liver function. It is not a positive reactant. Binding bilirubin does not cause renal loss, and the biuret reagent is not used to measure albumin."}, {"id": 1605, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "Interphase FISH on a bone marrow sample from a patient with suspected acute promyelocytic leukemia uses red PML and green RARA dual-colour dual-fusion probes. Four nuclei in the image are scored. How many nuclei show the abnormal pattern, and what does this indicate?", "options": {"A": "One of four nuclei, suggesting a small residual clone of uncertain significance", "B": "Three of four nuclei, supporting a PML::RARA fusion from a t(15;17)", "C": "Four of four nuclei, supporting a normal result with no PML::RARA fusion", "D": "Two of four nuclei, supporting trisomy 15 from extra red signals"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Three of four nuclei, supporting a PML::RARA fusion from a t(15;17)): three nuclei show one red, one green and two fusion signals, the dual-fusion pattern of t(15;17), while one nucleus shows the normal two red and two green signals. The other counts do not match the picture, and extra red signals would be needed for a trisomy 15.", "image_type": "fish_nuclei", "image_params": {"nuclei": [{"red": 1, "green": 1, "fusion": 2}, {"red": 2, "green": 2, "fusion": 0}, {"red": 1, "green": 1, "fusion": 2}, {"red": 1, "green": 1, "fusion": 2}], "legend": {"red": "PML probe", "green": "RARA probe", "fusion": "Fusion signal"}, "caption": "Interphase FISH, PML and RARA probes"}, "image": "question_images/q_1605.png"}, {"id": 1606, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A colony from a blood agar plate shows gram-positive cocci in chains on the smear. The technologist adds hydrogen peroxide to a portion of the colony and sees no bubbling. Which genus is most consistent with this catalase result and cell arrangement?", "options": {"A": "Staphylococcus, because all members of the genus are catalase positive and grow in chains", "B": "Streptococcus, because all members of the genus are catalase negative and grow in chains", "C": "Listeria, because all members of the genus are catalase negative and grow in clusters", "D": "Bacillus, because all members of the genus are catalase negative and grow in chains"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Streptococcus, because all members of the genus are catalase negative and grow in chains): Streptococcus is catalase negative and forms pairs and chains, while Staphylococcus is catalase positive and forms clusters. Bacillus and Listeria are gram-positive rods and are catalase positive, so neither fits a catalase-negative coccus."}, {"id": 1607, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A prenatal clinic reviews antibodies that can cause serious hemolytic disease apart from anti-D. Anti-D is the classic and most severe cause, but other specificities can also injure the fetus. Which of the following antibodies is a recognized cause of serious disease?", "options": {"A": "Anti-M active only at room temperature, which is a weak IgM.", "B": "Anti-I, which is directed against a common cold-reactive antigen.", "C": "Anti-c, which is directed against an antigen in the Rh system.", "D": "Anti-Lea, which is directed against an antigen of the Lewis system."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Anti-c, which is directed against an antigen in the Rh system.): Anti-c, along with anti-E, anti-Kell and antibodies in the Duffy, Kidd and MNS systems, can cause serious HDFN because they are IgG and the antigens are well developed on fetal cells. Lewis and I antibodies are usually IgM and do not cross the placenta."}, {"id": 1608, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "Many colourless colonies grow on the sorbitol MacConkey plate beside a few pink colonies, after overnight incubation of the stool specimen from the child in the case. The technologist is deciding which colonies to work up first. What do the colourless colonies indicate about the organism being isolated from this stool specimen?", "options": {"A": "It does not ferment sorbitol, which is typical of Escherichia coli O157:H7", "B": "It ferments lactose but not sorbitol, a feature that identifies Shigella species", "C": "It produces hydrogen sulfide, which bleaches the neutral red indicator in the medium", "D": "It is inhibited by bile salts, leaving damaged cells that cannot take up the dye"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It does not ferment sorbitol, which is typical of Escherichia coli O157:H7): Escherichia coli O157:H7 characteristically does not ferment sorbitol within 24 hours, giving colourless colonies, whereas most other E. coli ferment it and appear pink. Shigella does not ferment lactose, hydrogen sulfide does not bleach the neutral red indicator, and bile salt inhibition would not produce a heavy growth of colourless colonies.", "cluster_id": "4", "cluster_text": "A 5-year-old girl who visited a petting zoo is brought to an emergency department in Alberta with bloody diarrhea and abdominal cramps, without fever. Her stool is plated on MacConkey agar containing sorbitol, blood agar and a selective enteric medium. After overnight incubation, many colourless colonies grow on the sorbitol MacConkey plate beside a few pink colonies. A colourless colony is indole positive and agglutinates with O157 antiserum. The laboratory also tests the stool broth with a Shiga toxin immunoassay, which is positive. Her platelet count falls on the second day.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1609, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The isolate agglutinates with O157 antiserum and the Shiga toxin assay is positive for this child with bloody diarrhea after a petting zoo visit. In a Canadian laboratory, which action is the most appropriate next step for reporting and follow-up of this result?", "options": {"A": "Notify public health and send the isolate to the provincial laboratory for typing", "B": "Report as normal enteric flora because Escherichia coli is a common bowel inhabitant", "C": "Release a preliminary report to the family physician and discard the isolate after reporting", "D": "Repeat the stool culture on a fresh specimen before any report leaves the laboratory"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Notify public health and send the isolate to the provincial laboratory for typing): Verotoxigenic Escherichia coli infection is nationally notifiable in Canada, so the laboratory notifies public health and forwards the isolate to the provincial public health laboratory for typing and outbreak linkage. Reporting normal flora is wrong, discarding the isolate prevents typing, and delaying the report for a repeat culture postpones notification.", "cluster_id": "4", "cluster_text": "A 5-year-old girl who visited a petting zoo is brought to an emergency department in Alberta with bloody diarrhea and abdominal cramps, without fever. Her stool is plated on MacConkey agar containing sorbitol, blood agar and a selective enteric medium. After overnight incubation, many colourless colonies grow on the sorbitol MacConkey plate beside a few pink colonies. A colourless colony is indole positive and agglutinates with O157 antiserum. The laboratory also tests the stool broth with a Shiga toxin immunoassay, which is positive. Her platelet count falls on the second day.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1610, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The laboratory runs a Shiga toxin immunoassay on stool broth for every bloody diarrhea specimen, even when sorbitol MacConkey agar is used for screening, as in the case of the child with a petting zoo exposure. The supervisor asks why the toxin test is added. What is the main reason for adding it to culture?", "options": {"A": "Sorbitol MacConkey detects toxin directly, so the immunoassay is a duplicate for quality control", "B": "The immunoassay identifies the serotype, which the plate cannot separate from commensal Escherichia coli", "C": "Non-O157 toxin producers ferment sorbitol and look like normal flora on the screening plate", "D": "The assay detects Salmonella and Shigella, which grow poorly on sorbitol MacConkey agar"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Non-O157 toxin producers ferment sorbitol and look like normal flora on the screening plate): Non-O157 Shiga toxin-producing strains ferment sorbitol, so they grow as pink colonies like commensal Escherichia coli and can be missed on sorbitol MacConkey agar, while a toxin assay on broth detects them. The plate does not detect toxin, the immunoassay does not determine serotype, and it does not detect Salmonella or Shigella.", "cluster_id": "4", "cluster_text": "A 5-year-old girl who visited a petting zoo is brought to an emergency department in Alberta with bloody diarrhea and abdominal cramps, without fever. Her stool is plated on MacConkey agar containing sorbitol, blood agar and a selective enteric medium. After overnight incubation, many colourless colonies grow on the sorbitol MacConkey plate beside a few pink colonies. A colourless colony is indole positive and agglutinates with O157 antiserum. The laboratory also tests the stool broth with a Shiga toxin immunoassay, which is positive. Her platelet count falls on the second day.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1611, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A patient with group A blood who received several units of group O red cells during surgery now shows a forward group in which some cells agglutinate with anti-A and many others remain free. The reverse group and identification are otherwise consistent. How is this appearance described and explained?", "options": {"A": "Mixed field, caused by two populations after transfusion of non-identical group blood", "B": "Acquired B, caused by bacterial deacetylase enzymes modifying the A antigen on the patient's cells", "C": "Rouleaux, caused by high globulin levels after the surgical procedure", "D": "Polyagglutination, caused by exposure of a hidden antigen on all the cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mixed field, caused by two populations after transfusion of non-identical group blood): Agglutinated cells beside free cells indicate two cell populations, seen after transfusion of non-identical group blood or after marrow transplant. Rouleaux shows stacked cells that disperse in saline. Acquired B and polyagglutination produce reactions on the patient's own cells, not two distinct populations."}, {"id": 1612, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist is asked how often quality control must be run on the coagulation analyzer and at what levels. The lab follows accepted practice for routine testing. Which schedule meets the chapter's recommendation? Consider routine testing days, reagent changes and calibration events.", "options": {"A": "One normal level weekly, and after every patient result outside the normal range", "B": "Two levels, normal and abnormal, each testing day and after lot changes or maintenance", "C": "Two levels, both abnormal, monthly and after any analyzer error message", "D": "One abnormal level daily, and after every reagent bottle is opened on the analyzer"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Two levels, normal and abnormal, each testing day and after lot changes or maintenance): At least two levels of control, one normal and one abnormal, should be run daily and after lot changes, maintenance or calibration. Weekly or monthly schedules and single levels do not meet the standard."}, {"id": 1613, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "The director reviews a case in which a senior technologist, reading the marrow slides at the bench, found an abnormal karyotype. The laboratory policy requires an additional review step before release of abnormal findings. Which practice is correct for this report?", "options": {"A": "The report is released at once and reviewed only if the clinician questions it.", "B": "A second qualified reviewer, such as a senior technologist or director, signs off before release.", "C": "The same technologist who analyzed the case signs off to confirm the reading.", "D": "A billing clerk checks the identifiers and releases the report on the same day."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A second qualified reviewer, such as a senior technologist or director, signs off before release.): Abnormal findings are checked by a second qualified reviewer, which catches reading and labelling errors that the original analyst might miss. Self-review offers little independence, and releasing first and reviewing later exposes the patient to errors. A billing clerk is not qualified to review cytogenetic results."}, {"id": 1614, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Cells from a conventional smear of a 30-year-old woman with cervicitis and a negative history of abnormal screening are shown in the image at equal magnification. Cell 2 has a nucleus larger than the normal mature cell beside it, with a smooth outline and fine chromatin. Which Bethesda interpretation best fits Cell 2?", "options": {"A": "Atypical squamous cells, cannot exclude a high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion at this site", "B": "Negative for intraepithelial lesion, with reactive cellular changes associated with inflammation", "C": "Low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion, with koilocytotic change in the superficial cell layer", "D": "Atypical squamous cells of undetermined significance, with a recommendation for HPV testing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Negative for intraepithelial lesion, with reactive cellular changes associated with inflammation): Cell 2 has a mildly enlarged nucleus with fine chromatin, a smooth outline and nucleoli, the pattern of reactive change in inflammation, so the slide is negative for intraepithelial lesion. The cell lacks coarse chromatin, an irregular outline or a high N:C ratio, which would be needed for LSIL, ASC-US or an atypical category that cannot exclude high-grade change.", "image_type": "cell_micrograph", "image_params": {"cells": [{"label": "1", "nc_ratio": 0.05, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "squamous"}, {"label": "2", "nc_ratio": 0.15, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 2, "cell": "squamous"}], "caption": "Cells at equal magnification", "context": "cervical", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1614.png"}, {"id": 1615, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A clinician asks how to recognize a platelet or von Willebrand problem based on symptoms alone, before any testing is done. Which bleeding pattern most often suggests a platelet or von Willebrand factor problem instead of a coagulation factor deficiency?", "options": {"A": "Delayed bleeding several hours after injury or surgery despite an initially stable clot", "B": "Deep bleeding into joints and muscles after even minor tissue injury or exertion", "C": "Mucosal and skin bleeding such as nosebleeds, easy bruising and heavy periods", "D": "Bleeding limited to the intestinal lining after a large meal or fasting period"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mucosal and skin bleeding such as nosebleeds, easy bruising and heavy periods): Platelet and VWF problems typically cause mucosal and skin bleeding. Deep joint and muscle bleeding and delayed bleeding point toward coagulation factor deficiencies, and gastrointestinal-only bleeding after a meal is not a recognized pattern."}, {"id": 1616, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist finds endocervical cell groups with crowded overlapping nuclei, feathered edges, mitoses and apoptotic bodies, but no diathesis. The trainee suggests invasive adenocarcinoma. Which reasoning supports the correct interpretation and links it to the virus involved? The patient is thirty-eight years old.", "options": {"A": "Absence of diathesis favours adenocarcinoma in situ, which is closely linked to HPV type 18", "B": "Crowded nuclei favour reactive change, which is unrelated to any HPV type", "C": "Feathered edges favour Actinomyces, which is closely linked to type 11", "D": "Presence of mitoses excludes any precancer, so invasive disease must already be present"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Absence of diathesis favours adenocarcinoma in situ, which is closely linked to HPV type 18): Crowded feathered groups with mitoses and apoptotic bodies but no diathesis fit adenocarcinoma in situ, which is nearly always HPV-associated, especially type 18. Mitoses are common in the in situ lesion. Actinomyces is filamentous and unrelated to feathering."}, {"id": 1617, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A group AB patient who is actively bleeding needs frozen plasma to replace multiple clotting factors. The technologist is asked about the compatibility requirement that applies to plasma components in this situation. Which statement about plasma compatibility with the recipient is correct according to the chapter?", "options": {"A": "Frozen plasma must be ABO compatible with the recipient", "B": "Frozen plasma does not need any compatibility check with the recipient", "C": "Frozen plasma must come from a relative of the recipient to be safe", "D": "Frozen plasma must share the same Rh type and Kidd antigens as the recipient"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Frozen plasma must be ABO compatible with the recipient): Plasma must be ABO compatible with the recipient, because donor ABO antibodies in the plasma could otherwise attack the recipient's red cells. Skipping the check is unsafe, while Rh and Kidd matching are not required for plasma. Blood relatives are actually a reason for irradiation of cellular components."}, {"id": 1618, "domain": "histology", "question": "A physician's office asks to borrow a paraffin block and several slides from a lymphoma case for a second opinion at another institution. The laboratory approves the request after confirming the patient's authorization. Which procedure should be followed when lending the material?", "options": {"A": "Record the loan in writing, track the return, and refile materials in numerical order.", "B": "Send the block by regular mail with no record, since a copy of the slides remains.", "C": "Hand over the block informally, since the patient's own physician is requesting it.", "D": "Give the block to the physician's office and remove its entry from the tracking system"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Record the loan in writing, track the return, and refile materials in numerical order.): A written loan record with the recipient, date and expected return lets the laboratory find the material and confirm it came back, in keeping with retention rules. Informal hand-overs leave no evidence of who holds the block. Blocks are legal records that a laboratory keeps, so removing an entry breaks the audit trail."}, {"id": 1619, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A level 2 Levey-Jennings control chart is shown for a chemistry analyzer, with the points completing a Westgard rule marked in red on day 12. What does this rule violation suggest about the analytical process, and what should the technologist consider first before patient testing continues?", "options": {"A": "Random error, so repeat the control once and continue reporting if the repeat passes", "B": "Random error, so mix the sample more thoroughly and look for a short sample or bubble", "C": "Acceptable variation, so no action is needed because every point is within 2 SD", "D": "Systematic error, so review calibration status and recent reagent lot changes before more testing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Systematic error, so review calibration status and recent reagent lot changes before more testing): Four consecutive points beyond 1 SD on the same side form the 4-1s rule, which signals systematic error such as calibration drift or a reagent change. Random error causes would give scattered points, and a rule violation cannot be dismissed because the points sit inside 2 SD.", "image_type": "qc_chart", "image_params": {"points": [0.2, -0.4, 0.5, 0.1, -0.3, 0.6, -0.2, 0.4, 0.0, -0.5, 0.3, 0.1, -0.1, 0.2, -0.3], "violation_day": 12, "violation_type": "4_1s", "caption": "Level 2 control"}, "image": "question_images/q_1619.png"}, {"id": 1620, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with a potassium result of 6.2 mmol/L has a history of clenching a fist during the venipuncture, and the tourniquet was left on for several minutes. What action is most appropriate before reporting a high potassium? The physician is waiting on this result to decide whether to treat.", "options": {"A": "Report the value immediately as a true hyperkalemia because clenching cannot change potassium", "B": "Add sodium to the sample to correct for the extra potassium released from the muscle", "C": "Suspect pseudohyperkalemia from collection technique and recollect with less tourniquet time", "D": "Freeze the sample overnight so that the potassium is redistributed evenly in plasma"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Suspect pseudohyperkalemia from collection technique and recollect with less tourniquet time): Fist clenching and prolonged tourniquet use push potassium out of muscle cells and cause pseudohyperkalemia. The specimen should be recollected with proper technique before a critical value is called, unless the clinical picture indicates urgency. Adding sodium or freezing does not correct a pre-analytical error."}, {"id": 1621, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A screener finds refractile, polygonal structures on a slide that are birefringent and look like nothing in the patient's cells. The technologist recalls that gloves are often powdered. The slides were prepared by a careful technologist, and the shapes are clearly foreign to any human tissue. Which contaminant is most likely responsible?", "options": {"A": "Air-drying artefact caused by delayed fixation of the original smear", "B": "Tumour cells transferred from a positive slide in a shared staining dish", "C": "Bubbles trapped in the mounting medium during the coverslipping step", "D": "Starch granules transferred from powdered gloves during handling of the slides"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Starch granules transferred from powdered gloves during handling of the slides): Starch granules from gloves are a recognized contaminant that can be mistaken for cell structures or organisms. Floaters would be intact cells, and drying artefact or bubbles look different."}, {"id": 1622, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist is preparing air-dried FNA smears from a suspected lymphoma and stains one quickly for the procedure room. She is asked which stain type is used on air-dried smears and what it displays particularly well. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "Hematoxylin and eosin, which is applied to smears after they are air-dried.", "B": "A Romanowsky stain, which colours cytoplasm, mucin and stroma brightly.", "C": "The Gram stain, which shows cytoplasm and stroma more brightly on dried smears.", "D": "The Papanicolaou stain, which shows fine chromatin and nucleoli on dried cells."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A Romanowsky stain, which colours cytoplasm, mucin and stroma brightly.): Air-dried smears are stained with a Romanowsky stain such as Diff-Quik, which shows cytoplasm, mucin, colloid and stroma in bright colours. The Pap stain belongs to alcohol-fixed smears. Hematoxylin and eosin is used on cell block sections, and a Gram stain detects bacteria rather than displaying cell components."}, {"id": 1623, "domain": "histology", "question": "A pathologist requests electron microscopy on a kidney biopsy from a patient with protein in the urine. A kidney biopsy is being prepared for electron microscopy. The technologist has a piece of tissue about the size of a small pea. What should be done, and why?", "options": {"A": "Trim to 1 mm pieces and fix within minutes, since penetration is slow", "B": "Leave the tissue whole and fix it slowly overnight for better preservation", "C": "Cut it into 5 mm cubes and freeze them in isopentane", "D": "Place the tissue in formalin for a week before trimming"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Trim to 1 mm pieces and fix within minutes, since penetration is slow): Electron microscopy needs pieces about 1 mm in size because fixatives penetrate slowly, and fixation must occur within minutes to avoid degradation. Large pieces fix poorly, and freezing or long formalin delays are wrong for this method."}, {"id": 1624, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A mother with anti-D has a titre that reaches the laboratory's critical value during pregnancy. The obstetric team wants a noninvasive way to check whether the fetus is becoming significantly anemic. Which measurement is used next? Her last titre was drawn two weeks ago and the fetus is 26 weeks.", "options": {"A": "Direct antiglobulin test on the maternal red cells.", "B": "Doppler measurement of fetal middle cerebral artery peak velocity.", "C": "Repeat titration using a different method to confirm the value.", "D": "Kleihauer-Betke test to count fetal cells in the maternal circulation."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Doppler measurement of fetal middle cerebral artery peak velocity.): When the titre reaches the critical value, the middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity is measured by Doppler, since a high velocity indicates low fetal hemoglobin. Repeating the titre or testing maternal cells does not assess the fetus directly."}, {"id": 1625, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In a busy cytology laboratory during the morning batch, A cytotechnologist briefly looks over each slide in a batch to sort clearly normal slides from suspicious ones before full screening begins. Which term correctly names this practice within the cytology laboratory workflow?", "options": {"A": "Rapid review, a quick look that sorts normal from suspicious slides", "B": "Genotyping, separating HPV types 16 and 18 from other high-risk types", "C": "Reflex cytology, cytology done afterward on samples that test HPV positive", "D": "Cytohistologic correlation, comparing the cytology result with the later biopsy result"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rapid review, a quick look that sorts normal from suspicious slides): A rapid review or rapid prescreen is a quick look to sort clearly normal slides from suspicious ones. Correlation compares cytology with tissue results, reflex cytology follows a positive HPV test, and genotyping separates HPV types. Each is a different quality or testing step."}, {"id": 1626, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A serum reacts strongly at immediate spin and at room temperature with P1-positive cells but does not react at 37 degrees Celsius or by IAT. The autocontrol is negative and the patient has no transfusion history. The technologist needs the most likely specificity and a sensible comment. Which is best?", "options": {"A": "Anti-Jkb, since Kidd antibodies bind complement and react best at room temperature", "B": "Anti-D, an Rh antibody that needs D-negative units even though it reacts only in the cold", "C": "Anti-P1, a cold antibody that is usually insignificant, though its thermal range should still be confirmed", "D": "Anti-K, since Kell antibodies typically react at immediate spin and are clinically significant"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Anti-P1, a cold antibody that is usually insignificant, though its thermal range should still be confirmed): Anti-P1 usually reacts in the cold and is not clinically significant, but the technologist confirms the temperature range by testing before deciding no action is needed. Rh and Kell antibodies are IgG and react at 37 degrees Celsius or by IAT, and Kidd antibodies are also IgG. None of them fits a cold-only pattern."}, {"id": 1627, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A peritoneal fluid from a woman shows atypical epithelial cells, and no clinical history is provided. The technologist wants to suggest the most likely origins to the pathologist. Based on common patterns, which primary sources are most likely for a malignant peritoneal effusion in a woman?", "options": {"A": "Cutaneous squamous carcinoma first, then the central nervous system", "B": "Small cell lung carcinoma first, then primary brain tumours", "C": "Prostate and bladder cancers first, then the thyroid and other endocrine glands", "D": "Ovarian and other gynecologic cancers first, then the gastrointestinal tract"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ovarian and other gynecologic cancers first, then the gastrointestinal tract): Peritoneal fluid in women most often comes from ovarian and other gynecologic cancers, with the gastrointestinal tract next. Prostate cancer cannot occur in women. Skin, brain and small cell lung tumours are far less frequent sources of peritoneal effusion."}, {"id": 1628, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A constitutional study is requested on a child in whom the clinician suspects a low-level mosaic cell line, meaning a mixture of normal and abnormal cells. The analyst plans how many metaphases to count and analyse from the blood culture. Which approach is correct?", "options": {"A": "Analyse more cells than for a simple constitutional study to detect a small abnormal line", "B": "Analyse the same number as usual, since the clinical indication does not change the cell count", "C": "Analyse fewer cells than usual, since mosaicism becomes obvious in the first few metaphases", "D": "Analyse only the cells with the best bands, since poor cells carry the abnormal line"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Analyse more cells than for a simple constitutional study to detect a small abnormal line): Detecting low-level mosaicism requires counting and analysing more cells because a small abnormal line can be missed. The number of cells depends on the indication, and selecting only the best cells could bias the result."}, {"id": 1629, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The confirmatory disk test shown was set up on a Klebsiella pneumoniae urine isolate that screened resistant to third-generation cephalosporins. How should the technologist interpret the zone diameters in the table for the report, including the effect of clavulanate on each cephalosporin?", "options": {"A": "ESBL producer, since ceftazidime and cefotaxime zones grew at least five millimetres", "B": "Not an ESBL producer, since clavulanate needs to raise zones by at least ten millimetres", "C": "Carbapenemase producer, since the zones remained in the resistant range for both cephalosporins", "D": "Inconclusive result, since cefoxitin showed no change and the test needs repeating"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (ESBL producer, since ceftazidime and cefotaxime zones grew at least five millimetres): The table shows ceftazidime and cefotaxime zones that grew by 8 and 9 mm with clavulanate, and an increase of 5 mm or more confirms an extended-spectrum beta-lactamase. The unchanged cefoxitin zone is expected because ESBLs do not hydrolyze cephamycins. The required rise is 5 mm, not 10 mm, and nothing in the table suggests a carbapenemase.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "ESBL confirmatory disk test, K. pneumoniae", "columns": ["Disk", "Zone alone (mm)", "Zone with clavulanate (mm)"], "rows": [["Ceftazidime", "15", "23"], ["Cefotaxime", "18", "27"], ["Cefoxitin", "22", "22"]], "caption": "Confirmatory disk test"}, "image": "question_images/q_1629.png"}, {"id": 1630, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "Before collecting a blood sample, a phlebotomist checks the patient's wristband against the requisition and asks the patient to state their name. What is the purpose of confirming two identifiers at collection and again on receipt? The nurse is aware that mislabelled specimens are a leading cause of reporting errors and wants to follow procedure at every step.", "options": {"A": "To confirm the patient has consented to the treatment plan", "B": "To speed the analytic phase by skipping the later verification steps", "C": "To reduce the required volume of blood in each bottle", "D": "To prevent mislabelling and ensure the result belongs to the right patient"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (To prevent mislabelling and ensure the result belongs to the right patient): Two identifiers checked at collection and receipt protect against mislabelled specimens, the foundation of safe reporting, and any mismatch is resolved before testing. The check does not shorten verification, alter volume needs, or document consent to treatment."}, {"id": 1631, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient has high serum iron, high transferrin saturation and a high ferritin, with no signs of inflammation. Which condition does this pattern most strongly suggest? The physician is comparing this pattern with iron deficiency and anemia of chronic disease. Choose the most likely condition.", "options": {"A": "Iron deficiency with a raised production of transferrin by the liver and depleted body stores", "B": "Anemia of chronic disease with reduced release of iron from stores", "C": "Hemochromatosis or another form of iron overload without any inflammatory disease", "D": "Acute blood loss with a recovering bone marrow response to anemia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hemochromatosis or another form of iron overload without any inflammatory disease): Iron, saturation and ferritin are all high in hemochromatosis or iron overload. Iron deficiency shows the reverse, and anemia of chronic disease shows low iron with normal or high ferritin. Ferritin also rises with inflammation, so the clinical picture matters."}, {"id": 1632, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A sputum slide contains birefringent, Maltese-cross-like particles that appear bright under polarized light and do not match any cell type. The specimen was collected by a technologist wearing powdered gloves. Which explanation is most likely for these particles, and how should the finding be handled in the report?", "options": {"A": "Charcot-Leyden crystals from eosinophils, a finding that indicates an allergic process", "B": "Psammoma bodies from serous tumours, a finding that indicates ovarian cancer", "C": "Starch from powdered gloves, a contaminant that should not be mistaken for disease", "D": "Ferruginous bodies from asbestos, a lung finding that indicates malignancy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Starch from powdered gloves, a contaminant that should not be mistaken for disease): Starch granules from powdered gloves are birefringent and show Maltese-cross shapes under polarized light, and they are a contaminant. Ferruginous bodies are golden-brown beaded rods, Charcot-Leyden crystals are pointed needles, and psammoma bodies are layered calcified spheres, so those do not fit the description."}, {"id": 1633, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A urine sample from a patient in a workplace program screens positive for opiates, and the technologist notes that the sample temperature was 25 degrees Celsius when received, with a very low creatinine. What is the most appropriate response? The chain of custody form is complete and signed.", "options": {"A": "Discard the sample because temperature and creatinine are not relevant to drug screening", "B": "Suspect tampering or dilution, document findings and report following chain of custody", "C": "Report the sample as a strong confirmed positive because the low creatinine proves recent drug use", "D": "Release the result without comment because tampering can only be detected by mass spectrometry"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suspect tampering or dilution, document findings and report following chain of custody): When results may be used legally, chain of custody is required and the sample should be checked for tampering by temperature, creatinine and pH. A cool sample with very low creatinine suggests dilution or substitution. These findings should be documented and reported per procedure."}, {"id": 1634, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A patient with an unexpected drop in hemoglobin after a transfusion two days ago has the monospecific gel DAT shown in the image, run on a freshly drawn EDTA sample. The control well is included, and the clinician suspects a delayed serologic reaction. Which explanation best fits the reaction in the anti-IgG well?", "options": {"A": "Autoantibody coating each patient red cell evenly, with no complement bound to the cells", "B": "A mixed population of IgG-coated donor cells and uncoated patient cells", "C": "Reagent contamination from a damaged antiglobulin vial, giving a spurious reaction in the anti-IgG well", "D": "Passive IgG from donor plasma coating the patient cells uniformly without any hemolytic significance"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A mixed population of IgG-coated donor cells and uncoated patient cells): A mixed-field reaction means two cell populations are present, here transfused donor cells coated with IgG among uncoated patient cells. Uniform coating would give a smooth, even reaction, a negative control argues against a reagent problem, and passive plasma IgG does not selectively coat a subset of cells.", "image_type": "gel_agglutination", "image_params": {"wells": [{"label": "Anti-IgG", "reaction": "mixed_field"}, {"label": "Anti-C3d", "reaction": "negative"}, {"label": "Control", "reaction": "negative"}], "caption": "Gel DAT with monospecific reagents"}, "image": "question_images/q_1634.png"}, {"id": 1635, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A 40-year-old woman has a sudden fall in hemoglobin, jaundice and a high reticulocyte count. Her blood film is shown in the image. The physician wants to know whether the cell change is immune or hereditary in origin. Which test best separates an immune cause from a hereditary cause of these cells?", "options": {"A": "Osmotic fragility test to measure the membrane response to saline solutions", "B": "Hemoglobin electrophoresis to detect the presence of an abnormal hemoglobin variant", "C": "Prussian blue stain to detect iron deposits in the red cell cytoplasm", "D": "Direct antiglobulin test to detect antibody or complement on red cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Direct antiglobulin test to detect antibody or complement on red cells): Dense round cells without central pallor are spherocytes, produced in both immune hemolysis and hereditary spherocytosis. A positive direct antiglobulin test identifies antibody or complement on the cells and points to immune hemolysis. Fragility, electrophoresis and iron stains do not distinguish the two.", "image_type": "micrograph", "image_params": {"prompt": "Realistic Wright-Giemsa stained peripheral blood smear photomicrograph, 1000x oil immersion, many small dense round red cells that lack central pallor (spherocytes), some polychromatic larger blue-tinged red cells (reticulocytes), a few platelets, no schistocytes.", "caption": "Peripheral blood film", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1635.png"}, {"id": 1636, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A macrodissected biopsy is estimated by the pathologist at five percent tumour cells. Sequencing at 800x depth reports a KRAS variant at a fraction of three percent, and the laboratory's validated limit of detection is five percent VAF. How should the technologist handle this?", "options": {"A": "Flag it as below the limit of detection, so the result needs cautious reporting and confirmation", "B": "Report it as a confirmed hotspot variant, since the read depth is high", "C": "Report it as a confirmed KRAS positive result, since 800x depth is sufficient at this level", "D": "Change the limit of detection for this sample to make it reportable"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Flag it as below the limit of detection, so the result needs cautious reporting and confirmation): The validated limit of detection sets what the assay can call with confidence, and a variant below it needs cautious reporting with limitations, ideally confirmation on a richer specimen. High depth does not change the validated limit. Calling it noise without review may miss a real low level variant. Limits cannot be changed per sample."}, {"id": 1637, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist is preparing grids at the end of a long electron microscopy processing run in the laboratory. Ultra-thin sections for electron microscopy are cut on an ultramicrotome and mounted on grids. Which substances are typically used to stain these sections?", "options": {"A": "Fluorescein and rhodamine in buffered glycerol used for dark-room reading", "B": "Hematoxylin and eosin used at room temperature for a few minutes", "C": "Silver nitrate and toluidine blue used in acid solution for contrast", "D": "Heavy metals such as uranyl acetate and lead citrate for contrast"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Heavy metals such as uranyl acetate and lead citrate for contrast): Ultra-thin sections are stained with heavy metals such as uranyl acetate and lead citrate to give contrast in the electron beam. Hematoxylin and eosin, fluorescent dyes, and silver or toluidine blue are used for other methods."}, {"id": 1638, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A student is told that a cell with 69 chromosomes is euploid, and is confused because it does not have the normal count of 46. The instructor asks the class to define the terms euploid and aneuploid using the haploid number of 23 chromosomes. Which definition is correct?", "options": {"A": "Euploid means exactly 46 chromosomes, while aneuploid means any other count of chromosomes.", "B": "Euploid means a balanced structural change, while aneuploid means an unbalanced structural change.", "C": "Euploid means an exact multiple of 23, while aneuploid means any other number.", "D": "Euploid means a multiple of 22, while aneuploid means a sex chromosome change only."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Euploid means an exact multiple of 23, while aneuploid means any other number.): A euploid cell has a whole multiple of the haploid set, so 23, 46, 69 and 92 are all euploid, and counts such as 45 or 47 are aneuploid. Restricting euploidy to 46 would wrongly call triploidy aneuploid. Balanced and unbalanced describe structure, not number."}, {"id": 1639, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A physician asks the patient to collect sputum for cytology at home. The best specimen for detecting lung cancer follows specific instructions on timing and number of samples, and the physician wants adequacy to be as high as possible. Which instructions give the best sputum collection for cytology?", "options": {"A": "A single saliva sample collected after brushing the teeth in the evening", "B": "A sample taken immediately after a large meal on a single day of the week", "C": "A pooled sample from one week of daily coughing kept at room temperature", "D": "A deep-cough sample from early morning, collected on three separate days"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A deep-cough sample from early morning, collected on three separate days): The best sputum is a deep-cough early-morning sample collected on three separate days. Saliva contains squamous cells and bacteria rather than lower airway cells. A week of pooled sputum degenerates, and a post-meal sample increases food contamination."}, {"id": 1640, "domain": "histology", "question": "A pathologist asks for the best possible nuclear detail on a renal biopsy that will also receive immunohistochemistry. The technologist must set the microtome thickness. Which choice is most appropriate for this request on a paraffin block? The pathologist has requested clear cellular detail for the report.", "options": {"A": "A thick section chosen to overlap cells for depth", "B": "A section of variable thickness, adjusted while cutting", "C": "A thick section of about 10 micrometres for stronger colour", "D": "A thin section at the low end of the routine range"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A thin section at the low end of the routine range): Thinner sections give better nuclear detail and are preferred for kidney and immunohistochemistry. Thick sections look dark and blurry with overlapping cells, and variable thickness gives inconsistent staining and unreliable interpretation."}, {"id": 1641, "domain": "hematology", "question": "An EDTA tube collected at 06:00 and left at room temperature reaches the bench at 12:00 with an ESR request. The CBC was already run on it earlier. The technologist considers setting up the Westergren tube from this sample. What is the correct decision?", "options": {"A": "Request a fresh specimen, since it is past the four-hour limit", "B": "Run it, because the ESR stays stable for 24 hours like the CBC", "C": "Run it and add 20 percent to the result to allow for the delay", "D": "Warm the sample to 37 degrees Celsius and set up the tube"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Request a fresh specimen, since it is past the four-hour limit): The ESR must be set up within four hours of collection at room temperature, or the sample refrigerated for longer storage. At six hours this sample is too old, so a fresh one is requested. CBC parameters are stable up to 24 hours, but the ESR is not. An added percentage is invented, and warming does not reverse the change."}, {"id": 1642, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A collector cleans the antecubital area with 70% isopropyl alcohol, then immediately palpates the vein and inserts the needle while the skin is still wet. The clinic's hemolysis rate has recently risen. Which practice change would best address this cause of in vitro hemolysis?", "options": {"A": "Wipe the site with a dry cloth to speed drying and then puncture", "B": "Let the alcohol air dry before puncture so it does not enter the tube", "C": "Pull the plunger back quickly on the syringe to fill the tube sooner", "D": "Use a needle larger than 18 gauge so blood enters the tube faster"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Let the alcohol air dry before puncture so it does not enter the tube): Alcohol that remains on the skin can be carried into the tube and damage red cells, and it also stings the patient. Allowing it to air dry prevents both problems. A very large needle or a forceful pull creates turbulence and shear, which increases hemolysis, so these changes would make the problem worse rather than better."}, {"id": 1643, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A sequencing run shows that the percentage of bases at Q30 or higher, cluster density, and mean depth are all within limits. The contamination estimate for one sample is well above the accepted threshold, and no variants in the target genes are called for it. What should the laboratory do?", "options": {"A": "Report it as negative, since three of the four metrics passed for the run", "B": "Report it as negative, because the Q30, density and depth values show a good run", "C": "Not report the sample as negative; repeat it or report it as inadequate", "D": "Report the variants from the neighbouring sample to fill in the missing result"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Not report the sample as negative; repeat it or report it as inadequate): A sample that fails a metric is not reported as negative; it is repeated, or reported as inadequate with the reason. Passing the other metrics does not cancel the contamination failure. Contamination compromises confidence in identity and calls, and borrowing another patient's data would be a serious error."}, {"id": 1644, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A heparinized whole blood specimen was tested by multiplex PCR for a 400 bp target with a 150 bp internal control. The gel shown has a ladder, a positive control, and the patient lane. What is the best interpretation of the patient result?", "options": {"A": "Target not detected and the result is valid, because no 400 bp band is present", "B": "Invalid, because the internal control failed, so inhibition is suspected and cleanup follows", "C": "Target detected at low level, because faint amplification is possible below visible detection", "D": "Invalid result, because the specimen was too concentrated and needs a higher annealing temperature"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Invalid, because the internal control failed, so inhibition is suspected and cleanup follows): With no 150 bp internal control band, the reaction did not work, so a missing target band means nothing. Heparin carried into the DNA is a classic PCR inhibitor, so the extract is cleaned up and retested. A valid negative would still show the internal control, and invisible amplification cannot be reported as detected.", "image_type": "dna_gel", "image_params": {"lanes": [{"label": "Ladder", "bands": [100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600]}, {"label": "Positive", "bands": [150, 400]}, {"label": "Patient", "bands": []}], "caption": "Multiplex PCR: 150 bp control, 400 bp target"}, "image": "question_images/q_1644.png"}, {"id": 1645, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A laboratory receives a set of blood cultures from an adult with suspected sepsis. Each set is defined by the type of bottles it contains. Which pair of bottles makes up one complete adult blood culture set? The bottles were collected at the bedside by a nurse who wants to be sure that both groups of organisms can be recovered.", "options": {"A": "One aerobic bottle and one anaerobic bottle, drawn together from a single venipuncture site", "B": "Two anaerobic bottles collected from separate sites at two different times of the day", "C": "Two aerobic bottles drawn together from the same venipuncture site at the same moment", "D": "One aerobic bottle and one fungal slant tube drawn together from the same site"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (One aerobic bottle and one anaerobic bottle, drawn together from a single venipuncture site): A standard set has one aerobic and one anaerobic bottle so that both types of organisms can be recovered. Two bottles of one type, or a slant tube, would leave a group of organisms unrecoverable from that single draw."}, {"id": 1646, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A courier will deliver EDTA whole blood for DNA testing from a distant clinic, with an expected transit of three days. The laboratory procedure states a specimen stability limit for the test. Which transport condition is most appropriate, and what does it protect?", "options": {"A": "Warmed to 37 °C, since warmth preserves enzymes needed for the later extraction.", "B": "Room temperature in a sealed bag, because cold causes DNA strands to break.", "C": "Frozen and thawed twice before shipping, so cells lyse in transit and release DNA.", "D": "Refrigerated at 2-8 °C, which slows the degradation of DNA in the blood."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refrigerated at 2-8 °C, which slows the degradation of DNA in the blood.): DNA in EDTA blood is usually stable for days at 2-8 °C, and cold slows nuclease and bacterial activity. Warm storage speeds degradation, and repeated freezing and thawing adds no benefit. Cold does not break DNA strands."}, {"id": 1647, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist stores a box of FISH probes for the coming month in the department freezer area. Because the fluorescent labels can fade and the DNA can degrade, the storage instructions specify a particular condition. Which storage practice is correct for typical clinical probes?", "options": {"A": "Keep them in the dark, generally at -20 degrees Celsius", "B": "Keep them on the bench under the microscope lamp at room temperature", "C": "Keep them in a warm water bath at 37 degrees Celsius to stay active", "D": "Keep them in the refrigerator door at 4 degrees Celsius under bright light"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep them in the dark, generally at -20 degrees Celsius): Probes are light sensitive, so they are stored in the dark, generally frozen at -20 degrees Celsius. Light and warmth degrade the fluorophore and the DNA. Room temperature under lamp light would quickly damage the label."}, {"id": 1648, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "Other staff are working nearby. A blood culture bottle grows large gram-positive rods with spores. The colonies on blood agar are nonhemolytic and nonmotile, and a technologist suspects Bacillus anthracis. The plate is currently open on the open bench. What is the safest action?", "options": {"A": "Heat-fix the plate in an incubator and report the isolate as a probable Bacillus contaminant", "B": "Add a catalase reagent on the open bench and discard the plate if it is negative", "C": "Move all work into a biological safety cabinet and send the isolate to a reference laboratory", "D": "Continue work on the open bench with gloves and report the identification directly from the plate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Move all work into a biological safety cabinet and send the isolate to a reference laboratory): Bacillus anthracis is a risk group 3 pathogen under Canada's Human Pathogens and Toxins Act that must be handled in a biological safety cabinet, and suspected isolates go to a reference laboratory. Open bench work exposes staff. Bacillus species are catalase positive, so a negative catalase would not fit, and it must not be reported as a contaminant without referral."}, {"id": 1649, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A physician wants to distinguish diabetes insipidus from inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion in a patient with abnormal water balance. Which laboratory approach is described in the chapter for sorting out these disorders? Choose the laboratory approach that best answers this question.", "options": {"A": "Compare serum phosphate with urine protein measured in a random sample", "B": "Compare urine chloride with serum bicarbonate collected on different days", "C": "Compare serum cortisol with urine specific gravity obtained by a reagent strip", "D": "Compare urine osmolality with serum osmolality collected around the same time"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Compare urine osmolality with serum osmolality collected around the same time): Urine osmolality interpreted alongside serum osmolality helps separate disorders of water balance such as diabetes insipidus, where urine is dilute despite high serum osmolality, from inappropriate ADH secretion. The other pairings do not assess renal water handling."}, {"id": 1650, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist floats a stool sample in zinc sulfate solution at a specific gravity of 1.18 and examines the surface film. Later a colleague notes that some operculated eggs and several protozoan cysts on the slide look shrunken and collapsed. Which explanation fits this observation?", "options": {"A": "Flotation solutions denser than most eggs can distort operculated eggs and some cysts", "B": "Formalin fixation shrinks eggs, so the flotation solution played no part in the distortion", "C": "Trichrome staining dissolves the operculum, so the eggs look distorted after staining", "D": "Sedimentation with ethyl acetate crushes eggs, so the distortion came from centrifugation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Flotation solutions denser than most eggs can distort operculated eggs and some cysts): Flotation with dense solutions like zinc sulfate can distort operculated eggs and some cysts, which is a known limit. The other explanations do not apply to a flotation procedure."}, {"id": 1651, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A nurse suspects a transfusion reaction and stops the infusion. Before the line is disconnected from the patient, the technologist reminds the nurse what should be run through the intravenous line to keep it open. Which fluid is correct? Choose the best fluid.", "options": {"A": "Lactated Ringer's solution, since its calcium helps keep the blood from clotting", "B": "Normal saline, which keeps the vein open without harming the red cells", "C": "Sterile water for injection, since it flushes residual blood from the line best", "D": "Five percent dextrose in water, which is isotonic and safe with red cells"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Normal saline, which keeps the vein open without harming the red cells): Normal saline keeps the vein open without harming red cells. Dextrose in water can cause red cell swelling and hemolysis in the line, calcium in lactated Ringer's can clot citrated blood, and sterile water is hypotonic and would cause hemolysis."}, {"id": 1652, "domain": "histology", "question": "A community hospital sends kidney biopsies to a distant reference laboratory, so the transport time before freezing may be long. A renal biopsy portion for immunofluorescence cannot be frozen fresh at the collection site. Which option is sometimes used for transport of this portion?", "options": {"A": "Ethanol at room temperature, which is a dehydrating reagent rather than a transport medium", "B": "Xylene followed by paraffin wax, which is used only for routine processing", "C": "Glutaraldehyde in cacodylate buffer, which is the fixative used for electron microscopy", "D": "Michel's transport medium, a special medium used when fresh freezing is not possible"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Michel's transport medium, a special medium used when fresh freezing is not possible): The immunofluorescence portion is frozen fresh, or sometimes placed in a special transport medium such as Michel's medium. Glutaraldehyde is for electron microscopy, and xylene or ethanol are processing reagents that would spoil the fluorescence targets."}, {"id": 1653, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cytotechnologist screens gynaecologic slides for the full day and also spends part of the day training students and doing administrative tasks. The supervisor reviews the daily slide count against the limit. What is the appropriate way to apply the workload standard?", "options": {"A": "Remove the limit since training students improves skills and reduces errors overall", "B": "Double the limit once the person has completed several years of experience", "C": "Set the limit lower than usual because the person has other duties", "D": "Apply the standard maximum because other duties do not change screening capacity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Set the limit lower than usual because the person has other duties): Limits are set lower when slides are difficult or when the person has other duties, because fatigue and distraction raise missed abnormal cells. Experience and training do not remove the need for a limit, and accurate records of slides and hours are kept to prove compliance. Records of slides and hours provide the proof that the limit was followed."}, {"id": 1654, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "An official asks which government body regulates the blood suppliers in Canada and requires that serious adverse reactions and errors involving blood be reported. The transfusion service manager needs to name the correct regulator. Which one applies? Choose the correct regulator and its role.", "options": {"A": "Health Canada, which regulates the suppliers and requires serious adverse event reports", "B": "The provincial blood supplier, which reports only to its own hospital customers", "C": "The Public Health Agency of Canada, which runs the national surveillance system for injuries", "D": "The Canadian Standards Association, which writes the standard for blood and components"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Health Canada, which regulates the suppliers and requires serious adverse event reports): Health Canada regulates the blood suppliers and requires reporting of serious adverse reactions and errors. The Canadian Standards Association writes standards but is not the regulator, the Public Health Agency runs surveillance, and the suppliers are the regulated parties themselves."}, {"id": 1655, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A laboratory reviews its quality assurance program for non-gynecologic cytology after a missed malignancy on a negative pleural fluid. Managers want practical measures that catch such errors and improve accuracy. Which combination of activities best reflects sound quality assurance in this area?", "options": {"A": "Stop reviewing negative slides, since rescreening reduces productivity and adds delays to reporting times", "B": "Change stain baths only after visible failure, since regular filtering is unnecessary", "C": "Rely on one screener for difficult cases, since a second opinion adds risk", "D": "Correlate cytology with biopsy, rescreen some negatives, and track adequacy and positivity rates"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Correlate cytology with biopsy, rescreen some negatives, and track adequacy and positivity rates): Sound quality assurance correlates cytology with biopsy, rescreens some negatives, tracks adequacy and positivity rates, and checks staining and labelling. Skipping rescreening, changing stains only after failure, and avoiding second opinions all weaken error detection."}, {"id": 1656, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A fluid sample from a pregnancy in which the fetus had died in utero arrives for chromosome analysis, and the flasks show no colony growth after two weeks despite correct handling and a working incubator. The clinician asks whether the laboratory made an error. How should the technologist explain the failure?", "options": {"A": "The incubator was too cold, so the fetal cells could not attach to the flask", "B": "The heparin was too fresh, so the fetal cells could not divide in the culture", "C": "Old or dead fetal cells will not divide, so the failure has a biologic cause", "D": "The colcemid was too weak, so the fetal cells could not stop in metaphase"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Old or dead fetal cells will not divide, so the failure has a biologic cause): Cells from a fetus that has died are dead or non-viable and will not divide, so this failure has a biologic cause. Amniotic fluid is not collected with heparin, and colcemid or incubator faults would affect other cultures too."}, {"id": 1657, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A pathologist reviewing a thyroid aspirate lists supporting findings beyond the nuclear features: papillary fragments, dense ropy colloid, multinucleated giant cells and small round calcified structures. The nuclear features are already present in many cells. Which calcified structures support the diagnosis of papillary carcinoma?", "options": {"A": "Corpora amylacea, which are laminated bodies typical of prostatic secretions and benign hyperplasia", "B": "Psammoma bodies, which are round calcified structures that support papillary carcinoma", "C": "Schaumann bodies, which are calcified inclusions found in granulomas of sarcoidosis", "D": "Asteroid bodies, which are star-shaped inclusions found within multinucleated giant cells"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Psammoma bodies, which are round calcified structures that support papillary carcinoma): Psammoma bodies are round calcified structures that support papillary carcinoma when nuclear features are also present. Corpora amylacea occur in prostatic material. Schaumann and asteroid bodies are inclusions of granulomatous disease and are not features of thyroid papillary carcinoma."}, {"id": 1658, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A SNP array on a stillborn infant shows a balanced log ratio across all chromosomes, so the aCGH-style analysis appears normal. The genotype view, however, shows abnormal allele patterns with three different allele bands at many sites. Which conclusion is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Triploidy, which the allele pattern reveals even though the copy number ratio stays balanced", "B": "A normal result, because a balanced log ratio on every chromosome excludes a change in ploidy", "C": "A single large deletion, because allele bands are lost at every probe", "D": "A balanced translocation, because three allele bands cross each breakpoint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Triploidy, which the allele pattern reveals even though the copy number ratio stays balanced): Triploidy adds a full extra set of chromosomes, so the ratio to a normal reference stays even, but SNP genotypes show extra allele patterns. A normal ratio does not exclude ploidy change. Deletions would show loss of bands at specific regions, and balanced translocations remain invisible to arrays."}, {"id": 1659, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Some laboratories prefer EDTA plasma to serum for pretransfusion ABO and Rh testing and antibody screening. A technologist reviewing sample types notes that the plasma is collected in a lavender-top tube. What is the main advantage of EDTA plasma over serum from a clotted tube for these tests?", "options": {"A": "It stores at room temperature for a month without any change in reactivity", "B": "It contains more IgG antibody than serum, which strengthens weak reactions in the antiglobulin phase", "C": "It prevents in vitro complement activation and avoids small fibrin clots that mimic agglutination", "D": "It removes the need for reverse grouping because antibodies are neutralized"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It prevents in vitro complement activation and avoids small fibrin clots that mimic agglutination): EDTA binds calcium, which stops complement activation in the tube, and it prevents fibrin clot formation that can look like agglutination. It does not concentrate IgG, and it does not neutralize antibodies, so reverse grouping is still needed. Sample age limits remain the same, so long room temperature storage is not a benefit."}, {"id": 1660, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A hospitalized patient has a high calcium that is discovered on routine testing. According to the usual pattern of causes, which is the most common cause of hypercalcemia in hospitalized patients? The clinician asks which cause is the most likely explanation in this setting.", "options": {"A": "Low magnesium, whereas kidney failure is the most frequent cause in outpatients", "B": "Malignancy, whereas primary hyperparathyroidism is the most frequent cause in outpatients", "C": "Vitamin D deficiency, whereas hypoparathyroidism is the most frequent cause in outpatients", "D": "Hypoalbuminemia, whereas thyroid disease is the most frequent cause in outpatients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Malignancy, whereas primary hyperparathyroidism is the most frequent cause in outpatients): Hypercalcemia in outpatients is most often due to primary hyperparathyroidism, and in hospitalized patients malignancy is the leading cause. Vitamin D deficiency, hypoparathyroidism, low magnesium and kidney disease are associated with low calcium. Low albumin lowers total calcium, not raises it."}, {"id": 1661, "domain": "histology", "question": "After picking up a section on a slide, the technologist dries it on a warming plate or in an oven. Drying serves two purposes that help the section stay attached during the subsequent staining process. What does drying accomplish? The slides will be stained after drying.", "options": {"A": "It removes trapped water and slightly melts the wax", "B": "It converts hematoxylin into its active hematein form on the slide", "C": "It fixes the tissue proteins so that stains are more intense", "D": "It dissolves the mounting medium so the coverslip can be applied"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It removes trapped water and slightly melts the wax): Drying removes water under the section and softens the wax so the tissue adheres. Fixation happens earlier, hematein forms through ripening in the reagent bottle, and mounting medium is not applied until after staining."}, {"id": 1662, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient with bleeding into the knee joints has a prolonged aPTT and normal PT. The physician orders a factor level to classify the disorder. How are factor VIII and factor IX levels measured in the laboratory for this purpose?", "options": {"A": "By a latex-enhanced D-dimer assay using fibrin-deficient plasma", "B": "By a chromogenic anti-Xa assay using heparin-treated plasma", "C": "By a PT-based one-stage assay using calcium-deficient plasma", "D": "By an aPTT-based one-stage assay using factor-deficient plasma"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (By an aPTT-based one-stage assay using factor-deficient plasma): Factor VIII and IX activity are measured with an aPTT-based one-stage assay in which patient plasma corrects factor-deficient plasma. A PT-based assay would test the extrinsic pathway factors instead. Anti-Xa and D-dimer assays measure a drug effect and fibrin breakdown respectively, not intrinsic factor activity."}, {"id": 1663, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "Blood gas analyzers use separate electrodes for each measurement. Which pairing of electrode and measured parameter is correct? During a training session, a student is shown a blood gas analyzer and must match each electrode to the parameter it measures. The supervisor asks for the correct pairing.", "options": {"A": "The Severinghaus electrode measures pCO2 and the Clark electrode measures pO2", "B": "The Clark electrode measures bicarbonate and the Severinghaus electrode measures sodium", "C": "The Severinghaus electrode measures pO2 and the Clark electrode measures pCO2", "D": "The glass electrode measures pO2 and the Clark electrode measures pH"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The Severinghaus electrode measures pCO2 and the Clark electrode measures pO2): A glass electrode measures pH, a Severinghaus electrode measures pCO2 and a Clark electrode measures pO2. Bicarbonate is calculated from pH and pCO2. Daily quality control at low, normal and high levels and electrode maintenance are required."}, {"id": 1664, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A physician asks which type of wound specimen is preferred for culture of a deep abscess. The technologist reminds staff that swabs of the surface often recover organisms that are not causing the infection. Which specimen is best? The physician is worried that the infection lies deeper than the skin and wants the best possible chance of growing the cause.", "options": {"A": "A swab of the drainage that has collected on the bandage", "B": "A dry gauze dressing removed from the wound after two days", "C": "Aspirated pus or tissue taken from the deep part of the wound", "D": "A swab rubbed across the surface of the skin around the wound"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Aspirated pus or tissue taken from the deep part of the wound): Aspirated pus or tissue represents the true site of infection and avoids surface flora, and anaerobic transport is used if anaerobes are suspected. Surface swabs, dressings, and drainage contain colonizing organisms that mislead the workup."}, {"id": 1665, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A daily quality control check shows that the CO2 incubator is reading only 1 percent carbon dioxide instead of the required range for cultures of Neisseria. Patient plates are already incubating in it. What should the technologist do first? The plates were set up earlier today.", "options": {"A": "Investigate and document the failure, and hold patient results until corrective action is complete", "B": "Continue incubating the plates and release the results if colonies appear on them", "C": "Record the reading the next day and take no other action", "D": "Change the displayed setting to the correct level and release the plates for routine reading"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Investigate and document the failure, and hold patient results until corrective action is complete): A failed control must be investigated and corrected before patient results are released, and the corrective action is documented. Releasing anyway, delaying, or altering the display leaves results unreliable or falsified."}, {"id": 1666, "domain": "histology", "question": "A surgical team is planning an operation with several specimens expected soon. A surgeon requests a frozen section on a large piece of bone and a small calcified lesion. Which statement best describes how these specimens behave, and what the technologist should communicate?", "options": {"A": "Bone cuts more easily than soft tissue on a cryostat, so extra discussion is unnecessary", "B": "Any specimen can be cut on the cryostat if the section is made thicker", "C": "Calcified lesions produce the best frozen sections because they are dense and stable", "D": "Bone and calcified lesions cut poorly, so limits are discussed with the pathologist"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bone and calcified lesions cut poorly, so limits are discussed with the pathologist): Bone, fatty tissue, and small or calcified lesions do not freeze or cut well, so the limits are raised with the pathologist. Bone is not easy to cut. Density does not improve frozen sections, and thicker cutting does not overcome hardness."}, {"id": 1667, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "After replacing the lamp on a photometric analyzer, a technologist recalibrates the total protein method and the calibration passes acceptance criteria. Patient samples are waiting on the rack, and the supervisor asks what must happen before patient results are released. What is required?", "options": {"A": "Report patient results now because a passing calibration already proves accuracy", "B": "Repeat the calibration with a different calibrator lot to confirm the first", "C": "Run control materials and confirm they fall within acceptable limits", "D": "Verify the reference interval by testing twenty healthy volunteers"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Run control materials and confirm they fall within acceptable limits): After calibration, control materials are run to show the calibration is acceptable before any patient result is reported. A passing calibration alone does not test the method as patients experience it. Reference interval work and a second calibrator lot are not required after a lamp change."}, {"id": 1668, "domain": "histology", "question": "A gastroenterology pathologist wants a stain that colours only sulphated mucins and leaves carboxylated mucins unstained. Sections were fixed in neutral buffered formalin and cut at 4 µm, and the pathologist is reviewing the biopsy for goblet cell changes. Which stain condition is used to achieve this selective result?", "options": {"A": "Alcian blue used at a strongly acidic pH of 1.0 in the run", "B": "Alcian blue used at the milder acidic pH of 2.5 in the run", "C": "Mucicarmine applied at neutral pH with a hematoxylin counterstain afterward", "D": "PAS applied after diastase has digested the glycogen in the section"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Alcian blue used at a strongly acidic pH of 1.0 in the run): At pH 1.0 Alcian blue stains only sulphated mucins, while at pH 2.5 it stains both carboxylated and sulphated acidic mucins. PAS and mucicarmine do not give this selective separation."}, {"id": 1669, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "An oral glucose tolerance test is scheduled for a non-pregnant adult being evaluated for diabetes. The technologist prepares the standard glucose drink after the baseline fasting sample and checks the protocol. What glucose load is used for most diagnostic testing of this type?", "options": {"A": "75 g of glucose given after the fasting baseline sample", "B": "50 g of glucose, the familiar pregnancy screening load", "C": "25 g of glucose, a small load below diagnostic testing", "D": "100 g of glucose, the older pregnancy diagnostic load"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (75 g of glucose given after the fasting baseline sample): A 75 g load is standard for most diagnostic oral glucose tolerance testing, and pregnancy screening uses different doses. A 50 g load is a familiar pregnancy screening dose, and 100 g belongs to an older pregnancy diagnostic protocol. A 25 g load is too small to test glucose handling meaningfully."}, {"id": 1670, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A Sanger trace from a heterozygous patient shows single clean peaks up to position 150. From position 151 onward every position shows two overlapping peaks of different colours, and the overlap continues to the end of the read. What do these findings suggest, and how can the change be worked out?", "options": {"A": "A small insertion or deletion in one allele, worked out by separating the two sequences", "B": "Cross-contamination with a second patient's DNA, which would begin abruptly at a single position", "C": "A heterozygous single base change, which would explain overlapping peaks at every downstream position", "D": "Cross-contamination with a second patient's DNA, which begins abruptly at one position"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A small insertion or deletion in one allele, worked out by separating the two sequences): When mixed peaks begin at one position and continue to the end, one allele has shifted relative to the other, which suggests an insertion or deletion, and the two sequences can be separated and compared with the reference. A single base change gives a mixed peak at only one position. Contamination and poor quality would not begin so sharply with clean sequence before it."}, {"id": 1671, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "Two new tests are being added to the menu: cortisol, a small molecule with only one antibody binding site, and thyrotropin, a large protein with two distinct binding sites. The manufacturer offers both competitive and sandwich formats. Which pairing of format to analyte is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Competitive format for both because both are measured as hormones", "B": "Competitive format for cortisol and sandwich format for thyrotropin", "C": "Sandwich format for both, with two labels binding a single shared site", "D": "Sandwich format for cortisol and competitive format for thyrotropin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Competitive format for cortisol and sandwich format for thyrotropin): A molecule with one binding site cannot be bound by two antibodies at once, so it is measured by competition. A large protein has two sites and suits a sandwich. Hormone status does not decide format, and two antibodies cannot share one site."}, {"id": 1672, "domain": "histology", "question": "The electron microscopy service in the laboratory reviews its workflow with new staff on Monday. During electron microscopy processing, semi-thin resin sections are cut and stained with a dye before ultra-thin sections are made. What is the purpose of this step?", "options": {"A": "Remove the resin from the tissue before it goes into the ultramicrotome", "B": "Confirm that the diamond knife is free of nicks", "C": "Let the pathologist choose the area to study by light microscopy", "D": "Replace the post-fix in osmium tetroxide to save time"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Let the pathologist choose the area to study by light microscopy): Semi-thin sections stained with toluidine blue are reviewed by light microscopy so the pathologist can select the area to examine at high magnification. They do not remove resin, replace osmium fixation, or test the knife."}, {"id": 1673, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cytotechnologist is teaching a student about the settings in which unfixed fresh fluids should be delivered promptly. A pericardial fluid was drained in the morning and reaches the laboratory. The student asks why the fluid is refrigerated rather than left on the bench. What is the reason?", "options": {"A": "Cooling slows cell degeneration and bacterial growth while the sample waits for processing", "B": "Cooling kills tuberculosis organisms so that the fluid becomes safe to handle", "C": "Refrigeration fixes the cells in place, so no further preservation step is needed", "D": "Cooling dissolves any clots so that the fluid can be smeared directly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cooling slows cell degeneration and bacterial growth while the sample waits for processing): Refrigeration at 2 to 8 degrees Celsius slows degeneration and microbial growth. It does not fix cells, dissolve clots or make an infectious sample safe."}, {"id": 1674, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A voided urine has very few well-preserved urothelial cells, and heavy blood and inflammation obscure most of the slide. The screener finds no cells suitable for assessing nuclear features. The supervisor asks how the specimen should be reported and what should be requested from the clinician.", "options": {"A": "Low-grade urothelial neoplasia, because degenerated cells resemble monotonous papillary tumour cells", "B": "Atypical urothelial cells, because blood makes the few nuclei look hyperchromatic", "C": "Nondiagnostic or unsatisfactory, with a request for a repeat specimen", "D": "Negative for high-grade urothelial carcinoma, because no malignant cells were seen in the debris"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Nondiagnostic or unsatisfactory, with a request for a repeat specimen): When well-preserved cells are too few or obscured, the specimen is nondiagnostic or unsatisfactory and a repeat is requested. Reporting negative would falsely reassure when nothing assessable was present. Atypia and low-grade neoplasia require identifiable cells with specific features, which obscuring blood cannot provide."}, {"id": 1675, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "An Enterococcus faecium isolate grows at the edge of a vancomycin zone, and the technologist is unsure whether a faint haze inside the zone counts. Vancomycin resistance in enterococci is suspected. Which reading technique is required to avoid a falsely susceptible report?", "options": {"A": "Read the zone edge using reflected light from the front, and report the largest apparent zone", "B": "Reduce the incubation to 8 hours so that faint growth does not appear inside the zone", "C": "Disregard the haze and report the drug as susceptible based on the outer edge", "D": "Read the zone edge carefully using transmitted light, and confirm by MIC or agar screening"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Read the zone edge carefully using transmitted light, and confirm by MIC or agar screening): Vancomycin zones for enterococci should be read carefully with transmitted light, because faint growth in the zone can signal resistance. Confirmation by MIC or agar screening is recommended. Shortening incubation would miss the resistance."}, {"id": 1676, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Considering the scant lactobacilli and the coating seen on many squamous cells in this routine screening sample from a 29-year-old woman, together with the absence of trichomonads, yeast or viral changes, what change in the vaginal flora do these findings most likely reflect?", "options": {"A": "Overgrowth of lactobacilli with cytolysis, leaving bare nuclei in a clean granular background of debris", "B": "Colonization by Actinomyces-like organisms, which is linked to long-term use of an intrauterine device", "C": "Heavy neutrophilic exudate with fungal hyphae, which reflects candidal overgrowth of the vaginal surface", "D": "Loss of lactobacilli with overgrowth of mixed coccobacillary flora, consistent with bacterial vaginosis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Loss of lactobacilli with overgrowth of mixed coccobacillary flora, consistent with bacterial vaginosis): Few lactobacilli and a surface layer of tiny coccobacilli on squamous cells reflect a shift of the flora to mixed organisms, the picture of bacterial vaginosis. Lactobacillus overgrowth would cause cytolysis with bare nuclei, Actinomyces-like organisms form tangled filaments, and candida would bring hyphae and more inflammation.", "cluster_id": "5", "cluster_text": "A liquid-based cervical sample from a 29-year-old woman, sent for routine screening, has an unremarkable squamous population with no intraepithelial lesion. The background is clean to slightly granular, and lactobacilli are scant. Many squamous cells have their cell borders obscured by a layer of tiny coccobacilli, which gives them a stippled, shaggy look. Neutrophils are sparse, and no trichomonads, budding yeast or viral cytopathic changes are seen. The requisition lists no clinical history and no symptoms, and the collection date and fixative handling are unremarkable.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1677, "domain": "cytology", "question": "The cytotechnologist is reporting the case of a 29-year-old woman with a negative screening cytology, and a laboratory comment about the background organisms is being considered. What are the squamous cells covered with adherent coccobacilli called in cytology practice, and which feature makes them recognizable on the slide at the microscope?", "options": {"A": "Koilocytes, squamous cells with a sharp perinuclear halo and an enlarged raisin-like nucleus", "B": "Parakeratotic cells, small squamous cells with dense orange cytoplasm and a small dark nucleus", "C": "Clue cells, squamous cells whose borders are hidden under adherent coccobacilli", "D": "Skewered cells, squamous cells pierced by long pseudohyphae in a pattern like a kebab stick"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clue cells, squamous cells whose borders are hidden under adherent coccobacilli): Clue cells are squamous cells whose outlines are blurred by a coating of coccobacilli, giving a shaggy border. Koilocytes show a perinuclear halo from HPV effect, parakeratotic cells are small with dense orange cytoplasm, and skewered cells describe candida pseudohyphae threading through squamous cells.", "cluster_id": "5", "cluster_text": "A liquid-based cervical sample from a 29-year-old woman, sent for routine screening, has an unremarkable squamous population with no intraepithelial lesion. The background is clean to slightly granular, and lactobacilli are scant. Many squamous cells have their cell borders obscured by a layer of tiny coccobacilli, which gives them a stippled, shaggy look. Neutrophils are sparse, and no trichomonads, budding yeast or viral cytopathic changes are seen. The requisition lists no clinical history and no symptoms, and the collection date and fixative handling are unremarkable.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1678, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Considering that the squamous population itself shows no abnormality, and that the requisition lists no history or symptoms, how should this organism finding be placed in the Bethesda report for this slide in the laboratory? The cytotechnologist must choose the category and any comment that accurately reflects what the slide shows.", "options": {"A": "ASC-US, because organism-related changes are an atypical epithelial finding in these cells", "B": "LSIL, because the cells show an abnormal membrane pattern caused by the organisms present", "C": "Unsatisfactory for evaluation, because organisms obscure the squamous cell borders from clear view", "D": "Negative for intraepithelial lesion, with a comment on flora shift suggesting bacterial vaginosis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Negative for intraepithelial lesion, with a comment on flora shift suggesting bacterial vaginosis): A shift in flora toward coccobacilli is a non-neoplastic organism finding, so the slide is reported as negative for intraepithelial lesion or malignancy with a comment on the flora. The shaggy cell borders are caused by organisms, not an epithelial abnormality, so ASC-US and LSIL are not suitable, and the squamous cells are still adequate to read.", "cluster_id": "5", "cluster_text": "A liquid-based cervical sample from a 29-year-old woman, sent for routine screening, has an unremarkable squamous population with no intraepithelial lesion. The background is clean to slightly granular, and lactobacilli are scant. Many squamous cells have their cell borders obscured by a layer of tiny coccobacilli, which gives them a stippled, shaggy look. Neutrophils are sparse, and no trichomonads, budding yeast or viral cytopathic changes are seen. The requisition lists no clinical history and no symptoms, and the collection date and fixative handling are unremarkable.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1679, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist telephones a critical white cell count of 0.4 x 10^9/L to a ward nurse. After the nurse reads the values back correctly, the technologist opens the record to document the call. Which items must be documented for this critical result communication to meet laboratory quality standards?", "options": {"A": "The time of the call, the person notified, and the technologist's own name", "B": "Only the result value, since the notified person is recorded elsewhere", "C": "Only the time of the call, since the name of the person notified is optional", "D": "The nurse's opinion on the result, since a clinical interpretation must be recorded"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The time of the call, the person notified, and the technologist's own name): Critical results are documented with the time, the person notified and the technologist's name, after read-back. Recording only the value or the time leaves the record incomplete, and clinical opinions are not part of the required documentation."}, {"id": 1680, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A technologist explains to a student why serum from tiger-top and gold-top tubes with a gel barrier is often held for several hours before testing at a regional laboratory. The student asks what changes in the tube once it has been centrifuged, compared with a tube without gel. What is the change?", "options": {"A": "A gel layer separates serum from cells so metabolism and leakage stop", "B": "A gel layer neutralizes hemoglobin so hemolysis cannot occur afterward", "C": "A gel layer binds potassium so serum electrolytes read consistently", "D": "A gel layer removes fibrin so clotting can continue until testing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A gel layer separates serum from cells so metabolism and leakage stop): After centrifugation the gel forms a seal between serum and cells, so glucose is no longer consumed and potassium no longer leaks into the serum. It does not neutralize hemoglobin, remove fibrin or bind potassium. Hemolysis that occurred before spinning remains in the sample, and fibrin strands form only if the tube was spun before clotting was complete."}, {"id": 1681, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A wash buffer for FISH is prepared each week from saline sodium citrate concentrate and diluted with water. The pH meter used to adjust the buffer is included on the laboratory's quality schedule. Why does the laboratory calibrate and log the pH meter?", "options": {"A": "The meter determines the colour of the DAPI, so calibration keeps the counterstain blue", "B": "The meter counts the number of nuclei, so calibration keeps cutoff values correct", "C": "The meter sets the denaturation temperature, so calibration keeps the oven accurate", "D": "Buffer pH and salt affect wash stringency, so a poor meter can weaken true signal"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Buffer pH and salt affect wash stringency, so a poor meter can weaken true signal): Buffer composition, including pH, contributes to how stringent a wash is, so an inaccurate meter can shift results. The meter does not control counterstain colour, oven temperature, or nucleus counts."}, {"id": 1682, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "In a Sanger cycle sequencing reaction the four dideoxynucleotides are each labelled with a fluorescent dye of a different colour. An instructor asks how the instrument identifies the base at each position of the final sequence. What does the detector actually read as fragments pass the laser?", "options": {"A": "The pH change released as each nucleotide is incorporated into the growing strand", "B": "The dye colour on the final nucleotide of each fragment passing the laser", "C": "The mass of each fragment measured by its time of flight in the capillary", "D": "The colour of the primer, which differs for each of the four bases"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The dye colour on the final nucleotide of each fragment passing the laser): Each ddNTP carries its own dye, so the colour on the last nucleotide of every fragment names that base as the fragment reaches the detector. Mass spectrometry and pH detection are not how this instrument works. The primer is the same for the whole reaction and carries no base information."}, {"id": 1683, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory reclassifies variants automatically each time its annotation database is refreshed but keeps no record of the database version used for each report. A provider queries why a patient's variant classification differs from the earlier report. What is the underlying deficiency?", "options": {"A": "Missing tumour purity estimates, since these determine how each variant classification is assigned by staff", "B": "Missing Sanger confirmation, since it decides each classification tier", "C": "Missing external proficiency testing, since it sets each patient's classification", "D": "Missing version control of the reference build, software, and databases used for each report"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Missing version control of the reference build, software, and databases used for each report): The reference genome build, software, and databases used must be recorded, since a change in any of them can change a result. Without version records the laboratory cannot explain differences or reproduce a result. Purity, proficiency testing, and Sanger confirmation do not explain why a classification differs after an update."}, {"id": 1684, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with no pregnancy symptoms has a positive serum hCG on one platform, but a repeat on a different platform is negative. The patient has been exposed to animal antibodies at work. What is the best explanation? Choose the best explanation.", "options": {"A": "Heterophile antibodies can bridge the assay antibodies and cause a false positive", "B": "The positive result is right because hCG is produced only by a healthy placenta", "C": "A high-dose hook effect produced the false positive on the first platform in this patient", "D": "The second platform is faulty, since two hCG methods should give identical numeric results"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Heterophile antibodies can bridge the assay antibodies and cause a false positive): Heterophile antibodies in the patient's serum can link the capture and detection antibodies, causing a false positive. A different platform, a blocking reagent or a urine test can help. The hook effect produces a falsely low result, not a positive."}, {"id": 1685, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A very lipemic specimen gives a hemoglobin of 180 g/L with an MCH of 41 pg, though the smear appears normal. The technologist wants to remove the interference from the milky plasma before reporting the hemoglobin and indices. Which step corrects this problem?", "options": {"A": "Warm the specimen at 37 degrees Celsius and rerun it as received", "B": "Multiply the hemoglobin by 1.1 to allow for the plasma dilution", "C": "Replace the plasma with saline and rerun the red cells", "D": "Add a second dose of EDTA to clear the turbidity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Replace the plasma with saline and rerun the red cells): Lipemia falsely raises hemoglobin, MCH and MCHC because the turbid plasma adds to the absorbance reading, and replacing the plasma with saline removes the interference. Warming corrects cold agglutinins rather than lipemia. Extra EDTA does not clear lipids. The 1.1 factor belongs to citrate platelet recollections."}, {"id": 1686, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A clinic receives a cytology report describing pear-shaped grey-blue organisms consistent with Trichomonas vaginalis in a young woman. The nurse asks whether treating only the patient is enough or whether anything else about management applies, given how this organism is transmitted.", "options": {"A": "It arises from intrauterine devices, so removing the device resolves it", "B": "It is a normal commensal, so no treatment is needed for anyone", "C": "It is acquired from recent antibiotic use, so stopping the antibiotic is enough for cure", "D": "It is sexually transmitted, so the patient and her partners need treatment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is sexually transmitted, so the patient and her partners need treatment): Trichomoniasis is a sexually transmitted infection, so both the patient and her partners are treated to prevent reinfection. Antibiotic use is linked to Candida. Devices are linked to Actinomyces, and Trichomonas is not a harmless commensal."}, {"id": 1687, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A DNA extract from stool shows 60 ng/µL and purity ratios in the acceptable range on the spectrophotometer, yet the qPCR internal control amplifies weakly. The technologist suspects the reading overstates usable DNA. Which limitation of UV spectrophotometry best explains this discrepancy?", "options": {"A": "It reads at 230 nm and misses protein contamination, so the purity ratios are unreliable", "B": "It cannot distinguish DNA from RNA or free nucleotides, so the reading may overestimate.", "C": "It measures fragment length rather than mass, so short fragments appear falsely abundant.", "D": "It detects only single-stranded DNA, so the double-stranded content is underestimated."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It cannot distinguish DNA from RNA or free nucleotides, so the reading may overestimate.): Absorbance at 260 nm is read from all nucleic acid and free nucleotides together, so a contaminated extract can look better than it is. A fluorometric dye specific to double-stranded DNA is more accurate. Spectrophotometry does not measure fragment length or restrict itself to single strands."}, {"id": 1688, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A laboratory receives a new 150 mm Mueller-Hinton plate for a full Kirby-Bauer panel. The technologist wants to fit as many disks as possible so that fewer plates are used. What limit applies to the number of disks on a plate of this size, and why does it exist?", "options": {"A": "About 2 disks at most, so that the lawn density remains uniform", "B": "About 12 disks at least, so that the agar dries before incubation", "C": "About 30 disks at most, so that the plate surface is fully used", "D": "About 12 disks at most, so that neighbouring zones do not overlap"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 12 disks at most, so that neighbouring zones do not overlap): A 150 mm plate usually holds no more than about 12 disks so zones of inhibition do not overlap and can be measured. Overlapping zones would give unreliable diameters. Drying and lawn density are not what sets the limit."}, {"id": 1689, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In a small community hospital, A serous fluid arrives for cytology and flow cytometry, and only a small volume is available. The technologist is unsure how to divide it. Which approach follows the chapter's guidance for a fluid meant for both studies?", "options": {"A": "Freeze half of the sample and fix the other half in formalin overnight", "B": "Send the whole sample fresh to flow cytometry and prepare cytology slides from leftover cells", "C": "Split it, fix the cytology portion, and leave the flow portion fresh in medium", "D": "Fix the entire sample in alcohol so that both tests can be performed later"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Split it, fix the cytology portion, and leave the flow portion fresh in medium): A fluid intended for both studies is split so the cytology portion is fixed while the flow portion stays fresh, since fixation would destroy surface antigens needed for flow. Fixing everything or fixing nothing sacrifices one of the studies, and freezing damages cells. Planning the split before the sample is divided prevents a loss of either study."}, {"id": 1690, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "The laboratory manager plans to change from LISS to PEG enhancement for the antibody screen. New reagents have arrived and the technologists want to use them on patient samples the following morning. Which step does good practice require before PEG is used for patient testing?", "options": {"A": "Ask one experienced technologist to approve the change verbally, then record it later in the log", "B": "Begin patient testing at once, since commercial reagents are already licensed and need no local checks", "C": "Test three known negative samples and start using PEG if none reacts", "D": "Validate PEG against the current method and follow the manufacturer's instructions and local procedure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Validate PEG against the current method and follow the manufacturer's instructions and local procedure): Each enhancement medium is used according to the manufacturer's instructions and validated against the laboratory's own method, with written procedures and records, as Canadian standards require. Licensing does not remove the need for local validation. Three negative samples cannot show sensitivity, and an undocumented verbal approval is not acceptable."}, {"id": 1691, "domain": "cytology", "question": "During a program information session for community health staff, A public health nurse asks why a negative high-risk HPV result supports longer screening intervals in an HPV-based program. Which property of the HPV test best explains this reassurance for a screened patient?", "options": {"A": "Its low cost compared with any other cervical screening method available", "B": "Its high specificity, which shows the exact cell changes present on the slide", "C": "Its ability to replace colposcopy for every patient with abnormal cytology", "D": "Its high sensitivity, which makes precancer unlikely for years after a negative"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Its high sensitivity, which makes precancer unlikely for years after a negative): HPV testing is highly sensitive, so a negative result gives strong reassurance that precancer is unlikely for years ahead. Cytology is the more specific test and reveals cell changes. HPV testing does not replace colposcopy for abnormal results, and cost is not the reason for longer intervals."}, {"id": 1692, "domain": "histology", "question": "A laboratory wishes to lower staff solvent exposure and asks which clearing agents could replace xylene in its processor schedule. Staff also mention that xylene left on too long makes tissue hard and brittle. Which choice is a recognized alternative to xylene?", "options": {"A": "An aliphatic hydrocarbon or vegetable-based clearant, which lowers solvent exposure.", "B": "Neutral buffered formalin, since it mixes with both alcohol and paraffin wax.", "C": "Absolute ethanol, since it mixes with wax and also acts as a clearing agent.", "D": "Ammonium oxalate solution, since it makes tissue look translucent before infiltration."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An aliphatic hydrocarbon or vegetable-based clearant, which lowers solvent exposure.): Aliphatic hydrocarbon and vegetable-based clearants act as less toxic substitutes that are miscible with both ethanol and paraffin. Ethanol is a dehydrant and does not mix with paraffin, so it cannot bridge to wax. Formalin is a fixative, and ammonium oxalate is the reagent used in a decalcification end point test."}, {"id": 1693, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A skin scraping from a scaly ringworm lesion on the forearm grows a cottony colony with a yellow reverse after ten days. To see macroconidia and identify the dermatophyte, the technologist teases a small portion of the colony apart in a drop of blue stain in phenol and lactic acid, and adds a coverslip. Which stain is being used?", "options": {"A": "Lactophenol cotton blue, which allows conidiophores and macroconidia to be seen from culture growth", "B": "Modified acid-fast stain, which colours macroconidia red against a green background", "C": "Calcofluor white, which shows macroconidia only under bright-field illumination without a filter", "D": "India ink, which shows macroconidia as clear halos against a dark background"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lactophenol cotton blue, which allows conidiophores and macroconidia to be seen from culture growth): Lactophenol cotton blue is the routine stain for culture growth and shows fungal structures. India ink is for capsules in CSF, calcofluor needs ultraviolet light, and acid-fast stains are used on stool oocysts."}, {"id": 1694, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Landsteiner's rule describes a relationship between the antigens on a person's red cells and the antibodies in that same person's serum. A technologist relies on it when deciding whether the forward and reverse groups agree with each other. Which statement correctly summarizes the rule?", "options": {"A": "Serum contains IgG antibodies that appear only after an ABO-incompatible transfusion", "B": "Serum contains antibodies directed against the ABO antigens that the person's red cells lack", "C": "Serum contains antibodies against the ABO antigens that the person's red cells carry", "D": "Serum contains antibodies directed against the ABO antigens that the person's cells carry"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Serum contains antibodies directed against the ABO antigens that the person's red cells lack): A person lacking the B antigen makes anti-B, and one lacking A makes anti-A, which is why forward and reverse groups should agree. Antibodies against one's own antigens would destroy the person's own red cells. The antibodies form naturally without transfusion, mostly as IgM."}, {"id": 1695, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "The woman asks why she is healthy despite having only 45 chromosomes in all twenty cells analyzed. Which explanation best accounts for her normal physical examination, given the chromosome finding in this case, as the technologist prepares the interpretation for the genetic counsellor?", "options": {"A": "The fused chromosome carries an extra copy of chromosome 13 that offsets the gene loss", "B": "Mosaicism with normal 46-chromosome cells in other tissues protects her from clinical effects", "C": "The rearrangement is balanced, and the lost short arms carry redundant ribosomal genes", "D": "X inactivation silences the fused chromosome in each cell, so its genes are not expressed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The rearrangement is balanced, and the lost short arms carry redundant ribosomal genes): a Robertsonian fusion leaves 45 chromosomes but nearly all essential genetic material intact, since the lost short arms of acrocentrics hold repeated ribosomal genes. An extra copy of chromosome 13 is not present, mosaicism is not described because the twenty cells showed the same karyotype, and X inactivation does not silence autosomes this way.", "cluster_id": "6", "cluster_text": "A 31-year-old woman with three first-trimester miscarriages has peripheral blood chromosome analysis at a Canadian regional laboratory. Her partner's sample has not arrived. Twenty G-banded metaphases at about 450-band resolution all show 45 chromosomes, with a single large chromosome formed by fusion of the long arms of chromosomes 13 and 14, and no other abnormality. She has no dysmorphic features and a normal physical examination. The laboratory director asks the technologist to prepare the interpretation for the genetic counsellor at the clinic this week.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1696, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "The laboratory director asks the technologist to prepare the interpretation for the genetic counsellor, noting that her three first-trimester miscarriages are likely linked to this finding. Which mechanism most likely explains the pregnancy losses in this woman, considering how the fused chromosome from the case segregates during meiosis in her egg cells?", "options": {"A": "Unbalanced segregation in meiosis yields gametes with extra or missing 13 or 14 material", "B": "Loss of the satellite regions disrupts implantation by lowering ribosomal RNA output in early embryos", "C": "The fusion causes trisomy 21 in her embryos, which explains the repeated early losses", "D": "Loss of an X chromosome in her ovarian tissue prevents embryos from implanting normally"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Unbalanced segregation in meiosis yields gametes with extra or missing 13 or 14 material): a Robertsonian carrier is balanced herself, but during meiosis the fused chromosome can segregate so that gametes are unbalanced, and embryos with trisomy or monosomy for 13 or 14 material are often lost. Satellite loss is already tolerated in her, a 13;14 fusion does not predict trisomy 21, and her sex chromosomes are normal.", "cluster_id": "6", "cluster_text": "A 31-year-old woman with three first-trimester miscarriages has peripheral blood chromosome analysis at a Canadian regional laboratory. Her partner's sample has not arrived. Twenty G-banded metaphases at about 450-band resolution all show 45 chromosomes, with a single large chromosome formed by fusion of the long arms of chromosomes 13 and 14, and no other abnormality. She has no dysmorphic features and a normal physical examination. The laboratory director asks the technologist to prepare the interpretation for the genetic counsellor at the clinic this week.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1697, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "The technologist finalizes the report for this patient after the twenty metaphases have been counted and the karyotype has been described. The genetic counsellor will use the report with the patient and her family at the clinic. Which recommendation should the report include to address the finding for the patient, her partner and her relatives?", "options": {"A": "Advise that no further testing is needed because she is physically normal", "B": "Offer sequencing of her whole genome to find the gene responsible for the miscarriages", "C": "Recommend genetic counselling, testing of relatives and prenatal diagnosis in future pregnancies", "D": "Repeat the analysis on a skin biopsy because blood results cannot show such rearrangements"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recommend genetic counselling, testing of relatives and prenatal diagnosis in future pregnancies): a carrier of a Robertsonian translocation has a risk of unbalanced offspring, and relatives may share the rearrangement, so counselling, family testing and prenatal diagnosis are appropriate. Normal physical findings do not remove reproductive risk, a gene sequence will not reveal a chromosome rearrangement, and blood chromosome analysis already detects this finding reliably.", "cluster_id": "6", "cluster_text": "A 31-year-old woman with three first-trimester miscarriages has peripheral blood chromosome analysis at a Canadian regional laboratory. Her partner's sample has not arrived. Twenty G-banded metaphases at about 450-band resolution all show 45 chromosomes, with a single large chromosome formed by fusion of the long arms of chromosomes 13 and 14, and no other abnormality. She has no dysmorphic features and a normal physical examination. The laboratory director asks the technologist to prepare the interpretation for the genetic counsellor at the clinic this week.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1698, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A fasting lipid report is shown, and the laboratory does not measure LDL cholesterol directly on this routine outpatient sample, and the physician wants an estimate. Using the Friedewald equation in SI units, which estimated LDL cholesterol and calculation are correct for this report?", "options": {"A": "About 5.1 mmol/L, after subtracting only the HDL cholesterol from the total cholesterol", "B": "About 4.2 mmol/L, after subtracting HDL cholesterol and triglyceride divided by 2.2", "C": "About 3.1 mmol/L, after subtracting the HDL cholesterol and the full triglyceride value", "D": "About 4.7 mmol/L, after dividing triglyceride by five as done for mg/dL units"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 4.2 mmol/L, after subtracting HDL cholesterol and triglyceride divided by 2.2): In SI units LDL equals total cholesterol minus HDL minus triglyceride divided by 2.2, so 6.2 minus 1.1 minus 0.9 gives about 4.2 mmol/L. The other values come from omitting the triglyceride term, subtracting all of it, or using the divisor of five that belongs to mg/dL units.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Fasting lipid panel", "columns": ["Test", "Result", "Units", "Reference interval", "Flag"], "rows": [["Total cholesterol", "6.2", "mmol/L", "less than 5.2", "H"], ["HDL cholesterol", "1.1", "mmol/L", "above 1.0", ""], ["Triglycerides", "2.0", "mmol/L", "less than 1.7", "H"]], "flag_col": 4, "caption": "Fasting lipid results"}, "image": "question_images/q_1698.png"}, {"id": 1699, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A woman who carries a pericentric inversion of chromosome 5 asks the laboratory why her pregnancy has a risk of an unbalanced outcome even though she is healthy and her karyotype shows no loss of material. The counsellor asks the technologist for the most accurate mechanism. What should be said?", "options": {"A": "An inversion loop can form in meiosis, and recombination inside it can produce unbalanced gametes.", "B": "The inverted segment is lost during meiosis, so gametes carry a deletion of chromosome 5", "C": "Recombination is suppressed within an inverted segment, so gametes carry the full inversion and stay balanced", "D": "The centromere splits across the arms in meiosis, producing isochromosome gametes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An inversion loop can form in meiosis, and recombination inside it can produce unbalanced gametes.): Homologues pair by forming a loop around the inverted segment, and crossing over within the loop can produce gametes with duplications and deletions. The segment is not lost, and recombination is possible rather than blocked. Transverse splitting of the centromere produces isochromosomes and is not the usual meiotic result here."}, {"id": 1700, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A chorionic villus sampling specimen arrives at 11 weeks, and the technologist prepares a rapid direct study on the outer cytotrophoblast layer of the villi. The clinician later asks why a culture of the inner villus tissue is also set up when the direct result is already available. Which explanation should the technologist give?", "options": {"A": "The cultured mesenchymal core confirms the direct finding, since the two layers can differ", "B": "The inner layer replaces the direct study, since the outer layer gives poor metaphases", "C": "The inner layer is set up to detect maternal cell contamination, since it represents the mother", "D": "The inner layer is used only to test the medium, since it contains no fetal cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The cultured mesenchymal core confirms the direct finding, since the two layers can differ): Direct cytotrophoblast studies are rapid, and the cultured mesenchymal core confirms the result because the two layers can sometimes differ. The direct layer does give usable results, and the core is placental, not maternal, tissue."}, {"id": 1701, "domain": "histology", "question": "Before releasing two negative acid-fast stains on tissue biopsies, a technologist reviews the Ziehl-Neelsen run record shown in the image. The lab procedure requires a positive control and a negative control on every run. How should the negative patient findings be handled?", "options": {"A": "Withhold them because the failed positive control invalidates the run, then check reagents and restain", "B": "Release them because the clean negative control shows the stain was not contaminated", "C": "Release them with a comment that a repeat should be done on the next batch", "D": "Report them as negative after restaining only the two patient slides with extra carbol fuchsin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Withhold them because the failed positive control invalidates the run, then check reagents and restain): A positive control that shows no red bacilli means the stain cannot detect organisms in this run, so a negative patient result is not trustworthy. The reagents and procedure are checked and all slides, including controls, are repeated. A clean negative control does not prove sensitivity, and adding stain to patient slides alone is not a valid correction.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Ziehl-Neelsen run record", "columns": ["Slide", "Material", "Result"], "rows": [["Positive control", "M. tuberculosis smear", "No red bacilli"], ["Negative control", "Reagent blank smear", "No red bacilli"], ["Patient 1", "Lung biopsy", "No red bacilli"], ["Patient 2", "Lymph node biopsy", "No red bacilli"]], "caption": "Run record for a Ziehl-Neelsen stain"}, "image": "question_images/q_1701.png"}, {"id": 1702, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A 36-year-old has a smear with tight groups of vacuolated endocervical cells with high N:C ratios and nucleoli, and actinomyces-like organisms are present in the background. The requisition mentions an intrauterine device inserted two years ago. How should the technologist view this history?", "options": {"A": "It confirms a malignancy, so the history overrides the morphologic features seen", "B": "It matters mainly for billing, because device effects are not reported on cytology", "C": "It can explain the reactive-looking cells, so the history on the requisition is important", "D": "The device history does not explain these cells, so the technologist can leave it aside"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It can explain the reactive-looking cells, so the history on the requisition is important): An IUD is associated with vacuolated glandular cells in tight groups and with actinomyces-like organisms, so the history helps avoid overcalling adenocarcinoma. It does not confirm malignancy, and it should never be ignored. Device effects are clinically relevant, not merely administrative."}, {"id": 1703, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A laboratory report states an eGFR of 48 mL/min per 1.73 square metres, and a student asks which equation Canadian laboratories generally use, what units are reported, and how long a low value must persist before chronic kidney disease is indicated. Which summary is accurate?", "options": {"A": "CKD-EPI equation, reported in mg/mmol per gram, with a low value for three hours", "B": "Friedewald equation, reported in mmol/L per litre, with a low value for three days", "C": "Westgard equation, reported in mL/min per patient, with a low value for one week", "D": "CKD-EPI equation, reported in mL/min per 1.73 square metres, with a low value for three months"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (CKD-EPI equation, reported in mL/min per 1.73 square metres, with a low value for three months): Canadian laboratories generally use CKD-EPI, reported in mL/min per 1.73 square metres, and an eGFR below 60 for three months or more indicates chronic kidney disease. Friedewald is a lipid equation, and Westgard is a quality control rule set, not a filtration equation."}, {"id": 1704, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A pathologist asks how adequacy should be judged on a peritoneal fluid smear, since there is no fixed cell count for non-gynecologic specimens. The technologist describes what the assessment should be based on. The pathologist explains that a fixed threshold is not written for these specimens, unlike routine cervical samples. What is the appropriate standard?", "options": {"A": "Whether the smear is thick enough to cover the entire glass surface", "B": "Whether the gynecologic minimum of 5,000 well-preserved squamous cells has been reached on the smear", "C": "Whether enough well-preserved, representative material is present to answer the clinical question", "D": "Whether the slide has been stained with both Diff-Quik and Papanicolaou"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Whether enough well-preserved, representative material is present to answer the clinical question): Non-gynecologic samples have no fixed count, so adequacy depends on whether representative, well-preserved material can answer the clinical question. The gynecologic count and stain choice do not define adequacy here."}, {"id": 1705, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A liquid-based slide from a 36-year-old woman with a cervical polyp contains the four endocervical cells in the image at equal magnification. A reviewer wants a benign reactive or repair pattern separated from glandular neoplasia. Which cell shows the benign reactive pattern?", "options": {"A": "Cell 1, because its fine, smooth nucleus with visible nucleoli fits reactive or repair change", "B": "Cell 2, because its fine, smooth nucleus with visible nucleoli fits reactive or repair change", "C": "Cell 3, because its fine, smooth nucleus with visible nucleoli fits reactive or repair change", "D": "Cell 4, because its fine, smooth nucleus with visible nucleoli fits reactive or repair change"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cell 2, because its fine, smooth nucleus with visible nucleoli fits reactive or repair change): Cell 2 has a mildly enlarged nucleus with fine chromatin, a smooth outline and nucleoli, which is typical of reactive endocervical cells. Cell 1 is a normal columnar cell with no nucleoli. Cell 3 has dark chromatin, a high N:C ratio and an irregular outline, and Cell 4 has coarse chromatin with an irregular outline, so both raise concern for glandular neoplasia.", "image_type": "cell_micrograph", "image_params": {"cells": [{"label": "1", "nc_ratio": 0.2, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "columnar"}, {"label": "2", "nc_ratio": 0.3, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 2, "cell": "columnar"}, {"label": "3", "nc_ratio": 0.55, "chromatin": "hyperchromatic", "outline": "irregular", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "columnar"}, {"label": "4", "nc_ratio": 0.25, "chromatin": "coarse", "outline": "irregular", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "columnar"}], "caption": "Cells at equal magnification", "context": "endocervical", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1705.png"}, {"id": 1706, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A weightlifter has a total CK of 2,400 U/L, many times above the upper reference limit, the day after a strenuous training session and an intramuscular vitamin injection. The physician wonders about heart injury, and the troponin is normal. How should the technologist explain the raised CK?", "options": {"A": "Exercise and injection lower total CK, so heart injury is strongly indicated", "B": "Exercise raises CK from brain tissue, and heart injury is confirmed by CK-BB", "C": "Injection raises CK only through kidney filtration, and exercise has no effect", "D": "Exercise and intramuscular injection raise total CK from skeletal muscle, and heart injury is not indicated"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Exercise and intramuscular injection raise total CK from skeletal muscle, and heart injury is not indicated): Strenuous exercise and intramuscular injections raise total CK from skeletal muscle, so a high value alone does not indicate heart injury. They do not lower CK. CK-BB comes from brain, and the kidney does not generate the rise."}, {"id": 1707, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A safety talk covers the fire risks in the cytology laboratory. The trainer points out that the alcohols used for fixation and staining need special storage. What hazard does 95% ethanol present that requires approved containers and storage away from ignition sources?", "options": {"A": "It is radioactive, so it must be stored in a lead-lined cabinet with signage", "B": "It is flammable, so it must be stored in approved containers away from ignition sources", "C": "It is corrosive, so it must be stored in a container that resists acid damage", "D": "It is a carcinogen, so it must be stored in a sealed cabinet without any light"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is flammable, so it must be stored in approved containers away from ignition sources): Alcohols are flammable and need approved storage away from ignition. Formalin, not ethanol, carries the carcinogen concern, and ethanol is neither corrosive to skin in this sense nor radioactive."}, {"id": 1708, "domain": "histology", "question": "A laboratory assistant helps at the grossing bench, where a scalpel, open formalin containers and many specimens are present. A colleague eats a sandwich nearby, and the ventilation hood has been switched off to reduce noise. Which combination of measures would best protect staff at this bench?", "options": {"A": "Ventilation switched off to save energy, gloves optional, and reporting sharps injuries monthly.", "B": "Open windows, cloth gloves, and food allowed in a clean corner of the bench.", "C": "Only a lab coat, since gloves interfere with handling and formalin fumes disperse quickly.", "D": "Ventilated station, gloves, universal precautions, and no food or drink at the bench."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ventilated station, gloves, universal precautions, and no food or drink at the bench.): Grossing needs local exhaust ventilation to capture formalin vapour, gloves and universal precautions for biohazard and sharps, and a strict ban on eating or drinking. Sharps injuries are reported immediately, not monthly. Open windows are not a controlled airflow, cloth is permeable to formalin, and skipping gloves exposes skin to a sensitizer."}, {"id": 1709, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Eluates are about to be quantified on a microvolume spectrophotometer after a bead-based extraction. The DNA was eluted in a Tris-based elution buffer, the pedestal has been cleaned, and the instrument now asks for a blank before the first sample is read. Which choice is correct, and why?", "options": {"A": "Blank with a known DNA standard, so the instrument is set to an expected concentration.", "B": "Blank with distilled water, since water is the standard baseline for every buffer.", "C": "Skip the blank, since ratios such as A260/A280 cancel any buffer effect.", "D": "Blank with the same elution buffer, so buffer absorbance is subtracted from every reading."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Blank with the same elution buffer, so buffer absorbance is subtracted from every reading.): The blank should match the solution the sample is dissolved in, so that absorbance from the buffer itself is removed. Water does not match the buffer, a DNA standard is a calibration check and not a blank, and buffer absorbance can still shift the ratios."}, {"id": 1710, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist adds plasma to a suspension of Staphylococcus colonies in a tube and observes a clot after incubation. This tube test detects free coagulase. Which organism does a positive result identify? The isolate is a gram-positive coccus growing in clusters on a routine plate, and the tube is examined after a few hours of incubation.", "options": {"A": "Streptococcus pyogenes, which is a beta-hemolytic coccus that grows in chains", "B": "Escherichia coli, which is a gram-negative rod found in normal intestinal flora", "C": "Staphylococcus aureus, which is the species that produces free coagulase in a tube", "D": "Enterococcus faecalis, which is a gram-positive coccus that grows in pairs and chains"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Staphylococcus aureus, which is the species that produces free coagulase in a tube): Free coagulase makes plasma clot and identifies Staphylococcus aureus in a tube test. Coagulase-negative staphylococci do not clot plasma, and the other organisms named are not staphylococci and would not be tested this way."}, {"id": 1711, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "After a capillary electrophoresis instrument has run QF-PCR samples for months, the peaks from different dyes begin to overlap, and small peaks are hard to read. The laboratory has a maintenance schedule for polymer, capillaries, and calibration. Which routine step most directly addresses overlapping dye peaks?", "options": {"A": "Reducing the number of nuclei scored on each FISH slide", "B": "Raising the hybridization oven temperature for the next batch", "C": "Changing the DAPI counterstain to a darker blue dye", "D": "Performing the spectral calibration recommended for the instrument"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Performing the spectral calibration recommended for the instrument): Spectral calibration corrects how the instrument separates the emission of each dye, and it prevents bleed-through between colours. Oven temperature, counterstain, and scoring numbers belong to FISH work and have no effect on capillary electrophoresis dye separation."}, {"id": 1712, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist adds cold methanol and acetic acid fixative to a cell pellet after hypotonic treatment by pouring it in quickly and vigorously. The pellet forms large white clumps that will not resuspend, and the slides made from it are poor. What error caused this, and what is the correct method?", "options": {"A": "Fixative was added too fast, so it should be added slowly and mixed gently at first", "B": "Fixative was too fresh, so it should be aged for a week before use", "C": "Fixative was added too slowly, so it should be added rapidly to the pellet", "D": "Fixative was too cold, so it should be warmed to 60 degrees Celsius first"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fixative was added too fast, so it should be added slowly and mixed gently at first): Sudden addition of fixative can clump cells, so it is added slowly and gently at first, followed by several washes. Fresh fixative is correct practice, and warming it would be unsafe and unhelpful."}, {"id": 1713, "domain": "histology", "question": "A laboratory needs to cut very large or hard blocks that are difficult to handle on the standard instrument used for routine paraffin sections. Which type of microtome is described in this chapter as suited for large or hard blocks?", "options": {"A": "The rotary microtome, which is the routine paraffin instrument", "B": "The sliding microtome, where the knife moves across the block", "C": "The ultramicrotome, which cuts sections for electron microscopy", "D": "The cryostat, which cuts fresh tissue at freezing temperatures"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The sliding microtome, where the knife moves across the block): The sliding microtome is used for large or hard blocks. The ultramicrotome cuts extremely thin resin sections for electron microscopy, and the rotary instrument is the routine workhorse. The cryostat is used for frozen tissue."}, {"id": 1714, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A trauma centre updates its massive transfusion protocol. The medical director wants the pre-packed pack to follow current practice so that clotting factors are not diluted while red cells are replaced. What ratio of red cells, plasma and platelets does the protocol use?", "options": {"A": "Two units of red cells to one unit of platelets, with no plasma.", "B": "Red cells only until the bleeding stops, then plasma.", "C": "Ten units of red cells for each unit of plasma.", "D": "Balanced ratios close to one to one to one."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Balanced ratios close to one to one to one.): Modern massive transfusion protocols release red cells, plasma and platelets together in balanced ratios close to one to one to one, which limits dilutional coagulopathy. Giving red cells alone or a heavily skewed ratio worsens bleeding."}, {"id": 1715, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient with early iron deficiency has an MCV of 84 fL and a hemoglobin of 122 g/L. The analyzer reports a raised RDW-CV of 17.8 percent, above the usual range of 11.5 to 14.5 percent. What does the raised RDW show about the red cells?", "options": {"A": "Small and normal red cells mixed together, meaning anisocytosis", "B": "A uniform red cell size, because the mean volume is normal", "C": "A wide spread in platelet size that is being counted as red cells", "D": "A raised hemoglobin concentration inside each of the red cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Small and normal red cells mixed together, meaning anisocytosis): The RDW describes how variable the red cell sizes are, and a raised value means anisocytosis, as when small iron-deficient cells mix with older normal ones. A normal MCV can hide this mixture, which is why the RDW is useful. Uniform size would give a normal RDW. Hemoglobin concentration is the MCHC, and platelets are a separate distribution."}, {"id": 1716, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "During library preparation the extracted genomic DNA is broken into shorter pieces before adapters are attached. A trainee asks how this fragmentation can be carried out in the laboratory, since the pieces must be of a suitable size for the sequencer. By what means is DNA fragmented?", "options": {"A": "Physically, for example by sonication, or enzymatically, using a fragmenting enzyme mix", "B": "By PCR with random primers, which produces fragments of equal length automatically", "C": "By heating the DNA to 95 degrees Celsius, which cuts strands at random positions", "D": "By adding ddNTPs, which stops synthesis and produces fragments of different lengths"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Physically, for example by sonication, or enzymatically, using a fragmenting enzyme mix): Fragmentation is done physically or enzymatically. Heat denatures DNA rather than cutting it into sized pieces. PCR with random primers is not a fragmentation step, and ddNTPs belong to the Sanger reaction, which terminates synthesis instead of preparing a library."}, {"id": 1717, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A stool culture is plated on XLD agar. The technologist sees colourless colonies with black centres. The isolate is lactose negative, urease negative, and gives hydrogen sulfide on triple sugar iron. Which organism does the isolate most likely represent, and how should it be handled?", "options": {"A": "Escherichia coli, a lactose fermenter that is reported as normal flora in stool cultures", "B": "Salmonella, an enteric pathogen that is reported in any stool culture where it is recovered", "C": "Shigella, an enteric pathogen that is reported in any stool culture where it is recovered", "D": "Proteus mirabilis, a urease-positive organism that is reported only when the physician requests it"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Salmonella, an enteric pathogen that is reported in any stool culture where it is recovered): Salmonella produces hydrogen sulfide with black centres on XLD and is urease negative and lactose negative. Shigella does not produce hydrogen sulfide. Proteus is strongly urease positive. Escherichia coli ferments lactose and would not have black centres."}, {"id": 1718, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A sputum specimen from an older adult with lobar pneumonia grows alpha-hemolytic colonies, and a Gram stain of a colony is shown in the image. The colonies are catalase negative. Which test best differentiates the isolate from the viridans group streptococci?", "options": {"A": "Optochin disk test, with a zone of inhibition indicating the likely pathogen", "B": "Bacitracin disk test, with a zone of inhibition indicating the likely pathogen", "C": "Hippurate hydrolysis test, with a purple colour indicating the likely pathogen", "D": "Bile esculin test, with blackening of the agar indicating the likely pathogen"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Optochin disk test, with a zone of inhibition indicating the likely pathogen): The image shows gram-positive diplococci, and an alpha-hemolytic catalase-negative isolate with this look is tested with an optochin disk, where a zone of inhibition identifies Streptococcus pneumoniae while viridans streptococci grow through. Bacitracin separates group A streptococci, hippurate hydrolysis identifies group B, and bile esculin identifies enterococci and group D streptococci.", "image_type": "micrograph", "image_params": {"prompt": "Realistic Gram stain photomicrograph, 1000x oil immersion, pink-red background, neutrophils with purple lancet-shaped Gram-positive diplococci in pairs, some surrounded by a faint clear halo (capsule), sputum smear, pale pink background.", "caption": "Gram stain of a colony from a sputum culture", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1718.png"}, {"id": 1719, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A FISH test fails twice on a scarce marrow sample because too few intact nuclei were available. A colleague suggests scoring the few good cells anyway and reporting the result as a normal finding to avoid delaying the physician. What is the best professional response?", "options": {"A": "Score debris and overlapping nuclei until the required count is reached", "B": "Quietly delete the failed attempts from the record so the file looks clean", "C": "Report a normal result, since a low count cannot produce a false negative in any case", "D": "Document the failed test honestly, state the limitation, and discuss options with the director"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Document the failed test honestly, state the limitation, and discuss options with the director): Honest documentation of failures, clear statement of limits, and referral to the director when a result is unclear are part of professional practice. A tiny count can miss an abnormal clone, and counting debris or hiding failures violates both quality and integrity standards."}, {"id": 1720, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A parent asks the genetic counsellor how often Down syndrome is due to free trisomy 21, compared with a translocation or mosaicism. The laboratory technologist is asked to supply approximate proportions from the chapter. Which statement matches those proportions best?", "options": {"A": "About 50 percent are free trisomy 21, with 40 to 50 percent from mosaicism.", "B": "About 95 percent are free trisomy 21, with 3 to 4 percent from translocation.", "C": "About 75 percent are free trisomy 21, with 20 to 25 percent from translocation.", "D": "About 99 percent are translocation cases, with under 1 percent free trisomy."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 95 percent are free trisomy 21, with 3 to 4 percent from translocation.): Around 95 percent are free trisomy 21 from nondisjunction, 3 to 4 percent arise from a Robertsonian translocation, and 1 to 2 percent are mosaic. The other proportions greatly overstate translocation or mosaicism, and translocation is not the usual cause."}, {"id": 1721, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist collects capillary blood from the side of the middle fingertip of an adult with poor veins, for a CBC and a smear. The puncture is made and the first drop of blood appears at the site, mixed with some clear fluid. What should be done with that first drop and why?", "options": {"A": "Wipe it away, because it contains tissue fluid that would dilute the sample", "B": "Collect it into the microtube, since later drops are more likely to contain clots", "C": "Leave it in place to seal the wound and collect the second drop", "D": "Use it for the smear, because it holds the highest concentration of platelets"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wipe it away, because it contains tissue fluid that would dilute the sample): The first drop contains tissue fluid, so it is wiped away before collecting, and hard squeezing is avoided because it also dilutes the sample and hemolyzes cells. The first drop is not a platelet-rich sample for smears. Later drops are not clotted when collection is quick. Leaving it in place blocks the flow."}, {"id": 1722, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient has a low platelet count, schistocytes on the smear, and a normal PT, aPTT and fibrinogen. The physician wants to know whether this is DIC. Which condition best fits these laboratory findings? The patient is a young adult with fever and confusion.", "options": {"A": "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, because DIC would prolong clotting times and lower fibrinogen", "B": "Hemophilia A, because DIC would not affect platelets or produce any fragmented red cells", "C": "Vitamin K deficiency, because DIC would keep clotting times normal and would raise fibrinogen levels", "D": "Von Willebrand disease, because DIC would cause only a prolonged platelet closure time"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, because DIC would prolong clotting times and lower fibrinogen): TTP shows schistocytes and low platelets with normal coagulation tests, unlike DIC, which prolongs PT and aPTT and lowers fibrinogen. Vitamin K deficiency, hemophilia A and VWD do not present with schistocytes."}, {"id": 1723, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist prepares to pour and handle formalin and must select gloves. She has latex, nitrile and thin cloth gloves available, along with a lab coat, safety glasses and a fume hood. Which selection matches the standard protective practice for formalin?", "options": {"A": "No gloves, provided the technologist washes her hands after each formalin transfer.", "B": "Nitrile gloves, a laboratory coat and eye protection, with engineering controls.", "C": "Latex gloves alone, since a lab coat traps vapour against the skin.", "D": "Thin cloth gloves and safety glasses, since formalin does not penetrate fabric."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Nitrile gloves, a laboratory coat and eye protection, with engineering controls.): Gloves must resist the chemical in use, and nitrile holds up to formalin, so it is worn with a coat and eye protection after engineering controls such as the hood. Cloth is permeable and soaks formalin against the skin. Going without gloves exposes skin to a known sensitizer and carcinogen, and washing afterwards does not undo that contact."}, {"id": 1724, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist prepares a broth microdilution tray and adjusts the inoculum so that each well receives the standard concentration. A second technologist asks what the final density in each well should be. Which final concentration of organisms is targeted in broth microdilution wells?", "options": {"A": "About 5 x 10^5 CFU/mL in each well", "B": "About 1.5 x 10^8 CFU/mL in each well", "C": "About 3 x 10^9 CFU/mL in each well", "D": "About 5 x 10^2 CFU/mL in each well"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 5 x 10^5 CFU/mL in each well): Broth microdilution targets about 5 x 10^5 CFU/mL in each well after inoculation. The 1.5 x 10^8 CFU/mL value is the 0.5 McFarland suspension before dilution. Much higher or lower densities would distort the MIC."}, {"id": 1725, "domain": "cytology", "question": "On a busy day, During lunch, two technologists talk in a shared hospital cafeteria about an interesting cytology case, mentioning the patient's age, unusual diagnosis and hospital ward. Other staff from different departments are seated at nearby tables. What is the appropriate professional response?", "options": {"A": "Continue, provided the diagnosis is rare and educational for the people listening", "B": "Continue quietly, since all hospital staff are entitled to know the case details", "C": "Stop the conversation, since patient talk does not belong in public areas", "D": "Continue, because no patient name was spoken aloud in the whole discussion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the conversation, since patient talk does not belong in public areas): Conversations about patients do not happen in public areas, and personal health information is shared only with those directly involved in care or quality review. Leaving out the name does not remove identifying details, and educational interest or staff status does not grant access. Even without a patient name, details such as age, diagnosis and ward can identify a person."}, {"id": 1726, "domain": "histology", "question": "The pathologist and technologist review the remaining tissue together. A frozen section was performed on a very small biopsy, and the pathologist used all of the tissue from the block. What should the technologist do about the missing tissue for permanent processing?", "options": {"A": "Reprocess the tissue in formalin without informing anyone", "B": "Tell the surgeon that the specimen was lost and no diagnosis is possible", "C": "Discard the block because it cannot be used for permanent slides", "D": "Tell the pathologist, because the frozen block may be all that remains"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tell the pathologist, because the frozen block may be all that remains): When no separate tissue remains, the leftover frozen block may be all that is left, so the pathologist decides how to proceed. Discarding it or hiding the loss risks the final diagnosis. Telling the surgeon no diagnosis is possible is premature."}, {"id": 1727, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A construction worker seen after two days without fluids has hemoglobin 178 g/L and hematocrit 0.53. He has no splenomegaly, normal white cell and platelet counts, and a red cell mass study is normal. His hemoglobin returns to normal after rehydration. How should this polycythemia be classified?", "options": {"A": "Polycythemia vera, since hemoglobin above 165 g/L in men defines the disease", "B": "Relative polycythemia, since plasma volume fell while the red cell mass stayed normal", "C": "Secondary polycythemia, since hypoxia has increased erythropoietin and red cell mass", "D": "Primary familial polycythemia, since the rise reversed without any therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Relative polycythemia, since plasma volume fell while the red cell mass stayed normal): When dehydration reduces plasma volume, concentrations rise but the red cell mass is unchanged, which is relative polycythemia. Polycythemia vera needs a true increase in cell mass with other features. Secondary disease has raised mass from hypoxia, and the prompt reversal argues against inherited disease."}, {"id": 1728, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A lymphoid-rich background is found on a 27-year-old's smear. Among the small lymphocytes are larger macrophages containing dark nuclear debris. The technologist needs to name this macrophage and the condition it is associated with, to avoid confusing the pattern with a small cell malignancy.", "options": {"A": "Multinucleated giant cells, associated with a herpes simplex infection background", "B": "Tingible body macrophages, associated with a follicular cervicitis background", "C": "Signet-ring macrophages, associated with an intrauterine device background", "D": "Foamy histiocytes, associated with a postpartum atrophic background"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tingible body macrophages, associated with a follicular cervicitis background): Tingible body macrophages sit among lymphocytes of mixed maturity in follicular cervicitis. Signet-ring cells are a different finding linked to IUDs. Giant cells with molded nuclei suggest herpes, not a lymphoid follicle pattern."}, {"id": 1729, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A trauma patient is bleeding heavily and the physician orders blood before any sample has been tested. The transfusion service issues uncrossmatched red cells under its emergency policy. What must the physician do, and what does the laboratory do afterward?", "options": {"A": "Sign a document accepting responsibility, and the laboratory completes the crossmatch afterward", "B": "Notify the blood supplier, which then completes the crossmatch on the physician's behalf afterward", "C": "Sign a form that waives all testing, and the laboratory discards the sample afterward", "D": "Wait for the antibody screen result, and the laboratory completes ABO typing afterward"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sign a document accepting responsibility, and the laboratory completes the crossmatch afterward): Emergency release of uncrossmatched blood is allowed under a policy requiring the physician to sign that responsibility is accepted, and the crossmatch and other testing are completed afterward. Testing is not waived or delegated to the supplier. Waiting for the screen defeats the purpose of emergency issue."}, {"id": 1730, "domain": "histology", "question": "During cutting, sections curl and roll up as they leave the blade, and the technologist notices dry room air and a lot of static. The blade is sharp and the clearance angle is normal. What is the most sensible adjustment for this problem?", "options": {"A": "Reduce static by raising humidity or gently breathing on it", "B": "Warm the block face until the wax becomes very soft", "C": "Increase the section thickness to the maximum on the dial", "D": "Change the eosin so the section is easier to handle"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduce static by raising humidity or gently breathing on it): Curling from static electricity is helped by higher humidity or a gentle breath. Thicker sections do not stop static, and a very soft block causes compression. Eosin is applied later and has no effect on cutting."}, {"id": 1731, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A hematologist plans to treat a child with sickle cell disease who has had a stroke. The plan includes an apheresis procedure that replaces the abnormal cells with normal donor red cells. What is this procedure called? Her hemoglobin S level is 38 percent after her last simple transfusion.", "options": {"A": "Leukoreduction, which removes white cells from the child's blood.", "B": "Red cell exchange, which replaces sickled cells with normal donor red cells.", "C": "Therapeutic plasma exchange, which replaces the plasma with albumin only.", "D": "Extracorporeal photopheresis, which treats white cells with light exposure."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Red cell exchange, which replaces sickled cells with normal donor red cells.): Red cell exchange replaces sickled red cells with normal donor cells and is used for stroke or severe crises in sickle cell disease. Plasma exchange removes plasma, and photopheresis targets white cells."}, {"id": 1732, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "An acetaminophen level is drawn 6 hours after a single reported ingestion and plots above the treatment line of the nomogram. It is late evening and the physician has not yet been informed. What should the laboratory do? The patient is drowsy but stable in the emergency department.", "options": {"A": "Hold the result until the morning review, as acetaminophen levels change only slowly overnight", "B": "Repeat the level in 24 hours before deciding whether to communicate the current result", "C": "Report it promptly, since the antidote N-acetylcysteine should be started", "D": "Send the sample for confirmation by mass spectrometry before any notification to the ward"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report it promptly, since the antidote N-acetylcysteine should be started): A result above the treatment line means N-acetylcysteine should be started, and delay risks liver failure. The laboratory should report promptly and record the reported time of ingestion. Confirmatory testing should not delay a communication of this critical result."}, {"id": 1733, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A student confuses the two antiglobulin tests. One incubates the patient's serum with reagent red cells to look for circulating antibody, while the other uses washed patient red cells directly. Which test detects antibody or complement already coating the patient's own red cells in the body?", "options": {"A": "The osmotic fragility test, using patient red cells suspended in graded saline concentrations", "B": "The eosin-5-maleimide test, using patient red cells labelled and read by flow cytometry", "C": "The indirect antiglobulin test, using patient serum and reagent red cells", "D": "The direct antiglobulin test, using washed patient red cells and anti-human globulin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The direct antiglobulin test, using washed patient red cells and anti-human globulin): The DAT tests washed patient red cells with anti-human globulin to find in vivo coating by IgG or C3d. The indirect test finds antibody free in serum. Osmotic fragility and eosin-5-maleimide binding are used for membrane defects, not for immune coating."}, {"id": 1734, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A shipment of reagent anti-A arrives at the transfusion service. Before placing a new lot in use, the technologist opens the vial and looks at the contents against a white background. The liquid appears cloudy and slightly discoloured, and the expiry date printed on the vial is still 8 months away. How should the technologist handle this lot of reagent?", "options": {"A": "Filter the reagent through a fine mesh and use it after controls pass", "B": "Use the vial after running daily controls, since expiry is far away", "C": "Dilute the reagent with saline until the liquid appears clear again", "D": "Do not use the vial, quarantine the lot, and contact the supplier"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Do not use the vial, quarantine the lot, and contact the supplier): Each new lot is inspected for turbidity, colour change, and expiry, and cloudiness or a colour change suggests contamination or deterioration. A distant expiry date does not offset a visible defect. Filtering or diluting would change the reagent's potency and cannot be validated."}, {"id": 1735, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A karyotype report lists a deletion that removed the tip of a chromosome arm, while a second report lists a deletion that removed a segment from the middle of an arm, leaving both ends intact. The reviewer asks which term applies to the second report. What is the correct term?", "options": {"A": "Interstitial deletion, because the missing segment lies within an arm.", "B": "Interstitial deletion, because the missing segment includes the chromosome tip.", "C": "Terminal deletion, because the missing segment lies within an arm.", "D": "Isochromosome, because the remaining arm is duplicated as a mirror image."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Interstitial deletion, because the missing segment lies within an arm.): A segment lost from within an arm, with both ends retained, is an interstitial deletion. Loss of the end of the chromosome is a terminal deletion. An isochromosome has two identical arms and is not a type of deletion."}, {"id": 1736, "domain": "hematology", "question": "Considering the timing of the platelet fall on heparin, the normal smear and the new calf swelling described in the case, what is the most likely mechanism behind the fall in the platelet count in this patient receiving unfractionated heparin after cardiac surgery?", "options": {"A": "Direct heparin toxicity to megakaryocytes that reduces platelet production in the marrow of the patient", "B": "EDTA-dependent platelet clumping that falsely lowers the automated platelet count", "C": "Antibodies against platelet factor 4 and heparin that activate platelets and promote clotting", "D": "Splenic pooling from portal hypertension that reduces circulating platelet numbers in the blood"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Antibodies against platelet factor 4 and heparin that activate platelets and promote clotting): A platelet fall of more than half starting five to ten days after heparin, with new thrombosis, fits heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, in which antibodies to platelet factor 4 and heparin complexes activate platelets. The smear excludes EDTA clumping, and marrow toxicity or splenic pooling would not explain the timing and thrombosis.", "cluster_id": "7", "cluster_text": "A 58-year-old man in a cardiac surgery unit has received unfractionated heparin since his operation. On day 7 his analyzer platelet count is 92 x10^9/L, down from 280 x10^9/L on day 1. The smear shows no platelet clumps or fibrin strands, the EDTA sample was collected properly, and the count was confirmed on a second analyzer. PT and aPTT are unremarkable apart from the expected heparin effect. He now has new swelling of the left calf, and the physician suspects a drug-related complication.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1737, "domain": "hematology", "question": "The surgical team asks the laboratory and the physician what to do for this patient immediately, before any confirmatory results are available. What is the most appropriate action for the physician to take at this point in the patient's care?", "options": {"A": "Continue heparin at a lower dose and recheck the platelet count daily", "B": "Give platelet transfusions to correct the count before any anticoagulant", "C": "Stop all heparin and start a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban", "D": "Switch to low molecular weight heparin, which does not cross-react with antibodies"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop all heparin and start a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban): When heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is strongly suspected, all heparin is stopped and a non-heparin anticoagulant is begun without waiting for results, because thrombosis risk is high. Lower dosing still exposes the patient, platelet transfusion adds to thrombotic risk, and low molecular weight heparin cross-reacts with the antibodies.", "cluster_id": "7", "cluster_text": "A 58-year-old man in a cardiac surgery unit has received unfractionated heparin since his operation. On day 7 his analyzer platelet count is 92 x10^9/L, down from 280 x10^9/L on day 1. The smear shows no platelet clumps or fibrin strands, the EDTA sample was collected properly, and the count was confirmed on a second analyzer. PT and aPTT are unremarkable apart from the expected heparin effect. He now has new swelling of the left calf, and the physician suspects a drug-related complication.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1738, "domain": "hematology", "question": "The physician now wants the laboratory to confirm the suspected drug-related complication and exclude other causes of the falling platelet count and the new calf swelling in this cardiac surgery patient. The sample was collected properly and the count was verified. Which laboratory approach best confirms the suspected diagnosis in this patient?", "options": {"A": "A bleeding time and ristocetin platelet aggregation study to assess platelet adhesion", "B": "A prothrombin time mixing study followed by a factor VIII assay of the plasma", "C": "A bone marrow aspirate to count megakaryocytes and assess platelet production", "D": "A PF4 antibody immunoassay, then a functional platelet activation assay if positive"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A PF4 antibody immunoassay, then a functional platelet activation assay if positive): Testing starts with an immunoassay for antibodies to platelet factor 4 and heparin, and a positive result is confirmed with a functional assay such as serotonin release, which is more specific. Bleeding time, ristocetin aggregation, mixing studies and marrow examination do not detect these antibodies.", "cluster_id": "7", "cluster_text": "A 58-year-old man in a cardiac surgery unit has received unfractionated heparin since his operation. On day 7 his analyzer platelet count is 92 x10^9/L, down from 280 x10^9/L on day 1. The smear shows no platelet clumps or fibrin strands, the EDTA sample was collected properly, and the count was confirmed on a second analyzer. PT and aPTT are unremarkable apart from the expected heparin effect. He now has new swelling of the left calf, and the physician suspects a drug-related complication.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1739, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A patient develops fever, shaking and shock during a platelet transfusion. The physician asks the technologist what type of reaction to suspect and how the implicated unit should be handled. Which reaction and laboratory action is correct in this case?", "options": {"A": "Delayed hemolytic reaction, with the unit discarded and the patient observed", "B": "Febrile non-hemolytic reaction, with the unit returned to inventory after mixing", "C": "Bacterial contamination, with the unit cultured and the patient treated as septic", "D": "Transfusion-associated circulatory overload, with the unit kept for use in another patient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Bacterial contamination, with the unit cultured and the patient treated as septic): Fever, shaking and shock during or right after a platelet transfusion suggest bacterial contamination, so the unit is cultured and the patient is treated as septic. A delayed hemolytic reaction would appear days later, and the unit is never returned to inventory. Overload would not typically produce shock."}, {"id": 1740, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A technologist finishes reviewing a Sanger trace and finds a single base change in the middle of a clean read. Before the finding goes into a report, which step is required so the variant is described correctly and can be understood by any other laboratory?", "options": {"A": "Report the base change alone, since the position is obvious from the trace", "B": "Compare the sequence with the reference and describe the variant in standard nomenclature", "C": "Use the laboratory's own shorthand for the change, as agreed among local staff and ordering physicians", "D": "Describe the variant using the peak number on the trace file as its position"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Compare the sequence with the reference and describe the variant in standard nomenclature): Results to be reported are checked against the reference sequence and the variant is written in standard nomenclature so that any reader interprets it identically. A peak number depends on the read, not the gene. Local shorthand cannot be interpreted elsewhere, and a base change without its position is incomplete."}, {"id": 1741, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist collects a fresh cervical specimen vial and later notices leaked fixative on the bench and a cracked cap on the vial. A colleague suggests screening the sample anyway. Which action best reflects safety and quality practice? The bench is shared with two colleagues.", "options": {"A": "Continue screening because fixative already preserved the cells, and decontaminate the leaked bench area afterward", "B": "Follow the documented policy for compromised specimens, use protective equipment, and contain the spill", "C": "Screen the slide anyway and wipe the bench without gloves later", "D": "Pour the remaining fluid down the sink and report the sample as lost"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Follow the documented policy for compromised specimens, use protective equipment, and contain the spill): A compromised vial is handled under the documented policy, with protective equipment worn and the spill contained, since fixatives and fresh specimens carry safety risks. Screening a possibly compromised sample risks quality. Ignoring the crack or discarding the fluid carelessly breaks safety rules."}, {"id": 1742, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A transplant program discusses kidney transplants across the ABO barrier. The team notes that some centres use a strategy involving the donor's blood group subtype. What approach is used in some centres for these transplants? Recipient anti-A titres are followed weekly before surgery.", "options": {"A": "Donors of a subgroup with less A antigen, or plasma exchange.", "B": "Giving the recipient group AB plasma to neutralize the incompatible antigen.", "C": "Using donors of group O only, and giving red cells daily.", "D": "Removing the donor kidney's plasma using an irradiation step."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Donors of a subgroup with less A antigen, or plasma exchange.): Some centres transplant kidneys from donors with a subgroup carrying less A antigen, or remove the recipient's anti-A or anti-B by plasma exchange, and the transfusion service monitors titres and supplies compatible products. Group AB plasma does not neutralize antigen on a transplanted organ, and daily red cell transfusion or irradiating donor plasma has no role in lowering anti-A effects."}, {"id": 1743, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A technologist is asked how albumin and total protein are usually measured in a routine chemistry analyzer, and which chemical principle each method depends on. The student needs both methods and their principles stated correctly. What is the right answer?", "options": {"A": "Dye binding for total protein, and biuret reaction with copper for albumin", "B": "Nitroprusside reaction for total protein, and hexokinase phosphorylation for albumin", "C": "Jaffe reaction for total protein, and diazo reaction with sulfanilic acid for albumin", "D": "Biuret reaction with copper and peptide bonds for total protein, and dye binding for albumin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Biuret reaction with copper and peptide bonds for total protein, and dye binding for albumin): Total protein is measured by the biuret reaction, where peptide bonds react with copper in alkali, and albumin by dye binding with bromocresol green or purple. Swapping the methods is wrong. Jaffe, diazo, nitroprusside and hexokinase measure creatinine, bilirubin, ketones and glucose."}, {"id": 1744, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A transfusion service manager is reviewing which component carries the highest risk of bacterial contamination and why. Among red cells, platelets, frozen plasma and cryoprecipitate, one is stored at room temperature and is therefore most likely to support bacterial growth. Which component is it?", "options": {"A": "Platelets, because they are stored at 20 to 24 °C", "B": "Cryoprecipitate, because it is pooled from several donors", "C": "Red cells, because they are stored at 1 to 6 °C for 42 days", "D": "Frozen plasma, because it is thawed before every issue"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Platelets, because they are stored at 20 to 24 °C): Platelets are held at 20 to 24 °C, which allows bacteria to multiply, so they carry the highest risk of bacterial contamination. Refrigerated red cells and frozen products are stored where growth is slowed. Pooling and thawing do not make those components the highest-risk products."}, {"id": 1745, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "After a carbapenemase gene is detected on a Klebsiella isolate, the provincial reference laboratory requests the isolate for typing. The bench technologist is deciding what to do with the culture plate at the end of the shift. What is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Store the plate on the open bench with the other routine cultures until the courier arrives", "B": "Discard the plate after reporting, since the identification is already complete and reported", "C": "Autoclave the plate at once and send only the printed report to the reference laboratory", "D": "Preserve the isolate under proper storage conditions and document chain of custody for the referral"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Preserve the isolate under proper storage conditions and document chain of custody for the referral): Isolates may be sent for typing to a provincial or national reference laboratory, so the MLT must preserve the isolate under proper storage and document chain of custody. Discarding or autoclaving would destroy the material the reference laboratory needs."}, {"id": 1746, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A marrow shows full cellularity, unexplained cytopenias in the patient's blood, hypogranular neutrophils with pseudo-Pelger-Huet nuclei, and 8% blasts on a 500-cell count. The technologist prepares the preliminary comment. Which interpretation and reporting approach is most consistent with these findings?", "options": {"A": "Report acute myeloid leukemia, since any blasts in the marrow confirm it", "B": "Report a reactive marrow, since hypogranular neutrophils appear in every infection", "C": "Describe dysplasia and the blast percentage and refer, since blasts below twenty percent suggest myelodysplasia", "D": "Report chronic myeloid leukemia, since pseudo-Pelger-Huet nuclei define this disease"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Describe dysplasia and the blast percentage and refer, since blasts below twenty percent suggest myelodysplasia): Dysplastic features with unexplained cytopenias and blasts below 20% suggest myelodysplastic syndrome, and the technologist describes findings and refers. AML generally needs 20% or more blasts. Infection causes toxic granulation rather than hypogranulation, and CML has a different picture."}, {"id": 1747, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Aspirate from a firm 2 cm parotid mass in a 44-year-old woman yields mucinous background material with three cell types: mucus-producing cells, intermediate cells and squamoid cells. The stroma is not fibrillar and no lymphocytic background is seen. Which is the most likely diagnosis to report?", "options": {"A": "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma, a malignant salivary tumour with mixed glandular, intermediate and squamous differentiation.", "B": "Sialadenitis, an inflammatory condition with acinar and ductal cells and inflammation.", "C": "Warthin tumour, a benign tumour with oncocytes and abundant lymphocytes.", "D": "Pleomorphic adenoma, a benign salivary tumour with abundant fibrillar chondromyxoid stroma and myoepithelial cell sheets."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mucoepidermoid carcinoma, a malignant salivary tumour with mixed glandular, intermediate and squamous differentiation.): Mucus-producing, intermediate and squamoid cells together define mucoepidermoid carcinoma. Pleomorphic adenoma requires the fibrillar chondromyxoid stroma, which is absent. Sialadenitis lacks a tumour cell mixture, and Warthin tumour needs oncocytes and lymphocytes."}, {"id": 1748, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "An obstetrician needs a rapid answer on the common aneuploidies from an urgent amniocentesis, before the family can decide on next steps. The laboratory offers interphase FISH on uncultured amniotic cells and standard karyotype on cultured cells. Which combination best describes what each provides?", "options": {"A": "FISH reports chromosomes 13, 18, 21, X, and Y in days; karyotype takes longer", "B": "Karyotype gives the fastest answer because culture is complete in a few hours", "C": "FISH gives a full banded karyotype in one to two days, replacing culture", "D": "FISH detects every structural rearrangement on all chromosomes within one day"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (FISH reports chromosomes 13, 18, 21, X, and Y in days; karyotype takes longer): Interphase FISH on uncultured cells targets selected chromosomes and returns quickly, while culture-based karyotype takes many days but gives a whole-genome view. FISH does not produce banding or detect all structural changes, and cell culture is never complete within hours."}, {"id": 1749, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient who had a right-sided mastectomy two years ago presents for a chemistry panel. Both arms appear normal on inspection, with no bruising, no fistula and no scarring near the elbows. The requisition has no arm restriction noted. From which arm should the collector draw the blood?", "options": {"A": "Either arm, since lymph drainage does not change blood sample quality", "B": "The right arm, because the tissue there heals more slowly if punctured", "C": "The left arm, on the side opposite the mastectomy", "D": "The right hand, because hand veins avoid the surgical region altogether"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The left arm, on the side opposite the mastectomy): An arm on the side of a mastectomy is avoided because lymph drainage may be impaired and puncture can cause swelling or infection. The opposite arm is used. Hand veins on the same side share the same concern. Lymph disruption does not necessarily alter the sample, but the risk to the patient makes the arm a site to avoid."}, {"id": 1750, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A student reviewing the urea method on a routine analyzer asks which coupled enzyme system is used and which change in absorbance is followed to calculate the result. The instructor writes out the reaction steps, including the cofactor involved and the measured wavelength. Which reaction scheme does the instructor write?", "options": {"A": "Alkaline picrate forms an orange compound with urea, read as a rise at 490 nm", "B": "Urease produces ammonium, then glutamate dehydrogenase consumes NADH, read as a fall at 340 nm", "C": "Hexokinase produces urea, then peroxidase makes a coloured dye, read as a rise at 500 nm", "D": "Urease produces ammonium, then glutamate dehydrogenase makes NADPH, read as a rise at 560 nm"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Urease produces ammonium, then glutamate dehydrogenase consumes NADH, read as a fall at 340 nm): Urease releases ammonium, and glutamate dehydrogenase uses NADH, so the fall in absorbance at 340 nm is followed. NADPH production at 560 nm is incorrect. Hexokinase acts on glucose, and alkaline picrate is the Jaffe reagent for creatinine."}, {"id": 1751, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A molecular technologist wants a further decontamination step for the pre-PCR hood at the end of the day, after the work surfaces have been cleaned with dilute bleach and rinsed. The hood has an installed lamp above the work area. Which additional measure helps break down contaminating nucleic acid?", "options": {"A": "Ultraviolet light exposure inside the closed hood, which damages contaminating nucleic acid.", "B": "Wiping with 95% ethanol alone, which destroys nucleic acid faster than bleach does.", "C": "Heating the hood interior above 60 °C, which denatures contaminating DNA into harmless pieces.", "D": "Turning off airflow for an hour, so aerosols settle onto the removable tray."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ultraviolet light exposure inside the closed hood, which damages contaminating nucleic acid.): Ultraviolet light in a hood adds decontamination by damaging nucleic acid, and it is used after cleaning. Heating a hood and pausing airflow are not recognized steps, and ethanol alone does not destroy nucleic acid the way an oxidizing bleach solution does."}, {"id": 1752, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A slide from a woman treated with pelvic radiotherapy last year shows large cells with vacuoles and smudged nuclei. The cytoplasm within single cells stains both blue and red. The reviewing technologist wants to name this staining feature. What is it, and what does it suggest?", "options": {"A": "Metachromasia, a purple colour shift seen in mast cell degranulation", "B": "Polychromasia, a mix of colours in one cell seen in radiation change", "C": "Eosinophilia, a uniform pink cytoplasm seen in keratinizing superficial cells", "D": "Cyanophilia, a uniform blue-green cytoplasm seen in parabasal and intermediate cells"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Polychromasia, a mix of colours in one cell seen in radiation change): Polychromasia describes blue and red staining in the same cell and is a feature of radiation change. Uniform pink or blue-green cytoplasm is a normal staining trait of squamous cells. Metachromasia is not a cytology feature of radiation change."}, {"id": 1753, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient's anemia and reticulocyte results are shown in the image. The physician asks for the reticulocyte percentage corrected for the degree of anemia, using a normal hematocrit of 0.45 L/L. What corrected value should the technologist report to the physician?", "options": {"A": "6.7 percent, from multiplying the reticulocyte percent by 0.45 divided by 0.27", "B": "2.4 percent, from multiplying the reticulocyte percent by 0.27 divided by 0.45", "C": "4.0 percent, since the observed value already accounts for the anemia", "D": "1.1 percent, from multiplying the reticulocyte percent by the hematocrit alone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (2.4 percent, from multiplying the reticulocyte percent by 0.27 divided by 0.45): The corrected reticulocyte percentage is the observed percent times patient hematocrit over normal hematocrit: 4.0 x 0.27 / 0.45 = 2.4 percent. Inverting the ratio overstates the response, leaving the value uncorrected ignores the lowered red cell mass, and using the hematocrit alone omits the normal value.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Anemia and reticulocyte count", "columns": ["Test", "Result", "Units", "Reference interval", "Flag"], "rows": [["Hemoglobin", "80", "g/L", "120 to 160", "L"], ["Hematocrit", "0.27", "L/L", "0.36 to 0.46", "L"], ["Reticulocytes", "4.0", "%", "0.5 to 2.0", "H"], ["RBC", "3.1", "x10^12/L", "4.0 to 5.2", "L"]], "flag_col": 4, "caption": ""}, "image": "question_images/q_1753.png"}, {"id": 1754, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A screener has four uncertain cells on a liquid-based slide and wants a consistent method. Which set of cell features should she compare systematically, rather than trusting a general impression, to decide whether a suspicious cell is benign or abnormal?", "options": {"A": "Cytoplasmic colour, the number of neutrophils, slide thickness, and the printed label on the stain bottle", "B": "Cell height, mucus quantity, the clinic name, and the date the specimen was received", "C": "Nuclear size compared with an intermediate nucleus, chromatin quality, membrane contour, and nucleoli", "D": "Patient age, total cell count, cytoplasmic vacuoles, and a clean background"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Nuclear size compared with an intermediate nucleus, chromatin quality, membrane contour, and nucleoli): The most useful features are nuclear size against an intermediate nucleus, N:C ratio, chromatin, membrane contour, and nucleoli, along with the background. The other lists mix administrative details or minor features that do not separate benign from abnormal."}, {"id": 1755, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist receives a telephone call giving a critical result for a patient, and needs to relay a positive cerebrospinal fluid Gram stain to a physician on a busy ward. The physician is reading the result aloud quickly. Which step best completes the professional communication?", "options": {"A": "Hang up after the physician says thanks, since the result is already in the system", "B": "Ask the physician to confirm the ward name only, since that is faster", "C": "Have the recipient read back the patient name, identifier and result, then record time and recipient", "D": "Leave the result with a ward clerk, who will pass it on at rounds"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Have the recipient read back the patient name, identifier and result, then record time and recipient): A critical value must be phoned to the responsible clinician, who reads back the patient name, identifier and result, and the time and recipient are recorded. This prevents identification errors and documents responsibility. Leaving messages with a clerk delays urgent care."}, {"id": 1756, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "Two ways of labelling FISH probes are compared in a training session. One method attaches a fluorescent dye directly to the probe DNA. The other attaches a hapten that must be detected afterward with a fluorescent antibody, adding extra steps. Which statement describes why direct labelling is now standard for commercial clinical probes?", "options": {"A": "Direct labelling is faster because no antibody detection steps are needed after washing", "B": "Direct labelling removes the need for controls because the dye cannot bind wrongly", "C": "Direct labelling avoids the need for denaturation, so hybridization can run at room temperature", "D": "Direct labelling gives brighter signal because the antibody amplifies each probe molecule"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Direct labelling is faster because no antibody detection steps are needed after washing): With a dye already on the probe, the slide can be viewed straight after washing, saving time. Amplification by antibody belongs to indirect methods. Denaturation is still needed for any probe, and every FISH run still needs controls."}, {"id": 1757, "domain": "histology", "question": "A protein epitope is masked in an old block that was cut two years after embedding, and heat retrieval in citrate buffer is unsuitable for this particular delicate antigen. The validated procedure uses an enzyme instead. Which retrieval method does this describe?", "options": {"A": "Peroxidase-induced epitope retrieval using hydrogen peroxide as the unmasking reagent", "B": "Heat-induced epitope retrieval using citrate buffer at a set temperature", "C": "Chromogenic epitope retrieval using DAB at elevated pH after the blocking step", "D": "Proteolytic-induced epitope retrieval using an enzyme such as protease or trypsin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Proteolytic-induced epitope retrieval using an enzyme such as protease or trypsin): PIER uses an enzyme such as protease or trypsin to partly digest tissue and unmask epitopes. HIER uses heat and buffer, and hydrogen peroxide is used to block endogenous peroxidase, not to retrieve antigen."}, {"id": 1758, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "An NGS panel reports a likely pathogenic variant in a patient's BRCA2 gene. Before the result is released, the laboratory procedure calls for an independent method to confirm the variant on a fresh amplification. Which method remains widely used for this confirmation step?", "options": {"A": "Microarray analysis of the extracted DNA to verify the single base change", "B": "Flow cytometry of a fresh blood sample to check the protein level", "C": "Sanger sequencing of a freshly amplified region covering the variant", "D": "Repeating the same library on the same sequencer without any new amplification"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sanger sequencing of a freshly amplified region covering the variant): Sanger sequencing is still widely used to confirm variants found by other methods, since it reads a short region with very high accuracy. Rerunning the same library repeats the same systematic errors. A microarray is not designed to verify a single base change, and flow cytometry does not read DNA sequence."}, {"id": 1759, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A physician asks why an erythrocyte sedimentation rate came back very low in a patient with an active inflammatory illness and a very high fibrinogen. The blood film was reviewed, and the technologist looks at the red cell shapes. Which finding would explain a falsely low ESR?", "options": {"A": "Elevated immunoglobulins in myeloma, because they promote red cell stacking", "B": "Sickle cells, because cells with abnormal shape cannot stack into rouleaux", "C": "Raised fibrinogen in acute inflammation, because it promotes rouleaux formation", "D": "Anemia, because fewer red cells mean the column falls more slowly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sickle cells, because cells with abnormal shape cannot stack into rouleaux): Poikilocytes such as sickle cells, and spherocytes or polycythemia, lower the ESR because red cells cannot form rouleaux. Fibrinogen and immunoglobulins raise it by promoting stacking. Anemia also raises it. Those three cannot explain a low value in this patient."}, {"id": 1760, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A cardiologist wants to test a family with hereditary cardiomyopathy, a condition linked to many possible genes, and asks the laboratory which type of test scope is most appropriate as a first approach. Which recommendation fits? The director wants a clear recommendation for the family.", "options": {"A": "Single-gene Sanger sequencing of the most common gene, which excludes the whole condition if normal", "B": "A tumour panel run on blood, since the same genes underlie both conditions", "C": "A cardiomyopathy gene panel, which tests the genes linked to the condition together", "D": "Whole-genome sequencing only, since panels cannot cover more than one gene"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A cardiomyopathy gene panel, which tests the genes linked to the condition together): A gene panel targets the set of genes linked to a condition and gives focused, deep coverage. Testing one gene cannot exclude a condition caused by many genes. Panels do cover multiple genes, and a tumour panel is designed for different genes."}, {"id": 1761, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In a laboratory quality committee, A quality committee reviews the proportion of specimens reported as unsatisfactory, alongside other indicators such as the atypical squamous cell rate and positive predictive value of abnormal calls. What is the main purpose of tracking these indicators together?", "options": {"A": "To replace individual screener performance monitoring with group averages", "B": "To show that false negative results have been eliminated from the screening programme", "C": "To decide which patients need genotyping after a positive test", "D": "To monitor laboratory performance and detect drift that needs review"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (To monitor laboratory performance and detect drift that needs review): Programs track false negative and false positive proportions, unsatisfactory rates, atypical-to-lesion ratios and positive predictive value so that drifting performance is noticed and reviewed. False negatives cannot be fully removed, individual monitoring continues in addition, and genotyping decisions rely on patient results rather than laboratory indicators. Reviewing the indicators together lets the team notice drift before it harms patients."}, {"id": 1762, "domain": "histology", "question": "A trichrome run shows collagen and muscle both stained a similar muddy red, with poor colour separation. The controls were processed at the same time, and the Weigert hematoxylin step gave crisp black nuclei. Which step is the most likely cause that should be reviewed first?", "options": {"A": "The temperature of the tap water used for the final rinse after the Weigert hematoxylin step", "B": "The pH of the Alcian blue solution used in the previous run", "C": "The timing of the differentiation step in phosphotungstic or phosphomolybdic acid solution", "D": "The thickness of the polarizing filters on the microscope"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The timing of the differentiation step in phosphotungstic or phosphomolybdic acid solution): Phosphotungstic or phosphomolybdic acid differentiation decides whether collagen and muscle colours separate cleanly, so timing there is checked first. Alcian blue pH and polarizers are unrelated to trichrome."}, {"id": 1763, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A morning QC check shows that one drug's control zone is out of range. Earlier in the day, three patient isolates were already reported for that drug after the previous day's acceptable QC. The supervisor asks the technologist to decide how to handle the drug and the earlier reports. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Ignore earlier results, since they were released when the previous QC run was acceptable", "B": "Widen the published acceptable range to encompass the new zone diameter and continue reporting the antibiotic", "C": "Discard the QC strain and report the earlier results as confirmed by default", "D": "Review affected patient results, document corrective action, and issue amended reports if categories changed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Review affected patient results, document corrective action, and issue amended reports if categories changed): An out-of-range control means the cause must be found, corrective action documented and QC repeated before further results for that drug are reported. Patient results possibly affected are reviewed, and an amended report is issued if a category changed. Ranges are published and cannot be widened locally, and discarding the strain does not confirm anything."}, {"id": 1764, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A physician orders cerebrospinal fluid cytology for a patient with known breast cancer and new neurological symptoms. The trainee is asked about the main purpose of this test. Which is the main reason cytology is performed on this specimen type?", "options": {"A": "To identify the Papanicolaou-stained cells of the normal cervical transformation zone", "B": "To measure glucose and protein values that separate bacterial from viral meningitis", "C": "To count the alveolar macrophages that prove a deep-lung specimen for adequacy", "D": "To detect malignant cells from leptomeningeal spread, lymphoma or leukaemia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (To detect malignant cells from leptomeningeal spread, lymphoma or leukaemia): Cerebrospinal fluid cytology looks for malignant cells from leptomeningeal spread, lymphoma or leukaemia. Glucose and protein are chemistry tests. Cervical cells and alveolar macrophages are irrelevant to cerebrospinal fluid."}, {"id": 1765, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Screening an air-dried smear from an enlarged axillary node, a technologist sees small pale-blue fragments of cytoplasm scattered around the cells in the background. The pathologist asks what these fragments are called and what they generally suggest. What is the correct answer?", "options": {"A": "Lymphoglandular bodies, which are cytoplasmic fragments from lymphoid cells and support a lymphoid population.", "B": "Psammoma bodies, which indicate calcified concentric layers in papillary tumours such as thyroid or ovarian carcinoma.", "C": "Tingible-body macrophages, which indicate nuclear debris in a reactive germinal centre.", "D": "Crush artifact streaks, which indicate fragile small cell carcinoma nuclei."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lymphoglandular bodies, which are cytoplasmic fragments from lymphoid cells and support a lymphoid population.): Lymphoglandular bodies are small cytoplasmic fragments in the background of lymphoid smears and help confirm that the population is lymphoid. Tingible-body macrophages are whole large cells, psammoma bodies are calcified, and crush artifact streaks are smeared nuclei. Whole cells with debris or calcified layers would look different from small pale fragments."}, {"id": 1766, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A gram-negative rod from a urine culture is inoculated into urea broth, and the phenol red indicator turns pink within a few hours. The technologist must interpret this result. Which conclusion is best supported by the change? The organism was isolated from a urine culture, and the result was noted within a few hours instead of the usual overnight wait.", "options": {"A": "The organism lacks urease, leaving the pH unchanged, and is likely Escherichia coli", "B": "The organism produces indole, raising the pH, and is likely Klebsiella", "C": "The organism splits urea into ammonia, raising the pH, and is likely Proteus", "D": "The organism ferments lactose to acid, lowering the pH, and is likely Escherichia coli"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The organism splits urea into ammonia, raising the pH, and is likely Proteus): Urease splits urea into ammonia, which raises the pH and turns phenol red pink. Rapid positive results are typical of Proteus. Lactose fermentation lowers the pH, indole uses a different test, and a urease-negative organism leaves the medium unchanged."}, {"id": 1767, "domain": "hematology", "question": "After a bedside marrow collection, the technologist notices that two labelled slides carry only a handwritten first name and no second identifier. The patient has left the procedure room, and the marrow sample cannot easily be recollected. How should the technologist handle these slides?", "options": {"A": "Discard the slides quietly and tell no one about the labelling error", "B": "Report the labelling error through the incident system and follow policy on the irreplaceable specimen", "C": "Relabel the slides from memory and send them to the pathologist", "D": "Send the slides with a verbal note and skip any written documentation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the labelling error through the incident system and follow policy on the irreplaceable specimen): Marrow specimens are irreplaceable, and poor labelling is a serious event reported through the incident system, with the specimen handled per policy. Relabelling from memory, silent discarding, or verbal-only notes break identification and documentation rules."}, {"id": 1768, "domain": "histology", "question": "The surgical coordinator asks the laboratory about expected timing for routine intraoperative consultations. A laboratory has set a target for how quickly frozen section results should be available after a specimen arrives. Which target is a common benchmark, though it varies by institution?", "options": {"A": "About 2 hours from specimen arrival, matching the usual target for routine biopsies each day", "B": "About 5 working days from specimen arrival, matching the routine final report target", "C": "About 24 hours from specimen arrival, matching the overnight processing schedule used for biopsies", "D": "About 20 minutes from specimen arrival, though this varies by institution and block number"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 20 minutes from specimen arrival, though this varies by institution and block number): A common target is a result within about 20 minutes of arrival, though this varies with the institution and the number of blocks. Hours or days are targets for routine paraffin work, not intraoperative consultation."}, {"id": 1769, "domain": "histology", "question": "A run of slides shows pale, weak nuclei and the eosin looks normal. The hematoxylin was used for many days without replacement, and differentiation times were unchanged. What is the most probable cause, and what is the correct corrective step?", "options": {"A": "Bubbles under the coverslip, so remount with more medium", "B": "Excess bluing, so increase the acid alcohol rinse time", "C": "Exhausted hematoxylin, so refresh it and stain again", "D": "Dull blade chatter, so replace the blade on the microtome"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Exhausted hematoxylin, so refresh it and stain again): Pale nuclei suggest exhausted hematoxylin, too short a stain time or over-differentiation. Replacing the reagent and restaining fixes the cause. Bubbles and chatter affect other features, and excess bluing does not weaken nuclei."}, {"id": 1770, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A clinician asks why a cutoff for a FISH probe cannot simply be set at zero percent abnormal cells, since a normal person should show no abnormal nuclei. The laboratory has scored normal control samples for a locus-specific probe during validation. What does the cutoff actually represent?", "options": {"A": "The temperature at which the probe separates from its complementary target", "B": "A percentage of abnormal nuclei above which a specimen is called positive, set from normal controls", "C": "The number of hybridization hours needed before a slide can be scored by the technologist", "D": "The largest number of nuclei a technologist is permitted to score on each patient case"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A percentage of abnormal nuclei above which a specimen is called positive, set from normal controls): Normal specimens show some artifactual abnormal-looking nuclei, so the cutoff is set from control data, often mean plus three standard deviations. It is not a time, a temperature, or a limit on nuclei counted."}, {"id": 1771, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A hospital laboratory describes to new staff which patients may have screening swabs taken for MRSA on admission. The technologist explains the sampling sites used. Which sites are used for MRSA screening, according to this chapter? The list will be added to the admission screening policy.", "options": {"A": "Nares and groin swabs collected on admission of high-risk patients", "B": "Throat and ear swabs collected from every patient on the ward", "C": "Rectal and urine specimens collected after the patient is discharged", "D": "Blood and cerebrospinal fluid collected when infection is suspected"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Nares and groin swabs collected on admission of high-risk patients): MRSA screening uses nares and groin swabs, while rectal swabs are used for VRE and CPO screening. Blood and cerebrospinal fluid are diagnostic specimens, not screening sites, and throat and ear swabs are not the standard sites."}, {"id": 1772, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A solid thyroid nodule was aspirated and the smears contain mostly blood with only a handful of small follicular cell groups, well below the adequacy standard. No colloid or inflammation is seen. The pathologist assigns the nondiagnostic category. What is the usual next step for this category?", "options": {"A": "Routine clinical follow-up alone, since the malignancy risk is under 5 percent", "B": "Diagnostic lobectomy, since this category carries a malignancy risk of roughly 25 to 40 percent", "C": "Report benign because scant cells suggest an inactive colloid nodule", "D": "Repeat FNA under ultrasound guidance, since nondiagnostic carries a risk of roughly 5 to 10 percent"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeat FNA under ultrasound guidance, since nondiagnostic carries a risk of roughly 5 to 10 percent): A nondiagnostic aspirate usually leads to repeat FNA with ultrasound guidance, and the risk is roughly 5 to 10 percent. Lobectomy with 25 to 40 percent risk belongs to follicular neoplasm. Scant cells do not permit a benign call. Follow-up with under 5 percent risk describes the benign category."}, {"id": 1773, "domain": "hematology", "question": "Two consecutive normal-level MCV controls are plotted at 2.3 and 2.5 standard deviations above the mean, on the same side of the mean. The low and high level controls are within 1 standard deviation. No single result lies beyond 3 standard deviations. What is the correct response?", "options": {"A": "Accept the run since neither point exceeds 3 standard deviations", "B": "Reject the run by the 2-2s rule, hold patient results and troubleshoot", "C": "Recalibrate and release the patient results before repeating any controls", "D": "Accept the run and average the two points into one value"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reject the run by the 2-2s rule, hold patient results and troubleshoot): Two consecutive results beyond 2 standard deviations on the same side break a Westgard rule, so the run is rejected even though neither point exceeds 3 SD. Patient results are held, the cause is corrected and controls and affected samples are repeated. Averaging hides the violation, and releasing before verification risks reporting wrong results."}, {"id": 1774, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "During validation of an in-house qualitative assay, dilutions of a reference material are tested in replicates of twenty. The lowest concentration at which the target is detected in at least 95 percent of replicates is identified. Which performance characteristic has been established?", "options": {"A": "Analytical specificity, meaning absence of cross-reactivity with related organisms.", "B": "Accuracy, meaning agreement with a recognized reference method.", "C": "Precision, meaning agreement of repeat results between days and operators.", "D": "Analytical sensitivity expressed as the limit of detection of the assay."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Analytical sensitivity expressed as the limit of detection of the assay.): The lowest concentration reliably detected defines the limit of detection, which expresses analytical sensitivity. Specificity would be studied with related organisms and interferents. Precision requires repeat measurements over time and operators, and accuracy requires comparison with a reference method. None of those is established by a dilution series to detection failure."}, {"id": 1775, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "While reading a Kirby-Bauer plate, a technologist sees a few small colonies growing well inside the clear zone around one disk. The remainder of the zone is clear, and the isolate was identified as a single species. What is the best action before reporting that drug?", "options": {"A": "Investigate for a mixed culture or emerging resistance, and repeat the test from a pure subculture", "B": "Report the drug as susceptible, since the zone diameter is large enough to be acceptable", "C": "Ignore the inner colonies and measure the outer edge of the clear zone for reporting", "D": "Report the drug as resistant and skip repeat testing, since colonies in the zone indicate resistance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Investigate for a mixed culture or emerging resistance, and repeat the test from a pure subculture): Colonies inside a zone can signal a mixed culture or emerging resistance and should never be ignored. Repeating from a pure subculture clarifies which applies. Reporting without checking could give a false susceptible or false resistant result."}, {"id": 1776, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cytology laboratory adopts a new molecular assay for cell blocks, and a technologist who has not yet been trained on the method is asked by a busy colleague to run and sign out a batch. What is the most appropriate professional response to this request from a colleague?", "options": {"A": "Sign out the batch without running it, since the assay is automated anyway", "B": "Run the batch carefully and rely on the pathologist to catch any mistakes later", "C": "Run the batch after reading the manufacturer's leaflet, then sign the results at once", "D": "Decline to sign out work outside their competency, and ask for training first"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to sign out work outside their competency, and ask for training first): Working within scope and competence is a core professional duty, so the technologist asks for training before performing or signing out a new method. Relying on the pathologist or a leaflet does not replace competency. Signing work one is not competent to perform can be professional misconduct."}, {"id": 1777, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A 6-year-old has a paranasal mass, and FNA smears show small round cells and a few elongated cells with tail-like cytoplasm, while the operator is still sampling. Extra material is needed because the cell block will be used for immunostaining. Which cell type and marker favour rhabdomyosarcoma?", "options": {"A": "Lymphoglandular bodies with a monotonous population on flow cytometry results.", "B": "Glycogen-rich clear cytoplasm with positivity for CD99 on the cell block.", "C": "Strap cells on smears with positivity for the nuclear marker myogenin.", "D": "Rosettes in a fibrillar background with positivity for neuroendocrine markers."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Strap cells on smears with positivity for the nuclear marker myogenin.): Strap cells with myogenin positivity favour rhabdomyosarcoma. Rosettes in a fibrillar background belong to neuroblastoma, and glycogen-rich cells that are CD99 positive belong to Ewing sarcoma. Lymphoglandular bodies with a monotonous population suggest lymphoma."}, {"id": 1778, "domain": "cytology", "question": "During an external audit, an inspector asks a cytology laboratory to produce the records for a case reported several years ago so that the cytohistologic correlation can be reviewed. The manager explains how long documentation is kept. Which statement matches the chapter's guidance on keeping quality and case records?", "options": {"A": "Records are kept for the period set by the regulator or laboratory policy", "B": "Records are kept until the next proficiency test has been graded and filed", "C": "Records are discarded once the report is signed out and sent to the clinician", "D": "Records are kept for one week, since the pathologist holds the permanent copy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Records are kept for the period set by the regulator or laboratory policy): Documentation must be retained for the period set by the regulator or laboratory policy so past cases can be reviewed. Discarding records at sign-out or after a test removes the evidence an audit needs. A fixed one-week period is not a standard, and the technologist follows the local requirement."}, {"id": 1779, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A public health nurse asks the laboratory whether vaccinated patients still need cervical screening, since human papillomavirus vaccination is offered in school programs across Canada. The laboratory manager gives the standard answer about screening and the coverage of the vaccine.", "options": {"A": "Yes, but only until the age of twenty-five when protection is complete", "B": "No, vaccinated people only need screening if they develop symptoms", "C": "Yes, vaccinated people still need screening because the vaccine does not cover every high-risk type", "D": "No, vaccinated people are protected from cervical cancer and can stop screening after vaccination"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Yes, vaccinated people still need screening because the vaccine does not cover every high-risk type): The vaccine lowers high-grade lesions but does not protect against every high-risk type, so screening remains recommended for vaccinated people. Full protection and symptom-only screening are incorrect. Screening is not stopped at age 25."}, {"id": 1780, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A pathologist requests testing for a fusion in a solid tumour, and the laboratory has only a short read DNA panel based on depth analysis and single nucleotide calling. The technologist recommends an alternative for reliable fusion detection. Which method fits best?", "options": {"A": "Fluorometry of the extracted DNA, which detects the presence of a fusion product", "B": "RNA sequencing, or fluorescence in situ hybridization, which detect fusion events reliably", "C": "A microarray of germline DNA, which detects fusion events from the genome directly", "D": "Sanger sequencing of exon one, which detects fusions from any kind of tumour"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (RNA sequencing, or fluorescence in situ hybridization, which detect fusion events reliably): Fusions are found by RNA sequencing, fluorescence in situ hybridization, or RT-PCR. A short read panel built for single nucleotide variants and depth analysis may miss them. Sanger sequencing of one exon and fluorometry cannot detect a fusion, and a germline microarray does not target acquired tumour rearrangements."}, {"id": 1781, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A cytogenetics laboratory receives a new lot of culture medium, and the supervisor asks the technologist how to protect patient cultures from a defective batch. The lot has a valid certificate of analysis and the bottles are within date. Which quality practice is standard before the new lot is used routinely?", "options": {"A": "Test the new lot alongside the current lot using a known specimen before patient use", "B": "Skip testing if the lot number is printed, since manufacturers guarantee every bottle", "C": "Use it on a prenatal sample first, since amniotic cultures grow better than any other type", "D": "Freeze half the bottle for later use, since frozen medium supports better growth"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Test the new lot alongside the current lot using a known specimen before patient use): New lots are tested with a known specimen so problems are detected before patient use. Patient samples, especially irreplaceable prenatal ones, are not test material, and freezing medium does not improve it."}, {"id": 1782, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A laboratory receives a preserved vial from one manufacturer, but only the other manufacturer's processing instrument is available that day. A colleague suggests running the vial anyway to avoid delay. The manager knows the deadline is close, but the two vendors supply different vials for their own instruments. How should the manager handle the vial?", "options": {"A": "Run it, because a longer processing time can compensate for the different vial", "B": "Run it, because the instrument will automatically adjust to any vial that is loaded", "C": "Do not run it, because each manufacturer's vial is approved only for its own system", "D": "Run it, because all liquid-based vials contain the same preservative and behave alike"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Do not run it, because each manufacturer's vial is approved only for its own system): Each manufacturer has its own approved vial and the vial of one system must not be used in the machine of another. The preservatives and protocols differ, and instruments do not adapt on their own."}, {"id": 1783, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A technologist is asked to justify why a red cell unit that was out of storage for two hours without validated transport should not go back into general inventory. Which reason correctly reflects the chapter's teaching about returned units? Which reason is correct?", "options": {"A": "Red cell units cannot be reissued under any circumstance once they leave the laboratory", "B": "Red cell units lose their ABO antigens after two hours outside the refrigerator", "C": "Red cell units must be irradiated again whenever they return to storage", "D": "Its temperature history cannot be shown to be safe, so it is not returned to inventory"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Its temperature history cannot be shown to be safe, so it is not returned to inventory): A unit whose history cannot be shown to be safe must not go back to inventory, since bacterial growth and damage are possible. The rule is not an absolute ban, because units kept in a validated cooler within policy can be reissued. ABO antigens are not lost, and irradiation is unrelated."}, {"id": 1784, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A pediatric hospital laboratory reviews its case mix for childhood leukemia. A new technologist asks which leukemia is the most common cancer of childhood, made of small blasts with scant cytoplasm and no Auer rods or granules. Which disease is being described in this case example from the pediatric ward?", "options": {"A": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia, which is the most common childhood cancer", "B": "Acute promyelocytic leukemia, which is the most common childhood cancer overall", "C": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia, which is the most common leukemia in young children", "D": "Chronic myeloid leukemia, which is the most common leukemia in young children"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acute lymphoblastic leukemia, which is the most common childhood cancer): Acute lymphoblastic leukemia is the most common childhood cancer, with small blasts lacking granules and Auer rods. APL is uncommon, and the two chronic leukemias mainly affect adults and older adults, so they are not typical childhood diseases."}, {"id": 1785, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A catheter urine specimen grows 10^7 CFU/L of a single organism in a symptomatic patient. A colleague says the count is below the traditional midstream cutoff of 10^8 CFU/L and must be ignored. How should this be interpreted? The physician asked whether this is true infection.", "options": {"A": "The count is insignificant because it falls below the traditional midstream cutoff", "B": "The count proves the specimen was contaminated during collection", "C": "The count should be increased by repeating the incubation for another full day", "D": "Lower counts may be significant in a catheter specimen or a symptomatic patient"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lower counts may be significant in a catheter specimen or a symptomatic patient): The 10^8 CFU/L cutoff is traditional for midstream specimens, but lower counts may be significant in catheter samples, suprapubic aspirates, or symptomatic patients. It is not an absolute cutoff, and a single organism does not suggest contamination."}, {"id": 1786, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient's aPTT mixing study is corrected immediately but becomes prolonged after two hours of incubation at 37 degrees Celsius. The patient has no heparin exposure. Which interpretation fits this time and temperature dependent pattern? Interpretation guides which follow-up assays are ordered.", "options": {"A": "A specific factor inhibitor, such as an antibody against factor VIII", "B": "A lupus anticoagulant, which typically shows correction after incubation", "C": "A factor IX deficiency, which typically shows prolongation after incubation", "D": "A vitamin K deficiency, which typically shows prolongation after incubation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A specific factor inhibitor, such as an antibody against factor VIII): Specific factor inhibitors like anti-factor VIII often correct at first and prolong after incubation because they act in a time and temperature dependent way. Lupus anticoagulant shows immediate non-correction, and deficiencies correct."}, {"id": 1787, "domain": "histology", "question": "A researcher requests better preservation of nucleic acids from a tissue sample destined for molecular testing, and the pathologist agrees that the usual cross-linking fixative may not be the best choice. Which fixative type could the laboratory consider, following its validated protocols?", "options": {"A": "An alcohol-based fixative, which coagulates proteins and can preserve nucleic acids better.", "B": "Glutaraldehyde, which is the standard choice for light microscopy of routine biopsies.", "C": "Unbuffered formalin, which forms extra acid that protects nucleic acids from breaking down.", "D": "A picric acid mixture, which is chosen because it makes the tissue soft for cutting."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An alcohol-based fixative, which coagulates proteins and can preserve nucleic acids better.): Alcohol-based fixatives dehydrate and coagulate proteins without heavy cross-linking, so DNA and RNA are recovered more intact. Unbuffered formalin makes formic acid that fragments nucleic acids. Glutaraldehyde is reserved for electron microscopy, and picric acid mixtures such as Bouin are used for morphology, not to soften tissue or protect nucleic acids."}, {"id": 1788, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A 2-2s violation appears on a creatinine run at 08:00, and the last acceptable control was at 04:00. Patient creatinine results since 04:00 have been reported. The technologist finds that the calibrator vial was left uncapped overnight. What is the correct sequence of actions?", "options": {"A": "Stop testing, hold results, fix and recalibrate, verify with controls, re-run affected patients, document", "B": "Run new controls, and if they pass, release held results without re-running patients", "C": "Repeat controls until acceptable, then continue and document only the final passing values", "D": "Recalibrate the analyzer and continue testing, correcting earlier reported results if clinicians later question a value"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop testing, hold results, fix and recalibrate, verify with controls, re-run affected patients, document): A rejected run means stopping, correcting the cause, recalibrating, verifying with acceptable controls, and re-running patients affected since the last acceptable control, with the action documented. New controls passing does not fix earlier reported results. Repeating until a pass and waiting for complaints both leave possible errors uncorrected."}, {"id": 1789, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient has hemoglobin 70 g/L, reticulocytes 12 percent, indirect bilirubin raised, and LDH raised. The plasma appears pink, urine dipstick is positive for blood with no red cells seen microscopically, and urine iron staining shows hemosiderin. What type of hemolysis do these findings indicate?", "options": {"A": "Extravascular hemolysis, with destruction confined to macrophages of the spleen and liver", "B": "Blood loss into the intestine, with iron lost in the stool", "C": "Ineffective erythropoiesis, with precursors dying in the marrow before release", "D": "Intravascular hemolysis, with free hemoglobin released into plasma and urine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Intravascular hemolysis, with free hemoglobin released into plasma and urine): Free hemoglobin in plasma, hemoglobinuria and hemosiderinuria show red cells are lysing inside the vessels. Extravascular hemolysis does not put free hemoglobin into plasma or urine. Ineffective erythropoiesis lowers reticulocytes, and intestinal loss would not produce these hemolysis markers."}, {"id": 1790, "domain": "histology", "question": "A neuromuscular specimen has just arrived at the laboratory from the operating room. A muscle biopsy arrives and the technologist handles it roughly, squeezing it with forceps before freezing. The frozen sections show distorted fibres. Which handling practice would have prevented this artifact?", "options": {"A": "Soak the muscle in saline for an hour before it is frozen", "B": "Trim the muscle into thin slices with a scalpel before every freezing", "C": "Press the muscle flat with heavy forceps so that all the fibres align neatly", "D": "Keep the muscle slightly stretched and handle it gently, without squeezing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep the muscle slightly stretched and handle it gently, without squeezing): Muscle is kept slightly stretched and handled gently because squeezing or clamping creates artifacts. Pressing or heavy forceps make the distortion worse, and soaking in saline is not the recommended practice for these stains."}, {"id": 1791, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A cytomegalovirus viral load run for a transplant patient shows a level ten-fold higher than last week, and the laboratory policy classes this as a critical change. The ordering physician is off the ward. Which is the correct post-analytical step?", "options": {"A": "Post it to the electronic record and wait for the physician to view it.", "B": "Send it to the transplant patient's family so they can alert the doctor.", "C": "Withhold the result until a repeat determination on the next scheduled batch run confirms the increase", "D": "Communicate it promptly to the responsible provider per policy and document the notification."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Communicate it promptly to the responsible provider per policy and document the notification.): Critical results are escalated directly to a responsible provider within the required time, and the call, recipient and time are documented. Posting silently relies on someone noticing. Delaying for a repeat risks harm to an immunosuppressed patient. Releasing results to family bypasses the clinical team and privacy rules."}, {"id": 1792, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "An NGS report for a patient lists a deletion of six consecutive bases within an exon. A colleague assumes it must be a frameshift because bases were lost. Applying the rule for reading frames, how should the deletion be described?", "options": {"A": "Nonsense, since deleting six bases creates a premature stop codon", "B": "In-frame, since six is a multiple of three, removing two codons and preserving downstream sequence", "C": "Silent, since deletions within exons leave the amino acid sequence unchanged", "D": "Frameshift, since bases were removed and the downstream codons all change"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (In-frame, since six is a multiple of three, removing two codons and preserving downstream sequence): A loss of a multiple of three bases keeps the frame, so downstream codons are read unchanged, although amino acids are lost. A frameshift requires a number not divisible by three. A stop codon is not guaranteed, and losing codons changes the protein, so it is not silent."}, {"id": 1793, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A reference laboratory offers a test on maternal plasma to predict whether a fetus has inherited the RhD or another antigen. The obstetrician is interested because the test may avoid invasive sampling. What is this test called? Her sample was drawn at 16 weeks and her antibody screen is anti-D positive.", "options": {"A": "Maternal red cell genotyping, which predicts fetal antigen status directly.", "B": "Fetal genotyping from maternal plasma, which predicts fetal antigen status.", "C": "Fetal phenotyping from amniotic fluid, which requires needle sampling of the sac.", "D": "Cord blood typing, which is done at delivery and before birth."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fetal genotyping from maternal plasma, which predicts fetal antigen status.): Cell-free fetal DNA in maternal plasma can predict fetal antigen status without invasive procedures, and it is usually done by a reference laboratory or the blood supplier. Amniotic sampling is invasive, and cord blood testing takes place only after birth."}, {"id": 1794, "domain": "histology", "question": "A negative control in an IHC run shows staining, and background is high on every slide. The antibody was diluted according to the procedure and the slides were kept covered with reagent. Sections were cut at 4 µm, dried, and stained on one automated platform. Which cause should be investigated next?", "options": {"A": "An exhausted deparaffinization solution that left residual wax on the sections before antigen retrieval", "B": "Inadequate protein blocking, or unblocked endogenous peroxidase or biotin in the tissue", "C": "Use of a thinner section than the validated procedure calls for", "D": "Insufficient hematoxylin counterstain time after the DAB step"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Inadequate protein blocking, or unblocked endogenous peroxidase or biotin in the tissue): Non-specific binding, weak blocking, or unblocked endogenous peroxidase or biotin give high background even with a negative control. Counterstain time and wax affect appearance and weakness, not diffuse background."}, {"id": 1795, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cell block is prepared from a thyroid aspirate that looks like medullary carcinoma, because the tumour can imitate other neoplasms. The pathologist asks the laboratory to choose immunostains that support the diagnosis. Which immunostain result would be expected in this tumour?", "options": {"A": "Calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen, both expected to be positive in this tumour", "B": "Leukocyte common antigen, which is expected to be positive because the cells mimic lymphoma", "C": "Thyroglobulin, which is expected to be strongly positive because these cells make colloid", "D": "Cytokeratin 20 and CDX2, which are expected to be positive in medullary tumour cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen, both expected to be positive in this tumour): Medullary carcinoma arises from C cells and stains positive for calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen. Thyroglobulin marks follicular-derived tumours and is expected to be negative here. Cytokeratin 20 with CDX2 points toward a gastrointestinal origin. Leukocyte common antigen marks lymphoma and is negative in medullary carcinoma."}, {"id": 1796, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A neonatal serum sample for bilirubin was protected from light in an amber container and the technologist compares the result with an earlier sample that sat under bright light. The earlier result was lower. What does this comparison tell the technologist about the earlier sample?", "options": {"A": "Light had no effect on either sample, so the difference reflects true clinical change only", "B": "Light converted bilirubin to urobilinogen in the earlier sample, so its value is confirmed", "C": "Light concentrated bilirubin in the earlier sample, so its lower value was falsely high", "D": "Light degraded bilirubin in the earlier sample, so its lower value was falsely low"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Light degraded bilirubin in the earlier sample, so its lower value was falsely low): Bilirubin is degraded by light, so the exposed sample was falsely low and treatment thresholds could be missed. Light does not concentrate bilirubin, and urobilinogen forms in the intestine through bacterial action, not in the tube."}, {"id": 1797, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "Reviewing signal quality on a new FISH slide, the scorer notices a faint blue-white haze across the whole field, even in areas with no cells. The laboratory recently changed to a different bottle of immersion oil. What is the most likely source?", "options": {"A": "The DAPI has bound to the glass, which produces a blue haze in a valid slide", "B": "The probe lot is degraded, which produces haze only in empty regions of the slide", "C": "The new immersion oil autofluoresces and adds background under the fluorescence filters", "D": "The denaturation step ran too long and stripped the DNA from the slide"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The new immersion oil autofluoresces and adds background under the fluorescence filters): Some immersion oils fluoresce and add haze across the field, which is why oil must suit fluorescence work. Over-denaturation would affect nuclei, not empty areas. Probe degradation reduces signal, and DAPI binds DNA, not clean glass."}, {"id": 1798, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A trainee compares the two glandular cells in the image, which are drawn at equal magnification and share the same columnar shape. The supervisor asks the trainee to describe only the nuclear features that are visible, without naming a diagnosis. Which statement correctly contrasts Cell 1 with Cell 2 on those nuclear features?", "options": {"A": "Cell 1 has higher N:C ratio, darker chromatin and a rougher outline than Cell 2", "B": "Cell 2 has much higher N:C ratio, darker chromatin and a rougher outline than Cell 1", "C": "Cell 1 has a similar N:C ratio to Cell 2 but coarser chromatin and several nucleoli", "D": "Cell 2 has a similar N:C ratio to Cell 1 but smoother outline and paler chromatin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cell 1 has higher N:C ratio, darker chromatin and a rougher outline than Cell 2): Cell 1 has a nucleus filling a large part of the cell, with very dark chromatin and an irregular outline, whereas Cell 2 has a small, pale, smooth nucleus. Reversing the cells gives the opposite picture. The other two statements claim similar N:C ratios, which is not true, and describe nucleoli that neither cell shows.", "image_type": "cell_micrograph", "image_params": {"cells": [{"label": "1", "nc_ratio": 0.55, "chromatin": "hyperchromatic", "outline": "irregular", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "columnar"}, {"label": "2", "nc_ratio": 0.2, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "columnar"}], "caption": "Cells at equal magnification", "context": "endocervical", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1798.png"}, {"id": 1799, "domain": "cytology", "question": "On an air-dried Romanowsky-stained smear of a suspected lymphomatous effusion, the technologist sees small pale cytoplasmic fragments scattered among discohesive atypical lymphoid cells. A trainee asks what these fragments are called and why they matter. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Ferruginous bodies, which are typical of asbestos fibres in air-dried preparations", "B": "Psammoma bodies, which are typical of calcified layers in air-dried preparations", "C": "Lymphoglandular bodies, typical of lymphoid cells in air-dried preparations", "D": "Curschmann spirals, which are typical of coiled mucus in air-dried preparations"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lymphoglandular bodies, typical of lymphoid cells in air-dried preparations): Lymphoglandular bodies are small pale cytoplasmic fragments of lymphoid cells, best seen on air-dried Romanowsky stains. Curschmann spirals are mucus, ferruginous bodies are asbestos-linked rods, and psammoma bodies are layered calcified spheres, none of which describe lymphoid fragments."}, {"id": 1800, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Two patients with similar names have specimens in the same batch, and a technologist selects the wrong patient in the LIS while entering a result. The mistake is caught only after the report is released. Which safeguard would most directly have prevented this error?", "options": {"A": "A longer retention period for the laboratory's archived reports", "B": "Role-based access limiting the technologist to fewer patient records in the system", "C": "A longer retention period for the archived reports kept by the laboratory", "D": "Two-identifier checks at each step of data entry and release"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Two-identifier checks at each step of data entry and release): Similar names are a real source of harm, so two-identifier checks are used at every step. Role-based access, retention periods and audit trails help with security and review, but they do not stop the wrong patient from being selected at the moment of data entry. Security features protect records, but only identifier checks stop a wrong-patient selection at entry."}, {"id": 1801, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with diabetic ketoacidosis has a low pH and low bicarbonate, and the pCO2 is lower than normal. How should the low pCO2 be interpreted? The physician asks whether this result points to a second problem or to a normal response.", "options": {"A": "It is a second primary disorder, so supplemental oxygen alone should correct it", "B": "It is a sign of hypoventilation, because carbon dioxide levels fall when breathing slows", "C": "It is a sample error, because pCO2 should stay normal in metabolic acidosis", "D": "It is expected compensation, as faster breathing blows off carbon dioxide"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is expected compensation, as faster breathing blows off carbon dioxide): In metabolic acidosis, the lungs compensate by faster breathing, which lowers pCO2 and moves the pH back toward normal. A mixed disorder is suspected only when compensation is not the expected size. Falling pCO2 does not reflect hypoventilation."}, {"id": 1802, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist compares two IHC methods for a training manual on immunohistochemistry. In one, the primary antibody carries the label. In the other, an unlabelled primary antibody is followed by a labelled secondary antibody. What are these methods called, in order?", "options": {"A": "The direct method first, and then the indirect method second", "B": "The retrieval method first, and then the blocking method second", "C": "The indirect method first, and then the direct method second", "D": "The polymer method first, and then the biotin method second"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The direct method first, and then the indirect method second): When the primary antibody carries the label it is a direct method, and when a labelled secondary antibody binds an unlabelled primary it is indirect. Polymer and biotin describe detection chemistries, and retrieval and blocking are separate steps."}, {"id": 1803, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A patient's blood result is unclear, and a mosaic condition is suspected, so the physician asks the laboratory to culture a second tissue type. A trainee asks which specimen can be used to establish cultures of connective tissue cells in such a case, and how the cells are grown.", "options": {"A": "A saliva swab, which yields dividing fibroblasts without any tissue disaggregation step", "B": "A urine sample, which contains fibroblasts that divide readily in standard blood medium", "C": "A skin biopsy, which is used to grow a fibroblast culture for chromosome analysis", "D": "A hair sample, which grows a fibroblast culture when placed in heparin and mitogen"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A skin biopsy, which is used to grow a fibroblast culture for chromosome analysis): Skin biopsy tissue can be grown as fibroblast cultures when blood results are unclear or mosaicism is suspected. Urine, saliva and hair are not routine sources of dividing fibroblasts for karyotyping."}, {"id": 1804, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A term infant develops jaundice within hours of birth. The maternal and neonatal results in the table are available from the transfusion service, and the infant had no exchange transfusion or phototherapy before testing. Which cause of the infant's hemolysis is best supported by the table?", "options": {"A": "ABO hemolytic disease from maternal anti-A and anti-B crossing the placenta to the infant cells", "B": "Physiologic jaundice of the newborn, with the positive DAT being an incidental finding", "C": "G6PD deficiency in the infant, which causes hemolysis through oxidant damage to red cells", "D": "Maternal anti-D crossing the placenta and coating D-positive fetal red cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Maternal anti-D crossing the placenta and coating D-positive fetal red cells): The mother is D-negative with anti-D and the infant is D-positive with a positive DAT, anemia and early jaundice, which fits hemolytic disease caused by anti-D. Both are group O, so ABO disease is not possible, physiologic jaundice does not cause anemia with a positive DAT, and G6PD deficiency does not cause a positive DAT.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Mother and neonate results", "columns": ["Test", "Result", "Flag"], "rows": [["Mother ABO and D", "O D-negative", ""], ["Mother antibody identified", "Anti-D", "POS"], ["Neonate ABO and D", "O D-positive", ""], ["Neonate DAT (anti-IgG)", "POS", "POS"], ["Neonate hemoglobin (g/L)", "98", "L"], ["Neonate bilirubin (umol/L)", "205", "H"]], "flag_col": 2, "caption": "Mother and infant workup"}, "image": "question_images/q_1804.png"}, {"id": 1805, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A scheduled temperature verification shows that one block position of a thermal cycler reaches only 89 °C during the denaturation step, while other positions reach 94 °C. Patient runs were performed on this instrument since the last check. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Continue using it but avoid that block position for all future patient runs on the unit.", "B": "Wait for the next annual service visit before making any decision about its use.", "C": "Raise the denaturation set point for all runs and resume patient testing at once.", "D": "Take the instrument out of use, review affected earlier runs, and recalibrate or repair it."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take the instrument out of use, review affected earlier runs, and recalibrate or repair it.): Incomplete denaturation at one position can cause false negatives, so the instrument is taken out of service, results from affected wells since the last good check are reviewed, and the unit is repaired or recalibrated with documentation. Avoiding one position leaves a faulty instrument in use and ignores past runs. Changing set points without verification is uncontrolled, and waiting for annual service is too slow."}, {"id": 1806, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A lymph node biopsy from an older patient with painless enlarged nodes shows small cleaved lymphocytes, and the karyotype includes a translocation between the immunoglobulin heavy chain locus and the cyclin D1 gene. Which translocation and diagnosis are being described?", "options": {"A": "t(15;17), which is the recurrent change seen in acute promyelocytic leukemia.", "B": "t(11;14), which is the recurrent change seen in mantle cell lymphoma.", "C": "t(11;14), which is the recurrent change seen in follicular lymphoma.", "D": "t(14;18), which is the recurrent change seen in mantle cell lymphoma."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (t(11;14), which is the recurrent change seen in mantle cell lymphoma.): Cyclin D1 lies on chromosome 11, so the t(11;14) is the mantle cell lymphoma translocation. The t(14;18) belongs to follicular lymphoma, and t(15;17) belongs to acute promyelocytic leukemia. Mixing the diagnosis and the translocation partners gives the wrong pairings."}, {"id": 1807, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The biopsy came from a deep abscess wall. A technologist screens sputum smears for tuberculosis using a fluorochrome stain, which allows scanning at lower magnification. Under the fluorescence microscope, how do mycobacteria stained with auramine-rhodamine appear, and what happens to positive smears?", "options": {"A": "They glow yellow-green, and positive smears are confirmed before the result is reported", "B": "They glow yellow-green, and positive smears are discarded since the stain is not specific", "C": "They appear red on a blue background, and positive smears are reported without any confirmation", "D": "They appear purple on a pink background, and positive smears are confirmed before reporting"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They glow yellow-green, and positive smears are confirmed before the result is reported): Fluorochrome stains such as auramine-rhodamine make mycobacteria glow yellow-green and allow rapid screening, with positives confirmed. Red on blue is Ziehl-Neelsen, and purple on pink is the Gram method, which does not stain mycobacteria well."}, {"id": 1808, "domain": "histology", "question": "A GMS slide shows very dark sections in which organism shapes are hard to distinguish, even in the control. The section was cut at 4 µm and the light green counterstain step lasted thirty seconds. The technologist reviews the timing record for the run. Which error is the most likely explanation?", "options": {"A": "The sections were rinsed in distilled water rather than tap water", "B": "The sections were cut at 3 µm instead of 4 µm thickness", "C": "The sections were left too long in the light green counterstain", "D": "The sections were left too long in the silver solution"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The sections were left too long in the silver solution): Too long in silver makes sections dark and hides detail, while too short gives pale weak organisms. A short counterstain time or distilled water would not cause this."}, {"id": 1809, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A hairy cell leukemia patient and a patient with melanoma both carry the same hotspot change in a kinase gene. In melanoma this alteration predicts response to a specific targeted drug class. Which alteration and drug class are being described?", "options": {"A": "EGFR T790M, which predicts response to first generation EGFR inhibitors", "B": "BCR-ABL1, which predicts response to BRAF inhibitors in hairy cell leukemia", "C": "BRAF V600E, which predicts response to BRAF inhibitors in melanoma", "D": "JAK2 V617F, which predicts response to thiopurine drugs in melanoma"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (BRAF V600E, which predicts response to BRAF inhibitors in melanoma): BRAF V600E is a hotspot change found in many melanomas and in hairy cell leukemia, and it predicts response to BRAF inhibitors in melanoma. T790M causes resistance to first generation EGFR inhibitors rather than response. JAK2 V617F is a myeloproliferative neoplasm driver and BCR-ABL1 is a fusion rather than a point mutation."}, {"id": 1810, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A woman with heavy menstrual bleeding has a hemoglobin of 92 g/L, and the physician requests the single best laboratory test to confirm iron deficiency because it reflects the size of body iron stores. What result would be considered diagnostic?", "options": {"A": "Serum ferritin above about 300 micrograms per litre", "B": "Serum iron above about 30 micromoles per litre", "C": "Total iron binding capacity below the reference interval", "D": "Serum ferritin below about 15 micrograms per litre"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Serum ferritin below about 15 micrograms per litre): Ferritin reflects stores, and a value below about 15 micrograms per litre is diagnostic of iron deficiency. A very high ferritin indicates inflammation or iron overload. A high serum iron and a low TIBC would point away from iron deficiency, where TIBC is raised."}, {"id": 1811, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A hospital sends samples for trace element testing, such as zinc or copper, and the laboratory's collection manual specifies a particular tube colour manufactured to contain very low levels of metals. A new collector asks which tube colour that is and why ordinary tubes are avoided.", "options": {"A": "Light blue, made with citrate that chelates metals for stable storage", "B": "Lavender, made with EDTA that concentrates trace metals in the sample", "C": "Royal blue, made low in metals so the tube adds no contamination", "D": "Gray, made with fluoride that prevents metals from leaving the cells"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Royal blue, made low in metals so the tube adds no contamination): Royal blue tubes are manufactured to contain very little metal, so the tube itself does not add zinc, copper or other trace elements to the sample. Light blue tubes are for coagulation, gray tops for glucose, and lavender EDTA would bind trace metals and change the result rather than preserve it."}, {"id": 1812, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A rapid immunochromatographic test for histidine-rich protein 2 is positive for malaria in a febrile patient who returned from Nigeria yesterday. The clinician wants the parasitemia percentage in the report right away to decide on intravenous therapy. What is the correct laboratory response?", "options": {"A": "Skip microscopy, since a positive rapid antigen test already replaces the thick and thin blood films", "B": "Examine thick and thin films, since the rapid test cannot reliably report parasitemia", "C": "Repeat the rapid test until the line becomes strong enough to quantify", "D": "Report the percentage estimated from the intensity of the rapid test line"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Examine thick and thin films, since the rapid test cannot reliably report parasitemia): Rapid antigen tests supplement microscopy but cannot quantify parasitemia or identify the species reliably, so thin and thick films are still needed. Line intensity is not a measurement."}, {"id": 1813, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A whole exome result for a healthy adult tested for another reason shows a pathogenic variant in a gene that causes an actionable hereditary heart condition. The laboratory policy says such findings are reported. What is the correct way to handle this result?", "options": {"A": "Contact the patient's employer so that occupational risk can be reviewed", "B": "Communicate it directly to the ordering provider and record the communication", "C": "Hold the finding until a relative asks about the family history", "D": "File it in the routine report queue with no telephone call to anyone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Communicate it directly to the ordering provider and record the communication): Unexpected or critical results such as a pathogenic variant in an actionable gene are communicated directly to the ordering provider and recorded. Routine queuing risks delay. Employers and insurers must not be contacted without the patient's permission, and waiting for a relative to ask is not a communication plan."}, {"id": 1814, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory issues a corrected report because an incorrect variant nomenclature was typed in the interpretation of a hereditary panel. The correction is sent to the ordering provider. Which content must a corrected report clearly include to meet reporting standards?", "options": {"A": "What was changed and the reason for the change", "B": "Only the new text, without any reference to the original report", "C": "The name of the technologist who made the original error", "D": "A statement that the original report should be destroyed and discarded"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (What was changed and the reason for the change): Corrected reports clearly show what was changed and why so that the reader can act safely and the record stays traceable. Replacing the text without reference to the original hides the change. Destroying the original breaks the record, and naming the individual is not a reporting requirement."}, {"id": 1815, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A serum tube without gel was centrifuged, then left on the rack for five hours before testing because of a backlog. The patient has normal kidney function and no hemolysis was seen. Results show low glucose but raised phosphate and lactate dehydrogenase. What should have been done after centrifugation?", "options": {"A": "Leave the tube capped at room temperature and shake before analysis", "B": "Separate the serum from the cells within two hours, or use a gel barrier tube", "C": "Freeze the whole tube upright and thaw it after eight hours", "D": "Refrigerate the whole tube and re-spin it before analysis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Separate the serum from the cells within two hours, or use a gel barrier tube): Cells left in contact with serum keep working, consuming glucose and leaking phosphate, potassium and lactate dehydrogenase, so serum is separated within two hours unless a gel barrier is present. Refrigerating whole blood worsens potassium leakage, and freezing a tube with cells would lyse them. Shaking simply remixes the cells with the serum."}, {"id": 1816, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Library preparation for a run included a no-template control taken through every step. After sequencing, the control shows 8,000 reads aligning to the target genes, with variants matching one patient's sample in the same batch. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Report all samples as usual and add a comment on possible low-level contamination", "B": "Investigate the contamination and hold affected samples until the source is resolved", "C": "Ignore the control, since patients' own depth is far higher than the control's", "D": "Subtract the control's variants from every patient and report the remaining variants"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Investigate the contamination and hold affected samples until the source is resolved): A no-template control should yield essentially no reads, so reads matching a patient signal contamination during preparation, and affected results cannot be trusted until the source is found. Subtracting variants risks removing real findings, and ignoring or merely commenting on a failed control releases doubtful results."}, {"id": 1817, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A bronchoscopist rinses a small amount of saline through the scope over a visible area of interest in the airway and collects the fluid for cytology. A trainee is asked what this specimen is called and how it differs from brushing. Which answer is correct?", "options": {"A": "Bronchoalveolar lavage, which wedges the scope in a small airway and instils a large volume", "B": "Deep-cough sputum, which collects material coughed up from the lower respiratory tract", "C": "Fine needle aspiration, which collects cells from a peripheral mass through a needle", "D": "Bronchial washing, which rinses saline over a lesion rather than scraping it"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bronchial washing, which rinses saline over a lesion rather than scraping it): Bronchial washing rinses saline over a visible lesion and collects the fluid, whereas brushing scrapes cells directly. Lavage uses a wedged scope and larger volume. Sputum is coughed up, and needle aspiration uses a needle rather than rinsing."}, {"id": 1818, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A courier will carry liquid cytology specimens between two sites. A technologist prepares the packaging and wants to prevent leaks and protect the requisitions. The specimens are travelling by road between two hospitals, and the courier will be stopped by neither weather nor delay. Which packaging is correct?", "options": {"A": "Loosely capped containers inside a paper bag, with requisitions taped directly onto each individual primary container", "B": "Leak-proof tightly capped containers in a secondary container with absorbent material, requisitions kept separate", "C": "Open containers in a cooler with ice, with requisitions written on the lid", "D": "Sealed containers in a plain box, with requisitions folded inside the specimen"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Leak-proof tightly capped containers in a secondary container with absorbent material, requisitions kept separate): Liquid samples need tight caps, a secondary container with absorbent material and a separate requisition pocket. Loose caps or requisitions in contact with the specimen risk leaks and contamination."}, {"id": 1819, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A person presents four days after unprotected sex with a partner known to be HIV positive, and a fourth-generation combined antigen and antibody screen is nonreactive. The person is anxious and asks whether the result proves they are uninfected. What is the best laboratory or clinical advice regarding the negative result?", "options": {"A": "It should be replaced by a hepatitis serology, since that test shortens the window period", "B": "It may fall in the window period, so repeat testing later", "C": "It proves infection occurred, since fourth-generation assays turn positive within the first days", "D": "It rules out infection at four days, so repeat testing is unnecessary"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It may fall in the window period, so repeat testing later): The window period is the time before markers are detectable, and nucleic acid tests have the shortest window, followed by fourth-generation assays. A result soon after exposure should be repeated. It does not rule out infection, and hepatitis tests do not shorten it."}, {"id": 1820, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Anti-E, anti-c and anti-K are all present in one patient. Among donors, about 70 percent are E negative, 20 percent are c negative and 91 percent are K negative. Assuming the three antigens are inherited independently, about what percentage of random units lack all three, and how many units must be tested to find two?", "options": {"A": "About 91 percent lack all three, so testing two or three units will be enough", "B": "About 1 percent lack all three, so roughly 200 units must be tested for two", "C": "About 60 percent lack all three, so roughly 4 units must be tested for two", "D": "About 13 percent lack all three, so roughly 16 units must be tested for two"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 13 percent lack all three, so roughly 16 units must be tested for two): Multiply the frequencies: 0.70 x 0.20 = 0.14, and 0.14 x 0.91 = 0.127, or about 13 percent. Finding two compatible units then needs roughly 2 divided by 0.127, about 16 units. Averaging the three frequencies or using only the most common one ignores that every antigen must be absent at once."}, {"id": 1821, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "In a family with Klinefelter syndrome, a father asks how a boy could end up with an extra X chromosome without any inherited problem in the parents. The laboratory wants a short explanation of the usual chromosome constitution and typical clinical picture. Which karyotype and clinical picture fit this family?", "options": {"A": "It is 47,XYY, with tall stature, small testes and infertility.", "B": "It is 47,XXY, with tall stature, small testes and infertility.", "C": "It is 46,XY, with an extra X chromosome carried on an autosome.", "D": "It is 45,X, with short stature, gonadal dysgenesis and heart defects."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is 47,XXY, with tall stature, small testes and infertility.): Klinefelter syndrome is 47,XXY, with tall stature, small testes and infertility. The pattern with short stature and gonadal dysgenesis belongs to Turner syndrome. An extra Y gives 47,XYY, which is a different constitution, and 46,XY is a normal male count."}, {"id": 1822, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "In a leukemia report, a rearrangement joins the BCR gene to the ABL1 gene so that a single new gene product is produced by the joined sequence. What term describes this kind of variant, and why is it clinically important?", "options": {"A": "A copy number gain, because two genes were both amplified to raise dosage", "B": "A splice-site variant, because it changes how RNA from one gene is processed", "C": "A fusion, whose product can drive the cancer and serves as a diagnostic marker", "D": "A repeat expansion, because extra copies of a short sequence produce the product"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A fusion, whose product can drive the cancer and serves as a diagnostic marker): Two genes joined by a rearrangement form a fusion, and its product, such as BCR-ABL1, can drive cancer and define a diagnosis. Repeat expansions add copies of short sequences, copy number gains change dosage, and splice-site variants stay within one gene."}, {"id": 1823, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A liquid-based slide from a 27-year-old is clean and well stained, but the technologist estimates only about 1,500 well-preserved squamous cells across the whole preparation, with no blood or inflammation obscuring them. Identification and requisition are correct. How should the sample be classified?", "options": {"A": "Satisfactory but limited, because the slide is well stained and properly identified", "B": "Satisfactory, because five thousand cells are needed only on conventional smears", "C": "Unsatisfactory for evaluation, because the squamous cell count is below the adequacy minimum", "D": "Rejected, because the requisition lacks the information needed to begin screening"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Unsatisfactory for evaluation, because the squamous cell count is below the adequacy minimum): A liquid-based sample needs at least 5,000 well-visualized squamous cells. With about 1,500 it is processed and found to have too few cells, so it is unsatisfactory for evaluation. Good staining does not make up for cell number, and the requisition is complete."}, {"id": 1824, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A screening HPLC on a healthy 24-year-old athlete shows hemoglobin A at 58 percent, hemoglobin S at 38 percent, hemoglobin A2 at 2.8 percent and hemoglobin F below 1 percent. The CBC and smear are normal, and the patient has no symptoms. What should be reported?", "options": {"A": "Pattern consistent with beta thalassemia trait, with raised hemoglobin A2", "B": "Pattern consistent with sickle cell disease, with hemoglobin S the majority", "C": "Pattern consistent with hemoglobin C trait, with a variant near A2 position", "D": "Pattern consistent with sickle cell trait, with hemoglobin A the majority"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pattern consistent with sickle cell trait, with hemoglobin A the majority): One normal and one sickle beta gene gives a majority of hemoglobin A with S near 35 to 45 percent, and patients are usually well with a normal smear. Disease would have no hemoglobin A. The A2 is normal, so beta thalassemia trait does not fit, and no hemoglobin C was found."}, {"id": 1825, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A resident asks the transfusion service about the timing that separates the two pulmonary reactions. One occurs within six hours and the other within twelve hours of the transfusion. Which pairing of reaction and time window is correct? Choose the correct pairing.", "options": {"A": "Circulatory overload within 24 hours, and TRALI within one week", "B": "TRALI within 24 hours, and circulatory overload within three days after transfusion", "C": "Circulatory overload within six hours, and TRALI within twelve hours", "D": "TRALI within six hours, and circulatory overload within twelve hours"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (TRALI within six hours, and circulatory overload within twelve hours): TRALI appears within six hours of transfusion and TACO within 12 hours. Reversing them is wrong. The longer windows of 24 hours, three days or a week do not match either pulmonary reaction as defined in the chapter."}, {"id": 1826, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A leukemic marrow gives only a handful of poorly spread metaphases, and none of them are good enough to karyotype. The clinician wants to know whether any of the known prognostic changes are present. Which approach makes the best use of the remaining material?", "options": {"A": "Perform interphase FISH on the fixed cells for the known targets.", "B": "Report a failed study and discard the fixed cells to save storage space.", "C": "Report the karyotype as 46,XX since no abnormal cell was clearly seen.", "D": "Re-band the same poor metaphases at a higher temperature to improve them."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Perform interphase FISH on the fixed cells for the known targets.): FISH on interphase cells does not need metaphases, so it can test known targets when the karyotype fails. Discarding the fixed cells removes the only remaining material, and re-banding cannot improve spreading. Calling the result normal claims more than the data show."}, {"id": 1827, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Only 20 minutes after collection, a technologist receives 1.5 mL of CSF from a patient with suspected leukemia relapse only 20 minutes after collection, and the requisition asks for cytology. The cell count is very low and the specimen is clear. Which processing approach best preserves the few cells for cytologic examination?", "options": {"A": "Add formalin to the whole specimen and process it as a cell block.", "B": "Filter the CSF through a coarse membrane and air-dry the filter.", "C": "Process promptly by cytocentrifugation, then fix and stain the slides.", "D": "Hold the fluid overnight at room temperature so cells settle naturally."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Process promptly by cytocentrifugation, then fix and stain the slides.): CSF has few cells that begin to degrade within about an hour, so it is processed promptly, usually by cytocentrifugation, which concentrates cells onto slides. Waiting overnight lets them degrade. A coarse filter would lose the cells, and formalin on the whole specimen prevents proper smear staining."}, {"id": 1828, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cerebrospinal fluid specimen from a traumatic lumbar puncture contains many red cells, and a few atypical cells appear among them. The pathologist must interpret these findings carefully. What is the most appropriate laboratory action regarding the blood in this specimen?", "options": {"A": "Disregard the red cells, since blood contamination does not change how cells are interpreted", "B": "Note the traumatic tap in the report, since blood can add contaminating cells", "C": "Add fixative at once to dissolve the red cells and concentrate the tumour cells", "D": "Reject the specimen automatically because bloody cerebrospinal fluid cannot be examined"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Note the traumatic tap in the report, since blood can add contaminating cells): A traumatic tap should be noted, as blood adds contaminating cells and can complicate interpretation. Claiming blood never matters is false. Rejecting a precious specimen is wrong, and fixative does not dissolve red cells."}, {"id": 1829, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A student learns that all ABO antigens are constructed on a common precursor, and that people who lack it entirely have a rare phenotype. The student is asked which gene makes this precursor substance and on which chromosome it sits. Which gene and location fit the H precursor?", "options": {"A": "The ABO gene itself, located on chromosome 9", "B": "The A gene, located on chromosome 9 next to the B gene", "C": "The RHD gene of the Rh system, located on chromosome 1", "D": "The H gene, called FUT1, located on chromosome 19"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The H gene, called FUT1, located on chromosome 19): H substance is produced by the enzyme from the H gene, FUT1, on chromosome 19, and A and B enzymes build on it. The ABO gene on chromosome 9 codes for the A and B glycosyltransferases. The RHD gene on chromosome 1 controls the D antigen of the Rh system and has no role in H substance."}, {"id": 1830, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A patient aged sixty-eight had a total hysterectomy for a benign condition many years ago and has been screened regularly. Her clinic asks whether a vaginal vault sample showing benign-appearing glandular cells is cause for alarm. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "They indicate residual endometrial disease and need a repeat vault sample within about six weeks", "B": "They indicate recurrent adenocarcinoma and need urgent surgery", "C": "Such cells are usually vaginal vault changes after hysterectomy and are an expected finding", "D": "They indicate active herpes infection needing antiviral therapy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Such cells are usually vaginal vault changes after hysterectomy and are an expected finding): Benign-appearing glandular cells after hysterectomy are usually vaginal vault changes and are expected. Recurrent adenocarcinoma would show atypical cells with a diathesis. Herpes has viral nuclear features, and there is no endometrium after a total hysterectomy."}, {"id": 1831, "domain": "histology", "question": "A pathologist working with a Mohs team asks about the faster options used in the laboratory. For special uses such as Mohs surgery or smear preparations, some laboratories choose a stain faster than rapid hematoxylin and eosin. Which stain is commonly used for this purpose?", "options": {"A": "Gram stain with crystal violet, used mainly to classify bacteria on smears", "B": "Masson trichrome stain, used mostly to demonstrate collagen in fixed sections", "C": "Periodic acid-Schiff stain, which highlights mucins and takes several extra steps", "D": "Toluidine blue rapid stain, which takes less time than hematoxylin and eosin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Toluidine blue rapid stain, which takes less time than hematoxylin and eosin): Toluidine blue is a very fast stain used for special applications such as Mohs surgery and smears. Periodic acid-Schiff, Gram, and trichrome methods take too long for intraoperative use or serve unrelated purposes."}, {"id": 1832, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The results shown come from a two-step algorithm run on a liquid stool from an inpatient who recently received antibiotics. The antigen and toxin immunoassay results disagree. How should the technologist interpret and report the combined findings in the table?", "options": {"A": "Report as negative, since the toxin immunoassay did not detect free toxin in the stool", "B": "Report as colonization with a nontoxigenic strain, since the antigen is positive without toxin", "C": "Repeat the antigen assay on a second stool specimen collected from the same inpatient", "D": "Report as toxigenic Clostridioides difficile detected, since the toxin gene was amplified"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report as toxigenic Clostridioides difficile detected, since the toxin gene was amplified): The table shows a positive glutamate dehydrogenase antigen, a negative toxin immunoassay and a positive tcdB gene amplification. In a two-step algorithm the nucleic acid test arbitrates discordant results, so a positive tcdB means a toxigenic strain is present and is reported with clinical correlation. A negative report ignores the amplification, a nontoxigenic call contradicts the gene result, and repeating the antigen does not resolve the discordance.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "C. difficile testing, stool specimen", "columns": ["Test", "Result", "Method"], "rows": [["GDH antigen", "POS", "Immunoassay"], ["Toxin A and B", "NEG", "Immunoassay"], ["tcdB gene", "POS", "Nucleic acid amplification"]], "flag_col": 1, "caption": "Two-step algorithm results"}, "image": "question_images/q_1832.png"}, {"id": 1833, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A laboratory participates in an external proficiency testing program for hematology and receives unknown samples three times a year from the provider. A student asks what this program adds beyond the daily controls run on each analyzer. What is its purpose?", "options": {"A": "It replaces daily QC once the analyzer has shown stable performance for several months", "B": "It calibrates the analyzer to a reference method value", "C": "It sets the mean and limits used for the daily control chart", "D": "It compares the laboratory's unknown sample results with peer laboratories"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It compares the laboratory's unknown sample results with peer laboratories): External proficiency testing sends unknown samples to many laboratories and compares the results with peers, so it checks accuracy independently of internal QC. Control means and limits come from the laboratory's own data. It does not replace daily QC, and calibration is a separate procedure done by the laboratory."}, {"id": 1834, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient with severe burns has an analyzer platelet count of 210 x10^9/L, but the smear estimate is only 60 x10^9/L. The red cells on the film are shown in the image. What best explains the discrepancy between these two counts?", "options": {"A": "Red cell fragments are sized as platelets by impedance counting, falsely raising the count", "B": "EDTA-induced platelet clumping removes platelets from the count and falsely lowers the result in this sample", "C": "Giant platelets exceed the upper size limit and are excluded from the analyzer platelet count", "D": "Platelet satellitism around neutrophils hides the platelets from the aperture of the analyzer"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Red cell fragments are sized as platelets by impedance counting, falsely raising the count): Many small red cell fragments are similar in size to platelets and are counted as platelets by impedance, so the analyzer count is falsely high compared with the smear. Clumping, giant platelets and satellitism would lower the analyzer count, which is the opposite of the discrepancy seen.", "image_type": "micrograph", "image_params": {"prompt": "Realistic Wright-Giemsa stained peripheral blood smear photomicrograph, 1000x oil immersion, many fragmented red cells including helmet cells, triangular cells and small irregular fragments (schistocytes) among a few normal red cells, with only a few platelets seen.", "caption": "Peripheral blood film", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1834.png"}, {"id": 1835, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In the reporting area of a cytology laboratory, A clinician calls to say a cervical specimen was reported as unsatisfactory and asks what the laboratory should do next. Which response best reflects good reporting practice for the laboratory receiving this question about that patient?", "options": {"A": "Advise the clinician to wait for the patient's next routine visit later", "B": "Hold the report until the next scheduled batch of results is released", "C": "Change the result to negative because the sample was mostly clear on the slide", "D": "Give prompt notice so the patient can be recalled and resampled"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give prompt notice so the patient can be recalled and resampled): An unsatisfactory specimen needs prompt notice so the patient can be recalled and resampled. Changing the result to negative misrepresents the sample, and holding results or waiting for a routine visit delays care and leaves a screening gap. Documenting the notice creates a record that the recall was communicated."}, {"id": 1836, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A physiology lecture explains changes in red cells during 42 days of cold storage. Which two changes in stored red cells does the chapter describe, one of which reduces the ability of hemoglobin to release oxygen to the tissues? Which pairing is correct?", "options": {"A": "Slow potassium leakage and loss of 2,3-diphosphoglycerate, reducing oxygen release", "B": "Rapid calcium leakage and gain of fibrinogen concentration, which raises platelet counts", "C": "Slow potassium uptake and loss of factor VIII activity, which reduces clotting", "D": "Rapid sodium uptake and gain of 2,3-diphosphoglycerate, which improves oxygen release"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Slow potassium leakage and loss of 2,3-diphosphoglycerate, reducing oxygen release): Cold-stored red cells slowly leak potassium and lose 2,3-DPG, which helps hemoglobin release oxygen. Sodium and calcium shifts are not the described changes, and factor VIII and fibrinogen belong to plasma products, not to red cell metabolism during storage."}, {"id": 1837, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist has just finished cutting a series of frozen sections at the cryostat and is now working on the slides. After a frozen section is cut and picked up on a room-temperature slide, it thaws and adheres. Which step follows immediately, before the rapid stain is applied?", "options": {"A": "Store the slide in the dark for later fluorescence reading", "B": "Coat the section with paraffin wax and reheat it", "C": "Fix the section, often in alcohol or a formalin solution", "D": "Air dry the slide for several hours at room temperature"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fix the section, often in alcohol or a formalin solution): The thawed section is fixed immediately, often in alcohol or formalin, before staining. Several hours of air drying loses the rapid advantage. Waxing is part of paraffin methods, and dark storage applies to fluorescence slides."}, {"id": 1838, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A breast FNA yields scanty smears with only a few ductal cells and no lesional material, and the palpable mass is still present. The technologist asks how the report should be categorized and what should be advised. The cells present look bland. Which choice is correct?", "options": {"A": "Insufficient material, with repeat sampling advised rather than guessing a category", "B": "Atypical, since scanty material indicates hidden lesional cells", "C": "Benign, since no atypical cells were seen among the few ductal cells present", "D": "Suspicious for malignancy, since scanty smears are a feature of lobular carcinoma"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Insufficient material, with repeat sampling advised rather than guessing a category): When material is scanty, the correct answer is insufficient material and repeat sampling is advised, not a guessed category. Calling benign from a few bland cells falsely reassures. Scanty smears do not by themselves mean atypia. Suspicion of malignancy needs real atypical findings, not just low cellularity."}, {"id": 1839, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Master mix for a 25 µL reaction is prepared from separate components, and a technologist later realizes the magnesium chloride was omitted, though the buffer, primers, dNTPs, and Taq polymerase are all present and cycling was normal. What is the magnesium's role, and what is the expected result?", "options": {"A": "It supplies nucleotides for the new strand, so only very short products form.", "B": "It activates hot-start inhibitors, so amplification occurs normally but starts later.", "C": "It is an enzyme cofactor, so little or no amplification is expected.", "D": "It stabilizes the primers, so amplification occurs but with more non-specific products."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It is an enzyme cofactor, so little or no amplification is expected.): Magnesium chloride is a required cofactor for the DNA polymerase, and without it the enzyme has little activity. The dNTPs, not magnesium, supply the building blocks, and the extra non-specific products are a result of too much magnesium rather than too little."}, {"id": 1840, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "During identification of an antibody, the technologist tests the patient's serum against an 11-cell panel and adds a tube containing the patient's own red cells with the patient's own serum. What is this extra tube called, and what does a positive result in it suggest?", "options": {"A": "An autocontrol, and a positive result suggests an autoantibody or antibody already coating patient cells", "B": "An autocontrol, and a positive result confirms that the panel cells are homozygous for the antigen", "C": "A crossmatch control, and a positive result shows the donor unit is incompatible with the patient", "D": "A check cell control, and a positive result confirms the antiglobulin reagent was working correctly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An autocontrol, and a positive result suggests an autoantibody or antibody already coating patient cells): The autocontrol tests the patient's own cells with the patient's own serum. A reaction suggests an autoantibody, or an alloantibody or drug already coating the patient's cells, which changes the workup. Check cells verify the antiglobulin reagent, and a crossmatch involves donor cells."}, {"id": 1841, "domain": "hematology", "question": "During a cytochemical stain run for myeloperoxidase, the negative control slide shows strong positive staining, and the patient marrow slide also stains positive in the blasts. A colleague suggests releasing the result as supporting myeloid lineage. What action should the technologist take with this run?", "options": {"A": "Report the patient result with a comment that the negative control failed", "B": "Discard the negative control slide and report the patient result after remaking a new control", "C": "Treat the run as invalid, investigate reagents, and repeat the stain before reporting anything", "D": "Report the patient result because the positive control was acceptable"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat the run as invalid, investigate reagents, and repeat the stain before reporting anything): A negative control that stains positive means the reagent or technique is faulty, so the patient result cannot be trusted. The run must be repeated after investigation. Reporting with a comment or discarding a control breaches quality assurance."}, {"id": 1842, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Students at the teaching microscope are given a squamous cell about 35 µm wide with clear blue-green cytoplasm and a central nucleus of about 8 µm, with fine evenly distributed chromatin and a visible nuclear membrane. Which set of features defines this normal cell type?", "options": {"A": "Polygonal, about 30 to 50 µm, with a central vesicular nucleus and fine chromatin", "B": "Large and flat, about 40 to 60 µm, with a tiny pyknotic nucleus and eosinophilic cytoplasm", "C": "Round, about 15 to 30 µm, with dense basophilic cytoplasm and a large hyperchromatic nucleus", "D": "Tall and narrow, about 25 µm, with a basal nucleus and foamy cytoplasm"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Polygonal, about 30 to 50 µm, with a central vesicular nucleus and fine chromatin): The intermediate cell is polygonal, 30 to 50 µm, with cyanophilic cytoplasm and a central vesicular nucleus. The small dense round cell describes a parabasal cell. The large cell with a pyknotic nucleus is superficial, and the tall narrow cell is endocervical."}, {"id": 1843, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist stains a set of slides and includes one section from a tissue known to lack the target of the stain. The section is treated with the same reagents as the others and cut from a different block than the patient's tissue. What is the purpose of this negative control?", "options": {"A": "To test whether the microscope is correctly focused on the tissue plane", "B": "To show that any colour seen is specific and not due to background binding", "C": "To confirm that the tissue block was correctly labelled at the time of cutting", "D": "To prove that the reagents are still active and capable of producing colour"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (To show that any colour seen is specific and not due to background binding): A negative control should not stain, so a clean result shows the colour on patient tissue is specific and not from contamination or non-specific binding. Reagent activity is shown by the positive control instead."}, {"id": 1844, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A woman's cytology was reported as HSIL, but the colposcopy biopsy returned negative. The quality team is auditing the case as part of correlating abnormal results with histology. Which action is most consistent with the laboratory's quality assurance responsibilities in this discrepancy?", "options": {"A": "Take no action, since a sampling difference explains the discrepancy", "B": "Delete the cytology report because the biopsy result overrides it", "C": "Review the original slide with the pathologist, document the outcome, and share the learning", "D": "Reprimand the technologist without reviewing the slide or documenting anything about the discrepancy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review the original slide with the pathologist, document the outcome, and share the learning): Discrepancies between cytology and histology are reviewed with the pathologist, documented, and used for learning. Deleting a report destroys the record. Discipline without review is unfair and ineffective, and assuming sampling difference without checking the slide skips the audit."}, {"id": 1845, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with suspected aspirin overdose has ringing in the ears and rapid breathing. The first salicylate level, drawn 2 hours after ingestion, is just below the toxic range. The physician asks whether this single result is enough to exclude serious poisoning. What is the best laboratory advice?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the level later, because tablets keep dissolving and the value may still rise", "B": "Stop testing and rely on the anion gap alone because it excludes any toxicity", "C": "Accept the single value as final, because salicylate levels stay fixed after the first sample", "D": "Repeat the sample only after fasting overnight, because meals lower the measured salicylate level"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeat the level later, because tablets keep dissolving and the value may still rise): Salicylate levels can keep rising as tablets dissolve, so a repeat sample is often required to see the true peak. A single early value does not exclude poisoning. The anion gap and acid-base picture add information but do not replace a repeat level, and fasting is not required."}, {"id": 1846, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A supervisor explains the timing of an amniotic fluid culture to a new technologist. Unlike blood, the fetal cells grow as small colonies attached to coverslips or flasks, and the medium is changed periodically while the cultures are monitored under an inverted microscope. Approximately when is the first harvest usually performed?", "options": {"A": "After about 72 hours, because the same mitogen is used as in blood cultures", "B": "After about one week to ten days, once colonies have grown enough to harvest", "C": "After about two hours, because fetal cells divide as fast as marrow cells", "D": "After about two months, because colonies take that long to appear in flasks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (After about one week to ten days, once colonies have grown enough to harvest): Amniotic fluid cell colonies usually take about a week to ten days before the first harvest. Two hours or 72 hours is too short for colony growth, and two months would exceed the prenatal timeline."}, {"id": 1847, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A microbiologist explains how carbapenemase genes are identified quickly for infection control decisions. Rather than waiting for phenotypic confirmation, the laboratory uses cartridge-based tests that provide a result within hours. What can these rapid tests identify? The supervisor wants the answer for the infection control committee.", "options": {"A": "The specific carbapenemase gene, such as KPC, NDM, VIM, IMP, or OXA-48", "B": "The quality control range of the reference strain used on the same day", "C": "The patient's antibiotic allergy history and the ideal dose for the treating unit", "D": "The cefoxitin zone size that would be measured on a Mueller-Hinton plate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The specific carbapenemase gene, such as KPC, NDM, VIM, IMP, or OXA-48): Molecular assays, lateral flow assays and rapid colorimetric tests identify the specific carbapenemase gene within hours, so infection control can act quickly. They do not measure allergy, QC ranges or disk zone sizes."}, {"id": 1848, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A virology laboratory inoculates a throat specimen onto a monolayer of susceptible cells, and after four days the cells round up, fuse into multinucleated syncytia, and detach from the flask. What is this visible damage called, and what is a current use for culture?", "options": {"A": "Window period, and culture is now used only to measure antibodies in serum samples", "B": "Prozone effect, and culture is now the routine first test for all respiratory viruses", "C": "Cytopathic effect, and culture is now kept for viable isolates needed for susceptibility or surveillance", "D": "Cytopathic effect, and culture is now the fastest method available for routine respiratory viruses"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cytopathic effect, and culture is now kept for viable isolates needed for susceptibility or surveillance): Visible cell damage from viral growth is cytopathic effect. Culture is slow and mostly replaced by PCR, but it remains useful for isolates. The prozone effect concerns antigen excess, and the window period concerns serology."}, {"id": 1849, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A patient with a major ABO mismatch after stem cell transplant needs both red cells and plasma soon after engraftment begins. The patient's original group is O and the donor's group is A. Which products are compatible with both? Her current antibody screen is negative.", "options": {"A": "Group O red cells and group O plasma.", "B": "Group O red cells and group AB plasma.", "C": "Group A red cells and group O plasma.", "D": "Group AB red cells and group A plasma."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Group O red cells and group AB plasma.): Red cells must be compatible with both the recipient's anti-A and the donor-type cells, which means group O, and group AB plasma is the safest choice because it contains neither anti-A nor anti-B. Group O plasma contains anti-A that would hemolyze donor-type cells, and group A red cells would be attacked by the recipient's anti-A."}, {"id": 1850, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "The antibody screen is negative and there is no antibody history for a patient. The AHG crossmatch is compatible with two units but shows 2+ agglutination with a third unit, and the autocontrol is negative. Which explanation is most reasonable, and what is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "A warm autoantibody in the patient's serum; issue the unit since the autocontrol is negative", "B": "An antibody to a low frequency antigen on that unit; do not issue it and investigate", "C": "Contaminated reagent in the crossmatch; repeat once and issue it if the reaction disappears", "D": "Dosage on the donor cells; issue the unit, since heterozygous antigens are clinically insignificant"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An antibody to a low frequency antigen on that unit; do not issue it and investigate): A negative screen does not prove that no antibody exists, because an antibody against a low frequency antigen may not be on the screening cells. The incompatible crossmatch is the last check and must not be ignored, so the unit is not issued and the cause is investigated. A warm autoantibody would give a positive autocontrol and react with the other units."}, {"id": 1851, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A thin film shows enlarged red cells that contain fine pink dots and irregular, ameboid trophozoites. The stain was prepared with Giemsa at pH 7.2 from an EDTA sample collected on arrival. A trainee reads it as Plasmodium ovale because the host cells look large. Which finding supports Plasmodium vivax rather than ovale?", "options": {"A": "Maltese cross tetrads without pigment, rather than dotted enlarged cells", "B": "Band forms and rosette schizonts, rather than ameboid trophozoites", "C": "Crescent shaped gametocytes with several rings per cell, rather than ameboid forms", "D": "Round enlarged cells with Schuffner dots, rather than oval fimbriated cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Round enlarged cells with Schuffner dots, rather than oval fimbriated cells): Plasmodium vivax infects young, enlarged red cells that show Schuffner dots and ameboid trophozoites, whereas ovale infects oval cells with fimbriated edges. Band forms and rosettes describe malariae, Maltese crosses describe Babesia, and crescents with multiple rings describe falciparum."}, {"id": 1852, "domain": "hematology", "question": "Natural anticoagulants keep clotting under control by inactivating specific activated factors. A patient has a deficiency in a protein that, together with its cofactor protein S, inactivates factors Va and VIIIa. Which protein is described, and what is the consequence of its deficiency?", "options": {"A": "Plasmin, and its deficiency raises the risk of bleeding", "B": "Tissue factor pathway inhibitor, and its deficiency raises the risk of bleeding", "C": "Antithrombin, and its deficiency raises the risk of bleeding", "D": "Protein C, and its deficiency raises the risk of thrombosis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Protein C, and its deficiency raises the risk of thrombosis): Protein C works with protein S to inactivate factors Va and VIIIa, and deficiency increases thrombosis risk. Antithrombin blocks thrombin and Xa, and TFPI limits the tissue factor pathway. Plasmin is not a natural anticoagulant of this type."}, {"id": 1853, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "The chart shown is a level 1 control with the point marked in red on day 9, beyond the control limits of the chart. Before any patient results from this run are released to the clinicians, what is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Reject the run, fix the cause, then repeat the controls before reporting patients", "B": "Average the point with the previous day control and continue reporting the patient results", "C": "Accept the run, since one control outside the limits occurs by chance in many runs", "D": "Recalculate the control mean and standard deviation to include the new point and carry on"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reject the run, fix the cause, then repeat the controls before reporting patients): A control beyond 3 SD breaks the 1-3s rule, so the run is rejected, the cause is found and fixed, and controls are repeated before patient results are released. Accepting it as chance, averaging, or widening the limits by recalculating the statistics hides a real analytical problem.", "image_type": "qc_chart", "image_params": {"points": [0.2, -0.4, 0.5, 0.1, -0.3, 0.6, -0.2, 0.4, 0.0, -0.5, 0.3, 0.1, -0.1, 0.2, -0.3], "violation_day": 9, "violation_type": "1_3s", "caption": "Level 1 control"}, "image": "question_images/q_1853.png"}, {"id": 1854, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A physician orders a vitamin D status test. Which analyte is measured, and why is it chosen over the active hormone form? The physician is unsure why the active hormone is not used, and asks the technologist to explain the choice.", "options": {"A": "Ergosterol, because it is the main dietary precursor that is present in plasma", "B": "Calcitonin, because it directly reflects the amount of vitamin D absorbed from food", "C": "25-hydroxyvitamin D, because it is the stable circulating form reflecting status", "D": "1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, because it has a longer half-life than the storage form in serum"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (25-hydroxyvitamin D, because it is the stable circulating form reflecting status): Vitamin D status is judged by 25-hydroxyvitamin D, the stable circulating form, measured by immunoassay or mass spectrometry. Calcitonin is a different hormone. Methylmalonic acid, by contrast, is used to confirm borderline B12 deficiency."}, {"id": 1855, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "Installing a new dye-specific filter set on the fluorescence microscope, the technologist sees the orange probe signals appear very dim, though DAPI looks normal. The lot of orange-labelled probe passed all quality checks earlier that week. What should the technologist check first?", "options": {"A": "Whether the denaturation oven was left at 73 degrees for too long", "B": "Whether the cutoff value was set from too few normal control samples", "C": "Whether the filter set matches the excitation and emission of the orange fluorophore", "D": "Whether the DAPI counterstain concentration was too low in the mounting medium"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Whether the filter set matches the excitation and emission of the orange fluorophore): Filters must be matched to each dye, and a mismatched orange filter passes little of the emitted light. Normal DAPI shows the microscope itself works. Denaturation and cutoff values do not change the appearance of an already-hybridized signal under a filter."}, {"id": 1856, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A senior cytotechnologist tells a trainee who has begun sending nearly every difficult slide to the pathologist as abnormal, to be safe, that both overcalling and undercalling harm patients. The trainee asks what each error causes. Which statement describes the consequences?", "options": {"A": "Neither error matters once the pathologist reviews the slide, since review corrects every misclassification", "B": "Overcalling wastes reagents and screening time, while undercalling has no effect on patient care", "C": "Overcalling leads to unnecessary procedures, while undercalling delays treatment, so both harm patients", "D": "Overcalling delays treatment, while undercalling leads to unnecessary procedures, so both harm patients"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Overcalling leads to unnecessary procedures, while undercalling delays treatment, so both harm patients): Overcalling sends patients to procedures they do not need, and undercalling lets disease wait. The reversed statement swaps the consequences. Pathologist review is a safeguard but does not make either error harmless."}, {"id": 1857, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient's lipid panel shows total cholesterol 5.0 mmol/L and HDL cholesterol 1.3 mmol/L on a non-fasting sample. The physician requests a marker that does not require fasting and includes all atherogenic lipoproteins. What non-HDL cholesterol value should be reported?", "options": {"A": "Non-HDL cholesterol is 3.7 mmol/L after subtracting HDL from the total cholesterol", "B": "Non-HDL cholesterol is 3.8 mmol/L after subtracting HDL and rounding the total upward", "C": "Non-HDL cholesterol is 1.3 mmol/L, which is simply the HDL cholesterol value reported", "D": "Non-HDL cholesterol is 6.3 mmol/L after adding HDL to the total cholesterol"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Non-HDL cholesterol is 3.7 mmol/L after subtracting HDL from the total cholesterol): Non-HDL cholesterol equals total cholesterol minus HDL cholesterol, so 5.0 minus 1.3 equals 3.7 mmol/L, and it needs no fasting. Adding the two values gives 6.3, and reporting HDL alone gives 1.3. A value of 3.8 comes from an invented rounding step."}, {"id": 1858, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist prepares a plasma sample for freezing and later coagulation testing. The chapter states that the plasma must be centrifuged to a specific standard before storage. What is the required platelet count for plasma that will be frozen for testing?", "options": {"A": "Platelet-rich plasma with about 150 x10^9/L platelets remaining", "B": "Platelet-rich plasma with about 400 x10^9/L platelets remaining", "C": "Platelet-poor plasma with fewer than 10 x10^9/L platelets", "D": "Platelet-poor plasma with fewer than 100 x10^9/L platelets"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Platelet-poor plasma with fewer than 10 x10^9/L platelets): Samples must be centrifuged to platelet-poor plasma with a platelet count below 10 x10^9/L before testing or freezing, since residual platelets release phospholipid and skew results. The other counts are much higher."}, {"id": 1859, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A laboratory prepares to run serum iron on morning specimens. Which practice helps avoid metal contamination and improves the reliability of serum iron results? The laboratory supervisor is auditing procedures and wants the best practice for this test. Choose the correct pairing.", "options": {"A": "Use tubes stored in direct sunlight, and collect in the evening after a large meal", "B": "Use metal-free tubes, and collect in the morning after fasting", "C": "Use tubes that contain EDTA, and collect at any time of day without instructions", "D": "Use tubes that are frozen before collection, and collect after iron tablets"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use metal-free tubes, and collect in the morning after fasting): Serum iron follows a daily rhythm, is highest in the morning and rises after meals and iron tablets. Samples are best drawn in the morning after fasting in metal-free tubes. EDTA and sunlight are not appropriate, and iron tablets raise the result."}, {"id": 1860, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist sets up an anaerobic jar for a deep tissue culture. Before opening the jar days later, the technologist checks the indicator strip inside. What does this indicator show? The specimen is from a child, and the correct atmosphere is needed because the organism will not grow in ordinary room air.", "options": {"A": "That the carbon dioxide level has reached 20 percent", "B": "That the temperature inside the jar has reached 42 degrees Celsius", "C": "That oxygen has been removed from the jar atmosphere", "D": "That the plates inside are free of contamination"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (That oxygen has been removed from the jar atmosphere): The indicator, together with the catalyst, confirms that an anaerobic atmosphere was achieved. It does not report temperature or carbon dioxide levels, and it cannot show whether the plates are sterile or contaminated."}, {"id": 1861, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Pipette tips stocked in a pre-PCR area are the standard uncapped type borrowed from another department, while the written procedure specifies aerosol-barrier tips for every reagent and specimen transfer in that room. Which risk do filter tips mainly reduce in molecular testing?", "options": {"A": "Growth of bacteria inside the pipette barrel that would inhibit the polymerase in later reactions.", "B": "Binding of nucleic acid to the plastic, which lowers the recovered yield.", "C": "Carry-over of aerosolized droplets from the pipette shaft into later specimens or reagents.", "D": "Loss of volume accuracy caused by evaporation during long incubation steps."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Carry-over of aerosolized droplets from the pipette shaft into later specimens or reagents.): Aerosol-barrier tips stop droplets and vapour from reaching the pipette barrel, which would otherwise carry template or amplicon into later samples. They do not affect evaporation or plastic binding, and they are not an antibacterial measure."}, {"id": 1862, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "While processing an HIV confirmatory run, a technologist is approached by a colleague from another department who knows a mutual friend was tested that morning and asks to see the result on the laboratory information system out of curiosity. The colleague has no role in that patient's care. What is the correct professional response?", "options": {"A": "Share it if the colleague promises to tell no one else about it", "B": "Decline, since results are strictly confidential and released only to authorized recipients", "C": "Share it only after the patient is discharged from hospital care", "D": "Share it, since the colleague personally knows the patient and would soon hear the result anyway"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline, since results are strictly confidential and released only to authorized recipients): HIV results require strict confidentiality and release only to authorized recipients, so curiosity or friendship is not a reason. Discharge or promises do not change that duty."}, {"id": 1863, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Abundant thick colloid fills the smears from a nodule, along with only three follicular groups, each with fewer than ten cells. There is no atypia and no inflammation. The trainee wants to report nondiagnostic because the six-group adequacy rule was not met. How should this specimen be categorized despite the low group count?", "options": {"A": "Request a cell block before any category is assigned, since colloid interferes with staining", "B": "Report atypia of undetermined significance, since few groups indicate hidden atypia", "C": "Report nondiagnostic, since the six-group rule has no exceptions in any thyroid specimen", "D": "Report benign, since abundant thick colloid is a recognized exception to the cellularity criteria"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report benign, since abundant thick colloid is a recognized exception to the cellularity criteria): A specimen with abundant thick colloid can be called benign even when the six-group rule is not met, because colloid identifies a colloid nodule. The rule does have exceptions, including this one and specimens with significant atypia or thyroiditis. Few groups do not by themselves imply atypia. A cell block is not required to assign this category."}, {"id": 1864, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist receives a lavender tube that was never inverted after collection, and wooden applicator sticks pull out small fibrin strands from the blood. The analyzer reports lower counts than the patient's usual values for white cells and platelets. Which effect do partial clots in an EDTA tube have on the results?", "options": {"A": "Clots affect only the hemoglobin channel and leave the cell counts unchanged", "B": "Clots lyse the red cells, which raises the MCV and the hematocrit", "C": "Clots release stored platelets into the plasma, so the platelet count rises falsely", "D": "Clots trap platelets and white cells, so the reported counts fall"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clots trap platelets and white cells, so the reported counts fall): A poorly mixed tube can clot in part, and the clots hold platelets and white cells so the analyzer sees fewer of them. Clots do not release platelets. The hemoglobin is not the only parameter affected, since cell counts are the main victims. Clots do not lyse red cells, and the MCV and hematocrit do not rise from this."}, {"id": 1865, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In an immunocytochemistry laboratory, During staining for immunocytochemistry, the blocking step is performed before the primary antibody is applied to the slide. Which problem does blocking address when the specimen contains many red cells, such as a bloody pleural effusion cell block?", "options": {"A": "Loss of nuclear detail caused by a counterstain left on the slide too long", "B": "Natural peroxidase activity in red cells that would otherwise colour the background", "C": "Fixation cross-links that hide the antigen from the primary antibody", "D": "Weak signal from a primary antibody diluted beyond its validated working range"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Natural peroxidase activity in red cells that would otherwise colour the background): Blocking removes background reactions such as endogenous peroxidase in red cells, so the chromogen colours only true antigen sites. Antigen masking is handled by retrieval, counterstain timing affects nuclear contrast, and dilution problems cause weak signal rather than background. Blocking is done before the primary antibody so that only true antigen sites carry the coloured deposit."}, {"id": 1866, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist floats a ribbon and finds the section has spread out too much and appears to be melting at the edges. The wax is standard paraffin. What is the most likely cause, and what should be done to the flotation bath?", "options": {"A": "The water is too hot, so the temperature should be lowered", "B": "The water is too cool, so the temperature should be raised", "C": "The water is too clean, so a little tissue should be added", "D": "The water is too deep, so the ribbon should be cut thicker"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The water is too hot, so the temperature should be lowered): Water that is too hot causes the tissue to over-spread and even melt, so the bath is set a few degrees below the melting point of the wax. Water that is too cool causes wrinkles instead. Cleanliness and depth are unrelated."}, {"id": 1867, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "An RNA extract from a patient with suspected influenza is added to a master mix that contains Taq polymerase, primers, and dNTPs, but no reverse transcriptase. The cycler protocol is otherwise correct. What result is expected for a truly positive specimen?", "options": {"A": "Normal amplification, since Taq polymerase copies RNA templates efficiently at 72 °C.", "B": "Late amplification, because contaminating DNA in the extract serves as template instead.", "C": "No amplification, because the RNA target is never converted into cDNA.", "D": "Amplification with a much higher Ct, because RNA is unstable at annealing temperature."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No amplification, because the RNA target is never converted into cDNA.): Taq polymerase is a DNA-dependent enzyme and cannot copy an RNA template, so reverse transcriptase must first make cDNA. Without it a viral RNA target gives no product. Instability of RNA is not the reason, and a viral RNA target has no DNA counterpart to serve as template. The specimen would simply read as negative."}, {"id": 1868, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In a cell block laboratory, A laboratory builds cell blocks from small aspirates using a method in which the cell pellet is clotted with plasma and thrombin before processing. What is the main purpose of using this type of method in cell block preparation?", "options": {"A": "It keeps the sample fresh and unfixed for later flow cytometry analysis", "B": "It dissolves the red cells so the block contains only tumour cells in the end", "C": "It stains the nuclei before the pellet is embedded in paraffin wax", "D": "It holds the cells together in a clot so they survive processing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It holds the cells together in a clot so they survive processing): Plasma-thrombin clots, like agar embedding and automated systems, hold a small pellet together so that cells survive processing and sectioning. The method does not dissolve red cells, does not stain nuclei, and involves processing and fixation, so it is not used to keep a sample fresh for flow. Without a firm clot, cells from a small pellet are easily lost during processing."}, {"id": 1869, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "An automated extractor in the molecular laboratory has missed its scheduled maintenance by two weeks, and its last run showed a slightly lower yield. The operator is asked to run patient specimens today. Which action best fits quality assurance practice?", "options": {"A": "Run specimens since the yield is only slightly lower, and note it in the log.", "B": "Run patient specimens with an extra positive control and complete maintenance later.", "C": "Complete the maintenance and verify performance with controls before testing patient specimens.", "D": "Switch to manual extraction without documenting the change, since results are equivalent."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Complete the maintenance and verify performance with controls before testing patient specimens.): Instruments need scheduled maintenance, and an overdue one with a changed yield calls for maintenance and verification before patient use. Extra controls do not repair the instrument, noting a problem in the log does not solve it, and an undocumented method change bypasses evaluation of a major component."}, {"id": 1870, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A laboratory receives a chorionic villus sample and must give the referring obstetrician a result quickly, since a decision about the pregnancy is being made this week. The technologist explains that one preparation type yields results fast from cells already dividing in the villi. Which is it?", "options": {"A": "A long-term culture of the villi, which needs several days of growth.", "B": "A direct preparation of the villi, which needs no prior culture.", "C": "An amniocyte colony culture, which needs a flask of fluid cells.", "D": "A mitogen-stimulated culture of the villi, which needs 72 hours with phytohemagglutinin."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A direct preparation of the villi, which needs no prior culture.): Villi contain spontaneously dividing cells, so a direct preparation can be harvested quickly. Long-term culture takes days, colonies belong to amniotic fluid cultures, and a phytohemagglutinin culture is used to stimulate lymphocytes in blood rather than villi."}, {"id": 1871, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A resident asks how Rh immune globulin protects a mother who has not yet made anti-D. He suggests it works by blocking the mother's antibody production in the marrow. The technologist corrects him and describes the mechanism. Which mechanism does the technologist describe?", "options": {"A": "It clears D-positive fetal cells before immune response.", "B": "It suppresses maternal lymphocytes throughout the entire pregnancy.", "C": "It neutralizes maternal anti-D that has already formed in the plasma.", "D": "It converts fetal D-positive cells into D-negative cells before sensitization."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It clears D-positive fetal cells before immune response.): Rh immune globulin coats D-positive fetal cells and removes them before the mother's immune system can respond. It cannot reverse an existing immune response, and it does not modify fetal antigens or broadly suppress maternal lymphocytes."}, {"id": 1872, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Following a kidney transplant, one patient's urine contains many single decoy cells with homogeneous glassy inclusions and marginated chromatin. The screener recognizes the pattern, and the pathologist asks how the finding should be reported and what may follow clinically. Which response is correct?", "options": {"A": "Report decoy cells as a finding; SV40 large T staining or molecular testing confirms BK virus", "B": "Report high-grade urothelial carcinoma, and advise cystoscopy to search promptly for the tumour", "C": "Report atypical urothelial cells, and advise a repeat collection using a first morning void", "D": "Report cytomegalovirus infection, and advise Congo red staining to confirm the intranuclear inclusion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report decoy cells as a finding; SV40 large T staining or molecular testing confirms BK virus): Decoy cells are reported as a finding, and the clinician may send blood or urine for BK viral load, while SV40 large T immunostaining, which cross-reacts with BK, or molecular testing confirms identity. Calling carcinoma overcalls a viral effect. Atypia with a first morning repeat gives the wrong category and a poorer specimen. Congo red detects amyloid, not viral inclusions."}, {"id": 1873, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A targeted panel covers 6,000 bases. The run generated 2,400,000 aligned bases on target for one specimen, and the laboratory requires a mean depth of at least 300x. What is the mean depth for that specimen, and does it meet the requirement?", "options": {"A": "200x, from dividing by 12,000 since both strands count, so the minimum fails", "B": "400x, from dividing on-target bases by target size, so the minimum is met", "C": "4,000x, from dividing by 600 instead of 6,000, so the minimum is comfortably met", "D": "0.0025x, from dividing target size by on-target bases, so the minimum is not met"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (400x, from dividing on-target bases by target size, so the minimum is met): Mean depth = 2,400,000 / 6,000 = 400x, which exceeds 300x. Dividing by 600 gives a tenfold overestimate, and doubling the target for two strands is not part of the calculation. Inverting the ratio gives a meaningless fraction."}, {"id": 1874, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A neonatal unit asks the blood bank for components that are considered CMV-safe. In Canada, where suppliers routinely apply the same process to red cell and platelet components before storage, what is generally accepted as CMV-safe without extra testing for most patients?", "options": {"A": "Frozen components, since freezing destroys cytomegalovirus in stored units for good", "B": "Irradiated components, since radiation kills cytomegalovirus in the plasma of units", "C": "Washed components, since saline removes all cytomegalovirus from red cells and plasma", "D": "Prestorage leukoreduced components, since cytomegalovirus lives inside white cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Prestorage leukoreduced components, since cytomegalovirus lives inside white cells): CMV is carried in white cells, so universal prestorage leukoreduction makes components generally acceptable as CMV-safe. Irradiation is aimed at lymphocytes, not at CMV. Washing and freezing are not relied upon as CMV-safe processes in Canadian routine practice."}, {"id": 1875, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Technologists grade tube agglutination reactions from 0 to 4+ so that results are recorded consistently. A technologist reading a forward group sees, after gentle resuspension of the cell button, a single solid clump of cells with a clear supernatant. How should this reaction be graded?", "options": {"A": "3+, meaning several large agglutinates with a clear supernatant", "B": "2+, meaning medium agglutinates with a clear background", "C": "4+, meaning a single solid agglutinate with no free cells", "D": "Mixed field, meaning agglutinates among many free cells"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (4+, meaning a single solid agglutinate with no free cells): A 4+ reaction is one solid agglutinate with no free cells visible. Lower grades show progressively smaller agglutinates with more free cells present. Mixed field describes two cell populations and would show free cells beside clumps."}, {"id": 1876, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A fine needle aspirate of a breast lump from a 58-year-old woman yields epithelial cells, and four of them are shown in the image at equal magnification. Which cell has the nuclear combination most worrying for malignancy and most deserving of pathologist review?", "options": {"A": "Cell 1, because its large nucleus has coarse chromatin, an irregular outline and prominent nucleoli", "B": "Cell 2, because its large nucleus has coarse chromatin, an irregular outline and prominent nucleoli", "C": "Cell 3, because its large nucleus has coarse chromatin, an irregular outline and prominent nucleoli", "D": "Cell 4, because its large nucleus has coarse chromatin, an irregular outline and prominent nucleoli"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cell 3, because its large nucleus has coarse chromatin, an irregular outline and prominent nucleoli): Cell 3 shows a high N:C ratio, coarse chromatin, an irregular nuclear outline and nucleoli, so several malignant criteria occur together. Cells 1, 2 and 4 have small to modest nuclei with fine chromatin and smooth outlines, which is the bland appearance of benign ductal cells.", "image_type": "cell_micrograph", "image_params": {"cells": [{"label": "1", "nc_ratio": 0.15, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "round"}, {"label": "2", "nc_ratio": 0.2, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "round"}, {"label": "3", "nc_ratio": 0.65, "chromatin": "coarse", "outline": "irregular", "nucleoli": 2, "cell": "round"}, {"label": "4", "nc_ratio": 0.25, "chromatin": "fine", "outline": "smooth", "nucleoli": 0, "cell": "round"}], "caption": "Cells at equal magnification", "context": "breast", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1876.png"}, {"id": 1877, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A laboratory report listing karyotypes for four referred patients is shown in the image. A technologist is asked to interpret the result for the patient referred for developmental delay. Read the ISCN notation in that patient's row carefully. Which description matches the ISCN result listed for that patient?", "options": {"A": "A female with a deletion of the long arm of chromosome 5 in twenty cells", "B": "A female with loss of part of the short arm of chromosome 5", "C": "A male with an extra copy of chromosome 5 in twenty cells examined", "D": "A female with a balanced translocation involving the short arm of chromosome 5"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A female with loss of part of the short arm of chromosome 5): the result 46,XX,del(5)(p15.2) describes a female with a terminal deletion of the short arm of chromosome 5. The p symbol marks the short arm rather than the long arm, XX excludes a male, and the entry has no translocation symbol and the count is normal at 46.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Karyotype results, referred patients", "columns": ["Patient", "Referral", "ISCN result"], "rows": [["1", "Primary amenorrhea", "45,X[20]"], ["2", "Developmental delay", "46,XX,del(5)(p15.2)[20]"], ["3", "Positive screen", "47,XX,+21[20]"], ["4", "Azoospermia", "47,XXY[20]"]], "caption": "Constitutional karyotypes"}, "image": "question_images/q_1877.png"}, {"id": 1878, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A technologist is explaining the bilirubin method and why two results are reported. The direct bilirubin approximates one form, and the total bilirubin includes both forms because a reagent brings the second form into the reaction. What does the reagent do, and which form is this?", "options": {"A": "An accelerator converts conjugated bilirubin to urobilinogen so total includes stercobilin", "B": "An accelerator brings unconjugated bilirubin into the diazo reaction so total includes both forms", "C": "A fluoride reagent stops glycolysis so total includes both bilirubin and glucose", "D": "An oxidase reagent forms peroxide so total includes both bilirubin and cholesterol"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An accelerator brings unconjugated bilirubin into the diazo reaction so total includes both forms): Direct bilirubin approximates conjugated bilirubin in the diazo reaction, and an accelerator lets unconjugated bilirubin react too, so total includes both. Conversion to urobilinogen happens in the gut, and fluoride and oxidase reagents belong to glucose and cholesterol methods."}, {"id": 1879, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A collector finds that a batch of samples has visible in vitro hemolysis. Reviewing technique, the supervisor identifies that a very small 25 gauge needle was used with a large syringe, and tubes were shaken vigorously after collection. Which corrected practice reduces in vitro hemolysis?", "options": {"A": "Use a 16 gauge needle and mix tubes by tapping the side", "B": "Use a 27 gauge needle and mix tubes by vigorous shaking", "C": "Use a 25 gauge needle and force blood through the syringe", "D": "Use a 21 to 23 gauge needle and mix tubes by gentle inversion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use a 21 to 23 gauge needle and mix tubes by gentle inversion): A needle of about 21 to 23 gauge, gentle inversion mixing, full tube filling and dry skin all lower hemolysis. Very small needles and vigorous shaking or forcing blood through a syringe damage red cells. A needle that is much too large can also cause trauma to the vein, so the standard range is preferred."}, {"id": 1880, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory runs five tenfold standards in duplicate to check a new real-time PCR assay before clinical use, with the mean Ct results shown in the image. The validation lead asks the technologist to judge the assay performance. Based on the Ct change per tenfold dilution, what does the standard curve indicate about amplification efficiency?", "options": {"A": "Efficiency near 80 percent, below the usual acceptable range of 90 to 110 percent", "B": "Efficiency near 120 percent, above the usual acceptable range of 90 to 110 percent", "C": "Efficiency near 98 percent, within the usual range of 90 to 110 percent", "D": "Efficiency cannot be judged unless a no-template control is added to the standard series"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Efficiency near 98 percent, within the usual range of 90 to 110 percent): The Ct rises by about 3.4 cycles per tenfold dilution, giving a slope near negative 3.37 and an efficiency of about 98 percent, within the usual 90 to 110 percent range. Efficiencies of 80 or 120 percent would need a slope far steeper or flatter, and the slope is calculated from the standards alone.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Standard curve", "columns": ["Standard", "Copies/mL", "Mean Ct"], "rows": [["Std 1", "1 x10^6", "20.1"], ["Std 2", "1 x10^5", "23.5"], ["Std 3", "1 x10^4", "26.8"], ["Std 4", "1 x10^3", "30.2"], ["Std 5", "1 x10^2", "33.6"]], "caption": "Standard curve Ct values"}, "image": "question_images/q_1880.png"}, {"id": 1881, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A thyroid aspirate is finalized as malignant, consistent with papillary carcinoma, late on a Friday afternoon. The ordering clinician's office is about to close for the weekend, and the report has already been entered in the system. What should the laboratory do about communication of this result?", "options": {"A": "Leave a voicemail without documentation, since documentation is done by the ordering office", "B": "Wait for the surgeon's request, because the laboratory reports only when asked", "C": "Communicate to the clinician according to laboratory policy and document the call in the record", "D": "Mail the report with routine correspondence, because the result will be seen on Monday"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Communicate to the clinician according to laboratory policy and document the call in the record): A malignant or suspicious thyroid result is communicated to the ordering clinician according to laboratory policy, and the call is documented in the record. Routine mailing delays care. An undocumented voicemail leaves no proof of notification. Waiting to be asked ignores the laboratory's own reporting duty."}, {"id": 1882, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A technologist is teaching a student how to recognize the earliest recognizable cell of the granulocyte line on a marrow smear. This cell has a high nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio, fine open chromatin, and one or more nucleoli. Which cell is being described in this teaching example?", "options": {"A": "Myeloblast, the earliest recognizable granulocyte precursor, with open chromatin and visible nucleoli", "B": "Promyelocyte, the stage that shows prominent primary azurophilic granules and a larger cell size", "C": "Band neutrophil, the stage with a curved nucleus of even thickness and pink cytoplasm", "D": "Myelocyte, the stage where specific granules appear and nucleoli are no longer visible"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Myeloblast, the earliest recognizable granulocyte precursor, with open chromatin and visible nucleoli): The myeloblast has a high nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio, fine chromatin and nucleoli. A promyelocyte adds prominent primary granules. The myelocyte has lost its nucleoli, and the band shows a curved dense nucleus, so none of these match the description."}, {"id": 1883, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A conventional smear from a 37-year-old is being assessed under the microscope. It is well fixed with good staining and little blood. The technologist must judge whether it has enough squamous cells to be satisfactory. What is the approximate minimum on a conventional smear?", "options": {"A": "About 8,000 to 12,000 well-visualized squamous cells across the smear", "B": "About 50,000 well-visualized squamous cells, spread over both halves of the slide", "C": "About 1,000 to 2,000 well-visualized cells, provided an endocervical component is present", "D": "About 5,000 well-visualized squamous cells, the same standard used for liquid-based slides"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 8,000 to 12,000 well-visualized squamous cells across the smear): A satisfactory conventional smear has about 8,000 to 12,000 well-preserved squamous cells. The lower 5,000 figure applies to liquid-based slides. The other counts are not adequacy standards."}, {"id": 1884, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A report shows a cholesterol of 5.8 mmol/L flagged against a treatment target, while a creatinine on the same report is flagged against a range from healthy people. A student asks why these two flags come from different kinds of limits. How do clinical decision limits differ from reference intervals?", "options": {"A": "They are wider than reference intervals and vary with the analyzer chosen by the laboratory", "B": "They are set from outcome studies, and not by testing a healthy population", "C": "They are calculated as the central 95% of results from healthy volunteers", "D": "They are fixed by the manufacturer for each reagent lot and instrument"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (They are set from outcome studies, and not by testing a healthy population): Decision limits such as cholesterol targets come from outcome studies that link values to risk. Reference intervals describe the central 95% of healthy people. Neither is set per reagent lot, and decision limits do not change simply because a different analyzer is used."}, {"id": 1885, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A maturation index reads 80 / 20 / 0 on a sample from a 71-year-old. A student asks which pattern this represents and whether the value could appear on a young woman's mid-cycle sample. What does 80 / 20 / 0 show?", "options": {"A": "A completely atrophic pattern, with mostly parabasal cells and no superficial cells", "B": "A pregnancy pattern, with mostly navicular cells and few parabasal cells", "C": "A strong estrogen pattern, with mostly superficial cells and no parabasal cells", "D": "A luteal pattern, with mostly intermediate cells and scattered superficial cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A completely atrophic pattern, with mostly parabasal cells and no superficial cells): The index is written parabasal, intermediate, superficial, so 80 percent parabasal with no superficial cells is a fully atrophic pattern. A young woman at mid-cycle would show the reverse. Pregnancy and the luteal phase give mostly intermediate cells."}, {"id": 1886, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A trainee asks why cytology slides are labelled in pencil on the frosted end rather than with a standard ballpoint pen. A student found a slide where the identification was unreadable, and the supervisor asks why pencil is standard. What property of ballpoint ink explains the rule?", "options": {"A": "It can dissolve in alcohol and disappear during the staining steps", "B": "It can melt when the coverslip mounting medium is applied", "C": "It can fade under the microscope lamp during long screening periods", "D": "It can react with hematoxylin and turn the whole slide blue"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It can dissolve in alcohol and disappear during the staining steps): Pen ink dissolves in the alcohols of the staining sequence, so identification could be lost. Pencil is not affected. The other reasons are not why pencil is required."}, {"id": 1887, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A prenatal amniotic fluid specimen arrives in a sterile conical tube after an amniocentesis at 16 weeks, and the technologist checks whether the volume meets the usual requirement before setting up flasks and coverslip dishes. Approximately what volume of amniotic fluid is typically needed for cytogenetic culture?", "options": {"A": "About 15 to 20 millilitres, which allows several independent cultures to be set up", "B": "About 0.1 millilitre, which is enough for one very small culture", "C": "About 50 microlitres, which matches the volume used for a routine blood culture", "D": "About 100 millilitres, which is required to recover enough fetal cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 15 to 20 millilitres, which allows several independent cultures to be set up): Amniotic fluid needs are on the order of 15 to 20 millilitres so several independent cultures can be prepared. Tiny volumes leave no room for error, and 100 millilitres is excessive and not required."}, {"id": 1888, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A blood specimen for chromosome analysis was left on the dashboard of a courier's vehicle for several hours on a hot summer afternoon. It arrives in a correct green-top sodium heparin tube with proper labelling and a completed requisition. What should the technologist recognize about this specimen before setting up cultures?", "options": {"A": "Heat converts the heparin to EDTA, so the culture will grow but show extra bands", "B": "Extreme heat destroys cell viability, so the specimen may fail to grow", "C": "Heat activates the lymphocytes, so the specimen is likely to give an excellent culture", "D": "Heat is harmless when the tube is heparinized, so no comment is needed on receipt"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Extreme heat destroys cell viability, so the specimen may fail to grow): Extreme heat, like freezing, destroys viability, so the specimen may not grow, and the condition should be recorded and the clinician informed. Correct labelling and heparin do not protect the cells from heat damage."}, {"id": 1889, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with hemolytic anemia and a recent transfusion has an HbA1c result of 4.8 percent, yet home glucose readings are consistently high. The physician asks the laboratory to explain the mismatch and suggest an alternative. What should the technologist say?", "options": {"A": "Extended red cell lifespan makes HbA1c unreliable, so repeat it with a fasting sample", "B": "The method has measured ketones by mistake, so use the nitroprusside test", "C": "The specimen is lipemic, so ultracentrifuge it and report the same HbA1c", "D": "Shortened red cell lifespan makes HbA1c unreliable, so use fructosamine or plasma glucose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shortened red cell lifespan makes HbA1c unreliable, so use fructosamine or plasma glucose): Anything that changes red cell lifespan, such as hemolysis, blood loss or transfusion, makes HbA1c unreliable, and fructosamine or plasma glucose can be used instead. Fasting is not needed for HbA1c, and ketones and lipemia are unrelated to this mismatch."}, {"id": 1890, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A node aspirate from a young woman shows tight clusters of epithelioid histiocytes without necrosis, and no organisms are seen. The clinician asks the technologist what the pattern suggests and what to add. Which response is the most appropriate for the laboratory to give?", "options": {"A": "Metastatic squamous carcinoma, so add keratin stains and a head examination.", "B": "Reactive hyperplasia, so no further testing is needed on this material.", "C": "Granulomatous inflammation, so add acid-fast stains and culture to exclude infection.", "D": "Monotonous lymphoma, so send immediately for flow cytometry in alcohol."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Granulomatous inflammation, so add acid-fast stains and culture to exclude infection.): Clusters of epithelioid histiocytes form granulomas, which suggest sarcoidosis or infection such as tuberculosis, so special stains and culture are added. A carcinoma or lymphoma would show very different cells. Reactive hyperplasia does not produce granulomas, and alcohol would ruin flow cytometry."}, {"id": 1891, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient's plasma glucose two hours after a 75 g oral glucose load is 9.2 mmol/L, higher than normal but lower than the diabetes cut-off. The technologist is asked to name the category and give the two-hour range that defines it. Which category and range apply to this result?", "options": {"A": "Impaired glucose tolerance, a two-hour value from 7.8 to 11.0 mmol/L", "B": "Overt diabetes mellitus, a two-hour value from 11.1 to 13.0 mmol/L", "C": "Impaired fasting glucose, a two-hour value from 6.1 to 6.9 mmol/L", "D": "Normal glucose tolerance, a two-hour value below 7.8 mmol/L"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Impaired glucose tolerance, a two-hour value from 7.8 to 11.0 mmol/L): A two-hour value from 7.8 to 11.0 mmol/L is impaired glucose tolerance, a form of prediabetes. The 6.1 to 6.9 range describes fasting values. A two-hour value of 11.1 or higher meets the diabetes criterion, and a value of 9.2 is above the normal limit of 7.8."}, {"id": 1892, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A run report states that a base has an estimated one in one hundred chance of being wrong, and the run's quality table lists scores. A trainee must convert this statement into a Phred quality score to compare with the run's threshold. Which score does this error probability correspond to?", "options": {"A": "A Phred score of 100 for that base call", "B": "A Phred score of 20 for that base call", "C": "A Phred score of 10 for that base call", "D": "A Phred score of 30 for that base call"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A Phred score of 20 for that base call): Phred scores are logarithmic: Q20 means a 1 in 100 chance of error, and Q30 means 1 in 1,000. Q10 would mean a 1 in 10 chance of error. A score of 100 is not the reciprocal of the error chance."}, {"id": 1893, "domain": "hematology", "question": "An automated analyzer flags the blood of a patient recovering from acute hemorrhage. The smear shows larger red cells that appear blue grey rather than pink on a Wright stain, and a supravital stain shows reticulum in the same cells. What are these cells, and what do they indicate?", "options": {"A": "Spherocytes, which indicate immune destruction or a red cell membrane defect", "B": "Target cells, which indicate hemoglobin C, thalassemia or liver disease", "C": "Stomatocytes, which indicate an inherited or alcohol related membrane disorder", "D": "Polychromatic reticulocytes, showing the marrow is releasing young red cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Polychromatic reticulocytes, showing the marrow is releasing young red cells): Blue grey larger cells are polychromasia, the Wright stain appearance of reticulocytes, which a supravital stain confirms by showing reticulum. It shows the marrow is responding to blood loss. Target cells, spherocytes and stomatocytes are recognized by other shapes."}, {"id": 1894, "domain": "histology", "question": "The laboratory receives several nerve and renal biopsies for ultrastructural study this week. A technologist is asked to prepare a fixative containing glutaraldehyde for a batch of renal and nerve biopsies. Which precaution is most consistent with the hazards of this chemical?", "options": {"A": "Handle it without gloves because it is not absorbed through the skin", "B": "Store it uncapped beside the cryostat to prevent any buildup of pressure", "C": "Use it with good ventilation and keep the container closed when stored", "D": "Pour it into an open dish on the bench so the vapour clears quickly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use it with good ventilation and keep the container closed when stored): Glutaraldehyde is a sensitizer and irritant whose vapour is harmful, so it is used with good ventilation and stored closed. Open dishes, uncapped storage, and bare skin contact all raise exposure."}, {"id": 1895, "domain": "histology", "question": "In a Prussian blue stain of a liver biopsy from a patient with suspected hemochromatosis, the section was deparaffinized and treated with acid ferrocyanide for twenty minutes. Which colours should the technologist expect for hemosiderin, nuclei and background cytoplasm when the run is correct?", "options": {"A": "Hemosiderin red, nuclei brown, and cytoplasm colourless in the finished section", "B": "Hemosiderin bright blue, nuclei red, and cytoplasm pink in the finished section", "C": "Hemosiderin black, nuclei blue, and cytoplasm yellow in the finished section", "D": "Hemosiderin magenta, nuclei purple, and cytoplasm green in the finished section"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hemosiderin bright blue, nuclei red, and cytoplasm pink in the finished section): Acid potassium ferrocyanide forms a blue pigment with ferric iron, and nuclear fast red gives red nuclei and pink cytoplasm. Black, magenta and green belong to other methods."}, {"id": 1896, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A supervisor reviews the safety programme and asks staff at the monthly bench meeting to name the requirements for working with the potassium chloride, stains, trypsin and fixatives used in the cytogenetics laboratory. Which requirement is correct under the workplace hazardous materials information system?", "options": {"A": "Only fixatives need labels, since stains and salt solutions are not hazardous materials", "B": "Containers must be correctly labelled, and safety data sheets must be available to staff", "C": "Labels may be removed once the bottle is opened, since the contents are then known", "D": "Safety data sheets stay in a locked office, since staff do not need to read them"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Containers must be correctly labelled, and safety data sheets must be available to staff): Reagents must be labelled under the workplace hazardous materials system and safety data sheets must be accessible. Labels are needed on all hazardous reagents, not just fixatives, and sheets must be available to staff."}, {"id": 1897, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A mold from a wound culture has delicate conidiophores that the technologist wants to see with the spores still attached. Two earlier tease mounts in lactophenol cotton blue shredded the structures into scattered fragments. The technologist decides to lift growth from the colony surface without crushing it. Which technique is the better choice?", "options": {"A": "The cellophane tape method, which keeps the mold structures in their original arrangement", "B": "An India ink preparation, which preserves conidial heads as dark halos", "C": "A KOH preparation left overnight, which dissolves the spores into a clear solution", "D": "A larger amount of colony teased harder in stain with extra pressure on the coverslip"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The cellophane tape method, which keeps the mold structures in their original arrangement): Tape lifts the fragile growth intact so the conidiophores stay arranged, and it is useful for sticky or fragile molds. Harder teasing, India ink, and overnight KOH would further damage or dissolve the structures."}, {"id": 1898, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "The transfusion service uses a validated electronic crossmatch system. A patient's antibody screen is negative with no history of clinically significant antibodies, but only one ABO group result exists for this patient, taken from today's sample. What should the technologist do before using the electronic crossmatch?", "options": {"A": "Proceed, because one ABO determination and a negative screen are enough for electronic crossmatching", "B": "Override the system block and release the unit, since the screen shows no antibody", "C": "Obtain a second ABO determination from another sample or record before using the electronic crossmatch", "D": "Use an immediate spin crossmatch instead, because electronic systems require a positive antibody screen"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Obtain a second ABO determination from another sample or record before using the electronic crossmatch): Electronic crossmatching requires two separate ABO determinations, which can come from the current sample and a historical result. With only one, the requirement is not met, so a second determination must be obtained. Overriding a system block defeats the safeguard, and electronic crossmatch needs a negative rather than a positive screen."}, {"id": 1899, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The isolate came from a urine sample. A urine isolate is a lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod that forms large, mucoid colonies on MacConkey agar. Which combination of biochemical reactions best fits Klebsiella pneumoniae, and helps separate it from Escherichia coli in the routine identification scheme?", "options": {"A": "Indole negative, urease negative, and motile on the motility medium", "B": "Indole positive, urease positive, and nonmotile on the motility medium", "C": "Indole negative, urease positive, and nonmotile on the motility medium", "D": "Indole positive, urease negative, and motile on the motility medium in routine practice"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Indole negative, urease positive, and nonmotile on the motility medium): Klebsiella pneumoniae is indole negative, urease positive, and nonmotile, with mucoid colonies. Escherichia coli is indole positive and motile. The other combinations mix features of the two organisms and do not match either."}, {"id": 1900, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A patient has a high platelet count, and molecular testing reports a positive JAK2 V617F mutation with no BCR::ABL1 fusion detected. A student proposes that this proves chronic myeloid leukemia because the marrow is hypercellular. How should the technologist explain the result when discussing the case?", "options": {"A": "It confirms chronic myeloid leukemia, since JAK2 V617F is the defining mutation of that myeloproliferative neoplasm", "B": "It confirms acute promyelocytic leukemia, since JAK2 V617F creates the PML::RARA fusion gene", "C": "It confirms a leukemoid reaction, since JAK2 V617F is produced by severe bacterial infection", "D": "It points to another myeloproliferative neoplasm, since chronic myeloid leukemia needs the Philadelphia chromosome"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It points to another myeloproliferative neoplasm, since chronic myeloid leukemia needs the Philadelphia chromosome): JAK2 V617F is tied to polycythemia vera and essential thrombocythemia, and CML is separated from them by the Philadelphia chromosome and BCR::ABL1. APL is defined by PML::RARA, and a leukemoid reaction is not a mutation-driven disorder."}, {"id": 1901, "domain": "histology", "question": "The last frozen section of the day has been read and the pathologist has left. At the end of a frozen section session, the technologist prepares to clean the cryostat chamber and dispose of the blade. Which action correctly completes this shutdown?", "options": {"A": "Spray with formalin and close the lid so it fixes the debris", "B": "Wipe with water only and leave the blade in the holder for the next day", "C": "Defrost the chamber with the specimen still inside to keep it cold", "D": "Clean with 70 percent alcohol and dispose of the blade as a sharp"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clean with 70 percent alcohol and dispose of the blade as a sharp): After frozen sections, the cryostat is cleaned with an approved disinfectant, often 70 percent alcohol, and the blade goes to a sharps container. Water alone does not disinfect, formalin spray is hazardous, and specimens should not stay inside."}, {"id": 1902, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory report describes one abnormality as an inversion of a segment of a chromosome and another as a translocation between two chromosomes. Under which broad category of variant do these two changes fall? The referring geneticist wants the correct classification group for the report.", "options": {"A": "Single nucleotide variants, which change one base at a time within a codon", "B": "Copy number variants, which gain or lose the segment of DNA involved", "C": "Structural variants, which rearrange larger segments of DNA within or between chromosomes", "D": "Splice-site variants, which alter how the RNA transcript is cut and joined after transcription"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Structural variants, which rearrange larger segments of DNA within or between chromosomes): Inversions and translocations are structural variants. SNVs change single bases, and CNVs change how many copies of a segment exist, which an inversion does not. Splice-site variants concern RNA processing."}, {"id": 1903, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A patient is asked to provide a sputum sample for culture and is unsure how to collect it. The nurse explains the steps for a good lower-respiratory specimen. Which set of instructions is correct? The patient has a productive cough and has been told that the quality of the sample strongly affects the final culture result.", "options": {"A": "Rinse the mouth, cough deeply, and collect the first morning sample in a sterile container", "B": "Gargle with mouthwash, spit saliva, and collect a sample at bedtime", "C": "Drink hot water, clear the throat, and use any clean cup", "D": "Swab the tongue, cough gently, and place the sample in a dry paper envelope"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rinse the mouth, cough deeply, and collect the first morning sample in a sterile container): Rinsing the mouth and coughing from deep in the chest gives a lower-airway specimen, and first morning samples are the richest. Saliva, tongue swabs, and unsterile containers contaminate the sample with oral flora or environmental organisms."}, {"id": 1904, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with severe abdominal pain has serum amylase 4 times the upper reference limit and a normal lipase. The patient also has swelling of the salivary glands and fever. The physician asks what the results suggest and which marker is generally preferred. Which interpretation and marker should the technologist give?", "options": {"A": "Amylase is from the liver, and ALT is preferred for pancreatitis", "B": "Amylase confirms pancreatitis, and lipase is not useful in any pancreatic disease", "C": "Amylase is from the heart, and troponin is preferred for pancreatitis", "D": "Amylase may be from salivary glands as in mumps, and lipase is preferred for pancreatitis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Amylase may be from salivary glands as in mumps, and lipase is preferred for pancreatitis): Amylase is also found in salivary glands and rises in mumps, while lipase is more specific for the pancreas and is generally preferred, and here it is normal. Amylase does not come from the heart or liver, and troponin and ALT do not diagnose pancreatitis."}, {"id": 1905, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A yeast recovered from a blood culture bottle drawn from a patient on prolonged broad-spectrum antibiotics is germ tube negative, forms small colonies on Sabouraud agar, and lacks pseudohyphae on microscopy. Which species is suggested, and what is a key clinical feature of this organism?", "options": {"A": "Candida dubliniensis, which is easily identified by a positive germ tube test at three hours", "B": "Candida glabrata, which is often resistant to fluconazole and needs biochemical or mass spectrometry identification", "C": "Candida albicans, which is usually susceptible to fluconazole and forms germ tubes within three hours", "D": "Cryptococcus neoformans, which is identified by a brown colour on niger seed agar"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Candida glabrata, which is often resistant to fluconazole and needs biochemical or mass spectrometry identification): C. glabrata is germ tube negative, small, without pseudohyphae, and frequently fluconazole resistant. It is identified by assimilation panels, chromogenic agar, or MALDI-TOF. C. albicans and C. dubliniensis are germ tube positive, and Cryptococcus is a capsulated, urease-positive yeast."}, {"id": 1906, "domain": "histology", "question": "A bone biopsy is in an acid decalcifier, and the technologist records a daily chemical endpoint test on the used fluid, with the fluid changed after each reading. The log is shown in the image. The pathologist is waiting for sections. What is the most appropriate decision at the 72 hour reading?", "options": {"A": "Rinse and process the specimen now, since the precipitate has decreased at each reading", "B": "Continue decalcifying in fresh fluid and retest, since calcium remains in the used fluid", "C": "Switch to a stronger mineral acid so decalcification finishes within the next few hours", "D": "Stop decalcification and neutralize, since faint cloudiness shows the endpoint was just passed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continue decalcifying in fresh fluid and retest, since calcium remains in the used fluid): A precipitate with ammonium oxalate means calcium is still being removed, so even faint cloudiness shows decalcification is incomplete and should continue with a repeat test. Decreasing precipitate is not an endpoint, a stronger acid risks damaging tissue and later antigen testing, and an endpoint requires a clear result.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Decalcification endpoint log", "columns": ["Time (h)", "Used fluid test", "Reading"], "rows": [["24", "Ammonium oxalate", "Heavy precipitate"], ["48", "Ammonium oxalate", "Moderate precipitate"], ["72", "Ammonium oxalate", "Faint cloudiness"]], "caption": "Chemical endpoint test during decalcification"}, "image": "question_images/q_1906.png"}, {"id": 1907, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The isolate was recovered on day two. A throat culture grows a gram-negative diplococcus that is oxidase positive and DNase positive. It does not ferment any sugars and is also positive for butyrate esterase. Which organism does this pattern indicate in a respiratory specimen?", "options": {"A": "Moraxella catarrhalis, a gram-negative diplococcus that does not ferment sugars", "B": "Haemophilus influenzae, a gram-negative coccobacillus that needs both X and V factors", "C": "Neisseria gonorrhoeae, a gram-negative diplococcus that oxidizes glucose only", "D": "Neisseria meningitidis, a gram-negative diplococcus that ferments glucose and maltose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Moraxella catarrhalis, a gram-negative diplococcus that does not ferment sugars): Moraxella catarrhalis is a DNase-positive, butyrate esterase-positive diplococcus that does not ferment sugars. Neisseria meningitidis uses glucose and maltose, Neisseria gonorrhoeae uses glucose only, and Haemophilus is a coccobacillus requiring growth factors."}, {"id": 1908, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with nephrotic syndrome has 6 g of protein in a 24 hour urine collection, a serum albumin of 18 g/L and a serum protein electrophoresis tracing. The technologist reviews the pattern to check that it fits the clinical history. Which pattern is expected?", "options": {"A": "Low albumin with a narrow gamma spike from a monoclonal protein", "B": "Normal albumin with beta-gamma bridging from a raised IgA level", "C": "Low albumin with a prominent alpha-2 band from retained large proteins", "D": "High albumin with a flat alpha-2 band from loss of large proteins"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Low albumin with a prominent alpha-2 band from retained large proteins): In nephrotic syndrome albumin is lost in urine and the alpha-2 band is prominent because large proteins such as alpha-2 macroglobulin are retained. A narrow gamma spike suggests a monoclonal protein, and beta-gamma bridging is a feature of cirrhosis."}, {"id": 1909, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A physician sends a lithium level for a patient who takes lithium twice daily. At what time is a lithium level usually collected relative to the last dose? The pharmacist asks the technologist to confirm the correct timing for this drug level.", "options": {"A": "About 30 minutes after the last dose, when the peak absorption is reached", "B": "Immediately after the next dose, so that the level includes the new drug", "C": "About 4 hours after the last dose, when the distribution phase has ended", "D": "About 12 hours after the last dose, when the level is at a stable point"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 12 hours after the last dose, when the level is at a stable point): Lithium levels are usually drawn about 12 hours after the last dose, and the requisition should state the last dose time and the collection time. Levels at other times can give misleading results and would require a comment."}, {"id": 1910, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A patient with a suspected clone of 10 percent abnormal cells has peripheral blood tested by array. The platform reports no copy number changes, although the clinical picture is strong. The geneticist asks whether a negative array can be relied upon in this situation. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "No, because arrays detect mosaicism after abnormal cells are grown in culture and are dividing", "B": "Yes, because the array tests each cell separately and counts every clone", "C": "Yes, because arrays detect any abnormal clone however small the cell fraction", "D": "No, since low-level mosaicism can be missed depending on platform and abnormal cell fraction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No, since low-level mosaicism can be missed depending on platform and abnormal cell fraction): Arrays measure the average across all DNA in the sample, so a small abnormal population may not shift the signal enough to be called. They do not analyze single cells, and culture of dividing cells is not required because DNA is extracted directly from the blood."}, {"id": 1911, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist cutting a frozen section uses a cold anti-roll plate and then lifts the section directly onto a room-temperature slide. The section adheres at once. What role does the warmth of the slide play here, and what happens next?", "options": {"A": "It keeps the section frozen so the tissue can be stored for months", "B": "It melts the section onto the glass, which is then quickly fixed and stained", "C": "It forms hematein on the slide before the fast stain is applied", "D": "It thaws the section, which is then processed through overnight wax cycles"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It melts the section onto the glass, which is then quickly fixed and stained): Touching the room-temperature slide melts the section so it adheres, and it is then fixed and stained rapidly, usually with a fast H&E. Overnight processing defeats the purpose of a frozen section, and the slide does not keep tissue frozen."}, {"id": 1912, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A submandibular FNA in a 55-year-old shows small dark cells with scant cytoplasm arranged around glassy globules, producing a sieve-like pattern on the air-dried smears. Mucin and oncocytes are not present, and the background contains no lymphocytes. Which salivary gland tumour is best matched by these features?", "options": {"A": "Adenoid cystic carcinoma, a malignant salivary tumour of small basaloid cells.", "B": "Warthin tumour, a benign tumour with oncocytes, lymphocytes and cystic debris.", "C": "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma, a malignant tumour with mucus-producing and squamoid cells.", "D": "Pleomorphic adenoma, a benign tumour with fibrillar magenta stroma and myoepithelial cells."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Adenoid cystic carcinoma, a malignant salivary tumour of small basaloid cells.): Small basaloid cells around hyaline globules in a cribriform pattern are typical of adenoid cystic carcinoma. Pleomorphic adenoma shows magenta fibrillar stroma. Warthin tumour needs oncocytes and lymphocytes, and mucoepidermoid carcinoma needs mucinous and squamoid cells."}, {"id": 1913, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A urine specimen shows high-grade urothelial carcinoma cells, and some of them have dense, keratinized cytoplasm. The screener asks the pathologist whether the report should mention this finding, since some tumours show squamous or glandular differentiation. Should the pathologist include this feature in the report, and why?", "options": {"A": "Yes, squamous differentiation should be noted because it can affect treatment", "B": "Yes, but only by reclassifying the case as low-grade urothelial neoplasia", "C": "No, keratinized cytoplasm indicates vaginal squame contamination, so it is ignored", "D": "No, differentiation is assessed only on cystectomy specimens and not on urine"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, squamous differentiation should be noted because it can affect treatment): High-grade urothelial carcinoma may show squamous or glandular differentiation, and the report should mention it because it can affect treatment. Differentiation is not restricted to surgical specimens. Dense keratinized malignant cells are not merely contaminants. Differentiation does not turn a high-grade tumour into low-grade neoplasia."}, {"id": 1914, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A hospital receives a request for red cells donated by a patient's brother for a directed donation. The transfusion service asks which product modification is required for components donated by blood relatives, because relatives may share HLA types that permit donor lymphocytes to survive. What is required?", "options": {"A": "Washing to remove plasma proteins before issue to the recipient's bedside", "B": "Volume reduction to prevent circulatory overload in the recipient during infusion", "C": "Irradiation to prevent graft-versus-host disease caused by donor lymphocytes", "D": "Antigen-negative selection to prevent alloimmunization to Kidd antigens in the recipient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Irradiation to prevent graft-versus-host disease caused by donor lymphocytes): Components from blood relatives require irradiation because shared HLA types can let donor lymphocytes escape recognition and cause TA-GVHD. Washing is for allergic reactions, volume reduction is for infants at risk of overload, and antigen-negative selection deals with alloimmunization rather than lymphocyte activity."}, {"id": 1915, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Reviewing a variant call file from a tumour run, a scientist notes a single nucleotide change supported by twelve reads that all map to the forward strand only, with no reads from the reverse strand. The base quality at that position is low. Which filter would normally remove this call as an artefact?", "options": {"A": "A barcode filter, since real variants carry a different index for each read", "B": "A strand bias filter, since real variants appear on both strands", "C": "A population frequency filter, since real variants are seen in every population", "D": "A tumour content filter, since real variants require an estimated purity above ninety percent"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A strand bias filter, since real variants appear on both strands): Variants seen on only one strand, at very low quality, or in known problem regions are removed as artefacts. Real variants normally have supporting reads from both strands. Frequency filters address a different question, and purity and barcodes do not distinguish this pattern."}, {"id": 1916, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A student compares air-dried and wet-fixed cervical cells under the microscope. The air-dried cells are noticeably flattened and spread out. The trainee has been given one slide of each type on the same microscope, so the comparison is direct. Which description matches how air-dried cells appear compared with wet-fixed cells?", "options": {"A": "They look identical in size, with the same rounded three-dimensional shape", "B": "They look larger and flatter, with clear cytoplasmic texture", "C": "They look shrunken and dark, with cytoplasm that is orange in all cells", "D": "They look smaller and rounder, with sharp and crisp nuclear chromatin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (They look larger and flatter, with clear cytoplasmic texture): Air-dried cells flatten and spread, so they look larger and show clear cytoplasmic texture. Rounded, crisp cells are the result of wet fixation. The colour of the cytoplasm depends on the stain, not on drying."}, {"id": 1917, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A resident asks why a normal interphase FISH result on a leukemia sample cannot be used to say that the patient's cells have no chromosomal abnormalities at all, since hundreds of nuclei were scored and looked entirely normal. Which statement gives the correct reason?", "options": {"A": "FISH tests only the targeted region and says nothing about the rest of the genome", "B": "FISH is unable to detect any abnormality that is present in interphase cells", "C": "FISH examines every chromosome, but only in dividing cells", "D": "FISH results are valid only when a chromosomal array has also been normal for the patient"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (FISH tests only the targeted region and says nothing about the rest of the genome): A probe reports only on the sequence it targets, so other abnormalities elsewhere are unaddressed. Interphase FISH scores non-dividing cells and does detect targeted changes. Array results are independent and are not a precondition for interpreting FISH."}, {"id": 1918, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A thick, heavily keratinized skin scraping from the sole of a foot barely clears after ten minutes in 10 percent KOH at room temperature, and the fungal elements stay hidden behind squamous debris. The technologist wants to shorten the clearing time without heating the slide. Which reagent can be added to the KOH?", "options": {"A": "Ethyl acetate, which extracts fat and debris into a plug on stool concentrates", "B": "Dimethyl sulfoxide, which speeds the dissolving of keratin and debris", "C": "Lugol iodine, which stains the glycogen in fungal cell walls dark brown", "D": "Trichrome stain, which colours protozoan nuclei red and the background green"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dimethyl sulfoxide, which speeds the dissolving of keratin and debris): DMSO added to KOH accelerates clearing. Iodine highlights nuclear detail in cysts, ethyl acetate extracts fat in stool concentration, and trichrome is a permanent stain, so none of them clears keratin."}, {"id": 1919, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A child's whole-exome results include seven variants of uncertain significance and no clear diagnosis, while a previous three-gene panel found none. The parents ask why the larger test produced so many uncertain results. Which explanation is best? The genetic counsellor needs a plain explanation for the family.", "options": {"A": "Panels are validated with population data, whereas exomes are analyzed without consulting population frequency databases", "B": "Wider tests examine more genes and variants, so more findings have insufficient evidence for classification", "C": "The exome uses a different classification scheme, in which most variants are labelled uncertain", "D": "Exome sequencing has a lower base accuracy than panel testing, creating false variants"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Wider tests examine more genes and variants, so more findings have insufficient evidence for classification): The wider the test, the more variants turn up, and many have too little evidence to classify. Exome base accuracy is not lower than that of a panel. The same five-tier scheme and population databases are used in both."}, {"id": 1920, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "When classifying a copy number variant, a geneticist checks public databases of healthy people and finds that a very similar duplication is common among them, seen in many unrelated individuals without disease. This finding influences the classification in which direction?", "options": {"A": "It supports a benign or likely benign classification", "B": "It supports classifying the duplication as pathogenic for the patient", "C": "It removes the need to record the genome build", "D": "It shows the duplication arose de novo in the patient"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It supports a benign or likely benign classification): A variant seen often in healthy populations is unlikely to cause serious disease, so it leans benign. Database presence has no bearing on whether the change is new in this patient, and the genome build must still be recorded for the coordinates."}, {"id": 1921, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In an EBUS-TBNA specimen from a hilar node in a lifelong city resident, several macrophages contain black granular pigment, and lymphocytes are otherwise normal and mixed. The trainee worries the pigment is a tumour. How should this finding be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Formalin pigment from fixation, which indicates the sample was mishandled.", "B": "Melanin from metastatic melanoma, which requires urgent immunostains to confirm the diagnosis", "C": "Hemosiderin from old bleeding, which indicates malignant tissue invasion.", "D": "Anthracotic carbon pigment, a common benign finding in macrophages of city dwellers."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anthracotic carbon pigment, a common benign finding in macrophages of city dwellers.): Black carbon pigment in macrophages is normal in people who live in cities and is not a tumour. Melanoma would show malignant cells, not normal lymphocytes. Hemosiderin does not indicate invasion, and formalin pigment appears in tissue fixed in acidic formalin rather than in aspirated macrophages."}, {"id": 1922, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A Kleihauer-Betke count on a postpartum RhD-negative mother finds 1.2 percent fetal cells. The laboratory uses a maternal blood volume of 5,000 mL, one vial covers 30 mL of fetal blood, and one extra vial is added. How many vials are recommended?", "options": {"A": "Three vials, from a 12 mL bleed divided by 30 mL, rounded.", "B": "Three vials, from a 60 mL bleed over 30 mL, plus one.", "C": "Two vials, from a 60 mL bleed divided by 30 mL alone.", "D": "Four vials, from a 120 mL bleed divided by 30 mL."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Three vials, from a 60 mL bleed over 30 mL, plus one.): The fetal bleed is 0.012 x 5,000 = 60 mL, which is two vials at 30 mL each, and one extra vial brings the total to three. Two vials omits the extra vial, and 120 mL results from a decimal error."}, {"id": 1923, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A molecular assay for a lung aspirate returns negative for EGFR, but the tumour area on the cell block was estimated at only a very low percentage of the cells, mostly normal inflammatory cells. What is the most reasonable concern?", "options": {"A": "A valid result, since assay sensitivity is unaffected by the tumour percentage", "B": "A true negative, because the block contained many cells overall in the extract", "C": "A false negative, because normal DNA can dilute the mutant signal", "D": "A false positive, because inflammatory cells often carry the EGFR mutation itself"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A false negative, because normal DNA can dilute the mutant signal): Adequacy depends on tumour cell content, and a low percentage dilutes the mutant signal with normal DNA, risking a false negative. Total cell count is not the measure, inflammatory cells do not carry the tumour mutation, and sensitivity does fall as tumour proportion drops. Estimating and recording the tumour percentage before testing helps the pathologist interpret a negative result."}, {"id": 1924, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "The laboratory completes the initial reaction investigation described in the case, including the clerical check, the plasma appearance, the DAT and the Gram stain of the unit. Taken together with the fever, rigors and low blood pressure, these findings best support which type of transfusion reaction?", "options": {"A": "Acute hemolytic reaction caused by ABO incompatibility between the recipient and the transfused unit", "B": "Febrile nonhemolytic reaction caused by cytokines accumulated during storage of the red cell unit", "C": "Transfusion-related acute lung injury caused by donor antibodies directed against recipient leukocytes", "D": "Septic reaction caused by bacterial contamination of the red cell unit"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Septic reaction caused by bacterial contamination of the red cell unit): Fever, rigors and hypotension with organisms in the bag point to a septic reaction. The matching clerical check, clear plasma and negative DAT argue against hemolysis, a febrile nonhemolytic reaction does not cause hypotension or show organisms, and lung injury would present with respiratory distress.", "cluster_id": "8", "cluster_text": "A 58-year-old man, group A D-positive, is receiving his second red cell unit after surgery. Twenty minutes into the transfusion, his temperature rises from 37.0 C to 39.1 C, and he develops rigors and a blood pressure of 82/50 mmHg. The transfusion is stopped and the unit is returned to the laboratory. The clerical check matches the patient and unit. The post-transfusion plasma is not pink, the DAT is negative, and a Gram stain of the bag contents shows gram-negative rods.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1925, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "The unit in the case is a red cell component that was stored in a refrigerator for several weeks, and the recipient developed fever, rigors and hypotension soon after the infusion began. The bacteriology laboratory has been asked to work up the bag. Which organism is classically associated with contamination of refrigerated red cell units?", "options": {"A": "Staphylococcus epidermidis, a gram-positive coccus acquired from donor skin during venipuncture", "B": "Bacillus cereus, a spore-forming gram-positive rod found in contaminated platelet components", "C": "Streptococcus pyogenes, a gram-positive coccus in chains that survives refrigeration in stored blood", "D": "Yersinia enterocolitica, a gram-negative rod that grows at refrigerator temperature"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Yersinia enterocolitica, a gram-negative rod that grows at refrigerator temperature): Yersinia enterocolitica is a gram-negative rod that thrives in cold, iron-rich red cell units and is the classic contaminant. The other organisms are gram-positive, which does not match the stain, and skin flora and Bacillus are more typical of room-temperature platelets.", "cluster_id": "8", "cluster_text": "A 58-year-old man, group A D-positive, is receiving his second red cell unit after surgery. Twenty minutes into the transfusion, his temperature rises from 37.0 C to 39.1 C, and he develops rigors and a blood pressure of 82/50 mmHg. The transfusion is stopped and the unit is returned to the laboratory. The clerical check matches the patient and unit. The post-transfusion plasma is not pink, the DAT is negative, and a Gram stain of the bag contents shows gram-negative rods.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1926, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "The transfusion service is now acting on this suspected septic reaction in the patient. Considering the safety of this patient and of the other recipients of components made from the same donation, which response is the most appropriate right now, once the Gram stain result is known?", "options": {"A": "Quarantine co-components, culture the unit and patient blood, and notify the blood supplier", "B": "Restart the transfusion at a slower rate after giving acetaminophen and monitoring vital signs closely", "C": "Discard the unit after the Gram stain and transfuse a second unit from the same donation", "D": "Wash the red cells in saline and return them to inventory for another patient"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Quarantine co-components, culture the unit and patient blood, and notify the blood supplier): Quarantining co-components, culturing the unit and the patient, and reporting to the blood supplier protect other recipients and allow the source to be traced. Restarting the transfusion would give more bacteria, a second unit from the same donation may be contaminated, and washing does not make a contaminated unit safe.", "cluster_id": "8", "cluster_text": "A 58-year-old man, group A D-positive, is receiving his second red cell unit after surgery. Twenty minutes into the transfusion, his temperature rises from 37.0 C to 39.1 C, and he develops rigors and a blood pressure of 82/50 mmHg. The transfusion is stopped and the unit is returned to the laboratory. The clerical check matches the patient and unit. The post-transfusion plasma is not pink, the DAT is negative, and a Gram stain of the bag contents shows gram-negative rods.", "cluster_size": 3}, {"id": 1927, "domain": "histology", "question": "A dermatology clinic sends a skin biopsy for a study of blistering disease, and the technologist reviews the method. An immunofluorescence study of a skin biopsy uses antibodies labelled with a dye to detect immunoglobulins and complement. Which dye and microscope combination is standard?", "options": {"A": "Hematoxylin with a phase-contrast microscope used with polarizing filters", "B": "Fluorescein with a fluorescence microscope used in a dark room", "C": "Silver stain with an electron microscope used under high vacuum", "D": "Eosin with a bright-field microscope used in normal room light"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fluorescein with a fluorescence microscope used in a dark room): Immunofluorescence uses antibodies labelled with a fluorescent dye, usually fluorescein, read on a fluorescence microscope in a dark room. Eosin, hematoxylin, and silver are not fluorescent labels for this method."}, {"id": 1928, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A karyotype of an infant shows an abnormal chromosome where extra material is attached to the end of one arm, and the banding pattern of that material does not match any known chromosome region. The reviewer must select the ISCN symbol that describes additional material of unknown origin. Which symbol is correct?", "options": {"A": "mar, used for additional material of unknown origin.", "B": "dup, used for additional material of unknown origin.", "C": "add, used for additional material of unknown origin.", "D": "der, used for additional material of unknown origin."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (add, used for additional material of unknown origin.): The add symbol describes a chromosome with extra material whose origin cannot be identified by banding. Der names a derivative chromosome formed by a rearrangement with known breakpoints, mar denotes a whole unidentified extra chromosome, and dup is an extra copy of a known segment."}, {"id": 1929, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A SYBR Green assay is run on ten extracts. After amplification, the melt curve analysis shows one sharp peak at the same temperature in every positive well, and no peak in the no-template control, which also shows no amplification curve. What does this pattern indicate?", "options": {"A": "The reaction was inhibited, because a single peak means poor polymerase activity.", "B": "A single specific product was amplified, with little primer-dimer present.", "C": "Several different products formed, so the annealing temperature was too low.", "D": "Cross-contamination occurred, since every positive well melted at an identical temperature."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A single specific product was amplified, with little primer-dimer present.): One sharp peak at a consistent temperature means one product of one sequence and length, and a clean no-template control means no primer-dimer or contamination. Several products would give several peaks. Inhibition would lower or delay amplification, not shape the melt profile. Identical melting in real positives is expected because they carry the same target."}, {"id": 1930, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A newborn has a high total bilirubin that is mostly unconjugated, no bilirubin in the urine and normal stool colour. The technologist is asked to interpret the pattern with the newborn's age in mind. Which explanation is most consistent with these findings?", "options": {"A": "Excess urobilinogen reabsorption producing conjugated bilirubin in the urine and stool", "B": "Complete bile duct obstruction producing conjugated bilirubin that appears in the urine", "C": "Light exposure of the specimen producing false bilirubin with no clinical cause", "D": "Immature liver enzyme or hemolysis producing unconjugated bilirubin that cannot enter the urine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Immature liver enzyme or hemolysis producing unconjugated bilirubin that cannot enter the urine): Newborns have immature enzyme activity, and hemolysis also raises unconjugated bilirubin, which is albumin bound and not found in urine. Bile duct obstruction would raise conjugated bilirubin with dark urine and pale stool. Light exposure lowers bilirubin rather than raising it."}, {"id": 1931, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "Before a working dilution is made, a single-stranded DNA oligonucleotide primer stock is read on the spectrophotometer, giving 0.60 at 260 nm with no dilution on a 1 cm path. Using the standard conversion for a single-stranded oligonucleotide, what concentration should be recorded?", "options": {"A": "About 24 µg/mL, from 0.60 multiplied by 40 µg/mL per absorbance unit.", "B": "About 30 µg/mL, from 0.60 multiplied by 50 µg/mL per absorbance unit.", "C": "About 198 µg/mL, from 0.60 multiplied by 33 and then by 10.", "D": "About 19.8 µg/mL, from 0.60 multiplied by 33 µg/mL per absorbance unit."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 19.8 µg/mL, from 0.60 multiplied by 33 µg/mL per absorbance unit.): An absorbance of 1.0 at 260 nm corresponds to about 33 µg/mL of single-stranded oligonucleotide, so 0.60 x 33 = 19.8 µg/mL. The 50 and 40 factors belong to double-stranded DNA and RNA, and no dilution was made."}, {"id": 1932, "domain": "histology", "question": "The tissue processor in the laboratory has a written maintenance schedule that staff are expected to follow. A tissue processor is running with reagents used far beyond the number of cassettes it is meant to process. Which consequence is most likely, and which management step is required?", "options": {"A": "Excess hardness from fresh reagents, so the reagents must be diluted first", "B": "Improved processing quality, so the schedule can be extended", "C": "Poor dehydration and clearing, so reagents must be changed on schedule", "D": "Faster runs with no effect on the tissue, so no action is needed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Poor dehydration and clearing, so reagents must be changed on schedule): Reagents are changed on a schedule based on cassette counts or concentration checks, so that dehydration and clearing remain complete. Exhausted reagents cause poor processing rather than improvements, and diluting fresh reagents is not the correct remedy."}, {"id": 1933, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A neonatal unit protocol lists the characteristics of cellular components for premature and low-birth-weight infants. The infants have immature immune systems and small blood volumes. Which characteristic applies to their components? The transfusion service is updating its policy for the neonatal unit.", "options": {"A": "Washed and pooled, given only after twenty-four hours of storage.", "B": "Frozen and thawed, given rapidly to correct anemia quickly.", "C": "Irradiated and CMV-safe, given slowly to avoid overload.", "D": "Leukocyte-rich and pooled, given rapidly through a large needle."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Irradiated and CMV-safe, given slowly to avoid overload.): Components for premature and low-birth-weight infants are usually irradiated and CMV-safe, and are transfused slowly because of the small blood volume and risk of overload. Rapid transfusion or pooled products would raise risk."}, {"id": 1934, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "An NGS laboratory checks extracted DNA before library preparation and also handles RNA specimens for RNA sequencing. Because NGS is demanding about the amount and quality of its input, the laboratory applies specific checks to these materials. Which checks are described for these inputs?", "options": {"A": "Absorbance at 260 nm alone for both, since the 260/280 ratio separates DNA from RNA", "B": "Sanger sequencing of a spiked-in control gene, which verifies both the amount and integrity of input", "C": "Gel electrophoresis of the final library, with no assessment of the RNA quality", "D": "Fluorometry for amount, fragment analysis where needed, and an RNA integrity assessment for RNA"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fluorometry for amount, fragment analysis where needed, and an RNA integrity assessment for RNA): NGS input is checked by fluorometry and, where needed, fragment analysis, and RNA also needs an integrity assessment. Absorbance alone does not separate DNA from RNA reliably. Checking the finished library does not replace checking the input, and Sanger of a control gene measures neither amount nor integrity."}, {"id": 1935, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A solid tumour section is tested with a probe for a gene deletion, and 12 percent of nuclei show one signal. The validation record for tissue sections gives a stricter cutoff of 15 percent than the smear cutoff of 5 percent. How should the result be reported?", "options": {"A": "Deletion detected, because 12 percent exceeds the 5 percent smear cutoff used for cytology", "B": "Uninterpretable, because a tissue section cannot be compared with any validated cutoff value", "C": "Below the tissue cutoff, so not deletion, with cells scored and limits stated", "D": "Amplification detected, because the percentage of abnormal nuclei is above ten percent"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Below the tissue cutoff, so not deletion, with cells scored and limits stated): The correct comparison is with the tissue-specific cutoff of 15 percent, since truncated nuclei create false single signals in sections. Twelve percent falls below it. The smear cutoff does not apply to sections, and single signals suggest loss, not amplification."}, {"id": 1936, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A group O D-positive patient has anti-Fy(a) and anti-K identified and needs one red cell unit. The antigen typing and antiglobulin crossmatch results for four group O units are shown in the table. Which unit is the best choice to issue?", "options": {"A": "Unit 103, because it lacks both antigens and the antiglobulin crossmatch is compatible", "B": "Unit 102, because it lacks both antigens and the incompatible crossmatch is likely a technical error", "C": "Unit 101, because it lacks K antigen and the crossmatch is compatible at the antiglobulin phase", "D": "Unit 104, because it lacks Fy(a) antigen and the crossmatch is compatible at the antiglobulin phase"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Unit 103, because it lacks both antigens and the antiglobulin crossmatch is compatible): Unit 103 is negative for both antigens and crossmatch compatible. Unit 102 is also antigen-negative but incompatible, so it needs investigation rather than assumption, and units 101 and 104 each carry one of the two antigens, so they would be attacked by one of the patient's antibodies.", "image_type": "result_table", "image_params": {"title": "Donor unit typing and crossmatch", "columns": ["Unit", "ABO and D", "Fy(a)", "K", "IAT crossmatch"], "rows": [["101", "O POS", "POS", "NEG", "Compatible"], ["102", "O POS", "NEG", "NEG", "Incompatible"], ["103", "O POS", "NEG", "NEG", "Compatible"], ["104", "O POS", "NEG", "POS", "Compatible"]], "caption": "Unit selection"}, "image": "question_images/q_1936.png"}, {"id": 1937, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist is unsure how to orient a small, unusual specimen while the paraffin wax in the mould is still liquid. The grossing notes give no guidance, and the block will be trimmed afterwards. Whom should the technologist consult before the wax hardens in the chosen position?", "options": {"A": "No one, because any position can be corrected later by trimming", "B": "The laboratory supplier, who supplies the moulds and the cassettes", "C": "The receptionist, since the requisition form lists the specimen type", "D": "The grossing technologist or the pathologist, before the wax sets"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The grossing technologist or the pathologist, before the wax sets): Guessing risks a block hardened in the wrong position, and trimming to correct it may waste diagnostic tissue. The grossing notes or pathologist know what the reader needs to see. Front-desk staff and suppliers have no clinical basis for orientation."}, {"id": 1938, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A clinician orders a glucose and wants to limit glycolysis during transport to the laboratory over several hours, since the sample will travel from a distant collection site. Using the order of draw chart shown, which tube is the best choice, and why does that tube belong last in the draw order?", "options": {"A": "Heparin tube, because the anticoagulant stops glycolysis but can contaminate citrate coagulation tubes", "B": "Fluoride and oxalate tube, because the additives inhibit glycolysis but can contaminate other tubes", "C": "EDTA tube, because the chelator stops glycolysis but can falsely raise the potassium of other tubes", "D": "Serum SST tube, because the gel stops glycolysis but can carry over into later blood cultures"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fluoride and oxalate tube, because the additives inhibit glycolysis but can contaminate other tubes): The fluoride and oxalate tube is placed last because sodium fluoride and potassium oxalate can alter sodium, potassium and calcium if carried into earlier tubes, and fluoride blocks glycolysis so glucose stays stable. The other tubes do not have a glycolysis inhibitor, and the EDTA rule concerns potassium contamination rather than glucose.", "image_type": "tube_order", "image_params": {}, "image": "question_images/order_of_draw_chart.png"}, {"id": 1939, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In a cytology laboratory that follows structured reporting standards, Cytology reporting systems keep terms uniform so that clinicians understand results the same way. Besides the cervical Bethesda System, which statement about structured reporting systems in cytology is accurate for other body sites?", "options": {"A": "Structured systems replace the need for identifiers and specimen information on reports", "B": "No other body site uses structured categories because wording is left to each reader", "C": "Similar structured systems exist for thyroid, urinary, salivary gland and serous fluid samples", "D": "Structured systems apply to cervical samples and are prohibited at other body sites"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Similar structured systems exist for thyroid, urinary, salivary gland and serous fluid samples): Structured reporting systems exist for cervical cytology and also for thyroid, urinary tract, salivary gland and serous fluid samples, keeping wording consistent. They do not replace identifiers, specimen details or clinical history, and vague phrasing that readers could interpret differently should be avoided. Uniform categories help clinicians read reports the same way from one laboratory to another."}, {"id": 1940, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A technologist explains why the D antigen receives special attention in transfusion practice, even though it is not part of the ABO system. A D-negative recipient who receives D-positive red cells has a high chance of forming an antibody. Which statement best explains this special attention?", "options": {"A": "D is the most immunogenic red cell antigen outside the ABO system", "B": "D antibodies are naturally occurring and present in every D-negative person", "C": "D is the only antigen outside the ABO system that is found on red cells", "D": "D is inherited on chromosome 9 together with the A and B genes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (D is the most immunogenic red cell antigen outside the ABO system): The D antigen provokes anti-D more readily than any other non-ABO antigen, so D-negative patients receive D-negative blood when possible. Many other antigens exist on red cells. Anti-D forms after exposure and is not naturally occurring, and the RHD gene lies on chromosome 1."}, {"id": 1941, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A young leukemia patient is about to be prescribed a thiopurine drug, and the physician orders pharmacogenomic testing beforehand to predict the risk of severe bone marrow suppression. Which pair of genes is the laboratory most likely to test for this risk?", "options": {"A": "CYP2C19 and VKORC1, whose variants alter clopidogrel and warfarin response", "B": "TPMT and NUDT15, whose variants raise the risk of myelosuppression", "C": "CYP2D6 and CYP2C9, whose variants alter tamoxifen and phenytoin exposure", "D": "DPYD and HLA-B, whose variants alter fluoropyrimidine and abacavir response"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (TPMT and NUDT15, whose variants raise the risk of myelosuppression): TPMT and NUDT15 variants increase the risk of severe myelosuppression from thiopurine drugs. CYP2C19 and VKORC1 relate to clopidogrel and warfarin. DPYD relates to fluoropyrimidines and HLA-B to hypersensitivity reactions. CYP2D6 and CYP2C9 encode drug metabolizing enzymes for other medications."}, {"id": 1942, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Testing with anti-IgG gives a DAT of 2+, and testing with anti-C3d gives a negative DAT. The patient has not been transfused and takes a long-term medication list that includes several new drugs. The technologist considers the pattern. Which cause is most likely for this result?", "options": {"A": "Cold agglutinin disease, since IgM antibodies leave complement bound on the cells", "B": "A warm autoantibody or a drug-induced antibody coating the cells with IgG", "C": "Anti-Fya coating the cells, since Duffy antibodies bind complement and IgG together", "D": "Mixed-type autoimmune hemolysis, since that pattern needs both IgG and complement present"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A warm autoantibody or a drug-induced antibody coating the cells with IgG): An IgG-only DAT points to a warm autoantibody or a drug, and cold agglutinins give a complement only pattern. A mixed pattern requires both components to be positive, which is not the case here. Alloantibody coating would need a transfusion or pregnancy exposure."}, {"id": 1943, "domain": "histology", "question": "A liver biopsy shows metastatic adenocarcinoma with no known primary site, and it is stained with CK7 and CK20 together. The pathologist explains the reason for using both markers on the same case. What is the purpose of this pairing?", "options": {"A": "To measure the amount of glycogen stored in tumour cells", "B": "To help suggest a likely site of origin from the staining pattern", "C": "To count how many tumour cells are actively dividing at the time of biopsy", "D": "To show whether the tumour has amplified the HER2 gene"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (To help suggest a likely site of origin from the staining pattern): Cytokeratin 7 and 20 patterns help suggest where a carcinoma may have arisen. Ki-67 estimates proliferation, ISH shows gene amplification, and PAS-diastase shows glycogen."}, {"id": 1944, "domain": "histology", "question": "A lymph node biopsy shows a diffuse atypical infiltrate of large cells, and the pathologist wants to separate lymphoma from carcinoma with a broad first marker before any lineage-specific stains are ordered. Which marker recognizes most lymphocytes for this purpose?", "options": {"A": "Cytokeratin, the marker of epithelial cells and most carcinomas", "B": "S100, the marker of melanocytes and nerve sheath cells", "C": "CD45, the leukocyte common antigen found on most lymphocytes", "D": "Desmin, the cytoplasmic marker of smooth and skeletal muscle cells"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (CD45, the leukocyte common antigen found on most lymphocytes): CD45 marks most lymphocytes and helps separate lymphoma from carcinoma. CD20 and CD3 then subtype B and T cells. Desmin is a muscle marker, S100 a melanocyte marker, and cytokeratin an epithelial marker."}, {"id": 1945, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A laboratory that once sized PCR fragments on slab gels in an open post-PCR room now uses an automated genetic analyzer that moves labelled fragments through a polymer-filled capillary. Which advantage explains why this method has replaced gels for many fragment-analysis applications?", "options": {"A": "It quantifies RNA integrity without any need for a size standard.", "B": "It removes the need for a post-PCR area since tubes stay closed.", "C": "It offers better size resolution and automation than slab gels.", "D": "It detects fragments by charge rather than size, saving run time."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It offers better size resolution and automation than slab gels.): Capillary systems resolve fragments that differ by a single nucleotide and run unattended, which slab gels cannot match. Amplified product is still handled, so post-PCR separation remains. Separation is still by size, with charge simply driving movement. Size standards remain necessary for accurate fragment sizing."}, {"id": 1946, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A patient's forward group shows no agglutination with anti-A and anti-B. The reverse group shows 4+ agglutination with both A1 cells and B cells, and the autocontrol is negative. The tests have been repeated with the same result, and the sample identification is correct. What is the ABO group?", "options": {"A": "Group O, since antigens are absent and both antibodies are present", "B": "Group AB, since both reverse cells reacted with the patient's serum", "C": "Group A, since the reverse group shows a reaction with B cells", "D": "Group B, since the reverse group shows a reaction with A1 cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Group O, since antigens are absent and both antibodies are present): Cells that lack A and B antigens and serum containing anti-A and anti-B match Landsteiner's rule for group O. Group AB would show forward reactions and no reverse reactions. Groups A and B each have only one antibody, so both reverse cells would not react."}, {"id": 1947, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A trainee reviewing the smear sees the largest normal leukocyte in peripheral blood. It has a folded, brain-like nucleus and gray-blue cytoplasm containing fine vacuoles. Which normal white cell should the trainee record on the differential for this cell in the report?", "options": {"A": "Monocyte, which is the largest normal leukocyte and shows a folded nucleus", "B": "Large granular lymphocyte, which has extra cytoplasm holding a few red-purple granules", "C": "Eosinophil, which usually has a two-lobed nucleus with bright orange-red granules", "D": "Plasma cell, which has an eccentric nucleus and a pale perinuclear area"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Monocyte, which is the largest normal leukocyte and shows a folded nucleus): Monocytes are the largest normal blood leukocytes with a folded nucleus and gray-blue vacuolated cytoplasm. Large granular lymphocytes are smaller with round nuclei, eosinophils have orange granules, and plasma cells are not normally in blood."}, {"id": 1948, "domain": "cytology", "question": "During a tumour board review, A pathologist suspects a lymphoma rather than carcinoma in a pleural effusion and asks which type of testing should be added to the cytology workup. Which choice fits the question best given what this chapter describes about ancillary tests?", "options": {"A": "HPV genotyping on the fluid supernatant using a validated cervical screening assay kit", "B": "Flow cytometry on fresh fluid, with lymphoid markers on the cell block", "C": "A rescreen of the Papanicolaou slides by a different screener", "D": "Mesothelial and adenocarcinoma markers only, since carcinoma is more common than lymphoma"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Flow cytometry on fresh fluid, with lymphoid markers on the cell block): When lymphoma is the concern, flow cytometry on fresh fluid and lymphoid markers on a cell block answer the question. HPV genotyping belongs to cervical screening, rescreening checks missed abnormal cells rather than adding tests, and carcinoma markers alone cannot diagnose lymphoma. Choosing the ancillary test by the diagnostic question keeps limited sample material from being wasted."}, {"id": 1949, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cervical specimen requisition arrives with the patient's name and collection date but no clinical history, date of last menstrual period or hormone information. The cytology laboratory wants a complete request. The requisition template currently only lists the patient name, the date and the requesting clinician. What information should a cervical requisition also include?", "options": {"A": "The patient's occupation, travel history and preferred laboratory courier company", "B": "The technologist's screening quota, coffee break times and preferred stain", "C": "The last menstrual period, hormone use and any prior treatment such as radiation", "D": "The fixative brand, the box number of slides and the vehicle used for delivery"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The last menstrual period, hormone use and any prior treatment such as radiation): Menstrual, hormone and treatment information affects interpretation of cervical cells and belongs on the requisition. Occupation, courier details and internal laboratory data do not help the interpretation."}, {"id": 1950, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A molecular laboratory reports an MRD assay result on a patient with chronic lymphocytic leukemia as not detected, with no additional information. The clinician is unsure whether disease is truly absent. What should the report state so that the result can be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "The name of the instrument manufacturer used for the assay", "B": "The estimated age of the ordering provider's clinic records", "C": "The total number of reads generated on the sequencing run", "D": "The limit of detection of the assay for that specimen"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The limit of detection of the assay for that specimen): A result of not detected is meaningful only alongside the limit of detection, since a method may detect one cancer cell in ten thousand while another reaches one in a million. The report should state that sensitivity. The manufacturer and total read count do not tell the reader what level of disease was excluded."}, {"id": 1951, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A peripheral blood culture was set up correctly in a heparin tube, with medium and incubator in order, but the technologist used a phytohemagglutinin vial that had been stored at the wrong temperature and was likely inactive. What result should be expected at harvest after 72 hours?", "options": {"A": "Many overlapping metaphases, since mitogen activity affects only the hypotonic swelling", "B": "Few or no metaphases, because the T lymphocytes stay at rest without an active mitogen", "C": "Normal metaphases in fewer cells, since colcemid takes over the stimulation of division", "D": "An excellent yield of metaphases, since the mitogen is needed only for bone marrow"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Few or no metaphases, because the T lymphocytes stay at rest without an active mitogen): Without an active mitogen the T lymphocytes do not divide, so few or no metaphases appear. Mitogen is needed for blood rather than for marrow, and colcemid cannot replace it, since it only arrests cells that are already dividing."}, {"id": 1952, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A painter presents with abdominal pain and anemia. The smear shows coarse blue dots scattered throughout the red cells, and a blood lead level is requested. The dots are aggregates of ribosomal RNA. What is this inclusion called, and what does it suggest in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Cabot rings from mitotic spindle remnants, a sign of severe megaloblastic anemia or myelodysplasia", "B": "Howell-Jolly bodies from nuclear DNA remnants, a sign of absent splenic function after splenectomy", "C": "Heinz bodies from denatured hemoglobin, a sign of oxidant damage from a drug", "D": "Coarse basophilic stippling, a classic sign of lead poisoning that interferes with heme synthesis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Coarse basophilic stippling, a classic sign of lead poisoning that interferes with heme synthesis): Blue dots of RNA in red cells are basophilic stippling, coarse in lead poisoning and also seen in thalassemia. Heinz bodies are precipitated hemoglobin needing a supravital stain. Cabot rings and Howell-Jolly bodies have different appearances and meanings."}, {"id": 1953, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "During a procedure in the Class II type A2 biological safety cabinet, a technologist wants to understand how the air moves. Which description of the airflow is correct for this cabinet? The technologist is preparing to work with specimens that may generate aerosols and wants to know how air is directed by the cabinet.", "options": {"A": "Room air enters through the top and is released into the room without any filtration", "B": "Air is recirculated without any filtration and remains inside the cabinet", "C": "Unfiltered air is blown across the work surface and out into the room", "D": "Room air enters through the front grille, and HEPA-filtered air flows down over the work surface"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Room air enters through the front grille, and HEPA-filtered air flows down over the work surface): Inflow through the front grille protects the worker, HEPA-filtered downward air protects the specimen, and HEPA-filtered exhaust protects the environment. Unfiltered or unfiltered exhaust designs would expose the staff or room to aerosols."}, {"id": 1954, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A Gram stain of a colony from a skin abscess culture is shown in the image. The technologist wants the quickest slide test to confirm the likely genus of the isolate. Which reagent and result would best support that identification?", "options": {"A": "Clumping of latex particles coated with Lancefield group A antiserum", "B": "Purple colour within ten seconds on a cytochrome oxidase test strip", "C": "Bubbling when a colony touches hydrogen peroxide on a slide", "D": "Clearing of a colony suspension after addition of sodium deoxycholate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Bubbling when a colony touches hydrogen peroxide on a slide): The image shows gram-positive cocci in clusters, a morphology of staphylococci, which are catalase positive and bubble with hydrogen peroxide. Lancefield latex typing and bile solubility apply to streptococci, which form chains or pairs, and oxidase testing does not separate staphylococci from streptococci because both are negative.", "image_type": "micrograph", "image_params": {"prompt": "Realistic Gram stain photomicrograph, 1000x oil immersion, pink-red background, purple Gram-positive cocci in dense grape-like irregular clusters, clean pale pink background, from a bacterial colony smear.", "caption": "Gram stain of a colony from a skin abscess", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1954.png"}, {"id": 1955, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "Ten minutes after a platelet transfusion begins, a patient develops shaking chills, a temperature of 39.5 °C and a falling blood pressure. The nurse stops the infusion and calls the blood bank. Which laboratory action best supports diagnosis of this reaction?", "options": {"A": "Culture and Gram stain of the implicated unit, treating the patient as septic", "B": "Repeat crossmatch of the platelet unit with the recipient's stored red cells sample", "C": "Measure the natriuretic peptide and give a diuretic to the recipient right away", "D": "Perform a direct antiglobulin test only and resume the transfusion if it is negative"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Culture and Gram stain of the implicated unit, treating the patient as septic): Fever, rigors and falling pressure with a platelet unit suggest bacterial contamination, so the unit is cultured and Gram stained while the patient is treated as septic. Crossmatch and DAT address red cell reactions, and a natriuretic peptide with a diuretic addresses overload, none of which fits."}, {"id": 1956, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient who was told to hold a relaxed hand is instead pumping a fist vigorously during the draw. The potassium comes back at 5.6 mmol/L although two earlier results from the same patient were 4.0 and 4.2 mmol/L. What effect of the collection technique best accounts for the higher value?", "options": {"A": "Working forearm muscle released potassium into the blood being drawn", "B": "Fist pumping caused hemolysis by pulling the plunger back too quickly", "C": "Fist pumping delayed clotting and left fibrin strands in the serum", "D": "Fist pumping allowed EDTA carryover as blood flowed through the needle"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Working forearm muscle released potassium into the blood being drawn): Contracting muscle releases potassium into local blood, so vigorous fist pumping can raise the measured value even though the patient's true level is unchanged. The patient should hold a relaxed fist. Fist pumping does not cause EDTA carryover, which depends on order of draw, and it does not interfere with clotting to produce fibrin strands."}, {"id": 1957, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A therapeutic drug monitoring specimen arrives for phenytoin with no information about when the last dose was taken or when the sample was drawn. The physician wants a result within the hour. What should the technologist do? The nurse says the patient has been taking the drug for several weeks.", "options": {"A": "Report the result after adding a note that the value is presumptive until confirmed", "B": "Run the sample and report the level as a trough value without further comment", "C": "Contact the ward for dosing and collection times and record them first", "D": "Refuse to run any TDM sample and discard it without contacting the ordering unit"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contact the ward for dosing and collection times and record them first): Timing is the most important factor in interpreting drug levels, so the last dose time and collection time must be known. The technologist should query missing information. Reporting a level as a trough without knowing the timing could lead to an incorrect dose adjustment."}, {"id": 1958, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A 38-year-old with an intrauterine device has a smear that shows filamentous organisms with radiating branching morphology aggregated into small tangles on a background of neutrophils. The squamous and endocervical cells around them look normal, and there are no atypical epithelial cells. How should the report handle this finding?", "options": {"A": "Report an epithelial cell abnormality because the branching filaments distort the surrounding squamous cells", "B": "Omit the organisms since they are normal flora in women who have an intrauterine device", "C": "Call the sample unsatisfactory because the organisms hide the epithelial cells beneath them", "D": "Report actinomyces-like organisms as a non-neoplastic finding and note the association with the device"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report actinomyces-like organisms as a non-neoplastic finding and note the association with the device): Actinomyces-like organisms are more common with an IUD and are listed as non-neoplastic findings. They are reported when found. They do not make the epithelium abnormal and rarely obscure enough of it to cause rejection."}, {"id": 1959, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A screener notes small round to oval cells with dense cytoplasm on a smear from a 35-year-old, some with pointed cytoplasmic projections that give a spidery outline, lying near endocervical strips. What are these cells called and what do they represent?", "options": {"A": "Ciliated cells, which are tubal-type columnar cells with a terminal bar and cilia", "B": "Histiocytes, which are phagocytes with kidney-shaped nuclei and foamy cytoplasm", "C": "Stromal cells, which are small oval cells with scant cytoplasm and dark round nuclei", "D": "Spider cells, which are squamous metaplastic cells with cytoplasmic extensions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Spider cells, which are squamous metaplastic cells with cytoplasmic extensions): Squamous metaplastic cells are small and dense and may show cytoplasmic extensions, giving them the name spider cells. Ciliated cells belong to tubal metaplasia. Histiocytes and endometrial stromal cells have different nuclear shapes and cytoplasm."}, {"id": 1960, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A patient with dyspepsia is suspected of having an infection with a spiral organism that is strongly urease positive and lives in the stomach lining. How is Helicobacter pylori usually detected in practice, given that culture is not the routine approach?", "options": {"A": "By a Ziehl-Neelsen stain on stool, or a bile esculin test performed on biopsy tissue", "B": "By dark-field microscopy of blood, or a CAMP test performed on the gastric aspirate", "C": "By a urinary antigen test, or a coagulase test performed on the gastric aspirate", "D": "By a breath test, a stool antigen test, or a urease test on biopsy tissue"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (By a breath test, a stool antigen test, or a urease test on biopsy tissue): Helicobacter pylori is usually detected by breath test, stool antigen, or biopsy urease testing rather than culture. Acid-fast stains, dark-field microscopy, urinary antigen, coagulase, and CAMP tests do not identify it."}, {"id": 1961, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A laboratory decides between karyotype and microarray for a couple with recurrent pregnancy loss and a family history suggesting a chromosome rearrangement carried by a relative. The concern is a balanced rearrangement in one parent. Which test is better suited to the question?", "options": {"A": "Microarray, because it detects balanced rearrangements at high resolution", "B": "Whole chromosome paint on interphase cells, because interphase shows every rearrangement", "C": "Karyotype, because arrays cannot see rearrangements that leave the DNA amount unchanged", "D": "QF-PCR, because it detects balanced rearrangements from marker peaks"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Karyotype, because arrays cannot see rearrangements that leave the DNA amount unchanged): Balanced rearrangements change no DNA dosage, so arrays cannot see them, whereas banding shows the exchange of material. QF-PCR only looks at selected markers for aneuploidy, and paints do not work on interphase nuclei."}, {"id": 1962, "domain": "cytology", "question": "At a university-affiliated hospital where students train in the department, A laboratory receives a request from a teaching program to use leftover patient cytology material for student practice. Which policy statement fits the ethical requirements described for residual specimens in cytology?", "options": {"A": "Staff may remove leftover slides for private collections if the patient is unnamed there", "B": "It needs a policy covering consent or de-identification, and samples are not taken home", "C": "Leftover material must be destroyed on the same day the report is signed out", "D": "Leftover material may be used freely because the diagnostic work is already complete"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It needs a policy covering consent or de-identification, and samples are not taken home): Using leftover material for teaching, research or quality control needs a policy that covers consent or approved de-identification, and samples are not removed for personal use. Free use ignores autonomy and privacy, and material should be kept until the pathologist confirms nothing further is required. Consent or approved de-identification protects patient autonomy and privacy when material is reused."}, {"id": 1963, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A 72-year-old man with bone pain, fatigue and anemia has a markedly raised total protein of 105 g/L and an ESR of 110 mm/h. His blood film is shown in the image, and the technologist reviews it before it goes to the physician. Which test should the physician request next?", "options": {"A": "Cold agglutinin titre at 4 C to confirm agglutination of red cells in the sample", "B": "Direct antiglobulin test to look for antibody coating the red cell surface", "C": "Reticulocyte count with correction for hematocrit to assess marrow response", "D": "Serum protein electrophoresis with immunofixation to look for a monoclonal protein"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Serum protein electrophoresis with immunofixation to look for a monoclonal protein): Red cells stacked like coins are rouleaux, caused by raised globulins, and with a high total protein and ESR in an older patient they raise suspicion of a plasma cell disorder, so electrophoresis with immunofixation is requested. A cold agglutinin titre, antiglobulin test or reticulocyte count does not look for a paraprotein.", "image_type": "micrograph", "image_params": {"prompt": "Realistic Wright-Giemsa stained peripheral blood smear photomicrograph, 1000x oil immersion, red cells stacked in rows like coins (rouleaux), forming linear stacks, with a bluish proteinaceous background, few free red cells.", "caption": "Peripheral blood film", "quality": "medium"}, "image": "question_images/q_1963.png"}, {"id": 1964, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A neonate needs a skin puncture sample for a CBC and a second capillary tube for another test. The technologist wants the platelet count to be reliable, since platelets aggregate quickly when they touch the wound. Which tube should be filled first, and what reasoning supports that choice?", "options": {"A": "The heparin tube first, because it needs the largest volume of blood", "B": "The EDTA microtube last, because EDTA would dilute the tissue fluid", "C": "The EDTA microtube first, since platelets clump at the site", "D": "The serum tube first, because blood flow is fastest at the start"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The EDTA microtube first, since platelets clump at the site): When several capillary tubes are needed, the EDTA microtube is filled first because platelets clump at the puncture site if collection is slow. Flow rate and volume do not decide the order. EDTA does not dilute tissue fluid, and filling it last would risk clumped platelets and a falsely low count."}, {"id": 1965, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "In a SYBR Green qPCR, a no-template well shows a signal at Ct 34, and its melt curve peak sits at a lower temperature than the target peak seen in patient wells. A trainee asks what most likely caused the signal. Which is the best explanation?", "options": {"A": "Genomic DNA in the water, which produces a lower melting peak.", "B": "Amplicon carry-over, which typically produces a peak matching the target melting temperature.", "C": "Probe cleavage by the polymerase, which releases reporter dye without any amplification.", "D": "Primer-dimers formed when primers annealed to each other and were extended by the polymerase."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Primer-dimers formed when primers annealed to each other and were extended by the polymerase.): Primer-dimers are short double-stranded products, so a dye binds them and their lower melting temperature gives a separate, lower peak. Carry-over would match the target peak. SYBR Green assays use no probe, and water contains no template to create a lower peak."}, {"id": 1966, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A biliary stricture is brushed during ERCP, and the brush is rinsed into preservative. Smears show groups of cells with enlarged irregular nuclei among inflamed reactive cells. The pathologist mentions that inflamed mucosa is a known trap. Which tumour is being sought by biliary brushing?", "options": {"A": "Barrett esophagus, a premalignant change of the distal esophagus lining.", "B": "Hepatocellular carcinoma, a malignant tumour of the liver's polygonal cells and trabeculae.", "C": "Cholangiocarcinoma, a malignant tumour of the bile duct epithelium.", "D": "Gastric adenocarcinoma, a malignant tumour arising in the stomach lining glands."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cholangiocarcinoma, a malignant tumour of the bile duct epithelium.): Brushings of bile duct strictures are collected to detect cholangiocarcinoma, and reactive changes from inflammation are a well-known mimic. Hepatocellular carcinoma arises in the liver parenchyma. Gastric and Barrett lesions belong to other parts of the gastrointestinal tract."}, {"id": 1967, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "The plate was set up yesterday. A patient with advanced HIV infection has a fever and a sputum culture growing acid-fast bacilli. The nucleic acid test for Mycobacterium tuberculosis is negative. Which nontuberculous organism is the most likely cause in this immune setting?", "options": {"A": "Mycobacterium abscessus, which is typically seen in patients with advanced HIV", "B": "Mycobacterium avium complex, which is typically seen in patients with advanced HIV", "C": "Mycobacterium kansasii, which is typically seen in patients with advanced HIV", "D": "Mycobacterium leprae, which is typically seen in patients with advanced HIV"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Mycobacterium avium complex, which is typically seen in patients with advanced HIV): Mycobacterium avium complex is the nontuberculous organism associated with advanced HIV. Mycobacterium leprae cannot be cultured in the laboratory, and Mycobacterium abscessus is a rapid grower, so neither fits this presentation."}, {"id": 1968, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A technologist mistakenly loads EDTA plasma from a lavender tube onto the chemistry analyzer for an alkaline phosphatase along with calcium and magnesium. The results are far below expected values in a patient with a normal previous set. Why did the EDTA plasma give these low results?", "options": {"A": "EDTA precipitates the plasma proteins in the sample and strips out the enzymes before analysis", "B": "EDTA raises the pH of the plasma and denatures the enzyme in transit", "C": "EDTA binds calcium, magnesium and zinc, so the assay's metal-dependent reactions are blocked", "D": "EDTA activates thrombin and consumes the analytes during a slow clotting reaction in the tube"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (EDTA binds calcium, magnesium and zinc, so the assay's metal-dependent reactions are blocked): EDTA chelates divalent cations, so calcium and magnesium are measured falsely low, and enzymes that require metal ions, such as alkaline phosphatase, lose activity. EDTA prevents clotting rather than activating thrombin, and it does not precipitate protein. Its effect on pH is not the explanation for low metal-dependent results."}, {"id": 1969, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "A patient with an elevated osmolal gap needs a follow-up test. Which group of substances is most appropriate to measure to explain the gap? The toxicologist wants the laboratory to identify the substances that most likely explain the gap in this poisoning case.", "options": {"A": "Ethanol, methanol and ethylene glycol, which the formula does not include", "B": "Sodium, chloride and bicarbonate, which are used in the calculation of the anion gap", "C": "Ferritin, TIBC and transferrin saturation, which are measured for iron studies", "D": "Cortisol, TSH and hCG, which are measured for endocrine disorders in serum"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ethanol, methanol and ethylene glycol, which the formula does not include): A raised osmolal gap points to osmotically active substances not included in the equation, such as ethanol, methanol, ethylene glycol, isopropanol or mannitol. The laboratory measures these when a gap is present. The other groups belong to other tests."}, {"id": 1970, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A cytotechnologist records how much of the cell each nucleus occupies, using nuclear area compared with whole-cell area. Two round cells from a fluid are drawn at equal magnification in the image. What fraction of the whole cell area does the nucleus of Cell 1 occupy?", "options": {"A": "About three quarters of the cell area, leaving a thin rim of visible cytoplasm", "B": "About one half of the cell area, so nucleus and cytoplasm have nearly equal area", "C": "About one quarter of the cell area, so the cytoplasm is clearly the larger part", "D": "About one tenth of the cell area, so the nucleus is small within broad cytoplasm"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About three quarters of the cell area, leaving a thin rim of visible cytoplasm): The nucleus of Cell 1 covers about three quarters of the cell, leaving a thin rim of cytoplasm. A half would fit a cell with equal parts nucleus and cytoplasm, and one quarter is the nucleus of Cell 2. One tenth would describe a much smaller nucleus than either cell shows.", "image_type": "cell_micrograph", "image_params": {"context": "pleural", "quality": "medium", "caption": "Cells at equal magnification", "cells": [{"label": "1", "prompt": "Realistic high-power (400x) brightfield photomicrograph, Papanicolaou-stained fluid cytology. A single isolated round cell centred in the frame on a clean pale background, with a round blue-green cytoplasm and a single round nucleus. The nucleus occupies about 99 percent of the total cell area, an even smooth round outline. The whole cell fills about 60 percent of the frame. Nucleus clearly darker purple-blue than the cytoplasm with a distinct sharp border. Natural colours, crisp focus, true photographic realism, not a drawing. No text, no letters, no numbers, no labels, no arrows, no scale bar, no watermark anywhere in the image.", "measured_nc_ratio": 0.75}, {"label": "2", "prompt": "Realistic high-power (400x) brightfield photomicrograph, Papanicolaou-stained fluid cytology. A single isolated round cell centred in the frame on a clean pale background, with a round blue-green cytoplasm and a single round nucleus. The nucleus occupies about 45 percent of the total cell area. The whole cell fills about 60 percent of the frame. Nucleus clearly darker purple-blue than the cytoplasm with a distinct sharp border. Natural colours, crisp focus, true photographic realism, not a drawing. No text, no letters, no numbers, no labels, no arrows, no scale bar, no watermark anywhere in the image.", "measured_nc_ratio": 0.25}]}, "image": "question_images/q_1970.png"}, {"id": 1971, "domain": "cytology", "question": "Against a benign background of colloid and flat honeycomb sheets, but a handful of cells have enlarged nuclei with grooves and pale chromatin. These features appear in too few cells to make a definite diagnosis. The pathologist must pick the Bethesda category. Which is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Malignant, since any single cell with a nuclear groove proves papillary carcinoma", "B": "Benign, since the benign background outweighs a handful of atypical nuclei", "C": "Atypia of undetermined significance, since the features appear in too few cells for a diagnosis", "D": "Follicular neoplasm, since grooved nuclei prove the presence of microfollicles"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Atypia of undetermined significance, since the features appear in too few cells for a diagnosis): When nuclear features of papillary carcinoma appear in only a few cells within a benign background, the case goes to atypia of undetermined significance. One groove does not prove malignancy. Ignoring the atypical cells and calling benign would miss a possible carcinoma. Follicular neoplasm depends on crowded microfollicles, which grooves do not represent."}, {"id": 1972, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A two-year-old child has an absolute lymphocyte count of 8.5 x 10^9/L, well above the adult reference range. The smear shows small normal-looking lymphocytes and no blasts, and the child is well. The technologist must decide how to interpret and report this result. How should the result be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Report a left shift, since lymphocytes above the adult range indicate infection", "B": "Report a leukemoid reaction, since a high white count with normal cells is seen", "C": "Report chronic lymphocytic leukemia, since the lymphocyte count is above the adult reference limit", "D": "Compare with the age-specific reference range, since children normally have more lymphocytes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Compare with the age-specific reference range, since children normally have more lymphocytes): Children normally have more lymphocytes than adults, so results must be compared with age-appropriate ranges. CLL is a disease of older adults, and a leukemoid reaction and left shift describe neutrophil changes, not a normal child's lymphocyte count."}, {"id": 1973, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A flow cytometry tube for suspected acute leukemia arrives clotted and three days old after being left in a warm transport bag on a loading dock. The clinician needs immunophenotyping urgently for treatment decisions. What should the technologist do with the specimen?", "options": {"A": "Run the tube after filtering out the clot and report the results normally", "B": "Run only the CD45 gating plot and report the diagnosis from that plot", "C": "Reject the specimen, inform the clinician promptly, and arrange urgent recollection of a fresh sample", "D": "Freeze the tube to stop deterioration and run it later in the week"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reject the specimen, inform the clinician promptly, and arrange urgent recollection of a fresh sample): Delay, warm temperatures and clotting reduce flow accuracy, so the specimen is unsuitable. Prompt communication and recollection serve the patient best. Filtering, freezing (never done for flow cytometry) or partial testing produce unreliable results."}, {"id": 1974, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A trainee is told that chromosomes can be counted and studied clearly only at one particular stage of the cell cycle, and that the laboratory adds a spindle inhibitor to trap cells there. The trainee must name this stage and explain why it is used for analysis. Which description of this stage is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Metaphase, when chromosomes are tightly coiled and easy to count and analyse", "B": "Interphase, when chromosomes are fully uncoiled and spread through the nucleus evenly", "C": "Prophase, when chromosomes have not yet formed and only the nucleolus is visible", "D": "Telophase, when chromosomes are pulled far apart and the cell membrane has formed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Metaphase, when chromosomes are tightly coiled and easy to count and analyse): Metaphase chromosomes are condensed and clearly visible, so cells are arrested there. In interphase the chromatin is uncoiled and cannot be counted. Chromosomes are not yet condensed in early prophase, and telophase cells have separated."}, {"id": 1975, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A quality manager reviews a cytotechnologist's workload. The technologist screens for long stretches without breaks and the detection rate is falling. Which combination of regulatory practices addresses this fatigue-related risk most directly in a gynecologic cytology laboratory? Her last quality review was six months ago.", "options": {"A": "Set workload limits per technologist and cap the time spent screening each day", "B": "Assign only negative-looking slides to the technologist for a full month to speed recovery", "C": "Remove the random rescreen program because it increases stress", "D": "Raise the daily slide target so the technologist becomes faster"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Set workload limits per technologist and cap the time spent screening each day): Workload limits and a cap on daily screening time are required measures because fatigue lowers detection. Raising targets worsens fatigue. Removing rescreening removes a control, and selective assignment does not address the cause."}, {"id": 1976, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A wedge smear has just been air dried, and the technologist intends to stain it with Wright-Giemsa. Before staining, the slide must be prepared properly, and the identifying information on the slide must survive the staining procedure. Which steps are correct?", "options": {"A": "Fix in formalin vapour and label with a water-soluble marker", "B": "Fix in methanol, and label the frosted end in pencil", "C": "Heat fix over a flame and label with a ballpoint pen", "D": "Stain without fixing and attach the adhesive label afterward"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fix in methanol, and label the frosted end in pencil): Romanowsky stains need a methanol-fixed, fully air dried smear, and pencil or a frosted label survives the stain and rinse. Formalin vapour is not used, and water-soluble ink would wash away. Heat fixing distorts blood cells and ink may dissolve in the stain. Unfixed cells lose detail."}, {"id": 1977, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "Before an arterial puncture at the wrist, the person collecting the sample checks that the hand will still receive blood if the radial artery is damaged or blocked. Which test confirms collateral circulation to the hand before the radial artery is used for blood gases?", "options": {"A": "The modified Allen test of ulnar and radial collateral flow", "B": "The Rumpel-Leede test of capillary fragility under a tourniquet", "C": "The tourniquet test of vein filling time at the elbow", "D": "The delta check comparing this result with previous values"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The modified Allen test of ulnar and radial collateral flow): The modified Allen test checks that the ulnar artery can supply the hand if the radial artery is compromised, so a puncture is only done when collateral flow is present. The Rumpel-Leede test assesses capillary fragility, a delta check compares a result with previous values, and heel refill is not used to judge hand circulation."}, {"id": 1978, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "During daily quality control, the technologist tests reagent anti-D with a known D-positive cell suspension and a known D-negative suspension. The D-positive cells give no reaction, and the D-negative cells also give no reaction. Several patient samples are waiting for Rh typing. What is the best course of action?", "options": {"A": "Stop patient testing, investigate the anti-D vial and technique, and repeat controls", "B": "Proceed with patient testing and report every negative result as D negative", "C": "Proceed with patient testing but repeat only the negative patient results", "D": "Replace the D-negative control cells with new cells and test patients at once"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop patient testing, investigate the anti-D vial and technique, and repeat controls): A D-positive control that fails to react shows the reagent or the test is not working, so no patient testing continues until the cause is found and controls give the expected result. Reporting negatives from a failed reagent would mistype D-positive patients as D negative. Changing only the negative control does not address the failed positive control."}, {"id": 1979, "domain": "histology", "question": "A processor was loaded with many cassettes packed tightly into a single basket, and blocks from the middle of the load show soft, poorly infiltrated centres while blocks at the edges cut well. Reagents were fresh and the run log shows no alarms. What is the most likely explanation for the pattern?", "options": {"A": "The edge cassettes were over-fixed, which caused the middle cassettes to appear soft.", "B": "The wax temperature was uneven, which made all the edge blocks finish earlier.", "C": "The alcohols were too strong, which shrank only the tissue placed in the middle.", "D": "Tight stacking blocked fluid flow, so central cassettes received less reagent."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tight stacking blocked fluid flow, so central cassettes received less reagent.): When cassettes are stacked too tightly, reagents cannot circulate around the middle ones, so dehydration, clearing and infiltration are incomplete there even though the solutions are fresh. That location-specific pattern points to load and not to chemistry. Fixation and alcohol strength would affect the whole load evenly, not only the centre."}, {"id": 1980, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A technologist compares handling of fresh unfixed sputum with a specimen that is already in fixative. A new employee suggests that fixed specimens can be treated as completely safe. The new employee has just completed training and is confident about the answer, but the trainer disagrees. What is the most accurate response?", "options": {"A": "Risk falls once fixed, but fixation does not make a specimen entirely safe", "B": "Fresh specimens are safer because living cells cannot carry infection", "C": "Fixed specimens are safe, so gloves and eye protection are unnecessary", "D": "Fixation increases infection risk because it releases organisms from the cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Risk falls once fixed, but fixation does not make a specimen entirely safe): Unfixed samples carry a higher infection risk, which falls with fixation, but all specimens are treated as potentially infectious and standard precautions continue. Fixatives such as formalin reduce but do not eliminate the risk, because some organisms survive short fixation, so gloves and eye protection stay required."}, {"id": 1981, "domain": "cytology", "question": "One urine slide from a woman contains large flat squamous cells, a few sperm, yeast and fibres, with no urothelial atypia. A junior screener is unsure whether these elements need comment. The senior technologist says a good screener learns these mimics first. How should the elements be handled?", "options": {"A": "Reject the specimen as unsatisfactory since contaminants invalidate any urothelial assessment", "B": "Report each as atypical squamous cells and advise cystoscopy for every case", "C": "Add SV40 immunostain to every slide since contaminants suggest polyomavirus infection", "D": "Identify them as contaminants or normal mimics, and avoid overcalling malignancy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Identify them as contaminants or normal mimics, and avoid overcalling malignancy): Vaginal squames, sperm, yeast and fibres are contaminants or normal mimics, and learning them prevents overcalling. Labelling them atypical would create false alarms. Their presence does not make urothelial cells unassessable. SV40 stains polyomavirus-infected cells and has no role for these background elements."}, {"id": 1982, "domain": "histology", "question": "A technologist realizes she placed a block in the wrong storage drawer yesterday and, separately, sees a colleague photocopy a patient report to take home and study. She is unsure whether to speak up because neither event has caused visible harm. What does professional practice require in both cases?", "options": {"A": "Say nothing about either, since a misfiled block is found eventually and copies are harmless.", "B": "Report only the record, since a misfiled block is her own private concern.", "C": "Report the misfiled block and the confidentiality breach, since owning errors supports quality.", "D": "Fix the misfiled block quietly but ignore the record, since records belong to the physician."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the misfiled block and the confidentiality breach, since owning errors supports quality.): Professionalism means owning mistakes, reporting near-misses and protecting patient confidentiality, because a misfiled block may be impossible to recover when a case is requested later. Taking records home for personal use breaches privacy rules whether or not harm follows. A quiet fix conceals a quality problem that the laboratory could otherwise correct."}, {"id": 1983, "domain": "clinical_chemistry", "question": "An emergency patient with chest pain has a high-sensitivity troponin result above the 99th percentile upper reference limit at presentation. The physician asks what additional laboratory step supports a diagnosis of acute myocardial infarction. Which step does the technologist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Measure amylase to demonstrate a pancreatic source of the chest pain", "B": "Repeat the troponin after 14 days to demonstrate a normal baseline", "C": "Measure CK-BB to demonstrate that the injury arises from brain tissue", "D": "Repeat the troponin within a few hours to demonstrate a rise or fall"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeat the troponin within a few hours to demonstrate a rise or fall): A result above the 99th percentile with a rise or fall on serial samples supports myocardial infarction when the clinical picture fits, so repeat testing within hours is used. Waiting 14 days is too late. CK-BB and amylase do not confirm cardiac injury."}, {"id": 1984, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In a lecture on how the cervix changes through life, an instructor shows a newborn's cervix and that of a woman in her twenties under estrogen influence. Where does the squamocolumnar junction lie at birth and puberty, and what does estrogen do to the columnar epithelium?", "options": {"A": "On the ectocervix, with columnar epithelium extending outward as estrogen levels rise", "B": "Deep within the canal, with squamous epithelium retreating upward as estrogen levels rise", "C": "On the vaginal wall, with columnar epithelium converting to keratinized cells at puberty", "D": "At the internal os, with glandular epithelium staying fixed as hormone levels change"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (On the ectocervix, with columnar epithelium extending outward as estrogen levels rise): At birth and puberty the junction lies on the ectocervix, and estrogen allows columnar epithelium to extend outward. Later acid exposure drives metaplasia and the junction moves inward. The junction moves upward into the canal only after menopause."}, {"id": 1985, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist processes two patient blood specimens with similar surnames in the same biological safety cabinet during the morning after both arrived together on the same courier run. To prevent cross-contamination and specimen mix-ups, which practice should the technologist follow while handling the two specimens?", "options": {"A": "Label the flasks after set-up, and check identifiers only when the results are ready", "B": "Open both tubes at once, and share one pipette to save time between the two cases", "C": "Handle one patient at a time, change pipettes between cases, and confirm two identifiers each step", "D": "Keep both patients' tubes in one rack without labels, and separate them by colour"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Handle one patient at a time, change pipettes between cases, and confirm two identifiers each step): Working on one patient at a time, changing pipettes and checking two identifiers at each step prevents cross-contamination and mix-ups. Sharing pipettes, delayed labelling and unlabelled racks all risk errors."}, {"id": 1986, "domain": "cytology", "question": "In the cytology laboratory, A pleural fluid contains atypical cells, and the clinical question is whether a lung primary is responsible for the malignancy. Which marker pair on a cell block would best support a lung adenocarcinoma origin for the cells?", "options": {"A": "GATA3 staining with CK20, which suggest a breast or urothelial origin", "B": "Calretinin and WT1 staining, which point toward a mesothelial cell origin", "C": "TTF-1 and napsin A staining, which support a pulmonary primary origin", "D": "Kappa and lambda light chain staining, which suggest a lymphoid clonal process"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (TTF-1 and napsin A staining, which support a pulmonary primary origin): TTF-1 and napsin A support a lung primary. Calretinin and WT1 point toward mesothelium, GATA3 supports breast or urothelial origin, and kappa and lambda light chains are used to judge lymphoid clonality. Marker choice follows the diagnostic question being asked."}, {"id": 1987, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A teaching laboratory demonstrates the tube method for ABO grouping. Students mix reagent and patient red cell suspension, spin the tubes in a centrifuge, and gently resuspend the button to read agglutination. Which statement describes a strength and a limitation of the method?", "options": {"A": "It requires no centrifuge, but depends on antigen fixed to the walls of a well", "B": "It is well suited to full automation, but produces a mat of cells over the well", "C": "It is inexpensive and flexible, but depends on reader skill and even centrifugation", "D": "It gives stable results for later review, but must be protected from drying and bubbles"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It is inexpensive and flexible, but depends on reader skill and even centrifugation): The tube method is low cost and adaptable, though results rely on the reader's technique and on consistent spin conditions. Stable, reviewable results and protection from drying describe gel cards. The mat of cells over a well and automation describe solid phase testing."}, {"id": 1988, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A mycology request arrives for a skin scraping, and the technologist plans the incubation. Compared with routine bacterial cultures, how are fungal cultures usually incubated? The jar was set up carefully with a catalyst, and the technologist is checking whether the setup worked before opening it later.", "options": {"A": "At about 30 degrees Celsius for longer periods", "B": "At 4 degrees Celsius in a refrigerator for one day", "C": "At 35 degrees Celsius in a candle jar for six hours", "D": "At 42 degrees Celsius for exactly 24 hours"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (At about 30 degrees Celsius for longer periods): Fungi generally grow at about 30 degrees Celsius and need extended incubation because many grow slowly. The other temperatures and times listed are for other organisms or would not allow the fungi to grow at all."}, {"id": 1989, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A patient with a heart murmur and low-grade fever for several weeks has blood cultures that grow alpha-hemolytic gram-positive cocci. They are optochin resistant and bile insoluble. Which group is most likely, and what infection is classically associated with it?", "options": {"A": "Streptococcus pneumoniae, a well-known cause of subacute endocarditis and normal mouth flora", "B": "Viridans group streptococci, a well-known cause of subacute endocarditis and normal mouth flora", "C": "Group B streptococci, a well-known cause of subacute endocarditis and normal vaginal flora", "D": "Enterococcus faecalis, a well-known cause of subacute endocarditis and normal skin flora in routine practice"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Viridans group streptococci, a well-known cause of subacute endocarditis and normal mouth flora): Alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant, bile-insoluble cocci are viridans streptococci, normal mouth flora and a well-known cause of subacute endocarditis. Streptococcus pneumoniae is optochin susceptible and bile soluble. Enterococcus and group B do not match the alpha-hemolysis and optochin pattern."}, {"id": 1990, "domain": "histology", "question": "A safety officer audits the histology laboratory and checks emergency equipment near the formalin station, where several litres of concentrated solution are decanted every day. Which items must be within easy reach of workers and tested regularly according to good practice?", "options": {"A": "A spare set of cassettes and a phone list, checked once every few years.", "B": "A microtome cover and a spare knife blade, with a monthly inventory only.", "C": "A fire blanket and a first aid poster, with no need for regular testing.", "D": "An eyewash station, safety shower and formalin spill kit with neutralizer."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An eyewash station, safety shower and formalin spill kit with neutralizer.): Eyewash stations and showers must be close to the hazard and flushed and tested on a schedule so they work when needed, and a spill kit with neutralizer lets staff contain a formalin spill safely. Posters, spare cassettes and microtome supplies are not emergency safety equipment. A fire blanket does not treat chemical splashes."}, {"id": 1991, "domain": "transfusion_medicine", "question": "A hospital wants to identify weak points in its transfusion process. A quality lead proposes that staff who report near misses should be disciplined to discourage mistakes. What is the best response based on the chapter's view of hemovigilance culture?", "options": {"A": "Agree, since blame is the best way to lower the number of near misses over time", "B": "Disagree, since near misses have no value for improving the transfusion service overall", "C": "Disagree, since open reporting without blame is required for a safe service", "D": "Agree, since near misses should be reported only when they cause harm to a patient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Disagree, since open reporting without blame is required for a safe service): Open reporting without blame is required, because reviewing near misses shows where the system is weak. Discipline discourages reporting and hides the problems. Near misses are collected even when no patient is harmed, and they are valuable for improving the service."}, {"id": 1992, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A laboratory introduces an automated screening device that uses imaging to select fields of view for a technologist to review on gynaecologic slides. A staff member asks who holds responsibility for the final interpretation of each slide once the device is in use, and whether workload still needs recording.", "options": {"A": "The pathologist owns every negative interpretation, so the screener workload is not recorded", "B": "The device owns the final interpretation, so workload records are no longer required", "C": "The manufacturer owns the interpretation, and the laboratory records only the device output", "D": "The human reviewer owns the final interpretation, and the workload is still recorded"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The human reviewer owns the final interpretation, and the workload is still recorded): Automated devices change the workflow, but a qualified person still makes and owns the interpretation. Workload continues to be recorded so limits can be shown to have been followed. The device only selects fields for review, and a manufacturer or a machine cannot hold professional responsibility for a report."}, {"id": 1993, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A curator reads that a software tool predicts a newly found missense change to be damaging to the protein, and no other evidence exists yet for the variant. Population databases, family data, and laboratory studies are all silent. How much weight does the computational prediction carry in classifying this variant?", "options": {"A": "It is enough alone to classify the variant as benign under the guideline", "B": "It is enough alone to classify the variant as pathogenic under the guideline", "C": "It gives only supporting evidence and is insufficient on its own to classify", "D": "It carries no weight because software output is excluded from classification frameworks"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It gives only supporting evidence and is insufficient on its own to classify): Computational predictions of protein effect count as supporting evidence but are not enough by themselves. Classification combines several kinds of evidence, such as population frequency, segregation, functional studies, and prior reports. Guidelines do not exclude predictions entirely, and one prediction cannot establish either extreme tier."}, {"id": 1994, "domain": "hematology", "question": "A laboratory assays a patient on rivaroxaban for lupus anticoagulant with a clot-based test. The result is positive, yet the patient has no history of clots or pregnancy loss. What is the most appropriate interpretation? The physician asks whether the finding is reliable.", "options": {"A": "It may be falsely positive because DOACs interfere with clot-based lupus tests", "B": "The result is a true positive because rivaroxaban prevents factor Xa formation", "C": "The result is invalid because DOACs cannot be present in a plasma sample", "D": "The result is a true positive because DOACs strengthen clot-based lupus anticoagulant tests"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It may be falsely positive because DOACs interfere with clot-based lupus tests): DOACs can produce false results in clot-based tests including lupus anticoagulant, so a positive result may be false. They do not strengthen the assay, are of course present in plasma, and do not make the result a true positive."}, {"id": 1995, "domain": "cytology", "question": "A vaginal vault sample from a 58-year-old after total hysterectomy for benign disease shows squamous cells and no endocervical or metaplastic cells. A trainee flags the slide for lacking a transformation zone component. Why is this expected, and how is it explained?", "options": {"A": "It indicates atrophy, and the technologist should recommend a short course of estrogen", "B": "It suggests the brush was misused, and a repeat sample is needed to check", "C": "It is a sampling failure, so the slide should be rejected as unsatisfactory for evaluation", "D": "It is expected, because the cervix was removed, and the history explains the absence"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is expected, because the cervix was removed, and the history explains the absence): After hysterectomy there is no cervix, so no transformation zone cells can be collected. The clinical history on the requisition explains the finding. It is not a sampling failure and does not need repeating."}, {"id": 1996, "domain": "cytogenetics", "question": "A technologist making slides on a humid afternoon finds that the chromosomes spread poorly, with much cytoplasm still surrounding them and few well-separated metaphases. The suspension itself is clean and at a good density. Which adjustment reflects good practice when dropping cells onto slides?", "options": {"A": "Switch to the hypotonic solution, then drop again from the highest possible position", "B": "Drop the cells onto dry slides only, then discard the test slide immediately", "C": "Add more colcemid to the suspension, then make the remaining slides in one run", "D": "Adjust drop height, slide angle and environment, then check a test slide before making more"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Adjust drop height, slide angle and environment, then check a test slide before making more): Humidity, temperature, drop height and slide angle all affect spreading, so they are adjusted and a test slide is checked. Colcemid and hypotonic solution do not fix spreading at this stage, and skipping the test slide wastes material."}, {"id": 1997, "domain": "clinical_microbiology", "question": "A technologist stains a sputum smear for acid-fast bacilli and chooses a cold method with a higher phenol concentration in the carbol fuchsin, so the slide does not need heating over a flame. Which stain is this, and how do mycobacteria appear on it after counterstaining?", "options": {"A": "Ziehl-Neelsen stain, which needs heated carbol fuchsin, and mycobacteria appear red on a blue background", "B": "Kinyoun stain, and mycobacteria appear blue or green against a red counterstained background", "C": "Gram stain with a safranin counterstain, and mycobacteria appear as strongly purple gram-positive rods", "D": "Kinyoun stain, which uses cold carbol fuchsin, and mycobacteria appear red on a blue background"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Kinyoun stain, which uses cold carbol fuchsin, and mycobacteria appear red on a blue background): The Kinyoun method is the cold version of Ziehl-Neelsen, with extra phenol so heating is not needed. After acid-alcohol decolourization and a blue or green counterstain, mycobacteria stay red. Ziehl-Neelsen needs heat, reversing the colours is wrong, and mycobacteria do not take up the Gram stain well."}, {"id": 1998, "domain": "cytology", "question": "ROSE on a mediastinal node aspirate shows a monotonous atypical lymphoid population with lymphoglandular bodies. The operator has completed two passes. The technologist reports back to the procedure room. What is the most useful message that lets the team collect the right material for the next pass?", "options": {"A": "Nondiagnostic, so repeat the same pass in alcohol until epithelial cells appear.", "B": "Lymphoid population present, so collect extra passes into RPMI for flow cytometry.", "C": "Benign reactive node, so no further passes or ancillary testing are needed.", "D": "Adequate as carcinoma, so stop sampling and send all remaining passes in formalin."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lymphoid population present, so collect extra passes into RPMI for flow cytometry.): A monotonous lymphoid population raises lymphoma, and ROSE lets the team triage extra passes into culture medium for flow cytometry. Calling it carcinoma or a benign reactive node contradicts the smear. Alcohol would kill the cells needed for flow."}, {"id": 1999, "domain": "molecular_diagnostics", "question": "A panel run shows a mean depth of 480x, meeting the target, but exon 3 of a reported gene has only 12 reads at every position while the laboratory minimum is 100x. The other exons are well covered. What should the technologist do about a negative result for this gene?", "options": {"A": "Report exon 3 as negative but add a comment that depth was somewhat low", "B": "Report exon 3 as inadequately covered, not negative, and repeat or supplement the testing", "C": "Average exon 3 with neighbouring exons so that the gene passes the minimum", "D": "Report the whole gene as negative, since the mean depth of 480x meets the target"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report exon 3 as inadequately covered, not negative, and repeat or supplement the testing): Mean depth can hide poor uniformity, and a region below the minimum cannot support a negative call, so it is reported as inadequate and repeated or supplemented. Averaging across exons conceals the gap. A negative with a mild comment still overstates what was tested."}, {"id": 2000, "domain": "histology", "question": "Sections from a routine block emerge from the flotation bath with several persistent wrinkles that will not flatten. The technologist notes that the water feels cool to the touch and the wax is a standard grade. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Stain the wrinkled section as it is and add a comment", "B": "Dry the wrinkled section on the slide with extra oven heat", "C": "Raise the bath above the wax melting point and refloat", "D": "Warm the bath to the correct range and refloat or recut"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Warm the bath to the correct range and refloat or recut): Water that is too cool leaves wrinkles, so the bath is brought into the correct range and the section is reflattened or recut. Going above the melting point would melt the tissue. Drying or staining the wrinkles in place bakes in the artifact."}];
